{DrDave: 'Trying to refute an opponent's opening Sushant Banerji: 'The legendary Mikhail Tal was the finest ever exponent of attacking chess. His all-out sacrificial style took him all the way to the World Championship title in 1960, when he defeated Botvinnik in the World title match. Though the eighth World Champion Tal lost the return match, his keenly combinational and uncompromising style won him a big number of fans in the chess world.''} 1. d4 {1) Tal and Koblencs in the Tournament Bulletin.} Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. e3 b6 4. Bd3 Bb7 5. O-O Be7 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'This is the Modern Benoni Defence. Here Black develops his Bishop on g7 by playing... g6 and the move... c5 gives Black control of the dark squares in the centre.''} 6. Nbd2 c5 7. b3 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Not the best square for the Bishop. Better would have been 7. Nf3, 8.h3 and then placing the Bishop to d3 as per the latest theory.''} Nc6 (7... O-O) 8. a3 O-O 9. Bb2 d6 {DrDave: 'So far, so typical of the Modern Benoni. At the time this formation was still considered rather suspect (and maybe it still is in some circles), so Averbakh adopts a very active plan to 'punish' this cheeky opening, targeting d6. Sushant Banerji: 'Black puts pressure on the e - Pawn and also prevents its advance. Later Black plays on the Queenside for... b5,... b4 etc.''} (9... Rc8 10. Re1 d6 11. Nf1 Qc7 (11... g6) 12. Ng3 Rfd8 $13 13. c4 d5) 10. c4 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'White plans to develop his Bishop to f4 before playing Nd2. As will be seen, Tal brilliantly refutes this idea. It looks better to play 10. Nd2 as recommended by theory.''} (10. Qe2 $17) 10... Rc8 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Black's idea is to play... Nc7,... Rb8 followed by... b5. Also the Knight can be used for other purpose as demonstrated in the game.''} 11. Rc1 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Better was 11. Re1 Nc7 12. Bf4 Rb8 13. a4 a6 14. a5 b5 15. axb5 Rb6 with chances for both the sides.''} Qc7 12. Qc2 {[#] [#] DrDave: 'White's formation is just for the moment in need of a few moves for perfect comfort. Tal decides the time to strike is now!'} (12. Bb1 $14 {Koblencs}) 12... h6 $6 {Tal/Koblencs Tal/Koblencs DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '!!' Sushant Banerji: 'It was not possible to play 12...Nfd5 due to 13. exd Bc3 14. bxc Re2 15. cxb''} (12... g6) 13. Rfd1 (13. Rfe1) 13... Qb8 (13... cxd4 14. exd4 d5) 14. Qb1 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Forced. Since after 14. Bd3 Nd3 15 Qd3 Be4 Black remains a Pawn up. Black has compensation since he will get another Pawn on d5 with a tempo and also White pieces are in a cramped position. Practically it is difficult for White to find the best path.''} (14. d5 exd5 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. cxd5 Ne5 $13 17. Nxe5 Bxe5 18. f4 Bf6 19. Nf3) 14... cxd4 {[#] [#] DrDave: 'This sacrifice of a Knight for central Pawns is seen repeatedly in Tal's play, and his practical results have been excellent.'} 15. Nxd4 $2 {DrDave: '?!'} (15. exd4 d5) 15... Nxd4 $19 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '!''} 16. Bxd4 d5 17. Qb2 Ng4 18. Nf1 Bf6 {DrDave: 'The scrapping has led to a winning position for Black. Opposite coloured Bishops only draw if they are the only things on the board!'} (18... dxc4 19. Be2 $11 Nf6) 19. Be2 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Instead 19. Bb7? is met by 19...Rab8. Now White is having some hope in the opposite coloured Bishop endgame/middlegame.''} Bxd4 (19... Ne5 20. cxd5 Bxd5 21. f3 Rfd8) 20. exd4 Nf6 (20... Qf4) 21. c5 bxc5 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Black is a Pawn up but the most important factor is that it provides a fine outpost for the Bishop in the centre.''} 22. dxc5 e5 (22... Bc6 23. b4 Bb5) 23. Ne3 (23. b4) 23... d4 24. Nf5 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'White has placed all his Pawns on the light squares and avoids giving Black any target of attack. Now White is planning to exchange a pair of Rooks which will ease the pressure. Even after that the material on the board is enough for Tal to build up the decisive attack.''} Rc7 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '!' Sushant Banerji: 'Here 24...Qe6 would have been met by 25. Qc4 and 24...Qe7 with 25. Bc6.''} 25. b4 (25. f4) 25... Be4 (25... Nd5 26. Bf3) 26. Nd6 Rxc5 27. Rxc5 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'White puts his King on a White square and plans to play g3 as a counter to Blacks... Qf4 and... Be5.''} Qxd6 28. f4 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '(28. Bc4?, Be5 28. Qg6+ (29. g3, Qf3+ with the idea of 30...h3) 29...Kf8 wins for Black.)''} (28. f3 {with many threats -... g5 - g4,... d5 and... Nd4.} Bb7 29. f4 Nd7 30. Rb5 Qc6 31. Bf1 Ba6 32. Ra5 Bxf1 $5 33. Kxf1) 28... Nd7 29. Ra5 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Intending Bf3.''} (29. Rc4 Nb6 30. fxe5 Qg6 31. g3 Nxc4 32. Bxc4 {White can achieve equality since Black seems to have good attacking prospects on the Queenside.} d3 33. Qd4 $1 Qg4 34. Re1 d2 35. Qxd2 Qf3) 29... d3 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '!''} (29... Rc8 30. Bd3 Bxd3 31. Rxd3 $14 Rc4 32. fxe5 $16 Nxe5 {Sushant Banerji: 'Black has fully developed and centralised pieces, in addition to two Pawns for a piece.'} 33. Rg3 $1 Kh8) 30. Bf1 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: 'Better would have been 30. Kg2, since 30...Bf2 is met by 31. Qc2. So Black may continue 30...Bc3 31. Bd3 (Else 31...Rd2) 31...Qc6+ 32. Kg1 Qd5 33. Be2 Qg5 34. Qc1 Qf6 35. Rd1 Bd4 36. Rf1 hxg 37. hxg Qe5! winning a second Pawn.''} (30. Bxd3 $17 Bxd3 31. fxe5 Qg6 32. Rd5 $1 {and the Pawn on e3 is lost with a winning position for Black.} Bf5 $142 {DrDave: 'e.g. Sushant Banerji: '!' Sushant Banerji: 'Looks to be the best move.''} 33. Rxd7 Bxd7 34. Rxd7 Rc8 35. Rd2 Qe4) 30... exf4 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '!''} 31. Qf2 {[#] [#] DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '(31. Qf3, Qf3+ and 32...hxg is hopeless for White.)''} (31. Bxd3 Bxd3 32. Qe2 Qd4+ 33. Qf2 Qc4 34. Qd2 $19 {DrDave: 'Many alternatives were available at this stage.'} Be2 $2 35. Re1 Bg4) 31... a6 {DrDave: 'Sushant Banerji: '!''} (31... Rc8 32. Qe1 $19 Rc4 33. Rd2 Qg6 34. h3 f3 35. a4 Qb6+ 36. Kh2 fxg2 37. Bxg2 Rc2) 32. h3 (32. b5 $19 {DrDave: 'when 18. Qd1 and 18. Qc1 can be met by 18...Qg5 and 18...Re8 respectively.'} axb5 33. Rxb5 f5 34. Bxd3 Bxd3 35. Qa2+ Kh8 36. Qd5 Qxd5 37. Rxd5 Be2 38. Rxd7 Bxd1 39. Rxd1 $19) 32... Rc8 33. Rd2 Rc1 34. b5 axb5 35. Rxb5 {DrDave: 'Last try would have been 35. Qe5+ Kh7 36. Qf6! When 36...Qh3 leads to a draw after 37. Qf7+ Kh6 38. Qf8+ Kh5 39. Qh8+ Kg4 40. Qc8+ Kh4 41. Qh8+ etc. However after 36...Qd5+ 37. Bg2 Qg2+ 38. Kg2 Bd4+ Black wins.'} Ne5 {DrDave: 'After 36. Qb5 Rf1+ 37. Bf1 Qe4+ 38. Bg2 Qh4 Black wins. 1-0 (35) Averbakh,Y-Tal,M Riga 1958 [Sushant Banerji]'} 36. Rb6 Qc7 37. Rb4 f5 38. a4 f3 39. Qe3 {0-1 (35) Averbakh-Tal,M Riga 1958 [DrDave]} Rxf1+ 40. Kxf1 Qc1+ 0-1

