{Mikhail Tal: 'Although it had given me a one-point advantage, the start of the match had not completely satisfied me. In the last four games my opponent had been able to direct the play along his favourite channels and had invariably held the initiative. To expect that I would be able to continue gaining draws after prolonged defence would have been highly frivolous. Therefore we decided at all costs to change the character of the play. Once again the already tiresome question was on the agenda: which opening to play? Both the Nimzo-Indian Defence and the Modern Benoni had in general not produced the desired effect. There remained one more double-edged opening – the classical King's Indian Defence, and it was this that we chose. A definite role in this was played by the fact that against the King's Indian Botvinnik usually chooses a rather old-fashioned continuation with the fianchetto of the king's bishop, which, in my opinion, does not give White any opening advantage, and avoids the fashionable lines (the Samisch and Petrosian Variations) which are considered White's most dangerous weapons. The very first moves confirmed the correctness of our assumption. ' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E69: King's Indian: Fianchetto: 6...Nbd7 7 0-0 e5 8 e4 c6 9 h3 ' GM Mihail Marin: 'This is one of Tal's most famous and controversial games at the same time. I find it instructive because it illustrates one of the ideas mentioned at the end of the previous game. Tal conducted the strategic phase with only one aim: To sacrifice a knight on f4. He did everything right in the process, but his evaluation of the whole plan was over-optimistic. Little wonder that a few days after the game Botvinnik's second Goldberg found a refutation of the combination.' Mikhail Tal: 'Although it had given me a one-point advantage, the start of the match had not completely satisfied me. In the last four games my opponent had been able to direct the play along his favourite channels and had invariably held the initiative. To expect that I would be able to continue gaining draws after prolonged defence would have been highly frivolous. Therefore we decided at all costs to change the character of the play. Once again the already tiresome question was on the agenda: which opening to play? Both the Nimzo-Indian Defence and the Modern Benoni had in general not produced the desired effect. There remained one more double-edged opening – the classical King's Indian Defence, and it was this that we chose. A definite role in this was played by the fact that against the King's Indian Botvinnik usually chooses a rather old-fashioned continuation with the fianchetto of the king's bishop, which, in my opinion, does not give White any opening advantage, and avoids the fashionable lines (the Samisch and Petrosian Variations) which are considered White's most dangerous weapons. The very first moves confirmed the correctness of our assumption.  Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E69: King's Indian: Fianchetto: 6...Nbd7 7 0-0 e5 8 e4 c6 9 h3 '' Mueller,Karsten: '1.1 Hyperactive players For them, material is only a less important evaluation criterion. The young Mikhail Tal is a prime example and the only hyperactive player who has made it to the world championship throne so far. Typical quotes are: 'There are correct sacrifices and mine', 'Centralize and sacrifice', 'The opponent can only ever capture one piece per move' and 'If the opponent attacks one of your pieces, attack two of his'. This style makes for very entertaining games, and of course exciting endgames full of content are extremely rare. But spectacular mate attacks and intuitive sacrifices do, as in the following case:' Mueller,Karsten: '1.1 Hyperaktivspieler Für sie stellt Material nur ein weniger wichtiges Bewertungskriterium dar. Der junge Michail Tal ist ein Paradebeispiel und der einzige Hyperaktivspieler, der es bisher auf den Weltmeisterthron geschafft hat. Typische Zitate sind: "Es gibt korrekte Opfer und meine", "Zentralisiere und opfere", "Der Gegner kann stets nur eine Figur pro Zug schlagen" und "Wenn der Gegner eine deiner Figuren angreift, dann greife zwei von ihm an". Dieser Stil sorgt für sehr unterhaltsame Partien und spannende inhaltsreiche Endspiele entstehen natürlich nur äußerst selten. Dafür spektakuläre Mattangriffe und intuitive Opfer, wie im folgenden Fall:'} {Mikhail Tal: 'Thus after' Mikhail Tal: 'In the event of' Mikhail Tal: 'By maintaining the symmetry with'} 1. c4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s) 1) Kirillov,V: Mikhail Tal 1949-1961. Riga. 1996. p.217-229. GM Mihail Marin 1) Kirillov,V: Mikhail Tal 1949-1961. Riga. 1996. p.217-229. Mikhail Tal: 'This, along with 1 d4, is Botvinnik's favourite move. The aim of it, in particular, is to avoid undesirable opening lines, such as the Nimzo-Indian Defence, for example.' GM Mihail Marin: '1) Kirillov, V: Mikhail Tal 1949-1961. Riga. 1996. p. 217-229.'} e5 2. g3 {Mikhail Tal: 'To some extent White declares his intentions. He emphasises that he does not intend to play the Nimzo-Indian Defence, nor in the King's Indian Defence to play the sharp Samisch Variation, in which, as is known, a basic element of White's play is the propping-up of his centre by f2-f3.'} f5 {Mikhail Tal: 'The King's Indian Defence has an interesting history. It received wide recognition some twenty years ago. Before that the opening was only employed from time to time and, so to speak, spontaneously. In particular, such a set-up was chosen long ago by Chigorin, but White's superiority in the centre, the apparent passivity of the bishop at g7, running up against its own pawn at e5, and Black's cramped position did not inspire much confidence. In the late 1930s a group of Ukrainian players led by Konstantinopolsky, Boleslavsky and Bronstein set about making a detailed study of this rejected opening, and gave it a second wind. Which chess enthusiast is not familiar with David Bronstein's virtuoso combinations in his games against Pachman and Zita (Moscow v. Prague match, 1946), in which the 'inactive' bishop at g7 carried out its destructive work? For this, it is true, Black sacrificed 'only' a rook. Which chess enthusiast has not been enraptured by the brilliant ideas of the King's Indian devotees' 'younger brother', the Odessa grandmaster Yefim Geller, in whose hands this opening has become a formidable weapon? New systems of development for Black appeared, and the move ...e7-e5 ceased to be obligatory; in many games Black attacked the centre with ...c7-c5, or sometimes altogether avoided moving his c- and e-pawns, preferring piece pressure on the centre with ...Nc6 and ..Bg4. The idea of immediate counterplay on the queenside with ...a7-a6 and ...b7-b5 also appeared. As a rule, Black was able to carry out all these ideas most successfully with the white king's bishop at g2, and so players of the King's Indian for White also began employing other set-ups. They revived the Sämisch Variation, which usually leads to a very sharp battle with castling on opposite sides, where White tries to demonstrate that the move ...g7-g6 significantly weakens Black's kingside. Initially this variation brought White some success, but by the efforts of faithful King's Indian players (in particular Geller and Gligoric) sufficiently effective antidotes were found. Grandmaster Tigran Petrosian in turn suggested a very dangerous idea, involving the development of the bishop at g5 (from where it hinders Black's counterplay on the kingside), followed by the advance of the white pawns on the opposite wing, with the aim of opening lines. For the moment the last word for Black in this variation belongs to the talented Ukrainian master Leonid Stein, who played ...h7-h6 before Bg5 and thereby nipped White's idea in the bud, true, at the cost of a tempo. All the same, recent events have shown that the fashion for the King's Indian Defence has begun to pass, and even such devotees as Petrosian, Bronstein and Geller have frequently chosen other set-ups. Perhaps this has happened because its novelty has faded, perhaps because White's play has become more purposeful, but in any case it is far too early to write off the King's Indian Defence, since, even if does give White a slight advantage, Black achieves just as viable a position as in any other opening.'} 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. Nc3 c6 (4... d6 {GM Mihail Marin: 'White can play'}) {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 5. d4 {Mikhail Tal: 'White gradually determines his pawn configuration.' Mikhail Tal: 'Black has to decide which variation to choose. He can switch to the Grünfeld Defence with'} e4 6. Bg5 (6. Nh3 Bb4 (6... Na6 {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'is once again the best answer.'} 7. O-O {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): ''} Nc7 $1 {Mueller,Karsten: ''} 8. f3 {Mueller,Karsten: '?!'} d5 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Nf4 Ne6 {Mikhail Tal: ', when Black's three pawns do not outweigh White's extra piece. It would seem that in this case the chances of the two sides are roughly equal. But how could Black gain an advantage, if White played the initial part of the game slightly passively, but very solidly? Probably the creation of a position, in which White has to make do with a draw, is in itself a considerable achievement for Black. When I played the text-move, I reckoned that the game should end in a draw by repetition. Perhaps all those observing the game were of the same opinion, and it was only a few days later that grandmaster Salo Flohr found a beautiful winning possibility for White.' GM Mihail Marin: '=' GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal was unhappy because Black's three pawns do not outweigh White's extra piece.'} 12. Qa4+ {with a slight white advantage, but here too the black activity is very considerable. mit leichtem weißen Vorteil, aber auch hier ist die schwarze Aktivität sehr beachtlich.} Kf7 {GM Mihail Marin Mueller,Karsten: '!' Mueller,Karsten: 'Kasparov gives' Mueller,Karsten: 'Kasparov gibt'} 13. Nxe6 Bxe6 $11 {is also playable} 14. fxe4 dxe4 15. Kh1 h5 16. Rad1 Kg6 {and the black initiative at least outweighs the sacrificed material. und die schwarze Initiative wiegt das geopferte Material mindestens auf.} 17. h4 {GM Mihail Marin} Qd6 18. Rf4 Rhc8 {Unlike in the game and in the lines from the next comment, White does not have Be4, while Nb5 does not make sense with the e-file defended, either.} 19. Bh3 Rxc3 $1 20. bxc3 Nd5 {Black will soon have decisive threats along the second rank.} 21. Bxe7 {Mikhail Tal: 'is also unsuccessful. However, after' GM Mihail Marin: '?' GM Mihail Marin: 'exposes the bishop in the following sequence:'}) (6... Be7 7. d5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black is not obliged to sacrifice his queen, as Konstantinopolsky gives in his analysis, but can advantageously continue'} d6 $1 {Mikhail Tal: '. After'} 8. O-O Nbd7 9. b3 O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'Black's attack is irresistible – apart from anything else the rook at a1 is attacked.' GM Mihail Marin: ', with a decisive attack.'} {if White does not play 27 Qxc4 he has to reckon, among other things, with the positional threat of} 10. f3 Qa5 11. Qd2 {GM Mihail Marin: '?' GM Mihail Marin: 'Once again exposes the king along the back rank.'} cxd5 {Mikhail Tal: ', while after'} 12. cxd5 b5 13. Nf4 b4 14. Na4 Ne5 15. Bb2 Bd7 16. Ne6 Rfc8 {Mikhail Tal: 'the weakness of the queenside is again in evidence.'} 17. fxe4 {GM Mihail Marin} fxe4 18. Rad1 $1 $19 {Mikhail Tal: 'or'} Neg4 19. h3 {GM Mihail Marin: '!'} {Mikhail Tal: ', when' GM Mihail Marin: 'The point is that'} Ne5 {GM Mihail Marin: 'allows'} 20. Bxe5 $19 {Mikhail Tal: 'is not possible' GM Mihail Marin: ', pinning the knight.'} dxe5 {Mikhail Tal: ''' Mikhail Tal: 'It is hardly worth trying to convince the reader that Black had evaluated in detail all the variations and had decided that the knight sacrifice at f4 would win. Rather, 21...Nf4 was a purely positional sacrifice. Let us in fact look at the position that arises in the variation recommended by Goldberg after' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'JvR: 'A good try is'' GM Mihail Marin: 'But the intermediate move'} 21. Qg5 {Mikhail Tal: 'would have won the game'. This was the essence of an article by Goldberg with an analysis of this position, published in a bulletin after the match. Botvinnik's second goes on to give numerous interesting variations confirming this viewpoint. The critical position arises after' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'JvR: '!'' GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: ', analyzed by Goldberg in the match bulletin, would have questioned Black's whole approach. The point is that after' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''}) 7. O-O O-O $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'White obtains significantly the more pleasant ending. Thus the storm initiated by Black could have rebounded on him, but all this occurred because of his mistake on the 23rd move. As I have already pointed out, Botvinnik considered the capture on f3 to be favourable to Black, and he preferred the immediate exchange of queens. Psychologically this is quite understandable: when you are a piece up and are under attack, it is always pleasant to get rid of the opponent's queen, but in his calculations White underestimated the strength of Black's 26th move.' GM Mihail Marin: 'Flohr' Flohr} {.} 8. Qb3 {Mikhail Tal: 'not'} Na6 9. a3 {Mikhail Tal: ', which both players had considered'} Ba5 10. c5+ Kh8 {If} 11. Bf4 {GM Mihail Marin Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Bc7 12. f3 {White has a choice already.} Bxf4 $5 13. Nxf4 d5 14. cxd6 Qxd6 {, with a decisive counterattack.} 15. fxe4 {as in the later line also works.} {GM Mihail Marin: 'One of the reasons why Tal kept the e5-square free was that'} Qxd4+ {Mikhail Tal: 'would have lost to' GM Mihail Marin: 'can be met with'} 16. Kh1 $1 {Mikhail Tal: ', when there is no defence against the numerous threats, for example:'} Nc5 17. Qd1 Qe5 18. Nd3 Nxd3 19. Qxd3 {Mikhail Tal: '. White gives up the exchange, with the aim of seizing the initiative.' GM Mihail Marin: 'wins.'} {Mikhail Tal: 'or'} fxe4 20. Nxe4 {GM Mihail Marin: '!'} {Mikhail Tal: 'While I was thinking, I had already written down on my scoresheet' Mikhail Tal: 'After' Mikhail Tal: 'I rejected' GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal deviated from his initial intention'} Bg4 {Mikhail Tal: ', a move which, as it transpires, was the strongest. After it White cannot play' Mikhail Tal: 'in view of the variation' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'after establishing that it would only lead to equality:' Mikhail Tal: 'in view of the variation Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): ''' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'stays ahead.'}) 6... Bb4 {Mikhail Tal: 'Another committal move. Black demonstrates his intention to play the King's Indian in its 'original' form. This was the variation used when it began being played in important tournaments.'} 7. Qb3 Qe7 8. e3 Bxc3+ {Mikhail Tal: 'The most flexible. Black does not object to the closing of the centre, since in this case his knight obtains a comfortable post at c5 and, in addition, with the centre closed his hands are freed for play on the kingside – his knight moves from f6 to e8 or h5, making way for the f-pawn. The immediate capture on d4 promises a definite advantage for White, who gains more freedom in the centre and on the kingside.'} (8... Na6 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Nevertheless!'}) {Mikhail Tal: 'The prophylactic move'} 9. Qxc3 {Mikhail Tal: 'is after all necessary. White's plans include the harmonious development of his pieces in the region of the centre, and if sooner or later he should succeed in forcing the opponent to exchange on d4, he will gain the opportunity to exert pressure on the weak d6-pawn.'} {Mikhail Tal: 'After' Mikhail Tal: 'And after' Mikhail Tal: 'Thus if White wants to maintain the tension in the centre, he has to reckon with numerous counter-ideas by Black. It should not be forgotten that in some cases (after an eventual ...Ng4) the queen at b6 can create threats to the f2-pawn. Our choice of opening proved psychologically successful. Botvinnik again avoids double-edged tactical continuations, and prefers the immediate closing of the centre, hoping to gain time by attacking the queen. The other way of relieving the tension in the centre after'} O-O {[%csl Yc4][%cal Re5d4,Gf6e4,Yb6b4] Mikhail Tal: 'Black immediately begins action against d4.' Mikhail Tal: 'he is threatening a familiar combination, namely'} (9... h6 10. Bf4 g5 11. Be5 d6 {GM Mihail Marin: ', failing to notice that'} (11... O-O 12. c5) 12. Bxf6 $18 {Mikhail Tal: '(it was this move that escaped my opponent's attention).' GM Mihail Marin: 'wins material.'} {GM Mihail Marin: 'Later Flohr discovered the following beautiful line:'} Qxf6 $1 {Mikhail Tal: '!!' '!!' Mueller,Karsten: '!?' Mueller,Karsten: '(Flohr) to go on the attack himself:' Mueller,Karsten: '(Flohr) selber zum Angriff überzugehen:'} 13. Ne2 Be6 $3 {Mikhail Tal: 'Now after both'} 14. h4 g4 (14... Nd7 15. d5 cxd5 16. Qxf6 Nxf6 {Mueller,Karsten: '+-'} 17. hxg5 Ng4 18. Nf4 Bg8 19. cxd5 Rc8 {Flohr} {Mikhail Tal: 'and'} 20. Kd2 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Otherwise Nxd6, with a decisive attack.'}) 15. O-O-O) 10. Ne2 {GM Mihail Marin: 'For some reason this is considered to be premature, but Botvinnik had analyzed it especially for this match.'} Na6 11. h4 Nc7 {Mikhail Tal: 'The first virtue of the plan selected by Black is evident: his knight has occupied an active position and is already attacking a pawn.'} {Mikhail Tal: 'Later, however, I learned that'} 12. Nf4 {Mikhail Tal: 'During the game I assumed that this was a theoretical innovation.' Mikhail Tal: 'had been played by Petrosian in a game with Shiyanovsky in a USSR Championship Semi-final (Kiev 1957) which he won quickly (true, the virtues of the move 12 Ne1 were nothing to do with this). White does not intend to tolerate for long the 'annoying' knight at c5 and prepares to exchange it. The role of the piece that will gain a tempo is assigned to the bishop at c1. Nevertheless, 12 Ne1 has the drawback that White loses a certain amount of time and the opponent is able to complete his development unhindered. This is precisely what Shiyanovsky forgot to do in the above-mentioned game, deciding instead to try and conclude matters immediately with an attack on the kingside.' GM Mihail Marin: 'This reveals Botvinnik's plan. White intends to exchange the active knight on c5 as soon as possible.'} d6 13. Bf1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Botvinnik is of the opinion that frequent appearances in tournaments are not so essential. Of course, the methods of working during a preparation period are a matter of taste for every player. At the same time, modern chess has now reached such a high level that knowledge and ability alone are insufficient. Every game demands an enormous output of nervous energy, and therefore regular (more or less frequent) participation in competitions is necessary for a player to keep in form. The text-move, or, more precisely, the time spent on it, demonstrate that Botvinnik had not played competitive chess for a long time. On the quite obvious and undoubtedly strongest continuation of the manoeuvre begun with the previous move, did he really have to spend ten minutes out of his overall budget of two and a half hours? How many times has a player lacked precisely these ten minutes at a decisive moment!'} (13. h5 Ne6 14. Nxe6 Bxe6 15. h6 $18 {White has a strong attack with equal material.} {GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik feared'} g6 {Mikhail Tal: ', but in his calculations he overlooked the possibility of'} 16. O-O-O {Mueller,Karsten: '+-' Mueller,Karsten: 'however, also wins.' Mueller,Karsten: 'gewinnt allerdings auch.'}) 13... Nce8 (13... Ne6) 14. c5 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Even though Black does not have any immediate problems, it is not easy to find a way to develop his typical King's Indian counterplay. Therefore, Tal elaborated a well-masked plan.'} Be6 {Mikhail Tal: 'The critical position. Here Black spent a long time deciding which plan to adopt in the middle game. He wanted to play ...f7-f5, which gains in strength with the pawns at h3 and g3, since White's kingside is to some extent weakened.' GM Mihail Marin: 'He played precisely this rook to c8 to give Botvinnik the illusion that he was not going to open the kingside. But at this height, he had designed the later combination already.'} 15. h5 {Mikhail Tal: 'A highly revealing manoeuvre. White takes the opponent at his word, and also concentrates on queenside play.' GM Mihail Marin: 'With the black rook on c8, White has to defend the pawn on b2 before developing his bishop, as otherwise the knight would be hanging after ...Qxb2. It is obvious that in the long run the rook may be exposed on b1, but for short-term purposes Botvinnik's move is the most ambitious.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 10 Games. E69: King's Indian: Fianchetto: 6...Nbd7 7 0-0 e5 8 e4 c6 9 h3'} Bf7 {Mikhail Tal: 'Now the opponent can also be disturbed on the other wing. It transpires that, for the advance of the f-pawn, the support of the rook is not after all so necessary.' GM Mihail Marin: 'The second critical moment.'} 16. h6 g6 17. b4 {Mikhail Tal: 'The threat of ...f7-f5 has become unpleasant, because the white f-pawn is tied to the defence of the g3-pawn, and in addition, after the opening of the diagonal for his bishop, Black's pieces on the queenside would be very appropriately placed. It becomes evident that 15 Rb1 was inaccurate – White has lost an important tempo.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'White has an edge.' Mueller,Karsten: ''} Qd7 {GM Mihail Marin: 'While he was gradually activating his major pieces, seemingly without any concrete plan, Tal was move by move checking the possibility of the sacrifice. He can be praised for waiting until all his pieces reached the optimal positions.' Mueller,Karsten: '?!' Mueller,Karsten: 'This plan is objectively too ambitious, but it fits Tal's style very well. Activity is very important in his world view.' Mueller,Karsten: 'Dieser Plan ist objektiv zu ambitioniert, passt aber sehr gut zu Tals Stil. Aktivität ist sehr wichtig in seinem Weltbild.'} (17... d5 18. Be2) 18. Be2 {[%cal Gg2f1] Mikhail Tal: 'Completely trusting the opponent. White wants to begin 'smoking out' the black pieces by Bf1 and then to find a convenient moment to carry out mass exchanges. An ending will be very favourable for him, in view of the fact that Black's knight at h5 and bishop at g7 are stuck on the kingside and cannot easily be switched to the defence.'} Nd5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Sixteen minutes were spent considering this obvious move – Black was weighing up whether or not the idea, carried out a little later, would work now. No, it does not work. The variation has to be strengthened.' Mueller,Karsten: '?!'} 19. Nxd5 {Mikhail Tal: 'A move which would have been very useful (he moves his king off the back rank, defending in case of necessity the g3-pawn), were it not for the real danger suddenly impending over White's position. Botvinnik evidently assumed that there were absolutely no threats and decided to make a useful prophylactic move, intending to follow up with either Bf3 or Bf1.' GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik over-defends the pawn on h3 to prepare Bf3.' Mueller,Karsten: '?!' Mueller,Karsten: 'Botvinnik misses the moment for' Mueller,Karsten: 'Botvinnik verpasst den Moment, mit'} {Mikhail Tal: 'After this waiting move Black reckons that everything is ready for the combination, and he makes the introductory 'anti-positional' move.'} Bxd5 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Without opening a new front, the major pieces' concentration on the queenside would have no impact.'} 20. a4 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move'} a6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Botvinnik most probably assumed that after'} 21. Bf4 {Mikhail Tal: ', in view of the threat of 22 g4, the black pieces were now forced to retreat and that White would be able to occupy the e4-square in comfort, but here the prepared surprise awaited him.' GM Mihail Marin: 'Despite Black's apparent activity, the threat g3-g4 is likely to push him back and gradually give White control over e4. But Tal now unleashes the combination he had planned many moves earlier.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Strongly threatening a3.' Mueller,Karsten: 'What follows is one of Tal's many much-discussed sacrifices. From a practical point of view it was certainly correct, because over the board the white problems are hardly solvable. Objectively, however, it is not correct - true to Tal's motto there are correct sacrifices and mine.' Mueller,Karsten: ' Es folgt eines der vielen viel diskutierten Opfer Tals. Aus praktischer Sicht war es sicher richtig, denn am Brett sind die weißen Probleme kaum zu lösen. Objektiv ist es allerdings nicht korrekt - getreu dem Talschen Motto es gibt korrekte Opfer und meine.'} Kf7 {Mikhail Tal: 'The controversy provoked by this move was, in my opinion, rather pointless. It is good, in that all other continuations are bad, and if the knight sacrifice is incorrect, then a question mark should be attached not to Black's 21st move, but, say, to his 17th. All the same, after the knight sacrifice Black's pieces (this applies especially to the recently 'unemployed' bishop at g7) become very active over the entire board and White is obliged to switch to the concrete analysis of numerous sharp variations. The acceptance of the sacrifice is forced.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'JvR: '!?' JvR: 'A Tal only moves forwards! The correctness of the sacrifice has been heavily debated.'' GM Mihail Marin: '!?' GM Mihail Marin: 'There is no need for evaluating, criticizing or praising this move. It is simply the crowning of Black's previous play. The evaluation should refer strictly to the whole plan initiated by ...Rfc8.' Mueller,Karsten: '!?'} 22. Kd2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Kg8 {GM Mihail Marin: 'As a result of the sacrifice, Black has achieved impressive activity. The queenside pressure has turned real and the White kingside is also in some danger. The King's Indian bishop not only has the whole diagonal at its disposal but can also look towards White's king after ...Be5. Apart from the concrete technical difficulties, White has to adapt himself to the new circumstances. Just a few moves earlier he was in complete control and now things have turned chaotic. What's worse, such positions were precisely Tal's speciality, while Botvinnik preferred logical play.'} 23. b5 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'a transit from better to worse JvR: '?'' GM Mihail Marin: '?' GM Mihail Marin: 'After this natural move Black gets enough compensation for the piece.' Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'a transit from better to worse'' Mueller,Karsten: '?' Mueller,Karsten: 'However, the refutation was deeply hidden:' Mueller,Karsten: 'Die Widerlegung war allerdings tief verborgen:'} ({GM Mihail Marin: 'During the post mortem both players agreed that the game should end in a draw by repetition after'} 23. Rhg1 $11 {Mikhail Tal: 'etc.'} Qd8 $1 {GM Mihail Marin} 24. g4 fxg4 25. Rxg4 b6) 23... dxc5 {Mikhail Tal: '. It is interesting that on the move after the knight sacrifice Black thought for 15 minutes. Was this the result of an oversight? Or a desire to check the variations? Neither one, nor the other. It was merely a confirmation, so to speak, of the intuitive nature of the sacrifice, a confirmation that Black had not calculated variations beginning with the sacrifice and ending in mate. Also no less indicative was the fact that later analysis showed this move to be incorrect.' GM Mihail Marin: '?' GM Mihail Marin: 'Just two moves after sacrificing, Tal invested 15 minutes into this move, showing that he had planned everything intuitively, and only now started working out the concrete details. His choice could have led to trouble.' Mueller,Karsten: '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} 24. Qxc5 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' GM Mihail Marin: '!' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!'} {Mikhail Tal: 'I saw that after' Mikhail Tal: 'During the game Botvinnik too thought that his position was by no means better. After the game he showed the variation that he did not like –' Mikhail Tal: 'A few days later grandmaster Flohr found that after'} cxb5 {Mikhail Tal: 'It was on this move that Black was pinning all his hopes.' Mikhail Tal: 'White could not play' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'JvR: '!'' GM Mihail Marin: '!?' GM Mihail Marin: 'An elegant but insufficient resource.'} 25. axb5 $2 {Mikhail Tal: ',' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'makes life more difficult JvR: '?'' GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik finally cracks under pressure.' Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'makes life more difficult'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '??' Mueller,Karsten: 'Botwinnik hopes in vain to relieve himself by exchanging queens and misses the opportunity to use the active player solution' Mueller,Karsten: 'Botwinnik hofft vergeblich sich durch Damentausch zu entlasten und verpasst die Gelegenheit, mit der Aktivspielerlösung'} Rc8 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '-+' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+'} 26. Qb4 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik might have hoped that without queens he could keep his extra piece. But in fact, White is just lost, as his pieces are hanging and the pawn on e2 is too strong.'} axb5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'The unwieldy black rook has suddenly acquired colossal strength in the centre of the board. Since White has 'plugged' the c-file, the rook switches to the d-file, and in collaboration with the passed e2-pawn brings Black victory.'} 27. Bxb5 {Mikhail Tal: 'There is nothing better.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '?' Mikhail Tal: 'There is nothing better. Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '?'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} Qe6 (27... Qf7) 28. Rhc1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Bf3 would now be nice for White.' Mueller,Karsten: ''} Nf6 {Mueller,Karsten: '?!' Mueller,Karsten: 'misses the beautiful and directly fatal' Mueller,Karsten: 'verpasst das wunderschöne und direkt tödliche'} 29. Bd6 Rxc1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Decoy: c1'} 30. Rxc1 (30. Kxc1 Rc8+ {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'JvR: '(Tal).''} 31. Kd2 {Mikhail Tal: ', White was not obliged to go in for the repetition of moves, since he has the unexpected' Mueller,Karsten: ''} Ng4 {Mikhail Tal: 'and now'}) 30... Rc8 31. Rxc8+ {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} Qxc8 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Two active bishops and an extra pawn is an advantage more than sufficient for a win.' Mikhail Tal: 'White cannot play'} 32. Be5 Kf7 (32... Ng4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'might work better.'} 33. Qe7 Qf8 (33... Bf7 34. Bc4 $19 {Mikhail Tal: 'and considered the natural conclusion of events to be' GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik misevaluated the correct continuation.'} Qf8 $1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'is forced if you want to stay better.'}) 34. Qxf8+ {JvR: '!'} Kxf8 35. Bg7+ Kf7) 33. Bxf6 Kxf6 34. Qd6+ Qe6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Double Attack'} 35. Qd8+ Kf7 {Mikhail Tal: 'White has the possibility of'} 36. Be8+ {Mikhail Tal: 'now, since if'} (36. Qh8 Ke7 (36... Qb6 {Mikhail Tal: 'because of'} 37. Qg7+ {Mikhail Tal: '.'} {Mikhail Tal: 'Or'} Ke6 38. Qe5+ {Mikhail Tal: '. Botvinnik's last chance is somehow to exploit the activity of his knight.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Decoy Double attack' Mikhail Tal: '. Botvinnik's last chance is somehow to exploit the activity of his knight. Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Decoy Double attack''}) (36... Bc6 $140 37. Qxh7+ {Wins material} {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} Kf6 {Mikhail Tal: ', whatever the circumstances, Black could have finally decided on' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} 38. Qh8+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!'} Kg5 39. Qd8+ {Mikhail Tal: '.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+'} {Mikhail Tal: 'Unfortunately, Black misses an opportunity to conclude the game quickly and prettily with'} Kxh6 $1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'might be the shorter path' Mueller,Karsten: '!!'} (39... Qf6 $19 {Mikhail Tal: '. In this, it is true, a part was played by reasons not at all connected with chess: the noise in the auditorium prompted the match arbiters to carry out their threat and transfer the play to a closed room. This, of course, proved to be a very severe warning to the spectators, since during the subsequent games they did not give cause for such a measure, but the feeling, when the clocks are stopped and you are politely asked to leave the stage, moreover at the very height of the game, can also hardly be called pleasant. At any rate, I am somehow not yet used to playing in such 'nomadic' conditions. Such an unaccustomed transference could have led to an even worse blunder, and therefore I deliberately chose a less strong, but safer continuation, where there was no longer any need to calculate lengthy variations.' Mueller,Karsten: '(Tal)' Mueller,Karsten: '(Tal)'} 40. Qxf6+ $19 {Mikhail Tal: 'If'} Kxf6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black wins by'} 41. Bxc6 bxc6 42. Kc3 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 40. Qh8+ Kg5 41. Qh4#) 37. Qxh7+ Qf7 {Mikhail Tal: ', when White loses a piece. I, however, assumed that I could win 'anyhow'.'} 38. Qh8 Bc6 39. Qe5+ Kd7 (39... Kf8 {Mikhail Tal: '.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '!'} {Mikhail Tal: 'If'} 40. Qc5+ {Mikhail Tal: 'Black can reply'} Ke8 {Mikhail Tal: 'or, even simpler,'} 41. Bxc6+ {Mikhail Tal: '.'} {Don't play} bxc6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)} 42. Qxc6+) (39... Kd8 40. Qb8+ $19 {Not} Ke7 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)} 41. Qc7+ Kf8 42. Qd8+ $17) 40. d5 $19 Bxb5 {Mikhail Tal: 'he can interpose'} 41. Qe6+ {Mikhail Tal: '.'} Qxe6 {hoping against hope Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} 42. dxe6+ Kxe6 $19 {Mikhail Tal: 'Not' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'The pointe is'} 43. h7) 36... Qxe8 {Mikhail Tal: 'The white pieces are completely tied up, and the advance of the d-pawn will quickly decide the game. However, from the 26th move onwards Black had been replying instantly, and in the subsequent play he complicates his task somewhat. It would seem that here too the 'change of scene' had an effect.'} 37. Qxd5+ Kf6 38. Qxb7 Kg5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Even here it was not too late to return to g8, so as to transpose into the variation given in the previous note.'} 39. Qxh7 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black had overlooked this simple move. The white rook succeeds in switching to the e-file. Now I was obliged to think, since White has acquired certain drawing chances. Black reverts to the correct idea and begins simply making use of his passed pawn.'} Qb5 40. Qe7+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} Kxh6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Preventing the activation of the white king.'} 41. Kc3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' If only White now had time for Nd4+....'} (41. Qf8+ {(Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)) keeps fighting.} Kg5 (41... Kh7 42. Qf7+ $142 {secures the win} Kh6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Much simpler was' Mikhail Tal: 'or'} 43. Qa2) 42. Qd8+ Kg4 {Mikhail Tal: 'was also possible, of course, but Black simply could not wait to realise his passed pawn.'} {Less strong is} 43. Qh4+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)} Kf3 44. Qf4+ Kg2 $19 45. g4 (45. Kc3 Qc6+ $19) 45... Qb2+) 41... Qd3+ {Mikhail Tal: 'The 40 moves had been made, but the players remained in their seats. Botvinnik obviously reckoned that, if Black were to continue playing so rapidly, he would make a mistake somewhere, while I kept playing through 'inertia'.'} 42. Kb4 {Mikhail Tal: 'White finally escapes from the pin, but at a high price – the resulting rook ending is completely hopeless for him.'} Qd2+ 43. Kc5 Qxf2 44. Qh4+ Kg7 45. Qg5 Qd2 46. Kc6 Qb4 {Mikhail Tal: '. The game was adjourned in this position and White sealed his next move. On arriving home, my trainer and I set up the position and quickly convinced ourselves that Black's passed pawn was unstoppable. Therefore (I will say this in confidence) the following day we did not even take breakfast, but waited in our room for a call from the arbiter informing us of White's resignation. At midday the long-awaited call was received. White's sealed move was' Mueller,Karsten: 'Of course, I can only scratch the surface here. Another source is for example: Zaubern wie Schachweltmeister Michail Tal by Karsten Müller and Raymund Stolze, Edition Olms 2010. ' Mueller,Karsten: 'Ich kann hier natürlich nur an der Oberfläche kratzen. Eine weitere Quellen ist zum Beispiel: Zaubern wie Schachweltmeister Michail Tal von Karsten Müller und Raymund Stolze, Edition Olms 2010. '} 47. d5 {sealed move sealed move Mikhail Tal: '.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Accuracy: White = 36%, Black = 52%. '} Kf7 {(Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)) was worth a try.} 0-1

