1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 c5 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. O-O d5 5. c4 d4 6. d3 e5 7. e3 Be7 8. exd4 exd4 9. Bg5 (9. Bf4 O-O 10. Ne5 Nxe5 11. Bxe5 Nd7 12. Re1 a6 13. Nd2 Nxe5 14. Rxe5 Bd6 15. Re1 g6 16. Bd5 Ra7 17. Qf3 b6 18. Ne4 Kg7) (9. Na3 O-O 10. Re1) 9... O-O 10. Nbd2 {After a solid start, White has displayed certain territorial ambitions. But this involves a considerable loss of time, including c2-c3-c4 and the long bishop manoeuvre, which still has not come to an end. What is even more significant is that his queen's knight stands on d2, blocking his other bishop's development. Developing the bishop to b2 is not very effective, as it would hit into a defended pawn, so White still has to work a bit to bring his position in order. On the other hand, the centre is blocked and he has more space, elements he must have counted on.} (10. Re1 $15 Qb6 {runs into} 11. Nfd2 $1 Bg4 {Trying to keep the c4-knight defended.} 12. Qb3 Rfe8 13. Na3 Bf5 14. Qxb6 $19 {Black retrieves the piece, retaining a few extra pawns and a strategic advantage.} axb6 $6 15. Nb5 {mates soon.} Bf8 {does not work either, as after} 16. Bxf6 {he has to either give up the e4-pawn as in the game or return the piece with} Rxe1+ 17. Rxe1 $17 {Everything was ready for the resolute} gxf6 $1 {, with a very dangerous attack.} 18. Be4 Bg6 19. a3 Ne5 20. Nc7 Rd8 21. Nd5 Nxd3 22. Bxd3 Bxd3 23. Nxf6+ Kh8 24. a4 Rd6 $17 {With two pawns for the exchange, the better structure and active play.} {In view of the threat ...Qg5 there is hardly anything better. If} 25. Nh5 Re6 26. Rxe6 fxe6 $19 {, with a crushing attack.} {White should have kept manoeuvring with, say,} 27. Nf6 Bh6 28. f4 e5 29. Ng4 Bg7 {Even though Black's position is very active, we can notice that ... Bh5 also had a minor drawback: Black does not have ...Nh5 followed by ...f7-f5 anymore.} 30. Nxe5 {not only gives up the bishops' pair but also frees White's play after} Bxe5 {followed by Be3.} 31. fxe5 {allows the optimal regrouping with} Bc2 $1 {followed by Nc3 and Be3.} {The attempt to achieve optimal coordination with} 32. Kf2 {followed by Nc3 and Be3 allows} Kg7 {Due to the hanging bishop, White has to weaken his kingside structure with} 33. Kf3 {, when after} Bxa4 {Black has excellent play on the dark squares.} 34. Kf4 Kg6 35. g4 Bc6 36. h4 b5 37. h5+ Kg7 38. b3 bxc4 39. bxc4 b5) 10... h6 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. a3 Bf5 13. Qc2 a5 {The first critical moment. Black has developed harmoniously while White is still struggling to complete his development.} (13... Re8) 14. Rfe1 {The most natural decision.} Qc7 $3 {The result of an accurate judgement of the position, this is the only move to keep White under pressure. The text move involves a piece sacrifice which White would have better declined.} 15. Re2 $2 {Weakening the kingside with incomplete development can hardly be a good idea.} Rae8 $1 16. Rae1 Rxe2 17. Rxe2 {In view of the threat ...f7-f5 the bishop hurries to unpin the knight on f3.} b6 $6 {This unnecessarily offers White some free tempi for developing.} 18. Ne1 Bg4 19. Bf3 Bd7 20. Ng2 {Relatively best.} g5 $15 {Black has three pawns for the piece and attacking chances.} 21. Be4 $6 {Weakening the pawn on f2 and keeping the knight on f3 under pressure.} Be5 $5 {The introduction to another combination.} 22. Qd1 Kg7 $1 23. Bd5 Bd6 24. Ne4 Be7 25. Nd2 Bd8 {With four pawns for a relatively passive bishop, Black has excellent winning chances already.} 26. Qe1 Qd6 27. f4 Qg6 28. Be4 Qh5 29. f5 Ne5 30. Qd1 Re8 31. Re1 Qh3 32. Nf3 Ng4 33. b4 Bc7 34. b5 Nf6 35. Nd2 Re5 36. Nf3 Ng4 37. f6+ Kxf6 38. Bc6 Bf5 39. Rxe5 {was also strong.} Bxe5 $5 40. Qe2 Kg7 41. Nge1 Bd6 42. Nc2 Bxg3 43. hxg3 $15 Bxd3 $5 44. Nxg5 Qxg3+ 45. Qg2 Qxg2+ 46. Kxg2 0-1

