{John Emms: 'Meat and Potatoes: Benoni hero Tal plays simple chess to notch up a straight forward victory.''Meat and Potatoes: Benoni hero Tal plays simple chess to notch up a straight forward victory. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'Meat and Potatoes: Benoni hero Tal plays simple chess to notch up a straight forward victory.' Endre Vegh: 'Handling the Light-squared Bishop: One of the major positional concerns for Black in the Benoni is that the light-squared bishop does not have any good square or diagonal. In some respects it looks like a good bishop because of the black pawns on d6 and c5, but the important pawns here are the ones on f7, g6, h7 and e4 and d5. The dark-squared bishops are the good pieces in this structure. The main way to deal with the light-squared bishop's lack of scope is to exchange it for a knight, as Black did in the following example.'''} 1. d4 {John Emms John Emms'Bulletin Bulletin: 'John Emms John Emms John Emms: 'Bulletin'''} Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 g6 4. Nc3 d6 5. e4 Bg7 6. Be2 O-O 7. Bg5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. exd5 {John Emms: 'Nowadays everyone would play 9 h3, preventing the pin and transposing into the Modern Classical Variation.''Nowadays everyone would play 9 h3, preventing the pin and transposing into the Modern Classical Variation. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'Nowadays everyone would play 9 h3, preventing the pin and transposing into the Modern Classical Variation.'''} Re8 10. Nf3 { f3-d2-c4'' f3-d2-c4 John Emms: 'Bulletin: ' f3-d2-c4''' '} Bg4 11. O-O Nbd7 12. h3 Bxf3 {John Emms: 'And here either 13 Qe2 or 13 Qd1 are preferable.''And here either 13 Qe2 or 13 Qd1 are preferable. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'And here either 13 Qe2 or 13 Qd1 are preferable.'''} 13. Bxf3 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'The bishop is on its way to h2, but there it will leave the centre undefended.'''} Qb6 (13... Qe7 {John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'would have left White better off.''}) 14. Qc2 a6 15. b3 (15. Bd2) 15... Qa5 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'With the idea of ...c5-c4 and ...Nc5.'''} 16. Bd2 Qc7 {John Emms: '!' '!''Bulletin: 'John Emms: '!' '!'''} 17. Rae1 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'It is easy to say this move is ugly.'''} Rxe1 18. Rxe1 Re8 $17 {John Emms: 'With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7.''With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7. Bulletin: '''John Emms: 'With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7.' John Emms: 'With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7. Bulletin: ''' Endre Vegh: 'White's position is already close to collapse. We can clearly see that the bishop is not happy on h2.'' '} 19. Be2 $2 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: '?!'''} Re7 $1 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'All the weaknesses in White's camp are visible now.'''} 20. f4 Qb8 21. Rf1 (21. a4) 21... b5 $1 $19 {John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lo'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lost. Bulletin: '-+!''John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lost.' John Emms: 'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lost. Bulletin: '-+!'' Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'Now the knight enters the position and White is left with no defence.'' ' st.'} 22. cxb5 axb5 23. Bxb5 Ne4 24. Nxe4 (24. Bxd7) 24... Qxb5 {John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'It seems unusual to give up such a good Benoni bishop, but this is the clearest route to 'It seems unusual to give up such a good Benoni bishop, but this is the clearest route to victory. Bulletin: 'John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'It seems unusual to give up such a good Benoni bishop, but this is the clearest route to victory.' Endre Vegh: 'Once again Black parts with this bishop at the right moment and the white position falls apart.''' victory.'} 25. Rf3 ({John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'But''} 25. Nxd6 {'(Bulletin) John Emms: 'Bulletin''} Bd4+ $17 {John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'would also have been very strong for Black and prove the total success of his strategy. White's remaining bishop has no scope and his knight is rather passive too. Meanwhile Black will attack the white centre, both frontally with the rook, and from the sides with the queen, knight and bishop.''}) (25. Re1 $2 {'(Bulletin) John Emms: 'Bulletin''} Qa6) 25... Qa6 26. Bc3 (26. f5 Qe2 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: '.'''}) 26... f5 {John Emms: 'The c-pawn suddenly becomes a little monster.''The c-pawn suddenly becomes a little monster. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'The c-pawn suddenly becomes a little monster.'''} 27. Ng3 (27. Ng5 Re2) 27... Bxc3 28. Rxc3 Nf6 {John Emms: 'And White resigned. There is no defence, for example''And White resigned. There is no defence, for example Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'And White resigned. There is no defence, for example'''} {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'White resigned because of'''} 29. Qd2 Qa8 30. Re3 {John Emms: '!' '!''Bulletin: 'John Emms: '!' '!'''} 0-1

