{'E92: King's Indian: Classical: 6 Be2 e5: 7 dxe5, 7 Be3 and Petrosian System without 7...Nbd7 Bulletin: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E92: King's Indian: Classical: 6 Be2 e5: 7 dxe5, 7 Be3 and Petrosian System without 7...Nbd7 ''''} 1. d4 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s) Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Bulletin: 'ChessBase''} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 (5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Nd5 Nxd5 10. cxd5 c6 {Botvinnik - Tal, Moscow Wch match (13), 1961.}) 5... O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. dxe5 {Mikhail Tal: 'A rather unpleasant surprise. The exchange of queens is a cold shower on Black's aggressive intentions. Apart from this, two psychological factors probably influenced the choice of such a continuation. Firstly, practical experience in my match against Botvinnik showed that it was possible to reckon on success even with this modest plan, and secondly, a longish absence from tournament play made Ivkov cautious. Objectively speaking, a premature exchange in the centre can create no problems for Black.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Bulletin: 'Mikhail Tal: 'A rather unpleasant surprise. The exchange of queens is a cold shower on Black's aggressive intentions. Apart from this, two psychological factors probably influenced the choice of such a continuation. Firstly, practical experience in my match against Botvinnik showed that it was possible to reckon on success even with this modest plan, and secondly, a longish absence from tournament play made Ivkov cautious. Objectively speaking, a premature exchange in the centre can create no problems for Black.'''} dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 {Mikhail Tal: 'Obviously the variation with' Mikhail Tal: 'After' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Bulletin: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Obviously the variation with' Mikhail Tal: 'After'''} 9. Nd5 $146 {Mikhail Tal: 'I believe 9 Nd5 has never before been played in this position.' Mikhail Tal: 'in this game was influenced by the 13th game of my return match with Botvinnik, where the Sämisch variation was played. See above. The difference in the present game is that White's e-pawn is not defended by his f-pawn. If Black wants a sharp game, and that was the case in this game, he will never be satisfied with the variation' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Bulletin: 'N' Bulletin: 'Mikhail Tal: 'I believe 9 Nd5 has never before been played in this position.' Mikhail Tal: 'in this game was influenced by the 13th game of my return match with Botvinnik, where the Sämisch variation was played. See above. The difference in the present game is that White's e-pawn is not defended by his f-pawn. If Black wants a sharp game, and that was the case in this game, he will never be satisfied with the variation'''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Normal is'} 9. Bg5 Re8 10. O-O-O $10 {Mikhail Tal: 'with apparently equal play.'}) 9... Rd7 $5 $132 {Mikhail Tal: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'The move certainly appears very clumsy, but White's e-pawn is attacked. Although my opponent now thought for an hour and a half, his reply was psychologically wrong.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Bulletin: '!?' Bulletin: 'Mikhail Tal: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'The move certainly appears very clumsy, but White's e-pawn is attacked. Although my opponent now thought for an hour and a half, his reply was psychologically wrong.'''} (9... Nxd5 $5 $10 10. cxd5 c6 11. Bg5 {Mikhail Tal: 'since the most Black can then achieve is a draw, and only after extremely precise play.'}) (9... Na6 $6 10. Bg5 Rd6 11. Nxf6+ Bxf6 12. Bxf6 Rxf6 13. Nxe5 Re6 14. f4 f6 15. Ng4 $14 {Mikhail Tal: 'is no good either. I thought for 15 minutes and opted for an unusual continuation which, although not especially strong, decisively influenced the game's result.'}) 10. Nxf6+ {Mikhail Tal: 'This move could have been made after only two minutes' thought.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Bulletin: 'Mikhail Tal: 'This move could have been made after only two minutes' thought.'''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'The correctness of 9...Rd7 depends exclusively on the variation'} 10. Nxe5 Nxd5 11. Nxd7 Nb4 12. Nxb8 Nc2+ 13. 