[EventDate "1977.??.??"]
[EventType "team-tourn"]

{A New Olympiad  For the first Telechess Olympiad, the organising body, the ICCF, decided that the event would be run on a knockout format, with each team required to field five open boards, a correspondence player board, a woman's board and a junior board. Ties would be decided by removing the results on the lowest boards.  The first round began early in 1977, with only 14 countries entering. The only two countries from outside Europe, Australia and Guyana, were paired in the first round, with Australia scoring a comfortable 6 1/2 : 1 1/2 win. The match, played in Australia at Overseas Telecommunications Commission (OTC) House in Melbourne and Sydney, went for more than eight hours. Even so, most games were unfinished, having reached only move 30, and needed to be adjudicated by the ICCF panel.  Australia's next opponent was the top seed, the Soviet Union, which had received a first round bye.  Negotiations over the date proved to be unfortunate for lovers of Australian Rules Football (AFL), with the match to be played on the day of the final match of the season. To make matters even more tricky, the 1977 Grand Final was the first ever to be televised live. In Melbourne, from where I was competing, a small black and white television was placed just outside the main playing room in OTC House to enable players waiting for their opponent's move to watch the football match.  Australian team selection for the match was slightly controversial, with influential pessimist Cecil Purdy pushing to move me from board two to the junior board as Australia's only chance of scoring any points. Hoping to play against a big name such as Spassky or Smyslov rather than an unknown junior, I refused.  The match began not long after the scheduled 3pm Australian time, 9am in Moscow. It turned out that only one big name was playing for the favourites in the USSR Sport Committee conference room; Mihail Tal, up against Australia's dominant player of the 1970s, Robert Jamieson.} 1. e4 c5 2. c3 e6 3. d4 d5 4. e5 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Be2 f6 7. O-O fxe5 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. dxe5 Qc7 {Diagram [#]} 10. Bf4 ({Tal's opening choice was rather rare at the time and the critical line} 10. c4 $1 {had barely been explored.}) 10... Ne7 11. Re1 O-O-O 12. Bd3 Be8 13. Nd2 Bg6 {Solving all Black's worries.} 14. Nf3 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 Ng6 16. Bd2 Be7 {Diagram [#]} 17. b4 $1 {It would be easy for White to drift here but by securing the d4 square for his knight, Jamieson ensures that he will not be worse.} Rhf8 18. bxc5 Bxc5 19. Nd4 Nxe5 20. Qe2 Bxd4 {Now the game peters out.} (20... Nc4 21. Qxe6+ Qd7 {would keep more options open for Black.}) 21. cxd4 Nc4 22. Qxe6+ Qd7 23. Qe2 Qf5 24. Bb4 Rf6 25. Rac1 Rg6 (25... Rg6 {Tal's attack is not serious - White can simply defend with} 26. f3 {- so a draw is a fair result.  Apart from the junior board, where moves were being exchanged at (comparative) warp speed - less than 15 minutes transmission time per move - the match proceeded painfully slowly, with plenty of down time to watch the football. (The Soviets insisted on transmitting every move twice, on separate transmissions, which helped avoid clerical errors but increased the cost of the match and caused other delays.)}) 1/2-1/2

