1. e4 {Tal scored on board 2 for Latvia in the semifinal 3,5/7 and in the Final 4,5/7. This score enabled him to reach the qualifying norm for the title of USSR Master, but the All-Union Classification Comission set him the task of beating the established master Vladimir Saigin, champion of Byelorussia in a match (1954) to confirm this.} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d3 d6 4. g3 e6 5. Bg2 Nf6 6. O-O Be7 7. Re1 O-O 8. Nbd2 d5 9. e5 Nd7 10. Nf1 f6 11. exf6 Nxf6 12. c3 b5 13. a4 b4 14. c4 Rb8 15. Bf4 Bd6 16. Bxd6 Qxd6 17. N1d2 Bb7 18. Rc1 Rbd8 19. Nb3 dxc4 20. dxc4 Qe7 21. Qe2 e5 22. Nxe5 Nxe5 23. Qxe5 Qxe5 24. Rxe5 Bxg2 25. Kxg2 Rd3 26. Nxc5 $14 {[%csl Gc4]} Rd2 27. Rf5 Rxb2 28. Nd3 Rb3 29. Nf4 Rc3 30. Ne2 (30. Rb1 $5 Rxc4 31. Rb5 {[%csl Rb4]}) 30... Ra3 31. c5 b3 32. Rb1 Rc8 33. Nc1 Rxa4 34. Rxb3 Rc4 35. Nd3 a5 36. Ra3 a4 37. Rf4 Rxf4 38. gxf4 Ra8 39. Kf3 Nd5 40. Nb2 Rb8 41. Nxa4 Ra8 42. Kg4 Nb4 43. Kf5 Nc2 44. Ra2 Nb4 1/2-1/2

