1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 a6 6. g3 Qc7 7. Bg2 Nf6 8. O-O h6 $5 {Nigel Davies: 'A waiting move that prevents White from posting his bishop on g5 whilst maintaining the option of capturing on d4. The drawback is that it weakens Black's kingside, a factor which can become particularly significant should he later castle short. '} 9. Nb3 {Nigel Davies: 'Ruling out the exchange on d4 and preparing to advance his kingside pawns.'} ({White can also consider} 9. Re1 Nxd4 10. e5 $5 ({in the position that arises after} 10. Qxd4 Bc5 11. Bf4 d6 12. Qd2 e5 {Black's ...h7-h6 is more useful than White's Re1}) {, but after} 10... Nb5 $1 {he has to play} 11. Nxb5 ({by comparison with De la Riva-Plaskett, Black's bishop is usefully placed on f8 in the line} 11. exf6 Nxc3 12. fxg7 Bxg7 {so White has to exchange knights}) 11... axb5 12. exf6 gxf6 {, when Black has quite a solid position.}) 9... Be7 {Nigel Davies: 'A natural developing move, but possibly not the best.'} ({In order to fianchetto his queen's bishop Black should probably play} 9... d6 10. a4 b6 {as in Pavlovic-Cabrilo, Yugoslavia 1994. That game continued} 11. Be3 Rb8 12. Qe2 Be7 13. f4 Na5 {, after which} 14. Nd2 {, intending g3-g4, was probably best} ({in the game White played} 14. Nd4 {, which gave Black counterplay based on the occupation of c4.})) ({Expanding on the queenside with} 9... b5 $2 {is distinctly premature, White having a powerful answer in} 10. a4 b4 11. Nd5 $1 exd5 12. exd5 Na7 13. d6 $1 Qb8 14. Re1+ Kd8 15. Be3 {(threatening 16 Bxa8 Qxa8 17 Bb6+)} Nc6 16. a5 Bb7 ({and not} 16... Bxd6 17. Bb6+ Bc7 18. Bxc6 {etc.}) 17. Bb6+ Kc8 18. Bc7 Qa7 {, and now} 19. Qe2 Nd8 20. Qc4 Nc6 21. Nd4 {is just one way to win.}) 10. a4 d6 11. f4 {Nigel Davies: 'This is one way to stop 11...b6 because of 12 e5.'} ({Nigel Davies: 'White can also play'} 11. a5 {Ü 29/305 Ü 29/305 Nigel Davies: ', after which'} O-O ({and not} 11... b6 {because of} 12. axb6 Qxb6 13. Be3 Qc7 14. Nb5 Qb8 15. e5 $1) 12. Be3 Ne5 13. Bb6 Qb8 14. Qe2 Nfd7 15. Na4 {gave White a bind in Popovic-Rajkovic, Kladovo 1980.}) 11... O-O $2 {Nigel Davies: 'Black's king is not altogether comfortable on the kingside because of the weakness created with 8...h6. On the other hand it is difficult to see a superior alternative.'} ({Nigel Davies: 'In the game Popovic-Romanishin, Novi Sad 1982 Black delayed castling with'} 11... Bd7 {Nigel Davies: 'but after'} 12. a5 b5 13. axb6 Qxb6+ 14. Kh1 Rc8 15. Qe2 Nb4 16. Be3 Qc7 17. Nd4 {found himself with the inferior game because of his weak a-pawn.}) ({Meanwhile} 11... b6 {is very risky because of} 12. e5 {.}) 12. g4 $1 {Nigel Davies: 'Certainly the most direct move but not the only one.'} ({White can also prepare this advance with} 12. Be3 {, for example} b6 13. Qe2 Rb8 (13... Bb7 14. g4 Nd7 {is also possible}) 14. g4 Na5 (14... Nh7 $5 15. Rad1 Re8 16. Nd2 Nb4 17. Nf3 e5 18. f5 Bb7 {was also interesting in Van der Wiel-Lobron, Wijk aan Zee 1985}) 15. g5 hxg5 16. fxg5 Nh7 17. h4 Nc4 18. Bd4 Bd7 19. Rf2 b5 20. axb5 axb5 21. e5 d5 22. Raf1 g6 23. Nd1 b4 {produced a double-edged game in Benjamin-Djuric, Hastings 1984/85.}) 12... Bd7 $6 {Nigel Davies: '?' Nigel Davies: 'A serious mistake, after which White's kingside pawns roll on unimpeded.'} ({Nigel Davies: 'Black had to try'} 12... Nd7 {Nigel Davies: '!' Nigel Davies: ', after which'} 13. Be3 b6 14. Qe2 Bb7 {produces a tough middlegame in which both sides have chances. Nigel Davies: 'is also possible'}) 13. h4 $1 $16 {Nigel Davies: '!'} ({After the immediate} 13. g5 $2 hxg5 14. fxg5 Nh7 {Black would occupy the weakened e5-square. By preparing this with 13 h4, White gets ready to recapture on g5 with the h-pawn and storm down the h-file with his major pieces.}) 13... b5 14. g5 Nh7 ({And not} 14... hxg5 15. hxg5 Nh7 {because of} 16. Qh5 {intending Rf1-f3-h3.}) 15. Be3 b4 (15... bxa4 16. Rxa4 Rfb8 17. Rc4 $16) 16. Ne2 d5 {Nigel Davies: '!?' Nigel Davies: 'With White threatening Ng3 followed by Qg4, Black decides to break out at any cost. The pawn sacrifice might have been more effective had Black's knight on h7 not been so badly placed.'} ({An alternative method of trying to do this was with} 16... e5 {but then} 17. gxh6 Bxh4 (17... gxh6 18. fxe5) 18. hxg7 Kxg7 19. Qd2 $1 {leaves Black's king in a perilous state.}) 17. exd5 (17. e5 h5 $1 { g6}) 17... exd5 18. Qxd5 Rac8 19. a5 {Nigel Davies: '!' Nigel Davies: 'Introducing the possibility of Bb6, which stops Black from putting a rook on d8. '} Nb8 {[ e6]} (19... Be6 20. Qxc6 Qxc6 21. Bxc6 Rxc6 22. Ned4 $16) 20. Ned4 Bg4 21. Rae1 Rfd8 (21... Rfe8 $2 22. g6) 22. Qe4 {[ f5]} Re8 23. Bf2 {Nigel Davies: 'Pinning the bishop on e7 and threatening to embarrass Black's other bishop with 24 f5. '} (23. f5 $2 Bxg5) 23... Bd7 24. Qd5 {[ g6]} Qd6 {Nigel Davies: 'White was threatening 25 g6. The next move gives back the pawn, but White's mighty bishops pair soon decide matters. '} (24... Nf8 25. h5 { g6}) 25. Nf5 {Nigel Davies: '!'} Qxd5 26. Nxe7+ Rxe7 27. Bxd5 Rxe1 28. Rxe1 Rxc2 $2 (28... Nf8 $16) 29. Re7 $18 {Nigel Davies: 'Threatening 30 g6, 30 Ba7 and 30 Rxf7. '} (29. g6 Nf6) 29... Bc6 30. Bxf7+ Kf8 31. Rc7 {[ 32. g6, 32. c5]} hxg5 32. Bc5+ Rxc5 33. Nxc5 {not only wins the exchange but threatens 34 Ne6 mate. 1-0 (33) Nunn,J-Tal,M Wijk aan Zee 33/260 1982 CI1-130 [Nunn,John DM]} 1-0

