{Another typical sacrifice occurs when White desires to play f4-f5, but would be unhappy to hand the e5-square over to Black. The advance e4-e5 also gives White a beautiful square on e4 for his knights, and in some cases, as in this example, also opens up for the light-squared bishop.} 19. e5 $1 (19. f5 Ne5 20. f6 Bf8) 19... dxe5 ({Here} 19... f5 20. Bxf5 $1 gxf5 21. Nxf5 {would have given White an irresistible attack; for instance} Rf8 22. Nxg7 Kxg7 23. Qg3+ Kh8 24. Bd4 {and there is no way Black will ever be able to survive.}) ({Instead} 19... Rf8 $5 {was not a completely stupid suggestion, though White must be better.}) 20. f5 $1 {White achieves everything with this move: an attack against the king as well as keeping the black pieces in the dark.} (20. fxe5 Rf8 21. d6 Qc6 22. Nce4 Nd3 (22... Bxe5 23. Nf5 gxf5 24. Qxf5) 23. Bxd3 cxd3 24. Nf6+ Bxf6 25. exf6 Qd5 (25... Bb7 26. Nf5) 26. Qf4 Bb7) 20... Bb7 ({It is difficult to suggest better alternatives. If now} 20... Rf8 $2 21. f6 $1 {and White wins a piece, as} Bh8 22. d6 $1 Qxd6 {(otherwise Nd5-e7+)} 23. Nf5 $1 {wins for White.} Qxf6 24. Nd5) ({In some situations} 20... e4 {can be the way for Black to respond, giving up the e-pawn in order to get the e5-square for his knight. Here he is too late, and White wins at once with the simple} 21. fxg6 {because of Qf7+.}) 21. Rad1 (21. fxg6 fxg6 22. Bxg6 hxg6 23. Qf7+ Kh7 (23... Kh8 24. Qxg6) 24. Nh5 Rg8 (24... gxh5 25. Rf5) 25. Nf6+ Kh8 26. Rf5 $5) 21... Ba8 $6 ({It is not easy (impossible) to find a better move for Black. His pieces have no play.} 21... b4 22. Nce4 Nxe4 23. Nxe4 b3 24. Bb1 {is the computer's suggestion, but this hardly improves the black position.}) ({Probably} 21... e4 $1 {was worth a try. White has many ways to get a good game (e.g.} 22. Nxb5 {,} (22. Bf4 {,}) (22. f6 {), but maybe that in itself would be the problem for him. A direct win is not easy to spot.}) (22. fxg6 fxg6 23. Qf7+ Kh8 {is less critical now, as the white knights are not attacking.})) 22. Nce4 Na4 ({Already it is hard to find a salvation for Black. The text just loses a piece, but look at lines like} 22... Nxe4 23. Nxe4 f6 (23... Rbd8 24. fxg6 fxg6 25. Qf7+ Kh8 26. Ng5 Qb7 27. Nxh7 $1 (27. Bxg6 $5 {is also strong}) 27... Nf8 28. Ng5 Re7 29. Be4 $1 {and Black is being outplayed by the book.}) ({or} 23... Kh8 24. f6 $1 Bf8 25. Ng5 {and White is completely winning, one reason being that} Nc5 26. d6 $1 {wins a piece}) 24. fxg6 hxg6 25. Qg3 g5 26. Qg4) 23. Bxa4 bxa4 24. fxg6 fxg6 25. Qf7+ Kh8 26. Nc5 {Now White wins a piece and the game.} Qa7 27. Qxd7 Qxd7 28. Nxd7 Rxb2 29. Nb6 Rb3 30. Nxc4 Rd8 31. d6 Rc3 32. Rc1 Rxc1 33. Rxc1 Bd5 34. Nb6 Bb3 35. Ne4 h6 36. d7 Bf8 37. Rc8 Be7 38. Bc5 Bh4 39. g3 {1-0 (21) Penrose-Tal Leipzig Olympiad 1960 SO: The Modern Benoni [Endre Vegh]} 1-0

