{The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario. R.Scherbakov: 'A classic game, which was the origin of the strong attacking concept connected with sacrificing the central pawn. Lev Polugaevsky crushed Mikhail Tal in brilliant style and almost without a single inaccuracy - it's certainly one of the best games in the whole history of chess!''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Ein Läuferopfer auf h7 in einer Partie zweier Topgroßmeister sieht man nicht aller Tage. Doch hier ergibt es sich logisch aus einer Eröffnungsvariante - kein Wunder, dass es in späteren Partien zur Wiederholung des Opfers kam. Doch die Bilanz für die Nachziehenden ist schrecklich.'} 1. c4 {Belov Krasenkow,Michal Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov'' Krasenkow,Michal Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov'' Belov Krasenkow,Michal Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. d4 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 (6. e3 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'}) 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ (9... Qa5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'} 10. Rb1 $1 {'' !} Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 $16) 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 Nc6 (11... Nd7 {Belov} 12. O-O Nf6 13. Rfe1 b6 14. a4 Bb7 15. Bd3 Rc8 16. a5 $14 {Yusupov,A-Elson,J/San Pisafort/1981/1:0 (67)}) 12. O-O b6 13. Rad1 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!''} Bb7 14. Rfe1 {Diagram [#] Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Diagram [#]''} Na5 {This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.''} ({The aforementioned game went} 14... Rc8 15. d5 {!} exd5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'} ({I assume that both Petrosian during the game and Tal when commenting, thought that} 15... Na5 {was dangerous in view of} 16. dxe6 {but this sems to lead to a draw: Knaak,Rainer: '(Polugaevsky,Lev) but this sems to lead to a draw:'} Qxd2 17. exf7+ Kh8 18. Nxd2 Nxc4 19. Nxc4 Rxc4 20. e5 Bc8 21. e6 Bxe6 22. Rxe6 Rc7 $11 {Marin,Mihail. However, White can transpose to the main game with 16.Bd3}) 16. Bxd5 $16 {Spassky,Boris - Petrosian,Tigran V, Moscow (m/5) 1969 - 7/488} Qc7 {Belov} (16... Na5 17. Qf4 Qc7 18. Qf5 Bxd5 19. exd5) 17. Qg5 h6 18. Qg4 $14) (14... Ne7 {Belov} 15. d5 exd5 16. exd5 Nf5 $14) 15. Bd3 Rc8 {Diagram [#] Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Diagram [#]''} 16. d5 $1 $16 {The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration. Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration. R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'White has been trying many options here but this direct pawn push is certainly one of the best, if not the best.''' Knaak,Rainer: 'würde zur Partie führen.'} ({Of course, keeping good control over the center, White can make some preparations first and still get the result in case of Black's inaccurate play. For example,} 16. h3 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} Rc7 $6 (16... Qd6 $5) (16... Qe7 $5) (16... h6 $5) 17. Qf4 Rd7 $6 18. d5 exd5 19. e5 g6 20. Ng5 Re7 21. Qh4 $36 {with a strong initiative, Luther - Aring, Ditzingen 2009.}) 16... exd5 {Knaak,Rainer: '?!' '?!' '?!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Schwarz darf dieses Bauernopfer nicht annehmen.'} (16... Qe7 $5 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '(R.Scherbakov) is also possible:''} 17. Qf4 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} (17. dxe6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev}) 17... Rfd8 18. h4 h6 19. h5 Qf6 20. Qg4 {with the better chances for White, Sasikiran - Golod, Ajaccio 2006.}) ({In case of} 16... Nc4 $6 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) White could have won a pawn by} 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. dxe6 Qxd2 19. exf7+ Rxf7 20. Rxd2 {with reasonable winning chances as} Rxe4 $2 {was impossible due to} 21. Rxe4 Bxe4 22. Ng5 {'+-'} Re7 23. Rd8+) (16... Qd6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) is worth considering, but it cannot completely solve the problems. In the game Dokhoian - Webb, EU Cup 1989 White opted for} 17. dxe6 $5 ({the tempting} 17. e5 $2 {failed to} Qxd5 18. Qf4 (18. Bxh7+ $2 Kxh7 19. Qg5 Qc5 $1 (19... Qc6 $2 20. Rd4 $1 $40) 20. Qh5+ (20. Rd4 Bxf3 $19) 20... Kg8 21. Ng5 Qc2 $19) 18... Qc5 ({even} 18... h6 $5 {!} 19. Bh7+ Kxh7 20. Rxd5 Bxd5 {was quite good for Black}) 19. Ng5 h6 20. Nh7 Rfd8 21. Nf6+ Kh8 {and White cannot create any real threats}) ({but other options, such as} 17. Qg5 $5 {were playable for White}) (17. Qe2 {Belov} e5 $14) 17... Qxe6 (17... fxe6 $5 {deserved attention} 18. Bb5 $1 $14 {Belov}) 18. Nd4 Qe5 19. Nf5 g6 20. Nh6+ Kh8 21. Ng4 Qe6 22. Qf4 f6 23. Bb5 $1 {and White has obtained a clear advantage.}) ({Black could also pay more attention to} 16... Rc5 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov}) ({but perhaps the most reliable way was} 16... h6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 17. Qf4 Rc5 18. Nd4 exd5 19. e5 Bc8 $1 {- see the game Ponomariov - Vallejo Pons/Donostia 2009.}) 17. e5 $1 {Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. R.Scherbakov: 'This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.''' Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. R.Scherbakov: 'This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.''' Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. R.Scherbakov: 'This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.''' Belov: '' '' ''} Nc4 {Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time. R.Scherbakov: 'A natural reply.''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Mit 17...d4 wurde zweimal ein Remis geschafft, aber die Analyse ergibt forciert klaren Vorteil für Weiß:'} (17... g6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'} 18. Qh6 $16 f5 19. exf6 Qxf6 20. Ng5 $18) ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Black tried many possibilities. Let's briefly take a look at them:''} 17... d4 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} 18. Ng5 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?''} (18. Nxd4 $14 {Krasenkow,Michal}) 18... h6 19. Nh7 Nc4 (19... Re8 $2 20. Nf6+ gxf6 21. Qxh6 $18) 20. Qf4 Nb2 $1 21. Nxf8 Nxd1 22. e6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!''} Rc7 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'and here in the game Gulkov - Tkebuchava, Moscow 1994 White should have played''} 23. Nh7 $1 {droht 24.e7!} ({the complications after} 23. Qf5 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} Kxf8 24. Qh7 Ne3 $1 25. e7+ $1 Kxe7 26. Qxg7 Rc6 27. fxe3 dxe3 28. Rxe3+ Re6 29. Rxe6+ Kxe6 30. Bc4+ Kd6 31. Qxf7 Kc5 {ended with a drawish endgame}) 23... fxe6 (23... Nc3 $2 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} 24. e7 Rxe7 25. Rxe7 Qxe7 26. Qb8+) 24. Rxd1 {und obwohl der h7 nicht ziehen kann, sollte Weiß auf Gewinn stehen.} Qe7 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} 25. Re1 {and White should be winning}) (17... Qe7 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 18. Qf4 (18. Nd4 {Krasenkow,Michal Krasenkow,Michal Krasenkow,Michal Belov: '' ''} g6 19. Qh6 f5 20. h4 Nc6 {1-0 (38) Pogorelov,R (2415)-Magem Badals,J (2505) Sitges 1993} 21. Nf3 Nd8 22. h5 $44) 18... f5 19. Nd4 g6 20. h4 Nc6 21. Nb5 {with advantage, V.Bagirov - Zhuravliov, USSR 1974}) (17... h6 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 18. Qf4 Nc6 19. Qf5 g6 20. Qg4 {Belov: '' '' Belov: 'Popov,N-Rumiancev,G/URS/1978/'} Kg7 21. h4 (21. e6 f5 $14 {Pogorelov - Planas Ferret, Andorra 2003} 22. Qa4 {Krasenkow,Michal} Qe7 23. Bb5 $16 {1-0 (34) Pogorelov,R (2449)-Planas Ferret,R (2235) Andorra 2003}) 21... h5 22. Qg3 Qe7 23. Ng5 Nb4 24. Bb1 (24. Bf5 $1 Rc4 25. Ne6+ $1 {was even more energetic}) 24... Rc4 25. e6 f6 26. Nf7 Rg4 27. Qe3 Rxg2+ 28. Kf1 Rxf7 29. exf7 Qxf7 30. a3 {and White eventually converted his material advantage, N. Popov - Rumiantsev, USSR 1978.}) 18. Qf4 Nb2 {Knaak,Rainer: '?' '?' '?' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'The principled way - Black is in a hurry to get rid of White's dangerous bishop. However, it does not bring the desired result...''' Knaak,Rainer: ''} ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'A cautious''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Nach'} 18... h6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'is maybe the best defence - it is considered in the game Huzman - Parligras/Turin (ol) 2006.'''} 19. Qf5 (19. Bf5 Rc7 20. h4 Bc8 21. Nd4 Bxf5 22. Nxf5 Kh7 23. Nd6) 19... g6 20. Qh3 (20. Qg4 $1 {Knaak,Rainer}) (20. Qf4 {!} Kg7 21. Re2) 20... Kg7 21. e6 $16 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: ''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '' ''' Knaak,Rainer: 'wird es für Schwarz schwierig den Punkt g6 zu verteidigen.'} Qf6 {Krasenkow,Michal} (21... fxe6 {?} 22. Nd4 $18) 22. exf7 Qxf7 (22... Rc6 23. Bxc4 dxc4 24. Ne5 Bc8 {!}) 23. Qg3 Rce8 {1-0 (38) Bhat,V (2510)-Gerzhoy,L (2456) Palma de Mallorca 2009} 24. Nh4 $16) ({Here Black also tried many possibilities:} 18... f6 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 19. e6 Qe7 20. Nd4 g6 21. Qg3 Kh8 22. h4 $36 {with the initiative, Toth - Gasthofer, Baden 2000}) ({a provocative} 18... Rc6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) could nevertheless be met by} 19. Nd4 $1 {!} (19. Ng5 $6 h6 20. Bh7+ Kh8 21. Nxf7+ Kxh7 (21... Rxf7 22. Qxf7 Kxh7 23. Qxb7 $18 (23. e6 Nd6 24. Qd7 $18)) 22. Nxd8 Rxf4 23. Nxb7 (23. e6 $2 {'+-' Polugaevsky} Rf8 (23... Rc7 24. e7 Nd6 25. Nxb7 Rxb7 26. Rxd5 Ne8 27. Rd8 Rf7 28. Rxe8 Kg6 $13) 24. Nxb7 Rc7 25. e7 Re8 $17) 23... Re4 $13) 19... Rc7 {This position arose in the game Spasov - Kostakiev, corr. 1999. White could have secured the better chances by} 20. e6 $1 ({in the game White went for} 20. Bxh7+ $2 Kxh7 21. Rd3 Bc8 $19) 20... Nb2 (20... fxe6 21. Nxe6 $1 Rxf4 22. Nxd8 Nb2 23. Rb1 Rf8 24. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 25. Ne6) 21. Rc1 $1 Rxc1 (21... Nxd3 $2 22. Qxc7 Qxc7 23. Rxc7 Nxe1 24. e7 Re8 25. Rxb7 $18) 22. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 23. Qxc1 Qf6 (23... Nd3 24. Qb1) 24. Qxb2 Re8 25. Qb1+ Kg8 26. exf7+ Kxf7 27. Rxe8 Kxe8 28. Qe1+ Kf7 29. Qe3 {with a stable advantage for White.}) ({in case of an immediate} 18... Rc7 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) White would come up with the thematic} 19. Bxh7+ $1 (19. Ng5 $5 h6 20. Nh7 Nb2 $1 (20... Re8 $2 21. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 22. exf6 Re6 23. Qg3+ Kf8 24. Qg7+ Ke8 25. Rxe6+ $18) 21. Nf6+ (21. Nxf8 Nxd1 22. Nh7 Nc3 $1 (22... Nb2 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Qf5 g6 25. Qf4 Kg7 26. Bb5 $36) 23. Nf6+ Kf8 $1 24. e6 gxf6 25. Qxh6+ Ke7 26. exf7+ Ne4 27. Qh7 Qf8 28. f3 Kd8 29. fxe4 Rxf7 $13) 21... Kh8 22. Qf5 g6 23. Qf4 Kg7 24. Rd2 Nxd3 25. Rxd3 Rc4 { Qd8-e7}) 19... Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 21. h4 $1 Qc8 (21... Rh8 22. Rd3 $18) 22. h5+ $1 (22. Rd3 f6 23. h5+ Kh6 24. Ne6+ Kh7 25. Nxf8+ Qxf8 26. exf6 {was also promising for White in the game Flesch - L. Kovacs, Budapest 1969} Rf7 27. Qf5+ Kg8 28. Qe6 gxf6 29. Rg3+ Kh8 30. h6 Ne5 31. f4 Re7 32. Rg8+ Qxg8 33. Qxf6+ Kh7 34. Qxe7+ Nf7 35. Qxb7 Qf8 36. Re7 Kxh6 37. Rxf7 {1-0 Flesch, J-Kovacs, L/Budapest 1969}) 22... Kh6 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. e6 Rcxf7 25. exf7 {with a decisive advantage.}) (18... g6 19. Qh6 Qd7 20. Ng5 f6 21. exf6 Rxf6 22. Bxc4 Rxc4 23. Nxh7 {!}) {Diagram [#] Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Diagram [#]''} 19. Bxh7+ $1 {The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work. R.Scherbakov: 'Striking just in time!''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Das bekannte Läuferopfer auf h7 sieht man selten zwischen Weltklasseleuten, aber hier liegt der Erfolg keineswegs auf der Hand.'} ({A faint-hearted} 19. Rd2 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} Nxd3 20. Rxd3 Rc4 21. Nd4 Qe7 {was okay for Black.}) 19... Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Natürlich.'} (20... Kg8 $2 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} 21. Qh4 Re8 (21... Qxg5 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} 22. Qxg5 Nxd1 23. Rxd1 {was clearly insufficient for Black.}) 22. Qh7+ (22. e6 $1 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev !} fxe6 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. Qh8+ Ke7 25. Qxg7+ Kd6 26. Nf7+ $18) 22... Kf8 23. e6 {würde 24.e7+! drohen.}) 21. h4 $3 {According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#. R.Scherbakov: 'This cool pawn advance is the only way to maintain the initiative. Now Black has to parry a mating threat h4-h5.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '22.h5 h5 23.g4 g6 25.f5 h6 26.h7 g5 27.h5 f4 28.f5#/' ! 'with the idea' 22.h5! Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qh5 Kf4 27.Qf5# Polugaevsky Knaak,Rainer: 'mit der Drohung 22.h5+ K:h5 23.g4+ Kg6 25.Df5+ Kh6 26.Dh7+ K:g5 27.Dh5+ Kf4 28.Df5#.  Der Textzug und sogar noch 25.e6! waren von Polugaevsky vor der Partie analysiert worden. Die Stellung kam später noch mehrfach aufs Brett - aus schwarzer Sicht wohl immer unwissend. Die Korrektheit des Opfers konnte dabei nie in Zweifel gezogen werden.'} Rc4 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'The best and probably the only defence!'''} ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'or'''} 21... f6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '??'''} 22. h5+ {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!'''} Kxh5 23. g4+ Kh6 (23... Kg6 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 24. Qf5+ Kh6 25. Nf7+ $1 Rxf7 26. Qh5#) 24. Qh2+ $18) ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'other possible defences failed as well:''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Auf'} 21... f5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '?'''} 22. Rd4 $1 $18 {Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!' '+-!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: '(with the idea h4-h5!)''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' Knaak