{[%mdl 8192] E13: Queen's Indian/Nimzo-Indian hybrid: 4 Nc3 Bb7 5 Bg5 h6 6 Bh4 Bb4 Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E13: Queen's Indian/Nimzo-Indian hybrid: 4 Nc3 Bb7 5 Bg5 h6 6 Bh4 Bb4 '''} 1. d4 {Tal,Mihail Tal,Mihail Tal,Mihail: '1) Tal in Sahs (Riga) 1959. Reprinted in 2) Kirillov,V: Mikhail Tal 1949-1961. Riga. 1996. p.188-191.'} Nf6 2. c4 e6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'In this tournament the King's Indian Defence underwent certain crises, and even such an expert in this opening as Gligoric was subjecting it to major analysis. At the end of the tournament he again used it, but without success.''} 3. Nf3 b6 4. Nc3 Bb7 5. Bg5 Bb4 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'By a transposition of moves we find ourselves in a variation of the Nimzo-Indian Defence where the position, in the light of the most recent games, slightly favours White. Of this variation I should have been careful, because Gligoric was a witness to my game with Dückstein in the Zurich international tournament, where White got the better of it. Having burned my bridges as to the choice of opening I was forced to employ the same continuation as in the game against Dückstein.''} 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 g5 ({Mikhail Tal: 'International Master Konstantinopolsky, who annotated this game in the tournament bulletin, preferred'} 7... Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qe7 {Mikhail Tal: 'followed by ...d6, ...Nbd7 and ...0-0-0. I think that the text-move is stronger, since after 8...Qe7 White would continue'  d6, bd7 und 0-0-0 (Konstantinopolski)} 9. Nd2 {[%csl Ye4] Mikhail Tal: 'and the knight would control the very important square e4.'}) 8. Bg3 Ne4 9. Qc2 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 d6 11. Bd3 Nxg3 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'In Keres-Taimanov, 22nd USSR Championship, Black played'} 11... f5 {Mikhail Tal: 'with the idea of attacking on the kingside, but after'} 12. d5 $1 {1-0 Keres,P-Taimanov,M URS-ch22 Moscow 1955 Mikhail Tal: 'not only did the attack fail, but Black's position became untenable owing to his bad pawn structure. With the text-move Black maintains his pawn structure.'}) 12. hxg3 Nd7 13. a4 ({Mikhail Tal: 'More natural would be'} 13. Be4 {Mikhail Tal: 'but after'} Bxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qc6+ Ke7 {Mikhail Tal: 'the 'dangerous' position of Black's king is only illusory. Black's next move is positionally forced.'}) 13... a5 14. Rb1 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!''} g4 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'It is interesting that Black has no better move. If he moves his queen to enable queenside castling, then after Be4 and the exchange of bishops, the rook on b1 would be ideally placed and White would follow up with c5. The move ...Nf6 is impossible at the moment, because of Nxg5.''} (14... Nf6 15. Nxg5) (14... Qe7 { 0-0-0} 15. Be4) 15. Nh4 Nf6 16. d5 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'This move was necessary – otherwise Black would prepare for queenside castling, and then I could not find an active plan for White.''} ({More consistent is} 16. c5 $1) 16... Qe7 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'If Black takes the pawn on d5, then all White's pieces would become very dangerous.''} (16... exd5 $6) 17. O-O ({Mikhail Tal: 'Weaker would be'} 17. dxe6 $143 Qxe6 18. Bf5 Be4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 17... Nd7 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'After this logical move White takes over the initiative.''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Very tempting was the exchange sacrifice' Mikhail Tal: 'To be truthful I must say that after'} 17... O-O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'I would not have given up my active knight on h4 for the passive rook, but would have played the simple'} 18. dxe6 (18. e4 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 18... fxe6 19. Ng6 Qg7 20. Nxh8 Rxh8 {Mikhail Tal: ': in this position White would play'} 21. c5 {Mikhail Tal: 'activating his pieces. Of course the play would become very interesting, but Black's chances would not be bad.'}) 18. dxe6 Qxe6 {last book move Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move'''} 19. Bf5 {White threatens to win material: Bf5xe6 Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White threatens to win material: Bf5xe6'''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Immediately necessary, as after'} 19. Rfd1 Nc5 20. Bf5 Be4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black equalises.'}) 19... Qxc4 20. Rfd1 Nf6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Black still cannot castle.''} (20... Ne5 $5 $16) ({Mikhail Tal: 'In the position after' Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 20... O-O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'White would avoid the continuation' Mikhail Tal: 'much stronger would be'} 21. Rd4 (21. Rb5 Bc6 22. Rd4 Bxb5 23. Rxc4 Bxc4 24. Qe4 Be6 $11 {Mikhail Tal: ', when White would have no obvious continuation.'}) 21... Qc5 22. Rb5 Qa3 23. Bxg4 {Mikhail Tal: 'and White has an excellent position.'}) 21. Rd4 Qc6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The aim of''} 22. Be6 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: 'White's bishop cannot be taken.' Mikhail Tal: 'is to force the rook to g8, where it will become the object of White's attack.''} (22. Bd3 $142 {and White could have gained the advantage} Kd8 23. Rf4 $18) {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'After''} 22... Rg8 $14 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): ''' Mikhail Tal: ', if now''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Black cannot take the bishop,'} 22... fxe6 {Mikhail Tal: ', because of'} 23. Qg6+ Kd7 24. Qg7+ {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: '. There is also the threat of winning the queen by 23 Rc4.'}) ({Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 22... d5 {Mikhail Tal: 'I would have been greatly tempted to continue with'} 23. Bxf7+ Kxf7 24. Qg6+ $40 {Mikhail Tal: 'and it is easy to see that White's attack is very dangerous. How to cope with all White's threats? Gligoric finds the best answer.'}) (22... Z0) 23. Bc4 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!'} (23. Rc4 fxe6 24. Rxc6 Bxc6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black would have rook, bishop and pawn for queen.'}) 23... Kf8 {Tal,Mihail: '??' '??' '??' Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '?''} ({JvR: 'The pawn returns' Mikhail Tal: 'If'} 23... O-O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'then'} 24. Bb5 (24. Qf5+ Nd7 {(Gligoric)} 25. Bxf7 Rg5 {with a proper defence.}) 24... Qc5 25. Nf5 Kb8 26. Ne7 Rg5 27. Rc4 Qe5 28. Nc6+ {Mikhail Tal: 'and Black is forced to give up his best defensive piece. (Nunn: Actually, this seems to be fine for Black, for example'} Bxc6 29. Bxc6 Qe6 {(threat 30...Rc5)} 30. Qd3 Nd7 31. Re4 Nc5 $1 {with some advantage for Black.) Now we see how exposed Black's position is by the fact that he is forced to play 22...Rg8. After the text-move White regains the pawn and keeps his positional advantage.}) 24. Bb5 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!''} Qc5 25. Rc4 Qe5 26. Rxc7 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: 'White has regained the pawn and will win another one.''} Be4 27. Bd3 d5 28. Rc6 Rb8 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'If White takes the pawn on b6 then after the exchange of rooks his initiative would slowly disappear. At the moment White's knight appears to be inactive but it has great potential. With his next move White decides to open up the position even more.''} 29. c4 {[%cal Yc4c5] White plans c5 Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White plans c5'''} ({Nach} 29. Rcxb6 Rxb6 30. Rxb6 {versiegt die weisse Initiative.}) 29... Rg5 ({Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 29... dxc4 30. Rxc4 Bxd3 31. Qxd3 {[%cal Rh4f5] Mikhail Tal: 'Black would have difficulty in preventing Nf5.'}) {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'After''} 30. c5 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'White still cannot get the knight into play, but now follows a new threat and White creates a dangerous passed pawn.' Mikhail Tal: ', Konstantinopolsky's advice to continue''} d4 (30... bxc5 {Mikhail Tal: 'must be a blunder, for White would not play'} {Mikhail Tal: 'but first exchange rooks at b8 and then collect the knight:'} 31. Rxb8+ (31. Qxc5+) 31... Qxb8 32. Rxf6 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 31. exd4 Qxd4 32. Bxe4 Qxe4 33. cxb6 Qxc2 34. Rxc2 Nd7 35. b7 Nc5 36. Rb5 ({Mikhail Tal: 'It is possible that 36 f4 was even stronger, but after a tiring fight and being in time-trouble I did not want to go into the complications which would arise after' Mikhail Tal: 'This is not good enough, and after'} 36. f4 Rd5 37. Rb5 Rd1+ {Mikhail Tal: '. Now' Mikhail Tal: ', I did not like'} 38. Kh2 (38. Kf2 {Mikhail Tal: '. That is why I decided that two pawns were enough to win this game.' gefiel mir ganz und gar nicht.}) 38... Nxb7 39. Rc7 (39. Nf5 $1 {[%cal Rc2c7]} Rd7 40. Nxh6 $18 {Fritz}) 39... Re8 40. Rbxb7 {Mikhail Tal: 'is dangerous due to'} Ree1 {Mikhail Tal: ', e.g.'} 41. Rc8+ Kg7 42. Nf5+ Kh7 43. Rxf7+ Kg6 44. Rg7+ Kh5 $19 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 36... Nxb7 37. Rcb2 (37. Rb1 $142 $5 {and White can already relax} Rd5 38. Rc7 Kg7 $18) 37... Rxb5 38. Rxb5 Ke7 39. Nf5+ {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'At last the knight becomes active.''} Kd7 40. Nxh6 Kc7 41. Nxf7 Rf8 42. Nh6 Rd8 43. Rg5 Rd1+ 44. Kh2 Ra1 45. Rxg4 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The sealed move.'' Tal,Mihail: 'Der Abgabezug'} Nc5 46. Rc4 Kc6 47. Nf5 Rxa4 48. Nd4+ Kb6 49. Rxa4 Nxa4 50. g4 Nc3 51. Kg3 a4 52. Kf4 {Tal,Mihail: '?' '?' '?' Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: '??' Mikhail Tal: 'Carelessness: White forgot that Black's pawn was advancing.''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'After he simple'} 52. Nc2 $18 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black would have to resign.'}) 52... a3 (52... Kc7 53. Nc2 Kd7 $18) 53. Nc2 a2 (53... Kc5 54. Nxa3 Nd1 55. f3 $18) 54. g5 Kc5 55. g6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The only move to win for White.''} Nd5+ 56. Kg5 Kc4 (56... Kd6 {does not solve anything} 57. g7 Ne7 58. Kf6 Ng8+ 59. Kf7 Nh6+ 60. Kg6 $18) 57. g7 Ne7 58. f4 Kc3 59. Na1 Kb2 60. f5 Kxa1 61. f6 Kb1 62. fxe7 a1=Q {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'After''} 63. e8=Q {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'we see the idea of move 57 – Black's queen cannot take the pawn,''} Qa5+ (63... Qxg7+ {cannot change what is in store for White Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'because of Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'cannot change what is in store for White'''} 64. Qg6+ {Tal,Mihail: '+-' '+-' '+-' Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: '.''} Qxg6+ 65. Kxg6 Kb2 66. g4 Ka2 67. g5 Kb3 68. Kf5 Ka4 69. g6 Kb5 70. g7 Kc6 71. g8=Q Kc5 72. Ke5 Kb4 73. Kd4 Kb5 74. Qe6 Ka4 75. Kc4 Ka5 76. Kc5 Ka4 77. Qa2#) 64. Kh6 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: 'Tal played the endgame well.''} (64. Kh6 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: 'Tal played the endgame well.''} Qd2+ 65. Kh7 Qxg2 66. Qg6+ Qxg6+ 67. Kxg6 Kb2 68. g8=Q Kc3 69. Qd5 Kb4 70. Kh7 Ka4 71. Qc5 Kb3 72. Kg6 Kb2 73. Qc4 Kb1 74. Qb3+ Ka1 75. Qb4 Ka2 76. Kf5 Ka1 77. Ke5 Ka2 78. Kd4 Ka1 79. Kc3 Ka2 80. Qb2# {Tal,Mihail: '1-0 (80) Tal,M-Gligoric,S 30: Bled/Zagreb/Belgrade Candidates 1959 Life and Games of Tal [Mikhail Tal]'}) 1-0

