1. e4 {Glatman GM Mihail Marin Glatman} c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. d4 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Ng3 $5 e6 7. Bd3 c5 (7... Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Qe2 (9. b3 c5 10. Bb2 b6 11. Qe2 Bb7 12. c4 Re8 13. dxc5 Bxc5 14. Ng5 $14 {Tiviakov,S-Adams,A/New York(m/12)/1994/0,5 (58)}) 9... c5 10. Rd1 Qc7 11. c4 Re8 12. dxc5 Nxc5 13. Bc2 Ncd7 14. Ng5 $1 $14 {Tiviakov,S-Adams,A/New York(m/10)/1994/o,5 (59)}) 8. O-O cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bc5 10. Nf3 (10. Nb3 Be7 11. Re1 O-O 12. Qf3 a5 13. a4 Nb6 14. Bd2 $14 {Tiviakov,S-Adams,A/New York(m/1)/1994/0:1 (59)}) 10... O-O 11. Qe2 (11. b3 b6 12. Bb2 Bb7 13. Qe2 Qc7 $10 {Spassky,B-Filip,M/Nice(ol)/1974/1:0 (64) Inf 17/222/}) 11... b6 (11... Qc7 $5) 12. Bf4 Bb7 13. Rad1 Nd5 (13... Qc8 $5 14. Kh1 a6 $10) 14. Bg5 Qc7 15. Nh5 $1 Kh8 $1 16. Be4 (16. c4 N5f6 $1 17. Nxf6 gxf6 $6 (17... Nxf6 18. Ne5 Rad8 $14) 18. Bh4 Rg8 19. Be4 Be7 20. Bg3 Qc8 21. Nd4 $16) 16... f6 17. Bh4 Bd6 (17... Rac8 $5) 18. c4 Ba6 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal has built up an attacking position, but his queenside is hanging. For instance, 19.b3 allows .. .Nc3 and 19.Bd3 is met with 19...Nf4 20.Nxf4 Bxf4, as after 21.Qxe6 Nc5 the bishop is hanging. These aspects of the position suggest immediate action, so he was very much tempted to play the next sacrifice. But when trying to calculate its ultimate consequences he got lost in the forest of variations. Without any logical explanation, he remembered two verses of the Russian poet Chukovski: "Oh, how difficult this task is, to take the hippopotamus out of the swamp..." And suddenly in Tal's imagination, the chessboard was populated by wild animals, but he did not find a way of saving the hippopotamus. "Well, let it drown!" he concluded, and after the animals disappeared he played the move he had been dreaming of. Next morning he read with great interest in the newspapers that Tal had sacrificed a knight after 40 minutes of exhaustive calculating... Nothing like that, of course, the key moment was deciding that since the move looked reasonably good, it was the natural choice, and as there was no way of strengthening the position by simple means anymore, he should simply play it! Before moving on, we should underline that the combination is by no means winning. In the current position, White is a tempo ahead in development (...Rad8) and it's his turn to move. We could evaluate the position as something between "just some" and "a certain" advantage for White. In case of correct play from both sides, the same evaluation should be valid at a later stage, too, according to Steinitz' principle.'} 19. Nxg7 $1 Kxg7 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Black does not have any favourable intermediate moves at his disposal.'} (19... Bxc4 {GM Mihail Marin: '?'} 20. Nxe6 {GM Mihail Marin: ''} Bxe2 21. Nxc7 $16) (19... Nf4 $2 {GM Mihail Marin} 20. Qd2 $16) 20. Nd4 {GM Mihail Marin: 'The Achille's heel in Black's position (e6) is in danger. There are several ways to defend it, but Vasiukov's move looks the most natural.'} Nc5 $8 (20... Nf4 {(GM Mihail Marin) has the drawback of leaving the rook on a8 under attack.} 21. Qg4+ Kh8 22. Bxa8 Bxc4 $1 {It is essential to consolidate e6.} (22... Rxa8 $2 23. Nxe6 $18 {wins material due to the hanging minor pieces.}) 23. Bg3 {White has to reckon with ...Rg8.} (23. Bf3 Rg8 {traps the queen.}) (23. Bc6 Rg8 24. Qf3 Rxg2+ 25. Kh1 Bxf1 26. Rxf1 Rg6 {offers White just some compensation for the missing pawn.}) 23... Rxa8 24. Bxf4 Rg8 $1 25. Nxe6 $1 Rxg4 26. Nxc7 Bxf4 27. Rxd7 Bxh2+ 28. Kxh2 Bxf1 29. g3 Rg7 30. Rd8+ Rg8 31. Rd6 $14 {With a pleasant ending for White due to his active pieces and better structure. With hindsight, this would have been Black's best defence.}) ({An alarming symptom for Black is that the most natural move,} 20... Rae8 $6 {(GM Mihail Marin) , completing development, is not adequate. When a player is behind in development, it is really troubling if the opponent's advance tends to increase.} 21. Qh5 $1 (21. Nxe6+ {helps Black to activate his play and retain just a slightly worse position.} Rxe6 22. Qg4+ Kh8 23. Qxe6 Bxh2+ 24. Kh1 Nc5 25. Qxd5 Bb7 26. Qf5 Bxe4 27. Bxf6+ Kg8 28. Qg5+ Bg6 29. Qd5+ Bf7 30. Qd4 $14) 21... f5 22. Bxd5 exd5 23. Rfe1 $3 $16 {White has a very strong initiative for the piece. His main threats are Nxf5+ and cxd5 followed by Ne6+.}) 21. Qg4+ {GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal does not comment on this move. After his prolonged thought before sacrificing, he was probably inclined to play natural moves at an increased speed. The plan directed against the pawn on e6 has the drawback that it allows Black to complete his development with ...Rae8 at last. There were two important alternatives.'} (21. Nxe6+ $5 {GM Mihail Marin} Nxe6 22. Bxd5 Ng5 23. Bxa8 Rxa8 24. Rfe1 $14 {White plans b2-b3 and Bg3, keeping a rook and two pawns for two minor pieces in a position with an open centre and pawns on two wings. This would offer him certain chances in the endgame. Besides, Black does not have time to grab any of the pawns, even though this would be done with tempo.} Bxc4 $2 (24... Bxh2+ $2 25. Kh1 Bf4 {Otherwise Bxg5 followed by Qg4 with a decisive attack.} 26. Rd4 $16 {Black is seriously hanging.}) 25. Qc2 {Threatening Rxd6.} b5 26. Bxg5 fxg5 27. Qc3+ Kg8 28. b3 Bf7 29. Qf6 $16) ({White's strongest continuation would have been the non-committal} 21. b4 $1 {GM Mihail Marin} Bxh2+ (21... Nxb4 {offers White a decisive initiative.} 22. Bxa8 Rxa8 23. Nxe6+ Nxe6 24. Qxe6) 22. Kh1 Qf4 23. Bxd5 Qxh4 24. Qxe6 Be5+ 25. Qh3 Qxh3+ 26. gxh3 Bxd4 27. Rxd4 {It appears that Black cannot move with his knight.} Rad8 (27... Na4 $6 28. b5) (27... Nd7 $2 28. Bxa8) 28. bxc5 bxc5 29. Rg1+ Kh8 30. Rdg4 $16 {White retains an overwhelming activity in the endgame.}) 21... Kh8 22. Nxe6 Nxe6 23. Qxe6 $16 {GM Mihail Marin: 'White will soon retrieve the piece, but things remain tense.'} Rae8 ({GM Mihail Marin: 'As pointed out by Tal, Black cannot save both his hanging minor pieces:'} 23... Bxh2+ 24. Kh1 Nb4 {GM Mihail Marin: '?'} 25. Bxf6+ $18 {GM Mihail Marin: ', winning both rooks.'}) 24. Qxd5 Bxh2+ 25. Kh1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Another critical moment. White is a pawn up already and his pieces are active. But at the same time his queen and light-squared bishop are somewhat exposed.'} Qf4 {GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'This attempt at a counterattack leaves Black hanging.'} ({GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal mentions that retrieving the pawn was better, even though White's position remains preferable:'} 25... Qxc4 $5 26. Rfe1 $14 (26. Qxc4 {GM Mihail Marin} Bxc4 27. Rfe1 $14)) ({Or} 25... Bxc4 {GM Mihail Marin} 26. Qf5 $16 {[%cal Gh4f6,Gd1d7] Attacking f6 and threatening Rd7.}) ({Now best would have been to prepare the capture on c4 with} 25... f5 $1 {(GM Mihail Marin) , dismantling White's attacking mechanism:} 26. Bxf5 Bxc4 27. Qd7 Qxd7 28. Bxd7 Re4 29. g3 {The bishop is trapped, but Black can equalize completely:} Bxf1 30. Rxf1 Rxh4 31. gxh4 Bg3 32. Kg2 Bxh4 $11 {, with a probable draw.}) 26. Qh5 {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Obviously Tal is in his element now.'} Qxe4 (26... Rxe4 {GM Mihail Marin} 27. Rd7 {leads to mate.}) 27. Rfe1 $6 ({GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal criticized this move, giving the following line as better:'} 27. Rde1 $142 Qg6 28. Qxg6 hxg6 29. Bxf6+ Kg8 30. Rxe8 Rxe8 31. Kxh2 Bxc4 32. Rc1 Bxa2 33. Rc7 $16 {GM Mihail Marin: 'But as we will see, the game continuation is not worse.'}) 27... Qg6 28. Qxg6 {GM Mihail Marin: '?'} ({Tal's initial intention had been} 28. Bxf6+ {GM Mihail Marin} Qxf6 ({Or if} 28... Kg8 29. Qd5+ Qf7 30. Rxe8 Rxe8 31. Kxh2 $16) 29. Rxe8 {, but then he noticed that after} Bd6 30. Rxf8+ Qxf8 {the f2-pawn was hanging, preventing the rook's activation. And yet, the calm} 31. Kg1 $1 $16 {would have solved this problem, leaving White's attacking chances and small material advantage intact.}) 28... hxg6 $2 ({GM Mihail Marin: 'Being in severe time trouble and deeply worried about 28.Bxf6+, Vasiukov instantly took back.'} 28... Rxe1+ $5 29. Rxe1 hxg6 30. Kxh2 Bxc4 $14 {GM Mihail Marin: 'would have most likely led to a draw.'}) 29. Bxf6+ $1 Kg8 30. Rxe8 Rxe8 31. Kxh2 Bxc4 32. Rd7 $1 $16 {GM Mihail Marin: 'White has an improved version of the endgame from the comment to 27.Rfe1 and Tal confidently converted his advantage.'} Re6 33. Bc3 Bxa2 34. Rxa7 Bc4 35. Kg3 Bd5 36. f3 Kf8 37. Bd4 b5 38. Kf4 Bc4 39. Kg5 Ke8 40. Ra8+ Kf7 41. Ra7+ Ke8 42. b4 Bd5 (42... Bf1 43. g4 Be2 44. f4 $16) 43. Ra3 $1 Kf7 44. g4 Re2 45. Bc5 Re5+ 46. Kh6 Re6 (46... g5 47. Bd4 $18) 47. Rd3 Bc6 48. Rd8 Re8 49. Rd4 Re6 50. f4 Ke8 51. Kg7 $1 Be4 52. Bb6 $1 $18 Bf3 (52... Rxb6 53. Rxe4+ Kd7 54. f5 $18) 53. Rd8+ Ke7 54. Rd3 Be2 55. Bd8+ Ke8 56. Rd2 Re3 (56... Bxg4 57. Bg5) 57. Bg5 Bd3 $8 58. f5 {GM Mihail Marin: 'This game proves Tal's belief in the logical character of the game. If he felt that he had done everything right until the critical moment and if there was no way to improve the position by normal means, he would switch to tactical mode even when he could not calculate everything. The only problem (quite symptomatical in his case) is that one's inner wishes may make one subjective when planning and evaluating. He believed in the tight connection between strategy and tactics, but due to his temperament and artistic inclination could not always maintain the balance between them. But at the same time, he knew that his unparalleled imagination and tactical ability would serve him well after initiating the tactical operation, even when this would be not entirely sound. Tal surely loved sacrificing material for the initiative or an attack, and he often conducted the preparatory strategic phase precisely with this aim in mind. Sometimes he would also sacrifice prematurely, even when his inner voice would tell him not to do it. Asking Tal not to sacrifice would be less effective than prohibiting the wind to blow.'} (58. f5 Rg3 59. f6 Bc4 60. f7+ Bxf7 61. Rd8#) 1-0

