{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'What happens when a genius who attacks at any cost, meets a genius who is willing to suffer any indignity for material? Strangely enough, Tal was a regular customer of Korchnoi's, and accrued an abysmal lifetime score against him, mainly through games like this, where Tal's normally magical attacks, which worked against everyone else, just grew ill, coughed and died against his Laskerian opponent. Furthermore, Tal's tactics never seemed to work either on the computer-like number-crunching Korchnoi, who actually once had the colossal nerve to call Tal a weak calculator! '} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's favourite move in this position.'} ({His style isn't suited to the Karpovian} 5. exd5 exd5 {(Korchnoi's favourite response), when Black gets freedom, in exchange for a future isolated d-pawn.}) 5... cxd4 (5... dxe4 6. Nxe4 Bd7 7. Bg5 $1 Qa5+ 8. Nc3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bb4 10. O-O Bxc3 11. bxc3 Qxc3 $2 {(only Korchnoi could fall behind in development, and go pawn grabbing against Tal and get away with it). EXERCISE (combination alert): After Tal's next shot, his attack grew out of control. Can you find it? ANSWER: Line opening sac:} 12. Nf5 $3 exf5 13. Re1+ Be6 14. Qd6 $1 a6 15. Bd2 $1 {(the deep point behind the sac – Tal transfers his bishop to the a3-f8 diagonal with deadly effect)} Qxc2 16. Bb4 $1 axb5 17. Qf8+ Kd7 18. Red1+ Kc7 19. Qxa8 {1-0 M.Tal-W.Uhlmann, Moscow 1971.}) ({Tal had greater success against other moves:} 5... a6 6. exd5 axb5 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Qe2 Nf6 10. Nb3 Bb4+ 11. c3 Bd6 (11... Be7 {prevents future Nf5 tricks}) 12. Nbd4 Qc7 13. Nf5 Bf4 14. Bxf4 Qxf4 15. N5d4 Bd7 $6 ({Houdini suggests} 15... Ba6 $5 {and if} 16. Nxc6 {then} b4 17. Qe5 Qxe5+ 18. Nfxe5 bxc3 19. bxc3 Ne4 {, when Black may have enough compensation for the pawn}) 16. Ne5 {and Tal managed to impose a dark square bind, M.Tal-R.Debarnot, Las Palmas 1975.}) 6. Nxd4 Bd7 7. Nxc6 $6 (7. Bxc6 {!?} bxc6 8. c4 $14) 7... bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Shouldn't Black recapture with his bishop, following the principle: The cramped side should seek exchanges. ANSWER: Very true, but by recapturing with the pawn, Black gains something else: A tempo – which is also a valuable commodity when you lag behind in development. Actually both moves are playable.'} ({For example:} 7... Bxc6 {?} 8. Bxc6+ {!} bxc6 9. c4 Bd6 (9... Nf6 10. Qa4 Qd7 11. e5 $16) 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. exd5 exd5 12. O-O Ne7 13. Nf3 O-O 14. Qd3 Qd7 15. Rd1 Rfd8 16. Be3 {with just a tiny edge to White, who can play on the d4-square and Black's isolani, V.Topalov-G.Kamsky, Sofia 2009.}) 8. Bd3 Qc7 (8... e5 9. O-O Bd6 10. c4 d4 11. f4 Nf6 12. h3 Qc7 13. Nf3 $16) 9. Qe2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'intends to meet'} Ne7 ({In a later game Korchnoi played} 9... Bd6 10. Nf3 dxe4 11. Bxe4 (11. Qxe4 Nf6 12. Qh4 Qa5+ 13. Bd2 Qh5 14. Qxh5 Nxh5 15. O-O-O $16 {Adorjan - Webb, London, 1975}) 11... Nf6 12. O-O Rb8 13. Re1 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 f6 15. b3 O-O 16. Bd2 c5 17. Rad1 Rf7 18. h3 Bf8 19. Bf4 e5 20. Nxe5 fxe5 21. Bxe5 Qc8 22. Bxb8 Qxb8 {, when the bishop pair allowed Black to face the future with confidence, J.Timman-V.Korchnoi, London 2009.}) (9... Nf6 $6 {with} 10. e5 {, embarrassing the knight.}) 10. Nf3 Ng6 11. e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The e-pawn crosses into Black's territory. White seeds his ambitions upon a future kingside attack, while Black relies on his strong centre and open b-file to generate counter-chances.' ?!} (11. O-O Be7 (11... Bd6 12. Re1 Nf4 13. Bxf4 Bxf4 14. c4 O-O 15. g3 Bh6 16. e5 $14) 12. c4 dxe4 13. Bxe4 f5 14. Bc2 e5 15. Ng5 h6 16. Qh5 Qd6 17. Nf3 $16 {!}) 11... Rb8 12. O-O (12. c4 {!} Bb4+ 13. Kf1 {'with the idea' h4, Rh3}) 12... Be7 13. Re1 c5 14. c4 ({More natural than the passive} 14. c3 O-O 15. b3 f5 $1 {, when Black already looked better, J.De Mondragon-D.Sibbett, correspondence 1974.}) 14... O-O $11 {=} 15. h4 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Here he comes now. The players engage in radically divergent hopes and opposing primal energies soon collide. Tal begins the process of probing Black's defensive barrier.'} (15. b3 { d2, ad1=  d2, ad1=  Bd2, Rad1=}) ({QUESTION: Can White generate anything from} 15. Ng5 {- ? ANSWER: It looks like a waste of tempi. After} dxc4 $1 {(Principle: Meet a wing attack with a central counter)} 16. Bxc4 ({or} 16. Bxg6 hxg6 17. Qxc4 Rb4 18. Qc3 Bc6 {and Black looks better with the bishop pair and light square power}) 16... h6 17. Nf3 Bc6 {, maybe it's stylistic preference but I prefer Black and have little fear of White's kingside attacking chances.}) 15... dxc4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The same principle applies, as in the previous note.'} ({Of course the h-pawn isn't really hanging:} 15... Nxh4 $4 16. Nxh4 Bxh4 17. Qh5 {and Black can resign.}) 16. Be4 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not'} 16. Bxc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} Bc6 (16... Bxh4 $1 {, since the h-pawn really does hang with White's bishop off the b1-h7 diagonal.}) 17. h5 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 Nxe5 $17) (16. Bxg6 hxg6 17. Qxc4 Rb4 $15) 16... Kh8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Black threatens to take on e5.'} 17. Bg5 Bb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Securing c4 for the moment, while also threatening ...c4-c3 tricks.' '' !} 18. Qe3 Rfd8 19. h5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's attackers surge forward, all doing their own thing, but all with similar destinations – which merge into "That way!" as they point to Black's king in unison.'} Bxg5 20. Nxg5 ({After} 20. Qxg5 h6 $1 {(before White can play this)} 21. Qg3 Ne7 {, White's attack grows cold.}) 20... Nf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What was wrong with taking on e5?'} ({ANSWER: It would be inviting an early and sudden death to allow Tal a line like} 20... Nxe5 $6 21. Qf4 (21. Bxh7 Ng4 {!} 22. Qh3 Nxf2 $19) 21... Rd7 22. h6 $1 {with clear attacking chances for White.}) 21. Rad1 ({Now if} 21. h6 {, then} gxh6 22. Nf3 Kg7 $1 23. Qf4 Ng6 24. Qf6+ Kg8 {and Black threatens to remove queens from the board with ...Qe7 next.}) 21... h6 22. Nf3 Nd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black already stands better. He threatens the e5-pawn once again and prepares a defensive cheapo, into which Tal falls.'} 23. Qf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's coming woes are squarely attributable to this move.'} (23. Qc3 {would keep his disadvantage to a minimum.}) {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A dark malignance, seemingly relentless and indestructible, pursues Black's king with a vengeance. But Tal's last move was an error, adversely mutating the attacking matrix, after which his attack sags and sprawls in ungainly fashion. EXERCISE (planning): Korchnoi has a game-changing possibility which foils Tal's exertions by severing White's link to the attack? What is it? ANSWER: Pin – taking advantage of the fact that White can't capture en passant. '} 23... f5 $1 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi secures his perimeter, locking out unwanted intruders. For a sniper, patience is a crucial asset. Sooner or later, the target enters the crosshairs. The common notion that Tal out-tactic-ed his opposition 100% of the time is a fallacy.'} 24. Bc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop comes to the sad realization that he is empty of his previous blessings.'} Nf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal encounters unexpected truculence from the defenders, who no longer believe they are on the defence. I believe it was Shakespeare who warned of the ineffectuality of a twice-told tale, but in this case it just isn't so. Korchnoi works the same trick twice, exploiting the pin on the e5-pawn for a second time. Black's knight scythes through the illusionary barrier, attacking h5 and threatening to make itself comfortable on d5, while the f5-pawn secures his king, the way a car's bumper wards off accidental injury. '} 25. Rd6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal continues along, still under the sway of his now unprovable hypothesis.'} Nd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi knows when to grab and when to refrain.'} ({The hasty} 25... Nxh5 $2 26. Qh4 Be8 27. Rxe6 {allows the advantage to swing to White's side.}) 26. Qg3 Rb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hoping to eliminate White's only active piece.'} 27. Rxd8+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Just like that, White is busted. This stopgap measure fails to address the core issue at hand. Tal should have retained his rook on d6. Then if Black takes it, the-square e5 opens for White's knight. '} Qxd8 28. Rd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): How did Korchnoi coax Tal into handing over another pawn? ANSWER: Double attack b2 and h5. '} Be8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The rearguard scrambles in a poisonous retreat.'} 29. b3 cxb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I don't see any reason to activate White's bishop again.'} (29... c3 {was better.}) 30. Bxb3 Bxh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi continues to slurp up pawns, which to him taste like wet noodles in a bowl of Udon soup.'} 31. Rc1 Rb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Another powerful shot. A second squadron enters the fight.'} 32. Bc4 ({After} 32. Rxc5 $2 Nf4 $1 {(threatening a monster fork on e2)} 33. Rc2 (33. Kh2 Ne2 34. Qh3 Bxf3 $19) 33... Re4 $1 34. Kh2 Bxf3 35. gxf3 ({not} 35. Qxf3 $2 Qh4+ 36. Kg1 Re1#) 35... Rd4 {, Black's rook and knight dominate and White can comfortably resign.}) 32... Bxf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 33. Qxf3 Qg5 34. Qd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '"Be silent!" rages White's retreating queen to the taunting voices in her head, as the foraging expedition returns empty handed, tired and hungry. It's hard to shake off the feeling of inevitability in Black's play. Isn't it disorienting to watch Korchnoi strategically and tactically manhandle a legendary player such as Tal? '} Qf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The powerful rarely negotiate when they are in a position simply to reach out and take. The e5-pawn is destined to fall, and the queen emerges with the exultation of one who understands the degree of her true strength.'} 35. Bxd5 (35. Bd3 Rd4) 35... Rd4 (35... exd5 {?} 36. Rxc5) 36. Qc2 Rxd5 37. Re1 Rxe5 38. Rxe5 Qxe5 39. Qa4 Qe1+ 40. Kh2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Like this:'} Qxf2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: I realize that, at the top level, White's position is hopeless. But if I had Black, I wouldn't know exactly how to win the queen ending. How can Black push his c-pawn without walking into perpetual check?'} 41. Qe8+ Kh7 42. Qxe6 Qh4+ 43. Kg1 Qe4 $1 {(the perfect location for Black's queen, where it prevents all perpetual check attempts, while preparing to shepherd the c-pawn forward)} 44. Qa6 c4 $1 {(Principle: In queen endings, the number of pawns can be meaningless; all that matters is who promotes to a new queen first – here a7 isn't worth the bother to defend, since Black's c-pawn is way ahead in what is essentially a non-race)} 45. Qxa7 c3 46. Qc5 c2 47. Kh2 Qf4+ 48. g3 c1=Q {and wins. 0-1 (48) Tal,M-Kortschnoj,V Moscow 12/216 1971 CI1-130 [Kotov,Alexander]} 0-1

