1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Bg5 c6 5. Qd2 b5 6. Bd3 Bg7 7. Nf3 {Tal's setup features a mixture of positional and aggressive elements. Gufeld's reaction strongly neglects development, allowing White to take over the initiative.} h6 $6 (7... Bg4) (7... O-O) 8. Be3 Ng4 $6 9. Bf4 e5 {There is no way back already.} 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. Bg3 {With his central pawn hanging, Black has to opt for concrete play in a moment when his queenside is completely undeveloped.} h5 12. h3 h4 13. hxg4 hxg3 14. Rxh8+ Bxh8 15. Qh6 gxf2+ {After this forced sequence, all Black's pieces are passive (and some of them exposed) on the back rank. His main pride is the far advanced pawn, facing White with a difficult choice.} 16. Ke2 $5 {The most natural move, leaving the first rank available for the rook.} (16. Kxf2 $2 {should certainly be avoided, as in some lines this would offer Black a saving tempo after ...Qb6+. Let us compare this with similar lines examined below:} Bf6 17. g5 Be7 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxe5+ Be6 20. Nxb5 $2 ({The other combination does not work either:} 20. Nd5 $2 cxd5 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 22. exd5 Qb6+ 23. Nd4 O-O-O $1 $19) 20... cxb5 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 22. Rd1 Qb6+ 23. Kf1 O-O-O $19) ({But it appears that it would have been better to avoid placing the king on the e-file:} 16. Kf1 $1 Bf6 (16... Qf6 {allows} 17. Bxb5 {The bishop is taboo in view of Nd5, leaving Black hanging, while} Bg7 {is met with} 18. Nd5 $1 $16 {anyway.}) 17. g5 Be7 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxe5+ Qe7 20. Qf4 Bg7 21. e5 Nd7 22. e6 $1 {This is effective because the e-pawn is not pinned.} Qxe6 23. Kxf2 $18) 16... Bf6 (16... Qf6 {justifies the decision regarding the king's placement due to} 17. Rh1 $1 ({With the king on e2,} 17. Bxb5 Bg7 18. Nd5 {does not work anymore due to} Qxf3+ $1 {with check!}) 17... Bg7 18. g5 Bxh6 19. gxf6 Bf8 20. Nxe5 $16 {, with a crushing domination and soon an extra pawn.}) 17. g5 Be7 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxe5+ Be6 $2 {Gufeld fails to use his chance.} (19... Qe7 $1 {works much better now than in the similar line above:} 20. Qf4 (20. Qxe7+ Kxe7 21. Kxf2 Bg7 {Planning ...Nd7-e5, with reasonable compensation for the pawn.} 22. e5 Bg4 {offers Black reasonable play.}) 20... Bg7 21. Rh1 (21. e5 Nd7 22. e6 {runs into} Nc5 $1) 21... Nd7 22. Rh7 Kf8 {, and Black is out of immediate danger.}) 20. Nxb5 $5 {Thematic, but not the most effective.} (20. Nd5 $1 $16 {would have been an improved version of the game. The difference is that after} cxd5 $2 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 {White could insert his e-pawn into the attack with} 22. exd5 $18) 20... cxb5 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 22. Rd1 {Black is under a double pin and his position is critical. Some readers may remember the Morphy opera theatre combination with a similar pin system. But there still is hope for Black.} Qe7 $2 (22... Be7 $2 {allows White to use the first rank with} 23. Bxd7+ Bxd7 24. Rh1 $18) ({But Black could improve over this line with} 22... f1=Q+ $1 {, forcing White to obstruct the first rank with his king.} 23. Kxf1 Be7 24. Rd5 $1 {The only way to keep the pressure. Since Black cannot display too much activity at this stage, White uses the available time to control the fifth rank.} (24. Qh8+ Bf8 25. Bxd7+ Bxd7 {does not offer much, as if} 26. Ne5 $2 Bb5+ $1 {wins. The point is that the rook is hanging, so that} 27. c4 {does not work.}) 24... Rb8 {There is little else Black could do.} 25. Bxd7+ Bxd7 26. Qh8+ Bf8 27. Ne5 Rb7 (27... Bb5+ $2 28. c4 $18) 28. Ng4 {White has excellent compensation for the piece  (three pawns and attacking chances), but things are not entirely clear yet.} Qc8 29. Nf6+ Ke7 30. Nh7 {Threatening 31.Qe5+ Be6 32.Qd6+.} Be6 31. Nxf8 Rb8 $3 {The only defence against the threat Qf6+, which can now be met with ...Kxf8..} (31... Qxf8 $2 32. Qf6+ Ke8 33. Rd8#) (31... Bxd5 $2 32. Nxg6+ $1 {wins the queen.}) 32. Nxg6+ fxg6 33. Qf6+ Ke8 34. Qxg6+ {Black's position remains dangerous but by no means lost.}) 23. Rxd7 $1 {This retrieves the sacrificed material with interest.} Bxd7 24. Bxd7+ Kxd7 (24... Kd8 {just transposes after} 25. Qa5+ Kxd7 26. Qd5+) 25. Qd5+ Kc7 26. Qxa8 $18 {White has won several pawns and his king is relatively safe.} Qc5 27. c3 Qc4+ 28. Kxf2 Bc5+ 29. Kg3 Qxa2 30. Qd5 Qxd5 31. exd5 Kd6 32. c4 Be3 33. b4 a6 34. Kg4 Bc1 35. Ne1 Be3 36. Nd3 Bd2 37. g3 Kc7 38. c5 Kd7 39. Ne5+ Kc8 40. d6 Bxb4 41. d7+ Kc7 42. Nc6 Kxd7 43. Nxb4 a5 44. Nd3 1-0

