{Mikhail Tal: 'The following game was played in the preliminaries of the 20th Olympiad, when the points situation was relatively unimportant. The result of each game did not play a decisive role, and therefore the game was less influenced by 'distracting' factors. This probably explains, to a large degree, why my game with O. Jakobsen turned out better, in my opinion, than any other game. '} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Qxd4 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'My opponent made his first three moves suspiciously quickly, and so I decided not to venture into a theoretical dispute, preferring the relatively unusual (though fairly popular in recent times) capture with the queen. '} Bd7 5. c4 Nc6 6. Qd2 Nf6 ({In a number of games at the Olympiad, Black carried out here the curious manoeuvre} 6... g6 7. Nc3 Bh6 $5 8. Qd1 Bg7 {thus preventing the fianchetto development of White's queen's bishop.}) 7. Nc3 g6 8. b3 Bg7 9. Bb2 O-O 10. Be2 ({More accurate, perhaps, was} 10. h3 {, preventing Black's ...Bg4.}) 10... Qa5 11. Rb1 {Mikhail Tal: 'The only satisfactory defence against the threat of 11...Nxe4.'} (11. O-O $2 Nxe4 $1 $19) 11... a6 12. a3 ({Mikhail Tal: 'Black would have no difficulties after'} 12. O-O $6 b5 $1 $15 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'but now, in answer to 12...b5,'} 13. b4 {is unpleasant, and on} Qb6 {perhaps even} 14. c5 {(so that if} dxc5 {, then} 15. e5 {).}) 12... Rfc8 {Mikhail Tal: 'In answer to the advance of White's b-pawn Black intends to retreat his queen to d8, when both his rooks can take an active part in play on the queenside. '} 13. O-O ({Mikhail Tal: 'Here ag ain'} 13. h3 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'would have given White good chances of keeping some advantage.'}) 13... Bg4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'It is to Black's advantage to exchange off the white knight. In the first place, this knight has a strong influence on the centre, and secondly, it is easier to advance ...b5 with the white bishop off the f1-a6 diagonal. '} 14. Rfe1 (14. Qe3 Qc5 $11) 14... Bxf3 15. Bxf3 Rab8 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black's wish to carry out his queenside advance 'with every convenience' leads him into great difficulties, since now it has to be deferred for some time.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'In answer to'} 15... b5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'my opponent was evidently unhappy about the reply'} 16. e5 $5 {Mikhail Tal: '(which is what I would have played). However, in this case Black has a satisfactory game after'} (16. b4 $6 Qb6 17. cxb5 axb5 18. Be2 Ne5 $1 $15) 16... dxe5 ({the exchange sacrifice} 16... Nxe5 $5 {is also not without interest}) 17. Bxc6 Rxc6 18. Rxe5 $13 ({weaker is} 18. cxb5 axb5 19. Rxe5 Ng4 $1 20. Rxb5 Qc7) 18... Qc7 $1 19. Qe2 e6 {and the sacrifice of two pieces for a rook,} 20. cxb5 Rxc3 21. b6 Qc6 22. Bxc3 Qxc3 {, is rather double-edged.}) 16. Be2 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Naturally.'} Ne5 (16... b5 {leads to the loss of a pawn after} 17. b4 {.}) 17. f4 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Despite the apparent weakening of the f2-square, this move is perfectly playable, and in addition gives White the advantage.'} Qb6+ (17... Nfg4 18. b4 $2 (18. Bxg4 $14) 18... Nxc4 $3 (18... Qb6+ $2 19. c5 $1 Rxc5 20. Na4 $1 $18) 19. bxa5 Nxd2 20. Bxg4 Nxb1 21. Bxc8 Rxc8 22. Rxb1 Bxc3 23. Rc1 Bd4+ $19) (17... Ned7 $5) 18. Kh1 Neg4 19. Bxg4 Nxg4 20. h3 (20. Nd5 Nf2+ 21. Kg1 Nh3+ $11) 20... Nf6 {Mikhail Tal: 'An admission of the fact that there was an error in his previous calculations.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'After the game Jakobsen said that he had earlier been counting on'} 20... Nf2+ 21. Kh2 {Mikhail Tal: '. The following variation leads to a difficult rook ending for Black:'} Qd4 (21... Qxb3 $2 {Mikhail Tal: 'when'} {Mikhail Tal: 'but White has a forced win by'} 22. Nd5 $1 $18 (22. Qxf2 Bxc3 23. Re3 Bd4 $1 {is bad for White.})) (21... Z0) 22. Qxd4 Bxd4 23. Nd5 $1 Bxb2 24. Nxe7+ Kf8 25. Nxc8 Rxc8 26. Rxb2 Nd3 27. Rbe2 (27. Reb1 {.}) 27... Nxe1 28. Rxe1 $16) (20... Qf2 $5 21. Re2 Qh4 $17) 21. Nd5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Here White stood at the parting of the ways.'} (21. e5 {Mikhail Tal: 'was very tempting, but I rejected it since after'} dxe5 22. fxe5 Rd8 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'the white queen has no really good move. In particular, in answer to'} 23. Qg5 {Mikhail Tal: 'the piece sacrifice'} Qxb3 $5 $13 {Mikhail Tal: 'is possible. White settled for a quieter continuation, which, so he thought, should give him an irresistible attack. Things turned out to be not so simple, however.'}) 21... Nxd5 22. exd5 ({Mikhail Tal: 'The plausible intermediate move'} 22. Bxg7 $6 {Mikhail Tal: 'would have been an instructive mistake in view of'} Nxf4 $1 23. Bd4 Qc7 {Mikhail Tal: '('} 24. Qxf4 e5 $11 {Mikhail Tal: ').'}) 22... Bxb2 23. Rxb2 Qc7 24. f5 $16 {Mikhail Tal: 'This was the position that White had in mind when making his 21st move. With the queen able to move along the c1-h6 diagonal, and the rook along the second rank, things look hopeless for Black. Jakobsen cleverly finds some counterplay. '} b5 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Better late than never!'} 25. fxg6 ({Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 25. Qg5 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'immediately I didn't see how to give mate if Black continued'} f6 {Mikhail Tal: 'or'} 26. Qh6 (26. Qg4 bxc4 {.}) 26... g5) 25... hxg6 26. Qg5 bxc4 $1 (26... Re8 27. Rbe2 Rb7 28. Re6 $1 Kg7 29. Rxg6+ fxg6 30. Re6 $18) ({Now passive defence quickly leads Black to defeat, for example} 26... Rb7 27. Rbe2) 27. Rxe7 Qc5 {Mikhail Tal: '!'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'White would have only minor technical difficulties to overcome after'} 27... c3 28. Rf2 ({but not, of course,} 28. Rxc7 $4 cxb2) 28... c2 29. Rxc7 Rxc7 30. Qc1 {.}) {Mikhail Tal: 'After lengthy reflection I played ... '} 28. Qf6 ({Mikhail Tal: 'It was here that I realised, somewhat to my surprise, that Black's counterplay had become quite serious. In my preliminary calculations I had been planning'} 28. b4 c3 $1 29. Rbe2 Qd4 $1 30. Rxf7 Kxf7 31. Re7+ Kf8 32. Rh7 {Mikhail Tal: ', but at the last moment noticed that Black has the counter-sacrifice'} Qg7 $3 $11 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'which forces a draw. '}) 28... Qxd5 {Mikhail Tal: 'The only move.'} (28... Rf8 {Mikhail Tal: 'lost immediately to'} 29. Re4 $1 $18 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 29. bxc4 Qxc4 ({Mikhail Tal: 'Besides this move, I also had to consider'} 29... Qf5 $5 {Mikhail Tal: '. Time and again White worked through the variations of the complicated ending resulting after'} 30. Rf2 $1 (30. Rxb8 Qxf6 (30... Rxb8 31. Rxf7 $18 {Mikhail Tal: '!'}) 31. Rxc8+ Kg7 32. Rcc7 Kh6 $1 (32... Qf1+ 33. Kh2 Qf4+ 34. Kg1 Kh6 $1 35. Rxf7) 33. Rxf7 Qa1+ 34. Kh2 Qe5+ 35. Kg1 Qe3+ 36. Rf2 Qxa3 37. Kh2 $16) 30... Qxf6 31. Rxf6 $18) 30. Rf2 Rf8 {Mikhail Tal: 'Unfortunately, Black cannot defend f7 in a more active way.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'The point is that in answer to'} 30... Rc7 {Mikhail Tal: ','} 31. Rc2 $1 $18 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'is immediately decisive (with the black rook on c8 nothing was gained by this move, since the queen simply moves away).'}) 31. Rf4 $1 Qc1+ 32. Kh2 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black's counter-attack along the eighth rank may appear serious, but a closer examination shows that this is not so. Jakobsen came to the same conclusion, since he now played ... '} Qb2 (32... Rb1 33. Rg4 $1 Qh6 (33... Qg1+ 34. Kg3 Rb3+ 35. Kh4 $18 Qb1 36. Rxf7 $1 Rxf7 ({nothing is gained by} 36... Qe1+ 37. g3) 37. Rxg6+ Qxg6 38. Qxg6+ Kf8 39. Qxd6+ Kg8 {and} 40. Qxa6 {if there is nothing better.}) (33... Qc2 {loses immediately to} 34. Rxf7 $1 Rxf7 35. Rxg6+ Qxg6 36. Qxg6+) 34. Rxf7 $1 Rxf7 35. Rxg6+ $18) 33. Rxf7 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Once again a rook ending is reached.'} Qxf6 34. R7xf6 Rxf6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black cannot maintain material equality.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'It is not difficult to establish that the pawn ending after'} 34... Kg7 35. Rxf8 Rxf8 36. Rxf8 Kxf8 {Mikhail Tal: 'is hopeless for him.'} 37. Kg3 $18) 35. Rxf6 Kg7 (35... Rd8 36. Rxg6+ Kf7 37. Rg4 {also offers no hope of saving the game.}) 36. Rxd6 a5 37. Rd5 Ra8 38. a4 $1 $18 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'The final point. White restricts the mobility of the enemy rook, whereupon the outcome of the game is settled by the intrusion of his king. Within a few moves Black will be forced to give up a second pawn. The concluding phase of the game is easily understood. '} Ra6 39. Kg3 Kf6 (39... Kh6 $5) 40. Kf4 Ke6 41. Rb5 Kf6 42. Rc5 Rb6 43. Rxa5 Rb4+ 44. Kg3 {Mikhail Tal: '. Here the game was adjourned, but after a brief joint analysis, Black resigned. Jakobsen's sealed move was 44...Rb3+.'} Rb3+ 1-0

