1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O d6 6. c3 Bg4 7. Re1 Be7 8. h3 Bh5 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. d4 exd4 11. cxd4 O-O 12. Nc3 {To my taste, this is the most constructive move, developing the knight on an active square and leaving the c1-bishop's diagonal clear. But Portisch thinks this line is not dangerous as White has no pressure on the c-file. One way or another, Black has excellent equalizing chances in both cases, only that in this game the process is subtle and gradual while in the next one it has a forced character. This still speaks for the merits of 12.c3.} Re8 13. Bf4 ({It is not so easy to free the queen from the bishop's pressure:} 13. Qd3 Bg6 14. Qf1 h6 15. Bf4 Bf8 (15... Qd7 16. Rad1 Bf8 {Dany,A-Brodt,D Speyer 1997 (1-0, 24)} 17. Ne5 $1 $16) 16. Nd2 d5 17. e5 Nd7 18. Nf3 (18. Nb3 a5 19. Na4 Qb8 20. Bd2 Qb5 $11) 18... Bb4 19. Rac1 (19. a3 Bxc3 20. bxc3 c5 $132) 19... Nf8 {[%cal Gf8e6]}) 13... Qd7 14. Rc1 h6 15. Qd3 ({Another possible regrouping is} 15. Re3 Bf8 16. Qe2 a5 17. b3 g5 $5 {Taking advantage of the fact that the returning diagonal is obstructed, making a later h3-h4 inoffensive.} 18. Bg3 Bg7 19. Re1 Re6 20. Qd3 Rae8 21. Nd2 Bg6 $13) 15... Bf8 16. e5 (16. d5 cxd5 17. exd5 Rxe1+ 18. Rxe1 c5 19. dxc6 Qxc6 20. Nd4 Qb6 21. Qd2 Bg6 $11) 16... Nd5 17. Bg3 (17. Nxd5 cxd5 18. b3 a5 19. Qc3 a4 20. g4 Bg6 21. Qxc7 Qxc7 22. Rxc7 axb3 23. axb3 Be4 24. Nd2 dxe5 25. dxe5 Bb4 26. Rd1 Bxd2 $11 27. Rxd2 $2 {[%csl Ge5]} Ra1+ 28. Kh2 Rh1+ 29. Kg3 Rg1+ 30. Kh4 g5+) 17... Nb4 18. Qe3 Nd5 (18... Qf5 $132) (18... Bg6 $132) 19. Qe4 Rab8 20. Nxd5 cxd5 21. Qxd5 Rxb2 22. e6 (22. exd6 Bxf3 23. Qxf3 Rxe1+ 24. Rxe1 Bxd6 $11) 22... Qb5 23. Rc5 dxc5 24. Qxh5 Rxe6 (24... fxe6 25. Qg6 (25. Ne5 Re7 $17) 25... Re7 $17) 25. Rxe6 fxe6 26. Ne5 Bd6 27. Qf7+ Kh7 28. Qg6+ Kg8 29. Qxe6+ Kh7 30. Qg6+ Kg8 31. Qf7+ Kh7 32. dxc5 Qxc5 33. Nd7 Qd4 34. Bxd6 Qxd6 35. f4 Rb4 $2 (35... Rb1+ 36. Kh2 Rd1 37. Nf8+ Kh8 38. Ne6 Qd7 39. Qf8+ Kh7 40. Qf5+ g6 $2 41. Nf8+ Kg7 42. Qxd7+) 36. Nf8+ Kh8 37. Ne6 1-0

