{Tal frequently displayed his feeling for the advance in development and initiative until deep in the endgame. Even though this usually goes without any sparkling combinations, it certainly has high instructional and aesthetic value. Here is a typical game played two years before he passed away, against a much younger (and not by much less dynamic) opponent.} 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. d4 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. dxe5 {This is supposed to be an ultra-safe continuation, depriving Black of any early King's Indian counterplay. But as we will see, Tal did not play it to reach a quick draw.} dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Bg5 c6 10. Nxe5 Re8 {Due to White's hanging centre and the weaknesses on the dark squares, Black is supposed to have enough compensation for the pawn. With his next moves, Tal will focus on developing and activating his pieces instead of looking for a way to retreat with the knight or defend it.} 11. O-O-O $1 Na6 ({The knight is taboo:} 11... Rxe5 $2 12. Rd8+ Re8 ({Other moves are even worse:} 12... Ne8 13. Rxc8 $18 {, and Black will be underdeveloped forever.}) (12... Bf8 13. Bxf6 {wins material.}) 13. Bxf6 $1 Rxd8 14. Bxd8 $16 {White got rid of his hanging knight and exchanged Black's most active piece while retaining his material advantage.}) 12. Rd6 $1 {Same policy.} Rxe5 $6 {An unnoticeable inaccuracy. Black lets himself get carried away by the plan of retrieving the pawn, thus neglecting development.} ({The most consistent move is} 12... Be6 {, but then Black would have to rely on long-term compensation for the pawn.}) 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Rxf6 Nc5 {This is the position Black was aiming for. White does not have an adequate way to defend his extra pawn.} ({With the rook on e5} 14... Be6 {is not recommendable:} 15. f4 Rc5 16. a3 $3 $16 {White maintains his material advantage, as ...Kg7 is easily parried with e4-e5 while} Bxc4 17. b4 {wins an exchange.}) 15. Rd6 $3 {A fantastic way of using the available tempo. The rook returns to its main trajectory.} (15. Rf4 Be6 {leaves the rook misplaced.}) (15. Rf3 {Indirectly defends the pawn, but White cannot keep the material advantage for too long:} Bg4 (15... Nxe4 $2 16. Re3 Bf5 17. f3 Nxc3 18. Rxe5 Nxa2+ 19. Kd2 $16) 16. Re3 Bxe2 17. Rxe2 Nd3+ 18. Kc2 Nf4 {followed by ...Nxg2.}) (15. f4 $6 {allows} Rxe4 {, as if} 16. Nxe4 $2 Nxe4 {wins the rook.}) (15. f3 Be6 {, threatening ...Kg7} 16. f4 {is similar, even though less bad for White:} Rxe4 17. Nxe4 Nxe4 18. Rxe6 fxe6 19. Rd1 Rf8 20. g3 e5 {, with excellent counterplay.}) 15... Nxe4 16. Rd8+ Kg7 17. Nxe4 Rxe4 {White has used the time spent by Black on retrieving the pawn to delay Black's queenside development. But with so few pieces on the board and with the bishop on e2 and the pawn on c4 hanging, it may seem that nothing can prevent Black from ... b7-b6 followed by ...Bb7, with just a symbolic white advantage.} 18. Bf3 $3 {For the second time in the game Tal shows that to him placing his pieces on the right trajectories is more important than material. His last move sacrifices a pawn and a tempo!} ({If} 18. Bd3 Re7 19. Rd1 b6 20. c5 Bb7 {Black should not have any problems holding the draw.}) 18... Rxc4+ {Black has to get some material compensation for his delay in development.} ({If} 18... Re7 19. Rhd1 h5 20. R1d6 $18 {he would be completely paralyzed, without any chance of ever completing his development.}) 19. Kd2 c5 {Black prepares both .. .Rd4+ and ...Rc2+, hoping to exchange White's more active rook.} (19... Rc2+ $2 {does not work yet:} 20. Kxc2 Bf5+ 21. Rd3 $18) ({The most natural move was} 19... b6 {, planning ...Bb7, but the hanging pawn on c6 would offer White the possibility of developing his initiative with a forcing manoeuvre:} 20. b3 Rf4 (20... Rc5 {allows the b-pawn to become dangerous:} 21. b4 Rc4 22. Re1 Bb7 23. Rxa8 Bxa8 24. Re8 Bb7 25. b5 {, with a very unpleasant pin.}) 21. Re1 Bb7 22. Rxa8 Bxa8 23. Re7 a5 24. Ke3 $1 {Before forcing the enemy rook back to the defence of the bishop, the king steps closer to the centre.} Rf6 25. Ra7 Re6+ 26. Kf4 Re8 27. Ra6 $16 {White has a clear advantage, as he retrieves the pawn, retaining the better structure and the more active pieces. The point is that} Rb8 $6 {allows} 28. Ke5 {, with a decisive intrusion.}) 20. Rc1 $5 ({There are some very tiny differences between this and the main alternative.} 20. Re1 Rc2+ 21. Kxc2 Bf5+ 22. Rd3 c4 {This was Black's idea.} 23. Bxb7 cxd3+ 24. Kc3 {The pawn on d3 is more of a weakness, but if we compare it with the line from the next comment, Black is a bit more active.}) 20... Rxc1 $6 {The decisive mistake in a difficult position.} ({After Black has invested a tempo to advance his c-pawn, he should have used it for his advantage:} 20... Rd4+ 21. Rxd4 cxd4 22. Rc7 {Tal considers this to be winning, but maybe this is slightly exaggerated.} Be6 23. b3 a5 24. Rxb7 $16 {The pawn on d4 does not count for much and White is more active. But "a clear advantage" is a better evaluation.}) 21. Kxc1 {The game is basically over. White's advance in development will last as long as he wishes. The only change will take place when he decides that everything is ready for winning the exchange by placing the bishop on the h3-c8 diagonal.} h5 22. h4 Rb8 23. Re8 {Preventing the king's activation.} (23. Kd2 Kf6 24. Re8 {was another possible move order.}) 23... f6 24. Bd5 {Same issue. White does not allow ...Kf7.} g5 25. g3 gxh4 26. gxh4 Kg6 27. Kd2 {Before winning the exchange, Tal intends to improve his position to the maximum.} b5 28. Ke3 b4 29. a3 bxa3 30. bxa3 a5 {Unfortunately the b3-square is not available.} 31. Kf4 a4 32. f3 Kg7 33. Be6 $1 {The useful moves have come to an end, so Tal cashes in.} Bxe6 34. Rxb8 Bb3 35. Rb5 1-0

