{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In a lifetime of crazy, if I had to pick Tal's craziest game, this one would be my choice.'} 1. Nf3 {Bulletin} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. d4 c6 5. Bg5 dxc4 6. e4 b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Thank God that covering this opening is outside the jurisdiction of this book. Keller's game plan: "Okay then, I'm playing Mikhail Tal in his invincible prime, in the year 1959, Anno Domini. My best shot may be to play the Botvinnik Semi-Slav, one of the most complicated openings in chess. He is certain to get confused!" Did Keller commit the Van Helsing/Harker error? --- Oh, you don't know what that is? Then let me explain. I remember watching Dracula: Prince of Darkness at age 8. Professor Van Helsing and Jonathan Harker were about to ram a wooden stake through Christopher Lee's dark heart and, just then, the sun went down, allowing the enraged, undead count to burst out of his coffin and start kicking butt with his super vampire strength. The obvious question arises: why did the professor and Mr. Harker attack so close to sunset, when they could have waited until the next morning, at 9:00 a.m., after a leisurely pancake breakfast at IHOP?'} 7. a4 Qb6 (7... Bb4 8. e5 h6 9. exf6 hxg5 10. fxg7) (7... b4 $6 8. Nb1 Be7 9. e5 Nd5 10. Bxe7 Nxe7 11. Bxc4 a5 12. Nbd2 {Tukmakov-Pinter/Szirak/1985/i40/522  e4 ×c5, ×d6}) (7... Bb7 8. axb5 cxb5 9. e5 (9. Nxb5 Bxe4 10. Bxc4 Bb4+ 11. Nc3 Nbd7 $10 {Ribli-Inkiov/Dubai ol/1986/i42/542}) 9... h6 10. Bd2 (10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Nxb5 Bb4+ 12. Nc3 fxe5 13. Nxe5 Nc6 {Osnos-Ivanov,S/UdSSR/1985/i41/485}) 10... Ne4 11. Nxb5 Nxd2 12. Qxd2 Bxf3 13. gxf3 Nc6 {Schtscherbakov-Novikov/Blagoveschtschensk/1988/i46/(559)}) 8. Bxf6 gxf6 9. Be2 a6 (9... Nd7 $6 10. O-O (10. d5 b4 (10... Bb4 11. dxc6 Qxc6 12. Nd4 Qxe4 13. O-O) 11. a5 Qc7 12. dxe6 fxe6 13. Na4 Ne5 14. Nxe5 Qxe5 15. O-O $14 {Vachidov-Galachov/UdSSR/1984  ECO D (2. Auflage) 44/6, Fußnote 28}) 10... Bb4 (10... Bb7 11. d5 (11. axb5 cxb5 12. b3 {Dolmatov} b4 13. Na4 Qc7 14. Bxc4 Bxe4 15. d5 $36) 11... cxd5 12. exd5 b4 13. a5 Qa6 14. dxe6 fxe6 15. Na4 Rg8 16. Qc2 O-O-O {Eingorn-Dolmatov/UdSSR/1985/i40/(522)}) 11. d5 Bxc3 12. bxc3 Nc5 13. Qd4 (13. dxc6 $142 $14 {Clarke/Bouwmeester/Withuis} Nxe4 (13... Nxa4 14. Rxa4 bxa4 15. Qd6 {Clarke} Qd8 (15... Ba6 $2 16. Qd7+ Kf8 17. Ne5 fxe5 18. Bh5 Kg7 19. Qxf7+ Kh6 20. Qf6+ Kxh5 21. h3 Rhg8 22. g4+ Rxg4+ 23. hxg4+ Kxg4 24. Kh1 $18 {Körholz}) 16. Qg3 Kf8 17. Rd1 Qe7 $13) 14. Qc1 {! Clarke} (14. Qc2 {c1.} f5 15. axb5 Qxb5 16. Nd4 $14) 14... e5 $13) 13... e5 {Slepuchov-Bronstein/Moskau/1946/ 14.e5 b3}) (9... Bb7 10. O-O a6 {Siehe Partie} (10... Bg7 {Clarke} 11. axb5 cxb5 12. b3 b4 (12... cxb3 13. Bxb5+ { 14.b3 }) 13. Na4 Qc7 14. Bxc4 Bxe4 15. d5 $36) (10... Bb4 $16 { 10... d7,0-0, 0-0-0})) 10. O-O Bb7 {Siehe Partie} (10... Nd7 11. d5 Rb8 12. dxc6 (12. Nd4 Ne5 13. dxe6 fxe6 14. Bh5+ Ng6 { 15... c5}) 12... Qxc6 13. axb5 axb5 14. Nd4 Qc5 {Garcia-Santos/1976/ECO D (2.Auflage)/ 44/6/Fußnote 29} 15. b4 (15. Ra5 Be7 16. Rxb5 Rxb5 17. Ndxb5 O-O) 15... Qxb4 (15... cxb3 16. Ndxb5 Be7 $14) 16. Ncxb5 Rxb5 17. Ra4 Qb2 18. Nxb5 Qxb5 19. Rxc4 Qb8 20. Qc2 Ba6 21. Rb1 $16) (10... Ra7 { 11.d5 d7} 11. Qd2 Nd7 12. d5 cxd5 13. exd5 Nc5 14. Qe3 $10 {Kelecevic-Sifrer/Jugoslawien/1985/ECO}) 11. d5 $1 {Unzicker Unzicker Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Principle: Create confrontation and open the game when leading in development.'} (11. b3 cxb3 (11... b4 12. a5 Qc7 13. Na4 c3 14. Nb6 Ra7 $17) 12. Qxb3 Nd7 13. d5 cxd5 14. exd5 Nc5 (14... bxa4 15. Qxa4 (15. Qxb6 Nxb6 16. Rab1 Nxd5) 15... Qb4 {Sveshnikov/ECO} 16. dxe6 fxe6 (16... Qxa4 17. exf7+ Kxf7 $14 18. Nxa4 $16) 17. Qa2 $16) 15. Qb4 Nd7 16. Qh4 b4 17. dxe6 fxe6 18. Ne4 $16 {Lerner-Kajdeanov/Moskau II/1985/ECO D (2.Auflage)/44/6/Fußnote 30}) 11... cxd5 (11... Nd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is now considered to be in Black's favour.'} 12. dxe6 (12. dxc6 Bxc6 13. axb5 (13. Nd4 Rb8 (13... Rd8)) 13... axb5 14. Rxa8+ Bxa8 15. Nd4 (15. b3 Bb4) 15... Bc6 $15 1