. Qxd3 Bxb4 23. Na4 Qa5 24. Rh3 f5 $10 25. Rxh7+ {Dauerschach/Gorelov-Kischnjev/Barnaul/1984/ECO D (2.Auflage)/44/6/Fußnote 32} (25. Qd4+ f6 $17)) 15... Bb4 16. Qa1 (16. Na2 Bc5 17. bxc4 b4) 16... O-O $10) 13. a5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} (13. dxe6 bxc3 (13... fxe6 14. Bxc4 (14. Nb1 $6 Nd7 15. Bxc4 O-O-O $10) 14... bxc3 15. Nd4 $15 { Clarke} Rg8 16. g3 $8 (16. Qh5+ Rg6 17. Nxe6 Bxg2 $19) 16... Nc6 $1 17. Nxe6 Ne5 $19) 14. exf7+ Kxf7 15. Bxc4+ Kg7 16. Nh4 {+- Clarke} Bc8 $1 (16... h5 17. Qd3 $18) 17. Qd5 (17. Qf3 Bd6 18. Qxa8 Bb7 19. Nf5+ Kg6 20. Qxb7 Qxb7 21. Nxd6 Qb4 22. Bd3+ f5) (17. bxc3 $40) 17... Ra7) (13. Ne4 $6 exd5 {is slightly better for Black.}) 13... Qc7 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision): White is a pawn down. How should he proceed? --- ANSWER: The only thematic continuation is to offer a piece to open central lines and expose Black's king.'} (13... Qd8 14. dxe6 Qxd1 (14... fxe6 15. Nd4 bxc3 16. Bh5+ Ke7 17. Re1 Bd5 18. Nxe6 $16 {Unzicker}) 15. exf7+ Kxf7 16. Bxc4+ $16 {Unzicker} Kg7 17. Nxd1 Bxf3 $14 {Clarke} 18. gxf3 Nd7 19. Ne3 Ne5 20. Be2 Bc5 21. Rfc1 Rhc8 22. f4 Ng6 23. Rc4 $18) 14. dxe6 {! Tal ! Tal Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Engagement cannot be delayed. In irrational positions Tal never attempted to control chaos. Instead, the great chaoticist tended to ride the complications, the way a hawk glides upon the wind, in search of prey.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'b)'} 14. Na4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is not as strong as the move Tal played in the game. After'} exd5 (14... Nd7 15. dxe6 fxe6 16. Rc1 (16. Nd4 Rg8 17. Bh5+ Ke7 18. g3 Qxa5 19. Nxe6 $1 Kxe6 20. Bf7+ Kxf7 21. Qxd7+ Be7 22. Rfe1 Rge8 23. Qxb7 Rad8 {White only has a small edge.}) 16... Bd5 (16... c3 17. bxc3 Bh6 18. Rb1 $16) 17. Bxc4 (17. Nd2 Rg8 18. g3 Bh6 19. Bxc4 Bxc4 20. Rxc4 (20. Qh5+ Rg6 21. Rxc4 Qe5) 20... Qxa5) 17... Bxc4 18. b3 {Clarke, Unzicker} Qxa5 19. Rxc4 $16 Be7 (19... Qh5 20. Rc6 e5 (20... Qf7 21. Nd4) 21. Re6+ Be7 22. Qd6 Qf7 23. Rd1 Rd8 24. Nb6 $18