{ChessCalisthenics: 'B96: Sicilian Najdorf: 6 Bg5 e6 7 f4, lines other than 7...Qb6 and 7...Be7'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Nbd7 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O b5 10. Bxb5 {!?} axb5 11. Ndxb5 Qb8 (11... Qb7 12. e5 {!} dxe5 13. fxe5 Nxe5 14. Bxf6 {!}) (11... Qb6 12. e5 Bb7 13. Rxd6 Bxd6 14. Qxb7 Qxb7 15. Nxd6+ $16) 12. e5 {!} (12. Rhe1 h6 13. Bh4 Bb7 14. Qd3 Nc5) 12... Bb7 (12... dxe5 {?} 13. Qxa8 $18) (12... Ra5 {!?} 13. exf6 gxf6 14. Bh4 {!?} (14. Bh6 Bxh6 15. Nxd6+ Ke7 $15) 14... Rxb5 15. Nxb5 Qxb5 16. Qa8 $13) 13. Qe2 dxe5 (13... Ra6 {?!} 14. exf6 gxf6 15. Bh4 $16) (13... Be7 14. Bxf6 Nxf6 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Nxd6+ Kf8 17. Nxb7 Qxb7 18. Qe4 $16 {Kengis - Vitolinsh, Riga, 1981}) 14. Qc4 {!} Bc5 $6 {This looks attractive, but loses by force! ?} (14... Be7 $1 {is analysed in Naiditsch, A-Gelfand, B/Dortmund GER 2006.} 15. Bxf6 Nxf6 16. Nc7+ Kf8 17. fxe5 Nd5 18. N3xd5 exd5 19. Nxd5 Qxe5 20. Nxe7 Qxe7 $13) 15. Bxf6 gxf6 {%08DA} (15... Be3+ 16. Kb1 Nxf6 17. Nc7+ Kf8 18. Qb4+ Kg8 19. Nxa8 Qxa8 20. Qxb7 $18 {!}) 16. Rxd7 $1 {Dashing chess!} (16. Kb1 {is a bit slow,} Qc8 17. f5 Nb6 18. Qh4 Ke7 {and Black has consolidated, Atakisi, U-Soylu, S/Istanbul 2001.}) 16... Be3+ 17. Kb1 {!} (17. Rd2 $2 O-O $1 18. Qe2 exf4 19. Kb1 Bxd2 20. Qxd2 Rd8 {Black has a solid extra exchange, Clerides, P-Penttinen, J/Limassol 2000.}) 17... Kxd7 18. Rd1+ Bd4 $6 {A natural reaction, but it loses. ChessCalisthenics: 'N?'} (18... Ke8 {ChessCalisthenics: '!?'} 19. Nc7+ (19. fxe5 Qxe5 20. Nd6+ Kf8 $14) 19... Kf8 20. fxe5 {also gives White a strong attack, Vitolins-Anikayev/Riga 1973 '' Vitolinsh - Anikaev, USSR, 1973} (20. Qb4+ Kg8 21. Nxa8 Qxa8 22. Qe7 Bd5 23. Qxf6 exf4 24. Nxd5 exd5 25. Qg5+ Kf8 26. Rxd5 f3 27. Rd8+ Qxd8 28. Qxd8+ Kg7 29. Qxh8+ Kxh8 30. gxf3 Kg7 31. c4 Kf6 32. Kc2 Ke5 33. Kd3 {Bobras,P (2581)-Czakon,J (2504)/Lublin 2008/ CBM 123/1/2-1/2 (44)})) (18... Ke7 $5 19. Qb4+ Ke8 20. Nd6+ Kf8 21. Nf5+ Kg8 22. Nxe3 exf4 23. Ng4 {looks dangerous for Black, to say the least.}) (18... Bd5 19. Nxd5 exd5 20. Qxd5+ Ke7 21. fxe5 fxe5 22. Nd6 Bd4 23. Nf5+ $18) 19. fxe5 fxe5 (19... Qxe5 20. Nxd4 Ke8 21. Qb4 $1 Bd5 {might avoid what follows, but White has an attack plus a pawn for the exchange, and must be better.}) 20. Nxd4 exd4 21. Qxd4+ Ke7 (21... Bd5 {is no help:} 22. Nxd5 exd5 23. Qxd5+ Ke8 (23... Ke7 24. Re1+) 24. Re1+ Kf8 25. Qc5+ Kg8 26. Qg5+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Kg8 28. Re3 {and wins.}) 22. Qc5+ $1 (22. Qd7+ Kf8 23. Rf1 Qe8 24. Qxb7 {is also quite good, Krupenski, Y-Kanep, M/Tallinn 2000, but the text is much more incisive.}) (22. Qb4+ $1 {is the same:} Kf6 23. Qh4+ $6 (23. Rf1+ $1 Kg7 24. Qe7 {transposes to the main game}) 23... Kg7 $4 (23... Kg6 {might defend}) 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Rd7 Qe8 26. Qh6+ Kg8 27. Rd3 Qb8 28. Rd4 {Li Shilong-Becker, M/Amsterdam NED 2006.}) 22... Kf6 (22... Ke8 23. Nb5 {'+-'} f5 24. Nd6+ Ke7 25. Nc8+ $1 Kf7 26. Qe7+ {wins.}) 23. Rf1+ (23. Qd4+ $2 Kg6 24. Qg4+ Kf6 25. Rf1+ Ke7 {gives no more than a perpetual, Lacroix, S-Libersan, T/ Montreal 2005.}) 23... Kg6 (23... Kg7 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Qf6 $18) 24. Qe7 $1 f5 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: '??'} (24... Qe5 {puts up more resistance: ChessCalisthenics: ''} 25. Qxf7+ Kg5 {ChessCalisthenics: '+-'} 26. h4+ Kxh4 27. g3+ Kg5 28. g4 $1 h5 29. Qe7+ Kg6 30. gxh5+ Kh6 31. Qxb7 {but Black is hardly likely to survive long here.}) (24... Rf8 25. Qf6+ Kh5 26. g4+ Kxg4 27. Rg1+ $18) 25. Qxe6+ Kg7 26. Qe7+ Kg6 27. h4 $1 (27. g4 {also wins,} Rf8 $2 (27... f4 $1) 28. gxf5+ Kh5 {Dimitrov, V-Dichev, N/Plovdiv 1988, when} 29. Qxh7+ Kg4 30. Qg7+ Kh3 31. Nd1 {is the quickest.}) 27... Ra5 {ChessCalisthenics: '??'} (27... Rf8 28. g4 $1 (28. Qg5+ Kf7 29. Rxf5+ Ke6) (28. Rd1 h6 29. Rd6+ Qxd6 30. Qxd6+ Kh5 $18) 28... Qg3 (28... Qd8 29. Qxb7 $18) 29. gxf5+ Kh6 30. Ne2 Qg4 31. Rd1 {and there is little to do against the threat of Rd6+.}) (27... h6 28. g4 $18 {!}) 28. h5+ {Forcing mate in 7.} Kxh5 29. Qf7+ Kh4 30. Qf6+ Kg3 31. Qg5+ Kh2 32. Qh4+ Kxg2 33. Rf2+ Kg1 34. Ne2# 1-0

