1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Ng3 (6. Bd3 Nxe4 7. Bxe4 Nf6 8. Bd3 Bg4 9. c3) 6... e6 7. Bd3 c5 8. c3 $5 cxd4 $1 9. Nxd4 Bc5 $5 (9... Nc5 10. Bc2 $14) 10. O-O Bxd4 (10... O-O $5) 11. cxd4 O-O 12. Bg5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'This variation gives White a good chance to develop an initiative. Black is organised in a way that makes it impossible to put pressure on d4 and therefore White has time to place his pieces as he desires.' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'This variation gives White a good chance to develop an initiative. Black is organised in a way that makes it impossible to put pressure on d4 and therefore White has time to place his pieces as he desires.'} (12. Bf4 Nb6 13. Rc1 Nbd5 14. Be5 Bd7 15. Nh5 Nxh5 16. Qxh5 g6 17. Qh6 f6 18. Bd6 Rf7 19. Rfe1 {gave White good pressure in Adorjan-Flesch, Budapest 1971.}) 12... h6 (12... Qa5 $5 13. Bf4 $5 (13. Bd2 Qd5 $11) (13. h4 $6 h6 14. Bf4 b6 15. Re1 Bb7 {and Black was no worse in Howell-Kumaran, Eastbourne 1990. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black was no worse in Howell-Kumaran, Eastbourne 1990.'})) 13. Bf4 Nb6 14. Bc2 ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'The piece sacrifice after' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'The piece sacrifice after'} 14. Qd2 Nfd5 $1 15. Bxh6 gxh6 16. Qxh6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'only results in a draw after' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'only results in a draw after'} f5 $11 17. Qg6+ Kh8 18. Qh6+ {.}) 14... Nbd5 (14... Nfd5 $2 15. Qd3 $16) ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White has strong pressure following' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White has strong pressure following'} 14... Bd7 15. Qd3 { h5  Nh5} Rc8 16. Ne4 Nbd5 17. Be5 {.}) 15. Be5 Qb6 $5 {GM Mihail Marin: 'White is better developed and has two active bishops, but it is not easy to launch an attack due to Black's stable blockade on d5. Tal's solution is absolutely fantastic:'} 16. Qd3 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: '!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Threatening Nh5 and practically forcing the next move. But how many would think of inviting such a double attack, eliminating the c2-bishop with gain of time?'} ({White could also try to sacrifice a pawn with Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White could also try to sacrifice a pawn with'} 16. Bb3 $5 Bd7 17. Nh5 Nxh5 18. Qxh5 f6 19. Bg3 Qxd4 20. Rad1 {, with good compensation. Raetsky/Chetverik: ', with good compensation.'}) (16. Nh5 {is not dangerous:} Nxh5 17. Qxh5 Bd7 18. Qg4 (18. Rfe1 Rac8) 18... f6 19. Qg6 fxe5 20. Qh7+ Kf7 21. Bg6+ Kf6 {Tal}) 16... Nb4 $6 {GM Mihail Marin: 'The most principled.'} (16... Rd8 {GM Mihail Marin: '!?'} 17. Ne4 $14 Nd7 $14) (16... Bd7 $5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would be met by'} 17. Bxf6 (17. Nh5 $2 Bb5 $1) 17... Nxf6 18. Nh5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!'} Rfd8 $1 (18... Rfc8 19. Nxf6+ gxf6 20. Qh7+ Kf8 21. Bb3 $1 {and Black is in trouble Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black is in trouble'}) 19. Nxf6+ gxf6 20. Qh7+ Kf8 $13 {GM Mihail Marin: '(is complicated, according to Tal)'} 21. d5 $1 {and Black has great problems – Kasparov. GM Mihail Marin: '+-!?' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black has great problems – Kasparov.'}) 17. Qd2 $1 (17. Qf3 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?' '?' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'does not work: after'} Nxc2 18. Bxf6 e5 $1 $17 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!'} (18... Nxd4 $2 19. Qg4 $18) 19. Be7 Nxa1 20. Bxf8 Kxf8 {Black comes out on top.}) 17... Nxc2 (17... Nfd5 $2 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Nh5+ $18 {GM Mihail Marin: 'wins'}) (17... Ng4 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!?' '!?' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is probably okay for Black. White should not over-stretch with'} 18. Bxg7 $1 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?!' '?!' Raetsky/Chetverik: ', as after'} Kxg7 19. Nh5+ Kg8 20. h3 $1 $16 Nxc2 (20... e5 21. Bb3 Rd8 22. d5 {is not clear}) 21. Qxc2 e5 22. Qc5 exd4 23. Qxb6 axb6 24. hxg4 Rd8 {the passed pawn gives Black a good game.}) ({The safer} 17... Nbd5 {offers White a free tempo (Qd2) which he can now use with} 18. Rfe1 $5) 18. Bxf6 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'We see a similar pattern as in the game with Panno: Tal lured the knight from the perfect blocking square on d5 right into the corner to a1!'} (18. Qxc2 Nd5 19. Ne4 Rd8 20. Rfd1 Bd7 {and Black has no problems. The bishop will be very strong on c6. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black has no problems. The bishop will be very strong on c6.'}) 18... Nxa1 ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Not'} 18... gxf6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?' '?'} 19. Qxh6 $18 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Nh5 is coming.'}) 19. Nh5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is targeting g7 with all his ammunition.' GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Black is a whole rook up, but the knight on a1 does not count while the queenside is underdeveloped. In the meantime, White has dangerous kingside threats already.'} (19. Bxg7 {is premature:} Kxg7 20. Nh5+ Kg6) 19... e5 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Flesch is at the height of the moment. His best chance for survival is to speed up his development.'} (19... Qb5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would not work because of'} 20. Nxg7 Kh7 21. Rxa1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'offers White too much compensation for the exchange:'} Kg6 22. Qf4 $18 Qxb2 (22... Rg8 23. Be5 $18 {Tal}) 23. Qg3+ Kxf6 (23... Kh7 $2 24. Rf1 Qc2 25. Nxe6 Rg8 26. Nf8+ $1 {and Black is mated}) 24. Nh5+ Ke7 25. Qc7+ Bd7 26. Qc5+ Kd8 27. Qxf8+ Kc7 28. Qc5+ {and White's attack is very strong. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and White's attack is very strong.'}) (19... g5 $2 20. Bxg5 Qb5 21. d5 $1 $18 {[%cal Gb5d5,Gh5f6] cuts the queen off the kingside due to} Qxd5 $2 22. Nf6+) 20. dxe5 {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'A very instructive phase of the game. Both sides are following their plans consistently without letting themselves distracted by mirages.'} (20. Bxg7 Qg6 21. Bxf8 Kxf8 22. Qe2 Nc2 23. Qxe5 Qg5 24. Qd6+ Kg8 25. Nf6+ $11 {only leads to a perpetual.}) 20... g5 $1 (20... Nb3 {is not good, as Black might need the tempo White will have to use on Rxa1 later on: Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is not good, as Black might need the tempo White will have to use on Rxa1 later on:'} 21. axb3 Kh7 22. Nxg7 Qxb3 23. Rc1 {with an attack.}) 21. e6 $5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Once again the sharpest choice – White wants to retain the bishop.' GM Mihail Marin: '!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Another remarkable move while being a rook down. White prevents Black from developing naturally with ...Bg4 or ...Bf5. Other moves offer less.' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Once again the sharpest choice – White wants to retain the bishop.'} ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Also possible was'} 21. Rxa1 Bg4 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!'} 22. Ng3 Rfd8 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal'} 23. Bxd8 Rxd8 24. Qc3 Rc8 {and Black is probably okay despite the pawn deficit. GM Mihail Marin: '=' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black is probably okay despite the pawn deficit.'}) ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'A further alternative is'} 21. Be7 Bf5 $1 22. Nf6+ Kg7 23. Rxa1 (23. Bxf8+ Rxf8 24. Rxa1) 23... Rfd8 $1 24. Bxd8 Rxd8 25. Qe2 (25. Qc3 Rd3 $1 26. Qc2 Qa5 {and Black has reasonable counterplay. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black has reasonable counterplay.'}) 25... Bd3 $15 {GM Mihail Marin: '=' GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal'}) (21. Ng7 Bg4 22. Rxa1 Rfd8 23. Qc2 Rd5 $11) 21... Qxe6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Alternatively:' GM Mihail Marin: 'Black has to spoil his coordination in order to keep the queen's communication with the kingside alive.'} (21... fxe6 $2 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would weaken the king fatally:' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would weaken the king fatally:'} 22. Qd3 $18 Rxf6 23. Nxf6+ Kf7 24. Nh5 Qxb2 25. Qh7+ Ke8 26. Rd1 {and Black will be mated.}) (21... Bxe6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is met by' GM Mihail Marin: '?'} 22. Bxg5 $1 $16 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!'} (22. Bd4 $2 Rfd8 $1 23. Nf6+ Kf8 $11) 22... Qc5 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Bxh6 Rfd8 25. Qf4 Qc4 $1 26. Rxa1 Qxf4 27. Bxf4 Bxa2 28. Be5 {with an unclear game – Kasparov. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'with an unclear game – Kasparov.'}) 22. h3 $1 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!?' '!?' GM Mihail Marin: '!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'The double exclamation mark does not refer to the objective merits of this move (after which Black could have forced a draw) but to the whole concept. Tal simply parries the threat ...Qg4 without rushing in with his attack, nor with winning back the knight.'} ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is aiming to prevent ...Qg4 due to the following line:' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is aiming to prevent ...Qg4 due to the following line:'} 22. Rxa1 {GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'did does not offer much'} Qg4 $1 23. Ng3 Qf4 $1 24. Qc3 Be6 $13 (24... Bg4 {and Black is better. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black is better.'})) ({However, an interesting alternative was} 22. Bg7 $5 Qg6 23. Bxf8 Be6 (23... Qxh5 $6 24. Qd8 b6 25. Bb4+ Kh7 26. Rxa1 {is dangerous for Black}) 24. Be7 Qxh5 25. Rxa1 {and Black's worse king gives White some pressure.}) ({But} 22. Bc3 $1 {was stronger:} f6 23. Rxa1 {Black's development problems and White's pressure on f6 offer better chances despite the missing exchange.} Bd7 (23... a5 24. Re1 Qf5 25. h3 Be6 26. g4 Qd5 27. Qe2 Rae8 28. Rd1 Qc4 29. Qe3 $16) 24. Nxf6+ Rxf6 25. Bxf6 Bc6 26. Bc3 Qd5 27. Qxd5+ Bxd5 28. f3 $16) 22... Qf5 $2 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?!' '?!' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Or:' GM Mihail Marin: '?!'} (22... Bd7 23. Re1 Qc6 24. Bc3 f6 (24... Bf5 25. Nf6+ Kh8 26. Rxa1 {looks dangerous for Black.}) 25. Qd3 $18) (22... b6 $5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is right. After'} 23. Re1 $1 (23. Rxa1 $2 Bb7 { d5  Qd5}) 23... Qf5 $1 (23... Qc6 24. Rc1 $1 Qe6 25. Bc3 f6 26. Re1 Qf5 27. g4 $1 $16) 24. g4 Qc2 $1 25. Qd6 {( f8) ( Qf8)} Bb7 26. Bc3 Qg6 27. Nf6+ Kh8 $11 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is not better – Kasparov.' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is not better – Kasparov.'}) (22... Qxa2 $2 {is reckless. After} 23. Bxg5 Qa6 24. Nf6+ Kh8 25. Bh4 Kg7 26. Qd4 $1 Rg8 27. Qe4 {Black cannot survive.}) ({I could not find a way to avoid the perpetual queen pursual with} 22... Qd7 $1 23. Qc3 Qc6 24. Qe5 Qe6 $11) 23. Rxa1 $18 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Now everything goes back to normal. White's pressure on the dark squares outweighs Black's extra exchange.'} Be6 24. Re1 Qg6 (24... Rfc8 $5 25. Bc3 Rd8 26. Qe2 {was possible, though White clearly has enough compensation for the exchange. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'was possible, though White clearly has enough compensation for the exchange.'}) 25. g4 Rac8 26. Bc3 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!'} (26. Be7 $6 {would be bad, as Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would be bad, as'} Rc2 27. Qd4 Ra8 28. Nf6+ Kh8 {gives White no obvious way to continue the attack other than with perpetual check. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'gives White no obvious way to continue the attack other than with perpetual check.'}) 26... Rfd8 27. Qe3 Rd3 28. Qe5 Rcxc3 {GM Mihail Marin: 'What else?'} 29. bxc3 Kh7 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?' '?'} ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Black would also be lost after'} 29... Rxh3 30. Rd1 $18 Kh7 31. Nf6+ {GM Mihail Marin: '!'} (31. Rd8 $2 Qb1+ 32. Kg2 Qh1#) 31... Kg7 32. Ne4+ Kh7 33. Rd8 Qg7 34. Nf6+ Kg6 35. Rg8 {, when it's all over. GM Mihail Marin: '+-' Raetsky/Chetverik: ', when it's all over.'}) ({Playable, however, was} 29... Bxa2 30. Ra1 Rxh3 (30... Rd2 $2 31. Nf6+ Kf8 32. c4 $1 {would win for White}) 31. Nf6+ (31. Kg2 Rxh5 32. gxh5 Qc2 {with a mess was also possible}) 31... Kg7 32. Kg2 Rh4 33. Nh5+ Kh7 34. Rxa2 Rxg4+ 35. Ng3 {and Black has reasonable drawing chances, though he is of course worse. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black has reasonable drawing chances, though he is of course worse.'}) 30. Nf6+ Kg7 31. Nd5+ ({Also possible was} 31. Nd7+ Kg8 32. Qb8+ Kg7 33. Qf8+ Kh7 34. Ne5 {and Black must resign.}) 31... Kh7 $6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?' '?'} ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'A better defence was offered by' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'A better defence was offered by'} 31... Kf8 {Raetsky/Chetverik: ', but White could still win with'} 32. Qb8+ {GM Mihail Marin: '!'} (32. Qh8+ $2 Qg8 33. Qxh6+ Qg7 $11) 32... Kg7 33. Ne7 Qh7 34. Qe5+ Kf8 35. Nf5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!'} Bxf5 36. gxf5 Kg8 37. f6 Qg6 38. Qe8+ Kh7 39. Re7 $18 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '.'}) 32. Ne7 Qg7 33. Qe4+ {1-0 (33) Tal,M (2555)-Flesch,J Lvov 1981 [GM Mihail Marin] 1-0 (33) Tal,M-Flesch,J Lviv 31/183 1981 CI1-130 [Tal,Mihail]} 1-0

