{[%evp 0,65,27,29,22,24,9,13,23,-26,59,24,51,50,50,50,50,54,98,94,91,70,82,66,101,89,173,108,108,171,102,101,101,-23,5,-24,125,-30,-30,-120,25,-17,-116,-149,69,33,378,378,451,451,484,495,502,502,502,333,339,293,522,581,809,844,864,863,894,899,29995,899] Tal's opponent, Jack Miller (1937-2012) was the President of the San Diego Chess Club. He was also a member of Labate's Chess Centre, Anaheim, CA USA and it was here that the game was played.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d4 ({Years ago Tal had run into trouble after} 4. Ng5 Bc5 $5 {(Traxler Counterattack) in a famous game with young readers of a Soviet newspaper. That game ended in a fighting draw. Here he avoids the line as he wants to attack himself.}) 4... d6 $2 {This is a beginner's move, losing a pawn.} (4... exd4 {is standard.}) 5. dxe5 Nxe4 6. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 7. Qd5+ Be6 8. Qxe4 $16 {Tal emerges a pawn up in this well-known opening trap. No surprises so far.} Be7 9. O-O (9. Nc3 {completing development on the queenside is also possible.}) 9... d5 10. Qd3 Qd7 11. Re1 Raf8 {An unusual plan. He intends an all-out offensive against the king.} (11... Rhf8 {followed by...Kg8 is a safer alternative.}) (11... Bf5 {looks playable. But it's dangerous to allow the possibility of e5-e6.}) 12. Nc3 Ke8 13. Ng5 $2 {This move offers Black counterplay on the f-file.} (13. Bg5 $1 {would have kept everything under control.}) 13... Bc5 $1 {inaugurating a brilliant counterattack} 14. Nxe6 Bxf2+ 15. Kh1 Bxe1 16. Nxf8 $6 {One cannot judge this move strictly. It was a simultaneous display, after all.} ({Otherwise he would have played} 16. Nxd5 $1 Ba5 (16... Qxe6 $4 17. Nxc7+ $18) 17. Nxf8 Rxf8 18. Be3 Nxe5 19. Qb3 $16) 16... Rxf8 17. Bg5 $2 {This aggressive could have backfired.} (17. Bd2 $1 {would have eliminated the road block by the bishop on e1.}) 17... Nb4 18. Qe2 Nxc2 19. e6 $1 {Sheer intimidation!} ({not} 19. Rxe1 $2 Nxe1 20. Qxe1 Qf5 21. h4 Qf1+ 22. Qxf1 Rxf1+ $19) 19... Qd6 $6 {This move initiating a combination has a fatal flaw.} ({He would have won with} 19... Qc6 $1 20. Rd1 d4 {followed by ...Rf2.}) 20. Nb5 ({Tal saw} 20. Rxe1 $2 Nxe1 21. Qxe1 {losing to} Qe5 $1 $19) 20... Qe5 $1 {Tal's queen, rook and bishop are attacked and the king is threatened with mate in two.} 21. h4 $3 {A breezy move that puts out the fire round him.} Qg3 (21... Qxe2 $4 22. Nxc7# {is a lovely finish with Black queen being only a spectator.}) 22. Rd1 $1 Rf2 {Once again Black's attack threatens to turn into a towering inferno.} 23. Qxf2 $3 {Pouring cold shower on all his hopes.} Bxf2 24. Rxd5 Qxh4+ {Threatened by Rd8 mate, Black is forced to surrender the queen.} 25. Bxh4 Bxh4 26. Nxc7+ Kf8 27. Rf5+ Bf6 28. Rd5 a5 29. Rd7 Nb4 30. Rf7+ Kg8 31. Rxf6 $1 Nc6 {Never say die!} (31... gxf6 {allows} 32. e7 $18) 32. Rf7 g6 33. e7 {A bright finish! Tal remembered this game on his return from the USA and showed it to David Bronstein who insisted on having the whole score for posterity. Tal obliged and so it came to be preserved.} 1-0

