{[%mdl 8192] C10: French with 3 Nc3: Unusual Black 3rd moves and 3...dxe4} 1. e4 {GM Mihail Marin} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Nbd7 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal confesses that he did not expect this (then still) unpopular variation. He vaguely remembered having faced it three years earlier against Benko, but not how it actually went.'} 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. Nf3 c5 8. Bc4 cxd4 9. O-O Be7 10. Qe2 h6 {GM Mihail Marin: 'In many positions, this is a useful move (especially with gain of time), but here it offers White a clear target on the kingside. This is what the rest of the game will all be about.'} 11. Bf4 O-O 12. Rad1 Bd7 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Both sides have developed naturally and Black's position should be entirely viable. His compact kingside structure offers him compensation for White's superior piece activity.'} 13. Rxd4 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal spent half an hour on this move, trying to figure out the best way to retrieve the pawn. Since in the long run the rook will be exposed in the centre, his last move is a clear signal for an attack.'} Qb6 14. Qd2 ({Tal was surprised to find out from Portisch that they only now deviated from the aforementioned game:} 14. Rd3 {(GM Mihail Marin) (Completely pointless, according to Tal.)} Bb5 15. Bxb5 Qxb5 $11 {Tal, M-Benko, P Curacao 1962 (1-0, 33) The final phase of this game is interesting enough to include in the fragments section below.}) 14... Bc6 {GM Mihail Marin: 'The threat ...Bxf3 forces White's hand.'} ({Tal intended to meet} 14... Rfc8 {(GM Mihail Marin) in a similar way:} 15. Bxh6 ({He also mentions that if he had found that the combination was not working, he would have had} 15. Bb3 {as a reserve.}) 15... gxh6 16. Qxh6 Qxd4 $1 {The only defence.} 17. Nxd4 Rxc4 18. Rd1 {Tal's intuition was right. Despite his slight material advantage, Black's position remains dangerous from a practical point of view. His king's vulnerability prevents him from maintaining good coordination. But objectively, Black should be close to equality. For instance:} Rc5 ({Another reasonable defence is} 18... Nh7 19. Rd3 Bg5 20. Qh3) 19. Rd3 Ng4 20. Rg3 Rg5 21. Qh4 f5 22. Rh3 Kf7 23. Qg3 $5 {An interesting piece redeployment.} Rg7 24. Qc7 {, and White's initiative surely compensates for the sacrificed material.}) 15. Bxh6 {last book move last book move GM Mihail Marin: '!'} ({There was no way back, as after} 15. Ne5 {GM Mihail Marin} Rad8 {Black has very active play.}) 15... Ne4 {Black threatens to win material: Ne4xd2 Black threatens to win material: Ne4xd2 GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'The only adequate defence, obstructing the fourth rank before taking the bishop.'} (15... gxh6 {GM Mihail Marin} 16. Qxh6 {makes it hard for Black to parry the threats.} Bxf3 (16... Ne4 {comes too late:} 17. Bd3 $18) 17. Qg5+ Kh8 18. Rh4+ Nh7 (18... Bh5 {GM Mihail Marin} 19. Bd3 {, threatening Rxh5+, with mate in sight.}) 19. Qxe7 {The bishop cannot retreat in view of Qf6+ followed by Rg4+, and if} Rg8 20. Qf6+ {(Tal).} Rg7 21. Bd3 Kg8 22. Bxh7+ Rxh7 23. Rxh7 Kxh7 24. Qxf7+ Kh8 25. Qxf3 $18 {, with a decisive material advantage.}) 16. Qf4 gxh6 17. Rxe4 Bxe4 18. Qxe4 {GM Mihail Marin: 'The forced sequence has ended and we can draw the first conclusions. White has a pawn for the exchange and the exposed position of the enemy king with opposite-coloured bishops on the board offers him attacking chances. But the pawns on e6 and f7 restrict the white bishop, keeping the danger within bearable limits. The main problem for Black is to identify the real and the fake threats. Over the board, this can be very difficult.'} Rad8 {GM Mihail Marin: 'A natural and good developing move.'} (18... Qxb2 {(GM Mihail Marin) is as risky as it looks.} 19. Ne5 Bd6 (19... Bf6 $2 {allows} 20. Bd3 {, mating soon.}) 20. Nxf7 Kxf7 21. Qxe6+ Kg7 22. Qxd6 {, and Black's position remains dangerous.}) (18... Bf6 {(GM Mihail Marin) was a worthy alternative.} 19. Bd3 Rfd8 20. Qh7+ Kf8 21. Qxh6+ Bg7 ({Tal is right when claiming an advantage after} 21... Ke7 22. b3 $14 {, as White has two pawns for the exchange and a compact structure.}) 22. Qf4 {Tal considers this to favour White, but things are not entirely clear after} Qxb2 23. Ng5 Bf6 $13) 19. b3 {GM Mihail Marin: '!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'A move of huge practical strength. White defends the pawn and maintains all the possible plans in reserve. At the same time, it puts Black into a difficult psychological situation. He has no obvious way of freeing himself from the danger and displaying activity could be risky. In simpler words, what would you play? Sounds a bit like a psychological Zugzwang.'} ({Tal refrained from} 19. Bd3 {(GM Mihail Marin) on account of} Rxd3 {, followed by ...Qxb2.}) 19... Bc5 {GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'As tempting as it may seem to attack f2, this puts the bishop on an unfavourable square.'} (19... Bf6 $6 {(GM Mihail Marin) would also be bad due to} 20. Bd3) ({But it's remarkable that Black could instead play a semi-waiting move such as} 19... a6 {(GM Mihail Marin) , sending the ball back into White's camp.} 20. h3 $5 {Another waiting move.} ({It appears that} 20. Bd3 {only helps Black to secure his kingside with} f5 $1 $15) 20... Bb4 $5 {The bishop stands better here than on c5, as it prevents a later c2-c3 (see later comments) and allows a quick transfer to the long diagonal.}) 20. Qf4 {White threatens to win material: Qf4xh6 White threatens to win material: Qf4xh6 GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal immediately takes his chance.'} Kg7 {Black king safety dropped} 21. Qe5+ {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'With the bishop far from f6, this check forces a kingside weakening.'} f6 {Black threatens to win material: f6xe5 Black threatens to win material: f6xe5 GM Mihail Marin: 'The White bishop has new horizons now.'} 22. Qg3+ {GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal was not interested in taking the second pawn, as this would imply exchanging queens.'} Kh7 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Black's position has deteriorated a bit, but the general character of the fight has not changed too much. Both players should rely on apparently modest, prophylactical moves, before undertaking anything active.'} 23. Re1 {White threatens to win material: Re1xe6 White threatens to win material: Re1xe6 GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'This attacking move is premature because it weakens f2 in a moment when White still has other constructive options.'} (23. c3 $3 {(GM Mihail Marin) would have started the fight against the enemy bishop as a perfect complement to his own bishop's activity's increase after ...f7-f6.} a5 ({With the pawn on f2 defended,} 23... Rg8 {can be answered more actively than in the game:} 24. Qh3 f5 25. b4 Bf8 26. Re1 {, with strong pressure.}) 24. a3 $5 ({In fact,} 24. Re1 {would work better than in the game, as Black does not have ...Bb4 anymore.} Rg8 25. Qh4) 24... Bxa3 $2 25. Nd4 $16) 23... Rg8 {Black threatens to win material: Rg8xg3 Black threatens to win material: Rg8xg3 GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Black also places a rook on an open file, and also with gain of time. And funnily enough, this is also premature because it weakens the defence of a pawn, the one on f6.'} ({Tal considers} 23... Rd6 {(GM Mihail Marin) to be losing on account of} 24. Nh4 Rg8 25. Bd3+ ({In fact} 25. Qf4 {is better, with unclear play after} Qb4 $13 {Taking advantage of the missing c2-c3.}) 25... f5 26. Bxf5+ exf5 27. Re7+ {Obviously he overlooked that after} Kh8 28. Qe5+ {Black has} Rf6 $19) ({But the simplest way to reach clear equality was} 23... Bb4 $1 {GM Mihail Marin} 24. c3 ({Tal had initially calculated} 24. Bxe6 $2 Bxe1 25. Bf5+ Kh8 26. Qg6 Bxf2+ 27. Kf1 Rd1+ 28. Ke2 {"winning". But while Portisch was thinking over his previous move, Tal noticed that Black mates "first" with} Re1+ $1 {Therefore, he decided that if Portisch played this way, he would force a draw with the line indicated below.}) 24... Bxc3 25. Nh4 Bxe1 26. Qg6+ {, with a perpetual.}) 24. Qh4 {White threatens to win material: Qh4xf6} Rd6 25. Kf1 {GM Mihail Marin: '!?' GM Mihail Marin: 'This kind of prophylactic king move is typical for Tal. His idea was to make the threat Qxf6 real and increase his queen's mobility in general, as ...Bxf2 will not come with check anymore. But while this is not a bad move, the queenside plan would have been a useful intermediary:'} (25. c3 $1 {GM Mihail Marin} a5 26. a3 Rg6 {My suggestion. Tal analyses two weaker defences:} (26... f5 27. b4 axb4 28. axb4 Rg4 29. Qe7+ Rg7 30. bxc5 Qxc5 31. Qf8 Rg8 32. Qf7+ Rg7 33. Ng5+ $1 hxg5 34. Qh5+ Kg8 35. Rxe6 {wins.}) (26... Rc6 27. Qxf6 Bxf2+ 28. Kf1 Rxc4 29. Rxe6 {wins - Tal.}) 27. Kf1 $1 ({Now is a good moment for this, as} 27. b4 {runs into} Qc6 $1 {, threatening ...Qxf3 and clearing space for the bishop's retreat along the active diagonal.}) 27... Qc6 $6 28. Qh3 $1 f5 29. Ne5 $16) 25... f5 {GM Mihail Marin: 'This fails to neutralize White's initiative.'} (25... Bb4 {(GM Mihail Marin) would pose more practical problems, for instance} 26. Re3 Bc5 {, and it is not easy to avoid the repetition.}) 26. h3 {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Finally a constructive pawn plan, aimed at weakening the light squares in Black's camp even more.'} Rg6 27. g4 Rd7 $4 {hands over the advantage to the opponent hands over the advantage to the opponent GM Mihail Marin: '?' GM Mihail Marin: 'A terrible blunder after a tense phase.'} (27... Qc6 $142 {would hold out would hold out GM Mihail Marin: 'is stronger, when after'} 28. gxf5 exf5 $11 29. Qh5 $44 {(GM Mihail Marin) White has excellent compensation, but possibly no more, as his king is also somewhat exposed.}) (27... fxg4 {GM Mihail Marin} 28. hxg4 Qc6 {runs into} 29. Ne5 {, as h1 is defended.}) 28. Rxe6 {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Based on the knight fork on g5.'} Rd1+ 29. Kg2 Rxe6 30. Bxe6 fxg4 $4 {Black falls apart} (30... Qa6 31. c4 Qxa2 $18) (30... Qxe6 {GM Mihail Marin} 31. Ng5+) 31. Qxg4 Rd8 (31... Qd8 {what else?} 32. Qf5+ Kg7 33. Qxc5 b6 34. Qe5+ Qf6 35. Qg3+ Kf8 36. Qg8+ Ke7 $18) 32. Ne5 (32. Ne5 Qxe6 33. Qxe6 Rg8+ 34. Kf1 $18) 1-0

