1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. g3 Bb4 5. Nd5 a5 6. Bg2 d6 7. O-O O-O 8. e3 (8. d4 $6 Nxd5 9. cxd5 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 exd4 $15) (8. d3 Nxd5 9. cxd5 Nd4 $11) 8... Bc5 9. b3 Nxd5 (9... Ba7 $5 10. Nc3 $5) 10. cxd5 Nb4 $6 (10... Ne7 $5 11. d4 exd4 12. Nxd4 Nf5 13. Bb2 Re8 $14) 11. d4 exd4 12. Nxd4 Bxd4 13. exd4 $16 Re8 $2 (13... a4 14. Ba3 $1) (13... Bf5 14. a3 $16) (13... c6 $5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is also possible, with the idea that after'} 14. dxc6 bxc6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Black wants to play on the light squares with ...Be6-d5. If'} 15. a3 Nd5 $16 16. Bxd5 cxd5 {then Black has compensation due to the opposite-coloured bishops.}) 14. a3 Na6 15. Bd2 $1 (15. b4 $6 axb4 16. axb4 Bd7 17. Qb3 Bb5 { d7}) 15... b6 (15... Nb8 $5 16. Rc1 $16) 16. b4 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Now Black's pieces are a bit misplaced.'} Nb8 ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Another possibility for Black was to enter the following endgame, a pawn down, but with chances of survival:'} 16... axb4 17. axb4 Bd7 18. Re1 Bb5 19. Bf1 Qd7 20. Rxe8+ Qxe8 21. Bxb5 Qxb5 22. Qf1 Qxf1+ 23. Kxf1 {( b5!)} b5 $8 24. Ra5 Kf8 25. Rxb5 Rb8 $16 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '.'}) 17. bxa5 bxa5 (17... Nd7 $5) 18. Re1 a4 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?!'} 19. Qh5 $1 (19. Qc2) 19... Bd7 (19... Nd7 20. Bg5 $1 Rxe1+ 21. Rxe1 Qf8 22. Be7 Qe8 23. Qd1 $18 { d6} (23. Re3 $18)) 20. Be4 (20. Bg5 f6 21. Bf4 $1 { e6!} (21. Be4 Rxe4 $8 22. Rxe4 fxg5 23. Rae1 {( 24. e8, 24. e7)} g6 24. Qh6 Qf8 25. Qxg5 Na6 $13 26. Rf4 Bf5 27. g4 h6 28. Qe7 (28. Qh4 $2 g5) 28... Qxe7 29. Rxe7 Bc2 {and Black is OK.})) 20... g6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'A serious weakening of the dark squares around the king.'} (20... h6 21. Bxh6 $18) 21. Qh6 Qf6 22. Bg5 Qxd4 (22... Qg7 23. Qh4 { f6}) 23. Qh4 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!'} (23. Bf5 Qg7) 23... Qg7 (23... Rxe4 24. Qxe4 Qxe4 25. Rxe4 $18) (23... Kg7 $5 24. Rad1 Qb2 25. Bd3 $16 Na6 26. Rxe8 Bxe8 27. Bxa6 Rxa6 28. Bh6+ Kg8 29. Qe7 $18) 24. Bh6 (24. Bf6 { f4, h4-h5+-}) 24... Qh8 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White's position is superior. He has sacrificed a pawn, but as compensation he has activity, a development advantage and control of the dark squares.'} 25. Bf5 $1 $18 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!?'} ({Instead the simple} 25. Bd3 $1 {is very strong. Black cannot develop his queenside, as} Na6 {loses to} 26. Bxa6 Rxa6 27. Rxe8+ Bxe8 28. Qe7 {. Meanwhile, White can just improve his position step by step. Instead Tal converted the advantage with the creative text move.}) 25... Qc3 (25... Bb5 26. Rxe8+ Bxe8 27. Qd8 $18) 26. Rxe8+ Bxe8 27. Rc1 Qe5 28. Qd8 gxf5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'If'} 29. Kf1 $3 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White threatens Rf1 and this cannot be prevented.'} (29. Bd2 {( e1)} Nc6 $3 30. Qxa8 Nd4 $13) (29. Bf4 Nd7 (29... Qe4 $1) (29... Qe2 30. Be3 $1) 30. Qxa8 Nf8 31. Re1 Bb5 32. Kg2 $18) 29... Nd7 30. Qxa8 Nf8 {then White plays} 31. Re1 Bb5+ 32. Kg2 $1 {.} 1-0

