1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. h3 h6 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 ({In fact, the Smyslov variation had been played just once before, by the Bulgarian IM Atanas Kolarov. The game has quite a modern character, from the point of view of the fight for the centre and the initiative.} 11. a4 Bb7 12. d5 Na5 13. Ba2 c6 14. Na3 cxd5 15. exd5 Qd7 16. b4 Nc4 17. Nxc4 bxc4 18. Bxc4 Rac8 19. Bb3 Rxc3 20. Bb2 Rcc8 {1/2 Prahov,V-Kolarov,A/Sofia 1960/EXT 1999}) 11... Bf8 12. Nf1 Bd7 13. Ng3 Na5 14. Bc2 c5 {I have tried to give a possible explanation for the reason that made Smyslov try his new weapon precisely against Tal. The Magician from Riga was known for disliking blocked position, which made him refrain from d4-d5 in the Ruy Lopez regularly. In the book with his selected games, Geller explains with humour that he used to choose against Tal provocative systems of the Chigorin Ruy Lopez, because they could only be "refuted" with d4-d5 and he knew that Misha would not even take this move into account, not during the game, at least! Since the current position can be described as an improved Chigorin, Smyslov may have relied on the same judgement. If this is true, the next move may have come as a shock to him.} 15. d5 {Just a few months before this game was played, Tal had just finished his second World title match against Botvinnik. After so many games played in the company of the Patriarch, one could expect him to have changed quite a lot, become more complete as a player and, finally, not shy away from closed positions! Still, the way he treated the position was far from optimal and the game ended in a draw only because Smyslov did not find the strength to refute Tal's bluff on the 22nd move...} Nc4 16. Nh2 g6 17. Rf1 {Tal plays like if it was a Chigorin Variation, with the black queen on c7, somewhat isolated from the kingside. In this concrete position, the pressure along the f-file is not dangerous yet and the rook stiil had things to do on the f-file.} (17. b3 Nb6 18. f4 {is a more dangerous continuation.}) 17... Bg7 18. b3 Nb6 19. f4 exf4 20. Bxf4 Qe7 21. Qd2 $2 {This move is equivalent to a piece sacrifice (an incorrect one!) because of the insufficient defence of the e-file.} (21. Qc1 Nh7 (21... g5 $6 22. Bd2 {leaves the black kingside weakened.}) 22. Bxh6 (22. Bd3 $6 c4 $36) (22. Bd1 $6 h5 $36) 22... Bxc3 23. Nf3 b4 {Black should not weaken his king's defence by giving up the bishop for the (still) passive rook.} 24. Rb1 Bb5 25. Rf2 Nd7 {Black has regrouped in an optimal way and has little to fear .}) 21... g5 22. e5 (22. Be3 Nfxd5 $1) 22... gxf4 $6 (22... Nfxd5 $1 23. exd6 Qd8 $19 {leaves the f4-bishop trapped.}) 23. exf6 Qxf6 24. Ne4 $44 Qg6 25. Nxc5 Bf5 26. Bxf5 Qxf5 27. Rxf4 Qxd5 28. Nd3 Nc4 29. bxc4 bxc4 30. Rf3 cxd3 31. Rxd3 Qg5 1/2-1/2

