1. e4 {MIKHALCHISHIN DSZ} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 ({Usually I play} 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Bc4 {, trying to reach a set-up similar to the Rauzer system. Since this 'visiting card' of mine was well known to the young Yugoslav master, who carefully prepares for every game, I decided to choose another continuation.}) 5... Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 {Mikhail Tal: 'I once analysed this variation with B. Gurgenidze, during preparations for a student event. The Georgian master used to adopt this continuation regularly, and not without success. The variation has also been thoroughly tested by Yugoslav players, and, in particular, B. Parma is one of the specialists on it.'} 8. Be2 (8. Bg5 $1 Bg7 9. Qd2) 8... Bg7 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 {Mikhail Tal: 'With his move'} Be6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black wants to emphasise that the position of the pawn on c4 also has its drawbacks.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Gurgenidze prefers' Mikhail Tal: 'or' Mikhail Tal: 'It would be interesting to test the variation'} 10... Ng4 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'so as to exchange off one of White's bishops. After 10...Ng4, play can go as follows: Bulletin: '!?''} 11. Bxg4 (11. Bd4 Bh6 12. Qd1 Ne5 {which occurred, for instance, in Cardoso-Tal (Portoroz 1958) and Tal-Gurgenidze (26th USSR Championship). In each case Black had no reason to complain about the results of the opening.}) 11... Bxg4 12. O-O (12. Bd4 {Mikhail Tal: 'which, as far as I know, has not yet occurred in serious tournament practice.'}) 12... Rc8 13. b3 b5 14. Nxb5 {Geller,E-Gurgenidze/URS-ch25/ Geller,E-Gurgenidze/URS-ch25/ Mikhail Tal: '(Geller-Gurgenidze, 25th USSR Championship) Bulletin: 'Geller,E-Gurgenidze/URS-ch25/'' MIKHALCHISHIN: '!?'}) 11. Rc1 Qa5 12. b3 {Mikhail Tal: 'White is in no hurry to castle, preferring for the moment to strengthen his c-pawn and to keep open the possibility of moving his knight, thus offering to transpose into an ending in which his king will still be in the centre.'} Rfc8 ({Mikhail Tal: 'More accurate was'} 12... a6 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'and if Bulletin: '!''} 13. O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'then'} (13. f3 {MIKHALCHISHIN} b5 14. Nd5 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2) 13... b5 {Mikhail Tal: '. After the text, however, the situation has changed: Black has used an extra move preparing the advance ...b5 and the absence of the rook has weakened his kingside. Now White's play is directed against the enemy king.'}) 13. O-O a6 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Diagram '} 14. f4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'A natural move, and therefore I was astonished to discover that it was practically an innovation. Bulletin: '!''} ({In their games with Parma, Gligoric and Janoševic played} 14. Bf3 {which I consider to be inconsistent.}) 14... b5 15. f5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Bulletin: '!''} Bd7 16. fxg6 {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} hxg6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Following the opening of the f-file, it is clear that Black would be better off with his other rook on c8.' Mikhail Tal: 'the advance'} 17. c5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'White is stealing up on the black knight. This move has one other significant virtue: Black's queen is not only unable to assist the development of his queenside attack, but is also a long way from his kingside. Bulletin: '!''} (17. e5 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'was very tempting. After thinking for about half an hour, I rejected it because of Bulletin: '!?''} b4 18. exf6 (18. Na4 Ne4 19. Qd4 Bxa4 20. Qxe4 Bc6 21. Qf4 Qxe5 22. Qxf7+ Kh7) 18... bxc3 19. Rxc3 Bxf6 20. Rxf6 (20. Bd4 {MIKHALCHISHIN} Bxd4+ 21. Qxd4 Qc5 22. Qxc5 Rxc5 23. b4 Rcc8 24. Rfc1) 20... exf6 21. Bd4 Qg5 22. Qxg5 fxg5 23. Bf6 Re8 $44 {Mikhail Tal: ', and although White has compensation for the sacrificed exchange, Black has defensive possibilities. Bulletin: '''}) {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 17... Be6 {Mikhail Tal: ', Black threatens 18...dxc5. White cannot play'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Other continuations would also leave White with a threatening initiative, e.g.'} 17... b4 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. exd5 dxc5 20. Bc4) ({Mikhail Tal: 'or'} 17... Bg4 18. e5 dxe5 19. Rxf6 Bxe2 20. Rb6 {Mikhail Tal: ', and the situation of the black queen is tragicomic.'}) ({Mikhail Tal: 'In the case of'} 17... dxc5 18. e5 Ng4 {Mikhail Tal: 'White was planning to exchange queens by'} 19. Nd5 Qxd2 20. Bxd2 {Mikhail Tal: ', and Black has a difficult problem in deciding how best to sacrifice the exchange. Nunn: It is hard to see why Black is worse after'} Re8 21. Nc7 Bxe5 {.}) 18. Bf3 (18. cxd6 $2 {Mikhail Tal: 'Bulletin: '?''} exd6 19. Bd4 {Mikhail Tal: 'because of the usual combinative blow in such positions –'} (19. Bf3 {MIKHALCHISHIN} Ng4 20. Bxg4 Rxc3 21. Bxe6 fxe6 22. Rcd1) 19... Nxe4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: '. However, he is able, for the moment, to avoid exchanges.'}) 18... dxc5 {Mikhail Tal: 'There is nothing better.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'The passive'} 18... Rab8 {Mikhail Tal: 'leads to a difficult position after'} 19. cxd6 exd6 20. Bd4 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 19. e5 Ng4 ({Mikhail Tal: 'He cannot save the exchange. On'} 19... Rd8 {Mikhail Tal: 'there follows'} 20. Qf2 {Mikhail Tal: 'and Black cannot play'} Nd5 $2 {Mikhail Tal: '. Bulletin: '?''} 21. Nxd5 Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Rxd5 23. Qxf7+ Kh7 24. Qxd5) 20. Bxa8 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Diagram '} Bxe5 {Mikhail Tal: 'For the moment Black is a rook down, but both white bishops are attacked and at the same time the h-pawn is threatened. However, it is at this moment that the weakening of the f7-square by 12...Rfc8 is exposed.' MIKHALCHISHIN: '?'} (20... Rxa8 $142 {MIKHALCHISHIN}) 21. Bd5 {MIKHALCHISHIN: '!'} Nxe3 ({Mikhail Tal: 'After' Mikhail Tal: 'but in later notes after'} 21... Rd8 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'I was planning Bulletin: '!'' Mikhail Tal: ', Tal preferred the alternative line'} 22. Qe2 $1 (22. Bxe6 Rxd2 23. Bxf7+ Kh7 24. Bxd2 Qc7 $40 {e5-d4+, e5xc3} 25. Z0 Bd4+ ({and} 25... Bxc3 {.})) (22. Rfd1 {.}) 22... Nxe3 23. Bxe6 fxe6 24. Nb1 {Mikhail Tal: 'but even so this was Black's relatively best continuation. Nunn: Indeed, after'} (24. Kh1 {MIKHALCHISHIN} Nxf1 25. Qxe5) 24... Qc7 {White seems to be in quite a lot of trouble}) 22. Bxe6 Rd8 ({Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 22... Nxf1 {Mikhail Tal: 'White was considering, besides'} 23. Rxf1 {Mikhail Tal: ','} ({Mikhail Tal: 'the amusing variation'} 23. Bxf7+ Kg7 {Mikhail Tal: '!'} (23... Kxf7 24. Qd5+) 24. Qg5 Bxh2+ 25. Kxf1 ({after} 25. Kh1 $2 {Black even wins –} Rh8 26. Qxg6+ Kf8) 25... Kxf7 26. g3 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) (23. Qh6 {MIKHALCHISHIN} Bg7 24. Qxg6 Rf8 25. Nd5 Qd8 26. Rd1 Qe8 27. Nc7 Qb8 28. Rd7)) 23. Qf2 ({Mikhail Tal: 'As in the variation mentioned in the note to Black's 21st move, unfavourable for White is'} 23. Bxf7+ Kg7 24. Qxe3 Bd4 25. Qxd4+ cxd4 26. Ne4 Qb6 27. Ng5 d3+ 28. Kh1 d2 29. Ne6+ Kh6 {Mikhail Tal: 'and White does not succeed in setting up a mating net.'}) 23... Nf5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black had placed great hopes on this move. Certainly White's queen is threatened (24...Bd4), and also his two minor pieces, but he has a reply which consolidates his advantage.'} 24. Qe2 Bd4+ ({Black naturally did not care for} 24... Bxc3 25. Bxf5 gxf5 26. Qxe7 {.}) 25. Kh1 fxe6 26. Qxe6+ Kg7 {27...g3+ 28.hg3 h8# 27...g3+ 28.hg3 h8# Mikhail Tal: 'Threatening 27...Ng3+, but White defends by attacking. Bulletin: '27...g3+ 28.hg3 h8#''} 27. Ne4 Qc7 28. Ng5 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Diagram '} Rf8 $2 {Mikhail Tal: 'Short of time, Parma evidently overlooked White's next move. Bulletin: '?''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'However, Black's position is already lost, for example:'} 28... Rh8 29. Qf7+ Kh6 30. Rxf5 gxf5 31. Qxf5 $18 {Mikhail Tal: 'and White wins within a few moves. Bulletin: '+-''}) (28... Ng3+ 29. hxg3 Rh8+ 30. Nh3) 29. Qxf5 $1 {DSZ: 'A lovely final picture! The knight fork on e6 decides the day!' DSZ: 'Ein schönes Schlussbild! Die Springergabel auf e6 entscheidet den Tag!'} (29. Qxf5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Bulletin: '!''} Rxf5 30. Ne6+) 1-0

