{Tal treated the opening and early middlegame in a way that was rather untypical for him. He initiated active operations in the centre with incomplete queenside development. At this height, the only thing he can be proud of is the central knight, but otherwise Black has little to complain. Tal's play in the next phase is mathematically perfect. He will generate a series of mutual blows, resulting in exchanges (and some material losses for him) during which he loses the abovementioned undeveloped queenside pieces! Due to the fact that Black's pieces on a1 and b1 will be far from the main action, this operation will turn out to be better than simple development!} 17. Bb3 $1 {The pin created by this move can be dangerous only if Black neglects it, but White gains access to c6 and forces the knight to a1.} f4 $6 {This only helps the bishop reach a better square, without achieving anything.} (17... Be6 {was better, when White might have had to play} 18. dxc5 ({Or} 18. Bd2 {anyway. With an extra tempo compared to the game continuation, Black would have no problems at all.} Nxb3 19. Nc6 Qd7 {With the e-file defended by the bishop on e6 this is possible.} 20. Nxe7+ Qxe7 21. axb3 Nxd2 22. Nxd2 $11)) 18. Bd2 {Black has to reckon with the threat Bxa5 already.} Nxb3 {Forced.} (18... Bf5 {fails to gain time for development due to} 19. Bxa5 $1 Qxa5 (19... Bxd3 $2 20. Bxd8 {followed by Nxd3 wins a piece.}) 20. Bxc4+ $1 bxc4 21. Qxc4+ Kh8 22. Na3 $18 {Black is helpless against the threats Nc6 or Nf7+.}) 19. Nc6 $1 {Do you need the undeveloped rook on b1? Feel free to take it!} ({It is not hard to understand that} 19. Qxb3 {offers Black excellent play due to his bishops' pair, even if he will lose a pawn.} Bh4 $1 {My favourite move, as it puts pressure on a square far from White's undeveloped pieces.} (19... Bf6 {is also satisfactory, of course.}) 20. Nxc4 Bxf2+ $5 ({Strong, spectacular, but not absolutely necessary.} 20... bxc4 21. Qxc4+ Kh8 22. Nc3 f3 {also offers Black a strong initiative for the pawn, as if} 23. g3 $2 Bxg3 $1 $17) 21. Kxf2 Qxd4+ 22. Kf1 bxc4 23. Qc3 (23. Qf3 Bf5 24. Qf2 f3) 23... f3 24. Qxd4 fxg2+ 25. Kxg2 cxd4 {, with excellent compensation for the piece in both cases.}) 19... Nxa1 20. Nxd8 {For the time being, White has a queen for a rook and a bishop, but several pieces are hanging and it is Black's turn.} Bf5 {The most logical move, aiming to complete his development.} ({If} 20... Bxd8 $2 21. dxc5 $1 {the knight is doomed on a1 while many of his other pieces are exposed to the queen's attack.} Ra7 22. c6 $5 Bb6 23. Bb4 Rd8 24. Qc3 {White threatens Na3, completing development and grabbing the knight soon. Black does not have any satisfactory continuations:} Bd4 (24... a5 25. Be7 Re8 26. Bc5 $1 $18) 25. Qc1 Rc7 26. Bc3 $16) 21. Qf3 Raxd8 22. Rxe7 Bxb1 23. Bxf4 {The forced sequence has almost come to an end (Black still has to deal with the threat dxc5). White has lost material, but instead of two undeveloped pieces, there are two delayed black "visitors" on a1 and b1. True, the bishop can retreat to g6, but for the time being his pieces are exposed to Re1 and there are some worries with the g7-square, too. Tal's evaluation when embarking on this line was correct as White has the better chances.} Rxd4 ({Black cannot ignore the central tension with the regrouping} 23... Bg6 $2 24. dxc5 ({The move order} 24. b3 Na3 25. dxc5 {is also effective.}) 24... Nc2 25. b3 Nd4 26. Qg4 Nf5 27. Re6 Na3 {The only reasonable square for the knight.} 28. c6 $18) (23... cxd4 {is best met with} 24. h4 $3 {, preparing a "warm receival" for the bishop and maintaining the possibility of Qb7.} Bg6 (24... d3 $2 {allows} 25. Qb7 $1 d2 26. Rxg7+ Kh8 27. Bxd2 Rxd2 28. Rg5 {, with decisive threats, for instance} Rg8 29. Qf7 Rxg5 30. hxg5 Rd8 31. Qf6+) 25. h5 d3 26. Qg3 Rde8 ({Or if} 26... Rxf4 27. hxg6 $1 Kf8 28. Rc7 $18) 27. Rxe8 Bxe8 28. Bh6 Rf7 29. Qxd3 gxh6 30. b3 {White will retrieve the knight on a1 and Black will have to give up at least a pawn to avoid losing one more of his uncoordinated pieces.} Na3 31. Qg3+ Rg7 32. Qe5 Bd7 33. Qxa1 b4 34. Qf6 $18 {The queen is much stronger than the black army that is spread all over the board.}) 24. Qg4 $1 {After reaching a favourable position, Tal rushes in.} (24. Qg3 $143) ({It was better to defend the bishop with} 24. g3 $1 {[%cal Gf3b7] , maintaining the threat of Qb7, as in a line above.} Nd6 (24... Bg6 $2 {allows} 25. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 26. Bh6+ Kxh6 27. Qxf8+ Kh5 28. Qxc5+ {, with a decisive material advantage.}) 25. Qc3 Nf5 26. Re1 $1 $14 {White wins material.}) 24... Bg6 25. Qe6+ Bf7 26. Qf5 Nc2 $1 (26... Bg6 $2 {allows the familiar combination} 27. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 28. Bh6+ Kxh6 29. Qxf8+ {[%csl Yd4][%cal Gf8c5]} Kh5 30. Qxc5+) 27. b3 {The position is unclear already, as Black has initiated a global retreat. But there are still dangers on the way to safety.} Bg6 $2 (27... Rd1+ $1 28. Kh2 Nd2 {would have led to approximate equality after} 29. Bxd2 (29. h4 Nf1+ 30. Kh3 Rd3+ 31. g3 $13) 29... Rxd2 30. Qxc5 {Black is not perfectly coordinated yet and he might lose a queenside pawn, leading to material equality.}) 28. Rxg7+ $1 {This works under the given circumstances, too.} Kxg7 29. Bh6+ Kxh6 30. Qxf8+ Kg5 31. bxc4 bxc4 {Due to the king's exposure, White has the advantage again.} 32. g3 $2 {But this is a surprising misjudgement. Since Black has a light-squared bishop, White should place his pawns on light squares when building up an attack. There are several ways of doing this:} (32. g4 $1 Rd7 33. Kh2 Rf7 34. Qd6 $16) (32. f3 $5) (32. Kh2 $5 {, preparing g2-g4, but maybe Kg3 is also strong, as after} h5 33. g4 $1 $16 hxg4 $2 34. h4+ $1 {leads to mate:} Kxh4 (34... Kh5 35. Qh8+) 35. Qh6+ Bh5 36. Qf6# {In all these lines we see that the knight is still out of play.}) 32... Be4 33. h4+ Kg4 $1 (33... Kg6 {allows White to return to the right path:} 34. f3 $1 Bd5 35. g4 {[%cal Rf8f5] , threatening Qf5+}) 34. Kh2 Bf5 $1 (34... Bd5 $4 35. f3+ Bxf3 36. Qc8+ Kh5 37. Qf5+ Kh6 38. Qg5# {Tal}) 35. Qf6 (35. f3+ Kxf3 36. Qxf5+ Ke3 37. Qxh7 c3 $44) 35... h6 36. Qe5 Re4 37. Qg7+ Kf3 {White will win the knight, but the black king is very active now, helping Black to establish a saving domination.} 38. Qc3+ Ne3 (38... Kxf2 $1 39. Qxc2+ Kf3 $11) 39. Kg1 Bg4 40. fxe3 h5 41. Qe1 Rxe3 $2 (41... Re6 $1 42. e4 c3 $11 {Tal} 43. Qxc3+ Kxe4 44. Qxc5 $145 $18 Kd3 {would have established a fortress (Tal)}) 42. Qf1+ $16 {, and Tal eventually won.} 1-0

