1. e4 {Mihail Marin} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 Bf8 12. Nf1 Bd7 13. Bc2 {White defends the e4-pawn, avoiding Black's standard plan based on c6-a5. In practice, this has proven as an effective plan, but releasing the pressure along the b3-f7 diagonal should not be too dangerous.} g6 {This move is quite popular at a high level, but has the drawback of being somewhat slow and allowing White to consolidate.} 14. Bd2 ({The more active} 14. Ng3 Bg7 15. Be3 {is also good.} Qb8 {The queenside plan looks logical, but White has excellent development and should be able to retain an edge.} (15... Qe7 16. Qd2 Qf8 {is solid, but passive.} 17. Rad1 Rad8 18. Qc1 Bc8 19. a4 $14 {Gavrikov,V (2550)-Kuzmin,G (2520)/Tallinn 1985/MCL (1-0, 40)}) 16. Qd2 Kh7 17. Rad1 (17. b3 a5 18. Rac1 Qb7 19. Nh4 b4 $132 {De Wit,M (2306)-De Vreugt,D (2452)/Netherlands 2001/EXT 2002 (0-1, 40)}) 17... b4 $6 (17... a5 $5 18. Bb3 Nd8 19. a3 a4 20. Ba2 c6 $14 {Black is somewhat passive, but enjoys satisfactory stability.}) 18. c4 $2 (18. dxe5 $1 dxe5 19. Bb3 $16) 18... exd4 19. Nxd4 Qb7 20. f4 Rad8 $132 {Linskiy,O (2315)-Lesiege,A (2480)/Quebec 1996/EXT 98 (0-1, 63)}) 14... Bg7 15. Qc1 Kh7 16. Ng3 Na5 17. b3 c5 18. Be3 (18. d5 $14) 18... cxd4 19. cxd4 Nc6 20. Qd2 exd4 21. Nxd4 d5 22. exd5 Nxd5 23. Be4 Nxe3 24. Nxc6 Bxc6 25. Qxd8 1/2-1/2

