{ChessCalisthenics: 'B66: Sicilian: Richter-Rauzer: 7...a6 8 0-0-0 h6' Gary Lane: 'Here is another brilliant example showing that it can be a costly experience to neglect your development, as after the opening the attack is easier to prepare: '} 1. e4 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin Tal,Mihail''} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bg5 {Gary Lane: 'This is known as the Richter-Rauzer Attack. '} e6 7. Qd2 Be7 8. O-O-O a6 9. f4 Qc7 {Gary Lane: '?!' Gary Lane: 'After this sensational game the opinion of this queen move dipped dramatically and other ideas were formulated instead.'} ({Nowadays,} 9... Nxd4 10. Qxd4 b5 11. Bxf6 gxf6 {is the most popular line with top grandmasters.}) (9... Bd7 {!?}) 10. Be2 (10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. f5 $14 {Gary Lane: 'Bronstein,D-Suetin,A Moscow 1978 Tal,Mihail: ''' '' Bronstein - Suetin, Moscow, 1978}) 10... Nxd4 $6 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' Gary Lane: 'This releases the tension and allows White to exert pressure along the d-file. ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '?!'''} (10... Bd7 {%03'better is'} 11. Nb3 Rd8 ({maybe} 11... O-O-O {should be considered} 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Bh5 {Tal}) 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Bh5 b5 14. f5 O-O 15. Qh6 Ne5 16. Nd4 b4 (16... Rc8 $5 {might be better, preventing the c3-knight from moving due to the threat of mate on c2}) 17. fxe6 Bxe6 18. Nf5 $1 Bxf5 19. Nd5 {led to a big plus in M.Leon Hoyos-R.Folkeringa, Guingamp-Pabu 2010.}) (10... O-O 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. Nxc6 Bxc3 13. Qxd6 Qxc6 14. Qxc6 bxc6 15. bxc3 c5 16. Kd2 Bd7 17. Ke3 Ba4 18. Bd3 a5 19. Rb1 Rab8 20. Rhd1 g6 21. e5 Rfc8 22. Be4 Kf8 23. a3 Be8 24. Bb7 Rc7 25. Ba6 {Cuijpers,F (2497)-Klyuner,V (2395)/Germany 2004/EXT 2006/1-0 (46)}) 11. Qxd4 b5 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'Larsen played a rare line, featuring a not very inspired mixture of ideas. Ever since being a teenager, I had learned that ...Qc7 may be premature against many attacking systems. One way or another, Tal did not miss the opportunity to open the centre before the Black king got castled.'''} ({In the game B.Zminda-J.Hajnrych, Lubawka 2009, Black tried to avoid an attack by offering to exchange queens with} 11... Qc5 {, but it did not stem White's attacking prowess in the middlegame:} 12. Qxc5 dxc5 13. e5 $1 Nd7 14. Bxe7 Kxe7 15. Bf3 Rb8 16. Ne4 b6 17. Nd6 f6 18. exf6+ gxf6 (18... Nxf6 19. Rhe1 Bd7 20. f5 {is very strong}) 19. Rhe1 h5 20. Bc6 Nf8 21. Nxc8+ Rxc8 22. Bb7 {when the extra pawn gave White a winning ending.}) 12. e5 $1 {Gary Lane: 'Tal is keen to disrupt Black's pieces before the opponent can consolidate by castling. White is relentless in pursuing the king, hugely aided by the lack of defensive options available to Black due to having too many pieces still waiting to be moved from their starting squares.  ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!' GM Mihail Marin: '!'''} dxe5 13. fxe5 Nd5 $8 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: ''''} ({The attempt to fight back with} 13... Bc5 {backfires after} 14. Qf4 Nd7 15. Ne4 {with excellent play, especially since the pawn is taboo in view of} Nxe5 $2 16. Qxe5 $1 {, winning on the spot.}) 14. Bxe7 Nxc3 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'This was the move Larsen had relied on. Due to the threatened fork on e2, it may seem that White has to agree on mass exchanges and to spoiling his queenside structure.''' %08DA} (14... Nxe7 {ChessBase: '?' '?'} 15. Nxb5 $18 {!} axb5 {ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin'} 16. Bxb5+ Kf8 ({or} 16... Bd7 17. Qxd7+ {and wins}) 17. Qd8+ {ChessBase: 'mates.'} Qxd8 18. Rxd8# {.}) (14... Qxe7 15. Nxd5 exd5 16. Qxd5 Bb7 17. Qd6 $18) 15. Bf3 $1 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'Typically for him, Tal adds even more dynamic tension. A great part of both sides' pieces are hanging or in contact.''' !} Nxd1 {ChessCalisthenics: '??' Gary Lane: '?' Gary Lane: 'It is difficult to resist taking the rook but it soon turns out to be bad. The alternatives are hardly inspiring but at least there is a faint chance of Black surviving: ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'Black is a rook up and the bishop on e7 is hanging. But the knight on d1 is trapped and the rook on a8 is also hanging. Tal used to be very strong in these kind of piece confrontations.'''} ({Gary Lane: 'a)'} 15... Bb7 {Gary Lane: '?' Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'leads to interesting piece play, ending badly for Black:'''} 16. Bd6 $1 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!'''} {Gary Lane: 'or'} Bxf3 (16... Qd7 17. Qxc3 {and it is time for Black to count the pieces.}) 17. bxc3 $1 $18 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '+-!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Removing the threat .. .Ne2+ and winning a piece.''' '+-'}) ({Gary Lane: 'c) ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'The only practical chance was'''} 15... Ne2+ $1 { ChessCalisthenics: '!' Gary Lane: '(this is easily the best of the bunch) ChessBase: '' '' '' ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!' Tal,Mihail: '' GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: ', gaining some time to breathe. But White retains control in the centre anyway.'''} 16. Bxe2 Qxe7 {ChessCalisthenics: ''} 17. Bf3 Bb7 18. Bxb7 Qxb7 19. Qd6 Rc8 (19... Qxg2 $2 20. Qd7+ $1 (20. Rhg1 Qb7 21. Rxg7 $16) 20... Kf8 21. Rhg1 Qh3 ({or} 21... Qe4 {when} 22. Rgf1 f5 23. exf6 {leads to mate}) 22. Qd6+ Ke8 23. Qc6+) 20. Rd2 Qc6 21. Rhd1 (21. Qa3 {is possible to avoid the ending}) 21... Qxd6 22. Rxd6 Ra8 23. Rb6 $16 {Gary Lane: 'with a clearly superior ending for White. ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '' GM Mihail Marin: '' GM Mihail Marin: ', with a promising endgame.'''}) ({b)} 15... Kxe7 {ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'exposes the king.''} 16. bxc3 $1 ({more convincing than} 16. Qh4+ f6 (16... Ke8 $2 {?} 17. Bc6+ {GM Mihail Marin: '!' '+-'}) 17. exf6+ gxf6 18. Qb4+ ({or} 18. Qh6 {!} Rf8 19. Bh5 {''} Rf7 $1 {with the idea} 20. Bxf7 Qf4+ $1 21. Qxf4 Ne2+ {and Black fights on}) (18. bxc3 {GM Mihail Marin} Ra7 19. Qh6 $18 {, with a decisive attack.}) 18... Kf7 19. bxc3 Ra7) 16... Rb8 (16... Bb7 {runs into} 17. Qd6+ $1) 17. Qh4+ f6 18. exf6+ gxf6 19. Qh6 Rf8 {and now} 20. Bh5 $1 {intending Qg7+ is a winner.}) 16. Bd6 $1 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!' GM Mihail Marin: '!'''} Qc4 {Gary Lane: 'ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'Hoping to exchange queens at the cost of returning his material advantage.'''} ({Gary Lane: 'The other queen moves do not help either:'} 16... Qd7 {Gary Lane: 'allows ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail''} 17. Bc5 $18 {Gary Lane: ', ChessBase: '+-''} (17. Bxa8 $18)) ({while} 16... Qa7 17. Bc5 {is hopeless for Black in view of ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: '+-''} Qb8 $2 18. Bc6+ {and mates.}) 17. Qb6 $3 $18 {Gary Lane: '!' Gary Lane: 'Despite being a rook down, Tal's immediate priority is to avoid the exchange of the queens so the attack can carry on.  ChessBase: '!' '!' 'Tal,Mihail: '+-!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Finally switching to a direct attack against the king.''' '+-'} (17. Qxc4 $6 {Gary Lane: 'Tal,Mihail: '?!''} bxc4 18. Bxa8 Ne3 $16 {Gary Lane: 'Tal,Mihail: '' GM Mihail Marin: 'would allow Black to fight on in a worse ending.''}) 17... Nf2 (17... Ne3 {Gary Lane: 'is met simply by ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail''} 18. Qxe3 $18 {Gary Lane: 'and White can look forward to taking the queen's rook next move. ChessBase: '+-''}) (17... Bd7 18. Bxa8 Nc3 19. bxc3 f5 $18) 18. Bc6+ {Gary Lane: 'Tal has an abundance of good moves but spots a clearly winning one and wraps up the game. ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'The most clear-cut win.'''} ({Gary Lane: 'Naturally'} 18. Bxa8 $18 {Gary Lane: 'was also very good, as ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '+-'''} Nxh1 {allows} 19. Bc6+ {ChessBase: '+-' '+-' '+-'} Bd7 20. Qb8# {.}) ({Black was hoping for the line} 18. Qxf2 {?} Qf4+ 19. Kb1 Bd7 {, though I don't really see much hope after} 20. Qb6 $1 Rc8 21. Bb7 {winning.}) 18... Bd7 19. Bxd7+ Kxd7 20. Qb7+ Kd8 21. Qxa8+ Qc8 22. Qa7 {Gary Lane: '!' Gary Lane: 'Black resigned due to the twin threats of mate on e7 and against the knight on f2. "My head is filled with sunshine." – Mikhail Tal, after he had won the World Championship Candidates tournament in 1959. ChessBase: 'GM Mihail Marin: 'Due to the threat Qe7 mate White wins the knight while also retaining the enemy king in the centre.'''} Qd7 23. Qxf2 $18 1-0

