{Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'AUTHOR Douglas Griffin (Aberdeenshire, Scotland) Competitive player since the age of 12, representing Scotland at all levels from Under-14 to senior international during 1980-1989. Inactive over-the-board since the mid-1990s, but remains a keen student of chess history, and diligent collector of classic chess literature in the English and Russian languages. Chess Informant associate.  FROM THE CHESS INFORMANT ARCHIVES VINTAGE TAL'S MAGIC Mihail Tal (USSR) v. John van der Wiel (Netherlands), Moscow Interzonal, 1982 The last of the three FIDE Interzonals organised during July-September 1982 was held in the Soviet Capital, Moscow, at the Hotel "Sport" on the city's Leninsky Prospekt. (This hotel, which was built for the 1980 Moscow Olympics, has since been demolished.) Garry Kasparov convincingly won this 13-round event with 10/13, 1 1/2 points clear of Alexander Beliavsky, who also qualified for the 1983 Candidates' matches. In 3rd-4th place were Mihail Tal and Ulf Andersson. In the 4th round of the tournament (played on 11th September), ex-World Champion Tal won a fine game in his best style against the Dutch International Master John van der Wiel, who at the start of 1982 had finished in 3rd-4th place in the tournament at Wijk aan Zee, and who would have his grandmaster title ratified later in the year. Tal (White) introduced a novelty at the 9th move, sacrificing his pawn at e5 for the initiative. Perhaps unwisely, Black accepted the offer, but within a few moves he was in serious trouble, and by move 22 it was all over. The game later appeared in Volume 34 of "Chess Informant" with notes by Mihail Tal.'} 1. c4 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas} Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. e4 Bb7 5. Bd3 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'Diagram [#] This continuation had been introduced 7 years previously, in the 43rd USSR Championship final (Yerevan, 1975), where it had brought Romanishin,Oleg M 2485 a sensational victory against Petrosian, Tigran V 2645, so we refer you to Chess Informant 20/32, where it is annotated by Oleg Romanishin and Adrian Mikhalchishin.'} c5 6. O-O Nc6 7. e5 Ng4 8. Be4 Qc8 ({Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'In the 9th match game Kortschnoj,Viktor 2645 - Polugaevsky,Lev 2620  (Candidates' Semi-final, Evian 1977 - 24/(23)), Black had instead tried'} 8... f5 $142 9. exf6 Nxf6 $13 10. Bxc6 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas} Bxc6 11. d4 {White gained the better chances. Polugaevsky later introduced the text move in the 3rd game of his 1980 Candidates' Semi-final match v. Kortschnoj (Buenos Aires, 1980) see 9.Re1.}) 9. d3 $1 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'N!?' Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'Diagram [#] A novelty.'} ({In the above-mentioned game v. Polugaevsky, Kortschnoj had preferred} 9. Re1 {(Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas) Kortschnoj,Viktor 2695 - Polugaevsky,Lev 2635, Buenos Aires (m/3) 1980 see Chess Informant (vol. 30/24). Interestingly, this had also occurred in the encounter Kuligowski,Adam 2450 - Van der Wiel,John 2465 in a European Team Championship qualifier in Holzoster am See 1981 - 32/22; it may be that Tal was aware of that game, and had prepared the continuation in the text.}) 9... Ngxe5 ({Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'or'} 9... f5 $5 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'This was later tried in the game Stohl,Igor 2335 - Salov,Valery, European Junior Championship, Groningen 1983/84'} 10. exf6 Nxf6 11. Bf4 Be7 12. Nb5 (12. Ne5 O-O 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. Bxc6 Qxc6 $10) 12... O-O 13. Bd6 Bxd6 (13... Qd8 $5 $13) 14. Nxd6 Qc7 15. Nxb7 Qxb7 $6 (15... Nxe4 $10) 16. Bxc6 Qxc6 17. d4 Rac8 18. b3 $14 {Stohl,I-Salov,V,Groningen 1982}) ({Black may also consider} 9... d6 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas}) 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. f4 Nc6 $6 (11... Bxe4 $5 12. Nxe4 Nc6 13. f5 Be7 $13) 12. f5 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'Diagram [#]'} g6 $6 ({Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'Black ought to prefer'} 12... Nd4) ({Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'or'} 12... Be7 $5 $13 {Tal Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: '!?'}) 13. Bg5 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: '!'} gxf5 ({Possibly} 13... Bg7 {(Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas) and if} 14. f6 Bf8 {should be tried.}) 14. Bxf5 $1 $40 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: '' Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'White now has a clear advantage.'} (14. Qh5 $5 {(Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas) could also be considered.}) 14... Be7 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: '?' Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'After this Black goes down without a fight.'} (14... exf5 $142 15. Qe2+ (15. Nd5 $6 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas} d6 16. Re1+ Ne5 $13 {is less clear.}) 15... Ne7 16. Rae1 Rg8 17. Bxe7 Rxg2+ 18. Qxg2 Bxg2 19. Bh4+ Be4 20. Nxe4 fxe4 21. Rxe4+ $16 {Tal} Be7 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas} 22. Bxe7 $16 {Tal assesses the position as clearly favourable to White; modern engines are less convinced!} ({After the engines' preference,} 22. Rxe7+ Kd8 23. Rfxf7 {Black avoids mate by} Kc7 $13 {; the situation is far from clear.})) 15. Qh5 $18 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: '+-!'} Bxg5 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'There is no longer any defence.'} ({Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'If'} 15... exf5 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'then'} 16. Rae1 Kd8 17. Nd5 $18) 16. Qxg5 Ne7 (16... exf5 17. Rae1+ Kf8 18. Qh6+ Kg8 19. Nd5 $18) 17. Be4 {b5 Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: '!'} Bxe4 ({No better are} 17... f5 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas} 18. Nb5 $1) ({or} 17... Bc6 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas} 18. Nb5 $1) 18. Nxe4 Qc6 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'Diagram [#] Tal now finishes the game in style.'} 19. Rxf7 $1 Kxf7 20. Qf6+ Kg8 21. Qxe7 Rf8 22. Rf1 $1 {Tal,Mihail; Griffin,Douglas: 'Black resigned.'} 1-0

