{Mikhail Tal: 'I spent a long time considering my first move, but not at the board. Somehow, I very much wanted to surprise the German Grandmaster, but a consideration of various opening moves showed that this was virtually impossible. Therefore I decided to choose 1 e4 in the hope that my fifth move would have some psychological effect, since it leads to a game which is less 'French' in character. '} 1. e4 {ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang' ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane''} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 (3. Nc3 {(Gary Lane) is the main alternative, when the Winawer with} Bb4 {is considered a major line. One of the reasons why 3 Nd2 is so popular is that it avoids such a heavily analysed variation.}) 3... c5 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: 'A solid line of the French, which is to be expected from a world renowned expert on the opening. '''} 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'In the last USSR Championship, this continuation (which, incidentally, Alekhine recommended in his time) occurred twice, in Karasev's games with Tseitlin and Vaganian.' ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '!?'' ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'In the last USSR Championship, this continuation (which, incidentally, Alekhine recommended in his time) occurred twice, in Karasev's games with Tseitlin and Vaganian.''} dxe4 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Uhlmann is one of those chess players who does not attempt to be too sophisticated, and, as a rule, plays the opening quickly. The fact that he spent more than 20 minutes over his fifth move showed that the psychological 'mine' had worked. ' ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '!?'' ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'Uhlmann is one of those chess players who does not attempt to be too sophisticated, and, as a rule, plays the opening quickly. The fact that he spent more than 20 minutes over his fifth move showed that the psychological 'mine' had worked. ''} (5... cxd4 6. Nxd4 Bd7 7. Nxc6 Bxc6 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 9. c4 Nf6 $2 ({in our preparations we were also happy with White's position after the stronger (according to the commentators)} 9... d4 10. O-O c5 11. f4) 10. Qa4 Qd7 11. e5 {with advantage to White.}) 6. Nxe4 Bd7 7. Bg5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'First and foremost development! The open nature of the position makes every tempo precious.'} Qa5+ ({On ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'On Gary Lane: 'Tal intended to meet'''} 7... Qb6 {White was planning ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'White was planning Gary Lane: 'by'''} 8. Qe2 cxd4 9. O-O-O {. ChessBase: '' '' '' '' ChessBase: 'Tal' ChessBase: 'Tal Mikhail Tal: '. Gary Lane: 'with the initiative.'''}) ({Not} 7... Be7 $2 {(Gary Lane) due to} 8. Nd6+ $1 Kf8 9. Nxb7 Qb6 10. Nxc5 {and White is certainly on top.}) 8. Nc3 cxd4 {Mikhail Tal: 'In view of the threat of d5, this exchange is practically forced.'} 9. Nxd4 ({I very much wanted to sacrifice a piece here: ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'I very much wanted to sacrifice a piece here:''} 9. Bxc6 Bxc6 10. Qxd4 Bxf3 11. gxf3 {but in the first place I couldn't see anything concrete in the variation ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'but in the first place I couldn't see anything concrete in the variation''} (11. Qb4 $10) 11... Qxg5 12. Qa4+ b5 {ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' '!'} 13. Nxb5 Qe5+ 14. Kf1 Ke7 {and secondly, Black need not accept the sacrifice, but can play without danger, say, 11...Qb4. This second factor persuaded White to cut short any further searching. ChessBase: '' '' '' '' ChessBase: 'Tal' ChessBase: 'Tal Mikhail Tal: 'and secondly, Black need not accept the sacrifice, but can play without danger, say, 11...Qb4. This second factor persuaded White to cut short any further searching.'' =}) 9... Bb4 $6 {ChessBase: 'xc3?' ChessBase: 'xc3? Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '?!' Gary Lane: 'Black spots a chance to win a pawn, but this hinders development which was not a good idea against Tal who already had a reputation for giving away material – so a mere pawn would hardly be noticed as long as the position was encouraging an attack.'''} ({ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'During the game I considered that''} 9... Be7 {ChessBase: '' '' '' '' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'was sounder for my opponent, against which I was planning''} 10. Be3 (10. Qd2 {!} Nf6 ({not, of course, ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'not, of course,''} 10... Bxg5 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '?''' ?!} 11. Qxg5 Nxd4 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '?'''} 12. Bxd7+ {ChessBase: '+-' '+-' '+-' '+-' ChessBase: '(Tal 71)' ChessBase: '(Tal 71) Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: 'wins due to the discovered attack on the black queen'''} Kxd7 13. Qxa5 Nxc2+ 14. Ke2 Nxa1 15. Qb5+ Kc7 16. Rd1 Nf6 17. Qc5+ Kb8 18. Qd6+ Kc8 19. Nd5 Nxd5 20. Rc1+ Nc2 21. Rxc2+ Nc3+ 22. Rxc3#) 11. O-O-O {with at any rate a sharp struggle. ChessBase: '' '' '' '' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'with at any rate a sharp struggle.'' ''} Rd8 {Gary Lane} 12. Nb3 Qc7 {with equal chances, T.Horvath-W.Uhlmann, Austrian Team Championship 2005.}) (10. Z0 {Gary Lane}) (10. Bxe7 {Gary Lane} Ngxe7 11. O-O O-O (11... Qb4 {can be safely met by} 12. Nb3) 12. Nb3 Qc7 13. Re1 {also gave Black a perfectly acceptable position in P.Rooze-W.Uhlmann, World Seniors Championship, Bad Zwischenahn 2008.}) 10... Qc7 $11 {ChessBase: '=' '=' '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Uhlmann Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '=''}) 10. O-O {ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '!' Gary Lane: 'White carries on getting his pieces into the action, while making sure his king is out of the way. '''} Bxc3 11. bxc3 Qxc3 $4 {Mikhail Tal: '?' Mikhail Tal: 'Most probably the whole idea (borrowed from other variations) of exchanging the dark-squared bishop is bad as there are already too many open files and diagonals! Capturing the c-pawn simply forces White to begin an immediate attack. Evidently, in considering the likely reply 12 Nf5, the German Grandmaster overlooked something.' ChessBase: 'This optimistic pawn snatch results in weakening the dark squares and in a lack of development. Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '??'' ChessBase: 'This optimistic pawn snatch results in weakening the dark squares and in a lack of development. Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '??' Mikhail Tal: '?' Mikhail Tal: 'Most probably the whole idea (borrowed from other variations) of exchanging the dark-squared bishop is bad as there are already too many open files and diagonals! Capturing the c-pawn simply forces White to begin an immediate attack. Evidently, in considering the likely reply 12 Nf5, the German Grandmaster overlooked something. Gary Lane: 'If Black can get away with this then the extra pawn will prove to be useful. The warning signs are that White's pieces are well co-ordinated, while the black king is still sitting serenely on its starting square.''' ?!} ({Mikhail Tal: 'After the game' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'After the game Gary Lane: 'But if instead'''} 11... a6 {Mikhail Tal: 'was suggested, on which there can follow'} 12. Bxc6 Bxc6 13. Nxc6 Qxg5 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: ', then'''} 14. Qd6 {!} (14. Qd6 {ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '!'''} Nh6 15. f4 $1 {ChessBase: 'Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '!''} Nf5 16. Qc7 Qf6 17. Qxb7 O-O 18. Rf3 $16 {ChessBase: 'Uhlmann'}) {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: 'or'''} 14... Ne7 (14... Qd5 {Gary Lane} 15. Qxd5 exd5 16. Rfe1+ Kf8 ({not} 16... Kd7 $2 17. Ne5+ Ke7 18. Ng6+ {and wins}) 17. Nd4 Nf6 18. Reb1 $1 b5 19. a4 {and the ending favours White.}) 15. Rfd1 $1 {!} Nxc6 (15... bxc6 16. Qd7+ Kf8 17. Qd8+) 16. Qd7+ Kf8 17. Qxb7 $18 {Mikhail Tal: 'with advantage to White.' ChessBase: 'Tal Mikhail Tal: 'with advantage to White.'' ''}) 12. Nf5 $3 {Mikhail Tal: '!' ChessBase: '[#]' ChessBase: '[#] Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '!!' Gary Lane: 'This is a typical Tal move, which offers a piece in return for a lightning attack. I think it is safe to say that Black missed the depth and strength of the attack, because he apparently sank into thought for one hour and fifty minutes. '''} exf5 {Mikhail Tal: 'While it is clear that the opening of another file should lead to a win for White, it is even more obvious that refusing the sacrifice would also lead to Black's defeat. '} ({ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The refusal of the sacrifice leaves Black in dire straits. For instance:''} 12... Qc5 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane'' ?} 13. Nd6+ Kf8 14. Nxb7 (14. Be3 Qe5 (14... Qd5 15. Nxb7 Nf6 16. c4) 15. Rb1 (15. Nxb7 Qxb5 16. Qxd7 Nd4 17. Qd6+ Ne7 18. Bxd4 Qxb7 19. Rab1) 15... Rb8 16. Re1 {with a big advantage.}) 14... Qxb5 15. Qxd7 Qxg5 16. Qxc6 $16 {ChessBase: '(Uhlmann 71) Uhlmann,Wolfgang: '''}) (12... Qe5 {?} 13. Nd6+ Kf8 14. Nxf7 {!}) 13. Re1+ ({White could have also considered the immediate} 13. Qd6 {.}) 13... Be6 14. Qd6 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: 'The position is clearly awkward for Black – even if White did not calculate every possible line it is clear that, with so many pieces lined up against the king, something has to be right. Perhaps the key point is that it is far from obvious how Black can extract his king from such a perilous position without losing significant material. ''' ! 'with the idea' 15.Rad1} a6 {Mikhail Tal: 'There is nothing better. White's main threat is not so much 15 Rad1, but to transfer his bishop (again in analogy with other variations) onto the now open a3-f8 diagonal. Black is unable to prevent this.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'However, on' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'However, on Gary Lane: 'The natural move'''} 14... Nf6 {Mikhail Tal: 'White was nevertheless intending to play' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'White was nevertheless intending to play Gary Lane: 'is fatally undermined by'''} 15. Rad1 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: ', when White has a decisive grip on the position because the black king cannot move away from danger; e.g.''' '+-' 'with the idea' 16.Bd2 Qc2 17.Bb4} ({since} 15. Bd2 Ne4 {is not so clear.}) {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'or''} 15... h6 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane''} (15... a6 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane''} 16. Bd2 (16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. Rxe6+ fxe6 18. Qxe6+ Kf8 19. Rd7 Qa1+ 20. Bf1 {and Black will be mated.}) 16... Qxc2 17. Bb4 Nd5 18. Rxe6+ fxe6 19. Qxe6+ Kd8 20. Ba5+ b6 21. Bxb6#) 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. Rxe6+ $1 fxe6 18. Qxe6+ Kf8 19. Rd7 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'winning''} Qa1+ 20. Bf1 $18 {ChessBase: 'Uhlmann'}) (14... Rc8 15. Rxe6+) 15. Bd2 $1 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '!' Gary Lane: 'A crafty idea: the bishop changes diagonals to support the cause of checkmate.'''} ({Weaker was ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Weaker was Gary Lane: 'Instead,'''} 15. Ba4 {ChessBase: '?!' '?!' '?!' '?!' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '?!'''} b5 16. Bd2 Qc4 17. Bb3 Rd8 $1 {!} 18. Qc7 Rd7 {. ChessBase: '=' '=' '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Tal' ChessBase: 'Tal Mikhail Tal: '. Gary Lane: 'is equal according to Tal.'''}) 15... Qxc2 16. Bb4 $1 {!} ({Here there was a false trail: ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Here there was a false trail: Gary Lane: 'It would be wrong to proceed with the obvious'''} 16. Rac1 {ChessBase: '?' '?' '?' '?' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '?' Gary Lane: 'because of the shocking'''} Qxc1 {ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane: '!' Gary Lane: ', when the tactics suddenly go against White; e.g.'''} 17. Rxc1 axb5 18. Rxc6 Rd8 $1 {. ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Tal' ChessBase: 'Tal Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: '. Gary Lane: 'and White is in big trouble.''' ! 'with the idea' 19... bc}) 16... axb5 17. Qf8+ Kd7 18. Red1+ {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Accuracy to the end.' ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' '!'} (18. Rad1+ Kc7 19. Qxa8 Nf6 20. Bd6+ Kb6 21. Qxh8 Ne4 {would have given Black a little counterplay. ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'would have given Black a little counterplay.'' ''} 22. Bg3 $16) 18... Kc7 {Mikhail Tal: 'Now, however, on' ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Now, however, on Gary Lane: 'Black's cause is hopeless. For example:'''} 19. Qxa8 Nf6 (19... Qa4 {ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Gary Lane''} 20. Bd6+ Kb6 21. Qf8 Qh4 (21... Qg4 22. Bc5+ Kc7 23. Qd6+ Kc8 24. Bb6 $18) 22. Bc5+ Kc7 23. Rab1 (23. Rac1 Qf6 24. Qd6+ Kc8 25. Bb6 Qe7 26. Qd8+ Qxd8 27. Rxd8# {. "Tal doesn't move the pieces by hand; he uses a magic wand." – Viacheslav Ragozin.}) 23... b4 24. Bd6+ Kb6 25. Bxb4 Nge7 26. Bxe7+ Ka6 27. Qxh8 $18) (19... Z0) 20. Qxh8 Ne4 {there follows the simple ChessBase: 'Mikhail Tal: 'there follows the simple''} 21. Be1 {, and so the German Grandmaster stopped his clock, on which there remained a minute and a half. ChessBase: '+-' '+-' '+-' '+-' ChessBase: 'Tal' ChessBase: 'Tal Mikhail Tal: ', and so the German Grandmaster stopped his clock, on which there remained a minute and a half.'' '+-'} 1-0

