{Tal had met the Sveshnikov Sicilian with the then rare 7.Nd5 (nowadays almost the mainline). From a structural point of view, Black has an excellent position, as his pawns are more mobile than White's, but his queenside pieces are cut off and all of White's pieces are optimally placed for a kingside attack. From this perspective, the next move is just logical.} 22. g4 $1 e4 $2 {Hoping in vain that his chances to defend were better in a blocked position.} (22... fxg4 $2 {runs into} 23. f5 $16 {, gaining control over e4 and weakening Black's kingside.}) (22... exf4 {was the only chance to stay in the game.} 23. Rxe8+ (23. gxf5 Rxe1 24. Rxe1 {allows} g5 $1 {, followed by the consolidating . ..Ne5.}) 23... Qxe8 24. Bxf4 Bxc3 {The only chance to include his queenside pieces into play.} 25. Qxc3 Bxd5+ 26. Kg1 {Due to Black's weaknesses on the dark squares, White has obvious compensation for the pawn, but since the white king is also exposed, Black is still kicking.}) 23. gxf5 gxf5 24. Be3 {Blocking the e-pawn and regrouping for an attack.} Nc5 25. Rg1 {With the simple plan Rg3, R1g1, Bd4 and later Ne2-d4/ g3.} Nd3 (25... Kh8 26. Rg3 Bf6 27. Reg1 Rg7 28. Rxg7 Bxg7 29. Ne2 Bc8 {Preparing to meet Nd4 with ...Bd7.} 30. Ng3 {, followed by Nh5, with a decisive attack.}) 26. Ref1 Qh4 27. Rg3 Kh8 28. Bxb6 {With a material advantage already.} Rf7 29. Be3 Rf6 30. Qg2 Rg6 31. Rxg6 hxg6 32. Qxg6 Rg8 33. Rg1 Nxf4 34. Qg5 (34. Qf7 $1 {is more convincing.} Nh3 35. Rg2 Bc8 (35... f4 36. Rg4) 36. Ne2 $18 {Black is almost paralysed and can do little against White's natural attack.} a5 37. Rg3 $5 {puts Black in Zugzwang. For instance} Qh7 38. Bd4 {wins the knight soon.}) 34... Qxg5 35. Rxg5 Bxc3 36. Rxg8+ Kxg8 37. Bxf4 {The black pawns are blocked and the extra pawn will decide.} Be5 $5 (37... Bb4 38. Bh5 Kg7 39. Be8 Bc8 40. Kg2 Kf6 41. h4 $18) 38. Bxe5 dxe5 {Black's pawns did not gain more mobility while White's did.} 39. Kg2 Kg7 40. Bc2 Kg6 41. Kf2 Kg5 42. Ke3 1-0

