1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 ({The other occasion on which Tal succeeded in generating the kind of open tactical position which I assume he sought proceeded:} 3... Bf5 4. h4 h5 5. Ne2 $5 e6 6. Ng3 g6 (6... Bg6 7. Be2 c5 $132) 7. Nxf5 gxf5 8. c4 c5 $5 {An interesting decision - Black seems to cooperate in the opening of the game in exchange for material gain.} 9. cxd5 Qxd5 10. Nc3 Qxd4 11. Qf3 Nc6 12. Bb5 Nge7 13. Bg5 Qxe5+ 14. Kf1 Bh6 15. Re1 Qd4 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Qxf5 Rad8 18. Rh3 {Tal,M-Botvinnik,M/Moscow 1961/MainBase (33) With a sharp tactical battle and decent chances against Black's king.}) 4. dxc5 e6 5. Qg4 Nc6 6. Nf3 Qc7 7. Bb5 Bd7 8. Bxc6 Qxc6 9. Be3 Nh6 10. Bxh6 gxh6 11. Nbd2 Qxc5 12. c4 {Continuing the policy of trying to open the play. Black has the bishop pair, but the clear intention is that discomfort to his king will prove the principal factor.} O-O-O 13. O-O Kb8 14. Rfd1 $36 {and again Tal has realised his aim of opening the position and establishing a certain initiative. Sadly for the great tactical magician, in the other encounters Botvinnik's attempts to keep the game closed were much more successful!} *

