{Finally we will examine a classical game, illustrating the best romantic tradition of this type of sacrifice. The remarkable thing is that it was a rapid game, but if we look at the name of the player commanding the black army, there is nothing to be surprised about. ***Now, it is a good moment to talk about the relative character of an advance in development. In the first phase it looked as if White achieved quicker development, but after his mistake it will be the other way around.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. e4 O-O 6. Nf3 d6 7. h3 {In this game h2-h3 was played quite early, being a part of the theoretical variation. The mistake comes only much later, when White will underestimate Black's attacking potential.} a6 8. a4 e6 9. Bd3 exd5 10. exd5 Re8+ 11. Be3 Nh5 12. O-O Nd7 13. Qd2 Ne5 14. Nxe5 Rxe5 {Diagram [#] Black is very active, but strictly speaking White is better developed. Vladimirov must have perceived only the latter aspect, underestimating the former one.} 15. Bh6 $2 {Wasting a tempo, misplacing the queen and weakening the dark squares. Do we need more to call this move a mistake?} ({The developing} 15. Rfe1 {or}) (15. Rae1 {would have been better.}) 15... Bxh6 16. Qxh6 {Diagram [#]} Bxh3 $3 17. gxh3 Qh4 {Once again there is no immediate re-capture on h3 and actually there will never be.} 18. Kh2 $6 {Still underestimating the danger.} ({The best chance is} 18. Ne4 {even though after} Rxe4 19. Bxe4 Qxe4 $15 {Black has fantastic compensation for the exchange.}) 18... Rg5 {A brilliant tactical idea. Black traps the queen and creates the threat ...Qf4+ followed by ...Rg1+.} ({But completing the development with} 18... Rae8 {might have been just a bit stronger. White's queen cannot retreat as this would allow ...Nf4 and the same threats as in the game (...Rg5) remain valid. And if} 19. Ne4 Rxe4 20. Bxe4 Rxe4 21. Qd2 Rxc4 $19 {Black is clearly winning, as this is a more active version of the similar exchange sacrifice above.}) 19. Ne2 {Defending f4.} Re8 {Diagram [#] Preparing to remove the defender.} 20. Rae1 {After this move White is clearly lost.} ({He could have continued struggling with the far from obvious resource} 20. Ra3 $5 Rxe2 21. Bxe2 Qf4+ 22. Kh1 {Black does not have anything better than} Rg1+ 23. Kxg1 Qxh6 24. Bxh5 Qxh5 $17) 20... Rxe2 21. Bxe2 Qf4+ 22. Kh1 {Diagram [#]} Qe4+ $3 {This move makes all the difference! With the rook on a3 this check could have been parried with Bf3. But the way it is, White has to weaken his kingside even more and remove the pressure on the enemy knight.} (22... Rg1+ $17 {would be less ambitious.}) 23. f3 Qh4 {Diagram [#] The game is over. White has to sacrifice the queen under bad circumstances, as the enemy queen and knight will maintain decisive threats against the white king.} 24. Qxg5 Qxg5 25. Rg1 Qd2 26. Kg2 Nf4+ 27. Kf1 Nxh3 28. Rg4 h5 29. Re4 Qg5 30. Bd1 Qg1+ 31. Ke2 Qf2+ 32. Kd3 Qxb2 0-1

