{Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'B40: Sicilian: 2...e6, Unusual lines '} 1. e4 {ChessBase II ChessBase II Komodo 9 64-bit (20s) ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I' ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s) Keres,Paul; Nei,I Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II''} c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. b3 {'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The Finnish master adopts this move fairly often; indeed, two years ago here in Tallinn, he defeated L. Stein in this variation. Looking through some chess magazines literally half an hour before the start of the game, I came across the game Westerinen-Kaplan, Skopje 1972, which White had lost in 20 moves, and without checking any further I decided to follow the Puerto-Rican. ''''} Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. Bb2 Be7 6. c4 Nc7 ({ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'Of course, the normal continuation is'''} 6... Nb4 $1 {ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!'' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '(ChessBase II) Mikhail Tal: ', but after all, Kaplan had won quickly ...'''} 7. Nc3 {ChessBase II} f5 {½-½ Westerinen,H (2465)-Hartston,W (2480) Nice ol (Men) fin-A 1974 (3)}) 7. Nc3 f6 8. Ne4 fxe5 9. Nxe5 O-O {'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: 'At Skopje in this position Westerinen continued 10 Qg4?, and after 10...Ne8 Black quickly seized the initiative, as within two moves (11...d6 and 12...e5) the activity of the bishop on b2 was curtailed. Of course, it was extremely naive to expect that the Finnish master would repeat the game to the end. ''''} 10. d4 $1 {ChessBase II: 'N' 'N' 'N' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!'''Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: 'N' ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: 'It turns out that White has a certain advantage on his side.'''' 'N'} (10. Qg4 {ChessBase II: '' '' '' ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II'' ''} Ne8 { d6, e5 ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: ' d6, e5''}) 10... cxd4 ({On} 10... d6 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) (ChessBase II) there could follow} 11. Nf3 cxd4 12. Qxd4 e5 13. Qe3 {, and his pressure along the d-file gives White excellent prospects.}) 11. Qxd4 Bb4+ 12. Kd1 $2 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move. White loses the right to castle' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '?'' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '?' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move. White loses the right to castle''} ({ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'Westerinen is a player with a very active style, and he is not satisfied by the continuation'''} 12. Bc3 {ChessBase II: '' '' '' ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II'' ''} Nc6 {ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I'} 13. Nxc6 Bxc3+ 14. Qxc3 bxc6 $14 {ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): ', which only gives him a slight positional advantage. White's desire to keep his bishop also has a positional basis, but Westerinen's technical solution of the problem is inaccurate''}) ({ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Much more dangerous for Black was''} 12. Ke2 $5 {ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '(ChessBase II) . The difference becomes noticeable immediately.''}) 12... d6 $1 $17 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black threatens to win material: d6xe5' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black threatens to win material: d6xe5 ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'It is surprising, but apparently this simple move escaped Westerinen 's attention.'''' ''} 13. Nd3 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White has a mate threat'} ({ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'It turns out that after''} 13. Nc6 {ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II''} e5 $19 {ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White loses a piece. If White's king was on e2, then possible would be 13 a3 dxe5 (there is no other move) 14 Qd8 Rxd8 15 axb4 Nc6, with an unclear ending. Incidentally, in this variation 14 Qxe5 is also worth considering.''}) 13... e5 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black threatens to win material: e5xd4'} 14. Qe3 Ba5 $1 {ChessBase II: '' '' '' ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: ''Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: '' ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Yet another distinction. In this variation also, White's king would be better placed on e2, since he would then have the move Rd1.'''' !'' ''} ({The position of the king on the d-file caused Black to consider the immediate} 14... d5 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) (ChessBase II) , but I decided that after} 15. Nxb4 dxe4+ 16. Kc2 {White would succeed in completing his development. The modest move in the game is considerably more unpleasant as now the threat of the advance of the central pawn must be taken seriously.}) 15. Qg5 Qd7 $17 {ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: ''''Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Of course, the exchange of queens would also have given Black a certain advantage, but the queen is not badly placed on d7.''''} 16. Kc2 {ChessBase II: '?!' '?!' '?!''Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: '?!''' '?!'} Qc6 $1 {ChessBase II: 'Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!'''Keres,Paul; Nei,I: '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: 'Mikhail Tal: '!''''} ({Black spent quite a considerable time examining the immediate} 16... b5 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) (ChessBase II) and sharp variations such as} 17. Rd1 bxc4 18. Nxe5 Qf5 19. Bxc4+ d5 20. Rxd5 {etc, but then I noticed the apparently awkward queen move, which, as becomes clear, leads to a big, and perhaps decisive, advantage. Black straight away develops his pieces with gain of time, while c6 is not the only post for his knight.}) 17. f3 ({It is easy to see that White gains nothing by} 17. Nxd6 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) ChessBase II} Qxd6 18. Bxe5 Qd7 {.}) 17... Bf5 {ChessBase II: '' '' '''Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'ChessBase II: '''' ''} (17... Nba6 {ChessBase II ChessBase II: 'Komodo 9 6