{[%evp 0,121,21,29,60,41,69,52,56,40,26,23,40,49,52,44,48,34,38,37,37,29,1,2,2,-1,-7,-3,-3,-5,-6,-14,-31,-83,-83,-83,-83,-89,-63,-89,-45,-67,-42,-33,-29,-46,-59,-52,-68,-76,-68,-82,-36,-32,-32,-40,-47,-133,-110,-151,-55,-37,-37,-69,-69,-36,30,30,63,66,66,66,62,62,48,39,39,42,62,62,62,84,141,123,115,88,88,86,83,21,126,130,105,84,98,101,116,101,135,42,14,41,23,-342,-342,-342,-344,-336,-334,-437,-437,-603,-568,-572,-572,-567,-605,-567,-575,-575,-340,-382,-374,-374] Out of many great games played by ''The Best of the West'', I decided to annotate this one for two reasons. Firstly, it was an exciting game between the 6th and 3rd rated players in the world at that moment, with many tactical twists and turns. Secondly, it is an excellent example of Timman's versatile playing style - there were not many people in the world who could grind Ulf Andersson in a technical endgame while also being able to prevail against Tal in a sharp tactical position. True, in 1988, the ex-world champion was far from his prime attacking years, but he was still a force to be reckoned with.} 1. e4 {Ernst,T} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 {Timman has had a pretty wide opening repertoire throughout his rich career. The Classical Sicilian remained one of his pet defenses against 1.e4.} 6. Bg5 e6 7. Qd2 Be7 8. O-O-O Nxd4 9. Qxd4 O-O 10. e5 $5 {These days, this aggressive move does not have a great reputation for White, 10.f4 being pretty much the exclusive choice of strong players, but White had scored pretty well with it in the pre-engine times. Tal was surely acquainted with this idea from the old Soviet days since strong players like Kortschnoj and Vasiukov, as well as great attackers like Nezhmetdinov and his countryman Vitolinsh, have employed it with success.} dxe5 11. Qxe5 Bd7 {Black should not delay his development.} ({One of the classical games in this variation, 1-0 (55) Vasiukov,E-Boleslavsky,I Kiev 1957, continued:} 11... Qb6 $6 12. Be3 $1 {!?} Ng4 13. Bxb6 Nxe5 14. Bc7 $1 Ng4 15. Bg3 {'' and Black suffered in the endgame.}) 12. h4 $1 {According to my database, the Soviet international master Aronin was the first one to carry out this idea in this position. It is not just to support the g5-bishop, but also to lift the rook to the third rank via h3, from where it can join the game very effectively.} (12. Bd3 Ng4 {!} 13. Bxe7 Nxe5 14. Bxd8 Rfxd8 {=}) (12. Bb5 Qe8 13. Bxd7 Nxd7 14. Qg3 Bxg5+ 15. Qxg5 Nf6 16. Rd3 h6 17. Qe5 Qb8 {= Van Der Wiel - Leko, Wijk aan Zee, 1994}) 12... Qe8 $5 {!? An ambitious move, typical of Timman's general approach with black pieces. It should also be noted that Tal had a pretty comfortable tournament situation with a +1 score before this game, while Timman was on a hardly satisfying -1 score.} ({Thus, he was probably not eager to go for a slightly inferior, if pretty safe-looking, endgame after} 12... Rc8 13. Rh3 Qc7 14. Qxc7 Rxc7 15. Nb5 Bxb5 16. Bxb5 {which happened in the game ½-½ (70) Prandstetter,E (2430)-Trepp,M (2405) Prague 1987 one year earlier.} h6 17. Be3 Nd5 {=}) 13. Rh3 $146 {Technically, this move is a novelty, but it is really just a natural continuation of the abovementioned plan.} ({Two years earlier, Agdestein played the less critical} 13. f3 Rc8 14. Be3 a6 15. Bd4 Bc6 16. Be2 Qd8 17. Kb1 Qc7 18. Qxc7 {and a 'grandmaster draw' was agreed in ½-½ (18) Agdestein,S (2545)-Sosonko,G (2530) Jerusalem 1986}) (13. Qg3 Kh8 14. Bd3 a5 15. Ne4 Nxe4 16. Bxe4 $13) 13... Bc6 {[#]} {This may have been the critical moment in the opening. Tal began making threats on the kingside with} 14. Rg3 {Ernst,T: '?!' Ernst,T: 'Besser ist 14.Ld3 Sd7 15.De2 Sc5 16.Lc4 a6 17.Tg3 Kh8 18.Le3 Tg8 19.h5 mit Vorteil für Weiss, Sarink - Hovde, Fernpartie 1989.'} ({However, a serious alternative existed on the other side of the board:} 14. Nb5 $5 {Black has a pretty wide choice of options to defend against the fork on c7, but not all of them are good. In my view, the most interesting one is} Rd8 $5 ({In a later game, Black played} 14... Nd5 $6 {½-½ (45) Santo Roman,M (2420)-Relange,E (2445) Paris 1994.} {White could have gotten a nice advantage by simply playing} 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. Rg3 $16 {which would force some serious kingside concessions from Black.}) (14... Bxb5 {is a simplistic solution, but after} 15. Bxb5 Qb8 16. Qxb8 Raxb8 $14 {White gets the kind of risk-free plus in the endgame that we have already discussed.}) (14... Ng4 {is an attempt to solve the problems by force:} 15. Qf4 Bxb5 16. Bxb5 Bxg5 17. hxg5 Qxb5 18. Qxg4 Rfd8 {Here, the position of the rook on h3 gives White two good options. Of course, the more attractive one is} 19. Rdh1 {playing for checkmate,} ({Alternatively, White could try to squeeze a slightly better endgame after} 19. Rhd3 Rxd3 20. Rxd3 {etc.}) {although the engine indicates that Black can hold the balance with} 19... Rac8 $1 {Still, I don't think that many people would willingly enter this kind of position with Black.}) ({Finally, the engine also suggests} 14... Qc8 {with the idea to bring the other rook to d8. It looks scary from a human point of view to allow} 15. Nc7 Rb8 16. Rg3 {even though it is supposedly fine for Black after} Rd8) 15. Rxd8 Qxd8 16. Nxa7 Bd5 $132 {Black is planning to make his own threats with this pawn sacrifice, and we enter a position where both sides have to play accurately. A particularly interesting position arises after the best continuation:} 17. c4 $1 (17. Nb5 h6 18. Bd3 $1 Qa5 {also looks very complicated.}) 17... Qa5 18. cxd5 Qxa2 $1 {where Black can hold a dynamic balance with active play.}) 14... Qb8 $1 {A cool and strong defense.} (14... Kh8 15. h5 {!?} (15. Be3 {!? 'with the idea' 16.Bd4}) 15... Qb8 16. Qe3) 15. Qe3 {Naturally, Tal wants to keep the queens on the board.} ({The tactical basis of Timman's last move is} 15. Bxf6 $2 {?} Bxf6 16. Qxf6 Qxg3 $1 {'-+' and White loses material.}) (15. f4 {was also an option, but it would hardly promise White much after} Nh5 {followed by the queen trade on e5.}) (15. Qe1 {!?} Rd8 16. Bd3) (15. Qe2 {!?}) (15. Qxb8 {!?} Raxb8 16. Bb5 Bxb5 17. Nxb5 a6 18. Nd4 Rfd8 19. Rgd3 Rbc8 20. Kb1 $13) 15... Rd8 16. Bf4 $2 {?! The main criticism of this move is that it allows Black to seriously disrupt White's piece coordination.} (16. Bd3 {!? is a move that White would want to make, of course, but Tal probably disliked} Bd6 {when 17.f4 runs into 17...Nh5. However, it turns out that he had a good follow-up in:} (16... Kh8 17. h5 $36) (16... Qc7 17. Bh6 {!} Nh5 18. Bxg7 {!} Nxg3 19. Be5 $36) 17. Bxf6 $1 (17. Rh3 {?} Bxg2) (17. f4 {?!} Nh5 {!} 18. Rg4 Re8 {'with the idea' f5}) 17... Bxg3 (17... Bf4 18. Rxg7+ Kf8 19. Rxh7 $18) 18. Ne2 $1 {! Such backward moves are very difficult to spot when you are attacking. The point behind it is to take the f4-square under control and keep attacking despite the material deficit. Who knows, maybe a younger Tal would have found it?} ({Alternatively,} 18. fxg3 gxf6 19. Qh6 {may not prove to be enough attack to compensate for the exchange after} f5 (19... Rxd3 {!} 20. Rxd3 Qe5 $17) 20. Qg5+ Kf8 21. Re1 Rd6 $1 {planning to recentralize the queen via the 8th rank.}) (18. Bxh7+ {?} Kxh7 19. Rxd8 Bf4 $19) 18... Rxd3 $1 {Ernst,T: '!' A counter-exchange sacrifice is Black's best option, not only because it eliminates a potentially dangerous bishop, but also because it clears the 8th rank for the queen, similar to what Timman did in the game.} (18... gxf6 {keeps the material advantage, but it exposes the king, so after} 19. Nxg3 Kf8 (19... Qe5 20. Qh6 f5 21. Nh5 $18) 20. Re1 $1 $36 {White already poses some pretty serious threats.} (20. Qh6+ Ke7 21. Re1 Rd5 22. Nf5+ Rxf5 23. Bxf5 $16 {Tal})) (18... Bxf2 19. Qg5 (19. Qxf2 gxf6 20. Qxf6 Rd5 21. Qh6 $18) 19... Be3+ 20. Qxe3 gxf6 21. Qh6 f5 22. Nf4 $40) 19. Rxd3 Bc7 20. Qg5 Qf8 {After the best defense by Black, we still have a sharp position with chances for both sides.}) (16. Rxd8+ Qxd8 17. Bd3 (17. Bh6 Nh5) 17... Qa5 18. Kb1 Rd8 19. a3 e5 $13) 16... Rxd1+ 17. Nxd1 Qf8 {Timman's cool-headed opening strategy leaves a strong impression. Besides some forced piece exchanges, none of his pieces has even crossed the 5th rank so far. Yet, despite this apparently passive play, his position is already better! The key is in the superior piece coordination of his pieces compared to those of his opponent.} (17... Bd6 {?} 18. Rxg7+ Kxg7 19. Qg3+ $18) (17... Nd5 18. Rxg7+ {!} Kh8 (18... Kf8 19. Rg8+ {!}) 19. Rxh7+ Kxh7 20. Qd3+ $18) (17... Qd8 {!? Tal}) 18. Be5 (18. Bh6 {?} Nh5 19. Rg4 f5 {!} 20. Qxe6+ Qf7 $19) 18... Nh5 {Timman decides that it is finally time to push white pieces back.} ({Besides this direct move, it was also possible to resolve the pressure on g7 with} 18... g6 $5 $17 {preparing to move the knight more centrally - to d5 or d7, depending on what White does.} 19. Kb1 Nd7 20. h5 Nxe5 21. Qxe5 $15) 19. Rg4 $5 {?! Tal doesn't want to back up, lurking for a chance to complicate the game. The rook will be very exposed on the fourth rank,} ({but one can understand how a safer alternative} 19. Rh3 {!? 'with the idea' g4 didn't satisfy the great attacker. Black simply dominates positionally after} Rd8 $17 {etc.} (19... Nf6) (19... Bc5 20. Qg5 g6 {?} 21. Qxh5)) 19... f5 20. Rc4 {[#] A very interesting moment. Black can obviously win material with 20...Bb5, yet Timman decided not to call Tal's bluff as he played} Nf6 {! instead. Of course, recentralizing the knight with a tempo (21...Ng4 is threatened) is a good idea in such a sharp position,} ({however, it would be curious to see what the players have seen after} 20... Bb5 {since, objectively speaking, White does not seem to have a way to justify his exchange sacrifice. Perhaps, it was the variation} 21. Qf3 {!} ({In the event of} 21. Nc3 Bxc4 22. Bxc4 Kh8 23. Nb5 {Black is just in time to consolidate with} Nf6 $1 24. f3 Nd7 $19) ({while} 21. Bc3 Bxc4 22. Bxc4 Kh8 23. Bxe6 Bb4 {also looks just winning for Black.}) 21... Bxc4 22. Bxc4 {that Timman disliked. Several black pieces are hanging and White might just obtain good compensation on the light squares. However, a very nice concrete defense} Qf7 $1 (22... Nf6 23. Bxe6+ Kh8 24. Qxb7 $16 {Tal}) 23. Qb3 {''} Re8 $1 $19 {, indirectly defending both e6 and b7, outright refutes White's idea.}) 21. Bd4 {! While there were probably a couple of alternatives for each side along the way, the following sequence seems logical and forced enough:} (21. Qb3 {?!} Bd5 22. Ne3 Nd7 23. Nxd5 exd5 24. Qxb7 Nxe5 25. Qxd5+ Nf7 $15 {/}) 21... Bd5 {!?} (21... Kf7 {!?}) (21... Qf7 {'with the idea' Ng4} 22. Qe1 Ne8 23. g3 Nd6 24. Rc3 Ne4 $15) 22. Ra4 Ng4 23. Qe2 Bxh4 (23... Qf7 {!?}) 24. Nc3 Bc6 $1 {!} (24... Bg5+ 25. Kb1 a6 26. f3 Nh2 27. Nxd5 exd5 28. Qe6+ Qf7 29. Qxf7+ Kxf7 30. Bb5 Rc8 $15) 25. Rxa7 ({The e6-pawn is poisoned in the view of} 25. Qxe6+ $2 Kh8 26. Ra5 Bg5+ ({but not} 26... Re8 $2 27. Qxf5 {when Black also has issues with his back rank.}) 27. Kb1 Re8 $19) 25... Rd8 $5 {So, the material is equal again, but Black remains with a superior piece coordination. White rook is particularly awkwardly placed on a7 and with this clever intermezzo, Timman forces Tal to decentralize his bishop, as well. Needless to say, the e6-pawn remains taboo considering White's back rank issues.} (25... Re8 {!?} 26. g3 Bf6 27. Bxf6 Qxf6 28. f3 Qd4 29. Ra5 Ne3 $13) 26. Bb6 {Ernst,T: 'Der Bauer e6 ist vergiftet wegen der Mattdrohung auf der ersten Reihe.'} (26. Qxe6+ Kh8 27. Qc4 (27. Bb6 Bg5+ 28. Kb1 Re8) 27... Rxd4 {?} (27... Qe8 $17 {!}) 28. Qxd4 Bxf2 29. Qd6 {!}) 26... Re8 27. a3 {White needs to waste time playing like this because 27...Qb4 was a pretty serious threat,} ({as we can see in the variation} 27. g3 Qb4 $1 28. Ba5 Bg5+ 29. f4 Bxf4+ $1 30. gxf4 Qxf4+ 31. Kb1 Qd4 $19) 27... Qd6 28. g3 {!} (28. f3 Bxf3 29. Qxf3 Qxb6 30. Qxb7 Qe3+ 31. Kb1 Re7 32. Qa8+ Kf7 33. Rxe7+ Bxe7 34. Bb5 Qg1+ 35. Ka2 Ne3 36. Qe8+ Kf6 $17) 28... Bd8 $1 {! Another high-class move, keeping the momentum.} (28... Bf6 {would allow White to take a breather with} 29. Nb5 Qd7 ({or, in the case of} 29... Qd5 30. c4 $1 (30. c3 Be5 31. Qd1 Qa2 32. f4 Bf6 $13) 30... Qh1 31. Nd6 $132) 30. Qd3 $132) 29. Nb5 {! White cannot afford to exchange the bishops on d8 because his rook would remain vulnerable.} (29. Bxd8 Qxd8 $19 {with 30...Qb6 next, should be decisive.}) 29... Qd7 $5 {?! Again, a subtle move.} ({It was also possible to play} 29... Qd5 {at once, but perhaps Timman did not fancy playing a similar position with a hanging e6-pawn after:} 30. Bxd8 Rxd8 (30... Qxd8 $13) 31. Nc7 Qh1 32. b3 {Here, the direct} Nh2 $2 {does not work because of} 33. Qxe6+ Kh8 34. Qe7 Rg8 35. Ra8 $1 Qxf1+ 36. Kb2 $11 {but Black is winning on positional grounds with a simple 32...Be4 or 32...Qh6+.}) (29... Qe7) 30. Bc5 Qd5 {The queen is ready for the invasion!} (30... Bf6 31. Nd6 Rd8 32. f3 Be7 $13) 31. Qc4 Qh1 {[#] White should regret not developing his light-squared bishop in the opening. Its inactivity has been a source of positional problems ever since and now it runs into very concrete tactical ones. Black threatens to win it with 32...Nh2 or 32...Bg2.} (31... Qf3 {!?} 32. Qd3 Bg5+ 33. Kb1 Rd8 34. Qxf3 Bxf3 $13) {Tal tries to make the most out of a pretty desparate situation with a threat of his own:} 32. Nd6 {and, as so many times in his career, the magic had worked! Firstly, Timman missed a forced win when he played} Bd5 $6 {! As a matter of fact, this move does not spoil Black's decisive advantage, but it does make things practically more challenging because he is virtually obliged to sacrifice an exchange on the next move.} ({The winning continuation was based on simple tempo-play:} 32... Ne5 33. Qe2 Bf3 $1 34. Qb5 (34. Qe1 Bg5+ 35. Kb1 Bd2 36. Qxd2 Qxf1+ 37. Qc1 Qxc1+ 38. Kxc1 $14) 34... Bg5+ 35. Kb1 Rd8 {Black has achieved a full piece harmony and, even though he may not be winning the bishop by force after} 36. Ka2 {, he is just positionally winning because white pieces seem like they were randomly tossed around the board. The most clinical way to wrap up the game would be} Bd5+ $1 37. b3 Bc6 38. Qe2 Bf6 $19 {and White could not last much longer with the chekmate threat on a1 hanging over his head.}) 33. Qe2 Be7 $2 {Ernst,T: 'Schwarz ist verloren, denn wenn der Turm nach f8 zieht folgt c4 mit Eroberung des Bauern e6.' ! This follow-up, however, is a pretty serious mistake, letting the win slip.} ({Truth be told, calculating the forced win after} 33... Nh2 $1 {was far from a simple task, and would probably have to be played largely on intuitive basis. White grabs the material with} 34. Nxe8 {and, after} Nxf1 {, he has several attempts to protect his king. The most stubborn one is} 35. f4 (35. Qe1 {fails to nice tactics:} Bg5+ 36. f4 ({or} 36. Be3 Nxe3 37. Qxh1 Bxh1 $19) 36... Nxg3 $1 37. Qxh1 Bxf4+ {The key intermezzo.} 38. Kd1 Bxh1 39. Ra8 Kf7 {It is hard to imagine the endgame with 4 passed pawns being anything but winning for Black.}) ({Black also wins in the case of} 35. b3 {with} Nxg3+ 36. Qd1 Bg5+ (36... Ne2+ 37. Kd2 Bg5+ 38. Be3 Bxe3+ 39. fxe3 Qxd1+ 40. Kxd1 Nc3+ 41. Ke1 e5 $14) 37. Be3 Bxe3+ 38. fxe3 h5 $1 {Only this precise move secures a decisive advantage. White pieces are too far away to stop the h-passer.}) {Now Black would need to find a long sequence of only moves to obtain a winning position.} 35... Ne3+ $1 (35... Nxg3+ $6 {is much less clear after} 36. Qd1) 36. Kd2 Nc4+ 37. Kc3 Qa1 38. Rxb7 $1 {A tricky counter-tactic, but there is an antidote.} Ba5+ $1 (38... Bxb7 39. Qxe6+ Kh8 40. Nc7 Qxb2+ 41. Kxc4 Qxc2+ $11 {most likely ends with a prepetual check.}) 39. Bb4 Qxb2+ 40. Kd3 Bxb4 {It would be far from simple to calculate even this far from the initial position on move 33. It is obvious that Black keeps the initiative which ever way White recaptures on b4 now. However, it would be a tall order even for a top player like Timman to find the only winning moves 41...e5! and, particularly, 42...Qa7!! (in the lines below) from the starting position. The position is simply too complicated to calculate precisely that far. In that sense, one can understand why he went for the relatively more straightforward 33...Be7.} 41. Rxb4 (41. axb4 {looks like a decent defense, until Black finds} e5 $1 {simultaneously removing the implicit Qxe6 threat and preparing to close the mating net on the white king with 42...e4! White is forced to enter the line:} 42. Rxg7+ Kf8 43. fxe5 Nxe5+ 44. Ke3 {but after} Qxb4 $19 {he is bound to lose a lot of material due to the exposed king and loose pieces.}) 41... Qxa3+ 42. c3 {Here, Black can win only with a powerful quiet move} Qa7 $3 $19 {preparing to capture the e8-knight with ...Kg8-f8 next. Deep stuff.}) (33... Rf8 34. c4 $18 {!}) (33... Re7 34. Ra8 $18) 34. Nxe8 Bxc5 35. Ra8 {!} (35. Ra5 Bxf2 36. Nc7 Qh6+ 37. Kb1 Be4 38. Ra8+ Kf7 39. Qd1 Qf6 40. Bc4 $13) 35... Kf7 36. Nc7 {[#] If we compare this position to the one 5 moves earlier, it becomes obvious that White's chances have improved considerably - he is not only up an exchange, but his pieces coordinate much better than before, creating some threats around black king. Of course, Timman didn't go for this position with Black for no reason, either, since all his pieces remain very active. However, it feels like the momentum of his middlegame initiative is gone. It is, therefore, not surprising that he decided to simply collect a pawn with} Bxf2 {!?} ({instead of keeping things complicated with} 36... Be4 $5 {This would allow White to counterattack with} 37. Qc4 {but Black has a cool defense} Nf6 $1 {when suddenly it is not clear whose king is more exposed. White's best try would be} 38. Rd8 $1 {bringing the rook back into defense via the d-file. All other more 'forcing' moves would lose:} (38. Qxe6+ Kg6 39. Qc4 Bg2 $19) (38. Nxe6 Bd5 39. Ng5+ Kg6 40. Qxc5 Qxf1+ 41. Kd2 Kxg5 $19) (38. Qxc5 Qxf1+ 39. Kd2 Bf3 $1 40. Rf8+ Kg6 $19) {After} 38... Bxf2 39. Nxe6 Kg6 40. Rd1 Qf3 $13 {we would get a very tricky position with chances for both sides. I leave you the pleasure of analyzing it more deeply on your own!}) (36... Nh2 {!} 37. Qh5+ Ke7 {!} 38. Nxd5+ {!} exd5 39. Re8+ {!} Kd6 40. Re1 Bxf2 41. Rd1 Be3+ 42. Kb1 Qf3 43. Qxf3 Nxf3 44. c3 {!} Ke5 45. Bg2 Nd2+ 46. Kc2 Ne4 47. a4 $14) 37. Nxd5 (37. Rd8 Be3+ 38. Kb1 Ke7 39. Rg8 Kf7 40. Rd8) 37... Qxd5 38. Kb1 {[#] ! The downside of 36...Bxf2 is that it allowed Tal to completely neutralize potential threats to his king by trading off Timman's strong light-squared bishop. It is now White who has a better chance of dominating on the light squares, whereas Black is banking on his excellent control of the dark-squares and potentially dangerous mass of kingside pawns. Thus, the position remains double-edged, despite many piece exchanges that took place in the preceding tactical phase. And right at the start of the new phase of the game, Timman commits a big mistake.} (38. Qb5 Qe4 39. Kb1 Nf6 40. Rb8 Qe1+ 41. Ka2 Bxg3 42. Rxb7+ Kg6 $13) 38... Qh1 $2 {?! It is difficult to understand the reasoning behind this move. Perhaps time trouble played its part. Black doesn't even have a threat and, to make things worse for him, White can suddenly tuck his king away on a2.} ({He would have liked to take the pawn with} 38... Bxg3 $2 {but this would run into} 39. Bg2 {'' when the crucial b-pawn would be lost.}) ({Therefore, the most logical move (among a couple of decent alternatives) would have been} 38... b6 {simultaneously protecting the pawn and winning a tempo on white rook. The position would remain dynamically balanced, as we can see in the following sample variation:} 39. Ra7+ Kf6 40. Ra6 h5 41. a4 $13) (38... e5 {!?} 39. Qc4 Qxc4 40. Bxc4+ Kg6 41. Rb8 h5 42. Rxb7 Bxg3 $14) 39. Ka2 Qd5+ {!} (39... Bxg3 40. Bg2 {'with the idea' Bb7$16}) (39... h5 40. Qd3 Nf6 41. Be2 Kg6 42. Bf3 Qe1 43. Bxb7 Bxg3 $14) 40. c4 {! Basically, Timman lost two full tempi with this back-and-forth queen maneuver. In such a concrete position, this makes a big difference. For the first time in the game, Tal is in the driver's seat.} (40. Qc4 Qxc4+ 41. Bxc4 Bxg3 42. Ra7 Kf6 43. Rxb7 Ne3 44. Kb3 h5 $13) 40... Qc6 (40... Qh1 41. Rd8 h5 42. Qd3 Kg6 43. Rd6 Kf6 44. Be2 $16) 41. Bh3 {The light-squared bishop makes its debut on the 41st move - how often do you see that!?} Bd4 (41... b5 42. Bxg4 Qxa8 43. Qxf2 bxc4 44. Bf3 Qd8 45. Qe3 Qd3 46. Qxd3 cxd3 47. a4 Ke7 48. a5 $18) 42. Bg2 $5 {Ernst,T: '?!' The decision not to trade on g4 was probably the right one from a practical point of view.} ({When playing over this game for the first time, I wondered why Tal did not simply chop off the knight with} 42. Bxg4 {!} fxg4 {but it turns out that it is not that easy to bring the rook into the game because of Black's strong centralization. He would also have ideas like ...Qf3, and potentially dangerous counterplay with the kingside pawns. The engine, of course, remains unfazed and points out some resources that are supposed to promise White a nearly winning advantage, but I don't think that it would be so simple in an actual game. For example:} 43. Qd3 (43. Rd8 {Ernst,T !?} e5 44. b4 $18) (43. Rh8 h6 44. Rd8 Bf6 45. Rd1 h5 46. Rf1 $16) (43. Ra5 {!?}) 43... Bf6 44. Ra5 g6 45. c5 h5 46. Rb5 Bd4 $1 $132 {and it's still a fight!}) 42... Qc7 43. Qd3 Bb6 $2 {After this passive move, White's position should be technically winning. I suppose that Timman wanted to cover the d8-square, keeping the rook out of play.} ({However, it was essential to look for counterplay against the g3-pawn with} 43... Bf2 {Black position remains critical, but at least he would have a fighting chance in the case of} 44. Qd8 ({and, particularly, after} 44. Rd8 $2 Ne5 45. Qd6 Qxd6 46. Rxd6 b6 $132) (44. Rh8 Nf6 45. b4 b6 46. g4 fxg4 47. Kb3 $14) 44... Qxc4+ 45. Kb1 Nf6 46. Rc8 Qg4 $16) (43... Qb6 {?} 44. b4 $18) 44. Bf1 {?! A nice bit of schematic thinking. It makes sense to free up the queen by overprotecting the c4-pawn, after which it can invade the weak light squares via b3, a4/b5, etc.} ({Having said that, it should be noted that Tal could have broke through Black's defensive barrier even more quickly if he had played} 44. Qb3 $1 Ne3 45. c5 $1 {forcing the capture of the b7-pawn. In the endgame that arises after} Bxc5 46. Qxb7 Qxb7 47. Bxb7 Bd6 48. a4 Bxg3 49. a5 $18 {he would have a much quicker outside passed pawn.}) (44. b4 {!? 'with the idea' c5} Ne5 45. Qc3 Nxc4 46. Kb3 Bf2 (46... Nd6 47. Qxc7+ $18) 47. Bf1 b5 48. Bxc4 bxc4+ 49. Qxc4 Qxg3+ 50. Ka4 $16 {Tal}) (44. Rh8 h6 45. Qe2 Bd4 46. Bf3 Nf6 47. a4 $14 {/}) 44... Ne3 {Timman goes for the most ambitious setup,} ({although, objectively speaking, it was probably better to keep the knight closer to the king by playing} 44... Ne5 {so that after} 45. Qb3 Kf6 $16 {it would be better covered against attacks of his heavy pieces.}) 45. Be2 e5 46. Qb3 {?!} (46. g4 g6 47. b4 Bd4 $14) 46... Bc5 47. Qb5 Kf6 48. Qe8 Be7 {There was really not much that Black could do against the queen invasion, but at least his king looks safe enough. At the moment, a bigger problem are his pawns. Tal had a perfect opportunity to exploit this fact, but he chose the wrong target when he played} (48... Qd6 49. Rd8 Qe7 50. Qxe7+ Kxe7 51. Rc8 b6 52. Rc7+ Kf8 53. b4 Be7 54. Rb7 Bd8 $16) 49. Qg8 $2 {The h7-pawn is not so important - it is the b7-pawn that is the key to White's victory.} ({Therefore, the right continuation was} 49. Rc8 {!?} Qd6 ({or} 49... Qb6 50. Qd7 e4 51. Rb8 $18 {also winning the b-pawn.}) 50. Qb5 {and if Black launches a counterattack with} Qd2 {then, among several winning alternatives, the most convincing one is} 51. Qxb7 $1 Qxe2 52. Rc6+ Kf7 53. Qd7 $1 $18 {with irresistible threats to the black king.}) (49. Qc8 {!?}) (49. Qh5 {!?}) 49... e4 $1 {Black is not going to get another chance to win the g3-pawn, so this move simply begs to be played. Perhaps the fact that Tal missed or underestimated this resource was already a sign that his concentration began faltering at this stage of the game.} 50. Qxh7 (50. g4 {!?} Nxg4 51. Qxh7 Ne3 52. Rg8 Bd6 53. Qh4+ Kf7 54. Qh8 $14) 50... Qxg3 {[#] [#] [#] Ernst,T: 'Nun fängt Schwarz endlich an, besser zu stehen und im nächsten Zug macht Weiss den entscheidenden Fehler.' Suddenly, things are not that clear at all. As usual in such positions, Black king feels pretty safely guarded by the mass of pawns that surround it. In some cases, it can even confidently move up the board to e5/g5-f4, etc. White pieces are uncoordinated again and now he also needs to pay attention to opponent's connected passers. Timman was probably already happy with such a turn of events, but Tal's next move must have brought him a huge relief.} 51. Ra5 $4 {?? A blunder. While maneuvering his rook to the ideal b5-square, Tal missed a simple double attack on his two undefended pieces. He calculated so well almost the entire game, but only one moment of carelessness was enough to lose it.} ({It would have been much more interesting to see how this terrific tactical fight would have continued if he had played a better move like} 51. Ra7 $13) ({or} 51. Re8 $13 {but it was not to be.}) (51. Qh5 Qf2 52. Rg8 Qe1 53. Qh2 Kf7 54. Rh8 $13) 51... Qe1 {Ernst,T: '-+' '-+'} 52. Rb5 {There is nothing to be done, the piece is lost.} (52. Bh5 Qxa5 53. Qg6+ Ke5 $19) 52... Qxe2 {Timman collected the piece and, soon thereafter, an important pawn, and that was the end of it.} 53. Rb6+ Kf7 54. Qg6+ Kf8 55. Rxb7 Qxc4+ 56. Ka1 Qf1+ 57. Ka2 Qc4+ 58. Ka1 Qc1+ 59. Ka2 Qc8 60. Qb6 Qc4+ 61. Kb1 {Here, the game ended with 0-1, which probably means that Tal had resigned before the second adjournment.} ({Indeed, after} 61. Kb1 Qd3+ 62. Ka2 Kf7 63. Qc7 Nd5 {there is nothing left to play for - Black wins trivially. This was by no means a perfect game, but more like a street fight between two great players in which Timman came out on top after making the next-to-last mistake. To paraphrase Tal himself, he did ''take his opponent into a deep dark forest where 2+2=5, and the path leading out is only wide enough for one'', but this time it was not him who found the way out!}) 0-1

