1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 cxd5 5. Nf3 (5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 {is the suggested repertoire in this course}) 5... Nc6 6. Bf4 Bf5 (6... Nh5 $5 {This move is one of the main reasons why I prefer to keep my knight on g1 and play e3 instead.} 7. Bd2 (7. Bg5 {black can go after the bishop pair with} h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Nxg3 10. hxg3 Bg7 {Black has very good results here}) 7... e6) 7. e3 e6 8. Bb5 Bb4 9. Ne5 Qa5 $6 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. O-O $1 {completing development. Black now has to deal with the threat of Nxc6} Bxc3 12. bxc3 Qxc3 $2 13. Qc1 $2 (13. Rc1 Qa3 14. g4 Be4 15. f3 Bg6 16. Rxc6 O-O 17. h4 h6 18. Nxg6 fxg6 19. Qc2 $18) 13... Qxc1 14. Rfxc1 O-O $2 (14... Nh5 $1 15. Bg3 (15. Nxc6 Nxf4 16. exf4 f6) 15... Nxg3 16. hxg3 f6 17. Nxc6 Kd7) 15. f3 h6 (15... Nh5) 16. Nxc6 Rfe8 17. a4 Nd7 18. Bd6 Nb6 19. Bc5 {A critical moment.} Bd3 $2 (19... Nd7 {was correct. The point is that after} 20. Bxa7 Kh7 $1 {black has won the a-pawn but his pieces are coordinating badly.} 21. Bc5 Ra6 22. Nb4 Ra5 {when it is difficult to hold on to the extra a-pawn}) 20. Nxa7 Rxa7 $6 (20... Nd7) 21. Bxb6 $18 {I would have thought that black has some drawing chances in such an endgame but Leela gives a whooping +2.2 advantage to white here.} Ra6 22. a5 Bc4 23. Ra3 f6 24. e4 Kf7 25. Kf2 Raa8 26. Ke3 Reb8 27. Rac3 Rc8 28. g4 Rab8 29. h4 {Botvinnik is choosing to open a second front on the kingside. This is all very instructive, white's advantage on the queenside is not enough to win so weaknesses on the kingside are needed to make further progress.} Rc6 30. h5 Rbc8 31. e5 g6 32. hxg6+ Kxg6 33. R3c2 fxe5 34. dxe5 Rh8 35. Rh2 Rcc8 36. Kd2 Bb3 37. a6 Bc4 38. a7 Rh7 39. Ra1 Ra8 40. Be3 Rb7 41. Rxh6+ Kg7 42. Rah1 Rb2+ {1-0 Black resigns.} 1-0

