{A.Martin: 'A brevity. Tal tries out a sacrificial idea of Nezhmetdinov to devastating effect. Bobotsov seems completely ill at ease throughout the game.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The following game still brings me happy memories of my first years in chess, as I still remember how the simplicity and beauty of Tal's play left a strong impression on me as a child.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Die folgende Partie weckt in mir immer noch glückliche Erinnerungen an meine ersten Schachjahre, denn ich weiß noch, wie die Einfachheit und Schönheit von Tals Spiel einen starken Eindruck auf mich als Kind gemacht hat.'} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The Sämisch King's Indian.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The solid Saemisch Variation seems a wise choice against the Magician from Riga.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Die solide Sämisch-Variante scheint eine kluge Wahl gegen den Zauberer aus Riga zu sein.'} O-O 6. Nge2 (6. Be3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'suppressing ...c5 is more commonly played here:' and und} e5 7. d5 Nh5 8. Qd2 Qh4+ 9. g3 Nxg3 {(at this point, I thought my opponent had blundered)} 10. Qf2 (10. Bf2 $4 {hangs material to} Nxf1 $1 {: three unfortunates fell into this trap in my database}) 10... Nxf1 11. Qxh4 Nxe3 {. --- Compare this line with Tal's sacrifice. Black picks up the c4-pawn and gets two minor pieces and two pawns for the queen, with reasonable practical chances - although at the IM/GM level, the comps have worked it out to an advantage for White. In C.Lakdawala-S.Trump, Montreal, 1976. I grossly underestimated Black's initiative, thinking the benefits of Black's sacrifice are not commensurate with the high price tag. Of course, I went on to get clobbered.}) (6. Bg5 {are more popular, as both moves keep White's development more flexible. sind beliebter, da beide Züge die Entwicklung von Weiß flexibler gestalten.}) 6... c5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black looks for Benoni structures and play on the dark squares now.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Schwarz sucht jetzt nach Benoni-Strukturen und spielt auf den dunklen Feldern.'} (6... e5 $6 {doesn't seem good now, as it blocks the dark-squared bishop and White is very solid in the centre, making the typical f7-f5 advance less effective. scheint jetzt nicht gut zu sein, denn es blockiert den schwarzfeldrigen Läufer und Weiß steht im Zentrum sehr solide, was den typischen f7-f5-Vorstoß weniger effektiv macht.} 7. Bg5 c6 (7... h6 8. Be3 Nc6 9. d5 Ne7 10. Qd2 Kh7 11. g4 $18 {seems positionally lost for Black. White has a big space advantage, and Black's counterplay with f7-f5 is not working here. scheint für Schwarz positionell verloren zu sein. Weiß hat einen großen Raumvorteil, und das schwarze Gegenspiel mit f7-f5 funktioniert hier nicht.}) 8. Qd2 Nbd7 9. d5 $16 {Although some strong players went for this position as Black, I still think White's position should be more comfortable and easier to play. Obwohl sich einige starke Spieler für diese Stellung als Schwarz entschieden haben, denke ich immer noch, dass die weiße Stellung bequemer und einfacher zu spielen sein sollte.}) 7. Be3 ({Nowadays, the most principled option is Heutzutage ist die prinzipiellste Option} 7. d5 e6 8. Ng3 exd5 9. cxd5 a6 10. a4 {with a complex game, in which Black tries to develop dynamic, active play before White is able to complete the development and consolidate a comfortable positional advantage. mit einer komplexen Partie, in der Schwarz versucht, dynamisches, aktives Spiel zu entwickeln, bevor Weiß die Entwicklung abschließen und einen komfortablen positionellen Vorteil konsolidieren kann.}) 7... Nbd7 {Quintiliano,Renato: '?!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Armed by the opening knowledge of our times and also by my strong computer, I think this is a bit dubious.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Bewaffnet mit dem Eröffnungswissen unserer Zeit und auch mit meinem starken Computer, denke ich, dass dies ein wenig zweifelhaft ist.'} ({However, I can understand why Tal opted for the most modest development of his knight to d7. In case of Ich kann jedoch verstehen, warum Tal sich für die sehr bescheidene Entwicklung seines Springers nach d7 entschieden hat. Im Falle von} 7... Nc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is a much disputed theoretical sacrifice:' which is indeed the most played, White could make his life simpler by means of was in der Tat das Meistgespielte ist, könnte Weiß sich das Leben einfacher machen mittels} 8. dxc5 ({Still, the most principled is Die prinzipiellste ist jedoch} 8. d5 Ne5 9. Ng3 e6 (9... h5 10. Be2 h4 11. Nf1 e6 {leads to a Benoni-like position, with mutual chances, R.Ponomariov-M.Carlsen, Medias 2010.}) 10. Be2 exd5 11. cxd5 a6 12. a4 h5 $1 $13 {again with Benoni-type positions and a sharp game. wieder mit Benoni-typischen Positionen und einem scharfen Spiel.}) 8... dxc5 9. Qxd8 Rxd8 10. Bxc5 {Although both theory and tournament practice have proved that Black has enough compensation for the pawn here, It's fair to assume that Tal was not looking for such a calm queenless middlegame. Obwohl sowohl die Theorie als auch die Turnierpraxis bewiesen haben, dass Schwarz hier genügend Kompensation für den Bauern hat, kann man davon ausgehen, dass Tal nicht auf ein so ruhiges damenloses Mittelspiel aus war.} Nd7 11. Ba3 Nde5 12. Nf4 Nd4 {and Black's control over the central dark squares offers compensation for the pawn, J.Granda Zuniga-P.Glavina, San Fernando 1991.}) 8. Qd2 ({In case of Im Falle von} 8. d5 {Black can transpose to the previous note with kann Schwarz zur vorherigen Anmerkung überleiten mit} Ne5 ({It's also possible that Tal was looking for Benko-like play with Es ist auch möglich, dass Tal auf ein Benkö-ähnliches Spiel aus war mit} 8... b5 $5)) 8... a6 9. O-O-O {A.Martin: 'Naturally its possible to block the centre, play might then proceed' Quintiliano,Renato: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One must be a person of vast self-confidence to provoke Mikhail Tal!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In "normal" circumstances this could be considered a careless move by White, since Black can prepare a dangerous breakthrough on the queenside, after which the white king will be in danger. Considering that on the other side of the board we have such an attacking genius like Tal, we can easily conclude that castling long is simply a mistake. Bobotsov likely understood the dangers of his position, but backed his decision on a nice tactical detail. Unfortunately for him, Tal also had seen the idea - and more.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Unter 'normalen' Umständen könnte dies als ein unvorsichtiger Zug von Weiß angesehen werden, da Schwarz einen gefährlichen Durchbruch am Damenflügel vorbereiten kann, nach dem der weiße König in Gefahr ist. Wenn man bedenkt, dass wir auf der anderen Seite des Brettes ein solches Angriffsgenie wie Tal haben, kann man leicht zu dem Schluss kommen, dass eine lange Rochade einfach ein Fehler ist. Bobotsov war sich wahrscheinlich der Gefahren seiner Stellung bewusst, aber er stützte seine Entscheidung auf ein nettes taktisches Detail. Zu seinem Pech hatte auch Tal die Idee gesehen - und mehr.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Far safer are'} 9. d5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'could be met by' Quintiliano,Renato: 'könnte begegnet werden mit'} Qa5 (9... b5 $5) (9... Ne5 10. Ng3 h5 $1 $36 {also seems promising for Black. scheint auch für Black vielversprechend zu sein.}) 10. Nc1 Re8 11. Be2 Ne5 12. h3 e6 13. f4 Ned7 14. dxe6 Rxe6 15. Bd3 b5 $17 {Furman-Ciocaltea Bucharest 1954 White's game is over-extended}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} 9. Rd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' was a safe option for White, increasing his control in the centre and keeping the possibility of castling to the kingside. war eine sichere Option für Weiß, die seine Kontrolle im Zentrum erhöht und die Möglichkeit der Rochade zum Königsflügel bewahrt.}) ({The best solution seems to be Die beste Lösung scheint zu sein} 9. dxc5 $1 dxc5 (9... Nxc5 10. Nd4 $14 {is possible, even though in Maroczy positions White often keeps a small edge. ist möglich, auch wenn Weiß in Maroczy-Stellungen oft einen kleinen Vorteil behält.}) 10. Rd1 Qc7 11. Nf4 $16 {[%cal Gf1e2];1-0 Sjugirov,S (2643)-Fedoseev,V (2668) Lake Sevan Martuni 2014 (2.4) White had no problems to complete the development and kept a pleasant space advantage. This seems the best way of posing problems for Black after 7...Nbd7. Weiß hatte keine Probleme, die Entwicklung abzuschließen und behielt einen angenehmen Raumvorteil. Dies scheint der beste Weg zu sein, um Schwarz nach 7...Sbd7 vor Probleme zu stellen.}) 9... Qa5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Natural and good, stepping out of unnecessary complications and preparing the advance b7-b5.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Natürlich und gut, geht unnötigen Komplikationen aus dem Weg und bereitet den Vorstoß b7-b5 vor.'} ({Tal likely considered the immediate Tal erwog wahrscheinlich das unmittelbare} 9... b5 $6 {but the complications work in White's favour: aber die Komplikationen wirken sich zu Gunsten von Weiß aus:} 10. dxc5 b4 (10... bxc4 $2 11. cxd6 Ne5 12. dxe7 Qxe7 13. Nf4 $16 {is simply better for White. ist einfach besser für Weiß.}) 11. Nd5 dxc5 ({But not Aber nicht} 11... Nxd5 $2 12. Qxd5 Rb8 13. cxd6 Nb6 14. Qc6 $16) 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. Nf4 $1 (13. Bxc5 Qc7 14. Bxb4 Nb6 15. Nf4 Bb7 $13 {[%csl Rc4][%cal Gf8d8,Gb6a4] can be dangerous for White. kann für Weiß gefährlich werden.}) 13... e6 14. Nd3 $14 {[%cal Yh2h4,Yh4h5] and White keeps better chances in view of his play in the centre and potential play on the kingside. On the other hand, Black is unable to get counterplay on the queenside here. und Weiß behält bessere Chancen angesichts seines Spiels im Zentrum und möglichen Spiels am Königsflügel. Andererseits ist Schwarz hier nicht in der Lage, Gegenspiel am Damenflügel zu bekommen.}) 10. Kb1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Black must watch out for Nd5 tricks...'} b5 {A.Martin: 'This is one of the points of Black's play. Bobotsov completely misses the other........' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '...which Tal totally ignores! Of course, a Benko-like Gambit is infinitely more tempting when your opponent castles long.'} 11. Nd5 {Diagram [#] A.Martin: '?' A.Martin: 'After what happens next, it is possible to put a big, fat question mark by this move, but how many of us would have fallen into Tal's trap ? If White is going to allow a Queen sac, he should take on c5 first e.g.' Quintiliano,Renato: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"After what happens next, it is possible to put a big, fat question mark by this move, but how many of us would have fallen into Tal's trap?" writes IM Andrew Martin. I would add: the line is actually playable for White, but only if you are a comp! From a human perspective, White's moves are too difficult to find over the board, after Tal's coming sacrifice.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Bobotsov relied on this "trap", but...' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Bobotsov verließ sich auf diese 'Falle', aber...'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Martin suggests' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Objectively better would be' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Objektiv besser wäre'} 11. dxc5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'before playing Nd5. Even here Black gets loads of compensation and practical chances after'} dxc5 ({not} 11... Nxc5 $6 12. Bxc5 dxc5 13. Nd5 Qxd2 14. Nxe7+ Kh8 15. Rxd2 {and I don't believe in Black's full compensation}) 12. Nd5 (12. h4 h5 13. Nf4 e6 14. Ncd5 $1 {This idea works better now. Diese Idee funktioniert jetzt besser.} Qd8 (14... Nxd5 $4 15. Qxa5 Nxe3 16. Rc1 $18 {is a bad idea now as the c4-pawn is defended by the bishop. ist jetzt eine schlechte Idee, da der c4-Bauer vom Läufer verteidigt wird.}) 15. Nxf6+ Qxf6 16. Qc2 {with a dynamically balanced game. mit einem dynamisch ausgewogenen Spiel.}) (12. Nc1 $5 {[%cal Gc1b3] seems a safe option. scheint eine sichere Option zu sein.}) 12... Nxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 13. Qxa5 Nxe3 14. Rc1 {Also interesting is} (14. Rxd7 $5 Bxd7 15. Nc3 Rfc8) 14... Nxc4 15. Rxc4 bxc4 16. Nc3 Rb8 17. Bxc4 Ne5 18. Be2 Be6 19. Rc1 $1 {Better than 19 Rd1 Nc6 20 Qa3 Nb4! Nezhmetdinov} (19. Rd1 Nc6 20. Qa3 Nb4 {. Black has only two minor pieces for the queen, yet may have full compensation, since all his supercharged pieces take direct aim at White's king. In fact, if given a choice, I prefer Black's position.}) 19... Bh6 (19... Nc6 20. Qxc5 Nb4 21. b3 Rfc8 22. Qe3) (19... Nc4 20. Bxc4 Bxc4 21. Qxc5 Rfc8 22. Qe3 Rd8 23. Qf4 Bd3+ 24. Ka1) (19... Bc4 20. b3 Bd3+ 21. Bxd3 Nxd3 22. Rd1 Nb4 23. Na4 $1 $16) 20. Rd1 Be3 21. Nd5 $16) 11... Nxd5 $3 {At the time a really brilliant queen sacrifice. Nowadays it is thoroughly studied and considered to be completely correct. Therefore, White players tend to avoid this variation. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal, a discriminating shopper when it comes to sacrifice, knows a good deal when he sees one. A long standing idea, accepted by precedence and faith, can be upended by a single suggestion from a person with an open mind. Apparently this wasn't Tal's idea. Instead, he followed a suggestion which flowed from the imagination of Rashid Nezhmetdinov, one of the greatest attackers and combinational players ever. --- QUESTION: Rashid who? Why is it I have never heard of Nezhmetdinov? --- ANSWER: Many chess players haven't, since he never rose to world-class status. But if you want to improve in your ability to generate initiative, attack and see combinations, then you would be well advised to study Nezhmetdinov's games. A few pro-Nezhmetdinov endorsements: "Rashid Nezhmetdinov is a virtuoso of combinational chess" - David Bronstein; "Nobody sees combinations like Rashid Nezhmetdinov" - Mikhail Botvinnik; and "His games reveal the beauty of chess and make your love in chess not so much the points and high placing, but the wonderful harmony and elegance of this particular world" - Mikhail Tal.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'I was simply amazed when seeing this queen sacrifice for the first time.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Ich war einfach nur erstaunt, als ich dieses Damenopfer zum ersten Mal sah.'} ({Tal was not a retreater and wouldn't even consider the anti-initiative move} 11... Qd8 $2 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. dxc5 dxc5 14. Nc3 bxc4 15. Bxc4 e6 16. Na4 Rb8 17. Bb3 {. I don't think b2 will be so easy to attack and I prefer White's position, since he has readily available pawn targets and an open centre, which enhance his chances for a successful defence.}) 12. Qxa5 ({White may choose to ignore the offered queen, but in that scenario Black surely gets the edge. After a possible queen swap, Black should be capable of exploiting a really poor development of White`s pieces and execute a favourable break in the centre} 12. cxd5 {Quintiliano,Renato: '?' Quintiliano,Renato: 'would be a moral defeat for White, but is also objectively bad, as after' Quintiliano,Renato: 'wäre eine moralische Niederlage für Weiß, ist aber auch objektiv schlecht, denn nach'} Qxd2 13. Rxd2 f5 $1 $36 {Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: 'the initiative is with Black.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'liegt die Initiative bei Schwarz.'}) 12... Nxe3 {A.Martin: 'A fascinating position has been reached. Black has only two Knights for the Queen but he hopes to mount an attack against the White King, focusing on b2. What becomes clear is that whilst Tal is in his element, Bobotsov is all at sea.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: How can such a sacrifice be sound? Black only got two pieces for the queen. --- ANSWER: A temptingly original idea should be weighed with its utility. In this instance, I think the sacrifice holds up, even under the comps' ruthless scrutiny. They assess at nearly even - a very bad sign for the material up side. Let's frame our thoughts by first gathering information. Black gets the following for his queen: 1. Two pieces, but since White's rook and c4-pawn are simultaneously attacked, Black picks up at least one more pawn. --- 2. Black's unopposed dark-squared bishop rules its diagonal, which ends at White's king. --- 3. Queenside lines open rapidly, after which b2 may come under heavy assault. --- Conclusion: From a practical perspective, White's position is virtually impossible for a human to defend.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Right now, Tal has two pieces for the queen and is about to get at least one pawn. However, strong attacking players have the ability of seeing and evaluating the position beyond a purely materialistic perspective. They are able to ignore the mathematic part of the game and instead foccus on practical aspects like piece coordination, attacking schemes and creative ways of handling the initiative before the opponent manages to consolidate his position. After all, it was Tal who said that “you must take your opponent into a deep dark forest where 2+2=5, and the path leading out is only wide enough for one.”' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Im Moment hat Tal zwei Figuren für die Dame und steht kurz davor, mindestens einen Bauern zu bekommen. Starke Angriffsspieler haben jedoch die Fähigkeit, die Stellung über eine rein materialistische Perspektive hinaus zu sehen und zu bewerten. Sie sind in der Lage, den mathematischen Teil des Spiels zu ignorieren und sich stattdessen auf praktische Aspekte wie Figurenkoordination, Angriffspläne und kreative Wege, die Initiative zu ergreifen, bevor der Gegner seine Stellung konsolidieren kann, zu konzentrieren. Schließlich war es Tal, der sagte: 'Du musst deinen Gegner in einen tiefen, dunklen Wald führen, in dem 2 2=5 ist und der Weg, der hinausführt, nur breit genug für einen ist.''} 13. Rc1 {A.Martin: 'Here's another game in this line where the White Queen was completelyineffectua l:' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Defending is never a pleasant task in chess, so White is looking to give back some material before the pressure becomes too annoying.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Verteidigen ist im Schach nie eine angenehme Aufgabe, also versucht Weiß, etwas Material zurückzugeben, bevor der Druck zu lästig wird.'} ({Ten years later, an interesting correspondence game was published by Chess Informant} 13. Rd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'has been tried too. White didn't survive that one either:'} Nxc4 14. Qe1 Rb8 (14... cxd4 15. Nxd4 Nc5 16. Rd1 ({Objectively speaking, White's best option is Objektiv gesehen ist die beste Option für Weiß} 16. Rd2 $1 Nxd2+ 17. Qxd2 Bd7 $15 {[%csl Yg7][%cal Gf8c8,Ga8b8,Ya6a5] when Black still keeps the best chances, as the dark-squared bishop is going to become a monster. wonach Schwarz immer noch die besten Chancen hat, da der schwarzfeldrige Läufer zu einem Monster werden wird.}) 16... Rb8 $1 {Getting ready for the attack. Vorbereitung zum Angriff.} 17. Nc6 ({In case of Im Falle von} 17. Be2 {Black keeps increasing the pressure by means of erhöht Schwarz weiter den Druck durch} Na4 {as , denn} 18. b3 {allows him to launch a powerful attack: ermöglicht es ihm, einen mächtigen Angriff zu starten:} Na3+ 19. Kc1 Bd7 $1 20. bxa4 Rfc8+ 21. Kd2 Bxd4 22. Rc1 Nc4+ 23. Bxc4 bxc4 {[%csl Rd2][%cal Yc4c3,Gb8b2] and White can't hold the attack, for example: und Weiß kann den Angriff nicht aufhalten, zum Beispiel:} 24. Rb1 c3+ 25. Kd3 e5 26. Rxb8 Rxb8 27. Qd1 Rb2 $19) 17... Rb7 18. Na5 Nxb2 $1 {This is the beginning of a beautiful variation. Dies ist der Beginn einer schönen Variante.} 19. Rd2 (19. Nxb7 Nxd1 20. Qxd1 Bxb7 21. Bd3 Na4 $17 {[%cal Gf8c8,Ga4c3] also looks very bad for White. sieht auch sehr schlecht für Weiß aus.}) 19... Rc7 $1 20. Rxb2 Na4 $1 {Black has only one piece for the queen, and still the advantage is with him! Schwarz hat nur eine Figur für die Dame, und trotzdem ist er im Vorteil!} 21. Rc2 ({In case of Im Falle von} 21. Rb3 Be6 22. Bd3 Rfc8 {Black regains some material and keeps the attack: gewinnt Schwarz etwas Material zurück und hält den Angriff aufrecht:} 23. f4 Nc3+ 24. Rxc3 Bxc3 25. Qe2 Bxa5 $19) 21... Rxc2 22. Kxc2 Be6 {White can't avoid losing material in the next moves in view of Black's strong piece play. Weiß kann angesichts des starken schwarzen Figurenspiels nicht vermeiden, in den nächsten Zügen Material zu verlieren.} 23. Nb3 (23. Qe3 Rc8+ 24. Kd1 Bc3 25. Bd3 Bxa5 $19 {[%cal Ra5b6] and the attack goes on. und der Angriff geht weiter.}) 23... Rc8+ 24. Kd3 Rc3+ 25. Ke2 (25. Qxc3 Nxc3 26. Nd4 Nxa2 27. Nxe6 fxe6 $19 {seems a winning endgame for Black. scheint ein gewinnbringendes Endspiel für Schwarz zu sein.}) 25... Rc2+ 26. Ke3 Rxa2 {[%csl Rb3,Re3] at this point, White has nothing better than zu diesem Zeitpunkt hat Weiß nichts Besseres als} 27. Nc1 (27. Qa5 Bh6+ 28. f4 Ra3 $19) 27... Bc3 $1 28. Qxc3 (28. Qd1 Nb2 {and the queen is running out of squares. und der Dame gehen die Felder aus.} 29. Qe2 Nc4+ 30. Qxc4 Bxc4 31. Bxc4 Bd2+ 32. Ke2 Rc2 33. Bb3 Rxc1 34. Rxc1 Bxc1 $19) 28... Nxc3 29. Nxa2 Bxa2 $19 {and Black should win the endgame. und Schwarz sollte das Endspiel gewinnen.}) 15. h4 cxd4 $1 16. Nxd4 Nc5 17. Rd1 Na4 $17 {Beyen,Roland - Klompus,Grigory Yakovlevich, corr. 1968 - 5/650 A.Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and White found himself under heavy assault, R.Beyen-G.Klompus, correspondence 1964.'} 18. Bxc4 (18. b3 Na3+ 19. Kc1 Be6) 18... bxc4 19. b3 cxb3 20. axb3 Be6 21. Qa5 Bxb3 22. Rd2 Rfc8 23. Nxb3 Nc5 $1 24. Rc2 Rxb3+ 25. Ka2 Rb5 26. Qa3 Rcb8 27. f4 Rb4 28. e5 Ra4 29. Qxa4 Nxa4 30. e6 fxe6 {0-1 Beyen-Klompus Corr 1968}) (13. Qd2 $6 Nxd1 14. Qxd1 bxc4 $40 {Milev}) 13... Nxc4 14. Rxc4 {A.Martin: '?!' A.Martin: 'A sure sign that White is in panic mode. Better chances were offered by' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White gives immediate voice to his anxiety, with the thought: "No matter how high the pain, the wound must be cauterized, before I bleed out." Ah yes, as I recall in the seven steps of grief, first comes denial, then later anger. We busy ourselves with the little problem in front of us, at the peril of ignoring the long-term problem of a far greater threat. In this instance, White eliminates a dangerous attacker, at the too-high cost of allowing the b-file to open.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Now this is basically forced.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Nun ist dies im Grunde genommen erzwungen.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White had to try'} 14. Qe1 cxd4 15. Nf4 {and with the diagonal blocked, White can entertain some hopes. Cyrus Lakdawala: ', K.Bulski-R.Barski, Warsaw 2012. Black should continue'} Nde5 16. Nd3 (16. Rd1 d3 $1 17. Nxd3 Nxd3 18. Bxd3 Nxb2 19. Rd2 Na4 20. Rc2 b4 $1 21. Qxb4 Bd7 $19 {[%cal Ga8b8,Gf8c8]}) 16... d5 $1 {Black keeps opening the position in order to activate his pieces. Schwarz öffnet die Stellung immer wieder, um seine Figuren zu aktivieren.} (16... Be6 {when we note an almost imperceptible renewal of White's defensive chances. Komodo assesses at dead even - although I would take Black's side every time, if given a choice.}) 17. b3 dxe4 18. fxe4 {Now there is the beautiful reply Jetzt kommt die schöne Antwort} Be6 $3 19. bxc4 bxc4 20. Nxe5 Rfb8+ 21. Kc2 Bxe5 {when White is defenseless against the invasion of the enemy pieces in the next moves. For example: , wonach Weiß gegen die Invasion der gegnerischen Figuren in den nächsten Zügen wehrlos ist. Zum Beispiel:} 22. Rb1 Rd8 23. Kd2 Rac8 $19 {[%cal Gc4c3,Gd4d3,Gf7f5] Sooner or later, the passed pawns will advance and sweep everything in their path. A beautiful position, which illustrates the power of piece coordination over material. Früher oder später werden die Freibauern vorrücken und alles wegfegen, was sich ihnen in den Weg stellt. Eine schöne Stellung, die die Macht der Figurenkoordination über das Material verdeutlicht.}) ({Other moves would leave White in trouble: Andere Züge würden Weiß in Schwierigkeiten bringen:} 14. Qc3 $2 Ndb6 15. Qd3 d5 $1 16. b3 cxd4 $3 17. bxc4 bxc4 18. Qd2 d3 19. Nc3 Rb8 $19 {[%cal Rb6a4]}) 14... bxc4 $15 15. Nc1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The knight is misplaced on c1.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'As strange as it may look, this is the decisive mistake.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'So seltsam es auch aussehen mag, dies ist der entscheidende Fehler.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'He puts up greater resistance with' The counterintuitive Das kontraintuitive} 15. Nf4 $1 {was White's best chance. war die beste Chance für Weiß.} Bxd4 ({We can see the difference following the same path as in the game: Wir können den Unterschied sehen, wenn wir den gleichen Weg wie im Spiel gehen:} 15... Rb8 16. Bxc4 Nb6 (16... Bxd4 17. b3 Ne5 18. Be2 {although even here, I hate White's position.}) 17. Rc1 {[%csl Gc1] White has the c1-square available to the rook now, improving his defensive chances. Weiß hat jetzt das Feld c1 für den Turm zur Verfügung, was seine Verteidigungsmöglichkeiten verbessert.} Bxd4 18. b3 {In case of Im Falle von} Be3 {there is the nice resource gibt es die schöne Ressource} 19. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 20. Bxd5 Bxc1 21. Kxc1 $15 {when Black only has a small advantage. , wonach Schwarz nur einen kleinen Vorteil hat.}) 16. Bxc4 Ne5 17. Rc1 Bd7 ({Again Wieder kann} 17... Be3 {can be met by beantwortet werden durch} 18. Nd5 $1 Bxc1 19. Nxe7+ Kg7 20. Kxc1 Nxc4 21. Qc3+ Ne5 22. f4 f6 $11) 18. Rc2 $17 {Black still has promising chances on the queenside and White will be under pressure for some time, but this was his best chance of offering resistance, including the rook in the defense. Schwarz hat immer noch vielversprechende Chancen am Damenflügel und Weiß wird für einige Zeit unter Druck stehen, aber dies war seine beste Chance, Widerstand zu leisten, der Turm wird in die Verteidigung einbezogen.}) 15... Rb8 {A.Martin: '' A.Martin: 'It starts to look ugly. How exactly does White nullify the pressure on b2? And against Tal..... Bobotsov had plenty to think about.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Target: b2.'} 16. Bxc4 Nb6 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal didn't want to lose a tempo after'} 16... Bxd4 {was perhaps more precise. Maybe Tal didn't want to give White time to mobilize his pieces with war vielleicht etwas präziser. Vielleicht wollte Tal Weiß keine Zeit geben, um seine Figuren zu mobilisieren mit} 17. Nb3 Bg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'} 18. Rc1 {but Black can also increase the pressure here: aber Schwarz kann auch hier den Druck erhöhen:} Nb6 19. Be2 ({Or Oder} 19. Bxa6 Ra8 20. Qxb6 Bxa6 $19 {[%cal Gf8b8,Ga6d3,Gc5c4] and White is in trouble. und Weiß ist in Schwierigkeiten.}) 19... Be6 $19 {[%cal Gf8c8,Yc5c4,Yc4c3] and White's position soon collapses. und die Stellung von Weiß bricht bald zusammen.}) 17. Bb3 {A.Martin: 'Maybe 17 Be2 was better, but Black has enormous compensation for the Queen.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The problem is the bishop is unstable on b3, vulnerable to a future ...c4.'} ({The desperate attempt Der verzweifelte Versuch} 17. Bxa6 $5 {could be considered, although White is still in a very difficult situation after könnte in Betracht gezogen werden, obwohl sich Weiß immer noch in einer sehr schwierigen Situation befindet nach} Bxd4 18. Nb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'was his last chance. After'} Bg7 (18... Be5 19. Bb5 Be6 {both ...c4 and ...Nc4 are in the air, and I don't see White surviving.}) 19. Bxc8 Rfxc8 20. Rc1 c4 {when in order to avoid immediate loss White would have to find the difficult move , wonach Weiß, um einen sofortigen Verlust zu vermeiden, den schwierigen Zug} 21. Qa7 $1 {but even in this case he would be in a clearly worse position after finden müsste, aber selbst in diesem Fall wäre er in einer deutlich schlechteren Position nach} Re8 22. Na5 Na4 23. Rxc4 Rxb2+ 24. Kc1 Bh6+ 25. Kd1 Rxa2 $17) 17... Bxd4 $17 18. Qd2 ({In case of Im Falle von} 18. Ne2 {Tal certainly had seen the trick hatte Tal sicher den Trick} Bxb2 $1 {gesehen.}) 18... Bg7 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Now it's a matter of time: White can't avoid the advance c4-c3, after which Black's attack comes with decisive effect.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Jetzt ist es nur noch eine Frage der Zeit: Weiß kann den Vorstoß c4-c3 nicht verhindern, wonach der schwarze Angriff mit entscheidender Wirkung kommt.'} 19. Ne2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hoping to have time for Nc3, which eases the pressure on b2.'} (19. Qe2 c4 20. Bd1 Bd7 $19 {[%cal Gf8c8,Gb6a4]}) 19... c4 {A.Martin: '!'} 20. Bc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE  (combination alert): We note a snaking constellation of attackers around White's king. At this stage, within his mind, Tal must have been grinning evilly and rubbing his hands together, like a silent movie villain. Find the key attacking move. --- ANSWER: Clearance/line opening.'} c3 {A.Martin: '!' A.Martin: 'Opening lines! As 21 Nxc3?? Nc4! is immediately over, Bobotsov bites the bullet.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move openly illuminates the extent of the damage to the white king's safety.'} 21. Qd3 (21. Nxc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is crushed by the simple double attack'} Nc4 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Would-be defenders discreetly disperse upon the c-pawn's entrance. Past decisions made in the heat of passion are then repentantly amended in the light of the sober present. I will bet that at this stage Bobotsov deeply regretted his 14 Rxc4?!, opening the b-file for Tal.'}) 21... cxb2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One glance and we can see that White's king won't survive.'} 22. Nd4 Bd7 23. Rd1 Rfc8 {A.Martin: 'A picture postcard from Black, one you would wish to send to all your friends. It looks a bit like a Yugoslav Attack Dragon gone horribly wrong. Chris Ward would be ecstatic!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Martin compares this position to a Dragon Yugoslav Attack, gone terrible awry for White.'} 24. Bb3 {A.Martin: 'He tries to block. Fat chance.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Bobotsov clenches tightly to his final hope, which is to gum up the b-file.'} Na4 {A.Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The knight augments his prestige by climbing to a higher square, where it targets c3, which forces White to reopen the b-file by exchanging on a4.'} (24... a5 $19 {[%cal Ya5a4] was also enough. war ebenfalls ausreichend.}) 25. Bxa4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Well, White's hope didn't last all that long.'} (25. Ne2 $4 {fails miserably to} Bb5 26. Qd2 Bxe2 27. Bxa4 (27. Qxe2 $4 {walks into the fork} Nc3+) 27... Bxd1 28. Bxd1 Rc1+ 29. Qxc1 bxc1=Q+ 30. Kxc1 {with an extra rook for Black.}) 25... Bxa4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: ...Bxd4, and if the queen recaptures, then ...Bc2 mate.'} 26. Nb3 Rc3 {A.Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} ({There are multiple paths to the win, one of which is} 26... Bxb3 $1 27. axb3 Rb5 {(threat: ...Ra5 and ...Ra1 mate)} 28. f4 {(intending e5)} Rbc5 29. e5 dxe5 30. fxe5 Bxe5 {and White loses the fight for c1, the node of both sides' contention. There is no defence to the coming ...Rc1+.}) 27. Qxa6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): Black has two methods to force a win. Find one of them. --- ANSWER: Step 1: Eliminate the knight, a key defender of c1.'} Bxb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} ({ANSWER no.2: Also decisive is} 27... Bb5 $1 28. Qa7 Rbc8 29. Rd4 Rc2 30. Qb6 Bd3 $1 {, and if} 31. Rxd3 Rc1+ 32. Nxc1 bxc1=Q# {. "I continue to hear errant stupidity spouted from that direction," says the queen, pointing to her brother. She adds: "If it continues to irritate me, I will silence it at its source, by ordering my brother's wagging tongue cut out from his mouth."}) 28. axb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Step 2: Double rooks on the c-file, after which there is no defence to the coming check on c1.'} Rbc8 {A.Martin: '!' A.Martin: 'Tal's attacks were often based on unceasing momentum-not allowing the opponent any chance to catch breath. This game is one of many magnificent examples of his legacy.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 29. Qa3 Rc1+ 30. Rxc1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After'} Rxc1+ {A.Martin: 'Ouch! That really hurts.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Apart from its obvious beauty, this game is also instructive and a model of active piece play and piece coordination over material. The way as Tal was able to drag his opponents into complications and easily outplay them was really impressive, in this game he did that so early as move 11, and less than 10 moves later the game was already decided. To conclude, I would like to share something curious. Some years ago I checked this game with the top engine of the time, and I remember it didn't consider Tal's idea totally correct, evaluating White's position as totally playable after 11...Nxd5. I didn't buy that evaluation though, because the queen sacrifice seems very good both in practical and psychological terms. However, nowadays SF15 already evaluates the sacrifice as correct and says that White's position is already more difficult after 11...Nxd5. It takes time to understand the geniuses.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Abgesehen von ihrer offensichtlichen Schönheit ist diese Partie auch lehrreich und ein Musterbeispiel für aktives Figurenspiel und Figurenkoordination über Material. Die Art und Weise, wie Tal es schaffte, seine Gegner in Komplikationen zu verwickeln und sie leicht zu überspielen, war wirklich beeindruckend. In dieser Partie tat er dies bereits im 11. Zug, und weniger als 10 Züge später war die Partie bereits entschieden.. Zum Schluss möchte ich noch etwas Kurioses erzählen. Vor einigen Jahren überprüfte ich diese Partie mit der damaligen Top-Engine, und ich erinnere mich, dass sie Tals Idee nicht für völlig korrekt hielt und Weiß' Stellung nach 11...Sxd5 als völlig spielbar einschätzte. Ich habe diese Bewertung allerdings nicht geglaubt, weil das Damenopfer sowohl in praktischer als auch in psychologischer Hinsicht sehr gut erscheint. Heutzutage bewertet SF15 das Opfer jedoch bereits als korrekt und sagt, dass die Stellung von Weiß nach 11...Sxd5 bereits schwieriger ist. Es braucht Zeit, um die Genies zu verstehen.'} 31. Ka2 {, it would be inaccurate to state that White's defence missed by a hair's breath, since Black has no less than three separate mates in one, the prettiest of which is to underpromote with} b1=B# {. The bishop eyes White's king the way a cult member gazes upon an adored leader.} 0-1

