GM Mihail Marin: 'Otherwise Nxd6, with a decisive attack.'} 29. Kg2 Qxb1 30. Nxd6 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Nevertheless!'} Bxd6 31. Qe6+ Kg7 32. Qd7+ $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'White obtains significantly the more pleasant ending. Thus the storm initiated by Black could have rebounded on him, but all this occurred because of his mistake on the 23rd move. As I have already pointed out, Botvinnik considered the capture on f3 to be favourable to Black, and he preferred the immediate exchange of queens. Psychologically this is quite understandable: when you are a piece up and are under attack, it is always pleasant to get rid of the opponent's queen, but in his calculations White underestimated the strength of Black's 26th move.' GM Mihail Marin: 'Flohr' Flohr}) ({.} 28... Z0) 29. Nxd6 Rf8 30. Qe6+ Kh8 31. Nf7+ Rxf7 32. Qxf7 {Mueller,Karsten: '+-'} Qf5 33. Qxf5 gxf5 34. Kg3 Be5+ 35. Bf4 {Flohr}) 27... Qb2 ({GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik feared'} 27... Qf5 {Mikhail Tal: ', but in his calculations he overlooked the possibility of'} 28. Bg4 {Mueller,Karsten: '+-' Mueller,Karsten: 'however, also wins.' Mueller,Karsten: 'gewinnt allerdings auch.'} Qe5+ 29. Qxe5 Bxe5+ 30. f4 Rxc3 {GM Mihail Marin: ', failing to notice that'} 31. Bxc8 $18 {Mikhail Tal: '(it was this move that escaped my opponent's attention).' GM Mihail Marin: 'wins material.'}) {GM Mihail Marin: 'During the post mortem both players agreed that the game should end in a draw by repetition after'} 28. Rb1 $11 {Mikhail Tal: 'etc.'} (28. Bg4 $1 {GM Mihail Marin} Be5+ 29. Kg2 R8c7 30. Be6+ Kh8 31. Nd1 Qd4 32. Rxc4 Qxc4 33. Qf3 Kg7 34. Qe3 $18 {White has a strong attack with equal material.})) 25... fxe2 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '-+' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+'} 26. Rb3 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Botvinnik might have hoped that without queens he could keep his extra piece. But in fact, White is just lost, as his pieces are hanging and the pawn on e2 is too strong.'} Rd4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'The unwieldy black rook has suddenly acquired colossal strength in the centre of the board. Since White has 'plugged' the c-file, the rook switches to the d-file, and in collaboration with the passed e2-pawn brings Black victory.'} 27. Be1 {Mikhail Tal: 'There is nothing better.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '?' Mikhail Tal: 'There is nothing better. Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '?'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'If'} 27. Be3 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black wins by'} Rxc3 28. Rbxc3 Rd1 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) (27. Bg5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'might work better.'} Be5+ 28. f4 Rxf4 {JvR: '!'} 29. Bxf4 Bxf4+ 30. Kh1 Bxc1 31. Nxe2 $19) 27... Be5+ 28. Kg1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Bf3 would now be nice for White.' Mueller,Karsten: ''} Bf4 {Mueller,Karsten: '?!' Mueller,Karsten: 'misses the beautiful and directly fatal' Mueller,Karsten: 'verpasst das wunderschöne und direkt tödliche'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Unfortunately, Black misses an opportunity to conclude the game quickly and prettily with'} 28... Rxc3 $1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'might be the shorter path' Mueller,Karsten: '!!'} 29. Rbxc3 Rd1 30. Rc4 (30. Rc7 $19) 30... Bb2 $19 {Mikhail Tal: '. In this, it is true, a part was played by reasons not at all connected with chess: the noise in the auditorium prompted the match arbiters to carry out their threat and transfer the play to a closed room. This, of course, proved to be a very severe warning to the spectators, since during the subsequent games they did not give cause for such a measure, but the feeling, when the clocks are stopped and you are politely asked to leave the stage, moreover at the very height of the game, can also hardly be called pleasant. At any rate, I am somehow not yet used to playing in such 'nomadic' conditions. Such an unaccustomed transference could have led to an even worse blunder, and therefore I deliberately chose a less strong, but safer continuation, where there was no longer any need to calculate lengthy variations.' Mueller,Karsten: '(Tal)' Mueller,Karsten: '(Tal)'}) 29. Nxe2 ({Mikhail Tal: 'After'} 29. Ra1 {Mikhail Tal: ', whatever the circumstances, Black could have finally decided on' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} Rxc3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!'} 30. Rxc3 Rd1 {Mikhail Tal: '.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+'}) 29... Rxc1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Decoy: c1'} 30. Nxd4 ({Mikhail Tal: 'Or'} 30. Nxc1 Rd1 {Mikhail Tal: '. Botvinnik's last chance is somehow to exploit the activity of his knight.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Decoy Double attack' Mikhail Tal: '. Botvinnik's last chance is somehow to exploit the activity of his knight. Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Decoy Double attack''}) (30. Z0 $140 Rxe1+ {Wins material}) 30... Rxe1+ 31. Bf1 {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} Be4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Two active bishops and an extra pawn is an advantage more than sufficient for a win.' Mikhail Tal: 'White cannot play'} 32. Ne2 (32. Rxb7 {Mikhail Tal: 'because of'} Bd3 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 32... Be5 ({Not} 32... Bxd5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)} 33. Nxf4 Bxb3 34. axb3 $17) 33. f4 Bf6 ({Don't play} 33... Bxd5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)} 34. fxe5 Bxb3 35. axb3 $19) 34. Rxb7 ({Mikhail Tal: 'If'} 34. Kf2 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black can reply'} Bh4+ ({Mikhail Tal: 'or, even simpler,'} 34... Rb1 {Mikhail Tal: '.'})) 34... Bxd5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Double Attack'} 35. Rc7 ({Mikhail Tal: 'Not' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'The pointe is'} 35. Rxa7 Rxe2 {Mikhail Tal: '.' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '!'}) 35... Bxa2 {Mikhail Tal: 'White has the possibility of'} 36. Rxa7 {Mikhail Tal: 'now, since if'} (36. Ng3 {hoping against hope Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Bd4+ 37. Kh2 $19) 36... Bc4 {Mikhail Tal: 'The white pieces are completely tied up, and the advance of the d-pawn will quickly decide the game. However, from the 26th move onwards Black had been replying instantly, and in the subsequent play he complicates his task somewhat. It would seem that here too the 'change of scene' had an effect.'} (36... Rxe2 {Mikhail Tal: 'he can interpose'} 37. Ra8+ {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 37. Ra8+ Kf7 ({Mikhail Tal: 'Much simpler was' Mikhail Tal: 'or'} 37... Kg7 38. Re8 (38. Ra7+ Kh6 {Mikhail Tal: ', when White loses a piece. I, however, assumed that I could win 'anyhow'.'} 39. Kg2 Bxe2 40. Bxe2 Rxe2+ 41. Kf3 $19) 38... d5) 38. Ra7+ Ke6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Even here it was not too late to return to g8, so as to transpose into the variation given in the previous note.'} (38... Kg8) (38... Kf8 $142 {secures the win} 39. Ra8+) 39. Ra3 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black had overlooked this simple move. The white rook succeeds in switching to the e-file. Now I was obliged to think, since White has acquired certain drawing chances. Black reverts to the correct idea and begins simply making use of his passed pawn.'} d5 40. Kf2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} (40. Kg2 Be7 41. Rc3 Bc5 $19) 40... Bh4+ {Mikhail Tal: 'Preventing the activation of the white king.'} 41. Kg2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' If only White now had time for Nd4+....'} Kd6 {Mikhail Tal: 'The 40 moves had been made, but the players remained in their seats. Botvinnik obviously reckoned that, if Black were to continue playing so rapidly, he would make a mistake somewhere, while I kept playing through 'inertia'.'} ({Less strong is} 41... Bxe2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)} 42. Re3+ Kf5 43. Bxe2 $19) 42. Ng3 {Mikhail Tal: 'White finally escapes from the pin, but at a high price – the resulting rook ending is completely hopeless for him.'} Bxg3 43. Bxc4 dxc4 44. Kxg3 Kd5 (44... Re7 {Mikhail Tal: 'was also possible, of course, but Black simply could not wait to realise his passed pawn.'}) 45. Ra7 (45. Kf2 {(Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)) keeps fighting.} Rh1 46. Kg2) 45... c3 46. Rc7 Kd4 {Mikhail Tal: '. The game was adjourned in this position and White sealed his next move. On arriving home, my trainer and I set up the position and quickly convinced ourselves that Black's passed pawn was unstoppable. Therefore (I will say this in confidence) the following day we did not even take breakfast, but waited in our room for a call from the arbiter informing us of White's resignation. At midday the long-awaited call was received. White's sealed move was' Mueller,Karsten: 'Of course, I can only scratch the surface here. Another source is for example: Zaubern wie Schachweltmeister Michail Tal by Karsten Müller and Raymund Stolze, Edition Olms 2010. ' Mueller,Karsten: 'Ich kann hier natürlich nur an der Oberfläche kratzen. Eine weitere Quellen ist zum Beispiel: Zaubern wie Schachweltmeister Michail Tal von Karsten Müller und Raymund Stolze, Edition Olms 2010. '} 47. Rd7+ {sealed move sealed move Mikhail Tal: '.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Accuracy: White = 36%, Black = 52%. '} (47. Kg4 {(Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s)) was worth a try.}) 0-1

