1. d4 g6 2. e4 d6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nbd2 Bg7 5. Bc4 O-O 6. Bb3 Nc6 7. c3 e5 8. O-O Bg4 9. d5 Nb8 10. c4 {After a solid start, White has displayed certain territorial ambitions. But this involves a considerable loss of time, including c2-c3-c4 and the long bishop manoeuvre, which still has not come to an end. What is even more significant is that his queen's knight stands on d2, blocking his other bishop's development. Developing the bishop to b2 is not very effective, as it would hit into a defended pawn, so White still has to work a bit to bring his position in order. On the other hand, the centre is blocked and he has more space, elements he must have counted on.} Na6 11. Bc2 c6 12. a3 cxd5 13. cxd5 Rc8 {The first critical moment. Black has developed harmoniously while White is still struggling to complete his development.} 14. h3 {The most natural decision.} ({The attempt to achieve optimal coordination with} 14. Nb1 {followed by Nc3 and Be3 allows} Bxf3 {Due to the hanging bishop, White has to weaken his kingside structure with} 15. gxf3 {, when after} Nh5 {Black has excellent play on the dark squares.}) 14... Bh5 $3 {The result of an accurate judgement of the position, this is the only move to keep White under pressure. The text move involves a piece sacrifice which White would have better declined.} (14... Bxf3 {not only gives up the bishops' pair but also frees White's play after} 15. Nxf3 {followed by Be3.}) (14... Bd7 {allows the optimal regrouping with} 15. Nb1 $1 {followed by Nc3 and Be3.}) 15. g4 $2 {Weakening the kingside with incomplete development can hardly be a good idea.} ({White should have kept manoeuvring with, say,} 15. Re1 Qd7 16. Bd3 Nc5 17. Bf1 Bh6 {Even though Black's position is very active, we can notice that ... Bh5 also had a minor drawback: Black does not have ...Nh5 followed by ...f7-f5 anymore.}) 15... Nxg4 $1 16. hxg4 Bxg4 17. Bd3 {In view of the threat ...f7-f5 the bishop hurries to unpin the knight on f3.} Bh6 $6 {This unnecessarily offers White some free tempi for developing.} ({Everything was ready for the resolute} 17... f5 $1 {, with a very dangerous attack.} 18. Be2 Nc5 19. Ne1 Bh3 20. Ng2 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 fxe4 22. Bg4 Rf4 23. Nb3 ({In view of the threat ...Qg5 there is hardly anything better. If} 23. Nb1 Qg5 24. Bxf4 exf4 $19 {, with a crushing attack.}) 23... Rxg4+ 24. Qxg4 Nxb3 $17 {With two pawns for the exchange, the better structure and active play.}) 18. Nc4 Bxc1 19. Rxc1 Nc5 20. Be2 {Relatively best.} (20. b4 {runs into} Nb3 $1 21. Rc3 {Trying to keep the c4-knight defended.} (21. Qxb3 $6 Bxf3 {mates soon.}) 21... Nd4 22. Be2 Bxf3 23. Bxf3 b5 $19 {Black retrieves the piece, retaining a few extra pawns and a strategic advantage.}) (20. Rc3 {does not work either, as after} f5 {he has to either give up the e4-pawn as in the game or return the piece with} 21. exf5 e4 $17) 20... Nxe4 $15 {Black has three pawns for the piece and attacking chances.} 21. Re1 $6 {Weakening the pawn on f2 and keeping the knight on f3 under pressure.} (21. Nfd2 $5 Bxe2 22. Qxe2 Qg5+ 23. Kh1 Qh4+ 24. Kg2 Nf6 $15) (21. Ne3 $5 Bh5 22. Rxc8 Qxc8 23. Nd2 Bxe2 24. Qxe2 Ng5 $15) 21... Bxf3 $5 {The introduction to another combination.} (21... f5 {was also strong.}) 22. Bxf3 Nxf2 $1 23. Kxf2 Qh4+ 24. Kg2 Rxc4 25. Rxc4 Qxc4 {With four pawns for a relatively passive bishop, Black has excellent winning chances already.} 26. Re4 Qc7 27. Qa4 f5 28. Rc4 Qe7 29. Qxa7 e4 30. Bd1 f4 31. Bg4 Qg5 32. Kh3 Qh6+ 33. Kg2 f3+ 34. Kf1 Qg5 35. Be6+ Kg7 36. Qg1 Qh4 37. Qg4 Qh2 38. Rxe4 f2 0-1

