{John Emms: 'Meat and Potatoes: Benoni hero Tal plays simple chess to notch up a straight forward victory.''Meat and Potatoes: Benoni hero Tal plays simple chess to notch up a straight forward victory. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'Meat and Potatoes: Benoni hero Tal plays simple chess to notch up a straight forward victory.' Endre Vegh: 'Handling the Light-squared Bishop: One of the major positional concerns for Black in the Benoni is that the light-squared bishop does not have any good square or diagonal. In some respects it looks like a good bishop because of the black pawns on d6 and c5, but the important pawns here are the ones on f7, g6, h7 and e4 and d5. The dark-squared bishops are the good pieces in this structure. The main way to deal with the light-squared bishop's lack of scope is to exchange it for a knight, as Black did in the following example.'''} 1. d4 {John Emms John Emms'Bulletin Bulletin: 'John Emms John Emms John Emms: 'Bulletin'''} Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'My editor GM John Emms called the Benoni Tal's "meat and potatoes" line, which he played and understood better than anyone in the world, in his era. To my mind, a player who willingly entered into Tal-territory like this is akin to the ancient sailor who decides to enter uncharted waters, knowing full well that they may be infested with sea monsters. I have a feeling Tal's results would have fallen if all his opponents weaponized boredom and played either the London or Colle System on him!'} 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 g6 7. e4 Bg7 8. Bd3 (8. Be2 $142 {'(Bulletin) John Emms: 'Bulletin''}) 8... O-O 9. O-O {John Emms: 'Nowadays everyone would play 9 h3, preventing the pin and transposing into the Modern Classical Variation.''Nowadays everyone would play 9 h3, preventing the pin and transposing into the Modern Classical Variation. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'Nowadays everyone would play 9 h3, preventing the pin and transposing into the Modern Classical Variation.'''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Today,'} 9. h3 $1 {(John Emms) (Bulletin) has now been established as the main move here, in order to restrict the bishop on c8. After the text move Black is fine. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is the prevailing interpretation of what is considered White's best shot at an advantage.'}) 9... a6 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The immediate'} 9... Bg4 {(John Emms) (Bulletin) was fine as well. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is more accurate.'}) 10. a4 { f3-d2-c4'' f3-d2-c4 John Emms: 'Bulletin: ' f3-d2-c4''' '} ({White can fix his slightly inaccurate last move with the correct} 10. h3 $1 {.}) 10... Bg4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What is so great about Tal's last move? --- ANSWER: My friend GM Yasser Seirawan - after rudely beating me in a Benoni - revealed his Benoni principle: If Black achieves one swap of minor pieces, he equalizes; two swaps and Black stands better. Theory, back in the 1950's didn't recognize this fact, misinterpreting the correct plan, the way a book is misinterpreted from one language to another, by an incompetent translator.'} 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nbd7 {John Emms: 'And here either 13 Qe2 or 13 Qd1 are preferable.''And here either 13 Qe2 or 13 Qd1 are preferable. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'And here either 13 Qe2 or 13 Qd1 are preferable.'''} 13. Bf4 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'The bishop is on its way to h2, but there it will leave the centre undefended.'''} ({John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'Tal suggested it would have been better to play''} 13. Qe2 $142 { 14.f4 Tal'(Bulletin)  14.f4 Tal John Emms: '(Bulletin)  14.f4 Tal' Endre Vegh: 'followed by 14 f4 with a decent position, though Black has nothing to fear.''}) ({With} 13. Qd1 Re8 14. Re1 Rc8 15. a5 c4 16. Bc2 {White covers the b3-square, as well as his e-pawn, A.Aleksandrov-J.Gdanski, Warsaw 2008. I prefer Black after} Rc5 17. Ra4 Rxa5 18. Rxc4 b5 19. Rd4 Ra1 {.}) 13... Qc7 14. Qe2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Donner wants to suppress both ...c4 and ...b5 ideas, at the cost of placing his queen on the same file as Black's e8-rook.'} Rfe8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Nobody is comfortable when a rook stares at our queen on the same file, even when the queen is temporarily shielded by a pawn.'} 15. Bh2 ({Instead,} 15. a5 c4 $5 (15... Rac8 {is probably better}) 16. Bxc4 Nh5 17. Be3 Bxc3 18. bxc3 Rxe4 19. Bd3 Re5 20. c4 Nf4 21. Qc2 Nxd3 22. Qxd3 Rc8 23. Rfc1 Nc5 24. Qc3 {looks a bit better for White, whose dark-square power outweighs his weak c-pawn and Black's c5 outpost.}) 15... Rac8 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'With the idea of ...c5-c4 and ...Nc5.''' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Intending ...c4.'} 16. Bc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White returns the bishop pair to maintain the blockade of c4. On the down side, this means yet another swap, which benefits Black.'} ({I think White was better off playing a line like} 16. Bc2 c4 17. a5 {.}) 16... Ne5 {John Emms: '!' '!''Bulletin: 'John Emms: '!' '!'''} (16... Nxe4 $2 {'(Bulletin) John Emms: 'Bulletin''} 17. Nxe4 f5 18. Nxd6 Rxe2 19. Nxc8 {John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'would have left White better off.''}) 17. f4 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'It is easy to say this move is ugly.''' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White clings to his plan of an e5 break, and makes the extravagant assertion that he is the one with the initiative, despite a growing body of evidence which suggests an opposite view. His move is made with the philosophy: sometimes we must compromise structure to kindle piece activity. A single cell amoeba is attracted to an unfamiliar source of light and heat. Moral: living organisms are creatures of curiosity. It's very difficult to hold back from such an aggressive gesture. Sometimes we fight fiercely for some trivial point, just to prove a point - in this case Donner tells Tal: "I plan to push you around." --- QUESTION: From your tone, you disapprove of White's thematic looking move. What is wrong with it? White simply begins a central/kingside attack. --- ANSWER: How to draw the line between 'not enough' and 'too much'? To my mind White's would-be kingside initiative/attack just barely ekes out its survival, like a rattlesnake who needs to get lucky and find a careless mouse. I think the move weakens more than generates attacking chances, and unnecessarily places White under future strategic obligations of defending e4.'} ({John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'But''} 17. Bxe5 {'(Bulletin) John Emms: 'Bulletin''} Rxe5 $17 {John Emms: 'Endre Vegh: 'would also have been very strong for Black and prove the total success of his strategy. White's remaining bishop has no scope and his knight is rather passive too. Meanwhile Black will attack the white centre, both frontally with the rook, and from the sides with the queen, knight and bishop.''}) 17... Nxc4 18. Qxc4 Nd7 $17 {John Emms: 'With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7.''With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7. Bulletin: '''John Emms: 'With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7.' John Emms: 'With simple play Tal has accumulated a pleasant advantage. In particular the bishop on h2 is no match for his opponent on g7. Bulletin: ''' Endre Vegh: 'White's position is already close to collapse. We can clearly see that the bishop is not happy on h2.'' ' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Who stands better? --- ANSWER: I prefer Black, whose bishop is better than White's. --- QUESTION: What about the fact that White controls the centre? --- ANSWER: I think this factor is balanced by Black's chances to overextend and undermine with ideas like ...Qb6!, followed by ...Qb4!. Also, remember Seirawan's Benoni principle. Black achieved a swap of two pairs of pieces, which significantly reduces White's chances for a successful kingside assault.'} 19. Rfe1 $2 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: '?!''' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's intended e5 break will never happen.'} ({He is better off playing} 19. a5 {.}) 19... Qb6 $1 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'All the weaknesses in White's camp are visible now.'''} 20. Rab1 Qb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move breaks down White's blockade of c4.'} 21. Qf1 c4 $1 $19 {John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lo'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lost. Bulletin: '-+!''John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lost.' John Emms: 'The queenside attack is well and truly moving. White is already positionally lost. Bulletin: '-+!'' Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'Now the knight enters the position and White is left with no defence.'' ' st.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Just like that, White's lifeless position feels as inert as the passed out drunk, still clutching his drained bottle of rum. It felt like Donner made natural moves and is now mysteriously strategically busted. Is the Benoni really just a forced win for Black in Tal's hands?'} 22. Re2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision): Analyse 22...Bxc3 and its aftermath. Is it worth it for Black to give up his dark-squared bishop to damage White's structure?'} b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal retains his advantage with this move, but he could have had more. Both parties are guilty of dark-square bias of the Benoni bishop. White's last move was a mistake and in this case Black is justified in handing over the bishop.'} ({ANSWER: Why is it that an idea's actuality is sometimes so utterly alien from our deluded romantic notions?} 22... Bxc3 $1 {is a psychologically difficult move to make, since we all worship that dark-squared Benoni bishop. We all observe and process data, and our interpretation can vary wildly, from stylistic bias. In this instance, there is only one correct answer. After} 23. bxc3 Qxc3 24. e5 (24. Rxb7 $2 {only makes matters worse for White after} Nc5 25. Rb1 Nxe4 26. Rd1 f5 {when Black's pieces rule the board}) 24... Nc5 25. Rd1 f5 $1 26. Kh1 Qb3 {Black's c-pawn is about to run down the board and White is busted. If} 27. exd6 Rxe2 28. Qxe2 Ne4 {and Black dominates.}) 23. axb5 axb5 24. Kh1 Bxc3 {John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'It seems unusual to give up such a good Benoni bishop, but this is the clearest route to 'It seems unusual to give up such a good Benoni bishop, but this is the clearest route to victory. Bulletin: 'John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'It seems unusual to give up such a good Benoni bishop, but this is the clearest route to victory.' Endre Vegh: 'Once again Black parts with this bishop at the right moment and the white position falls apart.''' victory.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It soon becomes clear that the bishop's purposes are not so innocent. Now Tal sees the correct idea, and White's position convulses into a terminal level.'} 25. bxc3 Qxc3 26. Rxb5 Qd3 {John Emms: 'The c-pawn suddenly becomes a little monster.''The c-pawn suddenly becomes a little monster. Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'The c-pawn suddenly becomes a little monster.''' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Dual purpose: Black clears the way for his c-pawn to run down the board, while attacking White's e-pawn. Defensive preparation is not in White's power anymore.'} 27. Qe1 ({White can offer slightly more resistance with} 27. e5 Nc5 {, but the result is still not in doubt.}) 27... c3 28. Rb1 Nc5 {John Emms: 'And White resigned. There is no defence, for example''And White resigned. There is no defence, for example Bulletin: 'John Emms: 'And White resigned. There is no defence, for example''' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'That was almost too easy! We all love it when our position runs on cruise control, where the next move is as obvious as your last one. I risk belabouring the point when I mention that White is just as busted now, as we was about seven moves ago.'} ({John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: 'White resigned because of''' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Donner, recognizing that his pieces are an inherently dysfunctional unit, resigned, having seeing the line'} 28... Nc5 29. Qd1 (29. e5 c2 30. Rc1 Nb3 {wins}) 29... Rxe4 {John Emms: '!' '!''Bulletin: 'John Emms: '!' '!''' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 30. Qxd3 (30. Rc2 Re1+ 31. Qxe1 Qxc2 {is also hopeless for White}) 30... Nxd3 31. Rxe4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(if you are a person of scant financial means, then you may as well borrow money, since you don't ever intend to pay it back)'} c2 {John Emms: 'Bulletin: 'Endre Vegh: '.'''} 32. Rf1 c1=Q 33. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 34. Bg1 {and now Black forks with} Nf2+ {.}) 0-1

