1. e4 {Fischer,R} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 (5. Nb5 {Fischer,R-Najdorf,M Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966}) 5... Qc7 {Play usually continues} 6. g3 {This fianchetto line is one of the main replies to the Sicilian Taimanov.} Nf6 $2 {"Probably the losing move!" – Fischer. Interestingly, against 6 Be3 or 6 Be2 there is nothing wrong with playing 6...Nf6 before ...a7-a6, but here it proves to be a serious inaccuracy, as we will see next.} (6... a6 { Fischer,R: ''} 7. Bg2 Nf6 8. O-O {, when Black has several choices such as} d6 (8... Bc5) ({, and} 8... Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Bc5 {. Instead, with his next move Tal "modifies" the move order and is severely punished for the rest of the game.})) 7. Ndb5 $1 Qb8 {From now on Fischer will never let his opponent rest, creating new threats on every move, with a particular focus on the d6-square.} (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 Qd8 9. Bf4 e5 10. Bg5 $16) 8. Bf4 {'!' '!'} Ne5 ({Later it was discovered that the ugly} 8... e5 {offers more resistance. There can follow} 9. Bg5 a6 10. Bxf6 (10. Na3 b5 11. Bxf6 b4 $1) 10... axb5 (10... gxf6 11. Na3 b5 (11... Bxa3 {Fischer,R} 12. bxa3 Ne7 13. Qf3 {'!?' '!?'} (13. Bc4 Qc7 14. Qd3 d6 15. O-O Be6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Ke7 $11) 13... Qc7 14. Bg2 d6 15. O-O {, intending Nd1-e3, with a more comfortable game for White in Alexa. Ivanov-I.V.Ivanov, US Championship, Long Beach 1989. But if Black is aiming at such kind of play, I guess he should rather go for the Sveshnikov (1 e4 c5 2 Nf3 Nc6 3 d4 cxd4 4 Nxd4 Nf6 5 Nc3 e5) straight away!} Rg8 16. Nd1 $14 {Al.Ivanov-I.Ivanov,USA,1989}) 12. Nd5) 11. Bg5 $16) 9. Be2 $1 {From here the bishop covers the f3-square, thus creating the threat of 10 Qd4.} Bc5 {On most other moves 10 Qd4 is troublesome for Black;} ({e.g.} 9... a6 {'?' '?'} 10. Qd4 {'!' '!'} d6 11. Rd1 {'!' '!'} axb5 12. Bxe5 $18 {with a huge edge, now that the bishop is untouchable due to mate.}) (9... d6 10. Qd4 Nc6 11. Nxd6+ (11. Qxd6 $1 {Tal,M} Bxd6 12. Bxd6) 11... Kd7 12. Bb5 Bxd6 13. O-O-O {Fischer,R: '+-'}) (9... Ng8 {Tal,M} 10. Qd4 f6 11. O-O-O (11. Bxe5 fxe5 12. Qc4 Kd8 $1 $16) 11... a6 12. Nd6+ Bxd6 13. Qxd6 Qxd6 14. Rxd6 $16) (9... Bb4 {Fischer,R} 10. Qd4 Nc6 11. Bxb8 Nxd4 12. Nc7+ Ke7 13. Nxa8 Nxc2+ 14. Kd1 (14. Kd2 Nxa1 15. Rxa1 (15. Bd3 $142 {PS}) 15... Nxe4+ 16. Kc2 Nxc3 17. bxc3 Bc5 $13) 14... Nxa1 15. e5 Bxc3 16. Bd6+ Kd8 17. bxc3 Ne4 18. Rf1 Nxc3+ 19. Kd2 Nxe2 20. Kxe2 Nc2 21. Kd3 $18 {Tal}) 10. Bxe5 $1 {This exchange isn't favourable by itself but, together with the following two pawn moves, it lets White take a firm grip on the centre.} Qxe5 11. f4 {Fischer,R: ''} Qb8 12. e5 {Right on time, before Black consolidates with ...d7-d6.} a6 ({Aware that} 12... Ng8 13. Ne4 {, followed by Nbd6+, would be terrible for him, Tal opts to complicate the game.} Be7 14. Qd2 $18 { bd6+, 0-0-0}) 13. exf6 axb5 14. fxg7 (14. Ne4 {; Keres,P Fischer,R: ''} Bf8 15. Qd4 {Fischer,R: '' Fischer,R: 'Keres' was another good option – Keres.}) 14... Rg8 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Qd4 {'!' A move with several virtues: it protects the important g7-pawn, speeds up development and enables a future check on f6. The b5-pawn can of course wait. '!'} Ra4 {This inventive move won't solve Black's problems, but he was already short of a good defence.} ({On} 16... Qc7 {; Botvinnik,M Fischer,R: '' White could consider} 17. O-O-O $5 Rxa2 18. Kb1 {with similar play to later on in the game.}) (16... Qa7 {Fischer,R} 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 18. Qxf6 Qe3 19. Kf1 $18 {Tal}) 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 (17... Kd8 $2 18. Qb6+ $18) 18. Qxf6 Qc7 (18... Qa7 {Fischer,R} 19. Bh5 $18) 19. O-O-O {'!' Fischer has observed that his king will be safe on b1, irrespective of the capture on a2. '!'} ({while both} 19. Bh5 d5 {Fischer,R: ''}) ({In contrast,} 19. Bxb5 $2 {failed to} Qa5+ {Fischer,R: '-+'}) ({and} 19. Bd3 Qc5 {give Black more hope of survival.}) 19... Rxa2 20. Kb1 Ra6 (20... Ra5 21. Bh5 d5 (21... d6 22. Rxd6 $1) 22. Rxd5 $1 exd5 23. Re1+ $18) ({Or} 20... Qa5 21. b3 $1 { h5 with the strong threat of 22 Bh5.}) 21. Bxb5 ({Another good option was} 21. Bh5 {Fischer,R: '!'} d6 ({or} 21... d5 22. Rxd5 $1) 22. Rhe1 Qe7 23. Qh6 Kd7 24. Qxh7 $18 {with a quick win – Fischer.}) 21... Rb6 (21... Ra5 {Fischer,R} 22. c4 $1 $18 {Tal} (22. Qh6 $2 Rxb5 23. Qxh7 Rxb2+ 24. Kxb2 Qb6+ 25. Kc1 Qe3+ 26. Rd2 Qa3+ 27. Kd1 Qf3+ $1 {Tal})) 22. Bd3 {Tal tries to reach an inferior endgame after} e5 {Fischer,R: '!?'} (22... Qd8 23. Qh6 f5 24. Qh5+ Ke7 25. g4 $40) 23. fxe5 $1 (23. Qxe5+ Qxe5 24. fxe5 Rxg7 $16 {, but in vain...}) 23... Rxf6 24. exf6 { xh7 Now the threat of 25 Bxh7 proves irresistible.} Qc5 $8 {Fischer,R: ''} (24... Qb6 {, then} 25. Rhf1 $18 {'!' – Fischer. '!'}) 25. Bxh7 Qg5 26. Bxg8 Qxf6 27. Rhf1 Qxg7 28. Bxf7+ {With such a material advantage, the rest is easy.} Kd8 $18 29. Be6 Qh6 (29... Kc7 30. Bf5 $36) 30. Bxd7 {'!' '!'} Bxd7 31. Rf7 Qxh2 32. Rdxd7+ {. White soon won.} Ke8 33. Rde7+ Kd8 34. Rd7+ (34. Rxb7 $5 {Fischer,R} Qh1+ 35. Ka2 Qd5+ 36. b3 Qa5+ 37. Kb2 Qe5+ 38. c3 Qe2+ 39. Ka3 Qa6+ 40. Kb4 Qd6+ 41. Kb5 Qd3+ 42. Kb6 Qe3+ (42... Qd6+ 43. Ka7 Qa3+ 44. Kb8 Qd6+ 45. Rfc7 Qxg3 46. b4 $18 {[%cal Yb4b6,Yc3c6] PS}) 43. Ka6 Qe6+ 44. Ka7 $18 {Tal}) 34... Kc8 35. Rc7+ Kd8 36. Rfd7+ Ke8 37. Rd1 b5 38. Rb7 Qh5 (38... Qxg3 39. Rxb5 $18) 39. g4 Qh3 (39... Qxg4 40. Rh1 Qd4 41. Rh8+ $1 Qxh8 42. Rb8+ $18) 40. g5 Qf3 41. Re1+ Kf8 42. Rxb5 Kg7 43. Rb6 Qg3 44. Rd1 Qc7 45. Rdd6 Qc8 (45... Qe7 46. Rg6+ Kh7 47. Rh6+ Kg7 48. Rbg6+ Kf8 49. Rh8+ Kf7 50. Rh7+ $18) (45... Qc5 46. Rb7+ Kg8 47. Rd8+ $18) 46. b3 Kh7 47. Ra6 (47. Ra6 Kg7 48. Ra7+ Kg8 49. Rdd7 $18) 1-0

