{Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E30: Nimzo-Indian: Leningrad Variation ''} 1. d4 {Chekhov} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky's favourite Leningrad line of the Nimzo-Indian.'} h6 5. Bh4 c5 6. d5 b5 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Is this some kind of a Blumenfeld Gambit of sorts? --- ANSWER: Tal's move is more goading than a direct slap across the cheek. I guess you can call it the Nimzo-Blumenfeld Indian. It seems to be a hybrid. Black is willing to offer a pawn to seize central control.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Normal in this position is'} 6... d6 7. e3 {Chekhov} Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qe7 (8... e5 {.}) 9. Nf3 Nbd7 10. Nd2 O-O 11. Be2 Ne5 12. Ne4 $1 $36 {Bareev,E-Chandler,M/Hastings/1991/1:0 (37) Inf 53/528/}) ({or} 6... Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 d6) 7. dxe6 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'There is also the tricky version'} 7. e4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} g5 {Chekhov} 8. Bg3 Nxe4 9. Be5 $13 {Levenfish,G-Estrin,J/URS/1951/} O-O 10. Qh5 d6 11. Bd3 Nxc3 12. Qxh6 Ne4+ 13. Kf1 dxe5 14. Bxe4 f5 15. Qg6+ Kh8 16. Qh6+ {and the game ends in perpetual check, A.Yermolinsky-A.Shabalov, Parsippany 1996.}) 7... fxe6 8. cxb5 ({White can also decline with} 8. e4 O-O 9. e5 Qa5 10. Nge2 ({but not} 10. exf6 $4 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qxc3+ 12. Ke2 Qxc4+ {and White's h4-bishop falls}) 10... Ne4 11. Qc2 Bb7 12. f3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Ba3 14. cxb5 a6 $1 {when White's underdeveloped position appears to be in some danger, E.Bareev-B.Gelfand, Moscow 1990.}) 8... d5 $1 (8... Qa5 {Ü 12/575}) 9. e3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' E30: Nimzo-Indian: Leningrad Variation'} O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Do you think Black has full compensation for the pawn? --- ANSWER: More than enough. For only one pawn, Black gets: 1. A development lead in an open position. --- 2. Central dominance. --- 3. An open f-file.'} ({QUESTION: Can Black try to disrupt with} 9... d4 {- ? --- ANSWER: This confrontation is premature. For example,} 10. a3 Bxc3+ ({or} 10... Ba5 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Qh5+ Qf7 13. Qxc5 {and White wins material}) 11. bxc3 Qa5 12. Rc1 dxe3 13. Qf3 $1 O-O 14. Qxa8 Qxa3 15. Ne2 Nbd7 16. Bxf6 exf2+ 17. Kxf2 Nxf6 18. Kg1 {. Black doesn't have enough of an attack to justify handing over a full rook.}) 10. Nf3 $2 {Chekhov: '?!' '?!' Chekhov,Valery A: '?!' '?!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky also tried'} 10. Bd3 d4 11. exd4 cxd4 12. a3 Ba5 13. b4 dxc3 14. bxa5 Bb7 15. Nf3 Qxa5 16. O-O Nbd7 17. Qe2 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 Ne5 19. Qe2 Nxd3 20. Qxd3 Nd5 {. If anyone stands better, it's Black, since his knight is anchored powerfully on d5, B.Spassky-W.Unzicker, Bath 1973.}) (10. a3 {Chekhov: '!?' '!?' Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?'}) 10... Qa5 {Chekhov: '!' '!' Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'N' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 3 Games' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A new move at the time;'} ({Predecessor: Cyrus Lakdawala: 'improving upon'} 10... Nbd7 11. Bd3 e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} 12. Bf5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} e4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} 13. Be6+ Kh8 14. Bxd5 Nxd5 15. Bxd8 Nxc3 16. bxc3 Bxc3+ 17. Nd2 Rxd8 18. Rc1 Ba5 19. O-O Ne5 20. Qh5 Bxd2 21. Qxe5 Bxc1 22. Rxc1 Bb7 23. h4 {1-0 (23) Spassky,B-Liebert,H Sochi (Russia) 1967 Cyrus Lakdawala: '1-0, B.Spassky-H.Liebert, Sochi 1967.'}) 11. Bxf6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'The position is equal.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now the precarious balance of power begins to swing in Black's favour.'} (11. Qc2 $1 Ne4 {was K.Sasikiran-N.Short, British Championship, Torquay 1998. Now, instead of playing the rook to c1, Komodo found the shocking and unplayed trick} 12. Bd3 $3 Nxc3 13. O-O $1 {. Believe it or not, White, down a full piece (for now) stands better, no matter how Black plays. For example,} Nxb5 14. a3 {regains the piece with an edge.}) 11... Rxf6 $44 12. Qd2 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Can White try'} 12. Qc1 {Chekhov: '!?' '!?' Chekhov: 'a3/' Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', intending a3 next? --- ANSWER: It looks rather slow. For example,'} a6 $1 {and if White accepts with} 13. bxa6 $2 {then} Bxa6 14. a3 Bxf1 15. Kxf1 Nd7 16. Ne2 c4 17. Qc2 Bd6 {looks highly unpleasant for White, who has a problem unravelling his h-rook.}) 12... a6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A thematic move in the position, seeking to increase his development lead.'} 13. bxa6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When we grab material when behind in development, we offer nothing, yet expect something. As we all understand, the world doesn't work that way. Spassky's patience begins to fray and now is in danger of a route.'} ({It's high time to go into emergency concession mode with} 13. Be2 axb5 14. O-O {, returning the pawn with a slightly inferior position.}) 13... Nc6 $1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Strongly threatening ...d4.'} 14. Be2 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move'' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): How did Tal seize the initiative?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Kasparov suggested'} 14. Qc1 $5 {Chekhov Cyrus Lakdawala: ', which may not help White after'} d4 15. a3 Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Bxa6 17. Bxa6 Qxa6 {. I still hate White's position and I'm not sure he is any better off here than with the game's continuation.}) 14... d4 $1 $17 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Step 1: Play upon the principle: Open the position and create confrontation when leading in development. --- This move requires immense powers of calculation to pull off.'} 15. exd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Step 2: Sacrifice the exchange by undermining a key defender of d4.'} Rxf3 $1 (15... cxd4 16. Nxd4 Nxd4 17. Qxd4 Bc5 18. Qa4 $1 $13 (18. Qe4 Bxf2+ 19. Kd1)) 16. Bxf3 cxd4 17. O-O {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It isn't easy to make sense of the disorienting vista. Spassky makes the best of what he has.'} (17. Rc1 {Chekhov Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} Bxa6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!'} 18. Bxc6 ({or} 18. Be2 Rd8 19. O-O dxc3 20. Qe3 Nd4 $1 21. Bxa6 Qxa6 22. bxc3 Ne2+ 23. Kh1 Nxc1 24. Rxc1 {with an extra piece for Black, plus initiative after} Rd3 25. Qe4 Rxc3) 18... Rd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'leaves White helpless, since'} 19. Qc2 (19. Rd1 {is met with the crushing} Qe5+ {.}) 19... dxc3 20. bxc3 Qe5+ 21. Be4 Bd3 $19) ({Don't blunder} 17. Bxc6 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'loses instantly to'} dxc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when White lacks a reasonable response.'} 18. bxc3 Bxc3 $19) 17... dxc3 18. bxc3 ({Also highly unpleasant was} 18. Qd1 cxb2 19. Rb1 Rxa6 20. Rxb2 Bc3 {.}) 18... Bxc3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Double Attack'} 19. Qd6 Rxa6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Hoping for ...Ne5.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In preparation for a coming pin.'} 20. Bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision/combination alert): It looks like Black has two promising moves to disconnect White's queen from his bishop. Black can play 20. ..Bb4, or 20...Be5. It looks like both moves favour Black. This isn't the case. --- There are two trails, with one of them false. Which one would you play?'} (20. Rad1 {Chekhov Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} Nd4 21. Qg3 e5 {with a dominating position for Black.}) 20... Bb4 $1 $15 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER!'} (20... Be5 $4 {fails miserably to} 21. Qe7 $1 {, and the problem is} Rxc6 $4 {is harshly met with} 22. Qe8+ Kh7 23. Qxc6 {when White is up two exchanges.}) 21. Qb8 Rxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'So Tal emerged with two bishops for Spassky's rook and pawn - a clear plus for Black. However, matters are not so simple for Black since he must still untangle and complete development.'} 22. Rac1 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White threatens to win material: Rc1xc6''} Bc5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is forced to self-pin.'} ({Naturally not} 22... Rxc1 $4 23. Rxc1 {when the pinned c8-bishop falls.}) 23. Rc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Spassky is unable to restore order. If you are assigned a clandestine mission, then it's unwise to call your enemy ahead of time to let him know you are coming. Aggression from a position of inferiority is a sign that a player wants to distract from his own strategic vices. Spassky hopes to build on the c-file pin, underestimating Tal's coming tactical resources.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It's not all that easy for Black to unravel after the counterintuitive'} 23. Rfd1 $15 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Shockingly, Black is unable to unravel without allowing White serious counterplay:'} Kh7 ({after} 23... Bxf2+ 24. Kh1 $1 Bc5 25. Rc2 Bd6 26. Rxc6 Bxb8 27. Rxc8+ Kh7 28. Rxb8 Qxa2 {White stands no worse}) 24. Kh1 Qa6 (24... Ba6 {is met with the unpleasant} 25. Rd8) 25. Rd8 Bd6 26. Qb1+ g6 27. h4 $1 e5 28. Rxc6 Qxc6 29. Qb3 {, threatening mate on g8. Black has nothing better than to take a draw with} Qc1+ 30. Kh2 Qf4+ 31. Kg1 Qc1+ {.}) 23... Qa4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Intending to slip over to the kingside to generate threats against White's king.'} 24. Qb3 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky avoids the trap'} 24. Rfc1 {Chekhov Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??'} Bxf2+ $1 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', winning.'}) 24... Qf4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'How long can Tal continue to avert disaster along the c-file? A gradual accretion of details offers us a better overall picture of Black's correct plan: Tal intends to distract Spassky's c-file pin project with direct threats to White's king.'} (24... Qe4 $5 {Chekhov}) 25. Qg3 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky, exhausted from walking through a carnival of perils, has had quite enough of Tal's attempted cheapos and desperately tries to remove queens from the board.'} (25. Qf3 {Chekhov: '!?' '!?' Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?'} Qc7 {Chekhov}) (25. Qb5 $5 Qd6 $17) (25. Rd1 $17) (25. Rfc1 $4 {is once again met with the dirty trick} Bxf2+ $1 26. Kh1 (26. Kf1 Bg3+ 27. Ke2 Ba6+ {forces mate}) 26... Qxc1+ {with mate in two moves.}) 25... Qf5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+'} 26. Rfc1 Bb7 $1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Threatens to win with ...Bd6.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Both black bishops take aim at White's king. From the position emerges a tumble of unanswered questions, with each side toiling feverishly on concurrent and opposite preoccupations: 1. Can White make anything of his c-file pin? --- 2. Can Black's tied up forces still continue to generate threats on White's king?'} 27. Qf3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' And now Qxf5 would win.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's queen continues her erratic dance.'} (27. h3) (27. Qb8+ $2 {Chekhov} Kh7 28. Qxb7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is once again met with'} Bxf2+ $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', winning.'}) 27... Qg5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' aiming for ...Bxf2+!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's pinned dark-squared bishop makes a mockery of White's rooks, as long as he goes unarrested and unhanged.'} 28. Qb3 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White has lost his nerve... understandable when you consider his position'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky alertly dodges yet another cheapo.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If'} 28. Qg3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??'} Bxf2+ $1 29. Kxf2 (29. Qxf2 {Chekhov} Qxc1+ $1 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins.'}) 29... Rxc2+ $19 30. Rxc2 Qf5+ 31. Kg1 Qxc2 32. Qb8+ Qc8 33. Qxc8+ Bxc8 $19) (28. h3 { g4 Chekhov,Valery A: '' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'might work better.'}) 28... Rc7 $19 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '-+'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Triple purpose: 1. Threatening mate on g2. --- 2. Covering the b7-bishop. --- 3. Continuing to cover the c5-bishop. --- Tal, probably the most mate-obsessed of all the world champions, was born with a rare birth defect: an attack-addled brain.'} (28... Bxf2+ $6 {Chekhov Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'fails this time:'} 29. Kxf2 Rxc2+ 30. Rxc2 Qxg2+ 31. Ke3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black has no more than a draw.'} Qf3+ 32. Kd2) 29. g3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Spassky, who up to this point, managed to turn back Tal's violent challenge, soon discovered that it is impossible to snuff out an approaching forest fire through force of will alone. How did Tal continue?'} (29. Qg3 {doesn't do any good Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Bxf2+ $1 {Deflection: c2} 30. Kxf2 (30. Qxf2 Qxc1+ 31. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 32. Qf1 Rxf1+ 33. Kxf1)) (29. Qxe6+ $4 Rf7 30. g3 Bxf2+ $1 {overloads White's c2-rook.}) 29... Bxf2+ $1 {Chekhov: '-+!' '-+!' Chekhov,Valery A: '-+!' '-+!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Attraction/annihilation of defensive barrier. Tal unveils his long-planned subterfuge, as his long pinned fugitive bishop evades justice. --- The wizard raises his arms, and a whirl of dead leaves begins to spin around his aura of power.'} 30. Kxf2 (30. Rxf2 Rxc1+) 30... Qf6+ (30... Qf5+ $5 {Chekhov} 31. Kg1) 31. Ke1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The king quivers, like pudding with a face.'} (31. Kg1 {Chekhov} Qd4+ $19 32. Kf1 Rf7+ 33. Ke1 Qe4+ 34. Re2 Qh1+ 35. Kd2 Rd7+ 36. Kc2 Rc7+ {wins.}) (31. Ke2 {Chekhov} Ba6+ (31... Qe5+ 32. Qe3 Ba6+ 33. Kf2 Rf7+ {picks off White's queen.})) 31... Qe5+ 32. Kf1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'For the remainder of the game, White's king bounces around like a popped kernel of corn in the microwavable bag.'} Ba6+ $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not a serious slip.'} (32... Qf5+ $142 {and Black wins} 33. Ke2 Qe4+ 34. Kd1 Rd7+ 35. Rd2 Qh1+ 36. Kc2 Rc7+ {wins}) 33. Kg1 Qd4+ 34. Kg2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} Qe4+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Black is up to no good.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"Our fates have always been intertwined, my love," whispers the queen, into the king's ear.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal should play'} 34... Bb7+ $142 {keeps an even firmer grip Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 35. Kh3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} Qe5 $19 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(threatening mate on h4)'} 36. g4 Qe4 37. a4 ({or} 37. Rxc7 Qg2+ 38. Kh4 Qxh2+ 39. Qh3 g5+ 40. Kh5 Qxh3+ 41. Kg6 Be4+ 42. Kf6 Qf3+ 43. Kxe6 Qxg4+ {and Black wins}) 37... g5 $1 38. a5 h5 $1 39. Rg1 hxg4+ 40. Rxg4 Rh7+ 41. Kg3 Qe1+ 42. Rf2 Qg1+ 43. Rg2 Qxg2# {.}) 35. Kg1 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'leads to further unpleasantness'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead:'} 35. Kh3 {Chekhov Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} Rxc2 36. Qxc2 Bf1+ $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'overloads White's rook.'}) (35. Kf2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is White's best practical chance. After'} Rf7+ (35... Qd4+) 36. Kg1 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black must find'} Bd3 $1 {(threatening a big check on e3)} 37. h4 Rf3 38. Kh2 Bxc2 39. Qxc2 Qe3 {(threatening both ...Rf2+ and also ...Rxg3)} 40. Qc8+ Rf8 41. Qc6 Rf2+ 42. Kh3 Qe5 43. Rc3 Qf5+ 44. g4 Qb1 45. Rg3 Qxa2 46. Qh1 h5 $1 {when White's king won't survive.}) 35... Bb7 {Chekhov,Valery A: '-+' '-+' 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '-+'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Black is clearly winning.' Chekhov: '-+' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' '!' '!' '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Tal sees the correct idea.' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', winning on the spot.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which leaves White helpless. Tal finds this theme much later in the game.'} 36. h4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' intending Kh2.'} (36. Qxb7 {there is nothing better in the position} Qe3+ 37. Kg2 (37. Kh1 Rxb7 38. Kg2 $19) 37... Rxb7 38. a3 $19) 36... Qh1+ 37. Kf2 Rf7+ (37... Qg2+ 38. Ke1 Qe4+ 39. Kd1) 38. Ke2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Even Komodo was exhausted from all the calculation which took place in this game:'} Qe4+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Accuracy: White = 29%, Black = 83%. ' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"I see very clearly that in the past I have been far too kind," says the queen. "Kind?" thinks White's king, who wouldn't describe his sister in that way. If the following lines look familiar, it's because we looked at similar themes in the earlier notes.'} 39. Qe3 {Chekhov} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or'} 39. Kd1 {Chekhov} Rd7+ 40. Rd2 Qh1+ 41. Kc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and simplest is'} Be4+ $19 (41... Rc7+ 42. Qc3 Rxc3+ 43. Kxc3 Qxc1+ 44. Rc2 Qa3+ 45. Kd2 Qxg3 46. Rc4 Qf2+ 47. Kc3 Qxa2 48. Kb4 Qb2+ 49. Ka5 Qa3+ 50. Kb6 Qb3+ 51. Kc5 Ba6 52. Rg4 Qb5+ 53. Kd4 Qc4+ 54. Ke3 Qxg4 55. Kd2 Qb4+ 56. Ke3 e5 57. Kf3 Qf4+ 58. Kg2 Bb7+ 59. Kg1 Qg3+ 60. Kf1 Ba6#) 42. Rd3 Rc7+ 43. Kd2 Qxc1+ 44. Ke2 Bxd3+ 45. Qxd3 Rc2+ 46. Kf3 Rc3 47. Ke2 Rxd3 48. Kxd3 e5 49. g4 Qd1+ 50. Ke3 Qd4+ 51. Ke2 e4 52. g5 Qd3+ 53. Kf2 Qf3+ 54. Kg1 e3 55. Kh2 e2 56. gxh6 e1=Q 57. h7+ Kxh7 58. a4 Qeh1#) 39... Ba6+ 40. Kd2 Rd7+ $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins White's queen.'} 0-1

