{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Keres, like Korchnoi, gave Tal a lot a trouble - but not in this game.'} 1. e4 {Chekhov Chekhov,Valery A Chekhov,Valery A Chekhov,Valery A Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. O-O ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} 5. Bxc6+ {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'} bxc6 {Chekhov,Valery A} 6. d4 exd4 (6... f6 {.}) 7. Qxd4) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Also played here are'} 5. c3 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'}) (5. c4 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'}) 5... Bd7 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This line is more solid than the strategically suspect'} 5... Bg4 $6 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '?!'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} 6. h3 {Chekhov,Valery A} h5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. We all fall sway to extremes of stylistic bias, from time to time. When we play a line as sharp as this one, which has fallen into theoretical disrepute, our opening variation becomes inextricably entangled with our loyalty to logic. After'} (6... Bh5 7. c3 Nf6 8. d4 b5 9. Bc2 Be7 10. d5 Nb8 11. a4 {left White with a pleasant strategic advantage in G.Klompus-S.Arzumanyan, USSR 1967}) 7. d4 b5 8. Bb3 Nxd4 ({or} 8... Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nf6 10. c3 exd4 {, A.Gurevich-E.Rompteau, correspondence 1966, when I like White's chances after} 11. Qe2 dxc3 12. Rd1 cxb2 13. Bxb2 {with a kind of super-Danish Gambit for the two pawn investment; Black's king will never be safe and White leads big time in development}) 9. hxg4 hxg4 ({or} 9... Nxb3 10. axb3 hxg4 11. Ng5 Qd7 12. Qd3 c6 13. c4 b4 14. c5 f6 15. Nd2 fxg5 16. Nc4 Qe6 17. Rd1 Qh6 18. Kf1 Qh1+ 19. Ke2 Qxg2 20. cxd6 Qf3+ 21. Qxf3 gxf3+ 22. Kxf3 {when Black's ragged pawn structure and lag in development mean a busted ending, V.Krishnan-T.Taylor, South California State Championship 2012; this win allowed my student to go on to win the State Championship}) 10. Ng5 Nh6 11. g3 (11. Bd5 c6 12. c3 cxd5 13. cxd4 Be7 14. Qd2 dxe4 15. dxe5 d5 16. Nc3 e3 17. Qxd5 Bxg5 18. Qc6+ Kf8 19. Bxe3 Bxe3 20. fxe3 {and Black was fighting for survival, N.De Firmian-J.Timman, Reykjavik 2000.}) 11... Qf6 12. c3 Nxb3 13. axb3 Be7 14. Qd5 $1 O-O 15. Na3 Qg6 16. Nxb5 Rac8 {Ziatdinov,R-Katishonok,N/URS/1990/0,5 (31) Inf 51/315/} 17. Rxa6 Bxg5 18. Rc6 $16) 6. c3 (6. c4 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'}) (6. d4 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'}) 6... Nge7 7. d4 Ng6 8. Re1 (8. d5 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'} Nb8 9. c4) (8. Be3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is also played here.'} Be7 {Chekhov,Valery A} 9. Nbd2 h6 10. d5 Nb8 11. c4 O-O 12. b4 c5 $6 (12... a5 $5) 13. bxc5 $16 {Yudasin,L-Castro/JUG(ol)/1990/1:0 (36) Inf 50/357/}) 8... Be7 9. Nbd2 h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why ...h6? Black already covers g5. --- ANSWER: Black may be planning to 'goodify' his bad bishop with a future ...Bg5, in which case he has the ...hxg5 option.'} 10. Nf1 Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'And here it is already. White isn't so tempted to chop it, since that would open the h-file for Black's rook.'} 11. Be3 (11. Ne3 {Chekhov,Valery A Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} Bxe3 12. Bxe3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black rids himself of his bad bishop and can be satisfied with the opening's outcome, M.Sagafos-T.Gareev, Cappelle la Grande 2007.'} O-O $13) 11... Bxe3 (11... Qf6 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'}) 12. Nxe3 O-O $11 13. Bc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop backs up e4, since it isn't of much use on a4.'} Re8 (13... Nh4 $5 {Chekhov,Valery A}) 14. Qd2 Rc8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The idea is to keep c7 covered in case of a future Nd5.'} ({Also logical is} 14... Nh4 15. Nxh4 Qxh4 16. Nd5 Rac8 {. The swap on h4 helped Black, who was the more cramped side, I.Kopylov-B.Kovernikov, correspondence 2005.}) 15. g3 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Keeping Black's knight out of f4.'} Qf6 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Attacking f3.'} (15... Bh3 $11 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '='' Chekhov: '!?'}) 16. Nd5 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Chekhov: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hey, I said: "Attacking f3"! Tal's last move is the spark which is the precursor to a future fire which scorches the land. He intends to respond to ...Qxf3 with Bd1, trapping Black's queen.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'On'} 16. Kg2 {Chekhov,Valery A Cyrus Lakdawala: ', Tal probably was worried about the line'} Bh3+ $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which the comps like for White's side, after'} 17. Kxh3 Qxf3 18. Nd5 Qh5+ 19. Kg2 Qg5 $1 $10 {Keres/ Cyrus Lakdawala: '. This was given an exclam by Keres, who assessed this position as even, while Komodo gives White an edge after'} 20. Qxg5 hxg5 21. Rad1 Nf8 22. Ne3 {.}) 16... Qxf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's entire premise must now be rethought and re-evaluated. Keres cleverly self-traps his queen, having seen a resource.'} 17. Bd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): It's easy to pick off a stray soldier - but not so easy when she is part of a military formation. It looks like Keres is about to lose a miniature. His queen is trapped. Or is it? Black to play and not lose his queen. --- ANSWER: Knight fork/clearance. Black's queen is far from trapped.'} Nh4 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Chekhov: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's forces are drawn to f3, as if to a loadstone. Any retreat runs counter to our sense of honour, which in this case is even more apt, since Black has no viable retreat for his queen! Even near the end of his life, Keres remained tactically astute, having foreseen this trick which extricates his queen.'} 18. gxh4 ({Not} 18. Bxf3 $4 Nxf3+ 19. Kg2 Nxd2 {and White finds himself down two pieces for zero compensation.}) 18... Qh3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): For most of us, when we land in such weary looking positions, our imaginations go inoperative. Not so with Tal. Now what? It looks as if Tal's position is collapsing. After all, his kingside structure is decimated, seemingly without compensation. However Tal found a miraculous saving idea. What is it? --- ANSWER: Line opening/knight fork.'} 19. Nf6+ {Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Chekhov: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The criminal knight melts discretely into the crowd. Amazingly. Tal is the only player I know who found hidden ways of seizing the initiative, while fettered and gagged. I don't know about you, but when I play over Tal's games, I get that disoriented, out-of-place feeling, as if I'm at a party where I don't know anyone. --- EXERCISE (critical decision): No time to rest. Should Black chop the knight, or should he give up the exchange with ...Kh8?'} Kh8 $2 {Chekhov,Valery A: '?!' '?!' Chekhov: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This attempt to quell the kingside commotion is an overreaction. Keres, undoubtedly startled by Tal's last move, unnecessarily hands over the exchange and now stands worse.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: He should accept the challenge with'} 19... gxf6 $1 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '!'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(it's not so easy to find your plan when there is an absence of identifying characteristics within the position; here we only see a maze of seemingly random lines)'} 20. Qxh6 exd4 21. Kh1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(threatening a nasty rook check on g1)'} Ne5 $13 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: ''' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(any defensive formation is only as strong as its weakest square, and somehow Black manages to hold the game by reinforcing g4)'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'alternatively,'} 21... Bf5 {Chekhov,Valery A Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} 22. Rg1+ Bg6 23. Rxg6+ fxg6 24. Qxg6+ Kf8 25. Qxf6+ Kg8 26. Bb3+ $18 (26. Bh5 {when there is no defence to the coming Rg1+})) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'while after'} 21... Rxe4 {Chekhov,Valery A} 22. Rg1+ Bg4 23. Bxg4 Rxg4 24. Rxg4+ Qxg4 25. Rg1 $18 Qxg1+ 26. Kxg1 dxc3 27. bxc3 Re8 28. Qxf6 Re6 {Black still has chances to erect a fortress draw, despite Komodo's completely winning assessment for White}) 22. Rg1+ {Chekhov,Valery A} Bg4 (22... Ng4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} 23. Bxg4 Bxg4 24. Rg3 $1 Qxg3 25. hxg3 dxc3 26. bxc3 Rxe4 27. Qxf6 {and White has reasonable chances to convert}) 23. Rg3 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White should probably avoid'} 23. cxd4 Nf3 $1 24. Qf4 (24. Rg2 Rxe4) 24... Kf8 $1 25. Rxg4 Qf1+) (23. Qf4 Kf8 24. Bxg4 Qxh4) 23... Qf1+ 24. Rg1 Qh3 $10 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'with a draw by repetition.'}) 20. Nxe8 Rxe8 21. h5 $14 Bg4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Keres goes after the h5 straggler, after which he gets a pawn for the exchange.'} (21... f5 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'} 22. exf5 {Chekhov,Valery A} Rf8) 22. Qe3 ({Also promising was} 22. f4 $1 {.}) 22... Qxh5 23. Kh1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal, hoping to turn a problem into a virtue, plans to attack down the opened g-file.'} Bxd1 (23... Ne7 $5 {Chekhov,Valery A}) 24. Raxd1 Qh4 (24... exd4 {Chekhov,Valery A} 25. cxd4 f5 26. f3 fxe4 27. fxe4) ({I would try} 24... g5 $5 {which clamps down on any f4 break, while discouraging White down the g-file.}) 25. Qf3 Kg8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Covering f7, but in doing so, placing his king on a more vulnerable position on the g-file.'} ({Black can also try to randomize the position with a line like} 25... Rf8 26. Rg1 exd4 27. cxd4 f5 $5 {.}) 26. Re3 Rf8 27. Qg3 Qe7 $2 {Chekhov,Valery A: '?!' '?!' Chekhov: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's queen remains unassimilated to the position's new reality.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More accurate was'} 27... Qf6 $1 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '!'' Chekhov: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(pressuring d4, while suppressing White's f4 break)'} 28. Rf3 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'} (28. d5 Ne7 {, in which case White can play for a c4-c5 queenside pawn break.}) 28... Qe7) 28. f4 $1 exf4 29. Qxf4 {Chekhov,Valery A: '' '' Chekhov: '' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not only did Tal fix his structure, but he also opened the f-file for his rooks.'} Re8 30. Rg1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening a cheapo on h6.'} Kh7 31. Rg4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This rook laterally covers e4, which frees the e3-rook to operate along the third rank.'} Nd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The long-alienated knight wishes to return to society, hoping to make good use of itself for kingside defence.'} 32. e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal immediately takes advantage of the fact that Black's knight no longer controls e5.'} d5 $2 {Chekhov,Valery A: '?!' '?!' Chekhov: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move stabilizes the centre, which favours White, since he is the one attacking.'} (32... Qf8 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'}) ({Keres didn't like} 32... Ne6 33. Qe4+ {, when White's queen picked off b7 next.}) 33. Rh3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Rxg7+, followed by Qxh6+.'} Qf8 (33... Ne6 {Chekhov,Valery A Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'covering g7, fails to cover g7!. White has'} 34. Rxg7+ $1 $18 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'with mate in two moves, no matter which way Black recaptures.'}) 34. Rf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal prevents ...Ne6, by tying the knight to defence of f7.'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I strongly suspect this is simply a ChessBase input error, and Keres actually played'} Kh8 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', in which case I owe both Keres and Tal apologies for giving them both unjust double question marks!'} (34... Kg8 {Chekhov,Valery A} 35. Qf5 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If this was the actual game, then both sides missed'} 35. Qxh6 $18 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) 35... Qe7 (35... Ne6 {was necessary.}) 36. b4 ({Tal clamps down on ...c5 counterplay, when he had} 36. Rh3 $1 Qe6 37. Qh5 {(re-threatening h6)} Kh7 38. Qg5 $1 Qg6 (38... Rg8 $2 {is met with} 39. Rxh6+ $1 Qxh6 40. Rh4 {, winning}) 39. Qh4 Qb6 40. Qg3 Ne6 41. Qf2 Nd8 42. Qc2+ Kh8 43. Qg2 Ne6 44. b3 {, and if} c6 $4 45. Qd2 $1 {threatening Rxh6+. There is no defence, since} Kh7 46. Rxg7+ $1 {forces mate.}) 36... Rf8 37.