1. e4 c5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = B63 - Sizilianisch-Rauser Variante /Sicilian-Rauser Variation'} 2. Nf3 d6 (2... Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 {1-0 (37) Tal,M-Koblencs,A Riga 1957 [Fritz 5.00 (5s)]}) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5 e6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The Richter-Rauzer line of the Sicilian.'} 7. Qd2 Be7 8. O-O-O O-O 9. Nb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move sets up Bxf6 ideas.'} (9. f4 {is more commonly played today.}) 9... Qb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Doesn't this move block Black's b-pawn? --- ANSWER: It does. As compensation, Black's queen vacates d8 for the rook, in preparation for a future ...d5 break.'} ({Less accurate is,} 9... Qc7 10. Bxf6 gxf6 {(R.Bellin-N.Oud, Guernsey 1989), when White's coming attack looks rather scary after} ({I don't see compensation for the pawn after} 10... Bxf6 $6 11. Qxd6 Qb6 12. Qg3) 11. h4 {.}) 10. f3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move solidifies e4, while setting up an English Attack style plan with g4 and h4 to follow.'} (10. Be3 Qc7 {doesn't bother Black, since after} 11. Nb5 Qb8 {White is unable to capture d6, while Black regains tempi with ...a6 and ...b5.}) ({One of Black's ideas of placing his queen at b6 is that} 10. Bxf6 $2 {just hands Black a valuable bishop after} Bxf6 {and White is unable to grab d6, since} 11. Qxd6 $2 {is met with} Qxf2 {, with a strategically won game for Black.}) 10... a6 11. g4 (11. Bxf6 {offers Black full compensation after} Bxf6 $1 12. Qxd6 Rd8 13. Qc5 Rxd1+ 14. Kxd1 Qc7 15. Qe3 b5 16. f4 b4 17. Na4 e5 {. Black gained several tempi for his attack, owns the bishop pair and also dominates the dark squares, which more than compensates White's extra pawn, V.Jansa-V.Babula, Lazne Bohdanec 1995.}) 11... Rd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move covers d6, which eliminates White's Bxf6 ideas. Now White must be on constant alert for ...d5.'} 12. Be3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' B63: Sicilian: Richter-Rauzer: 7...Be7 and unusual Black 7th moves' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move actually sends Black's queen to the square she wants to go to. In return, White clears the path for g5.'} Qc7 13. h4 $6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'N' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Inaccurate.'} ({Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Predecessor:' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White should take the steam out of Black's ...d5 with the immediate'} 13. g5 $1 {Timman Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' Edouard,Romain: 'should be played first as in the game Black would have had time for ...d5.' Edouard,Romain: 'sollte zuerst gespielt werden, da Schwarz in dieser Partie Zeit für ...d5 gehabt hätte.'} Ne8 (13... Nd7 14. f4 b5) 14. Qf2 Rd7 15. Na4 Rd8 16. Bb6 Bxg5+ 17. f4 {1-0 Brodsky,M-Kasimdzhanov,R/Cappelle op 1996/EXP 51}) 13... b5 $6 (13... d5 $1 $10 {Timman Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'takes advantage of the white queen's unfavourable position:' Edouard,Romain: 'Timman' Edouard,Romain: 'Timman'} 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Rxd5 16. Qe2 Rxd1+ 17. Qxd1 {, M.Hebden-N.Giffard, Paris 1988. Black's queenside attacking chances look more promising than White's on the other side after} b5 {.}) 14. g5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now we transpose back into White's correct move order.'} Nd7 15. g6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'N' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 10 Games' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal takes trespass of his coach's territory lightly, by offering a pawn to rapidly open kingside lines.'} (15. f4 b4 (15... Nc5 16. Bg2 b4 17. Ne2 a5 18. Nbd4 Bb7 19. Ng3 Nxd4 20. Bxd4 b3 21. Kb1 bxa2+ {½-½ (21) Djurasevic,B-Bogdanovic,R Belgrade 1952}) 16. Ne2 a5 17. Kb1 Bb7 18. Ng3 a4 19. Nc1 d5 20. exd5 Nb6 21. Bxb6 Qxb6 22. Bg2 Na5 23. Qe2 Ba6 24. Qg4 Nc4 25. dxe6 Na3+ 26. bxa3 bxa3+ 27. Nb3 axb3 28. axb3 a2+ 29. Ka1 Bb4 {Kislov,M-Stirberg,S/URS-sf Voronez 1991/EXP 22/½-½ (61)}) (15. h5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Nf8 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. Qxd4 Bb7 18. Kb1 Rac8 19. h6 e5 20. Qb6 Nd7 21. Qxc7 Rxc7 22. hxg7 Kxg7 23. Bh3 Rh8 24. Bf5 Nf8 25. a4 bxa4 26. Nxa4 Ne6 27. Rh5 Bc6 28. Nc3 Nf8 29. Nd5 Bxd5 {Liang Jinrong-Chan Peng Kong/Th