{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A Buddhist nun once told me: "If you begin to see yourself as important, then just look up at the stars at night to get a perspective of just how small and insignificant we all are." Old age comes for us all, in order to mock us. Tal, far from his past glory, was nearing the end of his life when he played this game. Yet even in his almost unrecognizably weakened state, we catch a glimpse of his old tricks.'} 1. e4 {Boensch,U: 'Der symphatische Exweltmeister ist tot. In seiner letzten Turnierpartie besiegte er Akopian. Trotz seines schlechten Gesundheitszustandes spielte er, wie in allen seinen Partien, sehr ideenreich. '} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 d6 4. O-O Bd7 5. Re1 (5. c3 a6 6. Bxc6 Bxc6 7. Re1) 5... Nf6 6. c3 a6 7. Ba4 ({The heavily analysed gambit Rogers: ' The heavily analysed gambit ' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I'm surprised that Tal avoided the messy gambit'} 7. Bxc6 {Rogers: ' '} Bxc6 {Rogers: ' '} 8. d4 {Rogers: ' '} Bxe4 {Rogers: ' '} 9. Bg5 {was not to Tal's taste against the well prepared World Junior Champion. Rogers: ' was not to Tal's taste against the well prepared World Junior Champion.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. --- QUESTION: This line seems tailor made for Tal? Why would he reject such a gambit? --- ANSWER: Tal wanted complications, but not the kind where a theoretically well-prepared opponent may have out-worked him in pre-game prep. His opponent, the then reigning world junior champion, was (and is) known for his excellent opening preparation.'} Bd5 10. Nbd2 c4 11. b3 b5 12. bxc4 bxc4 13. Nh4 $1 $146 {Ricardi-Cifuentes Paranda Buenos Aires 1991 -Inf.53/183}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead,'} 7. Bf1 $5 Bg4 {is another path for White.}) {Rogers: ' '} 7... c4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The idea is to suppress d4.'} ({More common is} 7... b5 8. Bc2 Bg4 {.}) {Rogers: ' '} 8. d4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move allows Black to equalize rather easily.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Probably White's only shot at an edge lies in the line'} 8. Bc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', intending to pressure the c4-pawn with b3, Na3 and Qe2.'} g6 (8... Rc8 9. Na3 b5 10. b3 Ne5 11. Nxe5 dxe5 12. bxc4 bxc4 13. d4 cxd3 14. Qxd3 e6 15. Rb1 Bc5 $10 {Plaskett,J-Olafsson,H Kobenhavn 1985 0-1 32 -Inf.39/226}) 9. b3 cxb3 10. axb3 Bg7 11. d4 O-O 12. Nbd2 b5 13. Nf1 Qc7 14. Ne3 $14 {Pacl,V-Madl,I/Kobanya op (11)/1991/½-½/40/}) {Rogers: ' '} 8... cxd3 {Rogers: ' '} 9. Bg5 {Rogers: ' '} e6 {Rogers: ' '} 10. Qxd3 {Rogers: ' '} Be7 (10... Ne5 11. Nxe5 Bxa4 12. Nc4 Bc6 13. Nbd2 b5 14. Ne3 Be7 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Nd5 $10 {Bronstein,L-Timman,J/Rio de Janeiro (izt)/1979/}) {Rogers: ' '} 11. Bxf6 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A move made with the thought: strip a person of his theoretical armour, and he becomes someone else. Tal was the merchant who dealt with likelihoods and possibilities. In his younger days, he nearly always got the better of the bargain when he haggled with such unbalancing moves.'} {Rogers: ' '} gxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Is Black okay here after the damage to his structure? --- ANSWER: It's debatable if Black's structure has been damaged at all, since he gets greater central influence and an open g-file. This looks to me like a rather lame version of a Richter-Rauzer, for Tal's side, since White has a pawn, rather than a knight on c3 and his f-pawn, normally on f4, sits limply on f2. So I think Black is certainly no worse here.'} {Rogers: ' '} 12. Bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?'} ({Calmer is} 12. c4 Ne5 13. Bxd7+ Qxd7 14. Qe2 Rc8 15. b3 Rg8 {.}) {Rogers: ' '} 12... Bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I would be more inclined to take back with the b-pawn.'} {Rogers: ' '} 13. c4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'For now, Tal's knights seem to be holding their own against Black's bishop pair. The trouble with such positions is sooner or later, the game tends to open, favouring the bishops.'} {Rogers: ' '} O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Intending ...Kh8, ...Rg8 and at some point a possible ...f5 break.'} {Rogers: ' '} 14. Nc3 Kh8 $1 {Black's bishops are temporarily under control but, with Black preparing to attack along the g file, the game is likely to open up at any moment. Rogers: ' Black's bishops are temporarily under control but, with Black preparing to attack along the g file, the game is likely to open up at any moment.'} {Rogers: ' '} 15. Rad1 {Rogers: ' '} Rg8 {Rogers: ' '} 16. Qe3 {Rogers: ' '} Qf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black can maybe load up major pieces along the g-file.'} {Rogers: ' '} 17. Nd4 {Rogers: ' '} Rc8 (17... Bd7 18. a4 {keeps the bishops behaving for now.}) {Rogers: ' '} 18. f4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?'} ({I would have played} 18. Nxc6 Rxc6 19. b3 Qg7 20. g3 Bd8 21. Ne2 {, with an approximately balanced game.}) {Rogers: ' '} 18... Bd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I think it's a good idea to retain both bishops, now that Tal committed to the loosening f4.'} {Rogers: ' '} 19. b3 {Rogers: ' '} Bd8 {Boensch,U: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening to swing over to b6.'} {Rogers: ' '} 20. Nf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Cutting off ...Bb6.'} {Rogers: ' '} b5 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The game continues to open.'} (20... Rc6 $5) {Rogers: ' '} 21. Qa7 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now White's counterplay trickles through his fingers like water. Here we see Tal's old weakness: an overestimation of his chances. Sometimes a little fear is actually a sign of wisdom, since fear tends to keep us alive.'} {Rogers: ' '} Bc7 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Target: f4.'} ({Also promising was} 21... Ba5 $1 22. Qxd7 Bxc3 23. Re2 d5 $1 24. Qa7 $1 ({not} 24. exd5 $2 Qc5+ 25. Kf1 Rc7 {when unwanted suitors line up to woo White's queen; this move traps the queen for insufficient compensation after} 26. dxe6 Rxd7 27. exd7 Ba5) 24... dxc4 {Black stands clearly better since} 25. Qxa6 $2 Qc5+ 26. Kf1 Rcd8 {is busted for White, as...Rd3 is coming.}) {Rogers: ' '} 22. Qxa6 {Rogers: ' '} bxc4 {Rogers: ' '} 23. b4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This smacks of desperation;'} ({but after} 23. bxc4 d5 24. Kh1 f5 $1 {(threat: ...Qg7, with simultaneous attacks on g2 and c3)} 25. cxd5 Qg7 26. Rg1 Qxc3 27. d6 Qa5 28. Qe2 Bb6 29. Ne5 Rg7 $1 30. Nxd7 Bxg1 31. Rxg1 {forced;} (31. Kxg1 $4 Qa7+ {picks up the loose knight}) 31... Qd8 32. Ne5 Qxd6 {White is unlikely to survive.}) {Rogers: ' '} 23... Qg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening mate.'} (23... d5 $1 {is promising for Black, as} 24. exd5 {, is met with} ({while after} 24. Qb7 Qh6 {I don't think White will survive Black's bishops}) 24... Qxb4 25. dxe6 Bxe6 26. Nb5 Bxf4 {when Black's bishops come alive.}) {Rogers: ' '} 24. g3 {Rogers: ' '} d5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Uncovering an attack on f4.'} ({Akopian's move is more dynamic than} 24... f5 25. e5 d5 {.}) 25. exd5 $6 {A brave and perhaps foolhardy decision which tempts Black into a near decisive attack. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Clearly Tal is a long way from pleased in this souring position. There is nothing better.'} ({White's 23rd move, which tried to keep the position closed, clearly foreshadowed Rogers: ' A brave and perhaps foolhardy decision which tempts Black into a near decisive attack. White's 23rd move, which tried to keep the position closed, clearly foreshadowed '} 25. e5 {with an unclear position. Rogers: ' with an unclear position.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} Qg4 {with a double attack on f3 and f4:} 26. Kf2 fxe5 27. Nxe5 Bxe5 28. Rxe5 Qh3 {and White is lost. If} 29. Kg1 $2 Rxg3+ $1 30. hxg3 Qxg3+ 31. Kh1 Qxc3 {leaves White's king hopelessly exposed.}) {Rogers: ' '} 25... Bxf4 {Rogers: ' '} 26. Kf2 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision): Tal has been completely outplayed, but be careful: when a position grows more complex, the side who stands better - obviously Black, in this instance - has more to lose. This is that disorienting moment when the ocean sucks its own water from the beach, just moments before the tsunami. Black's candidate moves: a) 26...Bxg3+ sacrificing a piece to expose White's king. b) 26...f5 opening an attack on White's loose c3-knight. c) The calm 26...exd5, opening the game for his bishops. Only one of the possibilities leads to a decisive advantage for Black. Which?'} ({After} 26. Ne2 Be3+ 27. Kh1 Qh6 {White won't survive the attack.}) 26... f5 $2 {Black is looking for the knock-out blow when any retreat of the bishop would leave White's king exposed. Rogers: ' Black is looking for the knock-out blow when any retreat of the bishop would leave White's king exposed.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'An old template shouldn't be applied to new circumstances. When we miss a decisive combination, we are the person who is unexpectedly bequeathed a fortune by a distant relative. But then we irrationally reject the money, thinking: "The best things in life are free."'} (26... Bxg3+ 27. hxg3 Qxg3+ 28. Ke3 (28. Ke2 Qg2+ ({Black's main problem is that he is unable to open the e-file with} 28... exd5 $4 29. Qxf6+ Rg7 30. Rg1 Re8+ 31. Kd2 Qf2+ 32. Kc1 Reg8 33. Rxg7 Rxg7 34. Rg1 {and wins}) 29. Ke3 Rce8 {and the trouble is White can clog things with} 30. d6 e5 31. Rg1 Qc2 32. Qa3 {. White should survive this attack and consolidate.}) 28... exd5 29. Qxf6+ $18) ({ANSWER: Correct was 'c',} 26... exd5 $1 {(principle: open the game when you own bishops versus knights):} 27. Ne2 (27. gxf4 $4 Qg2+ 28. Ke3 Rge8+ {and White is slaughtered.} (28... Z0)) (27. Nxd5 $4 Bxg3+ 28. hxg3 Qxg3+ 29. Ke3 Rc6 $1 30. Qa7 {(inept white defenders fly like dust from a beaten rug)} Bg4 {when it becomes obvious that White's king has no prayer.}) 27... Bh6 28. Rxd5 Be6 29. Rc5 Ra8 30. Qc6 Rxa2 31. Rxc4 ({or} 31. Nfd4 Bd2 32. Rd1 Qh6 33. Kg2 Be3 {is decisive}) 31... Bxc4 32. Qxc4 Rga8 {with an extra exchange and a sustaining initiative.}) {Rogers: ' '} 27. gxf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} {Rogers: ' '} Qxc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black regained his piece and looks like he is just winning, until we see Tal's next move.'} {Rogers: ' '} 28. Qd6 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Attacking Black's bishop, while leaving open possibilities of a queen swap on e5. Black no longer stands even an iota better.'} {Rogers: ' '} Ba4 {Rogers: ' '} 29. Rd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Interfering with the black queen's coverage of the a1-h8 diagonal.'} {Rogers: ' '} Rg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In time pressure, those normally steady neurotransmitters in our brain speed up. The result? We either play brilliant, inspirational chess, or, we go haywire and short circuit, as is the case here.'} (29... Rg6 {was necessary to maintain the balance:} 30. dxe6 Rcg8 $1 31. Ng5 f6 $1 32. Nf7+ Kg7 33. Ng5 $1 {(threat: Qe7+ and Qxh7 mate)} Kh8 $1 (33... fxg5 $4 34. Qe5+ Kh6 35. fxg5+ Kh5 36. Rh4+ $1 Kxh4 37. Qxc3 {picks off Black's queen}) 34. Nf7+ {is drawn by perpetual.}) {Rogers: ' '} 30. dxe6 Bc6 {Black still believes he is attacking but Tal has a typical surprise in store. Rogers: ' Black still believes he is attacking but Tal has a typical surprise in store.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening mate on the move. This looks scary, but Tal has everything under control.'} {Rogers: ' '} 31. Ng5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A lock snaps shut on Black's attack, as Tal clogs the g-file.'} ({Akopian may have counted on} 31. Re3 $2 Rg2+ $1 32. Kxg2 Qxe3 33. Qe5+ Qxe5 34. fxe5 fxe6 35. Kf2 Bd5 36. Rd1 c3 37. a3 Ra8 38. Ra1 c2 39. Nd4 Rxa3 40. Nxc2 Rb3 {. White's pawns are loose and he is unlikely to save himself in the ending.}) 31... Rxg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): How did Tal force the win of heavy material?'} ({Clearly Rogers: ' Clearly ' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Alternatively:'} 31... fxe6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and now the simplest is'} {Rogers: ' '} 32. Qe5 {is hopeless for Black but now doesn't 32.fxg5 f3+ mate for Black? Cyrus Lakdawala: 'threat: Rd8+ and Qxc3;'} ({the comp likes the inhuman} 32. Rxe6 {and wins after a few impossible-to-find moves}) {is hopeless for Black but now doesn't 32.fxg5} 32... Qf3+ {mate for Black?} (32... Re8 33. Rd6 Qxe5 34. Rxe5 Bd7 35. Nxe6 {with a lost ending for Black, due to White's two connected queenside passed pawns, plus extra pawn.})) (31... f6 {allows} 32. Qd8+ $1 {, and if} Rxd8 33. Rxd8+ Rg8 34. Nf7+ Kg7 35. Rg1+ {. Now Black only gets two spite-blocks with} Bg2 36. Rxg2+ Qg3+ 37. Rxg3# {.}) 32. Qe5+ $1 {Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy. Rogers: ' Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy.' Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy. Rogers: ' Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy.' Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy. Rogers: ' Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy.' Boensch,U: '+-!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Queen trap/discovered attack/zwischenzug. Black has no defence to the coming Rd8+ and Qxc3. --- "Knock, knock!" sings the queen. Neither Black's king or queen, however, are in any mood to say "Who's there?".'} ({I hope nobody chose} 32. fxg5 $4 Qf3+ 33. Kg1 Qg2# {.}) {Rogers: ' '} 32... Rg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When we get swindled, we find ourselves playing the lead role in a comedy, which is funny to everyone else but us.'} {Rogers: ' '} 33. Rd8+ {Rogers: ' '} Rxd8 {Rogers: ' '} 34. Qxc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One can never bow low enough to satisfy White's queen. The rest is easy.'} {Rogers: ' '} f6 {Rogers: ' '} 35. e7 {Rogers: ' '} Ra8 {Rogers: ' '} 36. Qxf6 {Rogers: ' '} Be4 {Rogers: ' '} 37. Rg1 {Rogers: ' '} Rxa2+ {Rogers: ' '} 38. Ke1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Black faces two (three, if you count under-promotion with e8R+!) mate threats.'} 1-0

