{Breutigam,Martin: 'Three times I had the incredible pleasure of meeting Michail Tal. At the first meeting in 1986, I accompanied him shopping through my home town of Bremen. Tal had flown in from Riga to give a few simultaneous events in their twin city of Bremen. From his fee, he wanted to buy for the family that stayed at home, a coat for wife Angelina, a skirt for daughter Zhanna and the like. The warmth of the heart of the chain-smoking maestro with the wild grey hair could be felt from the first moment. How respectfully the famous former world champion treated a young, clueless person like me!  That morning, Tal also had a very special encounter. I quote from my book 'Geniuses in Black and White': "In a department store he suddenly rushed to a woman and she also greeted him warmly. When he returned, he asked: 'Do you know who that was?' I shook my head. 'That was Bella Korchnoi, Viktor Korchnoi's first wife.' She happened to be visiting Bremen. A few years earlier she had been abused as a political pawn. For years, the USSR refused to allow her to leave the country, while her then husband, the Soviet refugee Viktor Korchnoi, played against the Russian world champion Anatoly Karpov for the world championship." Bella and Mikhail apparently had no problem with each other at all, although Tal had been a second on the Karpov team during the 1978 World Championship duel.  Ten years later, in this game against Speelman, Tal incidentally chose the same variation as Karpov in the decisive 32nd World Championship game in 1978. It must have taken a genius to foresee the enormous power of a surprising rook shift like 23.Rb3!  ' Breutigam,Martin: 'Dreimal hatte ich die unfassbare Freude, Michail Tal treffen zu dürfen. Bei der ersten Begegnung im Jahr 1986 begleitete ich ihn beim Shoppen durch meine Heimatstadt Bremen. Tal war aus Riga eingeflogen, um in deren Partnerstadt Bremen ein paar Simultanveranstaltungen zu geben. Von seinem Honorar wollte er für die daheim gebliebene Familie einkaufen, einen Mantel für Ehefrau Angelina, einen Rock für Tochter Zhanna und dergleichen mehr. Die Herzenswärme des Kette rauchenden Maestros mit dem wilden grauen Haar war vom ersten Moment an zu spüren. Wie respektvoll der berühmte Exweltmeister einen jungen, unbedarften Menschen wie mich behandelte!  An jenem Vormittag kam es für Tal auch zu einer Begegnung der besonderen Art. Ich zitiere aus meinem Buch ‚Genies in Schwarzweiß‘: „In einem Kaufhaus eilte er plötzlich zu einer Frau, und auch sie begrüßte ihn herzlich. Als er zurückkam, fragte er: ‚Weißt du, wer das war?‘ Ich schüttelte den Kopf. ‚Das war Bella Kortschnoi, die erste Frau von Viktor Kortschnoi.‘ Sie war zufällig zu Besuch in Bremen. Ein paar Jahre zuvor hatte man sie noch als politischen Faustpfand missbraucht. Jahrelang verweigerte ihr die UdSSR die Ausreise, währenddessen ihr damaliger Ehemann, der Sowjetflüchtling Viktor Kortschnoi, gegen den russischen Weltmeister Anatoli Karpow um die Weltmeisterschaft spielte.“ Bella und Michail hatten offensichtlich überhaupt kein Problem miteinander, obwohl Tal während des WM-Duells 1978 Sekundant im Team Karpow gewesen war.  Zehn Jahre später, in dieser Partie gegen Speelman, wählte Tal übrigens die gleiche Variante wie Karpow in der entscheidenden 32. WM-Partie 1978. Man muss wohl ein Genie sein, um die enorme Kraft eines überraschenden Turmschwenks wie 23.Tb3! vorauszusehen. '} 1. e4 {Breutigam,Martin Beim} d6 2. d4 g6 3. Nf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is an annoying move order for Modern Defence players. --- QUESTION: Why? --- ANSWER: Because White can keep Black guessing about possible transpositions to queen's pawn lines by tossing in c4.'} Bg7 4. Be2 Nf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move transposes to the Pirc.'} ({I have faced} 4... a6 {I was more afraid of} 5. c4 {where I may be entering a King's Indian set-up with an early ...a6, which I may not need. --- QUESTION: How would you have continued if your opponent had played this way? --- ANSWER: I don't play the straight KID, so I would have probably attempted to muck things up with something like} (5. Nc3 Nd7 6. Bf4 e6 $5 7. Qd2 b5 8. a3 h6 9. O-O Ne7 10. Rae1 g5 $5 {with the messy kind of position the higher-rated player generally likes, when playing down, J.Choi-C.Lakdawala, San Diego (rapid) 2013.} (10... Z0)) 5... Bg4 6. Nc3 Nc6 7. Be3 e5 {, and if} 8. d5 Bxf3 9. Bxf3 Nd4 {with a strange KID hybrid position, C.Milton-C.Lakdawala, San Diego (rapid) 2006}) 5. Nc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I already mentioned this earlier in the chapter, in his win against Petrosian, that I found odd Tal's attraction to this very quiet white set-up against the Pirc and Modern Defences.'} O-O 6. O-O c5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The Schmidt Benoni is one of Black's most ambitious tries against White's set-up. This move was played in Korchnoi's ill-fated final game of his 1978 Baguio match loss to Karpov, when the score stood at a nail-biting 5-5, with many draws.' Breutigam,Martin: 'To this move, which offers a transition into Benoni structures, there are, of course, a number of alternatives that have become more popular not only since today. Above all ...' Breutigam,Martin: 'Zu diesem Zug, der einen Übergang in Benoni-Strukturen anbietet, gibt es natürlich eine Reihe von Alternativen, die nicht erst seit heute populärer sind. Vor allem ...'} (6... Nc6 {Breutigam,Martin and und}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} 6... a6 {Breutigam,Martin Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) ({Also played are} 6... c6) (6... Bg4) (6... Nbd7) 7. d5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is the only real path to a white advantage, since White gets a limp Dragon if he allows ...cxd4. Korchnoi famously accused GM Ray Keene, one of his seconds in the Baguio match, of "selling" 7 d5! to Karpov.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Tal, like his then 'boss' Karpov ten years earlier in the all-decisive 32nd World Championship game, goes straight for a Benoni type.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Tal leitet wie zehn Jahre zuvor sein damaliger "Chef" Karpow in der alles entscheidenden 32. WM-Partie schnurstracks in einen Benoni-Typ über.'} ({Korchnoi said after the said game that he was surprised by Karpov's decision and that he had calculated with Kortschnoi sagte nach der besagten Partie, dass ihn Karpows Entscheidung überrascht und er mit} 7. dxc5 {Breutigam,Martin gerechnet habe.}) 7... Na6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What is Black's intent behind this move? --- ANSWER: Black's source of counterplay stems from queenside counterplay based on a ...b5 push. So Black usually plays ...Nc7, ...Rb8, ...b6, ...a6, and if necessary ...Bd7 to ensure his ...b5 break. The obvious trouble with this plan is its slowness. If handed all this time, White generally enforces a successful e5 central break. Sometimes Black actually fianchettos his light-squared bishop to take the sting out of White's intended e5 break.' Breutigam,Martin: 'White relies on his stable center pawn duo; Black wants to attack this as soon as possible. Either with ...e6 or like Speelman here with ...Na6-c7 and possibly ...b5-b4 (to put the e4 point under pressure). In certain cases (e.g. when White plays a2-a4 and Nd2-c4) also ...Nc7, ...b6 together with ...Ba6 is a standard plan.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Weiß baut auf sein stabiles Bauernduo Zentrum; Schwarz will dieses möglichst bald angreifen. Entweder mit ...e6 oder wie Speelman hier mit ...Sa6-c7 und ggf. ...b5-b4 (um den Punkt e4 unter Druck zu setzen). In bestimmten Fällen (z.B. wenn Weiß a2-a4 und Sd2-c4 spielt) ist auch ...Sc7, ...b6 nebst ...La6 ein Standardplan.'} 8. Re1 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Alternatives:' Karpov played first Breutigam,Martin: 'Tal, by the way, was also familiar with this type of position as a black player, e.g.' Karpow hatte erst Breutigam,Martin: 'Tal war übrigens auch als Schwarzspieler vertraut mit diesem Stellungstyp, z.B.'} 8. Bf4 {Breutigam,Martin and reached an ideal position after a few moves: gezogen und nach wenigen Zügen eine Idealstellung erreicht:} Nc7 9. a4 (9. Nd2 {Breutigam,Martin} b5 10. Nxb5 Nxb5 11. Bxb5 Rb8 12. a4 Nh5 13. Bg5 Bxb2 14. Rb1 Bg7 15. Nc4 $14 {½-½ (42) Najdorf,M-Tal,M Moscow 1967}) 9... b6 10. Re1 Bb7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. --- QUESTION: Why did Black just fianchetto his bishop into a pawn wall on d5? --- ANSWER: This can be considered an anti-strategy. The idea is to pile up on d5, to make White's e5 break difficult. If White does nothing, then Black can generate play by later striving for an ...e6 break.'} (10... a6 11. h3 Nd7 (11... Bb7 12. Bc4 Qd7 13. Qd3 Rad8 14. Rad1 Qc8 15. Qe3 Rfe8 16. Bh6 e6 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. dxe6 Nxe6 19. Nd5 Bxd5 20. Bxd5 Nd4 21. Nxd4 Nxd5 22. Nf5+ Qxf5 23. Rxd5 Qxd5 24. exd5 Rxe3 {= Yusupov - Ljubojevic, Linares, 1992}) 12. Bc4 Rb8 13. Qd3 Bb7 (13... f6 {?!} 14. Rab1 {!} Ne5 15. Nxe5 fxe5 16. Be3 $16 {Khalifman - Ermenkov, Elenite, 1994}) 14. Rad1 $14) 11. Bc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(reinforcing d5)'} Nh5 12. Bg5 Nf6 13. Qd3 a6 14. Rad1 $14 {1-0 (41) Karpov,A (2725)-Kortschnoj,V (2665) Baguio City 1978 Karpov won this game flawlessly and thus defended his title. Korchnoi, however, was widely criticized afterwards for his risky choice of opening in this important game. Karpow gewann diese Partie tadellos und verteidigte damit seinen Titel. Kortschnoi indes wurde hinterher vielfach für seine riskante Eröffnungswahl in dieser so wichigen Partie kritisiert.} Rb8 {was A.Karpov-V.Korchnoi, World Championship (Game 32), Baguio City 1978. Korchnoi found himself caught in Karpov's icy cold embrace and lost the game. White at this point could have played an immediate} 15. e5 $1 {.}) (8. h3 Nc7 9. a4 b6 10. Bf4 $14) 8... Nc7 9. Bf4 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This may be inaccurate, due to Speelman's next move.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Surprisingly allows the exchange of a central pawn for a wing pawn.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Lässt überraschenderweise den Abtausch eines zentralen Bauern gegen einen Flügelbauern zu.'} ({Many years earlier, Tal on the other side of the board after Viele Jahre zuvor war Tal einmal auf der anderen Seite des Brettes nach} 9. h3 $5 {Breutigam,Martin got into an uncomfortable position: in eine unbequeme Lage geraten:} b6 ({Here Hier wäre} 9... b5 $2 {would be much weaker than in this game, because of viel schwächer als in dieser Partie, wegen} 10. Bxb5 Nxb5 11. Nxb5 $16) 10. a4 Bb7 11. Bf4 (11. Bc4 a6 12. Qd3 e6 $5 13. dxe6 Nxe6 14. Bd2 Qc7 15. Rad1 Rfd8 {was the later A.Karpov-E.Torre, Brussels 1987. Black would be in trouble if Karpov had found} 16. Ng5 $1 Bc8 17. Bc1 {when} h6 {could be met with} 18. Nxe6 Bxe6 19. Bxe6 fxe6 20. e5 dxe5 21. Qxg6 {with a winning structural advantage for White.}) 11... Qd7 12. Qd2 Rfe8 13. Bc4 Rad8 14. Bh6 e6 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. dxe6 fxe6 17. Bb5 Nxb5 18. axb5 $16 {½-½ (34) Alburt,L-Tal,M Leningrad 1974}) ({Three years after the game Tal-Speelman the Briton got faced with Drei Jahre nach der Partie Tal-Speelman bekam der Brite} 9. a4 {Breutigam,Martin Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'suppresses the ...b5 break, and is White's path to an edge.' and could be satisfied with the further course: vorgesetzt und konnte mit dem weiteren Verlauf zufrieden sein:} b6 10. a5 Rb8 11. axb6 axb6 12. e5 Ng4 13. exd6 exd6 14. Nb5 Nxb5 15. Bxb5 Bd7 16. Qd3 Ne5 17. Nxe5 Bxe5 18. Bxd7 Qxd7 19. c3 b5 20. Bh6 Rfe8 21. Re3 b4 22. Rae1 bxc3 23. bxc3 Bg7 24. Bxg7 Kxg7 25. c4 {½-½ (25) Huebner,R (2580)-Speelman,J (2507) Leon 2011}) 9... b5 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Speelman alertly seizes upon Tal's inaccuracy. He kind of gets a mild Benko Gambit idea, without having sacrificed a pawn.'} 10. Nxb5 ({Breutigam,Martin: 'In contrast to 9.h3 (cf. previous note)' Breutigam,Martin: 'Im Unterschied zu 9.h3 (vgl. vorige Anmerkung) wäre hier'} 10. Bxb5 $2 {Breutigam,Martin: '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'fails miserably to' Breutigam,Martin: 'would be a bad mistake - because of' Breutigam,Martin: 'ein schlimmer Fehler - wegen'} Nh5 $17 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when White loses material to the dual threat ...Nxf4, and also ...Bxc3, followed by ...Nxb5.' Breutigam,Martin: 'with figure gain, e.g.' Breutigam,Martin: 'mit Figurengewinn, z.B.'} 11. Bd2 {Breutigam,Martin} Bxc3 12. Bxc3 Nxb5 $19) ({Instead:} 10. a3 b4 11. axb4 cxb4 12. Na2 a5 13. Bc4 Nh5 14. Bc1 {also looks fine for Black, who forced several of Tal's pieces to retreat, while he gained valuable queenside space.}) 10... Nxe4 11. Nxc7 $146 Qxc7 12. Bc4 Nf6 $11 {= Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black swapped away a wing pawn for White's more valuable e-pawn. --- QUESTION: Does White get pressure on Black's backward e7-pawn? --- ANSWER: A little, but not enough to worry Black, who can cover the pawn as many times as White can attack it.'} 13. Rb1 (13. h3 {Beim} Re8 {1-0 (34) Tal,M (2610)-Speelman,J (2645) Reykjavik 1988 CBM 119 [Beim]}) (13. Qe2 Re8 14. Bb5 Nd7 (14... Bd7) 15. c4 Rb8 16. Rab1 Bb7 17. Rbd1 a6 $13) 13... Re8 (13... Nh5 14. Bg5) (13... Rb8 $13) 14. h3 a5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White can't really make any use of b5.'} (14... Nd7 $5 {!?} 15. Bb5) (14... Rb8 $5 { b4 !? 'with the idea' Rb4} 15. Bb3 Ba6 16. Ba4 Red8 17. b3 c4 18. Bc6 e5 $13) 15. Qd2 Qb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} (15... Ba6 {!?} 16. Bxa6 Rxa6 17. c4 $14 a4) (15... a4 {looks more consistent.}) 16. Re3 {Breutigam,Martin: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal prepares to load up heavy pieces on e7;' Breutigam,Martin: 'Pressure play in the e-file looks very logical. But strictly speaking, the plan b2-b3 together with a2-a4 (or vice versa) suggested by engines today would have been more accurate here. In fact, White could and should first reliably support the important d-pawn in this way, e.g.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Druckspiel in der e-Linie sieht sehr logisch aus. Doch streng genommen wäre hier der heute von Engines vorgeschlagene Plan b2-b3 nebst a2-a4 (oder umgekehrt) genauer gewesen. Tatsächlich könnte und sollte Weiß auf diese Weise zunächst den wichtigen d-Bauern zuverlässig unterstützen, z.B.'} (16. b3 $1 {Breutigam,Martin} Ba6 17. a4 Qb4 ({If Falls} 17... Bxc4 $6 {White could immediately use the control of the b5-square: , könnte Weiß die Kontrolle des Feldes b5 sofort nutzen:} 18. bxc4 Qc7 19. Rb5 $16) (17... Rab8 18. Bxa6 Qxa6 19. c4 {, when White will be tied down to b3, just as much as Black is to e7. I still slightly prefer Tal's side here, due to his extra space.}) 18. c3 Qb7 19. Bxa6 Rxa6 20. c4 Rb6 21. Qc2 Rb8 22. Nd2 $14 {[%cal Ge1e3,Gb1e1,Gd2b1,Gb1a3,Ga3b5]}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'rather than' Or Oder} 16. a4 $1 {Breutigam,Martin} Ba6) 16... Ba6 {Breutigam,Martin: '!'} (16... a4 $13) 17. Bxa6 Qxa6 18. Rbe1 Kf8 $6 {?!} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After' Breutigam,Martin: 'Worth considering was immediately pressing the unstable d-pawn:' Breutigam,Martin: 'In Betracht kam, sofort auf den labilen d-Bauern zu drücken:'} 18... Qc4 $142 {Breutigam,Martin: '!?' %03'better is' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 19. Rxe7 Rxe7 20. Rxe7 {Breutigam,Martin: ', and surprisingly because of' Breutigam,Martin: ', und zwar überraschenderweise wegen'} Bf8 ({Breutigam,Martin: 'but not' Breutigam,Martin: 'aber nicht'} 20... Nxd5 $2 {?} 21. b3 {Breutigam,Martin: '!'} Qxf4 22. Qxd5 $16 Rf8 {Breutigam,Martin}) (20... Qxa2 $1 {[%csl Rb2,Rd5] Breutigam,Martin Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black stands better, since if'} 21. Bxd6 $6 Bf8 $1 22. Bxc5 Bxe7 23. Bxe7 Qxb2 24. c4 Qxd2 25. Nxd2 Nd7 {. I think Black's extra exchange and passed a-pawn will beat White's two connected passed pawns.}) ({or oder} 20... Qxd5 $6 {Breutigam,Martin} 21. Bg3 Qxd2 22. Nxd2 $14) 21. Re1 Qxd5 $11 {= Tal}) (18... Ra7 {!?}) 19. Ng5 $1 $14 {Breutigam,Martin: '!?' '' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A spy's job is to observe, while remaining unseen. --- QUESTION: This almost looks like a random move. What does Tal's knight threaten? --- ANSWER: A good salesperson sells you what your imagination wants, and not what he or she actually has. In this instance Tal pedals paranoia for Black's king. The knight isn't really threatening anything - yet. This is the kind of move which made Tal's opponents nervous. He hopes to provoke the weakening ...h6. And if Black refuses, as in the game continuation, then Tal keeps his eye open for future sacrificial possibilities on f7 or e6. I haven't really decided if overconfidence (Tal's last move!) is a shortcoming or a strength in chess. For Tal, it was both a curse and blessing. His veins flowed with the blood of a natural con-artist, who lives for the joy of fleecing his marks.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Obviously this knight excursion is meant to provoke (the weakening) ...h6. And perhaps Tal already had the magical point on move 23 in mind here.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Offenbar soll dieser Springerausflug (das schwächende) ...h6 provozieren. Und vielleicht hatte Tal auch schon hier die zauberhafte Pointe im 23. Zug im Blick.'} (19. b3 {?!} c4) (19. Bg5 {'with the idea' 20.Bf6}) 19... Qb7 $6 {?! Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Black's position drifts like smoke carried by the vagaries of the wind. It's wrong to allow White c4.'} (19... Qc4 20. Rxe7 $1 {!} Rxe7 21. Bxd6 Nxd5 (21... Raa7 22. Rxe7 Rxe7 23. Qxa5 Ne8 24. Qd8 $18) 22. Bxe7+ Nxe7 23. Qd6 Re8 24. Qd7 $1 {( h7) ! 'with the idea' Nh7} Qh4 $8 25. Rxe7 Rxe7 26. Qd8+ Re8 27. Ne6+ fxe6 28. Qxh4 $18) ({Breutigam,Martin: 'If' Breutigam,Martin: 'Falls'} 19... h6 $5 {!? Breutigam,Martin: ', then' Breutigam,Martin: ', so'} 20. Ne4 {Breutigam,Martin: 'with the idea of 21.Nxf6 and 22.Bxh6,' Breutigam,Martin: 'mit der Idee 21.Sxf6 und 22.Lxh6,'} {Breutigam,Martin: 'not' Breutigam,Martin: 'nicht'} Nxe4 {Breutigam,Martin: '?'} (20... Nh5 21. Rf3 $14) (20... g5 {Breutigam,Martin} 21. Bg3 {[%csl Rf5]}) 21. Rxe4 Bxb2 (21... g5 {Breutigam,Martin} 22. Bxg5 $1 hxg5 23. Qxg5 $18 {[%cal Ge4g4]}) 22. Bxh6+ $14 {Tal}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The edge may be swinging to Black if he finds' Best was probably Am besten war wohl} 19... Qb5 $1 {[%cal Gc5c4] Breutigam,Martin again eyeing - possibly followed by ...c4! - the vulnerable d5-pawn. , schielt wieder - ggf. folgt ...c4! - auf den anfälligen Bauern d5.} 20. b3 (20. c3 $6 {is met with} c4 {which applies pressure to d5}) 20... Qb4 $1 21. Rd3 Qxd2 22. Bxd2 c4 $1 {with queenside pressure for Black.}) 20. c4 $16 Qb4 {Breutigam,Martin: '?' ?! Breutigam,Martin: 'A decisive mistake. Certainly Speelman had calculated (correctly) in his previous moves that now the knight sacrifice on f7 would bring White nothing. However, he overlooked a subtlety ...' Breutigam,Martin: 'Ein vorentscheidender Fehler. Sicherlich hatte Speelman bei seinen vorigen Zügen (korrekt) berechnet, dass nun das Springeropfer auf f7 Weiß nichts einbringen würde. Allerdings übersah er dabei eine Feinheit ...'} (20... Kg8 { f8 'with the idea' Bf8}) ({Tougher was immediately Zäher war sofort} 20... h6 {Breutigam,Martin} 21. Ne4 ({Here Hier wäre} 21. Nxf7 $2 Kxf7 {would be less powerful, e.g. weniger kräftig, z.B.} 22. Rb3 Qc7 $17) 21... Nxe4 22. Rxe4 Qxb2 $11) (20... a4 {!?}) 21. Qe2 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal, of course, isn't about to allow a queen trade when he has a potential attack brewing.'} h6 {%08DA Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE  (combination alert): It's one thing to be outmanoeuvred or outplayed by an opponent's superior ideas, and quite another when we bring on our own suffering deliberately. On his last move - which is akin to looking into the wrong end of the telescope to see the planets, moon and stars - Black provokes Tal into a sacrifice on f7. Should Tal play it, or is there something better?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black should try' Breutigam,Martin: 'Not much more pleasant looked' Breutigam,Martin: 'Nicht viel erfreulicher sah'} 21... Nxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Breutigam,Martin: 'whereupon Tal certainly had planned' Breutigam,Martin: ', worauf Tal gewiss'} 22. cxd5 {Breutigam,Martin: '!'} Qxf4 23. Ne6+ {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(knight fork/ attraction/pin)' Breutigam,Martin: ':' Breutigam,Martin: 'geplant hatte:'} fxe6 24. Rf3 {Breutigam,Martin: ''} e5 (24... Qxf3 25. Qxf3+ Kg8 26. Re2 {. Black got a rook, bishop and pawn, plus a compact position for the queen, and my feeling is he should hold the draw without too much difficulty.}) 25. g3 $16 {Tal}) (21... Qb7 22. Bxd6 exd6 23. Nxh7+ $18 {!}) 22. Nxf7 $1 $18 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: The sacrifice works. --- White's earlier Ng5 clearly passed the threshold of a warning, and now turns, uncomfortably for Black, into a direct threat. Oh, the bliss, when our once abstract concept gels into a reality on the board.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Excuse me? That should NOT work, Speelman must have thought.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Wie bitte? Das sollte doch eigentlich NICHT funktionieren, dürfte sich Speelman gedacht haben.'} (22. Rb3 $6 {?!} hxg5 $1 {!} 23. Rxb4 axb4 $13) (22. Ne4 {!?} g5 23. Nxf6 gxf4 24. Nd7+ $16) 22... Kxf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The stunned black king's sceptre slips from his nerveless fingers, clattering to the floor. Abundance can sometimes be deadly. If you don't believe me, then just ask Marie Antoinette. This extra piece is just borrowed money, which must be repaid later with extortionate levels of interest.'} ({The intermediate shot Der Zwischenzug} 22... g5 {Beim would be refuted by würde widerlegt durch} 23. Bxg5 Kxf7 (23... hxg5 24. Nxg5 $18) 24. Bxf6 {and wins. und gewinnt.}) 23. Rb3 $1 {! Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A crucial element in the combination, which allows Tal's rook to swing to the seventh rank.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Tal had probably had this astonishing rook shift in mind for some time. In any case, there is something magical about the fact that this rook, instead of immediately taking e7 with a check bid, had better take the detour b3-b7.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Diesen erstaunlichen Turmschwenk hatte Tal wohl schon länger im Sinn gehabt. Es hat jedenfalls etwas Magisches, dass dieser Turm, statt sofort auf e7 mit Schachgebot zu nehmen, besser den Umweg b3-b7 einschlagen sollte.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Speelman may have expected' Beim: 'The immediate' Breutigam,Martin: 'Speelman had apparently only calculated' Breutigam,Martin: 'Speelman hatte zuvor offenbar nur'} 23. Rxe7+ $2 {Breutigam,Martin: '??' Beim: 'would lead to an edge for Black after 23...xe7 24.xe7+ g8; '} Rxe7 {Beim} 24. Qxe7+ Kg8 {Breutigam,Martin: '' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', when White doesn't have enough for the piece.' Breutigam,Martin: 'calculated.' Breutigam,Martin: 'berechnet.'}) 23... Qa4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When you weigh your options, it's not such a good sign that you have only one.'} 24. Qe6+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'An unfulfilled ambition is not the same thing as a broken vow. This wins.'} ({Tal may (I stress the 'may'!) have missed an easier version with} 24. Rb7 $1 Kg8 25. b3 Qa3 26. Bc1 {. Black's queen is trapped, without sufficient compensation after} Qb4 27. Rxb4 axb4 28. Qc2 Kf7 29. Re3 {and Black won't survive. Then again, Tal may have seen this line and rejected it in favour of his own continuation, since in that one he gets to attack with greater gusto.}) 24... Kf8 25. Rb7 {[ d6] 'with the idea' Bd6 Breutigam,Martin: 'Now, in addition to 26.Rxe7/27.Bxd6, there is also the immediate threat of 26.Bxd6, possibly together with 27.Qf7 mate.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Jetzt droht neben 26.Txe7/27.Lxd6 auch sofort 26.Lxd6, ggf. nebst 27.Df7 matt.'} Qxc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Continue Tal's attack.'} (25... Rad8 26. Rxe7 $18) (25... Ra6 26. b3 Qxa2 27. Rxe7 Rxe7 28. Qxe7+ Kg8 29. Qb7 $18 {Tal} Ne4 30. Kh2 $18 {!}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is unlikely to survive' If Falls} 25... Ng8 {Breutigam,Martin , another rook shift decides: , entscheidet ein weiterer Turmschwenk:} 26. Re3 $1 {[%csl Rf8][%cal Ge3f3,Rf3f8] Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' followed by Te3-f3. gefolgt von Te3-f3.}) ({And on Und auf} 25... Rab8 {Breutigam,Martin} 26. Rxb8 Rxb8 {winning would be gewänne} 27. Bxd6 $1 exd6 28. Qxd6+ Kf7 29. Qxb8) 26. Bxd6 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Clearance. The black defenders, partially awake, partially dressed, and only partially armed, are unable to offer meaningful resistance.'} ({White must be careful not to play the rote} 26. Rxe7 $2 Rxe7 27. Qxe7+ Kg8 {, after which Black's king has excellent chances of slipping away.}) 26... Ng8 (26... Nxd5 27. Bxe7+ Rxe7 28. Rxe7 Nxe7 29. Qxc4 $18 {Tal}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Naturally not'} 26... exd6 {Breutigam,Martin Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??'} 27. Qf7# {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) 27. Re3 $1 {! Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The final attacker is lifted in.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Black will not be able to contain the many white attackers.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Schwarz wird die vielen weißen Angreifer nicht bändigen können.'} (27. Re4 $6 {?!} Qxe4 (27... Qc1+ 28. Kh2 Ra6 29. Bxe7+ Nxe7 30. Qxa6 Be5+ 31. g3 Qc2 $13) 28. Qxe4 exd6 29. Qxg6 Ne7 $13 {Tal}) 27... Bf6 28. Rf3 Kg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Proceed with Tal's attack. --- ANSWER: Removal of a key defender.'} ({Or Oder} 28... Qd4 {Beim} 29. Bxe7+ Rxe7 30. Rxe7 {and so on. und so weiter.}) (28... Qc1+ 29. Kh2 Qg5 30. Bxe7+ Rxe7 31. Rxe7 Nxe7 32. Rxf6+ Ke8 33. d6 $18) 29. Bxe7 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} (29. Be5 $5 {!?}) 29... Rxe7 (29... Qc1+ 30. Kh2 Rxe7 31. Rxe7+ Nxe7 32. Rxf6 Qxb2 33. Qxe7+ Kh8 34. Rxg6 $18) (29... Bxe7 30. Rf7+ Kh8 31. Qe5+ ({this mates faster than} 31. Qxg6) 31... Nf6 32. Rxf6 $18 (32. Rbxe7 {with a forced mate in five moves.})) 30. Rxe7+ Nxe7 ({Beim: 'Or' Beim: 'Oder'} 30... Bxe7 31. Rf7+ Kh8 32. Qxg6 {Beim: '+-' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and Black has nothing better than'} Qc1+ 33. Kh2 Bd6+ (33... Qf4+ {("The weak thrive on petty revenge" thinks White's king)} 34. Rxf4 Rf8 {with an easy win for White.}) 34. g3 $1 {!} (34. Qxd6 $6 {?!} Qg5) 34... Bxg3+ 35. Kg2 $18 {Tal}) 31. Qxf6+ ({Komodo prefers} 31. Rxf6 {.}) 31... Kg8 32. Qf7+ $1 {!} Kh8 33. Qxe7 Qxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): I assure you the solution to this problem won't tax the majority of readers. White to play and force the win. --- ANSWER: Seize the seventh rank with the rook - a solution so obvious that it doesn't even deserve an exclam.'} 34. Rf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Black must give up his queen to avoid mate.'} 1-0

