{ChessCalisthenics: 'B96: Sicilian Najdorf: 6 Bg5 e6 7 f4, lines other than 7...Qb6 and 7...Be7'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Nbd7 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The players enter the theoretically dense main line of the 6 Bg5 Najdorf.'} b5 (9... Be7 {is more commonly played here. The main line runs} 10. g4 b5 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. g5 Nd7 13. f5 $5 {.}) 10. Bxb5 {!? Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"How can an action which gives one such pleasure be a sin?" the bishop asks himself. The move choices we make are simply reflections of our inner nature. Tal unleashes a wave of confusion throughout the position, and for him, that was a good enough reason to sacrifice. Tal's last move is made with the philosophy: rarely is a monumental goal easily accomplished. It is human nature to respond with aggression when we feel the sting of wounded pride. But I ask: how did Stean's play give Tal offence this early in the game? The answer is that Tal was simply a man who considered anything but the most direct path a weakness in himself. --- QUESTION: Is this one sound? --- ANSWER: This is actually a book line which may be sound, when you play through it with the comps. Personally, I don't like to play into lines like this from either side. Why? You can enter a position, played hundreds of times, with thousands of players believing in it. Then one day, someone finds a comp-generated new move/refutation, and suddenly, nobody wants to play it again. I challenge my comp to refute the London System!'} (10. Bd3 {is an obviously calmer response.}) 10... axb5 11. Ndxb5 (11. e5 Bb7 12. Ndxb5) 11... Qb8 (11... Qb7 12. e5 {!} dxe5 13. fxe5 Nxe5 14. Bxf6 {!}) (11... Qb6 12. e5 Bb7 13. Rxd6 Bxd6 14. Qxb7 Qxb7 15. Nxd6+ $16) 12. e5 {!} (12. Rhe1 h6 13. Bh4 Bb7 14. Qd3 Nc5) 12... Bb7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move is necessary;' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'transposes to the game continuation.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'since'} 12... dxe5 {? Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'walks into White's cheapo'} 13. Qxa8 $18 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'with a winning position.'}) (12... Ra5 {!?} 13. exf6 gxf6 14. Bh4 {!?} (14. Bh6 Bxh6 15. Nxd6+ Ke7 $15) 14... Rxb5 15. Nxb5 Qxb5 16. Qa8 $13) 13. Qe2 dxe5 (13... Ra6 {?!} 14. exf6 gxf6 15. Bh4 $16) (13... Be7 14. Bxf6 Nxf6 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Nxd6+ Kf8 17. Nxb7 Qxb7 18. Qe4 $16 {Kengis - Vitolinsh, Riga, 1981}) 14. Qc4 {! Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Nc7+.'} Bc5 $6 {This looks attractive, but loses by force! ? Cyrus Lakdawala: 'GM Tony Kosten disliked this move and felt it lost by force. I disagree. This is the natural move, which blocks White's Nc7+. The move does, however, contain practical difficulties for Black, who must thread the needle with only-moves to secure equality.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is better off playing'} 14... Be7 $1 {is analysed in Naiditsch, A-Gelfand, B/Dortmund GER 2006.} 15. Bxf6 (15. Nc7+ Kf8 16. Rxd7 Nxd7 17. Rd1 Bxg5 18. fxg5 {(White is down a full rook, yet Komodo evaluates at '0.00'!)} Ke7 19. Qb4+ Kd8 20. Nxa8 Qxa8 21. Nb5 Bd5 22. c4 Bc6 23. Nd6 Rf8 24. Nf5 Rg8 25. Qe7+ Kc7 {½-½, F.Kunzelmann-J.Bokar, correspondence 2009. The game ends in perpetual check after} 26. Qd6+ Kd8 27. Qe7+ {.}) 15... Nxf6 16. Nc7+ Kf8 17. fxe5 Nd5 18. N3xd5 exd5 19. Nxd5 Qxe5 20. Nxe7 Qxe7 $13) 15. Bxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} gxf6 {%08DA Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The d7-knight must cover the c5-bishop.'} (15... Be3+ 16. Kb1 Nxf6 17. Nc7+ Kf8 18. Qb4+ Kg8 19. Nxa8 Qxa8 20. Qxb7 $18 {!}) 16. Rxd7 $1 {Dashing chess! Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Business is brisk for the lucky coffin makers and the journey to the cemetery becomes more and more familiar for Tal's pieces, who are dying off at alarming rates. As we all understand by now, income disparity never plagued Tal's mind during the game. Somehow he treated his pieces like Monopoly money. --- QUESTION: Isn't Tal pressing his attack with mounting recklessness? --- ANSWER: On the contrary, it's too late for second thoughts. His move - a not-so-subtle attempt to ferret out Black's king from his hole - is the only way to continue White's attack. The comps still evaluate at '0.00'.'} (16. Kb1 {is a bit slow,} Qc8 17. f5 Nb6 18. Qh4 Ke7 {and Black has consolidated, Atakisi, U-Soylu, S/Istanbul 2001.}) 16... Be3+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This zwischenzug saves the bishop.'} 17. Kb1 {! Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why didn't White play'} 17. Rd2 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'to get Black's bishop for the rook? --- ANSWER: The line works out badly for White after'} O-O $1 18. Qe2 exf4 19. Kb1 Bxd2 20. Qxd2 Rd8 {Black has a solid extra exchange, Clerides, P-Penttinen, J/Limassol 2000. Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Black remains up on material, with a relatively safe king, P.Clerides-J.Penttinen, Limassol 2000.'}) 17... Kxd7 18. Rd1+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision): For now, Black's overall goal (to convert his extra material and win) remains subordinate to the more urgent concern of survival. Black has a choice of 18...Ke7, 18...Ke8, or blocking with 18...Bd4. Two of the choices favour White, while only one saves Black. Which one would you play?'} Bd4 $6 {A natural reaction, but it loses. ChessCalisthenics: 'N?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Once your plan loses its dominating principle (in this case, for Black, it is merely survival) , either paralysis or chaos sets in. The bishop had long prayed for a sign, and to his chagrin, soon gets one. This is the worst of Black's choices.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead,'} 18... Ke8 {ChessCalisthenics: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'works out in White's favour after'} 19. Nc7+ (19. fxe5 Qxe5 20. Nd6+ Kf8 $14) 19... Kf8 20. fxe5 {also gives White a strong attack, Vitolins-Anikayev/Riga 1973 '' Vitolinsh - Anikaev, USSR, 1973 Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} (20. Qb4+ Kg8 21. Nxa8 Qxa8 22. Qe7 Bd5 23. Qxf6 exf4 24. Nxd5 exd5 25. Qg5+ Kf8 26. Rxd5 f3 27. Rd8+ Qxd8 28. Qxd8+ Kg7 29. Qxh8+ Kxh8 30. gxf3 Kg7 31. c4 Kf6 32. Kc2 Ke5 33. Kd3 {Bobras,P (2581)-Czakon,J (2504)/Lublin 2008/ CBM 123/1/2-1/2 (44)}) 20... Ba6 21. Qc6 $1 (21. Nxa6 Qxe5 {is okay for Black}) 21... Ra7 22. Rd7 $1 Rxc7 23. Rxc7 Kg7 24. exf6+ Kh6 25. a3 Rf8 26. g4 {. Advantage White: Black's king remains under fire and White still has his three connected passed queenside pawns for the piece.}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Correct is'} 18... Ke7 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', which should save Black after'} 19. Qb4+ Ke8 20. Nd6+ Kf8 21. Nf5+ Kg8 22. Nxe3 exf4 (22... Bc6 23. Qc5 exf4 24. Ng4 f5 25. Qxc6 fxg4 26. Ne4 {. White has enough counterplay to save the game, but no more:} Ra5 27. Nd6 Qa8 28. Nb7 {(threat: Rd8+)} Rd5 29. Rxd5 exd5 30. Qf6 Qxb7 31. Qg5+ Kf8 32. Qd8+ {with perpetual check.}) 23. Ng4 {looks dangerous for Black, to say the least.}) (18... Bd5 19. Nxd5 exd5 20. Qxd5+ Ke7 21. fxe5 fxe5 22. Nd6 Bd4 23. Nf5+ $18) 19. fxe5 fxe5 (19... Qxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'fails to save Black:'} 20. Nxd4 (20. Rxd4+ Bd5 21. a3 Rhc8 22. Qb4 Ra6 23. Rd1 h5 24. Nxd5 exd5 25. Re1 Ra4 26. Rxe5 Rxb4 27. Rxd5+ Kc6 28. Rd6+ Kxb5 29. axb4 {and Black can resign.}) 20... Ke8 21. Qb4 $1 Bd5 {might avoid what follows, but White has an attack plus a pawn for the exchange, and must be better.}) 20. Nxd4 exd4 21. Qxd4+ Ke7 (21... Bd5 {is no help: Cyrus Lakdawala: 'eliminates White's knight, which fails to help Black's king after'} 22. Nxd5 exd5 23. Qxd5+ Ke8 (23... Ke7 24. Re1+) 24. Re1+ (24. Qc6+ Kf8 25. Qh6+ Ke8 26. Re1+ Kd7 27. Qh3+ Kc6 28. Qc3+ Kb6 29. Qf6+ Ka5 (29... Ka7 30. Re7+ {is of no help either}) 30. Re5+ Qxe5 31. Qxe5+ {and White's three queenside passed pawns win easily.}) 24... Kf8 25. Qc5+ Kg8 26. Qg5+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Kg8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and the rook lift'} 28. Re3 {and wins. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is decisive.'}) 22. Qc5+ $1 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More accurate than'} 22. Qd7+ Kf8 23. Rf1 Qe8 24. Qxb7 {is also quite good, Krupenski, Y-Kanep, M/Tallinn 2000, but the text is much more incisive. Cyrus Lakdawala: ', which is still very much in White's favour, Y.Krupenski-M.Kanep, Tallinn 2000.'}) (22. Qb4+ $1 {is the same:} Kf6 23. Qh4+ $6 (23. Rf1+ $1 Kg7 24. Qe7 {transposes to the main game}) 23... Kg7 $4 (23... Kg6 {might defend}) 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Rd7 Qe8 26. Qh6+ Kg8 27. Rd3 Qb8 28. Rd4 {Li Shilong-Becker, M/Amsterdam NED 2006.}) 22... Kf6 (22... Ke8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'allows White's knight to enter with force after'} 23. Nb5 {'+-' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Now if'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or'} f5 (23... Bd5 24. Nc7+ Kd7 25. Nxd5 $1 exd5) 24. Nd6+ Ke7 25. Nc8+ $1 Kf7 26. Qe7+ {wins.} Kg6 27. Qxe6+ Kg5 28. Nd6 {and Black's extra rook won't help him at all}) 23. Rf1+ (23. Qd4+ $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'allows Black to escape after'} Kg6 24. Qg4+ Kf6 25. Rf1+ Ke7 {gives no more than a perpetual, Lacroix, S-Libersan, T/ Montreal 2005. Cyrus Lakdawala: '. White must agree to a perpetual check, S.Lacroix-T.Libersan, Montreal 2005.'}) 23... Kg6 (23... Kg7 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Qf6 $18) 24. Qe7 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's awareness hovers over f6 and f7 with watchful intensity.'} f5 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'With time pressure looming, there must be a lowering of our standards, or we risk flagging. Now the defence crumples like an unstrung puppet.'} (24... Qe5 {puts up more resistance: ChessCalisthenics: '' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is Black's last chance to offer resistance:'} 25. Qxf7+ Kg5 {ChessCalisthenics: '+-'} 26. h4+ (26. g4 $1 Kh4 $1 {(Black's king hopes to find his footing on still unstable ground)} 27. Qe7+ Qg5 28. Qxb7 Raf8 29. Rg1 Qe3 {(it feels as if a light, vagrant breeze of counterplay begins to emerge for Black, as he plays on White's weakened back rank; it's still not enough in the end)} 30. Qe7+ Kh3 31. Ne2 $1 Rf3 32. Qg7 Kxh2 {(threat: ...Qxg1+!)} 33. Re1 $1 Rhf8 ({the would-be back rank shot} 33... Qxe2 $4 {fails miserably, since} 34. Rxe2+ {comes with check}) 34. Qxh7+ Rh3 35. Qd7 Rhf3 36. Qd1 {and White still has some work to do before he scores the win.}) 26... Kxh4 27. g3+ Kg5 28. g4 $1 h5 29. Qe7+ Kg6 30. gxh5+ Kh6 31. Qxb7 {but Black is hardly likely to survive long here.}) (24... Rf8 25. Qf6+ Kh5 26. g4+ Kxg4 27. Rg1+ $18) 25. Qxe6+ Kg7 26. Qe7+ ({The comp found a quicker path with} 26. Qd7+ $1 Kh6 (26... Kg8 {is met with} 27. Rxf5) 27. Rxf5 Qe8 28. Rf6+ Qg6 29. Rxg6+ hxg6 30. Qxb7 {which wins.}) 26... Kg6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White has two ways to finish here. Tal utilizes his kingside pawns as attackers in the mating net.'} 27. h4 $1 (27. g4 {also wins, Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'also wins after'} Rf8 $2 (27... f4 $1 28. h4 {(threat: h4+ and Qf6 mate)} Qf8 29. Qg5+ Kf7 30. Qxf4+ Kg8 31. Qc4+ Kg7 32. Qc7+ Kg8 {, and simplest is} 33. Rxf8+ Rxf8 34. a4 {with an easy win.}) 28. gxf5+ Kh5 {Dimitrov, V-Dichev, N/Plovdiv 1988, when} 29. Qxh7+ Kg4 30. Qg7+ Kh3 31. Nd1 {is the quickest.}) 27... Ra5 {ChessCalisthenics: '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black desperately covers f5, but his position is past saving. --- EXERCISE (calculation/combination alert): It becomes painfully clear that Black's king will not be granted safe passage though hostile territory. Tal worked out a forced mate in seven moves. How does it begin?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead,'} 27... Rf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} 28. g4 $1 (28. Qg5+ Kf7 29. Rxf5+ Ke6) (28. Rd1 h6 29. Rd6+ Qxd6 30. Qxd6+ Kh5 $18) 28... Qg3 (28... Qd8 29. Qxb7 $18) 29. gxf5+ Kh6 30. Ne2 Qg4 ({or} 30... Rae8 31. Qxf8+ Rxf8 32. Nxg3 {and White wins}) 31. Rd1 {and there is little to do against the threat of Rd6+.} (31. Rg1 Qxg1+ 32. Nxg1 {which wins, since} Rxf5 $4 {is met with} 33. Qe6+ {.})) (27... h6 28. g4 $18 {!}) 28. h5+ {Forcing mate in 7. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Attraction/mating net.'} Kxh5 (28... Kh6 29. Qf6+ {allows White to deliver mate one move faster.}) 29. Qf7+ Kh4 ({Or} 29... Kh6 30. Qf6+ Kh5 31. Rh1+ Kg4 32. Qh4# {.}) 30. Qf6+ Kg3 31. Qg5+ Kh2 32. Qh4+ Kxg2 33. Rf2+ Kg1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The defence-starved king realizes in his dying thought: wealth is unable to relieve hunger if there is no food to buy.'} 34. Ne2# {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. "Oh no, please, no, it was nothing," murmurs the knight, as she bashfully poses for the clicking cameras, over the black king's body. Three against one doesn't strike me as a fair fight.'} 1-0

