{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After this, the first game of the 1960 world championship match, Tal revealed that he was shocked to win it, since he "had the charming habit of beginning a tournament with a defeat!".'} 1. e4 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak Knaak: 'Tal hatte schon geraume Zeit vor dem Match versprochen, mit dem e-Bauern zu eröffnen.'} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Botvinnik's specialty, the strategically unbalanced Winawer French.'} 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qc7 {Knaak: 'Dieser Zug hat nach wie vor eine hohe Bedeutung, obwohl er viel weniger gespielt wird als 6...e7. Der Punkt ist, dass in 70% der Partien nun nicht 7.g4 folgt und Schwarz gelangt damit in die ruhigen Positionsvarianten des Winawer-Farnzosen. '} ({Today, the Poisoned Pawn line of the Winawer is reached via the move order} 6... Ne7 {(the death of an old idea gives birth to a new one)} 7. Qg4 O-O {is also a major line which is nightmarishly complicated}) 7. Qg4 {Knaak: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Knaak: 'Das ist der prinzipielle Zug.'} ({Tal, of course, opts for White's sharpest option, rejecting the more controlled} 7. Nf3 {.}) 7... f5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In Botvinnik's era, this was the book move.'} (7... Ne7 8. Qxg7 {transposes to the modern version of the Poisoned Pawn line.} Rg8 9. Qxh7 cxd4 10. Ne2 Nbc6 11. f4 Bd7 12. Qd3 dxc3 {. This is a tabiya position, which sets the later stage where modern day GMs mine their computers in search of a fashionable trifle, in their hope to discover a theoretical novelty around the 26th move. In a sense, such positions are endless construction projects which are never destined to reach completion. I remind my theory-obsessed students: when everyone thinks alike, a single person (i.e. a writer of chess opening books!) represents the entire community. But, of course, they never listen to me and just keep on studying fashionable theory obsessively!}) 8. Qg3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Covering against ...cxd4 and ...Qc3+.'} ({Auch} 8. Qh5+ {Knaak} g6 9. Qd1 {wird gespielt.}) ({QUESTION: After} 8. exf6 Nxf6 {who is favoured? White has an open position for his bishops, while Black leads in development. --- ANSWER: Principle: A development lead tends to outweigh the bishop pair in open positions. White scores just under 40% with this line and Black already stands better.}) 8... Ne7 {Knaak: '!?' Knaak,Rainer: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Black gets an inferior version of the Poisoned Pawn line. --- QUESTION: What is the difference between this way and the modern main line? --- ANSWER: Two differences, both which favour White: 1. In this inferior version, f5 is blocked with a pawn, rather than open for a black piece. --- 2. Having played ...f5, Black is unable to implement a future ...f6 prying mechanism.' Knaak: 'Führt zu sehr verwickelten Varianten. Heute hat'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black should play'} 8... cxd4 {Knaak Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 9. cxd4 Ne7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' mit positionellen Stellungen eine höhere "Einschaltquote".} ({not} 9... Qxc2 $2 10. Bd2 Qc7 11. Rc1 Nc6 12. Bb5 a6 13. Bxc6+ {- principle: opposite-coloured bishops favour the attacker, which is obviously White, in this case -} bxc6 14. Ne2 {and Black's extra pawn provides little solace for her wretched position, full of dark-square punctures and grossly lagging development, A.Bodnaruk-A.Balaian, Belgrade 2013}) 10. Bd2 O-O 11. Bd3 b6 {, intending ...Ba6, is okay for Black.}) ({Another good path is} 8... Nc6 9. Nf3 cxd4 10. cxd4 {and now is the time for} Nge7 11. Rb1 (11. Qxg7 $2 Rg8 12. Qxh7 Nxd4 $1 {is in Black's favour}) 11... O-O {. In this version Black gets some play down the c-file, and also has the plan of ...Na5 and ...Nc4, occupying c4, S.Karjakin-V.Ivanchuk, Monaco (rapid) 2011.}) 9. Qxg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal willingly enters the maze, even when facing a theoretically better prepared opponent. In this instance he walks headlong into Botvinnik's feared opening preparation. Of course, today, when an opponent walks into our prep, it's our comps who defeat them. So to us, the game then has a wonderfully larcenous feel of a student who cheated on an exam and got away with an A+ grade. Believe it or not, Botvinnik was probably unprepared for this move, since it wasn't played at the time of the game.'} ({Tal's choice happens to be White's best move, and a clear improvement over Smyslov's safe choice,} 9. Bd2 {(White's normal response at the time)} O-O 10. Bd3 b6 $1 {(preparing to swap off his bad bishop via the a6-square)} 11. Nh3 Ba6 12. Nf4 Qd7 13. h4 Bxd3 14. cxd3 Nbc6 15. Be3 cxd4 16. cxd4 Rac8 {and Black's chances are no worse, since his healthy queenside pawn majority compensates for White's kingside attacking chances, V.Smyslov-M.Botvinnik, Moscow 1957.}) 9... Rg8 10. Qxh7 cxd4 11. Kd1 {Tal himself later appended a "?" to this move, although it is not clear whether he does so in jest." Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's move covers against Black's threat to check on c3 and pick off the a1-rook.' Knaak: 'Tal selbst tadelt diesen Zug, doch es gibt fast keine Partien mit dem Alternativzug 11.e2.'} ({I would go for} 11. Qh5+ $1 Ng6 12. Ne2 Nc6 13. f4 Bd7 14. Qf3 (14. cxd4 $4 {is met with} Nxd4 $1 {when White's knight must watch over a queen check on c3}) 14... dxc3 15. Qxc3 O-O-O 16. Be3 {and Black's position is inferior to a normal Poisoned Pawn French, since his knight is misplaced on g6, and he lacks an ...f6 prying mechanism, J.Palsson-H.Camilleri, correspondence 1997.}) 11... Bd7 {Knaak: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Botvinnik's prepared novelty. The bishop eyes the a4-square, where it takes a bead on c2.'} (11... Nbc6) 12. Qh5+ $14 {Knaak,Rainer: ''' (Thomas) '''} Ng6 {Knaak: '?' Knaak,Rainer: '?' '?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal was off form in the 1961 rematch, due to health concerns. The following win was one of his happier days, in an otherwise miserable rematch:' Knaak: 'Ein Jahr später beim Revanchematch kam'} 12... Kd8 {Knaak: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Botvinnik's attempted improvement, which perhaps took Tal by surprise; Black's king voluntarily forgoes castling rights to avoid the irritating pin of his knight on g6)' Knaak: 'aufs Brett. Dann gibt es drei Möglichkeiten:'} 13. Bg5 {(Tal) Knaak,Rainer: ''' (Tal)' Knaak: '(Tal)'} (13. Ne2 dxc3 14. f4 (14. Qf3 {Knaak} Nbc6 15. Qxc3 Nxe5 16. Qxc7+ Kxc7 17. Nd4 {Diese Stellung wurde von Keres als besser für Weiß angegeben. Doch die Praxis zeigte ein anderes Bild.} Ng4 $1 18. Ke1 e5 $17) 14... Qc5 $1 {(Euwe 76)}) (13. Nf3 {Tal,M-Botvinnik,M (12)/Wch24-Moscow/1961/1-0/ Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Knaak: 'Tal,M-Botvinnik,M (12)/Wch24-Moscow/1961/1-0/'} Ba4 $1 {Knaak (Keres)} (13... Qxc3 14. Ra2 ({awkward but necessary, as} 14. Rb1 $6 Ba4 {is a problem for White, since he is unable to play} 15. Bd3 $4 {due to} Qxd3+) 14... Nbc6 15. Rb2 Kc7 16. Rb5 $6 {. Now play proceeded} ({Tal sets up a trap, but he should actually play} 16. Rb3) 16... Rh8 $2 ({instead, Black stands clearly better after} 16... Qa1 $1) 17. Qxh8 $1 Rxh8 18. Bb2 {(White regains the queen)} Qxf3+ 19. gxf3 Ng6 $2 ({Black's chances are no worse after} 19... Rh4 $1 20. Rb3 Nxe5 {when he gets a pawn for the exchange, and formidable centre, while White must deal with the issue of multiple isolanis}) 20. h4 $1 {(the passed h-pawn is a big issue for Black)} Ngxe5 ({or} 20... Rxh4 21. Rxh4 Nxh4 22. f4 Ng6 23. Bc1 {and Black is busted in the ending}) 21. h5 Nf7 $6 ({Botvinnik should try} 21... Nxf3) 22. f4 {(Black's centre is not allowed to roll forward)} Nd6 23. Rb3 $1 {(going after Black's d4-pawn)} Ne4 24. Ke1 Rh6 25. Be2 Be8 26. Rd3 Nf6 27. Bxd4 Nxd4 28. Rxd4 Bxh5 $6 29. Rd3 $1 Rh7 30. Rdh3 Bg6 31. Rxh7+ Nxh7 32. Rh6 $1 Nf8 33. Rh8 $1 {(Tal finds a forcing way to win the e6-pawn, after which Black's game collapses)} Nd7 34. Rg8 $1 Bf7 35. Rg7 Be8 36. Re7 {and game over, since e6 falls. Botvinnik resigned in a few moves, M.Tal-M.Botvinnik, World Championship (Game 12), Moscow 1961.})) ({after} 13. cxd4 $1 Qc3 14. Rb1 Qxd4+ 15. Bd2 Qxf2 16. Nf3 Nbc6 17. Rxb7 {White stood clearly better, due to control over the dark squares, the safer king and passed h-pawn, V.Baklanov-R.Kwiecinski, correspondence 2008}) 13... dxc3 $13 {Knaak}) 13. Ne2 (13. Qh7 Ne7 14. Nf3 (14. Qh5+ $14 {(Thomas)}) 14... Qxc3 15. Ra2 {(Schwarz 67)}) (13. Bg5 Ba4 $1 14. Ra2 Nc6 $132 {(Ragosin)}) (13. cxd4 $2 Qc3 $1 $17 {(Schwarz 67)}) (13. Rb1 $1 {looks like an improvement for White:} d3 (13... Nc6 14. Nf3 dxc3 15. Bg5 {is also in White's favour}) 14. Bxd3 Nc6 15. f4 O-O-O 16. Qe2 {and Black lacked full compensation for his two missing pawns, P.Roth-F.Winiwarter, Austrian League 1992.}) 13... d3 $1 {"Botvinnik spent [37 minutes] deliberating over this position, from which it can be inferred that he had not covered all the subtleties of the variation in his home laboratory." (Tal 77) Knaak,Rainer: ''!' "Botvinnik spent [37 minutes] deliberating over this position, from which it can be inferred that he had not covered all the subtleties of the variation in his home laboratory." (Tal 77)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Played after 37 minutes of thought! When a strategist like Botvinnik suddenly begins to act erratically (and when I say "erratically", I mean act like Tal!), inviting complications, he becomes a person leading a double life, like Walter White, the mild mannered high school Chemistry teacher/meth dealer, in 'Breaking Bad'. --- Such a sudden shift in structure is an indication of the Poisoned Pawn Winawer's mutability. Black's last move was made with the thought: strip the enemy king of his concealment and you also degrade his protection. The study of chess leads us to a knowledge of our inner nature. Botvinnik's was one of always wanting to be in charge, no matter the cost. He refuses to accept his role as passive bystander and decides upon precipitous action. Tal gave this move an exclam in his book on the match, which I don't believe is merited. I just don't see this as a remunerative investment. When we engage in a shaky sacrifice of material, our 'compensation' can feel like we lost an item, and then it returns to us in a dream.' Knaak: 'Botvinnik hat hier 37 Minuten nachgedacht, aber vermutlich den 16. Zug von Weiß übersehen. Vermutlich scheute sich Botvinnik mit zwei Minusbauern zu spielen, doch das scheint unumgänglich zu sein.'} (13... Nc6 $5 14. cxd4 Rc8 (14... O-O-O $10 {(Thomas) Knaak,Rainer: ''=' (Thomas) '='' Knaak: 'Das ist fast die gleiche Stellung wie in der Anmerkung zum 14. Partiezug von Schwarz, nur steht der Bauer hier auf c2 statt c3. Das ist wohl eher ein Vorteil für Schwarz.'}) 15. Ra2 {(Tal 77)}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'As to Botvinnik's actual motivation for the sacrifice, I can only dimly imagine. He tended to shy away from any variation where he lost the initiative, rejecting a line like'} 13... dxc3 $2 14. Nf4 Kf7 15. Qh7+ (15. Bd3 Nc6 (15... Qxe5 16. g4 Nc6 (16... Qh8 17. Nxg6) 17. gxf5 exf5 18. Rb1 b6 19. Rb5 Nce7 20. Re1 $16 {(Schwarz 67, Euwe 76)}) 16. Bxf5 $1 exf5 (16... Ncxe5 17. Bxe6+ $1 Bxe6 18. Qh7+ Rg7 19. Qxg7+ $3 Kxg7 20. Nxe6+ $18 {(Schwarz 67)}) 17. e6+ $1 Bxe6 18. Qh7+ Rg7 19. Qxg7+ $1 {(Tal 77)} Kxg7 20. Nxe6+ $18 Kf7 21. Nxc7 Rc8 22. Nb5) (15. g4 Qxe5 16. Nxg6 Rxg6 17. Rg1 $1 {with attacking chances and the bishop pair for White. Of course, in this one, Black up a pawn, rather than down one, as in the game continuation. It's very easy for Black to go wrong here. For example,} Qd4+ $2 (17... Qf6 {is correct}) 18. Bd3 Qxf2 $4 {(Black attacks, when he should be defending)} 19. Rg3 {(threat: gxf5)} Kg7 20. Be3 {and Black's queen finds herself trapped.}) 15... Rg7 16. Qh6 Qxe5 17. Nh5 Nc6 18. Qxg7+ Qxg7 19. Nxg7 Kxg7 20. f4 {(Coles)}) (13... Qxe5 {Knaak: '?' Knaak,Rainer: '?' '?'} 14. cxd4) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Botvinnik probably originally intended'} 13... Ba4 {Knaak: '?' Knaak,Rainer: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', but then saw the response'} 14. Nf4 Qxc3 (14... Qf7 15. Nxg6 Rxg6 16. Rb1 $1 dxc3 (16... b6 17. cxd4 {is a strategically won game for White, who is just up two pawns for nothing}) 17. Rxb7 $1 {(deflection)} Qxb7 18. Qxg6+ Qf7 19. Qh6 {when White is up a pawn, owns the dark squares, and his h-pawn will soon roll down the board. Black probably can't generate enough against White's king to compensate these factors.}) 15. Bd3 (15. Ra2 Kf7 16. Bd3 $1 {(Schwarz 67)}) 15... Qxa1 16. Nxg6 Nc6 17. Nf4+ $1 {(Tal 77)} (17. Ne7+ Kd7 $1 18. Nxg8 Rxg8 $132 {(Tal 77)})) 14. cxd3 $8 {Knaak,Rainer: ''''} Ba4+ $6 {Knaak: '?' Knaak,Rainer: '?' '?' Knaak,Rainer: ''?!'' Knaak: 'Treibt den weißen König auf ein besseres Feld.'} ({Knaak: 'Nach'} 14... Nc6 $1 {(Tal) Knaak,Rainer: ''!' (Tal) '!''} 15. d4 {Knaak} O-O-O {kann sich Schwarz Hoffnungen auf ein Spiel gegen den weißen König machen, doch zwei Minusbauern sind eine schwere Hypothek.}) 15. Ke1 Qxe5 $6 {Knaak: 'Schwarz verbleibt danach mit nur noch einem Minusbauern, aber auch ohne Kompensation.'} (15... Nc6 $5 16. f4 (16. d4 O-O-O 17. Bg5 $14 {(Pachman 68)} (17. f4 Na5 18. Qf3 Bc2 $1 {(Boleslavsky)}) (17. h4 Ncxe5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Rh3 Be8 $1 $17 {(Kotov)}) 17... Rd7) 16... O-O-O 17. Bd2 $16 {(Tal) Knaak: '(Tal)'}) (15... Bb5 $6 {Podgorny} 16. Bg5 $1 (16. Bd2 Bxd3 17. f4 Nc6 {(Ragosin)}) 16... Bxd3 17. Kd2 $16 {(Tal) Knaak: '(Tal)'}) (15... Nd7 $1 16. d4 O-O-O 17. Bg5 $14 {(Pachman 68, eco 81)} (17. Bd2) 17... Rdf8 { (Euwe)   (eco 74)}) 16. Bg5 $1 {"The basic problem which now confronts White is how to keep the Black King in the center. In this respect, the loss of White's King Pawn played into White's hands, since new threats can be created by openning the King file." Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal ensures that Black's king won't castle long to safety.' Knaak: 'Verhindert die lange Rochade. Weiß kann ebenfalls nicht mehr rochieren, aber das ist auch nicht notwendig, denn der König steht auf e1 sicher. Doch für das schwarze Spiel (für den Minusbauern) wäre die lange Rochade lebensnotwenig.'} Nc6 (16... f4 $6 17. d4 $1 Qf5 (17... Qe4 18. Bxf4 $16 {(Schwarz 67)}) 18. Nxf4 Qc2 19. Ne2 $16 {(Tal 77) Knaak: '(Tal)'}) (16... d4 17. cxd4 Qa5+ 18. Bd2 $1 $16 {(Schwarz 67, Euwe 76)}) 17. d4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Preventing ...d4 ideas, while ensuring his dark-square domination, by fixing Black's central pawns on light squares.'} Qc7 (17... Qe4 $6 18. Rc1 $1 $16 {(Schwarz 67)}) (17... Qh8 $6 18. Nf4 $1 $16 {(Schwarz 67)}) 18. h4 $1 $16 {(Keres 69, eco 74/81, Suetin 88, mco 90) Knaak: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: ''!' (Keres 69, eco 74/81, Suetin 88, mco 90)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: How would you assess? --- ANSWER: Black is strategically lost for the following reasons: 1. Black is down a pawn. --- 2. White's passed h-pawn anchors his g5-bishop, while remaining a looming threat in the ending. --- 3. White rules the dark squares, which outweigh Black's light-square control. --- 4. White's king looks safer than Black's. --- 5. White's h1-rook may now enter the game by way of h3.' Knaak: 'Der h1 wird einfach über h3 entwickelt.'} (18. Nf4 $2 Nxd4 $1 $19) 18... e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Keep in mind that sometimes despair mimics defiance. The comps don't like this move, but just because a comp disapproves of an idea empirically, doesn't mean it won't work on a confused human. When our plan is based on an imaginary foundation, the end result never fits reality's requirements. In this instance the ignition switch which starts Black's initiative is non-existent. It's infuriating when we recognize our plan's outline, yet lack knowledge of the exact mechanism which sets it into motion.'} (18... Nce7 19. Bxe7 (19. Rh3 $1 Rh8 (19... O-O-O 20. Re3 {(Schwarz 67)}) 20. Qf3 O-O-O 21. h5 $1 $16 {(Schwarz 67)}) 19... Qxe7 20. Nf4 $5 (20. Qg5 $18 {(Tal) Knaak: '(Tal)'}) 20... Qf6 21. Nxg6 Rxg6 22. g4 $1 O-O-O 23. g5 $17 {(Petrosian)}) 19. Rh3 $1 $16 {(Pachman 68) Knaak,Rainer: ''!' (Pachman 68)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One incongruity piles upon another. Tal activates his sleeping h1-rook in unorthodox fashion.'} ({Maybe Botvinnik hoped for the rash} 19. dxe5 $2 Ncxe5 20. Rh3 Rh8 $1 {, forcing the awkward} 21. Bh6 O-O-O 22. Qg5 Rde8 23. h5 Rxh6 $1 24. Qxh6 Nf4 $1 25. Qxf4 Nd3+ 26. Rxd3 Qxf4 27. Rd4 Qh6 28. Rxa4 Qc6 $1 {. White is busted, since he must return a full rook, due to the dual threats ...Qxa4 and ...Qxc3+.}) 19... Qf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'We tend to get irritated when our 'threats' are ignored, because it is a gesture of the opponent's disdain.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Botvinnik allows the centre to open, not wanting to be ground down slowly in a line like'} 19... e4 20. Rb1 Qf7 21. Nf4 b6 22. Nxg6 Rxg6 23. Ba6 Kd7 24. Kf1 Rf8 25. Bf4 Bc2 26. Rb2 Ba4 27. Qe2 {. White has firm control over the game, while Black remains counterplayless.}) (19... f4 $2 20. Qg4 $1 $16 {(Tal)}) 20. dxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal picks the correct moment. Principle: Open the position when you own the bishop pair.'} Ncxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The cost of Black's decision is twofold: 1. He further opened the game, to the benefit of White's bishops. --- 2. Black condemned himself to long hours of servitude to the defence of his now chronically weakened d5-pawn.'} (20... Rh8 $2 {Knaak,Rainer: ''?''} 21. e6 $1 Qxe6 (21... Rxh5 22. exf7+ Kxf7 23. Ng3 $1 $16 {(Euwe 76)}) 22. Re3 Rxh5 23. Rxe6+ Kf7 24. Rxg6 $1 {(Tal 77)} Kxg6 25. Nf4+ $18) 21. Re3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Every white piece is in play. Tal centralizes his rook, threatening f4. The area around Tal's king is well policed and his king is secure, despite its central location.'} Kd7 (21... Rh8 $2 22. Rxe5+ Kd7 23. Re7+ Qxe7 24. Qxg6 $18 {(Tal 77)}) 22. Rb1 b6 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The weakening of a6 plays into multiple variations later on for White.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Correct was the non-weakening' Knaak: 'Auf das zähere'} 22... Bc6 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' Knaak: 'gibt Tal diese Variante an:'} 23. Nd4 (23. Rb4 $2 Rh8 $19) (23. Nf4 Rae8 24. Qd1 Ng4 25. Rxe8 Rxe8+ 26. Be2 Nxf4 27. Bxf4 Qf6 $132 {(Euwe 76)}) 23... f4 24. Rxe5 $1 Nxe5 25. Qxf7+ Nxf7 26. Bxf4 Rae8+ 27. Kd2 $16 {(Tal 77)}) 23. Nf4 {Knaak: 'Spätestens jetzt ist alles klar: Weiß hat einen gesunden Mehrbauern, das Läuferpaar und die bessere Königsstellung.'} Rae8 (23... Rh8 24. Nxg6 $1 Nxg6 25. Qe2 {(Tal 77)} Rxh4 26. Qa6 $16) 24. Rb4 $1 $16 {(Schwarz 67) Knaak,Rainer: ''!' (Schwarz 67)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gaining a tempo, while adding another attacker/defender into the mix. Tal's rooks display a marked bias for the lateral. Notice how his pieces operate on the weakened dark squares.'} ({Komodo likes the inhuman continuation} 24. Qe2 $1 {(intending Qa6)} Nc4 25. Nxd5 $3 Nxe3 26. Nxe3 Qg7 (26... f4 $4 {loses to} 27. Qa6) 27. Rc1 Re4 28. g3 {, when Bg2 is coming and Black is quite lost.}) 24... Bc6 25. Qd1 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: ''!'' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The queen, having done her job on h5, returns to d1 to pressure d5. Oddly enough, white king and queen return to their squares of origin, after wandering away earlier, in search of adventure. All of White's pieces breathe activity and work in harmony, including the undeveloped f1-bishop.'} Nxf4 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After'} 25... Ng4 26. Rxe8 (26. Re2) 26... Rxe8+ 27. Be2 $18 {(Tal 77) Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black has no way to improve his position and remains lost.'} Nxf4 28. Rxf4 $18) 26. Rxf4 Ng6 27. Rd4 Rxe3+ ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Alternatively,'} 27... f4 28. Qg4+ $18 (28. Rxe8 Rxe8+ 29. Kd2 $1 Kc8 30. h5 Qf5 31. Ba6+ Kb8 32. Bh6 Ne5 33. Bxf4 {, when a second pawn falls and Black can resign.})) 28. fxe3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Black has no chance of a future ...f4 or ...d4 break.'} (28. Bxe3 f4) 28... Kc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Find one strong move and Black's game collapses. --- ANSWER: Pin/undermining. Black can't afford to take on c4, but then again, he can't afford not to take it either.'} 29. c4 $1 dxc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Doesn't this just lose an exchange? --- ANSWER: When we are losing, it's hard to shake the feeling that we are a character in a tragedy, whose end has already been pre-written.'} (29... Ne7 30. cxd5 Bxd5 (30... Nxd5 31. Bc4 Kd6 32. Bf4+ Kc5 33. Qc2 $18 {(Euwe 76)}) 31. Bxe7 Qxe7 32. Qc1+ $18 {(Tal 77)} (32. Rxd5 Qxe3+ 33. Qe2 $18 {(Euwe 76)})) ({Botvinnik theorizes: a gangrenous limb must be amputated to save the rest of the body. It does lose the exchange, and technically the move is an error - but not really if everything else loses. For example,} 29... Kb7 30. cxd5 Bd7 31. a4 {, when White is up two pawns, while ruling the board, with the bishop pair, passed h-pawn and the safer king.}) 30. Bxc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The love-smitten bishop will do anything to move closer to the adored presence of the black queen.'} Qg7 31. Bxg8 Qxg8 32. h5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The wispy slip of a pawn, who is the culprit of the commotion in Black's camp, decides the game, since White seizes control over f4. The fact that Tal produced a strategic masterpiece is probably one of those signs of End Times, mentioned in scriptures of all religions.' Knaak: 'Quelle: "Im Feuer des Angriffs" (russ.) Tal, Moskau 1978'} Nf8 33. Bf4+ Kb7 34. Rd8 Qxg2 35. Rb8+ Ka6 36. Qe2+ $1 {(once queens come off the board, Black's resources border on emaciation)} Qxe2+ 37. Kxe2 {and the passed h-pawn decides the game's outcome.} 1-0

