{Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'B10: Caro-Kann: 2 d3 and 2 c4 ' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"This game was seemingly a paradox: one of the greatest masters of his time was unable to withstand a bluff - a semi-correct, risky attack, arising literally out of nothing!" wrote Kasparov of this game.' Hier bekommt man ein typisches Figurenopfer a la Tal zu sehen: in einer praktischen Partie schwer zu widerlegen, und 40 Jahre später erweist es sich bei der Überprüfung mit Fritz als unzureichend.} 1. e4 c6 2. d3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} ({QUESTION: Tal wasn't a closed games player. Why would he pick such a line against a strategist like Smyslov? --- ANSWER: My guess is that Tal didn't want to play his normal line} 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, since this line is named after Smyslov, who pioneered it and undoubtedly understood its intricacies better than Tal.}) 2... d5 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Nd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Today, this move is known to be an inaccuracy.' Viel häufiger wird 4...d6 gespielt - wahrscheinlich zu recht.} ({Correct is} 4... Bd6 $1 {, and if} 5. d4 exd4 6. exd5 cxd5 7. Nxd4 Nc6 8. N2f3 Nge7 {is a position which can be reached too via a Tarrasch French move order.}) 5. d4 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now the opening divides into a separate sub-species. Tal gets his open game after all, and a favourable one at that, since he leads in development.'} dxe4 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why can't Black play'} 5... exd4 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', just as you gave in the above note? --- ANSWER: The problem is Black committed his knight to d7 prematurely and is misplaced for a Tarrasch French formation after'} 6. exd5 cxd5 7. Nxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black gets an inferior version of the Tarrasch French, due to the d7-knight placement.' und der Stellungstyp des Tarrasch-Franzosen ist erreicht.}) 6. Nxe4 exd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move violates the principle: Avoid breaking the central tension without good reason.'} ({Black may be better off going into emergency mode with} 6... Ngf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 8. Bg5 Qe6 {, J.Hickman-N.Hutchinson, Gibraltar 2006.} 9. dxe5 {can be met with} f6 10. Bf4 fxe5 11. Bg3 Be7 {when Black, although still worse, is better off than in the game's continuation.}) ({Instead,} 6... f5 $6 {intending ...e4, next is overly optimistic:} 7. Neg5 e4 8. Bc4 $1 Nh6 (8... exf3 $2 9. Bf7+ Ke7 10. O-O $1 {gives White a winning attack}) 9. Ne6 {is quite awful for Black.}) 7. Qxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is in serious trouble: 1. He lags behind in development in an open position. --- 2. White soon menaces Nd6+, which gives him the bishop pair in the open position.'} Ngf6 8. Bg5 Be7 9. O-O-O {JvR: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A vow made over 1,000 times (i.e. I'm going to lose weight!) tends to diffuse its potency. Tal always went with the emotion-based response, when the position demands a Mr. Spock-like response, free from stylistic bias. This move, which glides away from the topic at hand, throws away the advantage. --- QUESTION: Why would Tal avoid such an obviously favourable line?' Die lange Rochade und die folgenden Züge beinhalten ein sehr schwieriges Konzept, denn es muss eine Figur geopfert werden.} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Once again, we see Tal's weakness crop up: He wants too much from a position and ends up with nothing. He gains a serious advantage with the continuation you, me and every other player in the world would make with' Tal weist auf diese Möglichkeit hin:} 9. Nd6+ Bxd6 10. Qxd6 Qe7+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Here lies the rub. An ending, Tal's least favourite part of the game! However, after'} 11. Qxe7+ Kxe7 {"und das Läuferpaar in dieser offenen Stellung zu neutralisieren ist nicht einfach."} 12. O-O-O {White owns the bishop pair and a development lead in the ending. Black will be hard pressed to hold the game.}) 9... O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Sometimes we are inclined to discount the data, simply because we don't like the conclusion to which it points. The problem is Tal sometimes irrationally avoided endings - even good ones, if he could get a messy middlegame position instead! Of course, Tal would argue: not every position is conducive to a straightforward application of logic, and it makes sense to play to one's strengths, rather than enter the opponent's domain. And it's hard to argue with success. Maybe Tal just found technical endings boring, which is a danger, since boredom's presence tends to spur us into a dangerous enlivening mechanism.'} 10. Nd6 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move. Black has a cramped position. Black's piece can't move: c8' JvR: '!?'} Qa5 (10... Nb6 $2 11. Nxc8 Rxc8 (11... Qxc8 12. Re1 Re8 13. Bd3 Nbd5 {also looks okay for Black.}) 12. Qh4 Qc7 13. Bd3 h6 14. Bxh6 gxh6 15. Qxh6 $18 {(Tal)}) 11. Bc4 $1 {JvR: '!?'} (11. Kb1 $2 Bxd6 12. Qxd6 Ne4) 11... b5 $1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black threatens to win material: b5xc4' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Both parties feel they are the ones entitled to ownership of the initiative. This is a little reminder to Tal that his own king may soon come under attack via a pawn storm.'} (11... Bxd6 12. Qxd6 Ne4 13. Qf4 Nxg5 14. Nxg5 Ne5 15. Bb3 h6 16. Ne4 {"ist Schwarz in einer schwierigen Lage". (Tal)}) 12. Bd2 $1 {JvR: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why did Tal retreat his bishop? --- ANSWER: He had two ideas in mind with the retreat: 1. Tal plans to switch the bishops to c3, where it takes aim at Black's king. --- 2. Tal vacates g5 for a knight to launch his kingside attack.'} Qa6 $1 {JvR: '!?' JvR: 'Smyslov accepts the provocation.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Smyslov goes for the full point!'} (12... Qa4 13. Nxc8 Bc5 (13... Raxc8 14. Bb3 Qxd4 15. Nxd4 {gives calmness.}) 14. Ne7+ Bxe7 15. Bb3 Qxd4 16. Nxd4 {"und die weißen Figuren stehen ideal". (Tal)}) (12... Qb6 {should lead to a pretty forcing draw after} 13. Nf5 Re8 (13... Bc5 14. Qf4 bxc4 15. Bc3 Bb4 16. Qg5 g6 17. Nh6+ Kg7 18. Nf5+ Kg8 19. Nh6+ {is drawn}) 14. Rhe1 Bc5 15. Rxe8+ Nxe8 16. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 17. Ng5+ Kg8 18. Bb4 $3 Bxd4 19. Ne7+ Kf8 (19... Kh8 $4 20. Nf7#) 20. Ng6+ Kg8 21. Ne7+ {with perpetual check.}) 13. Nf5 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White threatens to win material: Nf5xe7' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Bd8 $1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White has a very active position' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ambitious. Smyslov continues to play for the full point.'} ({JvR: 'Quickly loses' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Doesn't' Nach} 13... Bc5 {JvR: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'win a piece? --- ANSWER: It wins a piece, but not the game, which ends in a draw after'} 14. Qh4 (14. Qf4 bxc4 15. Bc3 Re8 16. Qg5 Bf8 17. Nh6+ Kh8 18. Nxf7+ Kg8 19. Nh6+ {.}) 14... bxc4 15. Bc3 {ist Schwarz hilflos gegen die verschiedenen Drohungen, z.B.} Qxa2 16. Rxd7 $3 {JvR: '!'} Be3+ (16... Bxd7 17. Nh6+ $1 {#6} gxh6 18. Qxf6 {(Khalifman).}) 17. fxe3 Qa1+ 18. Kd2 Qxh1 19. Bxf6 Qxg2+ 20. Kc3 Bxd7 21. Ne7+ Kh8 22. Ng5 Qxg5 23. Qxg5 $18) 14. Qh4 $1 {[%mdl 512] Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Loses material' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The previous mild tremors begin to turn into violent eruptions. In emergency situations, we prioritize to immediacy. One idea is free-associated with its next step. When my son was in pre-school, he would constantly come home with a sheet of construction paper, with glued uncooked pasta in strange patterns only he understood. I would invariably think: "Italian food sounds good tonight."'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal finds the only viable move, where he sacrifices a bishop for a single tempo, rather than:'} 14. Bb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} c5 15. Qh4 c4 16. Qg5 Nh5 $1 17. Qxh5 cxb3 18. a3 bxc2 19. Rde1 Nf6 20. Qg5 Bxf5 21. Qxf5 g6 {and White's king faces serious trouble, while Black's remains safe.}) (14. Bd3 $2 Qxa2 {, and if} 15. Bc3 $4 c5 16. Qh4 b4 $1 17. Bxf6 Nxf6 {when White is busted.}) 14... bxc4 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black wins a piece'} 15. Qg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Queen and knight give g7 a significant look.' Nach einer Reihe von folgerichtigen Zügen ergibt sich eine Stellung mit guten Angriffschancen für Weiß, doch vermutlich kann sich der Nachziehende noch völlig ausreichend verteidigen.} Nh5 {JvR: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} ({JvR: 'Black draws in'} 15... g6 $2 16. Nh6+ (16. Bc3 {JvR: '!' JvR: '(Ragozin)'} Qxa2 (16... Re8 $1 17. Rhe1 Rxe1 18. Rxe1 Qxa2 19. Qh6 gxf5 $1 20. Qg5+ Kf8) 17. Nh6+ Kg7 (17... Kh8 $4 {loses instantly to} 18. Rxd7) 18. Nf5+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is once again drawn.'} Kh8 $11) 16... Kg7 17. Bc3 Qxa2 18. Nh4 Re8 19. Rhe1 $40) ({Not:} 15... Ne8 $2 16. Qxd8 Qxa2 17. Bc3 $1 ({Tal said he intended this move, rather than to go for the favourable ending after} 17. Ne7+ Kh8 18. Qa5 $1 Qxa5 19. Bxa5 {when Black struggles in the ending, despite his (temporary) extra pawn}) 17... Ndf6 (17... Nef6 $4 18. Rxd7 $1 Bxd7 19. Nh6+ $1 Kh8 20. Qxf6 $1 {forces mate}) 18. Ne7+ Kh8 19. Qa5 ({the best move, although Tal may have opted for the more complex} 19. Rhe1 {with advantage there, as well}) 19... Qxa5 20. Bxa5 {with the same good ending for White.}) 16. Nh6+ Kh8 17. Qxh5 Qxa2 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black has a mate threat'} ({Spielbar ist auch} 17... Bf6 18. Nxf7+ (18. Bc3 $2 Bxc3 19. Ng5 Bxb2+ $1 {Tal gibt nur 19...g6 an.} 20. Kxb2 (20. Kb1 g6 21. Nhxf7+ Rxf7 22. Nxf7+ Kg8 23. Nh6+ Kf8 24. Qf3+ Bf6 $19) 20... gxh6 21. Qxh6 Qb5+ 22. Kc1 Qf5 $19) (18. Ng5 Bxg5 19. Qxg5 Qxa2 20. Bc3 f6 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '=' siehe Anmerkung beim 18. Zug von Schwarz} 21. Qg3 Nc5 $1 (21... gxh6 $4 22. Rhe1 {leaves Black's king helpless against the coming assault}) 22. Nf7+ Kg8 23. Nh6+ {is once again perpetual check.}) 18... Kg8 19. N7g5 h6 20. Ne4 Qxa2 21. Nxf6+ Nxf6 22. Qa5 Qxa5 23. Bxa5 Bg4 $11) ({Not} 17... gxh6 $4 18. Bc3+ f6 19. Qxh6 {winning, since} Rf7 20. Rhe1 {Black is mated.}) 18. Bc3 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'The bishop blocks c4' JvR: 'The critical position of the game.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Control over the kingside dark squares is a necessary precondition for White's attack.'} Nf6 $2 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's defence, once thought unimpeachable, is found to have a giant hole in it. I learned from long and painful experience that positional players who attempt to out-combine gifted tacticians are like the audience at a magic show, determined to discover the magician's sleight-of-hand, yet usually fail to do so. --- EXERCISE  (combination alert): Smyslov just blundered on his last move, mixing up a 'must do' with a 'must not do'. How did Tal respond?' Verliert klar.} ({JvR: 'A plus gains'} 18... Bf6 {JvR: '!' Vermutlich muss Weiß hier sogar um den Ausgleich kämpfen.} 19. Nxf7+ (19. Ng5 $6 Bxg5+ 20. Qxg5 f6 21. Qh5 (21. Qf4 $5 {Dies soll den Zug d7-e5 verhindern.} Nc5 $1 (21... gxh6 $2 22. Rhe1 $18) 22. Nf7+ Kg8 23. Nd6 Na4 $17) 21... gxh6 22. Rhe1 {Tal gibt nun nur 22...b8 an.} (22. Qxh6 Rf7 $1) {Doch nach} 22... Ne5 $1 23. Rxe5 Bg4 $1 {kann von weißem Vorteil keine Rede sein.}) 19... Kg8 20. N3g5 {JvR: '(Khalifman)'} Qa1+ $1 {[%mdl 256]} ({Tal gibt nur} 20... Bxg5+ 21. Nxg5 h6 22. Qg6 {mit weißer Gewinnstellung an.}) 21. Kd2 Bxc3+ 22. bxc3 Nf6 $13 {JvR: '!'} 23. Qh4 Qa5 24. Qxc4 Qd5+) 19. Qxf7 $1 $18 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Mate attack' JvR: '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Queen sacrifice/weak back rank/smothered mate. --- That which was once just conjecture, now forms into absolute certainty. Black has no saving ideas. This loss must have been frustrating for Smyslov, since his opponent's randomly crazy ideas become all the more infuriating when they happen to work!'} Qa1+ (19... Rxf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??'} 20. Rxd8+ {Mate attack Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is mate in two moves.'}) (19... Z0 $140 20. Qxf8+ {Mate threat}) 20. Kd2 Rxf7 ({Otherwise:} 20... Qxd1+ 21. Rxd1 Rxf7 22. Nxf7+ Kg8 {(the king's constant weeping doesn't inspire confidence in his defenders)} 23. Nxd8 {leaves White up a piece.}) (20... Re8 $4 {(grief can make us do crazy things)} 21. Qg8+ $1 Nxg8 22. Nf7# {is mate.}) 21. Nxf7+ Kg8 22. Rxa1 Kxf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White is up a clean exchange in the ending and Smyslov could have resigned here.'} 23. Ne5+ Ke6 (23... Kg8 {doesn't change anything anymore} 24. Nxc6 Bb6 25. Bd4 $18) 24. Nxc6 Ne4+ 25. Ke3 Bb6+ 26. Bd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Quellen:  Tal: "Im Feuer des Angriffs" (russ.), Moskau 1978 Suetin: "Schachstrategie der Weltmeister" Sportverlag 1983, S. 109} ({More accurate than} 26. Kxe4 Bb7 {when Black can play on for a while. Tal deservedly won the brilliancy prize for this game.}) 26... Bb7 27. Bxb6 axb6 28. Nd4+ Ke5 29. Rxa8 Bxa8 30. Ra1 $18 1-0

