 us to do our least favourite thing in the world: maths!). Rogers, whose optimism outruns the position's truth, hopes that a line involving great dangers, generally implies great rewards, as well. He hopes to complete his development, while luring Tal into misadventures on c6. The flaw with the plan is that White is by no means obliged to comply with an immediate jump into c6.'} ({Correct was} 12... Rd8 {, keeping White's edge to tolerable levels.}) 13. Bb5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In order to solve a riddle, it first becomes necessary to speak the language in which it is asked. It isn't easy to fathom the position's inner workings, and such a level of complications is frost on a window, which occludes vision of the world outside. If either side overlooks a single minuscule detail, he risks complete ruin. Now Nc6 becomes a very real threat;'} ({Both Rogers: ' Both ' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'whereas if:'} 13. Nc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(the bait always looks delicious to the fish; the problem, of course, is the hook hidden within the worm)'} {Rogers: ' '} Qxg5 {Rogers: ' and '} 14. Nxe7+ Kh8 15. Nxc8 Qxg2 $1 {(zwischenzug)} 16. O-O-O Raxc8 {and I don't believe in White's compensation for the pawn, even with the open g-file, since g7 is too easy to defend.}) ({and} 13. Bxf6 {Rogers: ' '} Bxf6 {Rogers: ' '} 14. Nc4 {Rogers: ' '} Qg5 {Rogers: ' '} 15. Qe4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(double attack)'} Qf5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and White must avoid'} {Rogers: ' '} 16. Qxa8 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??'} {Rogers: ' '} Qxd3 {are fine for Black, so Tal sets his sights on trapping the Black queen. Rogers: ' are fine for Black, so Tal sets his sights on trapping the Black queen.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Black wins, since'} 17. Ne3 {is met with the crushing} Ba6 {.}) {Rogers: ' '} 13... a6 (13... Bb7 $4 14. a4 $1 a6 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. b4 Bxb4 {is met with} 17. Qg4+ Kh8 18. cxb4 {and Black's queen is trapped.}) {Rogers: ' '} 14. Bc6 {Rogers: ' '} Ra7 {Rogers: ' '} 15. b4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's queen is close to trapped.'} {Rogers: ' '} Qa3 16. Qc2 {Rogers: ' "And wins," continued Tal's analysis (when playing 13.Bb5), e.g. 16...b5 17.Nc4! bxc4 18.Bc1 etc. However Black has another way to save his queen.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"If this yapping dog makes another sound, then beat it," orders the queen, as she points to her a3 sister. White's pieces crowd around Black's queen like the faithful entering a temple on prayer day. Threat: Bc1, and also Nc4, in both cases trapping Black's wayward queen.'} Nd5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Altered circumstances forces us all to bend our previous intent. Rogers finds Black's only method of playing on, with his pincushion-like position still absorbing all of Tal's pokes.'} ({"And wins," continued Tal's analysis (when playing 13.b5), e.g.} 16... b5 17. Nc4 $1 bxc4 18. Bc1 {etc. However Black has another way to save his queen.}) {Rogers: ' '} 17. Bxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} {Rogers: ' '} exd5 (17... Bxg5 $4 {is met with} 18. Nc4 {, trapping the queen.}) {Rogers: ' '} 18. Bc1 Bf5 $1 {The point behind Black's play. I was beginning to feel confident for the first time in the game but Tal hadn't finished with the tactical tricks. Rogers: ' The point behind Black's play. I was beginning to feel confident for the first time in the game but Tal hadn't finished with the tactical tricks.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In times of desperation, 'later' is synonymous with 'never'. The only move. By some form of arcane magic, Black's defence appears to remain erect, but this is still an illusion upon an illusion. "I was beginning to feel confident for the first time in the game, but Tal hadn't finished with the tactical tricks," wrote a dismayed Rogers.'} {Rogers: ' '} 19. Bxa3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It may feel as if Tal's unfulfilled desires continue to stock up. If we look closer, we see that in reality, Tal sells each concession dearly. He may not have been successful in his queen-trapping project, but he didn't come away empty handed either.'} {Rogers: ' '} Bxc2 20. Nc6 {Rogers: ' White was now anticipating 20...Rc7 21.Nxe7+ Rxe7+ 22.Kd2 Be4 23.b5 1-0. However White's underdeveloped army and king in the centre gives Black a chance to confuse matters with a rook sacrifice.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Temporary relief returns to apprehension for Black.'} Bf6 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The best practical chance. --- EXERCISE  (critical decision): Black's move is made with the thought: you can only be put to death once. Making a move like this one, Black becomes the condemned death row inmate, who is now convicted of a second murder, and a second death sentence. Should Tal play 21 Nxa7, which wins a piece? Or should he first toss in 21 Kd2? Only one line leads to a white advantage.'} ({White was now anticipating} 20... Rc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'loses to'} 21. Nxe7+ Rxe7+ 22. Kd2 Be4 (22... Ba4 23. b5 {, winning the exchange, in a simplified position.}) 23. b5 {1-0. However White's underdeveloped army and king in the centre gives Black a chance to confuse matters with a rook sacrifice.}) 21. Nxa7 {Played reluctantly after great thought. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: He should take on a7 immediately.'} ({It is too late to bail out with Rogers: ' Played reluctantly after great thought. It is too late to bail out with ' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The zwischenzug'} 21. Kd2 {since Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'fails to'} {Rogers: ' since '} Rc7 {is good for Black, e.g.} {Rogers: ' is good for Black, e.g. '} 22. b5 {Rogers: ' '} Ra8 $3 (22... Rfc8 $1 {. Now White must avoid} 23. Kxc2 $2 ({correct is} 23. Bd6 Rd7 24. Be5 axb5 25. Bxf6 Rxc6 26. Bd4 Bf5 {with a likely draw}) 23... axb5 {which regains the lost material advantageously, due to White's insecure king position.}) {Rogers: ' '} 23. Kxc2 {Rogers: ' axb5.'} axb5) {Rogers: ' '} 21... Bxc3+ ({After} 21... Ba4 22. Bb2 Re8+ 23. Kf1 Ra8 24. g3 Rxa7 25. Kg2 {White should eventually consolidate his extra exchange.}) {Rogers: ' '} 22. Ke2 {Rogers: ' '} Re8+ {Rogers: ' '} 23. Kf3 Re6 $2 {Rogers: ' the game is essentially over.'} ({When considering 21.xa7 Tal was very worried by Rogers: ' When considering 21.Nxa7 Tal was very worried by ' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal feared'} 23... d4 $1 {keeping alive options such as ...e4+ and ...a8. Post-game analysis tended to give White an edge after Rogers: ' was the only consistent way to play since after Tal's reply to ' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', intending ...Be4+ and ...Ra8. The comp says White still wins after'} {Rogers: ' keeping alive options such as ...Be4+ and ...Ra8. Post-game analysis tended to give White an edge after '} 24. Rac1 {Rogers: ' '} Be4+ {Rogers: ' '} 25. Kg3 {Rogers: ' '} Ra8 {Rogers: ' '} 26. Rhe1 $1 {but in any case, 23...d4 was the only consistent way to play since after Tal's reply to 23...e6? the game is essentially over.} (26. Rhd1 $1 h5 (26... Rxa7 $4 {loses to the dirty back rank trick} 27. Rxd4) 27. Rxc3 $1 dxc3 28. Rd7 {when Black was unable to regain his lost piece.})) {Rogers: ' '} 24. Rhc1 $1 {Rogers: ' '} Be4+ {Rogers: ' '} 25. Ke2 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's king escapes.'} {Rogers: ' '} d4 (25... Bxg2+ 26. Kd3 d4 27. Rxc3 $1 dxc3 28. b5 {and White's once stranded knight escapes.}) 26. f3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. This is no shared misfortune, as there is no way to trap White's knight, since b5 is coming.'} ({After Rogers: ' '} 26. f3 {Rogers: ' 1-0 After '} Bf5+ {Rogers: ' '} 27. Kf2 {Rogers: ' '} Re3 $5 {Rogers: ' '} 28. Rab1 $1 {wins comfortably enough. Rogers: ' wins comfortably enough.'}) 1-0

