1. c4 {Knaak,R Knaak Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's attacking set-up is standard issue for the London System.'} ({Compare it to this game:} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bf4 c5 4. c3 d5 5. e3 Be7 6. Nbd2 O-O 7. Bd3 b6 8. Ne5 Bb7 9. Qf3 $1 Nbd7 10. Qh3 $1 Re8 $6 11. Ndf3 a6 $4 (11... Nxe5 {was necessary}) 12. Nxf7 $1 Qc8 (12... Kxf7 {is met with} 13. Qxe6+ $1 {when taking on e6 walks into 14 Ng5 mate, but if} Kf8 14. Ng5 {, forcing mate}) 13. Qxe6 Rf8 14. Nd6+ {, C.Lakdawala-R.Bruno, San Diego (rapid) 2010. I have won about ten very similar games to this one, using exactly the same attacking themes. When we repeat a combinational pattern, we are the thief who walks by the place where he successfully pulled off the last job - this time in broad daylight - just for the joy of the memory.}) 1... e6 2. Nc3 d5 3. d4 Be7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The idea behind this move is to deprive White's bishop of early g5 options.'} 4. cxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal enters a version of the Exchange Queen's Gambit Declined.'} (4. Nf3 {is also played here.}) 4... exd5 5. Bf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is a game which warms my London System-loving heart.'} Nf6 6. e3 O-O 7. Bd3 b6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Dieser Aufbau wird hier eindeutig widerlegt.} ({Normal ist} 7... c6) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} 7... c5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'are played more often.' ist nicht jedermanns Sache.}) 8. Nf3 c5 9. Ne5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Doesn't this knight jump simply encourage a swap? --- ANSWER: White made the move with two ideas: 1. It clears the way for Qf3 and possibly later Qh3, when White's queen enters the attack zone. --- 2. White isn't afraid of a future ...Nc6 (or ...Nbd7) and ...Nxe5, since White may recapture with his d-pawn, chasing away the f6-knight, a key defender.'} Bb7 10. Qf3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'As usual, Tal chooses the kingside as his preferred arena for settling disputes.'} Nbd7 11. O-O a6 $6 ({Necessary was} 11... Qc8 12. Bf5 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Qc6 {with a manageable disadvantage.}) 12. Qh3 $3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I have played this attacking plan so often from similar positions in London and Exchange Slav, that when I see someone else play it, it feels like looking in a mirror. I discovered early on that you can only control that which is already known and understood (I'm not interested in a fair fight in crazy complications!). This attacking theme is so worked out in detail, that it's a bit like the domesticated pet who is almost part human, having lost its ability to be an animal in the wild.' Der folgende schwarze Zug ist nun erzwungen.} g6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The only move, probably made with great hesitation, since the display of weakness psychologically marks you as prey. Black must weaken the dark squares around his king, and also give White a line-opening hook with f5 later on.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead:'} 12... Nxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} 13. dxe5 Bc8 14. Qf3 $18 (14. g4 $1 Bxg4 15. Qg2 {and White wins a piece.})) (12... Bc8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with the overloading shot'} 13. Nxd5 $1 $18 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) (12... h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} 13. Bxh6 $1 (13. Bf5 $1 {(even stronger than the immediate sacrifice on h6)} Nxe5 14. dxe5 Ne4 15. Nxe4 dxe4 16. Bxh6 {is decisive.}) 13... gxh6 14. f4 {(!!) So habe ich es für Schach analysiert; das war vielleicht damals eine Analyse nach der Partie, die ich beobachtet habe.} (14. Qxh6 $1 Nxe5 15. dxe5 Ne4 16. f4 $18) 14... Kg7 15. Rf3 Rh8 (15... h5 16. Rg3+ Kh8 17. Rg5 $18) 16. Rg3+ Kf8 17. Nxf7 Kxf7 18. Bg6+ Kg7 $1 {(Fritz)} (18... Kf8 19. Qe6 $18 {soweit meine Analyse für 'Schach'}) 19. Qe6 Rf8 20. Bc2+ (20. Bf5+ Kh8 21. Rg6 Nh7 22. Qxd7) 20... Kh8 21. Rg6 (21. Qh3) 21... Rg8 22. Rxh6+ Kg7) (12... Re8 $4 {walks into the trap, which three unfortunates fell for in my database:} 13. Nxd7 Qxd7 14. Bxh7+ Kf8 15. Bf5 Qd8 16. Qh8+ Ng8 17. Bh7 Bf6 18. Bg5 $1 {winning.}) 13. Bh6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Is this really a tempo gain if Tal chased Black's rook to a good square on an open file? --- ANSWER: Tal's last move was not played to gain a tempo. Instead, he got: 1. By chasing the rook to e7, Black's f7-pawn is weakened. --- 2. By playing Bh6, Tal cleared the way for his next move, f4, which backs up his e5-knight, and also prepares an assault down the f-file with a future f5.'} Re8 14. f4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black faces the Stonewall from hell and one look tells us that Black isn't likely to survive White's coming kingside attack.' Ähnliche Stellungen hat Weiß auch im Nimzoinder, allerdings mit dem Läufer auf c1 statt auf h6.} Bd6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This has the appearance of a wasted move, in a position which certainly can't afford it.'} ({Even at this early stage, it feels to me as if Black's defensive resources are close to exhausted:} 14... Bf8 15. Bg5 ({stronger was to eliminate the defender of the dark squares around Black's king with} 15. Bxf8 $1 Nxf8 16. f5 {, with a dangerous attack brewing around Black's king}) 15... Bg7 16. Qh4 Qc7 17. f5 $1 cxd4 {, H.Hernandez Carmenates-Y.Gonzalez Vidal, Santa Clara 2007, and if} 18. Nxd7 $1 Qxd7 19. Na4 $1 Qxa4 20. Bxf6 Bxf6 21. Qxf6 Qc6 22. Qxd4 Qc5 23. Rae1 Qxd4 24. exd4 {with only a minimal edge for White in the ending, due to Black's slightly bad bishop.}) (14... Qc7 $6 15. f5 $1 cxd4 16. Nxd7 $1 Qxd7 17. exd4 Bc6 {, J.Bartos-L.Jinova, Zdar nad Sazavou 2007. White has a winning attack after} 18. Bg5 Ne4 19. Bxe7 Rxe7 20. Bxe4 dxe4 21. d5 Bb5 22. Qh6 $1 {when the threat of f6 is decisive.}) (14... b5 {may be Black's best hope to survive: for example,} 15. f5 cxd4 16. exd4 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Bc5+ 18. Kh1 Rxe5 19. Bf4 Qe7 20. Bxe5 Qxe5 21. Rae1 Ne4 22. Bxe4 dxe4 23. fxg6 hxg6 24. Rxf7 $1 Kxf7 25. Qh7+ Ke8 26. Qxb7 Ra7 27. Qc8+ Kf7 28. Nxe4 Be7 29. Qc1 Kg7 {.}) 15. Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Nxd7.'} Be7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The scariest way to lose is when your position gets worse and worse, without an easily discernible cause. The problem with this move is that it takes the pressure off Tal's e5-knight, allowing him f5.'} ({Komodo suggests desperation mode by allowing White his intent with} 15... b5 $5 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. Bxf6 Qxh3 18. gxh3 Rxe3 19. Bc2 {. Black only has two pawns for the piece and should avoid taking the third with} Rxh3 $2 20. Rad1 c4 21. Rde1 {with a dominating position and extra material for White.}) 16. f5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Such promising Stonewall-style attacks are so self-explanatory, that even a normally attack/initiative-challenged player like your writer plays them with surprising competence.'} cxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): That which has been accomplished isn't nearly as important as that which needs to be accomplished. Black's arrow-bristled defensive shield can't hold any longer and Tal has two promising attacking ideas in the position. Find one of them. --- ANSWER: Annihilation of defensive barrier.'} 17. fxg6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The weakness of f7 and h7 are the central facts, from which all other arguments flow. Tal's sacrifice comes with all the subtlety of a bell's clanging.'} ({ANSWER no.2: Also crushing was} 17. Nxf7 $1 {(this intrusive knight reminds me of my gigantic Akita, Emma, who is actually closer to a nanny, than a dog; each morning she barks at daybreak to get me up, when my preference would be to get up at the crack of noon)} Kxf7 18. exd4 {(White has time for this calm recapture, since Black's king isn't going anywhere)} Nf8 19. fxg6+ hxg6 20. Qh6 {(threat: Rae1, followed by Bxg6+!, and then Qh7+)} Ke6 21. Bxg6 Nxg6 22. Rxf6+ $1 Bxf6 23. Qxg6 Rf8 24. Re1+ Kd7 25. Qf5+ Kc7 ({or} 25... Kc6 26. Re6+ Kc7 27. Bf4+ Kc8 28. Rc6# {when rook and queen deliver the dreaded double checkmate; the red-faced black king is unable to look his sister in the eye, and pretends to study the ceiling with minute interest}) 26. Bf4+ {and wins.}) 17... Nxe5 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Well, why not? You can only be damned once. I wouldn't describe this knight as a coiled, rattlesnake-like defender, striking without conscience. To attempt to use an optimistic piece grab to eradicate an otherwise miserable position, is similar to attempting to bring a terminally ill patient back to health with positive affirmations.'} ({Nach der Partie entbrannte noch eine Diskussion, ob Schwarz mit} 17... hxg6 {Haltechancen hätte.} 18. Nxf7 (18. exd4 {ist einfach und gut.}) ({Fritz favorisiert} 18. Bxf6 $1 Bxf6 (18... Nxf6 19. Bxg6 fxg6 20. Qe6+ Kh8 21. Rf3 $18) 19. Nxd7 Bg7 20. Na4 Rxe3 21. Rf3 Rxf3 22. Qxf3) (18. Nxg6 $1 fxg6 19. Bxg6 {is crushing. If} dxc3 ({or} 19... Nf8 20. Bxf6 Bxf6 21. Bxe8 dxc3 22. Qg4+ Bg5 23. Bf7+ Kg7 24. h4 Bc8 25. Qg3 {and White wins}) 20. Rxf6 Nxf6 21. Bxf6 {forces mate.}) 18... Kxf7 19. Qh7+ Ke6 20. Qxg6 dxc3 21. Bf4 Bf8 22. Bf5+ Ke7 $13) 18. Bxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Clearing away the final defender of h7.'} Bxf6 ({Instead:} 18... fxg6 19. Qe6+ Nf7 20. Bxd4 Rf8 21. Bxg6 {forces mate.}) (18... hxg6 $4 {makes life easy for White after} 19. Qh8# {. "It seems that your code of chivalry has been rendered obsolete," mocks the queen.}) 19. Qxh7+ Kf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The king feels as if he is about to cry. So he does.'} 20. exd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal goes after the sole defender of f7.'} Nxg6 21. Bxg6 fxg6 22. Rf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In preparation for Raf1.'} ({Tal, at the moment is uninterested in table scraps, and doesn't bother taking the hanging b7-bishop with} 22. Qxb7 {.}) 22... Re6 23. Raf1 Rc8 (23... Bc6 {is met with} 24. Qxg6 {.}) 24. Qxb7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. What? A pragmatic decision from Tal? At this stage Black playing on is as pointless an exercise as sweeping the floor after your house burned to the ground.'} ({However, it's shocking that Tal rejected the flashier continuation} 24. Qxg6 $1 Rcc6 25. Rh3 {, which leaves Black defenceless.}) 24... Kg8 {is met with the overload trick} 25. Rxf6 $1 Rxf6 26. Rxf6 {, winning a piece.} 1-0

