{Alexander Volzhin: 'Finally we'll consider the Classical set up with Nf3 and Be2. This system is Karpov's career long favourite. The outstanding Soviet Grandmaster Efim Geller has also greatly contributed to the theory of this line.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The ideological space of interpretation is as vast as is possible, in a game between Tal and Petrosian.'} 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Nf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Our stylistic biases normally find ready outlets in the openings we choose to play, so this move, which exerts a calming influence on the position, comes as an odd choice for Tal. It strikes me as surprising that he would play one of the quietest systems White has at his disposal (Tal, as we all know, considered quiet positions akin to captivity), rather than risk a more theoretically intense line like 4 f4, the violent Austrian Attack.'} Nf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A Modern Defence transposes to a Pirc.'} 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O Nc6 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Provocative.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Petrosian goads Tal early in the game.'} ({Playable alternatives are found in} 6... c6) (6... Bg4) (6... Nbd7) (6... c5) ({and} 6... a6 {.}) 7. d5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The only way to fight for the advantage.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's most principled move. If your opponent provokes you, then it is only just that he should be punished!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White can also refrain with'} 7. h3 {allows} e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. QUESTION: Why not gain a tempo here with'} 8. dxe5 ({After} 8. d5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '- ? --- ANSWER: After'} Ne7 {the position looks similar to a main line King's Indian, but with White's pawn on c2. Now it's difficult for White to organise a Queen-side attack, so by playing Nh5 (or Nd7) and f5 Black obtains good counterplay. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I like Black's game since White essentially gets an inferior King's Indian Defence, since his c-pawn sits on c2, rather than on c4. Black's natural ...f5 break comes a lot quicker than White's painfully slow c4-c5 push.'}) 8... dxe5 {with equality. Cyrus Lakdawala: ', E.Rozentalis-J.Ehlvest, Koszalin 1998. White may claim a microbe of an edge after'} 9. Bc4) 7... Nb8 ({Instead,} 7... Ne5 8. Nxe5 dxe5 9. Bg5 {looks a shade more comfortable for White, G.Milos-R.Del Bosco, Sao Paulo 2000.}) 8. Re1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal plays for an eventual e5 break.'} (8. h3 {Ü 16/139}) ({After} 8. a4 Bg4 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Bxf3 {(possession of the bishop pair is normally our inviolable birthright in this line)} c6 11. a5 a6 12. Be3 Nbd7 13. Bd4 Re8 14. Re1 Qc7 15. Na4 {, White's bishop pair and extra space gave him the edge, G.Kasparov-N.Short, Saint Louis (blitz) 2015.}) 8... e5 $6 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I would have expected'} 8... c6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', to keep Black's structure fluid.'}) 9. dxe6 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move helps Black's development, but as we all know, Tal favoured open positions.'} ({I would be more inclined toward} 9. Bg5 h6 10. Bh4 g5 11. Bg3 Nh5 12. Nd2 Nf4 13. Nc4 f5 {, M.Brooks-J.Veal, Stillwater 2001. I prefer White after} 14. Bf1 Nxg2 15. Bxg2 f4 16. h3 fxg3 17. fxg3 {since he may be able to make use of the f5-square later on.}) 9... Bxe6 ({Petrosian correctly avoids} 9... fxe6 {, N.Gurieli-M.Meyer, New Delhi 2000. Now White can favourably play the disruptive} 10. e5 $1 {.}) 10. Bf4 h6 {Alexander Volzhin: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What is the point of this move? --- ANSWER: I'm not really sure. Perhaps Petrosian wanted to prevent Ng5, or perhaps Qd2 and Bh6. In any case, the loss of time isn't justified when his queenside remains undeveloped. The unravelling of an article of clothing begins imperceptibly, through the fraying of a lone thread. Petrosian allows himself to get preoccupied in an obvious waste of time, made that much more dangerous in a semi-open position. When we are not sure of the correct plan, then the place we begin is to eliminate the discards.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black looks fine after'} 10... Nc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', E.Geller-G.Kuzmin, Moscow 1974.'} 11. h3 (11. Qd2 $5) 11... h6 12. Qd2 g5 13. Bg3 ({According to Efim Geller,} 13. Bh2 {is preferable, intending to meet} d5 {with} 14. e5 Ne4 15. Qe3 {keeping an opening advantage.}) 13... d5 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Qxd5 {with equal chances in Geller-Kuzmin, USSR,1974.}) 11. Nd4 Bd7 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Now all Black's pieces are very passive.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Dire need adds savour to an otherwise mundane project. More wasted time, but I don't see a preferable alternative.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'GM Alexander Volzhin suggested'} 11... Qd7 {should be preferred, although White's advantage is undisputable. Cyrus Lakdawala: '. I think White still has a large advantage after'} 12. e5 $1 dxe5 13. Bxe5 {when Black's queen and bishop are awkwardly placed.}) 12. Qd2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Menacing h6.'} Kh7 13. e5 $1 $16 {Alexander Volzhin: '!' Alexander Volzhin: 'Very logical: White has a development advantage, so he opens the central files. Other moves allow Black to complete his development (Nc6), when White only has a small advantage.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Principle: Open the game and create confrontation when leading in development.'} dxe5 14. Bxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The surest way to lure your opponent into a trap is to tell him exactly what he wants to hear. In this case the bishop pours a vial of odourless poison in the black king's morning tea, allowing Black a ...Ne4 discovery trick, after which Black's counterplay - if you can call it that - isn't enough, and when it does come, it arrives in irregular intervals.'} Ne4 $2 {Alexander Volzhin: 'It's not easy to find a sufficient defence.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Petrosian - seemingly oblivious to his development lag - indulges in an extravagance by wasting yet more time in pursuit of the bishop pair.'} (14... Nc6 $5 $16 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 15. Nxc6 bxc6 (15... Bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. --- QUESTION: Doesn't this move just hang a pawn? --- ANSWER: I think Black is better off handing over a pawn, rather than submitting to the wretched 15...bxc6?! 16 Rad1, even if'} 16. Qxd8 Raxd8 17. Bxc7 {gives White an extra pawn} Rd7 18. Bf4 {is an extra pawn for White in the ending. Even so, this may be Black's best option, since a sliver of hope - no matter how faint - is still better than no hope at all.}) 16. Rad1 {, with a decisive advantage.}) ({Black's unappetizing options:} 14... Ne8 $6 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. Ne4 Bc6 $4 {this move hangs a queen;} ({Black had to try} 16... Nd6 17. Qc3 $1 Kh7 18. Bf3 {when he is under intense pressure}) 17. Nf5+ {1-0, A.Kolybin-Z.Janda, Decin 1998.}) 15. Nxe4 Bxe5 16. Nf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Bg7 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The only move.' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Petrosian fends off persistent annoyances, the way a man brushes off summer flies buzzing around his head. He finds the only move within a hive of losing moves.'} ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Grabbing a pawn with'} 16... Bxb2 {Alexander Volzhin: 'fails to' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(the last thing Black needs is more loss of time; this move exhausts Black's resources past tolerable limits)'} 17. Rad1 { b4+-  b4+- Alexander Volzhin: 'threatening Nc5' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(threat: Nc5)'} Qe7 18. Bc4 Kg7 19. Rb1 Bf6 20. Nxf6 Qxf6 21. Rxb7 {and Black's game is on the verge of collapse.}) ({and} 16... Nc6 {blunders a piece: Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'hangs material to'} 17. Nxe5 Nxe5 18. f4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Nc6 19. Qxd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'winning a piece, due to the f6-knight fork.'}) 17. Rad1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Nc5.'} Qc8 (17... Qe7 {fails to} 18. Bc4 {(threat: Neg5+ and Rxe7)} Kh8 (18... Kg8 {fails to save Black after} 19. Neg5 Qf6 20. Nxf7 $1 Rxf7 21. Ne5 Be6 22. Bxe6 Qxe6 23. Nxf7 Qxf7 24. Qd8+ Kh7 25. Qe8 $1 Qxe8 26. Rxe8 Bf6 27. Rde1 {and Black is hopelessly tangled up on his queenside development}) 19. Neg5 Qf6 20. Nxf7+ $1 Rxf7 21. Bxf7 Qxf7 22. Ne5 Bxe5 23. Qxh6+ Qh7 24. Qxh7+ Kxh7 25. Rxe5 {Threat: Re7+ and either rook takes d7. Now if} Bc6 26. Re7+ Kh6 27. Rd8 {when Black can resign.}) 18. Bc4 Be8 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Again Black is ready to complete his development with Nc6 or Nd7, but again Tal doesn't allow it!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This awful contortion is not what Black needs to complete his development project. Yet all of us are sometimes driven to extreme measures when we feel the pin prick of need. --- EXERCISE (combination alert): White's position experiences multiple states of well being: 1. Black is missing a chunk of his army (his knight and a8-rook). 2. Tal's hidden attackers lean their ears closer to Black's king, listening to everything he has to say. The presence of number one on the list, means that Black's king is insufficiently protected. Petrosian plans to finally develop his queenside with ...Nc6 next move. Tal never gave him the opportunity. How should White continue? --- ANSWER: Annihilation of the defensive barrier. This sacrifice is justified since half of Black's army is AWOL on the queenside.'} (18... Bg4 $6) 19. Neg5+ $1 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This knight weaves and dances like a candle's flame, left near a draughty window.'} hxg5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Loses immediately, but other moves are also hopeless:'} (19... Kg8 20. Ne6 $18) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Declining is no better:'} 19... Kh8 20. Qf4 hxg5 (20... Qf5 21. Qxf5 gxf5 22. Rd8 $1 {The rest is easy} Bc6 (22... Bd7 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. Re7 Rxd8 25. Nxd8 {leaves Black hopelessly busted.}) 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. Re7 Bxf3 (24... Bf6 25. N7g5+ Kg6 26. Rxf8 Bxe7 27. Rg8+ Kf6 28. Nh7#) 25. Ng5+ Kg6 26. Rxg7+ Kxg7 27. Ne6+) 21. Nxg5 Bh6 22. Nxf7+ $1 $18) 20. Nxg5+ Kg8 21. Qf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In a single move, Tal redeems the entirety of his previously unpaid debt. Threat: Qh4 and Qh7 mate. The black king's deepest fear has come to fruition: he has fallen short of his ruthless f4 sister's expectations.'} Nd7 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Too late...' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Black hopes to swing his knight to f6, but never gets the chance. White to play and force mate. --- ANSWER: Removal of a key defender.'} 22. Rxd7 $1 Bxd7 ({Mate is unavoidable after} 22... Qxd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'entombs Black's king and allows White to force a quick mate after'} 23. Qh4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) 23. Bxf7+ Rxf7 24. Qxf7+ Kh8 25. Qxg6 Bf5 {is met with} 26. Nf7+ Kg8 {(Black's king, beset by enemies, searches frantically for an improvised weapon; at last he found one: a plastic McDonald's coffee stir stick, which somehow still fails to compensate for the white queen and knight's war axe and spiked morning star)} 27. Nh6+ {wins.} 1-0

