{Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E77: King's Indian: Four Pawns Attack: 6 Be2 incl 6...c5 7 d5 e6 8 Nf3, but not Benoni '} 1. d4 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O 5. f4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The King's Indian Four Pawns Attack. Its prime directive: to gain everything, we must in turn risk everything (which just happens to be Tal's prime directive as well!). It's an opening designed for those players with a low boredom threshold. He or she is the eternal teenager, looking for thrills, who never grows up, and never wants to. Still, I logically ask: if you are the 50:1 underdog against Tal, is it really wise to pick the sharpest possible line, which ensures tactics and crazy complications - Tal's specialty?'} ({QUESTION: Why can't White gain a tempo with} 5. e5 {- ? --- ANSWER: He can, at serious risk of overextension. It feels to me like this is a lousy version of the Alekhine's Defence Four Pawns Attack for White, after} Ne8 6. f4 d6 7. Be3 c5 $1 {(a completely sound pawn sacrifice, which exploits White's development lag, by forcing the centre open)} 8. dxc5 Nc6 $1 {. Fischer went on to exploit his development lead with a brilliantly famous queen sacrifice, R.Letelier Martner-R.J.Fischer, Leipzig Olympiad 1960. I didn't put this game in 'Fischer: Move by Move', since Everyman continues its unreasonable and arbitrary policy of limiting my books to under 1,000 pages.}) 5... d6 6. Nf3 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. exd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Why bother to play an ultra-sharp line, and then chicken out with a safe move?'} ({Sharper and probably stronger is} 9. cxd5 Bg4 {(this move follows the Benoni rule of swapping away the light-squared bishop - Black's worst piece)} 10. O-O Nbd7 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 Re8 13. g4 $5 h6 14. h4 {when all hell soon breaks loose, Ding Liren-R.Rapport, Biel 2013.}) 9... b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When we follow theory, we are supported by past knowledge. When we bang out a new idea, we leap joyfully into the dark abyss of the unknown. By making a sacrifice like this one, we basically declare: "I am willing to base a very important decision on a hunch." This isn't quite a novelty, since a game was played with this move the previous year. I don't know if Tal was familiar with the game, or he came up with this idea independently. As usual, he shakes up the divinely established order of things with this sacrifice. I will pre-empt your coming question by telling you this is a rare instance of a sound sacrifice from Tal. Normally, ...b5 sacrificial ideas are only played when White is forced to take b5 with a pawn, rather than the c3-knight. --- QUESTION: What does Tal get for the pawn? --- ANSWER: The following: 10 Nxb5 will be followed by 10...Ne4!. This creates several issues for White: 1. The c3-square is covered, so his b5-knight must then retreat to the awkward a3-square, where it costs heavy time to get the knight back into the game. --- 2. Black's dark-squared bishop has been unleashed, which in conjunction with the coming ...Rb8 means that White may experience difficulty in developing his queenside, since b2 needs protection. --- 3. Black's unchallenged e4-knight is a formidable piece. --- 4. As a result of the sacrifice Black gains open lines, active pieces and a development lead. --- Conclusion: We don't have to make every pawn offer based on exact measurement. Tal's pawn sacrifice looks sound to me.'} 10. Nxb5 (10. cxb5 {gives Black a nice looking Benko Gambit after} a6 11. O-O axb5 12. Bxb5 Na6 {, G.Bagaturov-A.Shirov, Frunze 1989.}) 10... Ne4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The position is familiar and strange in equal parts. It looks 50% Benoni and 50% Benko Gambit.'} 11. O-O a6 12. Na3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White decides to press the matter and hang on to his extra material.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It may have been wiser to return the pawn and complete development with'} 12. Nc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Nxc3 13. bxc3 Bxc3 14. Rb1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when White certainly stood no worse, and might even be a shade better, due to his extra space, J.Pinter-J.Banas, Stary Smokovec 1974.'} Bg4 $11) 12... Ra7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move was new. The rook may swing over to b7 or, more likely, e7.'} (12... Re8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'was played in the only game which preceded Tal's game:'} 13. Bd3 Ra7 14. Qc2 Rae7 15. Bd2 ({rather than hand over the important dark-squared bishop, perhaps White should consider the cumbersome} 15. Nb1 $5 {, intending to challenge the e4-knight next move, with either Nc3, or Nbd2}) 15... Nxd2 16. Qxd2 Bg4 17. Nc2 $6 {White is just worse if he returns the pawn;} ({he should try} 17. h3 Bxf3 18. Rxf3 Nd7 19. Raf1) 17... Bxb2 18. Rab1 Bg7 {and Black's domination of the dark squares gave him the edge and he went on to win, A.Chaikovskaia-V.Rozlapa, Moscow 1963.}) 13. Bd3 Re7 14. Nc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White brings his knight somewhat back into play. It covers d4 and e3, yet is unable to challenge Black's e4-knight.'} Rfe8 15. Re1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White is intent on retaining his extra pawn.'} ({Once again, I would think about returning the pawn in order to disperse Black's pressure with} 15. Nd2 Nxd2 16. Bxd2 $1 Bxb2 17. Rb1 Rb7 18. Re1 {, when White stands no worse.}) 15... Nd7 16. Ne3 ({QUESTION: Why didn't White challenge the e4-knight with} 16. Nd2 {- ? --- ANSWER: It loses on the spot. Let's pause for an exercise: EXERCISE (combination alert): White's last move was a blunder. Black to play and win. --- ANSWER: Clearance:} Nf2 $3 17. Kxf2 Rxe1 18. Nxe1 Qh4+ 19. Kf1 Bd4 {. We have seen this theme before in Tal's Benonis. Mate is threatened on f2 and White loses material, no matter what he plays.}) 16... Ndf6 17. Qc2 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White has an active position'} Nh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Inducing a weakening of his opponent's light squares, which become a harbinger of White's future decline.'} 18. g3 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Controls f4' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'We all hate that feeling where we are certain that our opponent is up to something, but what that 'something' is may be beyond our ability to prognosticate.'} (18. Nf1 Ng5 $1) 18... Bd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'And here is Tal's 'something'. His grand adventure is, of course, accompanied by risk of loss, and the cost of Black's war is underwritten by the sacrifice of the defender of his dark squares. When rational thought is disengaged, the result will either be confused chaos, or flawless intuition, steering us infallibly in the right direction. I believe Tal's move is a case of the latter. --- QUESTION: Isn't Black's dark-squared bishop the holy grail of the Benoni? --- ANSWER: It is, and Tal's nonconformist last move shows just what lengths he was willing to take to seize the initiative. It's an attempt to distil an abstract notion into solid form. The move is so exceptionally deep, that the comps initially prefer White's position, and only later apologize and prefer Black.'} 19. Nxd4 cxd4 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White has the pair of bishops'} 20. Ng2 ({It may have been better to try} 20. Nf1 Nhf6 21. b4 Qb6 22. Bb2 Bf5 23. a3 h5 {, and if} 24. Rac1 $6 Nc3 $1 25. Rxe7 Rxe7 26. Bxf5 Re2 $1 27. Nd2 gxf5 28. Qd3 Nfe4 {with huge complications and approximately even chances.}) 20... Ng5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal finds a clever zwischenzug shot which seizes control over White's kingside light squares.'} 21. Rxe7 Nh3+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Zwischenzug.'} 22. Kf1 (22. Kh1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is scary since White's back rank is eternally weak, as long as the h3-knight remains in place.'} Qxe7 23. Bd2 Nf6 24. Re1 Ng4) 22... Rxe7 ({Tal didn't like} 22... Qxe7 23. Bd2 {when Re1 follows.}) 23. Bd2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!'} (23. f5 $11 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '=!?' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'is an interesting alternative' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'prevents Black's coming manoeuvre.'}) 23... Nf6 $15 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal immediately seizes upon the slip, transferring his knight to g4.'} 24. Nh4 {[#] Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} (24. f5 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'would allow White to play on'} Ng4 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)} 25. Nf4 $17 Nhf2 $1 26. h3 Nxd3 27. hxg4 Ne5 28. c5 Nxg4 29. cxd6 Ne3+ 30. Bxe3 Rxe3 31. Qc7 Qe8 {leaves White somewhat overextended. Yet this line is still better than the game's continuation for White.}) 24... Ng4 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '"Two riders were approaching and the wind began to howl." Black's knights menace the h2-, f2- and e3-squares. There are insects that mimic the attributes of twigs and leaves to camouflage their location. Black's knights qualify for this status, as they seem to 'belong' in White's territory, when in reality, they don't.'} 25. Nf3 (25. Kg2 Qe8 26. f5 Re2+ 27. Bxe2 Qxe2+ 28. Kxh3 h5 29. Rh1 Nf2+ 30. Kg2 Nxh1+ 31. Kxh1 Qf1#) (25. b4 {may be slightly more accurate, yet still favours Black. The comp found the line} Qe8 26. Nf3 Re3 $1 27. Nxd4 Qe7 28. Qc3 Bf5 $1 29. Nxf5 gxf5 30. Be1 Rxg3 $3 31. hxg3 Ne3+ 32. Ke2 Nxd5+ {which wins White's queen.}) 25... Re3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Another uninvited attacker slips into White's territory.'} 26. Kg2 (26. Nxd4 $2 {is met with the crushing shot} Nxf4 $1 27. gxf4 Qh4 {which leaves White helpless.}) 26... Qe7 27. Re1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): It appears as if White is rallying. This would be true, if not for Black's coming combination...'} (27. Kxh3 $4 {is unplayable due to} Rxd3 $1 {, winning instantly.}) 27... Nxf4+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Annihilation of defensive barrier.'} 28. gxf4 Rxe1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This forces a retreat of a key defender.'} 29. Nxe1 ({Obviously the rook is taboo, due to} 29. Bxe1 $4 Ne3+ {, forking king and queen.}) 29... Qh4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The Czarina is displeased. Now the queen enters, threatening h2, f2 and even e1, which becomes apparent next move.'} 30. Bc1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Unfortunately for White, this is the only move, since f2 must be covered, as well as the e3 fork square.'} Qxe1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A predator looks for one in the herd with a weakened gait. So Tal regained his sacrificed piece, while retaining his winning attack.'} 31. h3 (31. b3 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)} Qh4 32. Kg1 Ne3 $19) 31... Nh6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Seizing control over f5.'} 32. f5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It's uncharitable to give a player a question mark when he or she is busted anyway. This is complete desperation, which actually speeds White's defeat.'} (32. Qd2 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) is the last straw} Qh4 33. f5 Nxf5 34. Qf4 $19 (34. Bxf5 Bxf5 {is also hopeless for White, since h3 falls, and his king remains exposed.})) 32... Nxf5 33. Bf4 (33. Bxf5 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) is one last hope} Bxf5 34. Qf2 Qxc1 35. Qxd4 $19) 33... Nh4+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} 34. Kh2 Nf3+ 35. Kg2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): White's king acutely feels the presence of malevolent, watchful eyes all around. Black has two clear wins in the position. Find one of them. --- ANSWER: Attraction.'} Bxh3+ {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): '!' Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'the end of the story' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop strives to exact obedience from White's king. Tal just had a way of mesmerizing his prey with a hypnotic stream of sacrifices.'} ({ANSWER no.2: Also winning is} 35... Qg1+ $1 36. Kxf3) 36. Kxf3 (36. Kxh3 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)} Qh4+ 37. Kg2 Ne1+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins the house.'} 38. Kf1 (38. Kg1 Qg4+ {Decoy} (38... Qxf4 {Zwischenzug})) 38... Qh3+ {Double attack} (38... Qxf4+ {Zwischenzug})) 36... Qg1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"Even as a child, you were always slow to understand. So I will patiently explain your new circumstances," lectures the queen, to her brother, who has heard all this before. This deadly finishing touch threatens ...Bg4+ and ...f5 mate.'} 37. Bxg6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): I assure you that coming combination won't heavily tax you mentally. Black is winning if he simply recaptures on g6. However, he has a much stronger move. What is it?'} ({If} 37. Ke2 Bg4+ 38. Kd2 Qf2+ 39. Kc1 Qe1+ 40. Qd1 Qxd1# {.}) (37. Bh6 f5 $1 {(threat: ...Bg4+ and ...Qe3 mate, which leaves White's king curiously helpless)} 38. Bc1 Bg4+ 39. Kf4 Bd1 $1 {, and if White attempts to save his queen with} 40. Qd2 {, Black plays} Qg4# {.}) 37... Qg4+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Zwischenzug. Black picks up both white bishops.'} 38. Kf2 Qxf4+ 39. Kg1 (39. Ke1 {(Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)) doesn't get the cat off the tree} Qf1+ 40. Kd2 Bg4 41. b4 Qf4+ 42. Ke1 Qe3+ 43. Kf1 Bh3+ 44. Qg2 fxg6 45. Qxh3 Qxh3+ 46. Kf2 Qe3+ 47. Kf1 d3 48. Kg2 d2 49. c5 d1=Q 50. cxd6 Qdg1#) 39... hxg6 (39... Qg3+ {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)} 40. Kh1 hxg6 41. b3 Bg4 42. Qg2 Bf3 43. Qxf3 Qxf3+ 44. Kh2 d3 45. c5 d2 46. c6 d1=Q 47. a4 Qdh1#) 40. Qf2 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s)} Qc1+ 41. Kh2 d3 42. Kxh3 d2 43. Qe2 d1=Q 44. Qxd1 Qxd1 45. c5 dxc5 46. Kg3 Qxd5 47. Kf2 f5 48. Ke2 f4 49. a3 Qe4+ 50. Kd1 f3 51. Kd2 f2 52. Kc3 f1=Q 53. b4 Qc1+ 54. Kb3 Qcc2# 0-1

