{Smash and Grab: Benoni hero Mikhail Tal plays a brilliant miniature. Endre Vegh: 'The following famous game shows what the Modern Benoni was able to offer Tal, that made him use it again and again throughout his career:'} 1. d4 {Marcin Sieciechowicz Postny,Evgeny} Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 {Postny,Evgeny: 'Benoni was one of Tals' pet openings, especially in his younger years.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Benoni war eine von Tals' Lieblingseröffnungen, vor allem in seinen jüngeren Jahren.'} 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 g6 7. e4 ({Today, top players generally play} 7. Bf4) ({or} 7. Nd2 {.}) 7... Bg7 ({QUESTION: Why did Tal avoid} 7... Bg4 {which allows him to swap away his traditional problem piece in the Benoni, his light-squared bishop? --- ANSWER: A cautionary guideline: the trouble is 7...Bg4?! fails to achieve its swapping goal after} 8. Qa4+ $1 Bd7 (8... Nbd7 $2 9. Nd2 {leaves the g4-bishop dangling, with no safe squares;} Bh5 10. h3 g5 11. e5 $1 dxe5 12. Nde4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 {is a winning position for White}) 9. Qb3 b6 10. a4 {. Black's bishop is completely misplaced on d7, since it blocks his queenside development, I.Nikolaidis-L.Rama, Ilioupolis 1995.}) ({Black can also play the move order} 7... a6 $1 8. a4 {and only now} Bg4 $1 {, which prevents White's annoying Qa4+ ideas.}) 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O Re8 10. Nd2 {Postny,Evgeny: 'The classical line.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Die klassische Variante.'} Na6 ({Endre Vegh: 'Slightly more common is'} 10... Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5] Postny,Evgeny Endre Vegh: '.' is another route for this knight. ist ein weiterer Weg für diesen Springer.}) 11. Re1 {The super-solid 11 f3 is the most popular move nowadays. Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It isn't clear if the rook belongs on e1.''} (11. f4) ({Endre Vegh: 'More solid is'} 11. f3 {!} Nc7 12. a4 b6 (12... Nd7 13. Kh1 b6 14. Ra3 Qe7 15. a5 {when White pursues queenside ambitions, S.Mamedyarov-V.Ivanchuk, Khanty-Mansiysk 2010.}) 13. Nc4 $14) 11... Nc7 {Endre Vegh: 'It was the invention of this plan by Nezhmetdinov which inspired Tal to use the Modern Benoni. As we know now this is often a slightly dubious way of developing, but in this game it worked well. The main problem is that the knight takes two moves to get to c7, where it has little influence over the important squares in the centre. Generally speaking, it is more often better placed at d7. '} 12. a4 b6 {! Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'The idea behind this move is to play ...Bc8-a6, as well as to prepare ...b6-b5 with ...Bc8-d7, ... Ra8-b8 and ...a7-a6.'} ({Endre Vegh: 'The idea behind this move is to play ...Bc8-a6, as well as to prepare ...b6-b5 with ...Bc8-d7, ... Ra8-b8 and ...a7-a6. If Black had played'} 12... a6 {Endre Vegh: 'White would have been able to secure himself a small structural advantage with'} 13. a5 {Endre Vegh: '!?' Endre Vegh: ', although Black still has an interesting plan in'} Rb8 (13... Bd7 $5 {with the idea of ...Bd7-b5.}) 14. Qb3 $14) 13. Qc2 {Endre Vegh: 'It seems that White should be able to find a better waiting move. The idea behind the text is to play Nd2-c4 without leaving e4 hanging, but it weakens White's control over the kingside too much.'} ({Endre Vegh: 'It seems that White should be able to find a better waiting move. The idea behind the text is to play Nd2-c4 without leaving e4 hanging, but it weakens White's control over the kingside too much.'} 13. h3 {Postny,Evgeny ! Endre Vegh: '!?' Endre Vegh: 'has been suggested as an improvement.' is objectively more accurate. Here is a good illustration how White can break through: ist objektiv genauer. Hier ist eine gute Illustration, wie Weiß durchbrechen kann:} Rb8 (13... Ba6 $13 14. Nb5 {?!} Bxb5 15. Bxb5 Nxb5 16. axb5 Qd7 $15) 14. Bb5 Re7 15. Bf1 Re8 16. Nc4 Ba6 17. Bg5 h6 18. Bh4 Bxc4 19. Bxc4 a6 20. e5 $1 Rxe5 (20... dxe5 21. d6 Ne6 22. Rxe5 $16) 21. Rxe5 dxe5 22. Bxf6 Bxf6 23. d6 Ne8 24. d7 Nc7 25. Qd6 $18 {1-0 (43) Khairullin,I (2657)-Andriasian,Z (2600) Yerevan 2014}) (13. Bf3 Nd7 14. Nc4 Ne5 {=}) (13. Rb1 {!?}) 13... Ng4 {Postny,Evgeny: '?!' Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'The knight is doing little on f6, as the pressure against e4 is only of fleeting importance. Instead the weakness of the dark squares on the kingside is important. Also the knight will find a good square on e5.  Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's reckless knight comes running at full speed, like a puppy who hears her name called. It isn't easy to creep closer to our secret goal, without inadvertent communication of its intent. It is only because of your writer's kindly nature that I give Tal's last move an interesting mark, rather than the dubious it actually deserves! --- EXERCISE (critical decision): Tal's provocative last move gives White a choice: swap away Black's knight with 14 Bxg4, or chase it away with 14 h3. One line gives White a strategic advantage, while the other allows Black a strong attack. Which move should he play?'' Postny,Evgeny: 'This knight jump worked perfectly in the current game, but objectively not the best move.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Dieser Springersatz funktionierte in der aktuellen Partie perfekt, war aber objektiv nicht der beste Zug.'} (13... Rb8 {Postny,Evgeny} 14. Nc4 Ba6 15. Bf4 Bxc4 16. Bxc4 a6 17. Qd3 Ng4 18. Qg3 Ne5 19. Bf1 b5 $132 {0-1 (57) Paragua,M (2521)-Shabalov,A (2622) Ledyard 2008}) 14. h3 $2 {This walks into a typical Tal attack. Postny,Evgeny: '?!' ? %08DA Endre Vegh: 'White completely ignores the threats to his king. This game was played at the start of Tal's career and sacrifices on f7, e6 and h7 (or f2, e3 and h2) were still allowed - in a fashion that seems unreal to today's youngsters. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is no time for vacillation. Gurgenidze walks into a vicious attack. Is Tal going to back down and retreat his knight? Not likely!''} ({Endre Vegh: 'Instead Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: White secures a clear strategic advantage by immediately removing the intruder with''} 14. Bxg4 {Endre Vegh: '!?' Endre Vegh: 'was an interesting possibility. White would not usually want to part with this bishop, but here he does so as Black then has less control over e5 and problems in finding a good square for his own bishop after Nd2-c4 and Bc1-f4. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(a laying out of facts tends to unsettle a guilty conscience; this move ruins Tal's trap and turns his 13...Ng4?! into a dubious idea)'' Marcin Sieciechowicz: '!?' Postny,Evgeny: 'is simple and effective.' Postny,Evgeny: 'ist einfach und effektiv.'} Bxg4 15. Nc4 {'' Endre Vegh: 'when Bf4 is coming and d6 is vulnerable. Also Black's g4-bishop just dangles on the square, not sure just where he is going. Following' Marcin Sieciechowicz: '' Postny,Evgeny: 'This is strategical dream for White.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Das ist ein strategischer Traum für Weiß.'} (15. h3 Bc8 {!} 16. Nf3 f5 17. Bg5 Qd7 $15) 15... Qf6 (15... Bc8 16. Bf4 Bf8 17. Qd3 $1 {(intending Qg3)} Ba6 18. b3 {White exerts unpleasant pressure on d6, while preparing an eventual e5 break.}) 16. Be3 Bd7 17. Qd2 {would have left White with a small advantage. Postny,Evgeny: '+-' Postny,Evgeny: '½-½ (57) Hort,V-Pribyl,J Luhacovice 1971' Postny,Evgeny: '½-½ (57) Hort,V-Pribyl,J Luhacovice 1971'}) (14. Nf3 {Marcin Sieciechowicz} f5 15. h3 Nf6 (15... Ne5 $5 16. Bf4 Nxf3+ 17. Bxf3 g5 18. Bd2 g4 19. hxg4 fxg4 20. Be2 Bd4 $13) 16. exf5 (16. Bg5 h6 17. Bxf6 Qxf6 18. Bd3 Bd7 19. Rad1 Rad8 $11) 16... Bxf5 17. Qb3 Ne4 18. Nb5 a6 19. Nxc7 Qxc7 20. Bf1 c4 21. Qxc4 Qxc4 22. Bxc4 Nc5 $44) 14... Nxf2 $3 {Postny,Evgeny: '!' !! Endre Vegh: 'A brilliant sacrifice that draws the white king into the open.  Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Annihilation of king's cover. Unlike many of Tal's other sacrifices, this one is completely sound. By now in the book, Tal's motivation pattern is well established. His motto: taunt, then engage.'' Marcin Sieciechowicz: '!'} ({Did Gurgenidze really believe Tal would meekly retreat with} 14... Ne5 $2 {Marcin Sieciechowicz: '?!'} 15. f4 Nd7 {after which White gained time, as well as the advantage?} 16. Nf3 {Marcin Sieciechowicz} a6 17. Bd3 Rb8 $14) 15. Kxf2 {Postny,Evgeny: '?' Endre Vegh: 'White cannot decline the sacrifice either. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now White's fatally weakened king draws his enemies near to him, as if with a beckoning finger.'' Marcin Sieciechowicz: '?' Postny,Evgeny: 'Too naive - losing by force.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Zu naiv - verliert forciert.'} (15. Nf3 $1 {Postny,Evgeny} Bxc3 {Marcin Sieciechowicz: '!?'} (15... Bd4 $6 16. Nxd4 cxd4 17. Bb5 $1 Nxh3+ 18. gxh3 dxc3 19. Bxe8 Nxe8 20. Qxc3 $16) (15... Nxh3+ {Marcin Sieciechowicz} 16. gxh3 Bxh3 17. Bf4 Qd7 18. Bg3 Bg4 19. Nb5 Bxf3 20. Bxf3 Nxb5 21. axb5 Bd4+ 22. Kh2 Be5 23. Bxe5 Rxe5 24. Rf1 Qxb5 $11) 16. bxc3 Nxe4 17. Bd3 {White is two pawns down, but has wonderful compensation thanks to the pair of bishops. The dark squares in Blacks' camp are especially vulnerable. Weiß hat zwei Bauern weniger, aber dank des Läuferpaars eine wunderbare Kompensation. Die dunklen Felder im Lager von Schwarz sind besonders verwundbar.} Ng3 (17... Nf6 18. Bg5 Ncxd5 19. Qb2 $1 $36 {[%cal Gc3c4]}) 18. Bf4 (18. Bg5 {Marcin Sieciechowicz} f6 19. Bf4 Nh5 20. Bh6 Qd7 21. c4 Ba6 22. g4 Rxe1+ 23. Rxe1 Ng7 $13) 18... Nf5 19. c4 $44) ({White cannot decline the sacrifice either. After} 15. Rf1 Nxh3+ 16. gxh3 Bxh3 {his kingside is torn apart.}) 15... Qh4+ (15... Bd4+ 16. Kg3 Be5+ 17. Kf2 Qh4+ 18. Kf1) 16. Kf1 {This is forced as Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'No choice, since his e1-rook requires protection.''} (16. g3 {allows a brilliant mate with ? Endre Vegh: '??' Endre Vegh: 'walks into'} Bd4+ 17. Kg2 ({Endre Vegh: 'or'} 17. Kf3 {Postny,Evgeny} Qh5+ {'-+'} 18. Kg2 Bxh3+ 19. Kh2 Bg4+ 20. Kg2 Qh3#) 17... Qxh3+ 18. Kf3 Bg4+ (18... Re5 $1 {the quickest path to mate;} 19. Nc4 Bg4+ 20. Kf4 g5# {.}) 19. Kf4 g5+ 20. Kxg5 Be3+ 21. Kf6 Qh6# {Endre Vegh: 'is also mate'}) 16... Bd4 {Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening mate on f2, which forces White's next move.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'transposes to the game continuation.''} 17. Nd1 {The only move to avoid mate in one with Qf2. Endre Vegh: 'The only way to defend against the mate.  Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE  (combination alert): White's fragile hope of remaining alive has about the same chance as a flower, after a freshly fallen snow storm. How would you continue Black's attack? --- ANSWER: Queen sacrifice.''} Qxh3 $3 {Postny,Evgeny: '-+!' Endre Vegh: 'This queen sacrifice must have come as a surprise to White. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The first rule of a prosecutor is to never ask the witness a question to which he or she doesn't already know the answer. This is bargaining from a position of strength, since it isn't a real queen sacrifice. Black threatens mate again, this time on h1.'' Postny,Evgeny: 'Neat tactics. The more obvious 17...Bxh3 is less effective.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Nette Taktik. Das offensichtlichere 17...Lxh3 ist weniger effektiv.'} ({Endre Vegh: 'This queen sacrifice must have come as a surprise to White. But even after' After Nach} 17... Bxh3 $2 {Postny,Evgeny Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'lacks the same force as Tal's move and allows White to fight on after''} 18. Ra3 {White survives überlebt Weiß} (18. Bf3 Bc8 {Black would have a reasonable attack for his piece. So it is really difficult to spot exactly what was the reason for White's mistake.}) 18... Bf5 (18... Bd7 {Marcin Sieciechowicz} 19. Ne3 Qh1+ 20. Kf2 Qh4+ 21. Kf1 $11 Bxe3 $2 22. Rxe3 Nxd5 23. exd5 Rxe3 24. Nf3 Qe4 25. Qd2 Re8 26. Qxe3 Qxe3 27. Bxe3 Rxe3 $16) 19. Ne3 {.}) 18. Bf3 {Endre Vegh: 'The only move. White has to cover himself against ...Bc8-g4+. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A wounded veteran friend once told me that you don't actually feel the pain during the battle, since you don't have the time to waste on it.''} ({Endre Vegh: 'The only move. White has to cover himself against ...Bc8-g4+. Of course not Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White avoids''} 18. gxh3 {?? Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??''} Bxh3# {is a very pretty checkmate. Endre Vegh: ', Cyrus Lakdawala: '.''}) ({while after} 18. Bb5 Qh1+ 19. Ke2 Bg4+ 20. Kd3 Qxe1 {Black wins again.}) 18... Qh2 {Endre Vegh: 'Black has sacrificed a piece for only two pawns. But once you stop and consider which pawns we are talking about, you realise the permanent danger in White's position. The white king will never feel safe again. '} 19. Ne3 (19. Nf2 Ba6+ {Endre Vegh: '?'} (19... Nxd5 {! Endre Vegh: '!?' Endre Vegh: 'is very direct:'} 20. exd5 Rxe1+ ({worse is} 20... Ba6+ $2 21. Be2 Bxf2 22. Nf3 $1 ({not} 22. Kxf2 $2 Qh4+ 23. Kf1 Rxe2 24. Rxe2 Re8 {and Black wins after} 25. Nf3 Qh1+ 26. Kf2 Rxe2+ 27. Qxe2 Bxe2 28. Kxe2 Qxg2+ 29. Ke3 g5 {- White might have three pieces for the queen, but this is the only good thing to say about his position which is in a state of general collapse}) 22... Qg3 23. Bxa6 Bxe1 24. Bb5 Bb4 25. Bxe8 Rxe8 26. Bg5 {and White seems to have beaten off the attack and the position remains unclear}) 21. Kxe1 Qg1+ 22. Nf1 Ba6 $19 23. Nd3 Re8+ 24. Be2 Bc8 $1 {and White cannot defend himself satisfactorily against the threat of ...Bc8-g4.}) (19... Z0) (19... f5 {, as in the game, is the most logical move.} 20. Z0 ({The white centre cannot hold together.} 20. Nc4 $6 fxe4 21. Rxe4 Bf5 {would give Black a strong attack against the white king,}) ({while after} 20. Re2 {Black has many ways to strengthen his attack.} Qh4 $5 {as suggested by Fritz is one move, which leads to a strong pressure for the piece.} ({Another is} 20... Ba6 $1 21. Nc4 fxe4 22. Rxe4 Rxe4 23. Nxe4 Nxd5 {and the white king is completely stripped of cover. That Black will win seems beyond doubt.}))) 20. Nc4 (20. Be2 {Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'is still dangerous for White, but it is not easy to prove a direct win, e.g.'} Qh4 21. Nh3 Bc8 {!} 22. Nf3 {Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'would be OK for White, and the game might end in a draw after'} Qxh3 {!} 23. Nxd4 Qh1+ 24. Kf2 Qh4+ $19 {Endre Vegh: '.'}) 20... Nxd5 21. exd5 Rxe1+ 22. Kxe1 Qg1+ $19) 19... f5 $1 {! Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal ruthlessly opens central lines, casting his f-pawn into a supporting role in the attack.'' Postny,Evgeny: 'Crushing through the center.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Bricht im Zentrum durch.'} 20. Ndc4 ({The attack is relentless. Endre Vegh: 'After'} 20. exf5 $2 {Endre Vegh: '??'} Bxe3 {wins for Black. Endre Vegh: 'and White is slaughtered.'}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why can't White's king make a run for the queenside with'} 20. Ke2 {is the computer's move. Obviously this is very risky and in the 1950's no one would consider such a move, but these days nothing is too strange for the well-educated tournament player. However, White's position is falling apart all the same: Cyrus Lakdawala: '- ? --- ANSWER: He won't make it across the hostile centre: for example,'} Bxe3 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 21. Kxe3 g5 {would be one way for Black to continue the attack against the white king.} (21... Nxd5+ 22. Ke2 (22. Kd3 $4 Nb4+ {forks king and queen}) (22. Kf2 Qh4+ {is decisive as well}) 22... Nb4 23. Qb3+ d5 24. Kd1 Ba6 $1 {(White is paralysed, with his king stuck in the middle of the board)} 25. Nf1 Qe5 26. Bd2 Bc4 27. Qc3 Qxc3 28. bxc3 Bb3+ 29. Ke2 Nc2 30. Kf2 fxe4 31. Be2 Nxa1 32. Rxa1 d4 {when Black's rook and four pawns overwhelms White's two minor pieces.})) 20... fxe4 21. Bxe4 Ba6 $1 {Introducing another pin. White is totally tied up. 'with the idea' 22... Re4 23.Qe4 Re8 24.Qe8 Ne8$19; |22... Bc4 23.Nc4 Rf8 24.Bf3 Rf3!$19 Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Pinning the c4-knight, while preparing to bring out his last undeveloped piece, his a8-rook.'' Postny,Evgeny: 'The black bishops are so powerful. The white monarch will not survive.' Postny,Evgeny: 'Die schwarzen Läufer sind so stark. Der weiße Monarch wird nicht überleben.'} 22. Bf3 Re5 {Endre Vegh: '?' Endre Vegh: 'This automatic move is rather careless as it allows White still to offer some resistance. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The human move, preparing to double rooks on the e-file, which adds further pressure to White's troubled e3-knight.''} ({This automatic move is rather careless as it allows White still to offer some resistance. Black has many better moves, one of which is} 22... Qf4 $1 {winning at once.}) ({The comp found} 22... Rf8 $1 23. Ke2 Rxf3 24. Kxf3 Re8 $1 25. Bd2 Qh4 26. Rh1 Rf8+ 27. Ke2 Rf2+ 28. Kd3 Qf4 29. Rae1 Bxe3 {forcing mate.}) 23. Ra3 (23. Ke2 Rae8) 23... Rae8 24. Bd2 $2 {Endre Vegh: 'Now White collapses. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The comp doesn't like this move, but everything else loses as well. --- EXERCISE  (combination alert): Continue Tal's attack. --- ANSWER: Pin.'' Marcin Sieciechowicz: '?!'} ({Endre Vegh: 'Now White collapses. After'} 24. Rd3 {was the last chance. Endre Vegh: '!' Endre Vegh: 'Black still has the edge and a strong attack, but the direct win is missing.' Endre Vegh: 'the preliminary'} Nxd5 {Endre Vegh: 'was given as winning in the four-volume collection of Tal's games published by Chess Stars, but did not take into account the only move'} (24... Rg5 {is probably better.}) 25. Rxd4 (25. Nxe5 $1 {when the position remains unclear. One continuation is} Nf4 26. N5c4 Qh1+ 27. Kf2 Qh4+ 28. Kf1 {when Black does not seem to have more than a draw.}) 25... cxd4 26. Bxd5+ Rxd5 27. Qd3 (27. Nxd5 Qh1+ 28. Kf2 Qxe1+ 29. Kf3 Rf8+ 30. Bf4 Qf1+ 31. Kg3 Bxc4 $19) 27... Qh1+ 28. Ke2 Qxg2+ {'with the idea' 29... de}) (24. Ke2 {Postny,Evgeny} Bxe3 (24... Qf4 25. Kd1 Bxc4 26. Qxc4 Bxe3 $19 27. Qxf4 {Marcin Sieciechowicz} Bxf4 28. Rxe5 Bxe5 $19) 25. Rxe3 Qh4 $1 {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4c4]} 26. b3 Rxe3+ 27. Bxe3 Rxe3+ 28. Kxe3 Qxe1+ $19) 24... Nxd5 $1 {The winning move. White has no defence to all the threats. ! Endre Vegh: 'Not a very difficult combination. '} (24... Qf4 {!?}) 25. Bxd5+ (25. Qc1 Nxe3+ 26. Bxe3 Rxe3 27. Raxe3 Rxe3 28. Rxe3 Qh1+ 29. Ke2 Qxc1 $19) ({And not} 25. Nxd5 $4 Qg1# {.}) 25... Rxd5 {Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} 26. Ke2 ({Endre Vegh: 'No time to grieve for the fallen, since if'} 26. Nxd5 {Postny,Evgeny Endre Vegh: '??'} Qh1# {Endre Vegh: '. After the king's murder, the authorities consider his sister a "person of interest", which is police speak for: "person who committed the crime, but we don't yet have enough evidence to arrest and convict".'}) (26. Re2 Qh1+ 27. Kf2 Bxc4 28. Qxc4 Rf8+ 29. Kg3 Be5+ $19) (26. Rd3 Rf5+ 27. Ke2 Qh5+ 28. g4 Qxg4#) 26... Bxe3 {Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} 27. Rxe3 {Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Black attackers continue to ransack the white king's home. How did Tal end the game? --- ANSWER: Removal of key defenders.''} Bxc4+ {Z0 Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'First the sermon, then comes the bishop's favourite part: the collection plate.''} ({It's mate after Endre Vegh: 'On'} 27... Bxc4+ {Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} {Endre Vegh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or''} 28. Qxc4 (28. Kd1 {Postny,Evgeny} Rxe3 29. Rxe3 Qg1+ 30. Re1 Qf2 $19 {Endre Vegh: '(threat: ...Bb3)'} 31. Kc1 {(White's king clings to his hope of escaping to the queenside, the way a drowning person clings to a plank of wood in the middle of a Tsunami)} Bb3 32. Qc3 Qxe1+ $1 33. Bxe1 Rd1# {is mate. Games like this make me want to chuck the Slav and take up the Benoni!}) 28... Qxg2+ {Endre Vegh: 'mates.'} 29. Kd1 Qxd2#) ({Qxc4} 27... Qxg2+ 28. Kd1 Qxd2+) ({Kd1} 27... Rxe3+ $19 {Rxe3 Qxc4 Rxe1+ Kxe1 Qg1+ Qxg2}) (27... Z0 {Tal is not the only World Champion to have used the Modern Benoni successfully in important games. Fischer used it in his World Championship match against Spassky, and Kasparov has played it several times too, such as a crucial game against Korchnoi at the Lucerne Olympiad 1982, where he daringly sacrificed a piece and won. --- Among modern players the Modern Benoni has less support than previously. Chess is like all other aspects in human life: fashions are very important. In the 1950's Tal's sharp, tactical chess replaced, for a short while, the long strategic games of Botvinnik and Smyslov. Then came Petrosian with his very prophylactic style, though the 1960's were dominated just as much by the more modern dynamic play of Spassky, Larsen, Korchnoi and Tal. Fischer and Karpov both had a very strong emphasis on technique and the 1970's saw many short draws. In the 1980's Kasparov changed the understanding of chess with his dynamic play, while in the late 1990's Kramnik and the computer programs changed chess again, and a more defensive style has once again become predominant, even in the games of such wonderful attacking players as Kasparov and Judit Polgar. The latter recently said in an interview in New in Chess that she had changed her style as it gave more points to counter-attack. As can be seen, it is merely fashion that makes Topalov the only top player in the third millennium to employ the Modern Benoni regularly. Other sharp openings, such as the King's Indian and Benko Gambit, have also lost popularity lately and find fewer and fewer followers. This does not mean that they are bad systems, however. Nimzowitsch declared the Scandinavian Defence (1 e4 d5) as losing in My System in the 1920's, but it still gave Anand a reasonable position in his 1995 World Championship match against Kasparov. Fashions change and many follow them, while some do not. --- The Modern Benoni is a difficult opening to master. It offers Black a lot of piece activity in return for a rather fragile pawn structure in the centre. But as the game played by Tal above illustrates, it also offers good winning chances! In his old book on the Modern Benoni, Nunn wrote that God playing White would probably beat God playing Black in this opening. But between humans there is no reason why this opening should not offer you great results. It is a difficult opening to master for both colours and should therefore be treated with respect.}) 0-1

