{'Myers: This game is given as #39 (in date order) by Burgess/Nunn/Emms in "The World's Greatest Chess Games".'} 1. e4 c5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = B63 - Sizilianisch-Rauser Variante /Sicilian-Rauser Variation'} 2. Nf3 d6 (2... Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 {1-0 (37) Tal,M-Koblencs,A Riga 1957 [Fritz 5.00 (5s)]}) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5 e6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The Richter-Rauzer line of the Sicilian.'} 7. Qd2 {Myers: 'Rauzer'} Be7 8. O-O-O O-O 9. Nb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move sets up Bxf6 ideas.' Myers: 'Burgess: This is known as the Podebrady Variation, after the town where it was first played (by Alekhine). White avoids an exchange of knights and increases the pressure on the d6-pawn. However, White must be wary of actually grabbing this pawn if the positional price is too high.'} (9. f4 {is more commonly played today.}) 9... Qb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Doesn't this move block Black's b-pawn? --- ANSWER: It does. As compensation, Black's queen vacates d8 for the rook, in preparation for a future ...d5 break.'} ({Less accurate is,} 9... Qc7 10. Bxf6 gxf6 {(R.Bellin-N.Oud, Guernsey 1989), when White's coming attack looks rather scary after} ({I don't see compensation for the pawn after} 10... Bxf6 $6 11. Qxd6 Qb6 12. Qg3) 11. h4 {.}) (9... Na5 10. Kb1 Nxb3 11. axb3 Ne8 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Nb5 Bd7 14. Nxd6 Nxd6 15. Qxd6 Qxd6 16. Rxd6 Bc6 17. f3 Rfd8 18. Rxd8+ Rxd8 19. Bd3 e5 20. Kc1 Kf8 21. Kd2 Ke7 22. Ra1 a6 23. Ke3 Rd6 24. Ra5 f6 25. b4 Kd7 26. g3 g5 27. f4 gxf4+ 28. gxf4 Re6 29. f5 Re7 30. b5 axb5 31. Bxb5 Rg7 32. Bxc6+ Kxc6 33. Ra8 Rg2 34. Rf8 Rxh2 35. Rxf6+ Kc5 36. b4+ Kc4 37. Rd6 Rh3+ 38. Ke2 Rh4 39. Kf3 h5 40. Re6 Rf4+ 41. Ke3 h4 42. Rxe5 h3 43. Rd5 Rh4 44. Rd4+ Kc3 45. Rd1 h2 46. Rh1 Rh3+ 47. Kf4 Rh4+ 48. Ke5 Kd2 49. f6 Ke3 50. Kd6 Rxe4 51. Rxh2 Rd4+ 52. Ke5 {1-0 Alekhine,A-Frydman,P/Podebrady 1936/}) 10. f3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move solidifies e4, while setting up an English Attack style plan with g4 and h4 to follow.'} (10. Be3 Qc7 {doesn't bother Black, since after} 11. Nb5 Qb8 {White is unable to capture d6, while Black regains tempi with ...a6 and ...b5.}) ({One of Black's ideas of placing his queen at b6 is that} 10. Bxf6 $2 {just hands Black a valuable bishop after} Bxf6 {and White is unable to grab d6, since} 11. Qxd6 $2 {is met with} Qxf2 {, with a strategically won game for Black.}) 10... a6 11. g4 (11. Bxf6 {offers Black full compensation after} Bxf6 $1 12. Qxd6 Rd8 13. Qc5 Rxd1+ 14. Kxd1 Qc7 15. Qe3 b5 16. f4 b4 17. Na4 e5 {. Black gained several tempi for his attack, owns the bishop pair and also dominates the dark squares, which more than compensates White's extra pawn, V.Jansa-V.Babula, Lazne Bohdanec 1995.}) 11... Rd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move covers d6, which eliminates White's Bxf6 ideas. Now White must be on constant alert for ...d5.'} 12. Be3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' B63: Sicilian: Richter-Rauzer: 7...Be7 and unusual Black 7th moves' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move actually sends Black's queen to the square she wants to go to. In return, White clears the path for g5.'} Qc7 13. h4 $6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'N' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Inaccurate.'} ({Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Predecessor:' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White should take the steam out of Black's ...d5 with the immediate'} 13. g5 $1 {Timman Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' Edouard,Romain: 'should be played first as in the game Black would have had time for ...d5.' Edouard,Romain: 'sollte zuerst gespielt werden, da Schwarz in dieser Partie Zeit für ...d5 gehabt hätte.'} Ne8 (13... Nd7 14. f4 (14. h4 Nce5 15. f4 Nc4 16. Bxc4 Qxc4 17. h5 b5 18. Qg2 b4 19. Ne2 a5 20. Kb1 a4 21. Nd2 Qa6 22. Nc1 Bb7 23. Qe2 Qc6 24. Nd3 b3 25. Rc1 Qa6 26. c4 a3 27. axb3 d5 28. Bd4 dxc4 29. Nxc4 axb2 30. Bxb2 Nb6 31. Qe3 Qa2+ 32. Kc2 Nxc4 33. bxc4 Ra3 {0-1 Nikolaidis,K-Gershon,A/Kavala 2003/}) (14. Qf2 Nce5 15. Kb1 b5 16. Rg1 b4 17. Na4 Rb8 18. Nd2 Bb7 19. b3 Nc5 20. Nxc5 dxc5 21. f4 Nd7 22. h4 Nb6 23. Bh3 Qc6 24. h5 a5 25. g6 fxg6 26. f5 exf5 27. hxg6 hxg6 28. Qg3 fxe4 29. Qe5 Bc8 30. Bxc8 Rbxc8 31. Qxe7 Nd5 32. Qg5 Nc3+ 33. Ka1 a4 34. Rdf1 axb3 35. Nxb3 Qa4 36. Nc1 Qxc2 {0-1 Kaiumov,D-Lerner,K/Tashkent 1984/}) 14... b5) 14. Qf2 Rd7 15. Na4 Rd8 16. Bb6 Bxg5+ 17. f4 {1-0 Brodsky,M-Kasimdzhanov,R/Cappelle op 1996/EXP 51}) 13... b5 $6 {Myers: 'Tal: A position has arrived where nothing is hidden. The opponents' aims are completely evident--to reach the other's  as soon as possible. These games remind one of short-distance sprints, where the deciding elements are speed and energy.'} (13... d5 $1 $10 {Timman Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'takes advantage of the white queen's unfavourable position:' Myers: '=!' Edouard,Romain: 'Timman' Edouard,Romain: 'Timman'} 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Rxd5 16. Qe2 Rxd1+ 17. Qxd1 {, M.Hebden-N.Giffard, Paris 1988. Black's queenside attacking chances look more promising than White's on the other side after} b5 {.} (17... Bd7 18. Kb1 Nb4 19. c3 Nd5 20. Bg5 f6 21. Bc1 Rd8 22. Bd3 Nb4 23. cxb4 Bb5 24. Nd4 Rxd4 25. Bxh7+ Kxh7 26. Qxd4 Qc4 27. Qxc4 Bxc4 28. Bd2 e5 29. Re1 Bd6 30. Kc2 Bd5 31. Re3 Bc6 32. b3 Bb8 33. Rc3 Kg6 34. h5+ Kf7 35. Rxc6 bxc6 36. Kd3 Ke6 37. Ke4 Bd6 38. a3 Bc7 39. Be3 Bd6 40. Bc5 Bc7 41. a4 Bd8 42. Bf8 Kf7 43. Bd6 Ke6 44. Bc5 Bc7 45. Kd3 f5 46. gxf5+ Kxf5 47. Bf8 Kf6 48. Kc4 Bb6 49. Bc5 Bc7 50. Be3 {1-0 Hebden,M-Giffard,N/Paris 1988/})) 14. g5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now we transpose back into White's correct move order.'} Nd7 (14... b4 15. gxf6 bxc3 16. Qf2 cxb2+ 17. Kb1 Bxf6 18. Bb6 Qb8 19. Bxd8 Nxd8 20. f4 a5 21. Bg2 a4 22. Nd2 Ra6 23. Nc4 Nb7 24. Rhg1 Na5 25. Ne3 Qb6 26. e5 Nc4 27. Rd3 dxe5 28. Rgd1 Nxe3 29. Qxe3 g6 30. fxe5 Qxe3 31. exf6 Qxd3 32. Rxd3 h5 33. Rd8+ Kh7 34. Rxc8 e5 35. Rc6 Ra7 36. Kxb2 Rd7 37. Ka3 Rd4 38. Rc7 Kg8 39. Rc8+ Kh7 40. Rf8 Rxh4 41. Rxf7+ Kh6 42. Bd5 Rf4 43. Rf8 {1-0 Yang Zi-Ji Yunqi/Kina 1987/}) 15. g6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'N' 'N' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 10 Games' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal takes trespass of his coach's territory lightly, by offering a pawn to rapidly open kingside lines.' Myers: '!?'} (15. f4 b4 (15... Nc5 16. Bg2 b4 17. Ne2 a5 18. Nbd4 Bb7 19. Ng3 Nxd4 20. Bxd4 b3 21. Kb1 bxa2+ {½-½ (21) Djurasevic,B-Bogdanovic,R Belgrade 1952}) 16. Ne2 (16. Na4 Nc5 17. Naxc5 dxc5 18. Qf2 c4 19. Bxc4 Rxd1+ 20. Rxd1 a5 21. Kb1 a4 22. Nc1 Na5 23. Bd3 Nc4 24. Bxc4 Qxc4 25. Rd4 Qc6 26. Qd2 Ba6 27. h5 g6 28. hxg6 fxg6 29. Nd3 Bxd3 30. cxd3 Qb5 31. Rc4 Rd8 32. Qc2 b3 33. Qc3 bxa2+ 34. Kxa2 Bf8 35. Bd4 Rd7 36. Be5 Rd8 37. d4 a3 38. b3 Ra8 39. Qc2 Re8 40. Qh2 Re7 41. Bd6 Rb7 42. Rb4 {1-0 Flack,R-Altrichter,S/corr DDR 1979/}) 16... a5 17. Kb1 Bb7 18. Ng3 a4 19. Nc1 d5 20. exd5 Nb6 21. Bxb6 Qxb6 22. Bg2 Na5 23. Qe2 Ba6 24. Qg4 Nc4 25. dxe6 Na3+ 26. bxa3 bxa3+ 27. Nb3 axb3 28. axb3 a2+ 29. Ka1 Bb4 {Kislov,M-Stirberg,S/URS-sf Voronez 1991/EXP 22/½-½ (61)} 30. exf7+ Kf8 31. Ne4 Bd3 32. c3 Qe3 33. Qf3 Qxe4 34. Rc1 Qxf3 35. Bxf3 Ba3 36. Rcd1 Ra7 37. Be4 Rad7 38. Bxd3 Rxd3 39. Rxd3 Rxd3 40. Kxa2 Be7 41. Rc1 Rd2+ 42. Kb1 Rh2 43. c4 Rxh4 44. c5 Bd8 45. Rd1 Ba5 46. Rd4 Bc7 47. Rd7 Bxf4 48. Kb2 Be5+ 49. Ka3 Rd4 50. Rb7 Rf4 51. b4 Rxf7 52. Rb6 Rf5 53. b5 Rxg5 54. Rb7 h5 55. b6 Bd4 56. c6 Bxb6 57. Rxb6 Rc5 58. Kb4 Rc1 59. Kb5 Kf7 60. Ra6 g5 61. Kb6 {½-½ Kislov,M-Stirberg,S/Voronezh 1991/}) (15. h5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Nf8 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. Qxd4 Bb7 18. Kb1 Rac8 19. h6 e5 20. Qb6 Nd7 21. Qxc7 Rxc7 22. hxg7 Kxg7 23. Bh3 Rh8 24. Bf5 Nf8 25. a4 bxa4 26. Nxa4 Ne6 27. Rh5 Bc6 28. Nc3 Nf8 29. Nd5 Bxd5 {Liang Jinrong-Chan Peng Kong/Thessaloniki olm 1984/BIG 80/1-0 (61)}) ({A more cautious route would be} 15. Kb1 b4 16. Ne2 {with mutual chances, V.Anand-A.Muir, Barcelona 1993.}) 15... hxg6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This recapture doesn't look traversable in full safety, and offers White huge attacking compensation.'} ({Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Black should try'} 15... fxg6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '=' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus 