{[%mdl 8192] E13: Queen's Indian/Nimzo-Indian hybrid: 4 Nc3 Bb7 5 Bg5 h6 6 Bh4 Bb4 Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E13: Queen's Indian/Nimzo-Indian hybrid: 4 Nc3 Bb7 5 Bg5 h6 6 Bh4 Bb4 '''} 1. d4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Tal,Mihail Tal,Mihail Tal,Mihail: '1) Tal in Sahs (Riga) 1959. Reprinted in 2) Kirillov,V: Mikhail Tal 1949-1961. Riga. 1996. p.188-191.'} Nf6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = E13 - Damenindisch-5.g5 /Queen's Indian-5.g5'} 2. c4 e6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'In this tournament the King's Indian Defence underwent certain crises, and even such an expert in this opening as Gligoric was subjecting it to major analysis. At the end of the tournament he again used it, but without success.''} 3. Nf3 b6 4. Nc3 Bb7 5. Bg5 Bb4 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'By a transposition of moves we find ourselves in a variation of the Nimzo-Indian Defence where the position, in the light of the most recent games, slightly favours White. Of this variation I should have been careful, because Gligoric was a witness to my game with Dückstein in the Zurich international tournament, where White got the better of it. Having burned my bridges as to the choice of opening I was forced to employ the same continuation as in the game against Dückstein.''} 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 g5 ({Mikhail Tal: 'International Master Konstantinopolsky, who annotated this game in the tournament bulletin, preferred'} 7... Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qe7 {Mikhail Tal: 'followed by ...d6, ...Nbd7 and ...0-0-0. I think that the text-move is stronger, since after 8...Qe7 White would continue'  d6, bd7 und 0-0-0 (Konstantinopolski)} 9. Nd2 {[%csl Ye4] Mikhail Tal: 'and the knight would control the very important square e4.'}) 8. Bg3 Ne4 9. Qc2 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 d6 11. Bd3 Nxg3 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: '!''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'In Keres-Taimanov, 22nd USSR Championship, Black played'} 11... f5 {Mikhail Tal: 'with the idea of attacking on the kingside, but after'} 12. d5 $1 {1-0 Keres,P-Taimanov,M URS-ch22 Moscow 1955 Mikhail Tal: 'not only did the attack fail, but Black's position became untenable owing to his bad pawn structure. With the text-move Black maintains his pawn structure.'}) 12. hxg3 Nd7 13. a4 ({Mikhail Tal: 'More natural would be'} 13. Be4 {Mikhail Tal: 'but after'} Bxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qc6+ Ke7 {Mikhail Tal: 'the 'dangerous' position of Black's king is only illusory. Black's next move is positionally forced.'}) 13... a5 14. Rb1 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'last book move'} g4 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'It is interesting that Black has no better move. If he moves his queen to enable queenside castling, then after Be4 and the exchange of bishops, the rook on b1 would be ideally placed and White would follow up with c5. The move ...Nf6 is impossible at the moment, because of Nxg5.'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'It is interesting that Black has no better move. If he moves his queen to enable queenside castling, then after Be4 and the exchange of bishops, the rook on b1 would be ideally placed and White would follow up with c5. The move ...Nf6 is impossible at the moment, because of Nxg5.''} (14... Nf6 15. Nxg5) (14... Qe7 { 0-0-0} 15. Be4) 15. Nh4 Nf6 16. d5 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'This move was necessary – otherwise Black would prepare for queenside castling, and then I could not find an active plan for White.'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} ({More consistent is} 16. c5 $1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=!?'}) 16... Qe7 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'If Black takes the pawn on d5, then all White's pieces would become very dangerous.'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): '='} (16... exd5 $6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!?'} 17. cxd5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} Bxd5 $15) 17. O-O {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Weaker would be'} 17. dxe6 $143 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!?'} Qxe6 18. Bf5 (18. Nf5 $11 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)}) 18... Be4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 17... Nd7 {Tal,Mihail: 'JvR: '!?' Mikhail Tal: 'After this logical move White takes over the initiative.'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Very tempting was the exchange sacrifice' Mikhail Tal: 'To be truthful I must say that after' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Very tempting was the exchange sacrifice Mikhail Tal: 'To be truthful I must say that after''} 17... O-O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'I would not have given up my active knight on h4 for the passive rook, but would have played the simple'} 18. dxe6 (18. e4 {Mikhail Tal: '.'}) 18... fxe6 19. Ng6 Qg7 20. Nxh8 Rxh8 {Mikhail Tal: ': in this position White would play'} 21. c5 {Mikhail Tal: 'activating his pieces. Of course the play would become very interesting, but Black's chances would not be bad.'}) (17... exd5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 18. cxd5 Bxd5 19. e4 $17) 18. dxe6 Qxe6 {last book move Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move'''} (18... fxe6 $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 19. Ng6 Qf6 20. Nxh8 Qxh8 21. Be4 $18) 19. Bf5 {White threatens to win material: Bf5xe6 Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'White threatens to win material: Bf5xe6''' Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: '!''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Immediately necessary, as after'} 19. Rfd1 Nc5 20. Bf5 Be4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black equalises.'}) (19. Nf5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} Qe5 $15) 19... Qxc4 20. Rfd1 Nf6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Black still cannot castle.''} (20... Ne5 $5 $16) ({Mikhail Tal: 'In the position after' Mikhail Tal: 'After' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'In the position after Mikhail Tal: 'After''} 20... O-O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'White would avoid the continuation' Mikhail Tal: 'much stronger would be' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'White would avoid the continuation Mikhail Tal: 'much stronger would be''} 21. Rd4 (21. Rb5 Bc6 22. Rd4 Bxb5 23. Rxc4 Bxc4 24. Qe4 Be6 $11 {Mikhail Tal: ', when White would have no obvious continuation.'}) 21... Qc5 22. Rb5 Qa3 23. Bxg4 {Mikhail Tal: 'and White has an excellent position.'}) 21. Rd4 Qc6 {Tal,Mihail: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The aim of''} 22. Be6 {Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' '!' Tal,Mih