{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In a lifetime of crazy, if I had to pick Tal's craziest game, this one would be my choice.'} 1. Nf3 {Bulletin Huebner: 'Huebner,Robert'} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. d4 c6 5. Bg5 dxc4 6. e4 ({If} 6. a4 Bb4 7. e4 {, then} c5 $1 8. Bxc4 cxd4 9. Nxd4 h6 {with equality, Bronstein-Botvinnik, Moscow 24th matchgame 1951.}) 6... b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Thank God that covering this opening is outside the jurisdiction of this book. Keller's game plan: "Okay then, I'm playing Mikhail Tal in his invincible prime, in the year 1959, Anno Domini. My best shot may be to play the Botvinnik Semi-Slav, one of the most complicated openings in chess. He is certain to get confused!" Did Keller commit the Van Helsing/Harker error? --- Oh, you don't know what that is? Then let me explain. I remember watching Dracula: Prince of Darkness at age 8. Professor Van Helsing and Jonathan Harker were about to ram a wooden stake through Christopher Lee's dark heart and, just then, the sun went down, allowing the enraged, undead count to burst out of his coffin and start kicking butt with his super vampire strength. The obvious question arises: why did the professor and Mr. Harker attack so close to sunset, when they could have waited until the next morning, at 9:00 a.m., after a leisurely pancake breakfast at IHOP?'} 7. a4 ({Another attempt to avoid the main line of the Botvinnik Variation} 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Nxg5 hxg5 10. Bxg5 Nbd7 11. exf6 {etc.}) 7... Qb6 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'The critical line, aimed at holding the pawn;''} (7... Bb4 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: '?!' '?!''} 8. e5 h6 9. exf6 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' '!''} hxg5 10. fxg7 Rg8 11. h4 $1) ({Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'or''} 7... b4 $6 8. Nb1 Be7 9. e5 Nd5 10. Bxe7 Nxe7 11. Bxc4 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'is advantageous to White.''} a5 12. Nbd2 {Tukmakov-Pinter/Szirak/1985/i40/522  e4 ×c5, ×d6 Huebner: 'Tukmakov-Pinter/Szirak/1985/i40/522  e4 ×c5, ×d6 Huebner,Robert: 'Tukmakov-Pinter/Szirak/1985/i40/522  e4 ×c5, ×d6''}) ({Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'although it is more solid to play''} 7... Bb7 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' '!' Garry Kasparov: ', for example:''} 8. axb5 (8. e5 h6 9. Bh4 g5 10. exf6 gxh4 11. Ne5 (11. axb5 cxb5 12. Nxb5 Bb4+ 13. Nc3 Qxf6 14. Qa4+ Nc6 15. Ne5 Qf4 16. Nxc6 Bxc3+ 17. bxc3 Qe4+ 18. Be2 Bxc6 {with equality, Kir.Georgiev-Nogueiras, Sarajevo 1985}) (11. Be2 c5 $5 12. dxc5 Nd7 {is unclear, Dokhoian-Kuijf, Wijk aan Zee 1989}) 11... Nd7 $1 12. Qh5 (12. Be2 Nxe5 13. dxe5 Qc7 14. Qd4 h3 $1 {etc.}) 12... Qxf6 13. Nxd7 Kxd7 14. axb5 cxb5 15. Qxb5+ Bc6 16. Qxc4 Bd6 {with a comfortable position (Van Wely-Kramnik, Manila Olympiad 1992).}) 8... cxb5 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'or''} 9. e5 (9. Nxb5 Bxe4 (9... Bb4+ 10. Nc3 Bxe4 11. Qa4+ Nc6) (9... Qb6 $5 10. Bxc4 Nxe4) 10. Bxc4 Bb4+ 11. Nc3 Nbd7 $10 {Ribli-Inkiov/Dubai ol/1986/i42/542} (11... O-O 12. O-O {, and Black is close to equalising after both} Bb7 {(D.Garcia-Kramnik, Pamplona 1992)} ({and} 12... Bxc3 13. bxc3 Nbd7 {.}))) 9... h6 10. Bd2 ({Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'or''} 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Nxb5 Bb4+ 12. Nc3 fxe5 13. Nxe5 Nc6 {Osnos-Ivanov,S/UdSSR/1985/i41/485'Osnos-Ivanov,S/UdSSR/1985/i41/485 Garry Kasparov: 'with equality, Osnos-S. Ivanov, USSR 1985' Garry Kasparov: 'with equality, Osnos-S. Ivanov, USSR 1985 Huebner: 'Osnos-Ivanov,S/UdSSR/1985/i41/485'''}) 10... Ne4 11. Nxb5 Nxd2 12. Qxd2 (12. Nxd2 a6 13. Nc3 Qxd4 14. Nxc4 Nc6 {is also level, Veingold-Oll, Tallinn 1988}) 12... Bxf3 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' '!''} 13. gxf3 Nc6 {Schtscherbakov-Novikov/Blagoveschtschensk/1988/i46/(559) Huebner: 'Schtscherbakov-'Schtscherbakov-Novikov/Blagoveschtschensk/1988/i46/(559) Garry Kasparov: 'Huebner: 'Schtscherbakov-Novikov/Blagoveschtschensk/1988/i46/(559)''' Novikov/Blagoveschtschensk/1988/i46/(559) Huebner,Robert: ' Schtscherbakov-Novikov/Blagoveschtschensk/1988/i46/(559) ''} 14. Rd1 Qb6 15. Bxc4 Bb4 16. Nc3 O-O-O {and ...Rxd4  (Shcherbakov-Novikov, Blagoveshchensk 1988)}) 8. Bxf6 gxf6 9. Be2 {Huebner,R: 'Lutz: Weiß spielt also ein echtes Gambit, da er den Bauern vorerst nicht wiedersehen wird. Aber dafür hat er Entwicklungsvorsprung, Zentrumskontrolle und der schwarze Königsflüegel ist zerstört. Eine der ersten Partien zu diesem Komplex war die berühmte Partie Tal-Keller (Zuerich 1959), die gewisse Zweifel an der schwarzen Stellung aufkommen ließ. Allerdings ist die Variante alles andere als klar.'} a6 ({Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Back in 1946 Bronstein tried''} 9... Nd7 $6 10. O-O (10. d5 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: '!?' '!?''} b4 (10... Bb4 11. dxc6 Qxc6 12. Nd4 Qxe4 13. O-O) 11. a5 Qc7 12. dxe6 fxe6 13. Na4 Ne5 14. Nxe5 Qxe5 15. O-O $14 {[%emt 0:00:28] Vachidov-Galachov/UdSSR/1984  ECO D (2. Auflage) 44/6, Fußnote 28 Huebner: 'Vachidov-Galachov/UdSSR/1984 ECO D (2. Auflage) 44/6, Fußnote 28 Vachidov-Galachov/UdSSR/1984 ECO D (2nd Edition) 44/6, footnote Huebner,Robert: 'Vachidov-Galachov/UdSSR/1984  ECO D (2. Auflage) 44/6, Fußnote 28''}) 10... Bb4 ({Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'if''} 10... Bb7 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'there is the typical undermining move''} 11. d5 (11. axb5 cxb5 12. b3 {Dolmatov Huebner: 'Dolmatov Huebner,Robert: 'Dolmatov '''Dolmatov Garry Kasparov: '!' '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Huebner: 'Dolmatov'''} b4 13. Na4 Qc7 14. Bxc4 Bxe4 15. d5 $36) 11... cxd5 12. exd5 b4 13. a5 Qa6 14. dxe6 fxe6 15. Na4 Rg8 16. Qc2 O-O-O {Eingorn-Dolmatov/UdSSR/1985/i40/(522) Huebner: 'Eingorn-Dolmatov/UdSSR/1985/i40/(522) Huebner,Robert: ' Eingorn-Dolmatov/UdSSR/1985/i40/(522)''}) 11. d5 Bxc3 12. bxc3 Nc5 {Huebner: 'Garry Kasparov: 'with a double-edged struggle.''} 13. Qd4 (13. dxc6 $142 $14 {Clarke/Bouwmeester/Withuis} Nxe4 (13... Nxa4 14. Rxa4 bxa4 15. Qd6 {Clarke} Qd8 (15... Ba6 $2 16. Qd7+ Kf8 17. Ne5 fxe5 18. Bh5 Kg7 19. Qxf7+ Kh6 20. Qf6+ Kxh5 21. h3 Rhg8 22. g4+ Rxg4+ 23. hxg4+ Kxg4 24. Kh1 $18 {Körholz Huebner: 'Körholz Huebner,Robert: 'Körholz''}) 16. Qg3 Kf8 17. Rd1 Qe7 $13) 14. Qc1 {! Clarke} (14. Qc2 {c1. Huebner: 'c1. Huebner,Robert: ' c1.''} f5