{Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'B10: Caro-Kann: 2 d3 and 2 c4 ' Cyrus Lakdawala: '"This game was seemingly a paradox: one of the greatest masters of his time was unable to withstand a bluff - a semi-correct, risky attack, arising literally out of nothing!" wrote Kasparov of this game.' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'Hier bekommt man ein typisches Figurenopfer a la Tal zu sehen: in einer praktischen Partie schwer zu widerlegen, und 40 Jahre später erweist es sich bei der Überprüfung mit Fritz als unzureichend.'' Hier bekommt man ein typisches Figurenopfer a la Tal zu sehen: in einer praktischen Partie schwer zu widerlegen, und 40 Jahre später erweist es sich bei der Überprüfung mit Fritz als unzureichend.} 1. e4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} c6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = B10_09 Caro-Kann Vert 2.d3 / Caro-Kann Def 2.d3'} 2. d3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'Koblents's suggestion – the fruit of some thorough home preparation.'} ({QUESTION: Tal wasn't a closed games player. Why would he pick such a line against a strategist like Smyslov? --- ANSWER: My guess is that Tal didn't want to play his normal line} 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, since this line is named after Smyslov, who pioneered it and undoubtedly understood its intricacies better than Tal.}) 2... d5 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Nd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Today, this move is known to be an inaccuracy.' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'Viel häufiger wird 4...d6 gespielt - wahrscheinlich zu recht.'' Viel häufiger wird 4...d6 gespielt - wahrscheinlich zu recht.} ({Correct is Garry Kasparov: 'Later the more natural'} 4... Bd6 $1 {, and if Garry Kasparov: 'came to be preferred.'} 5. d4 exd4 6. exd5 cxd5 7. Nxd4 Nc6 8. N2f3 Nge7 {is a position which can be reached too via a Tarrasch French move order.}) 5. d4 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now the opening divides into a separate sub-species. Tal gets his open game after all, and a favourable one at that, since he leads in development.' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Tal's eagle-eye spotted a lack of harmony in the opponent's ranks. ChessBase: '!?'' Adam Hunt: 'It looks peculiar that such an approach can be good – White moves his d-pawn for a second time and in doing so liquidates the centre. However, this gives his bishops freedom and in the hands of such a gifted attacking player as Tal this proves effective. '} (5. g3 {would lead to a Kings Indian Attack, which results in very different positions to the game continuation.}) 5... dxe4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why can't Black play'} 5... exd4 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', just as you gave in the above note? --- ANSWER: The problem is Black committed his knight to d7 prematurely and is misplaced for a Tarrasch French formation after' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: '!?''} 6. exd5 cxd5 7. Nxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black gets an inferior version of the Tarrasch French, due to the d7-knight placement.' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'und der Stellungstyp des Tarrasch-Franzosen ist erreicht.'' und der Stellungstyp des Tarrasch-Franzosen ist erreicht.} Ngf6 {was much safer, but Smyslov never liked to create weaknesses in his own position.}) 6. Nxe4 exd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move violates the principle: Avoid breaking the central tension without good reason.'} ({Black may be better off going into emergency mode with} 6... Ngf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 8. Bg5 Qe6 {, J.Hickman-N.Hutchinson, Gibraltar 2006.} 9. dxe5 {can be met with} f6 10. Bf4 fxe5 11. Bg3 Be7 {when Black, although still worse, is better off than in the game's continuation.}) ({Instead,} 6... f5 $6 {intending ...e4, next is overly optimistic:} 7. Neg5 e4 8. Bc4 $1 Nh6 (8... exf3 $2 9. Bf7+ Ke7 10. O-O $1 {gives White a winning attack}) 9. Ne6 {is quite awful for Black.}) 7. Qxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is in serious trouble: 1. He lags behind in development in an open position. --- 2. White soon menaces Nd6+, which gives him the bishop pair in the open position.' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'last book move. . ' As a result of some pawn exchanges, a position with an open centre has occured on the board. White's pieces have already occupied the central squares, so his position is preferable. Mehrere Bauern wurden getauscht und es ist eine Stellung mit offenem Zentrum entstanden. Die weißen Figuren haben die Zentralfelder bereits besetzt; seine Position ist daher vorzuziehen.} (7. Bc4 {is also good; White's rapid development and domination in the centre guarantee him an enduring initiative.}) 7... Ngf6 8. Bg5 (8. Nd6+ Bxd6 9. Qxd6 Ne4 10. Qa3 Ndf6 {would be okay for Black.}) 8... Be7 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (8... Qa5+ {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 9. c3 Nd5 10. Bc4 $11) 9. O-O-O {JvR: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A vow made over 1,000 times (i.e. I'm going to lose weight!) tends to diffuse its potency. Tal always went with the emotion-based response, when the position demands a Mr. Spock-like response, free from stylistic bias. This move, which glides away from the topic at hand, throws away the advantage. --- QUESTION: Why would Tal avoid such an obviously favourable line?' Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'Die lange Rochade und die folgenden Züge beinhalten ein sehr schwieriges Konzept, denn es muss eine Figur geopfert werden.'' Die lange Rochade und die folgenden Züge beinhalten ein sehr schwieriges Konzept, denn es muss eine Figur geopfert werden.} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Once again, we see Tal's weakness crop up: He wants too much from a position and ends up with nothing. He gains a serious advantage with the continuation you, me and every other player in the world would make with' Garry Kasparov: ''A big advantage would have been given by ChessBase: 'Tal weist auf diese Möglichkeit hin:'' Attention deserved Tal weist auf diese Möglichkeit hin: Aufmerksamkeit verdiente} 9. Nd6+ Bxd6 10. Qxd6 {Garry Kasparov: 'This would indeed have been simpler and wiser – since also after'} Qe7+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. Here lies the rub. An ending, Tal's least favourite part of the game! However, after'} (10... Ne4 11. Bxd8 Nxd6 {Lorin D'Costa: 'is okay for Black.'} 12. Bc7 {, Black is condemned to a difficult struggle for a draw. But Tal, with amazing psychological insight, unusual for one so young, guessed that Smyslov would feel more confident in an inferior endgame than in a double-edged middlegame, and he decided to keep the queens on.}) 11. Qxe7+ Kxe7 {Garry Kasparov: ', when the two bishops in an open position are not easy to neutralise.' (Tal) ChessBase: '"und das Läuferpaar in dieser offenen Stellung zu neutralisieren ist nicht einfach."'' "und das Läuferpaar in dieser offenen Stellung zu neutralisieren ist nicht einfach."} 12. O-O-O {White owns the bishop pair and a development lead in the ending. Black will be hard pressed to hold the game. and the two bishops in the open position secure White's advantage. und das Läuferpaar in der offenen Stellung sichert Weiß Vorteil.} (12. Z0)) 9... O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Sometimes we are inclined to discount the data, simply because we don't like the conclusion to which it points. The problem is Tal sometimes irrationally avoided endings - even good ones, if he could get a messy middlegame position instead! Of course, Tal would argue: not every position is conducive to a straightforward application of logic, and it makes sense to play to one's strengths, rather than enter the opponent's domain. And it's hard to argue with success. Maybe Tal just found technical endings boring, which is a danger, since boredom's presence tends to spur us into a dangerous enlivening mechanism.' Adam Hunt: 'Black is forced to castle kingside as he cannot keep the king on the open file in the centre for too long.   EXERCISE: How would you continue now?   ANSWER:'} 10. Nd6 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'last book move. Black has a cramped position. Black's piece can't move: c8' JvR: '!?' Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=' Garry Kasparov: 'Now this leap is not so effective, since Black is not obliged to exchange on d6.' Adam Hunt: '!?' Adam Hunt: 'We needed to be aware that Black had a threat in this position, using the indirect attack on the bishop on g5. With the text move White looks for an opportunity to gain the bishop-pair, which in a situation with an open centre is to his advantage.'} (10. Bd3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Adam Hunt: 'would avoid the loss of material, but '} Nxe4 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Bxe4 Nc5 $11 {Adam Hunt: 'would be comfortable for Black.'} (12... Nf6 13. Rhe1 Be6 {is fine for Black, as he has completed his development. Tal's move applies more pressure by not allowing him to develop freely and by discouraging exchanges.})) (10. Bc4 $4 {, for example, would lose a piece to} Nxe4 11. Bxe7 ({or} 11. Qxe4 Bxg5+ 12. Nxg5 Qxg5+) 11... Qxe7 {.}) 10... Qa5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 10... Nb6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'White might have regretted rejecting the highly promising endgame. Tal gave the variation ChessBase: '?''} 11. Nxc8 Rxc8 (11... Qxc8 12. Re1 Re8 13. Bd3 Nbd5 {also looks okay for Black.}) 12. Qh4 {Garry Kasparov: 'but after'} Qc7 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (12... Nfd5 $1 {Smyslov would undoubtedly have held this slightly inferior position. However, he was already hoping for more...}) 13. Bd3 h6 14. Bxh6 {Garry Kasparov: ', 'apparently with a decisive attack';'} (14. Z0) 14... gxh6 15. Qxh6 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '(Tal)' (Tal)}) (10... Bxd6 11. Qxd6 Qa5) 11. Bc4 $1 {JvR: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'Seemingly an oversight, but in fact a logical continuation of the tactics of complicating the position at any price. ChessBase: '!'' Adam Hunt: 'Tal develops his final minor piece to an aggressive square.' By means of piece manoeuvres White has constrained his opponent's forces. Durch Figurenmanöver hat Weiß seinen Gegner stark eingeengt.} ({Garry Kasparov: ''If'} 11. Kb1 $2 {Garry Kasparov: ', then ChessBase: '?''} Bxd6 12. Qxd6 Ne4 {Garry Kasparov: ', if there is nothing better. Therefore it became clear to me: my initiative could be maintained and developed only with the help of a piece sacrifice.' (Tal)'}) 11... b5 $1 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Black threatens to win material: b5xc4' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Both parties feel they are the ones entitled to ownership of the initiative. This is a little reminder to Tal that his own king may soon come under attack via a pawn storm.' Garry Kasparov: 'A rapid and natural reply. ChessBase: '!'' Lorin D'Costa: 'This position appears to be very good for White but Black is solid enough and if White doesn't play actively, he could be pushed back. Tal finds some interesting ways to keep the initiative. '} (11... Bxd6 12. Qxd6 Ne4 13. Qf4 (13. Qe7 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} Nxg5 14. Nxg5 $14) 13... Nxg5 14. Nxg5 Ne5 15. Bb3 h6 16. Ne4 {Garry Kasparov: '"ist Schwarz in einer schwierigen Lage". (Tal)' "ist Schwarz in einer schwierigen Lage". (Tal)}) ({Instead of trying to drive the white pieces away from the centre with for example Anstatt die weißen Figuren im Zentrum zu attackieren mit beispielsweise} 11... Qc5 {Black begins a flank attack. beginnt Schwarz einen Angriff am Flügel.}) 12. Bd2 $1 {JvR: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why did Tal retreat his bishop? --- ANSWER: He had two ideas in mind with the retreat: 1. Tal plans to switch the bishops to c3, where it takes aim at Black's king. --- 2. Tal vacates g5 for a knight to launch his kingside attack.' Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=' Garry Kasparov: ''No one in the auditorium expected this move!' (Koblents) The bishop switches with gain of tempo to the long diagonal, where it will be well employed and will have two key functions: to terrorise the opponent's king and defend its own. In addition, the g5-square is vacated for the queen – the main conductor of the impending attack.' Lorin D'Costa: 'It looks strange to go backwards, but White is rerouting the bishop to c3 and it proves to be quite strong down the a1-h8 diagonal. ' White uses the opponent's loss of time for a prompt attack on the kingside. The