avourable in view of} 23. Nxg4 Rad8 24. Rd4 $1 c5 25. Nxe5 cxd4 26. Ng6+ Kg8 27. Ne7+ Kh8 {('with equality' – 'Shakhmaty v SSSR')} 28. Qa5 $1 {(the instantaneous recommendation of the machine!)} Qxa5 29. Bxa5 {etc.}) 22. Rhe1 {Tal gibt nun nur 22...b8 an.} (22. Qxh6 Rf7 $1) {Doch nach} 22... Ne5 $1 (22... Rb8 23. Re7 ({but} 23. Re8) ({or} 23. Qxh6 {wins}) {, although this is bad because of} 23... Rxb2 $1 24. Bxb2 c3 $1 {.}) 23. Rxe5 Bg4 $1 {kann von weißem Vorteil keine Rede sein.} ({not} 23... Qa1+ $6 24. Kd2 Rd8+ 25. Bd4 Qxd1+ 26. Kxd1 Bg4+ 27. Qxg4 fxe5 28. Qe6) 24. Qxg4 Qa1+ 25. Kd2 Rad8+ 26. Bd4 Qxb2 (26... fxe5 $6 27. Rxa1 Rxd4+ 28. Qxd4 exd4 29. f3 {favours White}) 27. Ke1 Rxd4 28. Qxd4 Qxd4 29. Rxd4 fxe5 30. Rxc4 {with complete equality.}) 19... Kg8 20. N3g5 {JvR: '(Khalifman)'} (20. N7g5 {is better, although after} h6 $1 (20... Qa1+ $6 {is no longer so strong:} 21. Kd2 Bxc3+ 22. bxc3 Nf6 23. Rxa1 Nxh5 24. Rhe1 Nf4 25. Nh4 $1 h6 26. Ne4 g5 27. g3 gxh4 28. gxf4 Rxf4 29. Ke3 {and all White's fears are behind him}) 21. Qg6 $1 hxg5 22. Nxg5 Bxg5+ 23. Qxg5 Nf6 $1 (23... Rf7 $2 24. Rhe1 $1) 24. Bxf6 Rxf6 25. Rd8+ Kh7 $1 {, White's attack only brings him perpetual check;} ({but not} 25... Rf8 $2 26. Rxf8+ Kxf8 27. Qd8+ Kf7 28. Re1 {.})) 20... Qa1+ $1 {[%mdl 256]} ({Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'Tal gibt nur'' Tal gibt nur} 20... Bxg5+ 21. Nxg5 h6 {Garry Kasparov: '(hara-kiri)'} ({correct is} 21... Nf6 $1 22. Bxf6 Bf5 $1 23. Bxg7 $1 Qa4 $1 {– G.K.}) 22. Qg6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'mit weißer Gewinnstellung an.'' mit weißer Gewinnstellung an.}) 21. Kd2 Bxc3+ 22. bxc3 Nf6 $13 {JvR: '!' Adam Hunt: '!' Adam Hunt: '.'} 23. Qh4 (23. Rxa1 Nxh5 24. Ra5 h6 25. Ne4 Rxf7 26. Rxh5 Bf5 {with an obvious advantage to Black (Fritz).} (26... Z0)) 23... Qa5 24. Qxc4 Qd5+) (18... f6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 19. Nf7+ Kg8 20. Nxd8 Rxd8 $17) (18... Bc7 $5 {with the threat of ...Bf4+ and ...Qa1 mate. This clever reply was not considered at all by any of the commentators.} 19. g3 ({If White now rejects the perpetual check} 19. Nxf7+ Kg8 20. Nh6+ Kh8 21. Nf7+) ({and plays} 19. Qh4 $6 f6 20. Kd2 {(threatening 21 Ra1)} Qa4 21. Rhe1 {('Shakhmaty v SSSR') , an unpleasant surprise awaits him:} Qb5 $1 ({but not} 21... Ne5 $2 22. Rxe5 $1 Bxe5 23. Nxe5 fxe5 24. Qe7) ({or} 21... Nc5 $2 22. Ng5 $1 gxh6 23. Re7 {etc}) 22. Re7 $6 Qc5 $1 23. Rde1 Qd6+ 24. Kc1 Qf4+ {and White's hopes are dashed.}) 19... Nf6 20. Qh4 Bg4 $1 {is unclear. Smyslov's fatal mistake proved to be his first and last...}) 19. Qxf7 $1 $18 {Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'Mate attack' JvR: '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Queen sacrifice/weak back rank/smothered mate. --- That which was once just conjecture, now forms into absolute certainty. Black has no saving ideas. This loss must have been frustrating for Smyslov, since his opponent's randomly crazy ideas become all the more infuriating when they happen to work!' '!' Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'The mate threat is Qxf8' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(a thunderous blow) ChessBase: '+-!'' Lorin D'Costa: '!' Adam Hunt: '!!' The shortest way to victory! Der kürzeste Weg zum Sieg!} (19. Nxf7+ $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} Kg8 20. Nh6+ gxh6 21. Qxh6 Bf5 $19) ({Perhaps Smyslov only saw} 19. Rxd8 Qa1+ 20. Kd2 Rxd8+ 21. Ke3 Qxh1 {when Black is winning easily.}) 19... Qa1+ ({Garry Kasparov: 'Otherwise a smothered mate:'} 19... Rxf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' is not playable in view of geht nicht wegen} 20. Rxd8+ {Mate attack Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is mate in two moves.' Lorin D'Costa: 'leads to mate. '} Ng8 21. Nxf7#) (19... Z0 $140 20. Qxf8+ {Mate threat}) ({or} 19... Re8 20. Qg8+ $1 {and 21 Nf7 mate! Years later Tal tore Portisch to pieces in almost identical fashion (Biel Interzonal 1976).}) 20. Kd2 Rxf7 ({Otherwise:} 20... Qxd1+ 21. Rxd1 Rxf7 22. Nxf7+ Kg8 {(the king's constant weeping doesn't inspire confidence in his defenders)} 23. Nxd8 {leaves White up a piece. Garry Kasparov: 'was even more hopeless.' Lorin D'Costa: 'leaves White a piece ahead with an easy win.' Adam Hunt: 'is no better.'}) (20... Re8 $4 {(grief can make us do crazy things)} 21. Qg8+ $1 Nxg8 22. Nf7# {is mate.}) 21. Nxf7+ {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!'} (21. Rxa1 $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} gxh6 22. Ne5 Rg7 $19) 21... Kg8 22. Rxa1 Kxf7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White is up a clean exchange in the ending and Smyslov could have resigned here.'} 23. Ne5+ {Adam Hunt: 'The smoke has cleared, leaving White an exchange up and winning easily.'} Ke6 (23... Kg8 {doesn't change anything anymore} 24. Nxc6 Bb6 25. Bd4 $18) 24. Nxc6 Ne4+ 25. Ke3 Bb6+ 26. Bd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Garry Kasparov: '. This game was awarded a special prize as the most brilliant in the tournament. ChessBase: 'Quellen: Tal: "Im Feuer des Angriffs" (russ.), Moskau 1978 Suetin: "Schachstrategie der Weltmeister" Sportverlag 1983, S. 109'' Lorin D'Costa: 'The dust has cleared and White is left an exchange and a pawn up.' And Black resigned. This game shows that the side which has seized the open centre with his pieces can very quickly throw them over for attack on the opponent's king. Quellen:  Tal: "Im Feuer des Angriffs" (russ.), Moskau 1978 Suetin: "Schachstrategie der Weltmeister" Sportverlag 1983, S. 109 Und Schwarz gab auf. Diese Partie zeigt, dass die Seite, die das offene Zentrum mit ihren Figuren besetzt hat, diese schnell zum Angriff auf den gegnerischen König herüberwerfen kann.} ({More accurate than} 26. Kxe4 Bb7 {when Black can play on for a while. Tal deservedly won the brilliancy prize for this game.}) 26... Bb7 (26... Nc5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 27. Rhe1 $18 {1-0 (27) Tal,M-Smyslov,V Bled/Zagreb/Belgrade 1959 Tal Games [ChessBase]}) 27. Bxb6 axb6 28. Nd4+ Ke5 29. Rxa8 Bxa8 30. Ra1 $18 1-0

