{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 17 A very near miss. This is one of the four games that Fischer lost to Tal who, in winning this tournament, earned the right to meet and trounce Botvinnik for the world championship. In jest the whimsical Tal signed Fischer's name, in addition to his own, when asked for an autograph. "Why not?" he quipped; "I've beaten Bobby so often ... that gives me the right to sign for him!" A careful reading of Fischer's notes will reveal a clear echo of the strong emotions that engulfed him during this tense encounter. He misses a win in the opening and several draws along the way, demonstrating dramatically how a continuously advantageous position can abruptly be turned into defeat by seemingly insignificant miscalculations. ' Pachman: The 1959 Candidates Tournament was a four-leg marathon over 28 rounds, played alternately in three Yugoslav towns--Veldes, Agram, and Belgrade. After the event FIDE discussed the possibility of changing the system [because many of the participants found that it was too exhausting]. ///  Myers: Tal won the tournament by the fantastic score of 20/28, a full 1½ points ahead of Keres, despite the fact that Keres was able to beat Tal 3 out of 4 in their individual encounters. Tal made up for this by beating Fischer in all four of their games, including this 27th round victory that put Tal beyond reach.} 1. e4 {Myers Polugaevsky Fischer Purdy Mednis Pachman Khalifman} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 {Pachman: Tal really only needed two draws from the last two rounds, for it was unlikely that Keres would win against both Gligoric and Olafsson. Many players in Tal's position would have proceeded cautiously and aimed at attaining a safe position in the opening. For two reasons Tal adopted different tactics. First a quiet game does not suit his style, and second he apparently wanted to be instrumental in deciding the outcome of the tournament. He therefore chose one of the sharpest systems of the Sicilian defense. His daring was crowned with success, but only after some nerve-racking moments.} 6. Bc4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer's favourite move, which brought him many wins. At that time the theory of this sharp variation was still in the 'stone age', and the players began improvising from literally the 8th-9th move (which suited Tal perfectly well). The real development of the variation occurred only in the early 1990s. I myself had played 6 Bc4 several times, and then Short played it against me in the 1993 world championship match, which marked a colossal leap forward for the variation! Black had to solve numerous problems...'} e6 {Garry Kasparov: 'To avoid'} 7. Bb3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'I had no better luck against Blackstone, in an exhibition game at Davis, California, 1964, with'} 7. O-O Be7 8. Bb3 Qc7 (8... O-O 9. f4 b5 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Nfd7 12. Qh5 {(Anand-Kasparov Moscow rapidplay 1996)} ({or} 12. Be3 {(Short-Kasparov, Novgorod 1997), although in both cases Black has sufficient counter-chances.})) 9. f4 b5 10. f5 b4 11. fxe6 $5 (11. Nce2 e5 12. Nf3 Bb7 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'is bad for White)'}) 11... bxc3 12. exf7+ Kf8 13. Bg5 Ng4 $1 $19 {Fischer,R-Blackstone,J Davis sim 1964 Bobby Fischer: '! and Black should win. Fischer,R-Blackstone,J Davis sim 1964'}) 7... b5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Certainly the most critical reply.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'This reaction must be prompt! In our first lap game here Tal played the weaker' Garry Kasparov: ''In our first lap game here Tal played the weaker'} 7... Be7 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'In fact 7...Be7 is dangerous, not on account of 8 f4, but in view of'} 8. f4 (8. g4 $1 {– a kind of modification of the Keres Attack.}) 8... O-O {Bobby Fischer: '(for 8 ... b5 see the note to Black's 8th move)'} 9. Qf3 Qc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now'} 10. f5 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'instead of'} 10. O-O $2 b5 11. f5 b4 $1 12. Na4 e5 13. Ne2 Bb7 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'and Black stands better)' Garry Kasparov: 'and Black stands better'}) 10... e5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not' Garry Kasparov: 'But it is better to play'} 10... Nc6 11. Be3 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a bind'} Nxd4 12. Bxd4 b5 {, or even earlier, 8...Nc6.}) 11. Nde2 b5 12. a3 Bb7 13. g4 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a strong attack.' Garry Kasparov: 'with a strong attack.' (Fischer)'} (13. Z0)) ({In my match with Short I tried} 7... Nbd7 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} 8. f4 Nc5 {;}) ({and then} 7... Nc6 8. Be3 Be7 9. f4 O-O 10. O-O (10. Qf3 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 $1) 10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 12. e5 $1 (12. a3 Bb7 {is equal, Fischer-Spassky, Reykjavik 4th matchgame 1972}) 12... dxe5 13. fxe5 Nd7 14. Ne4 Bb7 15. Nd6 Bxd6 16. exd6 Qg5 {with a struggle for equality, but in the end I switched to 7...b5 .}) 8. f4 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Premature aggression.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'R.Byme-Evans, US Championship 1967 went'} 8. Qf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'but White got nothing after'} Bb7 9. Bg5 b4 10. Na4 Nbd7 11. O-O Qa5 12. Bxf6 Nxf6 13. Rfe1 Be7 $10 {Byrne,R-Evans,L ch-USA 1967 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Byrne, R-Evans,L ch-USA 1967'}) ({Against Olafsson, at Buenos Aires 1960, I continued Garry Kasparov: 'As it later transpired,'} 8. O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'is more accurate, for example:'} Be7 ({if} 8... b4 9. Na4 Nxe4 (9... Bd7 10. Re1 Be7 11. a3 (11. f4) 11... a5 12. axb4 axb4 13. Bd2 Na6 14. c3 $146 (14. Qe2) 14... bxc3 15. Bxc3 (15. Nxc3 $5 $11) 15... O-O 16. Qe2 Qb8 $1 17. Bc4 Nb4 $1 {#5E#CDa4} 18. Bb5 (18. b3 d5 $1 $36) 18... Bxb5 19. Nxb5 Nc2 $1 20. Qxc2 (20. e5 Nxa1 $5 (20... dxe5 21. Bxe5 Qb7 22. Qxc2 (22. Bxf6 $2 Nxe1 $1 $19) 22... Qxb5 $11) 21. exf6 Rxa4 22. fxe7 Re8 $17) 20... Qxb5 21. Bd2 Rab8 22. Bc1 Rfc8 23. Nc3 Qb7 $15 {Ardeleanu,A (2420)-Rodriguez,A (2495) Ubeda 71/247 1998 [Rodriguez,Am]}) 10. Re1 Nf6 11. Bg5 $40 {with attack}) ({if} 8... Bb7 {, then} 9. Re1 Nbd7 10. Bg5 {Fischer-Rubinetti, Palma de Mallorca 1970}) 9. Qf3 $5 Qc7 ({NOT} 9... Bb7 $2 10. Bxe6 $1) ({or} 9... Qb6 $5 10. Be3 Qb7 11. Qg3 b4 12. Na4 Nbd7 13. f3 O-O {Short-Kasparov, London rapidplay 1993}) 10. Qg3 b4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (10... O-O 11. Bh6 Ne8 12. Rad1 Bd7 {and then} 13. f4 {(A.Sokolov)} (13. a3 {(Short)}) ({or} 13. Nf3 $5 {(which I had already tried out in Kasparov-Gelfand, Linares 1993)} a5 $1 {with double-edged play  (Short-Kasparov, London 18th matchgame 1993).})) 11. Nce2 {Garry Kasparov: 'is inferior, Fischer-Olafsson, Buenos Aires 1960'} g6 12. a3 $2 (12. Bh6 $1 {is very strong)}) 12... Nxe4 13. Qe3 Nf6 14. axb4 O-O $13 {with a double-edged position. Fischer,R-Olafsson, F Buenos Aires 1960 Fischer,R-Olafsson,F Buenos Aires 1960}) (8. Bg5 {- 71/(247)}) 8... b4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer attached a '!' to this move, but it should have been '?!';'} ({it was demonstrated long ago that after} 8... Bb7 $1 {White has not even a hint of an advantage:} 9. f5 ({or} 9. O-O Be7 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Bc5 $1) 9... e5 10. Nde2 Nbd7 11. Bg5 Be7 {.}) 9. Na4 Nxe4 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'is also playable'} 9... Bb7) ({or} 9... Be7 {is again more solid, although today experts on this variation prefer altogether not to hurry with ...b5-b4. On the morning of the day this game was played, Tal analysed this variation with Averbakh, Koblents and Petrosian, who had called in. 9...Nxe4 appealed to Tigran: 'Such a nice little central pawn... hmmm, hmmm... I think I would take it!' But Mikhail had his doubts, and it was only after sitting down at the board and glancing at Fischer, that he mentally waved his hands: 'Ah, what the devil - I'll take it!' Such preparation is not so much an indication of Tal's flippancy, as of the level of opening theory at that time...}) 10. O-O g6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'An audacious challenge to the opponent!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 10... Bb7 $142 {Bobby Fischer: '>=' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 11. f5 e5 12. Qh5 (12. Ne6 $1 fxe6 13. Qh5+ $40 {Vukovic} Kd7 ({or} 13... g6 14. fxg6 Kd7 15. Be3) 14. fxe6+ Kc8 {(6)} 15. Be3 {with an attack;}) 12... Qe7 13. f6 Nxf6 14. Qh4 exd4 15. Bg5 Nbd7 16. Rae1 Ne5 17. Nb6 h6 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Ba4+ Kd8 20. Qxd4 Rg8 21. Nd