Qxd5 Qxa7+ 18. Kf1 Rg4 19. Re1 Qe7 20. Qa8 {– G.K.}) 15. Qh5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Also in White's favour is'} Rg6 (15... Nxa4 $2 16. Bxa7 exd5 $4 17. Rae1+ $18) (15... Rc7 $5 16. Qxh7 Rg4 (16... Rg6 17. Nxc5 dxc5 18. Bc4 exf5 19. Bxf7+ $1 Rxf7 20. Qxg6) 17. Qh5 Rg6 (17... exf5 $2 18. Bxc5 dxc5 19. Rae1+) (17... Rg8 18. Nb6 exd5 19. Bxc5 Be6 20. Rae1 {etc}) 18. Nh4 Rg8 19. Nxc5 dxc5 20. Bc4 {, although this is hardly much stronger than the continuation in the game.}) (15... exd5 16. Bxc5 dxc5 17. Rae1+) 16. Rae1 $1 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'and White's every piece is bearing down on Black's King (KEVITZ).' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'and White's every piece is bearing down on Black's king.''} (16. Z0) 16... Rc7 17. Nxc5 dxc5 18. Bb3 exf5 19. Bf4+ $16) 14... exf5 15. Bxf5 {Mednis: This is clearly off the mark. White exchanges off a second piece, this time against Black's undeveloped c8. White's light-square Bishop has been moved five times to do this, and it just can't be right. In addition, White's g-pawn will experience some weakness with Black's rook pointed against it.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Probably it's better to avoid exchanges with'} 15. Bd5 $142 $5 $16 {Fischer Mednis Polugaevsky Garry Kasparov: '!?'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 15. Bf3 $142 $5 $16 {Fischer}) (15. Be3 $143 $2 Re7 16. Bb6 Qd7 17. Bxf5 Qxa4 18. Bxc8 Qc6 $1 $19 {Khalifman}) 15... Re7 $1 16. Bxc8 (16. c4 $5) 16... Qxc8 17. Bf4 $2 {Polugaevsky: This is an oversight. Fischer fails to see his opponent's shrewd plan and falls into a clever trap. Garry Kasparov: 'This essentially blunders a piece.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'The right move is simply' Garry Kasparov: 'Of course, White should have played'} 17. c3 $142 $1 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'and if'} Qc6 $2 (17... Qg4 18. g3 Qxd1 19. Rxd1 bxc3 20. Nxc3 $14 {Myers}) ({or} 17... Qb7) {Garry Kasparov: 'then'} 18. Rf2 $16 {Garry Kasparov: ', after which the offside knight at a4 gradually comes back into play and Black has simply a 'threadbare' position with a guaranteed inferior endgame.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'not' Garry Kasparov: 'But not'} 17. Qxd6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'because of'} Rxg2+ 18. Kxg2 Re2+ 19. Kf3 Bxd6 20. Kxe2 Qxc2+ $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins. if 17 ...Qc6 18 Rf2.'}) (17. Rf2 $5 $14 {[%cal Gc2c3,Yc1f4] Myers}) 17... Qc6 $1 18. Qf3 $6 (18. Qd2 $142 $5 {Myers}) 18... Qxa4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ''Such a surprise that I didn't dare believe my eyes! Tal finds an inspired defence!' (Fischer) A staggering revelation: the capture of the piece had not even been considered by White – a vivid illustration of how non-concretely they thought in those times. Naturally, Tal took the knight: concrete, calculating play was his main trump!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Such a surprise that I didn't dare believe my eyes! 1 had expected' Garry Kasparov: 'It is curious how he himself explained his decision: 'I was faced with a choice: should I go into a slightly inferior endgame'} 18... Qxf3 $2 19. Rxf3 Re2 20. Rf2 Rxf2 21. Kxf2 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'l and White has a slight edge after a3 because of Black's disconnected Pawns.' Garry Kasparov: 'or, after accepting the piece sacrifice, subject myself to a very strong attack? I could not see a forced mate, it is true, but, perhaps, only because I wasn't looking for one. If I had been playing White, I would have considered the attack to be decisive. Nevertheless, I chose the second path...''}) (18... Rg6 $2 19. c4 bxc3 20. Nxc3 Qxf3 21. Rxf3 $14) 19. Bxd6 Qc6 $1 20. Bxb8 $8 (20. Bxe7 $2 Qxf3 21. Rxf3 Bxe7 $15 {Khalifman}) 20... Qb6+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'White remains a clear Pawn ahead after 20 ..' Garry Kasparov: 'But not'} 20... Qxf3 $2 21. Rxf3 Bg7 (21... Re2 22. Bg3 (22. g3 $2 Rg6 $1 $132) 22... Bc5+ 23. Bf2 Rxc2 24. Bxc5 $14 {Khalifman}) 22. c3 $16 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'with an extra pawn.'}) 21. Kh1 Qxb8 {Mednis: Unbalanced, wild, crazy attacking positions are very difficult to judge correctly during the progress of the game. Wisdom and clarity often appear only after exhaustive post-mortems. Fischer, incorrectly, thought that White had a winning attack here; and Tal, incorrectly, thought that Black was lost. Why then did not Tal exchange Queens on move 18? Well, reason number 1 is that Tal is a very brave person, and reason number 2 is that he felt that Fischer could very well misplay the attack. Fischer: The crowd was shouting and whistling with each move. Later I was informed that many sports fans were in the audience. Maybe some soccer match had been canceled. As a consequence, chess was the main attraction that day in Belgrade. Garry Kasparov: ''The crowd was shouting and whistling with each move. Later I was informed that many sports fans were in the audience. Maybe some soccer match had been cancelled. As a consequence chess was the main attraction that day in Belgrade.' (Fischer) I should point out that the hall accommodated about 2000 spectators... 'A famous position: it was here that a widely-known psychological duel took place between us,' related Tal. 'Fischer first wrote down the move 22 Rae1!, without doubt the strongest, and wrote it not in his usual English notation, but in European, almost Russian! And not very deftly he pushed the score-sheet towards me. "He's testing me out," I thought: would I frown or display my nervousness. I could force myself to smile, but Bobby was no longer a child, he would understand. What then was I to do? I very much wanted him to change his decision. And so I calmly stood up and began walking alongside the board. I shared a joke with someone, glanced for appearance's sake at the demonstration board and with a contended expression returned to my position. I am sure that all this time Fischer was looking at me, and not calculating variations. He cast one more glance at me (I endured it), crossed out the move on his score-sheet and gave the queen check!''} 22. Qc6+ {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: ''When after the game I asked Fischer why he didn't play 22 Rae1, he replied: "Well, you laughed when I wrote it down!" (Tal)'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Many annotators believed that' Garry Kasparov: 'Well, at that time it was customary to turn any annotated game into a poem, whereas now the impassive machine instantaneously parries'} 22. Rae1 $142 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'was the winning move. Tal himself confessed he thought Black was lost after that. But 22 .' Purdy Garry Kasparov: 'with'} Kd8 $1 {Fischer: I've studied this position for ages, it seems, and this is the best I can find.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'holds all lines' Garry Kasparov: 'but not'} 22... Rg6 $2 23. Qxf7+ Kd7 24. Rd1+ $1 Rd6 (24... Kc6 25. Qxf8 Qxf8 26. Rxf8 $18) 25. Rxd6+ Kxd6 26. Rf6+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins. I've studied this position for ages, it seems, and the best I can find is' Garry Kasparov: '!'} Ke5 27. Qxf8 Qxf8 28. Rxf8 Rd7 $16 {Myers}) (22... Kd7 $6 23. Rd1+ (23. Qd5+ $5 Qd6 24. Qb7+ Ke8 25. Qc8+ Qd8 26. Qxa6 $14 {Myers})) 23. Rd1+ $2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'also after'} 23. Rxe7 $5 Bxe7 24. Qxf7 (24. Qd5+ {White can only dream of a draw}) 24... Re8 (24... Rg6 25. Rd1+ Bd6 26. Qxh7 $14) 25. Rd1+ Bd6 26. Qxh7 $11 {Khalifman}) 23... Kc7 $1 {Fischer} (23... Kc8 $2 24. Qc6+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins'}) 24. Qf4+ (24. Rd4 Qb7 $1) 24... Kb7 25. Rd6 {Garry Kasparov: 'stronger than Fischer's variation'} Qc7 (25... Qe8 $1 $17 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 26. Qf3+ (26. Qxb4+ Qb5 27. Qxb5+ axb5 {, and White faces a struggle for a draw.}) 26... Re4 27. Rf6 Kb8 28. a3 Rg6 29. Rxf7 Bd6 30. Qf2 Qc6 31. axb4 Rh6 $17 {Myers}) (25... Qc8 $2 26. Qxb4+ Ka8 27. Qa5 $1 $18 {Haygarth}) 26. Qxb4+ Kc8 27. Rxa6 Qb7 $1 28. Qxb7+ Kxb7 29. Raf6 Rg7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw'}) 22... Rd7 23. Rae1+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'Black holds after' Garry Kasparov: 'White also misses the mark with'} 23. Rad1 $2 Bd6 (23... Qb7 $1 24. Rfe1+ Be7 25. Rxd7 Qxd7 26. Qa8+ Qd8 27. Qxa6 Kf8 28. Qb7 Rg6 $1 $19 {Myers}) (23... Qc7 24. Rde1+ Be7 25. Qf6 Kd8 $15) 24. Rxf7 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 24. Rf6 $2 Rg6 $19 25. Rdxd6 $2 Qxd6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) (24. Rfe1+ $1 Kd8 25. Qxa6 Rg5 $15) 24... Qc7 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.' Garry Kasparov: '!'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'and on'} 23. Rxf7 $4 Qd6 $1 $19 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 23... Be7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Finally Tal "develops" his Bishop. Not' Garry Kasparov: 'Of course, not'} 23... Kd8 $4 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 24. Rxf7 $1 Be7 25. Rfxe7 Rxe7 26. Rd1+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 24. Rxf7 $2 (24. Qf6 $142 $1 $11 {[%csl Ra6,Rf7] Myers}) 24... Kxf7 25. Qe6+ Kf8 $1 {This move caught Fischer by surprise.} (25... Kg7 $2 26. Qxd7 $18 {Fischer}) 26. Qxd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 26. Rf1+ {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is bad due to'} Kg7 27. Rf7+ Kh8 28. Qxd7 Rd8 29. Qg4 $2 (29. Rxh7+ Kxh7 30. Qxe7+ Kg6 31. Qe6+ Kg5 32. h4+ Kxh4 $19 {Khalifman}) (29. Qxe7 $4 Rd1+ $19) 29... Qe5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Garry Kasparov: ', when Black wins.'}) 26... Qd6 27. Qb7 Rg6 28. c3 $1 {Mednis: White's attack is gone, and he is left with a material disadvantage of only two pawns for Black's Bishop. Thus the dream of a win is gone. Well, how about a draw? The chances for this are excellent, mainly becasue of the inadvertent situation on the kingside where Black has the wrong Bishop for his h-pawn. Thus an endgame with the queenside pawns off the board would be drawn. Because of the exposed nature of Black's King, it is also very questionable if Black can mount a decisive attack in the middle game. With his 28th move White starts on the right plan for exchanging queenside pawns.} a5 $6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'on' Garry Kasparov: 'Or'} 28... bxc3 29. Qc8+ Bd8 (29... Kg7 30. bxc3 Bd8 $19 {Myers}) 30. Qxc3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw.'}) 29. Qc8+ {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On the wrong track. Right is' Garry Kasparov: ''Right is'} 29. cxb4 $1 Qxb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 29... axb4 30. a3 $1 bxa3 31. bxa3 Qxa3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'draws'}) 30. Qf3+ Kg7 31. Qe2 $146 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws, since Black can't possibly build up a winning K-side attack and his own King is too exposed.' Garry Kasparov: 'drawing, since Black can't possibly build up a winning kingside attack and his own king is too exposed.' (Fischer)'} Bd6 $19) (29. Qa8+ $5) 29... Kg7 30. Qc4 Bd8 31. cxb4 axb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'on'} 31... Qxb4 32. Qe2 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'White should draw with best play.' Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw.'} Bc7 $17) 32. g3 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'Overlooking Black's 34th move.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Creating losing chances. I don't see how Black can make any progress after'} 32. Qe4 $142 Bc7 (32... Qd2 $1 33. Rb1 Bf6 34. a4 Bxb2 35. Rf1 h5 36. Qf3 Rf6 37. Qg3+ Kh6 $19 {Myers}) 33. Qe7+ Kg8 34. Qe8+ Qf8 35. Qe4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'etc' Garry Kasparov: 'was more tenacious.'} Bd6 $19) (32. a3 Qd2 $1 33. Qe4 b3 34. a4 Bf6 $19 {Myers}) (32. Qe2 $5) 32... Qc6+ 33. Re4 Qxc4 34. Rxc4 Rb6 $1 {Fischer: I overlooked this.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I overlooked this. Now Black has winning chances. I had planned on a draw after' Garry Kasparov: 'But not'} 34... Be7 $2 35. a3 {Bobby Fischer: 'dissolving Black's b-Pawn' Garry Kasparov: '!'} b3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is answered by'} (35... bxa3 36. Rc7 Kf7 37. bxa3 Ra6 $19 {Myers}) 36. Rc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'followed by' Garry Kasparov: 'and 37 Rb7.'} Re6 37. Rb7) 35. Kg2 Kf6 (35... b3 $5) 36. Kf3 Ke5 37. Ke3 (37. a3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is met, as always, by ..'} b3 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'Once White can eliminate Black's b-Pawn it's a theoretical draw.' Garry Kasparov: '!'} (37... bxa3 $2 38. bxa3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '='})) 37... Bg5+ 38. Ke2 Kd5 39. Kd3 Bf6 40. Rc2 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Too passive. I wanted to avoid ilnmobilizing my Q-side Pawns with' Garry Kasparov: 'Drawing chances would have been retained by'} 40. b3 $142 {Bobby Fischer: 'but it's the best hope now. On' Garry Kasparov: ', and if'} Be7 {Garry Kasparov: ', then'} (40... Be5 41. Ke3 Rb8 $19 {Myers}) 41. Rd4+ $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'preserves drawing chances.'}) 40... Be5 (40... b3 $1 41. axb3 Rxb3+ 42. Ke2 Ke4 $19 {Myers}) 41. Re2 Rf6 42. Rc2 Rf3+ 43. Ke2 Rf7 44. Kd3 Bd4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ''To watch all this tightrope-walking was too much for me, and I appeared in the hall only after three hours' play,' recalls grandmaster Averbakh, one of the Riga player's seconds. "Tal's position was now absolutely won, but Koblents and Mikenas, Keres's second, were reclining in their seats, clutching their hearts and sucking tranquillisers..." ''} 45. a3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'on' Garry Kasparov: 'Or'} 45. b3 Rf3+ 46. Ke2 Rf2+ 47. Kd3 Rxc2 48. Kxc2 Ke4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Garry Kasparov: 'and wins.'}) 45... b3 46. Rc8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Equally hopeless is' Garry Kasparov: 'nor'} 46. Re2 Rf3+ 47. Kd2 Bxb2 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc' Garry Kasparov: 'would have helped.'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'Neither'} 46. Rd2 Rf3+ 47. Ke2 Rf2+ $19 {Fischer}) 46... Bxb2 (46... Rf3+ 47. Ke2 Rf2+ 48. Kd3 Be5 49. Rd8+ Ke6 50. Re8+ Kd6 51. Rd8+ Ke7 52. Rd5 Ke6 53. Rb5 Rxb2 $19 {Myers}) 47. Rd8+ Kc6 48. Rb8 Rf3+ 49. Kc4 Rc3+ 50. Kb4 Kc7 (50... Ba1 51. a4 b2 $1 {0-1 (51) Fischer,R-Tal,M Bled/Zagreb/Belgrade 1959 My 60 Memorable Games [Bobby Fischer]} ({Garry Kasparov: 'In view of'} 51... b2 $1 52. Kxc3 b1=Q+ $1 {[%emt 0:00:31] If 53 Kxc3 b1=Q+! The discovered-check theme is strangely reminiscent of the finale of game Garry Kasparov: '.'})) 51. Rb5 Ba1 52. a4 b2 $1 (52... b2 $1 53. Kxc3 b1=Q+ $1) 0-1

