1. c4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Ripperger''} Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. d4 Bb4 4. a3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: This move just doesn't appear logical, does it? White expends a precious tempo, only to force Black into inflicting damage upon the white structure. ANSWER: In a sense this line is the epitome of dynamism. White willingly hands over a tempo and allows damage, but he gets both bishop pair and strengthened centre in return. Today, satisfactory defensive plans have been worked out for Black, but at the time it was mysterious territory to most of Botvinnik's opponents. In this game, Botvinnik displayed far deeper understanding of the opening, from which Tal never managed to recover equilibrium. '} Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 b6 ({Today} 5... c5) ({and} 5... O-O {are more popular.}) 6. f3 Ba6 ({Instead: a)} 6... d5 {enters similar waters to Botvinnik-Capablanca from Chapter One, and indeed} 7. cxd5 exd5 8. e3 O-O 9. Bd3 c5 10. Ne2 Ba6 11. O-O {transposes to the note with 11 f3 in that game.}) ({b)} 6... Nc6 {(going directly after White's sore spot, the c4-pawn)} 7. e4) 7. e4 d5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?!''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The last chance for'  Spielbar ist auch} 7... Nc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which seems like a superior way for Black to play this position.' is also good.} 8. e5 (8. Bg5 Na5 9. e5 h6 10. Bh4 g5 11. Bf2 Nh5 12. h4 f5 13. exf6 Qxf6 14. c5 Bxf1 15. Kxf1 g4 16. Qd3 O-O 17. Re1 Nf4 18. Qc2 Nc4 19. g3 Qf5 20. Rc1 Qd3+ 21. Qxd3 Nxd3 22. Rd1 Ndb2 23. Ra1 gxf3 24. Nh3 bxc5 25. Kg1 Nd3 26. Kh2 Rab8 27. Ra2 Rb3 28. dxc5 e5 29. g4 e4 30. g5 e3 31. gxh6 exf2 32. Nxf2 Nxf2 33. Rxf2 Kh7 34. Rd1 Rf7 35. c6 d6 36. Rd3 Rb2 37. Kg3 Rxf2 38. Kxf2 Ne5 39. Rd4 Nxc6 40. Ra4 Kxh6 {0-1 Simagin,V-Petrosian,T/Moscow URS 1950/}) 8... Ng8 9. Nh3 Na5 10. Qa4 Ne7 {reaching an unbalanced position with mutual chances, A.Kotov-P.Keres, Budapest Candidates 1950.} 11. Bd3 Kf8 12. O-O d5 13. exd6 cxd6 14. Re1 Rc8 15. Nf4 Bxc4 16. Bxc4 Rxc4 17. Qb5 Qe8 18. a4 Qxb5 19. axb5 Rc7 20. Ra2 Ke8 21. Nh5 Rg8 22. Ba3 Kd7 23. Bb4 Nd5 24. Bxa5 bxa5 25. Rxa5 Nxc3 26. Kf2 Rb8 27. Nxg7 Rxb5 28. Rxb5 Nxb5 29. Ke3 Rc3+ 30. Ke4 Rc4 {0-1 Sadorra,J-Izoria,Z/Dallas USA 2009/}) 8. cxd5 Bxf1 9. Kxf1 exd5 10. Bg5 ({Retaining central tension feels stronger intuitively than} 10. e5 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?'} Nfd7 (10... Ng8 11. Ne2 Qd7 12. Kf2 Nc6 13. Re1 O-O-O 14. Qd3 Kb7 15. Nf4 Ka8 16. e6 fxe6 17. Nxe6 Rc8 18. Qb5 g6 19. Rb1 Nb8 20. Bf4 Qxb5 21. Rxb5 c6 22. Rb2 h6 23. Nc7+ Kb7 24. Ne8 Ka8 25. Rbe2 g5 26. Bg3 Rd8 27. Re6 Rc8 28. Nd6 Rf8 29. Ne8 Rf7 30. Bxb8 Kxb8 31. Nd6 Rf6 32. Re8+ Kc7 33. Nc8 Rg6 34. Ne7 Rg7 35. Nf5 Rf7 36. R1e5 b5 37. Ra8 Kb6 38. Nd6 Rf6 39. Nc8+ Kb7 40. Rxa7+ Kb8 41. Re8 Ne7 42. Rexe7 {1-0 Lilienthal,A-Steiner,L/Budapest HUN 1933/}) 11. Nh3 O-O {, after which Black is ready for central counters with ...c7-c5, R.Meulders-R.Douven, Hilversum 1997.}) 10... h6 ({Botvinnik frowned upon this move; but I don't much care for Black's game after} 10... dxe4 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?'} 11. Qe2 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?!'} (11. fxe4 $5) 11... Nbd7 12. fxe4 h6 13. Bh4 {, F.Gonda-R.Hirr, correspondence 1990.} g5 14. Bg3 O-O 15. h4 Nh7 16. e5 Re8 17. Qh5 Re6 18. Nf3 Ndf6 19. exf6 Nxf6 20. Qxh6 Ne4 21. Qxg5+ Nxg5 22. hxg5 Qd5 23. Be5 Qc4+ 24. Kf2 Rxe5 25. dxe5 Qxc3 26. Rac1 Qa5 27. Rxc7 b5 28. Rc6 Kg7 29. Rch6 Rg8 30. Rh7+ Kf8 31. Rc1 Rg6 32. Rh8+ Ke7 33. Rhc8 Kd7 34. Rb8 Rb6 35. Rf8 Ke7 36. Rcc8 Ra6 37. Rce8+ Kd7 38. Rb8 Ke6 {½-½ Gonda,F-Hirr,R/corr DDR 1990/}) (10... Nbd7 $2 11. e5 $1 Ng4 12. Qd2 f6 {Euwe} 13. Bh4 g5 14. Bg3 Nh6 15. Re1 fxe5 16. dxe5 Qe7 17. Qxd5 $18 {Myers}) 11. Qa4+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Botvinnik employs a crafty psychological ploy, correctly guessing that Tal's love of complications and distrust of simplification would be his own worst enemy in the position. Chess isn't always about playing the intrinsically best move. Sometimes there is a best move for a given opponent, which supersedes the overall best move.'} ({QUESTION: Why didn't White win a pawn with} 11. Bxf6 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?'} Qxf6 12. exd5 {- ? ANSWER: That is probably the line Tal hoped to enter. After} O-O {Black's lead in development somewhat compensates for the missing pawn. Botvinnik writes: "True to my match tactics, I declined the pawn sacrifice, so as not to concede the initiative to my opponent."} 13. Ne2 Qg5 14. c4 Re8 15. Nc3 Re3 16. Ne4 $14 {Myers}) ({White can also speculate with} 11. Bh4 dxe4 12. Qc2 $5 {(entering a strange Nimzo-Blackmar-Diemer hybrid)} exf3 ({perhaps declining is better:} 12... O-O 13. fxe4 Ng4 $1 14. Bxd8 Ne3+ 15. Ke2 Nxc2 16. Rc1 Nxd4+ 17. cxd4 Rxd8 18. Nf3 c5 $1 19. dxc5 Na6 20. cxb6 axb6 {with at least equal chances for Black, who has fewer weaknesses to nurse}) 13. Re1+ Kf8 14. Nxf3 Nbd7 15. Kf2 {, when White obtained full compensation for his pawn, G.Khodos-M.Mukhitdinov, Novosibirsk 1962.} c5 16. Rhf1 cxd4 17. cxd4 Rc8 18. Qb3 g5 19. Bg3 Kg7 20. Kg1 Re8 21. Ne5 Re7 22. Qf3 Qe8 23. h4 Ng8 24. hxg5 hxg5 25. Qh5 f6 26. Qxe8 Rexe8 27. Nxd7 Red8 28. Nxf6 Nxf6 29. Re7+ Kg6 30. Re6 {1-0 Khodos,G-Mukhitdinov,M/Novosibirsk URS 1962/}) 11... c6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal is understandably nervous about challenging Botvinnik in the ending. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?''} ({Nevertheless, he should remain resolute and play the best move:} 11... Qd7 $1 12. Qxd7+ Nbxd7 13. Bxf6 (13. Bh4 $2 dxe4 14. Re1 c5 $1 15. fxe4 cxd4 16. cxd4 Rc8 $36 {Myers}) 13... Nxf6 14. Re1 $1 {(stronger than pushing forward, according to Botvinnik)} (14. e5 {Botvinnik} Nd7 (14... Ng8 $5) (14... Nh5 15. g3 g6 16. f4 O-O 17. Nf3 c5 18. dxc5 bxc5 19. Rb1 g5 20. fxg5 hxg5 21. Kg2 f6 22. Rhe1 g4 23. Nh4 Rab8 24. Ng6 Rfe8 25. Rxb8 Rxb8 26. Ne7+ Kf7 27. Nxd5 Re8 28. h3 gxh3+ 29. Kxh3 Rxe5 30. Rxe5 fxe5 31. Kg4 Ng7 32. Kg5 Ke6 33. c4 e4 34. Kf4 Nh5+ {½-½ Herzel,A-Rudnick,W/corr DDR 1982/}) 15. f4 b5 16. Ke2 Nb6 17. Kd3 c5 18. Ne2 Rc8 19. f5 Ke7 20. Nf4 Rc6 21. Rhb1 cxd4 22. cxd4 Rhc8 23. Rxb5 Rc3+ 24. Ke2 Rc2+ 25. Ke1 g5 26. fxg6 fxg6 27. Nxg6+ Ke6 28. Rab1 Rg8 29. Rc5 Rxc5 30. Nf4+ Kf5 31. dxc5 Nc4 32. g3 Kxe5 33. Rb7 a5 34. Re7+ Kd4 35. c6 Nxa3 36. c7 Nb5 37. Nxd5 Rc8 38. Rd7 Kc5 39. g4 a4 40. Rd8 Nd6 41. Nc3 Rxc7 42. Nxa4+ Kd5 43. Kf2 Ke5 44. Kg3 h5 45. gxh5 {½-½ Hollis,A-Hecht,H/corr FIN 1977/}) 14... Kd7 15. Nh3 {with a microbe of an edge for White. I have a feeling Tal wouldn't have lost this position.}) (11... Kf8 $2 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. exd5 g6 14. Ne2 Kg7 15. h4 $1 $36 {Myers}) (11... Nbd7 $2 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. exd5 O-O-O 14. Qxa7 Rhe8 15. Ne2 $16 {Myers}) 12. Bh4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Botvinnik, wisely distrustful, isn't fooled a bit by his opponent's falsely benign manner.'} ({Sac'ing a pawn is much stronger than grabbing one with} 12. Bxf6 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?'} Qxf6 13. exd5 O-O 14. dxc6 Nxc6 {, when Black's lead in development more than compensates. Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Euwe'} 15. Ne2 Rae8 16. Ng3 $11 {[%cal Yf1f2] Myers}) 12... dxe4 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Somewhat forced, since e4-e5 is in the air. Botvinnik gives this move a "?!" mark and writes: "A highly dubious decision. The opening of lines in the centre merely assists the development of White's initiative." But Kasparov very sensibly responds with: "It is easy to criticize – but what should Black play?" I agree. The big question: "Now what?" finds no easy answer. Ripperger: '?!'' [Botvinnik judged this opening line to be doubtful. But what else should Black move here?] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[Botvinnik judged this opening line to be doubtful. But what else should Black move here?]'' [Botvinnik verurteilte diese Linienöffnung als zweifelhaft. Aber was sollte Schwarz hier ziehen?] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[Botvinnik verurteilte diese Linienöffnung als zweifelhaft. Aber was sollte Schwarz hier ziehen?]''} 13. Re1 g5 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '?' Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?!'' [This is the actual error. The black king position has now been permanently weakened.] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[This is the actual error. The black king position has now been permanently weakened.]'' [Dies ist der eigentliche Fehler. Die schwarze Königsstellung ist nun dauerhaft geschwächt.] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[Dies ist der eigentliche Fehler. Die schwarze Königsstellung ist nun dauerhaft geschwächt.]''} (13... O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?''} 14. fxe4 Qc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!''} 15. Nf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Ripperger''} (15. Bxf6 Qf4+ $132 {Euwe} 16. Nf3 Qxf6 17. Kf2 Nd7 18. Rhf1 b5 19. Qb3 a5 20. Kg1 $11 {Myers}) 15... Nbd7 {White has a strong pawn centre, but the king's position is weak. Weiß hat zwar ein sehr starkes Bauernzentrum, dafür aber eine schlechte Königsstellung.}) 14. Bf2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Covering d4 and reinforcing against a future ...c6-c5 break. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '''} ({Botvinnik's move is more accurate than} 14. Bg3 {, which allows Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?!'} Qd5 $1 {. Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Euwe'} 15. Be5 b5 16. c4 Qxe5 17. dxe5 bxa4 18. exf6 Nd7 $15 {Myers}) 14... Qe7 ({Now} 14... Qd5 $6 {is met by} 15. fxe4 (15. c4 Qe6 16. fxe4 (16. Qc2 {Botvinnik} O-O 17. fxe4 Nh5 18. Nf3 Nd7 19. d5 cxd5 20. cxd5 Qg6 21. Nd4 Rac8 $132 {Myers}) (16. Ne2 $5)) 15... Nxe4 16. c4 {(only possible because of White's bishop on f2)} Qe6 17. Qc2 f5 18. g4 $1 {and Black finds himself in danger of being undermined on e4.} Kd8 $1 19. gxf5 Qxc4+ 20. Qxc4 Nd2+ {Botvinnik} 21. Kg2 Nxc4 22. Nf3 $11 {Myers}) (14... Nbd7 $5) 15. Ne2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The knight heads for g3, where it eyes both the e4- and f5-squares. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is much stronger than'} 15. fxe4 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '?' Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?'' would be an instructive error. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: 'would be an instructive error.'' wäre ein lehrreicher Fehler Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: 'wäre ein lehrreicher Fehler''} Nxe4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(this time Black gets away with the crime)'} 16. Qc2 f5 17. g4 Nd7 18. gxf5 Ndf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and Black looks okay, despite the pin on the e4-knight. Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Botvinnik'' and Black has built up a blockade. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: 'and Black has built up a blockade.'' und Schwarz hat seine Blockade errichtet. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: 'und Schwarz hat seine Blockade errichtet.''} 19. Nf3 O-O-O $1 $132 {Myers}) 15... b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?''} (15... Nbd7 16. Ng3 O-O $2 17. h4 $1 g4 18. fxg4 b5 19. Qd1 Qxa3 20. g5 Qxc3 21. gxf6 Qd3+ 22. Qxd3 exd3 23. Nf5 $16 {Myers}) 16. Qc2 Qxa3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: This looks crazy. Why would Tal, under fire, take time out for pawn grabbing? ANSWER: It may appear outlandishly greedy but it's played under the theory: it's better to have something without need, rather than need something you don't have.'} ({Tal didn't like the looks of his position after} 16... e3 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?' Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Botvinnik'} 17. Bxe3 Qxa3 18. h4 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!'} g4 19. Ng3 {, when Black is in deep trouble.}) (16... Nbd7 $5 {Bronstein} 17. a4 O-O 18. axb5 exf3 19. Ng3 fxg2+ 20. Kxg2 Qd6 21. Nf5 Qd5+ 22. Kg1 Rfe8 $11 {Myers}) 17. h4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '!' Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!''} gxh4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's pieces hold back and mill about, none willing to be at the forefront of the fight. QUESTION: Why did Black just allow the decimation of his already poor structure? ANSWER: I don't understand the motivation behind the move, except to say: in a position where there are no good answers, all moves are bad.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or'} 17... Rg8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. In all cases, White has a firm grip on the advantage. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?' Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Botvinnik''} 18. Ng3 $1 Qa4 19. Qd2 Nbd7 20. Nf5 O-O-O 21. Ne7+ Kc7 22. Nxg8 Qc4+ 23. Kg1 Rxg8 $44 {Myers}) ({Kasparov called Tal's last move "negligent" and recommended} 17... g4 {(which would be my choice)} 18. fxe4 Nbd7 (18... a5 $6 19. c4 bxc4 20. Qxc4 O-O 21. Ng3 a4 22. Qe2 Kh7 23. Kg1 Rg8 24. e5 $16) 19. Ng3 Qa4 20. Qxa4 bxa4 21. Ra1 O-O 22. Rxa4 Rfb8 23. Ra2 Rb1+ 24. Be1 a5 25. Ke2 $16 {[%cal Ye2d3,Rh1f1] Myers}) (17... exf3 $143 $2 18. Qf5 $1 Nbd7 19. Nf4+ Kd8 20. hxg5 Qxc3 21. gxf3 Qc4+ 22. Kg2 $18 {[%csl Rf6,Rh8] Myers}) 18. Bxh4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's opening experiment goes horribly awry, much like the scientist who builds a time machine, goes back 60 years, and falls in love with and marries his own grandmother, becoming his own grandfather. Tal's aversion to endings (especially versus Botvinnik) costs him this game. Tal said afterwards: "For the first time in my life I was knocked out in the opening!" '} Nbd7 19. Ng3 O-O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's king runs away, tired of the flow of White's reproaches down the e-file. There is no safe haven for the king across the board, but Tal continues to cling to his shred of faith. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?!''} (19... Qa4 $5) 20. Nxe4 Rhe8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal attempts to mix it up with a piece sac. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?!''} ({He is unlikely to save himself in the line} 20... Nxe4 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?'} 21. Qxe4 {Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Botvinnik'} Qxc3 22. Bxd8 Rxd8 23. Rxh6 Nb8 24. Rh4 {, when White's extra exchange should be decisive, despite Black's two connected passers.} (24. Qf5+ Kb7 25. Qxf7+ Rd7 26. Qf4 $16 {Myers})) (20... Rde8 $2 21. Nxf6 Rxe1+ (21... Nxf6 $2 22. Bxf6 Rxe1+ 23. Kxe1 Qa1+ 24. Kf2 $18) 22. Kxe1 Qa1+ 23. Qd1 Qxc3+ 24. Kf2 $18 {Euwe}) 21. Kf2 $1 { Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A great practical decision, which deems material inconsequential over initiative and board control. Each of Tal's frantic attempts to complicate run aground on the reef of ineffectuality, and his hopes fall one by one. Ripperger: '' Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?' Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Ripperger: '!' Ripperger: '''' [Botvinnik is following a logical positional plan: the restoration of his powerful pawn centre.] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[Botvinnik is following a logical positional plan: the restoration of his powerful pawn centre.]'' [Botvinnik verfolgt einen logischen positionellen Plan: Die Wiederherstellung seines mächtigen Bauernzentrums.] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[Botvinnik verfolgt einen logischen positionellen Plan: Die Wiederherstellung seines mächtigen Bauernzentrums.]''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead, after'} 21. Nxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '!?''} Rxe1+ 22. Kxe1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '?''} (22. Bxe1 $3 {[%csl Gc3,Gf1]} Nxf6 23. Qf5+ Nd7 24. Rxh6 f6 25. Rxf6 Kc7 26. Qf4+ Kb7 27. Rd6 $18 {Myers}) 22... Qa1+ 23. Qd1 Qxc3+ 24. Kf1 Ne5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Myers Euwe Ripperger: '' Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Botvinnik'' is very complex and not easy to judge. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is very complex and not easy to judge. Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Ripperger: 'is very complex and not easy to judge.''' ist äußerst komplex und nicht eindeutig zu beurteilen. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ist äußerst komplex und nicht eindeutig zu beurteilen. Myers Euwe Ripperger: 'Ripperger: 'ist äußerst komplex und nicht eindeutig zu beurteilen.'''} (24... Nc5 25. Bf2 a5 {, Black would still retain fishing chances.})) (21. c4 $1 b4 22. Nd6+ Kb8 23. Nxe8 Rxe8 24. Kf2 Kb7 25. Rxe8 Nxe8 26. Re1 $18 {Myers}) 21... Nxe4+ 22. fxe4 f6 (22... Qd6 23. Bxd8 Qf4+ 24. Kg1 Kxd8 25. e5 f6 26. e6 Nb6 27. Qg6 $18 {Myers}) 23. Ra1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's initiative grows to lethal levels.'} Qe7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After the rebuff, the queen's dour silence makes everyone around her uncomfortable. Now she cocks her ear as she listens for the approach of enemy footsteps near her king's chambers.'} (23... Qd6 24. Bg3 $1 {Euwe} Qe6 25. Rxa7 $18) (23... Qf8 24. Rxa7 Nb6 $2 25. Qa2 Na4 26. Bg3 $18) 24. Rxa7 Qxe4 {[This smashes the pawn centre. Botvinnik has recognized that even without queens he has a powerful attack.] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[This smashes the pawn centre. Botvinnik has recognized that even without queens he has a powerful attack.]'' [Damit ist das Bauernzentrum zerschlagen. Botvinnik hat jedoch erkannt, daß er auch ohne Damen einen mächtigen Angriff erhält.] Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Ripperger: '[Damit ist das Bauernzentrum zerschlagen. Botvinnik hat jedoch erkannt, daß er auch ohne Damen einen mächtigen Angriff erhält.]''} 25. Qxe4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One can be a pragmatic realist, and at the same time believe in miracles. Botvinnik's attack continues, even queenless.'} Rxe4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal must have breathed a bit easier at this point, having forced queens off the board. However, Botvinnik had seen deeper and soon surges forward with attack-induced élan. '} 26. Ra8+ Nb8 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is forced into the unpleasant pin, since'} 26... Kc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} 27. Bg3+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins on the spot. Myers Euwe Ripperger: '+-''}) 27. Bg3 Kb7 28. Rha1 Rc8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'What was once thought to be a safe haven is now a bomb-scarred, tangled abstraction of fragmented metal and concrete. Black's king is caught in a box, despite the comforting absence of queens on the board. EXERCISE (combination alert): Find White's most accurate path to victory. ANSWER: The black king's survival skills, accumulated over a long life of hardship and deprivation, are still not enough to save him. '} (28... c5 29. R1a7+ Kc6 30. Rc7+ Kd5 31. Rxc5+ Ke6 32. Kf3 $1 $18) 29. R8a7+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} (29. R1a7+ Kb6 {(the king, cowering in his closet, claps hands to ears, the sounds of White's army's forced ingress unbearable to his ears)} 30. Bd6 c5 31. dxc5+ Kc6 32. Rxb8 {is still winning but not as strong as the game continuation.}) 29... Kb6 30. Bxb8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop adheres to b8.'} b4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Dazed and dejected, Tal can only muster token resistance.'} ({Botvinnik's point: he procures a piece from his labours since the automatic recapture} 30... Rxb8 $4 {runs into} 31. R1a6# {.}) 31. Bd6 bxc3 32. Bc5+ Kb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): Black's king loathes his doctor's enforced callisthenics. White to play and force mate. ANSWER: The creature, inconceivable in size and scope, looms above the horizon, blotting out the entire sky. The first principle of the king hunt: Cut off the enemy king, rather than give chase. '} 33. R1a4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. The other rook delivers mate on a5 and Tal's king floats belly up, a dead fish in a polluted bay.'} 1-0

