{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The 1961 world championship rematch, as we all know, was nothing short of a disaster for Tal, who struggled with health issues and just couldn't reach his kind of positions. This game and the following were two where Tal managed to achieve the complication level necessary to defeat Botvinnik.'} 1. e4 {Tal: 'Myers Euwe Tal'} c6 ({We shape our opening repertoires in our own stylistic images. Botvinnik realized that the Caro-Kann's ultra-solidity was his best bet against Tal's 1 e4. Compare Tal's game with this Nimzowitsch classic, which arose from a similar structure from the French Defence:} 1... e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. Qg4 cxd4 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bd3 f5 ({today,} 6... Qc7) ({and} 6... Nge7 {are generally played}) 7. Qg3 Nge7 8. O-O Ng6 $6 9. h4 Qc7 10. Re1 Bd7 $6 11. a3 O-O-O 12. b4 $1 a6 $6 13. h5 Nge7 14. Bd2 h6 15. a4 g5 16. b5 f4 17. Qg4 Nb8 18. c3 Re8 19. cxd4 Kd8 20. Rc1 Qb6 21. a5 Qa7 22. b6 Qa8 {(it's generally a bad sign when your opponent chases your queen into a corner, without access to a single square)} 23. Rc7 Nf5 24. Nc3 Be7 25. Nxd5 $1 Nxd4 26. Nxd4 exd5 {. --- EXERCISE  (combination alert): Herr. Hakansson played the French with less-than-stellar accuracy. How did Nimzo force mate? --- ANSWER: Queen sacrifice/smothered mate:} 27. Qxd7+ {1-0, A.Nimzowitsch-A.Hakansson, Kristianstad 1922, since} Nxd7 28. Ne6# {is mate.}) 2. d4 d5 3. e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A sign of Tal's desperation against Botvinnik's dreaded Caro-Kann. Of course, attempting to defeat Botvinnik in a closed, strategic position was akin to the attempt to drown a fish.'} ({Against Botvinnik, Tal generally got nothing from the opening when he played his normal} 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 {.}) 3... c5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'At the time, this rare move was considered an odd inversion of what was considered normal.'} ({The other occasion on which Tal succeeded in generating the kind of open tactical position which I assume he sought proceeded: Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Today, it's the height of fashion. I think Botvinnik should have stuck with the'} 3... Bf5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'line. --- QUESTION: Why? --- ANSWER: Because in that version, White has a much harder time opening the position. And as we all know, Tal's forte was open - not closed! - positions. Later in the match, Botvinnik returned to the normal'} 4. h4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(today, this is considered one of White's most dangerous lines against the Advance Caro)'} h5 (4... h6 5. g4 Bd7 6. c3 c5 7. Bg2 {(the bishop ends up hitting a wall on d5)} e6 8. Ne2 Bb5 9. Na3 $2 {(Botvinnik, in the 8th game of the match, committed the same strategic error in reverse with his 9...Nh6?)} Bxe2 {(Botvinnik rids himself of his bad bishop)} 10. Qxe2 cxd4 {(principle: knights operate better in locked pawn structures)} 11. cxd4 Bxa3 $1 {(good strategic judgement; Botvinnik understands that he can tame Tal's bishops in this rigid pawn structure)} 12. bxa3 Nc6 13. Be3 Qa5+ 14. Kf1 Nge7 15. Rb1 Rb8 16. Bh3 Qa4 17. Rd1 Qxa3 18. Kg2 Qa6 19. Qxa6 bxa6 20. h5 Kd7 {(principle: centralize your king in an ending)} 21. Rb1 Rb6 22. Kg3 Na5 23. Rxb6 axb6 {, when White had no visible compensation for his missing pawn, and Botvinnik efficiently converted to earn the full point, M.Tal-M.Botvinnik, World Championship (Game 18) Moscow 1961. This game is annotated in 'Botvinnik: Move by Move'.}) ({Botvinnik isn't a likely candidate for the beginner's trap} 4... e6 $4 5. g4 Bg6 6. h5 Be4 7. f3 {and White wins a piece}) 5. Ne2 $5 e6 6. Ng3 g6 (6... Bg6 7. Be2 c5 $132) 7. Nxf5 gxf5 8. c4 c5 $5 {An interesting decision - Black seems to cooperate in the opening of the game in exchange for material gain.} 9. cxd5 Qxd5 10. Nc3 Qxd4 11. Qf3 Nc6 12. Bb5 Nge7 13. Bg5 Qxe5+ 14. Kf1 Bh6 15. Re1 Qd4 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Qxf5 Rad8 18. Rh3 {Tal,M-Botvinnik,M/Moscow 1961/MainBase (33) With a sharp tactical battle and decent chances against Black's king.}) 4. dxc5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I think this is one of White's best lines, to enter a line of the Advance French, but a move up, since Black took two moves to play ...c5.'} ({Instead,} 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 cxd4 6. cxd4 Bg4 {gives Black exactly what he wants, an Advance French position, with his traditionally bad bishop on the outside of his pawn chain.}) 4... e6 5. Qg4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Isn't this a violation of principle? --- ANSWER: A deliberate violation by Tal, who wants to tie Botvinnik's bishop down to defence of g7. This is a variant on an old Nimzowitsch theme, which normally ran 1 e4 e6 2 d4 d5 3 e5 c5 4 Qg4. Tal gets a superior version in this one, since he is up a tempo, because Black took two moves to achieve ...c5.'} ({White can also play to hang on to c5, with} 5. Be3 Nh6 6. c3 Nf5 7. Bd4 Bd7 8. Nf3 Nc6 9. a3 a5 10. Be2 a4 {. Black achieved light square compensation for his pawn, J.Nunn-J.Speelman, British League 2001.}) 5... Nc6 {Tal: 'N'} 6. Nf3 Qc7 7. Bb5 {'Tal: This move fits well into White's strategic plan. He continues his development, and by removing Black's c6 he reduces pressure on his e-pawn. White's advantage in development and space creates difficult problems for Black.'} ({White can also try} 7. Nc3 {, intending Nb5, and then:} Nxe5 $2 (7... a6 8. Na4 Nxe5 9. Nxe5 Qxe5+ 10. Qe2 Qxe2+ 11. Bxe2 Bd7 12. Nb6 Rd8 13. b4 {. I like the queenside majority in the ending, and also White's entrenched knight is a pain for Black}) 8. Nb5 Qa5+ 9. b4 Nxg4 10. bxa5 Bxc5 $1 11. Nc7+ Kd8 12. Nxa8 Nxf2 13. Rg1 f6 14. Rb1 Ne4 15. Rh1 {and I don't think that Black can so easily pick off the a8 straggler. --- QUESTION: Can't Black force a draw with} Nf2 {- ? --- ANSWER: The move is a blunder. White wins with} 16. Rb5 $1 Ne4 17. Bd3 Bd6 18. Be3 {when his knight will escape its imprisonment from a8.}) 7... Bd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move is considered inaccurate by today's standards.'} ({After} 7... Nge7 8. O-O Bd7 9. Re1 (9. c4 h5 10. Qg5 (10. Qh3 dxc4 11. Na3 Nf5 12. Bxc4 Bxc5 13. Nb5 Qb6 14. Nc3 Ncd4 15. Ng5 Qc7 16. Bd2 Be7 17. Bd3 Bc6 18. Rac1 Rd8 19. Nce4 Qxe5 20. Rfe1 Qb8 21. Nc5 Rd5 22. b4 Qd8 23. Ncxe6 fxe6 24. Nxe6 Qd7 25. Nf4 Rd6 26. Rc5 g6 27. Bc3 Rh7 28. Rce5 Kd8 29. Bc4 Bf6 30. Nxg6 Rg7 31. Qxh5 Bxe5 32. Nxe5 Bxg2 33. Qh8+ Kc7 34. Bxd4 Nxd4 35. Bf7 Qxf7 36. Rc1+ {0-1 Conquest,S-Dyce,A/4NCL ENG 1998/}) 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 Nf5 12. Qf4 Bxc5 13. Nc3 Nce7 14. Bb5 Bxb5 15. Nxb5 Qb6 16. Qa4 Nc6 17. Bg5 a6 18. Rac1 Qxb5 19. Qxb5 Bxf2+ 20. Rxf2 axb5 21. Rd2 O-O 22. a3 f6 23. exf6 gxf6 24. Bh4 b4 25. Bf2 bxa3 26. bxa3 e5 27. Bc5 Rfc8 28. Rf1 Na5 29. Bb4 Ne3 30. Re1 Nac4 31. Rd7 Nc2 32. Re4 b5 33. Rh4 Nxb4 34. axb4 Ra1+ 35. Kf2 Ra2+ 36. Kg3 Ne3 37. Rxh5 Rxg2+ 38. Kh3 Rcc2 39. Rd3 Rge2 40. Rh4 Nf1 41. Kg4 Kf7 {0-1 Kemp,P-Douthwaite,B/corr ICCF 2000/}) (9. Bxc6 Nxc6 10. Re1 Qa5 11. c3 Qxc5 12. Be3 Qa5 13. Nbd2 Qc7 14. Bg5 Qb6 15. b4 a5 16. a3 Qa6 17. Rab1 b5 18. c4 dxc4 19. Nxc4 bxc4 20. b5 Qb7 21. bxc6 Qxc6 22. Rec1 c3 23. Rb3 c2 24. Nd4 Qd5 25. Rxc2 Bc5 26. Rd3 Qxe5 27. Re2 Qc7 28. Nxe6 Bxe6 29. Rxe6+ fxe6 30. Qxe6+ Be7 31. Re3 Ra7 32. Bxe7 Qxe7 33. Qc8+ Kf7 34. Rxe7+ Rxe7 35. Qf5+ Kg8 36. Qc8+ Kf7 37. Qf5+ Ke8 38. h4 Rf8 39. Qb5+ Kf7 40. Qxa5 Rfe8 41. g3 h6 42. a4 Kg8 43. Kg2 Re5 44. Qc3 R5e7 45. Qc4+ Kh8 46. Qc5 {½-½ Cumbers,P-Partington,A/West Bromwich ENG 2003/}) (9. Nc3 a6 10. Bxc6 Nxc6 11. Re1 h5 12. Qf4 Bxc5 13. Nxd5 $1 exd5 14. e6 O-O-O 15. exd7+ Rxd7 16. c3 Qb6 17. Ng5 f6 18. Nh3 d4 19. Qf5 dxc3 20. bxc3 Qa5 21. Qc2 Rhd8 22. Nf4 Ne5 23. Be3 Bxe3 24. Rxe3 Rd2 25. Qe4 Rd1+ 26. Re1 Rxa1 27. Rxa1 Qxa2 $1 28. Rb1 Qb3 29. Qe1 Qf7 30. Qe4 Rd7 31. h4 Kb8 32. Qe2 Qc4 $3 33. Qxc4 Nxc4 34. Nxh5 b5 $19 35. Nf4 Kb7 36. Ne6 Kb6 37. h5 a5 38. f3 a4 39. Kf2 Nd2 40. Ra1 Nb3 41. Ra2 Re7 42. Nf4 Kc5 43. Ne2 Kc4 44. g4 Rd7 45. Ke1 Rd3 {0-1 Bulgarini Torres,M-Recoulat,E/corr ICCF 2000/}) 9... Ng6 10. Bxc6 Qxc6 11. b4 b6 12. cxb6 axb6 13. c3 Be7 14. Qg3 O-O {, Black's queenside pressure compensates for his missing pawn, E.Schreiber-J.Barrios Troncoso, correspondence 2001.} 15. Bg5 Bxg5 16. Qxg5 Qc7 17. Qe3 f6 18. a3 Nxe5 19. Nxe5 fxe5 20. Qxe5 Qxe5 21. Rxe5 b5 22. Nd2 Rfc8 23. Re3 Ra7 24. Nb3 Rca8 25. Na5 Rc8 26. f4 Kf7 27. Rf1 g6 28. Rff3 h6 29. Kf1 Ke7 30. Ke2 Rf8 31. Kd3 h5 32. h4 Rc8 33. f5 gxf5 34. Rxf5 Rac7 35. Kd4 Kd6 36. Rxh5 Rg8 37. g3 Be8 38. Rhe5 Rg4+ 39. Kd3 Bg6+ 40. Kd2 Be4 41. Rh5 Rcg7 42. Nb3 Rxg3 43. Rxg3 Rxg3 44. Nd4 e5 45. Nxb5+ Ke6 46. Rh6+ Kd7 47. Nd6 Rg2+ 48. Ke3 Rg3+ 49. Ke2 Rg2+ 50. Kf1 {1-0 Schreiber,E-Barrios Troncoso,J/corr ICCF 2001/}) (7... f5 8. Qd4 Bd7 9. Bxc6 Qxc6 10. Be3 f4 11. Qxf4 Bxc5 12. Bxc5 Qxc5 13. c3 Ne7 14. Nbd2 Ng6 15. Qd4 Qb5 16. O-O-O O-O 17. g3 Qa6 18. Kb1 Bb5 19. Ne1 Ne7 20. Nb3 Nc6 21. Qe3 Bc4 22. Nc2 Bxb3 23. axb3 Qb5 24. Nd4 Nxd4 25. Rxd4 a5 26. c4 dxc4 27. bxc4 Qc6 28. Rhd1 a4 29. Qa3 b5 30. Qd6 Qe8 31. cxb5 Qg6+ 32. R1d3 Rxf2 33. b6 a3 34. bxa3 Raf8 35. b7 Rf1+ 36. Kb2 R8f2+ 37. Kc3 Rc1+ 38. Kb4 Rb1+ 39. Rb3 Rxb3+ 40. Kxb3 Rf3+ 41. Kb2 Rf2+ 42. Rd2 Rxd2+ 43. Qxd2 Qe8 44. Ka2 Qb5 45. Qd8+ Kf7 46. Qc7+ Kg6 47. b8=Q Qd5+ 48. Qb3 {1-0 Apicella,M-Bernard,V/Bois Colombes FRA 2003/}) 8. Bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal hands over the bishop pair to reduce the pressure on e5.'} Qxc6 (8... Bxc6 9. Nbd2 (9. O-O Ne7 10. b4 a5 11. c3 $14 {Myers}) 9... Nh6 10. Qf4 Nf5 11. Nb3 Be7 12. h4 h5 13. c3 Bb5 14. Nbd4 Bd3 15. Nxf5 Bxf5 16. Nd4 Be4 17. f3 Bd3 18. b4 b6 19. c6 a5 20. b5 a4 21. Qg3 g6 22. Bg5 Bxg5 23. Qxg5 Ra5 24. Qf6 Rg8 25. Kd2 Bxb5 26. g4 Bxc6 27. gxh5 Bb5 28. Rab1 Qc4 29. Rxb5 gxh5 30. Rxa5 Rg2+ 31. Ke1 bxa5 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qa8 Qxc3+ 34. Kf1 Qb2 35. Nc6+ Kd7 36. Nb8+ Kc7 37. Qa7+ Kc8 38. Qd7+ Kxb8 39. Qe8+ Kc7 40. Qxf7+ Kc6 41. Qxe6+ Kc5 42. Qe7+ Kd4 43. Qa7+ Kd3 44. Qa6+ Ke3 45. Qh6+ Kd4 46. Qf4+ Kd3 {0-1 Hamilton,D-Klugel,J/corr ICCF 1997/}) 9. Be3 (9. O-O $5 Qxc5 10. Nc3 Ne7 11. Be3 Qc7 12. Rfe1 Nf5 13. Nd4 Qc4 14. Rad1 Qb4 15. Qf3 Qxb2 16. Nxd5 exd5 17. Nxf5 Be6 18. c4 Qc2 19. Nd6+ Bxd6 20. exd6 O-O 21. cxd5 Bd7 22. Bd4 Qg6 23. Qa3 Rfd8 24. Re3 Qg4 25. Qb3 f6 26. h3 Qf4 27. Qxb7 Qxd6 28. Bxa7 Bc6 29. Qb3 Bd7 30. Qb6 Qxb6 31. Bxb6 Rdb8 32. Bc7 Re8 33. Rxe8+ Rxe8 34. Rd2 Kf7 35. f3 Rc8 36. Bg3 h5 37. Kf2 Ba4 38. Ke3 Rc2 39. Rxc2 Bxc2 40. Kd4 Ke8 41. Bf4 Kd7 42. h4 Ba4 43. Bd2 Bd1 44. Kc5 Ba4 45. d6 Bd1 46. Ba5 Ba4 47. Bc7 Bd1 48. Ba5 {½-½ Engl,H-Gretencord,U/corr desc 2001/}) 9... Nh6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'An attacker utilizes violence as a method of imprinting a position with his or her stylistic stamp, while the strategist prefers gentle coercion. Botvinnik sinks all his karma - both good and bad - into this over-designed contrivance, which lands Black in a strategically inferior position.'} ({He would have been better off playing the more patient} 9... h5 10. Qg5 Ne7 {, intending ...Nf5.}) (9... Qb5 $5) (9... Ne7 10. O-O Qa4 11. Qxa4 Bxa4 12. Nc3 Bxc2 13. Nb5 Kd7 14. Nd6 Bg6 15. b4 Nc6 16. b5 Na5 17. Nxb7 Nxb7 18. c6+ Kc7 19. cxb7 Kxb7 20. a4 Rc8 21. Nd4 Bc5 22. Nc6 Bxe3 23. fxe3 a6 24. e4 Rhf8 25. Ne7 Rce8 26. Nxg6 hxg6 27. Rad1 axb5 28. axb5 dxe4 29. Rd7+ Kb6 30. Rd6+ Kxb5 31. Rb1+ Kc5 32. Rc1+ Kb5 33. Rb1+ Kc5 34. Rc1+ Kb5 {½-½ Sadowski,M-Wheeler,J/corr IECG 2005/}) 10. Bxh6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Each asymmetrical swap on the chess board, is in a way, an exchange of hostages.'} gxh6 {Cyrus'Tal: Often weak pawns are compensated by open lines and active play. Here, however, this is not the case and, all important, Black cannot exert pressure against White's center.' Lakdawala: 'Good judgement on Tal's part. Black's structure, once without blemish, now sees impurities creep within. White's knights adequately deal with Black's bishops, mainly since White dominates the d4 - the ideal blockading square. --- QUESTION: Is this a correct assessment? Won't the game later open up in favour of Black's bishops? --- ANSWER: I think White's development lead and control over d4, plus Black's weakened h-pawns assure Tal of the advantage. The odd thing is in this, the eighth game, Tal played with two knights versus two bishops and won; in the 18th game, Botvinnik ended up with two knights versus Tal's two bishops, and won. Moral: knights are better than bishops! My San Diego IM buddies John Watson and Keaton Kiewra tease me all the time about my knight-over-bishop bias. Well, these two games are clear proof that I was right all along!'} 11. Nbd2 Qxc5 (11... Bxc5 12. Qg7 O-O-O 13. O-O {Euwe} Rdg8 14. Qf6 Kb8 15. Nb3 Bf8 16. Nbd4 Qc7 17. a4 $1 $36 {Myers}) 12. c4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move is strong for two reasons: 1. The black king is sure to castle long, since everywhere else on the board is unsafe. So White threatens to open that sector of the board. --- 2. Principle: When it's a battle of development lead versus the opponent's bishop pair, development lead takes precedence, and you should open the game.' Tal: '!' Continuing the policy of trying to open the play. Black has the bishop pair, but the clear intention is that discomfort to his king will prove the principal factor.} O-O-O (12... dxc4 13. O-O (13. Ne4 $2 Qa5+ $1 14. Kf1 O-O-O $17 {Myers}) 13... Bc6 14. Rac1 (14. Rad1 $5) 14... O-O-O $2 (14... b5 $5) 15. Nxc4 Qd5 16. Ne3 $18 {[%cal Yf3d4] Petrosian}) 13. O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's dream has been achieved: opposite wings castling.'} Kb8 14. Rfd1 $36 {and again Tal has realised his aim of opening the position and establishing a certain initiative. Sadly for the great tactical magician, in the other encounters Botvinnik's attempts to keep the game closed were much more successful!} (14. Rac1 $5) (14. Qf4 $5) 14... Qb6 {Tal: '?'} ({I would think about handing White control over d6 to open the game with} 14... dxc4 $5 15. Nxc4 Be7 16. Qf4 Bc6 17. b3 Bd5 18. Rac1 Qb4 {. I still like White's game, but at least here Black's bishops achieve some degree of activation.}) (14... Be7 $5 {[%cal Yh8g8,Yg8g6,Rd8g8] Bronstein}) 15. Qh4 {Multi'Tal: This strong move lends the most scope to the White Queen. It prevents Black's 15...e7, protects the f-pawn, and ties down Black's pieces to the protection of the d8 and h-pawn.' purpose: covering f2 against a future ...Bc5, while keeping Black's queen tied to defence of her d8-rook. Tal: '!'} a5 $6 {Botvinnik suppresses b4 at a high cost of allowing White a future b4 prying-mechanism. Tal: '?'} ({He may have been better off playing} 15... Rg8 {. Tal: '!?'}) (15... Rc8 $5 {Kotov}) (15... Bc5 $2 16. cxd5 $1 exd5 17. Nb3 Rhg8 18. Rxd5 $16 {Myers}) 16. Rac1 Rg8 17. Nb3 $1 a4 $6 ({I think Botvinnik should open the game  (not an easy decision against Tal!) with} 17... dxc4 18. Rxc4 a4 19. Nbd4 Bc5 20. Rc2 Bb5 21. Rcd2 {(threat: Nc6+)} Rd5 22. Nxb5 Qxb5 23. Rxd5 exd5 24. Rd2 Bb4 25. Nd4 Rc8 26. Rd1 Qd3 27. Qg4 Qe4 28. Qxe4 dxe4 {, with an inferior but playable ending for Black.}) (17... Rc8 18. cxd5 Rxc1 19. Nxc1 exd5 20. Rxd5 $16 {Tal}) 18. c5 $1 {Terrific strategic judgement from Tal  (yes, I realize this is a strange statement to make). The move acts as a binding agent which solidifies White's strategic advantage: 1. White dominates d4. --- 2. White's c5-pawn cramps Black's dark-squared bishop. --- 3. Now Black must be on constant alert for c6 line-opening ideas.} Qc7 {The b6-square was only a temporary way station for Black's queen. This is the only move, since Black's queen must remain on the d8-rook.} 19. Nbd4 Rc8 {Contemplating ...Bxc5;} ({rather than} 19... Bxc5 $5 20. b4 axb3 21. Nxb3 b6 22. Qf4 (22. a4 $1 $16 {Euwe}) 22... Qa7 23. Qxf7 Rdf8 24. Qxh7 Ba4 25. Qd3 Bxb3 26. axb3 {. Although I don't see full compensation for Black's missing pawn, I would still take this path over the game continuation.}) 20. b4 $1 (20. Qf6 $5) 20... axb3 21. axb3 {Now the a-file opens, an ominous sign for Black's king. A key goal of any war effort is to manufacture new copies to replace the old war machines which are destroyed in battle. White now follows with b4, which not only covers c5, but also threatens to press forward later on, with b5 and either c6 or b6 pushes. This position is an example of two knights choking their counterpart bishops, who have no scope.} Qd8 {Botvinnik, rightfully fearing Tal's coming attack, forces queens off the board. Unfortunately for him, White's strategic domination remains in the ending. Tal: 'Myers: Tal calls this move "an error" but Black does not seem to have anything better.'} 22. Qxd8 Rxd8 23. b4 Rg4 $6 {This move, attempts to colonize barren territory. Botvinnik plans ...Bg7, ...Re4 and ...Bxe5, which is too slow.} ({He should play} 23... Rc8 {to suppress b5. Tal: '!?'}) 24. b5 $1 {When we commit to such a move, there is no override command which halts the process, once it begins.} Rc8 {Pursuit of c5 turns out to be a fleeting enthusiasm for Black, since provoking c6 only benefits White.} 25. c6 $1 {Forced, but strong. The game opens, yet disorientingly, Black's bishops seem to grow more and more pathetic.} Be8 {Tal: ''} ({Black can't open lines with} 25... bxc6 $2 26. bxc6 Be8 27. Rc2 h5 28. Rb1+ Kc7 29. h3 Re4 30. Nb5+ Kd8 31. Nfd4 {. Black is crushed, since} Rxe5 32. Na7 Rc7 33. Ndb5 {is decisive.}) 26. Rc2 Bg7 ({If Black attempts to set up a dark-square blockade with his king on b6, with} 26... Ka7 $4 {, White meets it harshly with the line opening} 27. b6+ $1 Kxb6 28. Rb1+ Kc7 ({or} 28... Ka7 29. Rxb7+ Ka6 30. Ra2+ Ba3 31. Rxa3#) 29. cxb7+ {forcing mate.}) 27. Ra1 $1 {Tal gathers energy for his coming attack, ignoring Black's attack on his e5-pawn.} Bxe5 $2 {Black reaches for the honey, deep inside the hive. Botvinnik continues to fixate on the fiction that he will pick off e5 and get away with the crime. Such surges of adrenaline tend to wipe away subtle distinctions. Thoughts are the fastest thing in the universe, except that no one knows where they come from, or where they go. And with the help of such irrational thoughts, we humans are masters of rationalizing - and therefore justifying - our own self-destructive actions. Botvinnik inexplicably decides to call Tal's 'bluff', forgetting that such last minute rescues only happen in movies and novels.} ({Black offers greater resistance with the bleak yet superior options:} 27... Rxd4 28. Nxd4 Bxe5 29. Rca2 $1 Bxd4 30. Ra8+ Kc7 31. Rxc8+ Kxc8 32. Ra8+ Kc7 33. cxb7 Kxb7 34. Rxe8 {Tal: 'Euwe'} Kb6 35. Re7 Kxb5 36. Rxf7 {and White converts the ending.}) (27... Re4 28. Kf1 Bxe5 29. Nxe5 Rxe5 30. Rca2 Kc7 31. b6+ $1 Kd6 (31... Kxb6 32. Rb1+ Kc7 33. cxb7 Rb8 34. Rc1+ $1 Kd6 35. Ra6+ Kd7 36. Ra7 Re4 37. Nc6 {wins}) 32. cxb7 Rb8 33. Ra8 Rxb7 34. Rxe8 Rxb6 35. Nf3 {when White should convert.}) 28. Nxe5 Rxd4 {EXERCISE (combination alert): Both sides had foreseen this position, but only Tal calculated it with accuracy. White wins by force. How? --- ANSWER: Attraction/deflection.} 29. Nd7+ $1 {As it turns out, the relationship of Black's king behind his defensive wall, is that of content within a sealed container. 1-0 (29) Tal,M-Botvinnik,M 26: World Championship, Moscow 1961 [Cyrus Lakdawala]} (29. Nd7+ $1 Bxd7 (29... Kc7 30. b6+ Kd8 31. cxb7 {is catastrophic for Black Tal: '+-'}) 30. cxd7 Rd8 31. Rc8+ $1 {(attraction)} Rxc8 32. Ra8+ $1 {(deflection; as you may have guessed, the c- and a-files are popular hiking trails) Tal: '+-!' Tal: 'Euwe'} Kxa8 33. dxc8=Q+ {and White promotes with check, cruelly denying Black a back-rank mate.}) *

