{Andrew Soltis: '"Chess players, as a rule, are very revengeful people," Tal wrote in his game collection, meaning that they are always looking for a chance to retaliate against an opponent who inflicted a particularly memorable defeat on them. When Tal met Fischer in the second round at Bled, it was three months since he had lost a World Championship rematch (literally "revenge match" in Russian) to Botwinnik. Now it was Fischer's turn for payback, to avenge the 0-4 humiliation he suffered at Tal's hands in the 1959 Candidates Tournament.'} 1. e4 {Fischer,R} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 ({Garry Kasparov: '"No doubt Tal expected'} 5. Nb5 {Fischer,R-Najdorf,M Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Garry Kasparov: 'which I had played exclusively at Buenos Aires 1960. I still think that might be best," wrote Fischer, and later he confirmed this (Game Nos.91 and 98) .'}) 5... Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: 'The ...Nc6, ...e6 system was very new and only given half a page in Rolf Schwarz's 587-page book on the SIcilian (1961).'} {Play usually continues} 6. g3 {This fianchetto line is one of the main replies to the Sicilian Taimanov.} Nf6 $2 {"Probably the losing move!" – Fischer. Interestingly, against 6 Be3 or 6 Be2 there is nothing wrong with playing 6...Nf6 before ...a7-a6, but here it proves to be a serious inaccuracy, as we will see next. Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'A purely 'manual' mistake.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Tal plays a natural move - and finds himself lost by move 10. In fact, he gives 6. ...Nf6 two question marks in the tournament book. He explained that he planned to play' Garry Kasparov: 'Tal intended the usual'} 6... a6 { Fischer,R: '' Andrew Soltis: 'and 7. ...Nf6 but "once a year or so" he reversed the move order in his calculations and paid the price. Essential was 6. ...a6 - yet several strong GMs have fallen into the same trap since 1961 - including Benko, Judith Polgar and Yevgeny Vasiukov. Fischer,R: '''} 7. Bg2 Nf6 {Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.93) and had already written down 6...a6 on his score-sheet, but, by his own admission, "roughly once a year it would happen that I would write down the first move of a variation, but make the second." "Probably the losing move!" states Fischer. "Tal looked worried immediately after having made it, but I'm not sure he was convinced he had been really careless." Well, let's see...'} 8. O-O {, when Black has several choices such as} d6 (8... Bc5) ({, and} 8... Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Bc5 {. Instead, with his next move Tal "modifies" the move order and is severely punished for the rest of the game.})) 7. Ndb5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'And among the players who failed to punish 6. . ..Nf6? by playing other seventh moves are Keres and Vlastimil Hort.'} Qb8 {From now on Fischer will never let his opponent rest, creating new threats on every move, with a particular focus on the d6-square. Andrew Soltis: 'Black hopes to recoup with ...a6'} (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 Qd8 9. Bf4 e5 10. Bg5 $16 {Garry Kasparov: 'is bad for Black – Fischer.'}) 8. Bf4 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} Ne5 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: '"Tal took a long time on this risky reply.'} ({Later it was discovered that the ugly Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer considered' Garry Kasparov: 'Moreover, in the variation'} 8... e5 {offers more resistance. There can follow} 9. Bg5 a6 10. Bxf6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 10. Na3 b5 11. Bxf6 b4 $1) 10... axb5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 10... gxf6 11. Na3 {Garry Kasparov: ', Black should go in for'} {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of'} b5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (11... Bxa3 {Fischer,R Garry Kasparov: '!'} 12. bxa3 Ne7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer was wrong this line is better for Black than what happens to him in the game.' Garry Kasparov: '!'} 13. Qf3 {'!?' '!?' Johan Hellsten: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '!?'} (13. Bc4 Qc7 14. Qd3 d6 15. O-O Be6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Ke7 $11) (13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. Qxd5 (14. exd5 Qc7 15. c4 d6) 14... b5 ({or} 14... d6 {, with a somewhat inferior, but by no means lost position.})) 13... Qc7 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 14. Bg2 d6 15. O-O {, intending Nd1-e3, with a more comfortable game for White in Alexa. Ivanov-I.V.Ivanov, US Championship, Long Beach 1989. But if Black is aiming at such kind of play, I guess he should rather go for the Sveshnikov (1 e4 c5 2 Nf3 Nc6 3 d4 cxd4 4 Nxd4 Nf6 5 Nc3 e5) straight away!} Rg8 16. Nd1 $14 {Al.Ivanov-I.Ivanov,USA,1989}) 12. Nd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'to be clearly in White's favor and indicated it was no better than the knight move. Yet two decades later in a similar position in the Sveshnikov Sicilian was regarded as quite playable.'} f5 13. Nb1 $1) 11. Bg5 $16 Bb4 12. Bxb5 Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 d6 {."} ({Or first} 13... Qc7 {– indeed, the weakness of White's queenside greatly hinders the conversion of his extra pawn.})) 9. Be2 $1 {From here the bishop covers the f3-square, thus creating the threat of 10 Qd4. Johan Hellsten: 'From here the bishop covers the f3-square, thus creating the threat of 10 Qd4. ' Andrew Soltis: '!!' Andrew Soltis: 'Botwinnik, who annotated this game for Chess Life, thought this was part of a trap set by White's sixth move (!). By watching h5 and f3, WHite prepares both Qd4 and BXe5/f4 Fischer,R: '!'' Garry Kasparov: '"Perhaps Tal underestimated this simple move. It prepares Qd4 and keeps an eye on the b5-square." (Fischer)'} ({The immediate} 9. Bxe5 Qxe5 10. f4 Qb8 11. e5 a6 12. exf6 axb5 {is better for Black than the game}) 9... Bc5 {On most other moves 10 Qd4 is troublesome for Black; Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'But this definitely leads to defeat,'} ({e.g. Andrew Soltis: 'For instance' Garry Kasparov: 'as do the lines indicated by Fischer:'} 9... a6 {'?' '?' Johan Hellsten: '?'} 10. Qd4 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} d6 11. Rd1 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} axb5 12. Bxe5 $18 {with a huge edge, now that the bishop is untouchable due to mate. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-''} dxe5 13. Qd8#) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 9... d6 10. Qd4 Nc6 11. Nxd6+ (11. Qxd6 $1 {Tal,M} Bxd6 12. Bxd6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Tal'}) 11... Kd7 {Garry Kasparov: '(?)'} ({of course,} 11... Bxd6 12. Qxd6 e5 {is essential, but after} 13. Qxb8 Rxb8 14. Bg5 {White is simply a pawn up – G.K.}) 12. Bb5 ({or} 12. Qc4) 12... Bxd6 13. O-O-O {Fischer,R: '+-' Andrew Soltis: '+-' Garry Kasparov: '.'}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black's position is so bad Tal suggested' Garry Kasparov: '"In the tournament book Tal suggested the rather startling'} 9... Ng8 {Tal,M Andrew Soltis: '?!' Andrew Soltis: 'in his notes although White's superiority is clear after Fischer,R: 'Tal,M'' Garry Kasparov: 'to avoid material loss. After'} 10. Qd4 f6 11. O-O-O ({Garry Kasparov: 'if'} 11. Bxe5 fxe5 12. Qc4 Kd8 $1 $16 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'holds'}) 11... a6 12. Nd6+ Bxd6 13. Qxd6 Qxd6 14. Rxd6 $16 {Garry Kasparov: 'leads to a promising endgame," writes Fischer. Even so, this was Black's last chance: he would at least have retained material equality.'}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Also poor is the tricky'} 9... Bb4 {Fischer,R} 10. Qd4 {Andrew Soltis: 'becuse of'} Nc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'because of'} 11. Bxb8 Nxd4 12. Nc7+ Ke7 13. Nxa8 Nxc2+ 14. Kd1 (14. Kd2 Nxa1 15. Rxa1 (15. Bd3 $142 {PS}) 15... Nxe4+ 16. Kc2 Nxc3 17. bxc3 Bc5 $13) 14... Nxa1 15. e5 Bxc3 16. Bd6+ Kd8 17. bxc3 Ne4 18. Rf1 Nxc3+ 19. Kd2 Nxe2 20. Kxe2 Nc2 21. Kd3 $18 {Tal}) 10. Bxe5 $1 {This exchange isn't favourable by itself but, together with the following two pawn moves, it lets White take a firm grip on the centre. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '!''} Qxe5 11. f4 {Fischer,R: ''} Qb8 12. e5 {Right on time, before Black consolidates with ...d7-d6.} a6 ({Aware that Andrew Soltis: 'Black is positionally crushed after'} 12... Ng8 13. Ne4 {, followed by Nbd6+, would be terrible for him, Tal opts to complicate the game.} Be7 {Garry Kasparov: 'is also hopeless in view of'} 14. Qd2 $18 { bd6+, 0-0-0 Andrew Soltis: 'and Nbd6+ Fischer,R: '+-' Fischer,R: ' bd6+, 0-0-0'' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer),'} (14. Qd3) ({, or even the immediate} 14. Nbd6+ {.})) 13. exf6 axb5 14. fxg7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Also excellent is'} 14. Ne4 {; Keres,P Fischer,R: ''} Bf8 15. Qd4 {Fischer,R: '' Fischer,R: 'Keres' was another good option – Keres.} g6 {but Fischer's way is surer.} (15... Qa7 16. Qe5 g6 17. Bxb5 Qe3+ 18. Kf1 Qf3+ 19. Kg1 b6 20. Be2 $1 Qe3+ (20... Qxe2 21. Nd6+) 21. Kf1 Bc5 22. b4 $1 {and wins.})) 14... Rg8 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Qd4 {'!' A move with several virtues: it protects the important g7-pawn, speeds up development and enables a future check on f6. The b5-pawn can of course wait. '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} Ra4 {This inventive move won't solve Black's problems, but he was already short of a good defence. Garry Kasparov: 'A loss of a tempo,'} ({On Garry Kasparov: 'but the slightly more tenacious'} 16... Qc7 {; Botvinnik,M Fischer,R: '' White could consider Andrew Soltis: '' Garry Kasparov: '(Botvinnik) would also have lost in view of'} 17. O-O-O $5 (17. Bd3 b6 18. Nf6+ Bxf6 19. Qxf6 Qc5 20. O-O-O Qh5 21. h3 {etc.}) 17... Rxa2 18. Kb1 {with similar play to later on in the game.}) (16... Qa7 {Fischer,R} 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 18. Qxf6 Qe3 19. Kf1 $18 {Tal}) 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'After'} 17... Kd8 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'White wins easily with Fischer,R: '?''} 18. Qb6+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-''} ({of the flashy} 18. Nxg8 Rxd4 19. Nxe7)) 18. Qxf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threats include 19.Bh5, 19.Bd3 and 19.Qh6'} Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (18... Qa7 {Fischer,R} 19. Bh5 $18) ({If} 18... Qd6 {White could reply} 19. Bd3 {.}) 19. O-O-O {'!' Fischer has observed that his king will be safe on b1, irrespective of the capture on a2. '!' Johan Hellsten: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'An accurate move, which essentially concludes the game.'} ({while both Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 19. Bh5 {Garry Kasparov: ', then'} d5 {Fischer,R: '' Andrew Soltis: '' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer).'} (19... Re4+ {and 20. ...d5!})) ({In contrast, Andrew Soltis: 'IN each of their two previous games, Fischer found enough sparkling moves to win most games. But he failed to follow them up and had to settle for a loss and a draw. This time his play remains at a consistantly high level - with only one slip to come. Here he avoids'} 19. Bxb5 $2 {failed to} Qa5+ {Fischer,R: '-+' Andrew Soltis: 'which hangs a bishop Fischer,R: '-+''}) ({and} 19. Bd3 Qc5 {give Black more hope of survival. Andrew Soltis: '!'} 20. Bxh7 Qe3+ 21. Kf1 Qf3+ 22. Kg1 Qe3+ 23. Kg2 Qe2+ 24. Kh3 Rxg7 $1 25. Qxg7 Qh5+ {with perpetual check}) 19... Rxa2 ({Or} 19... Qd8 20. Qh6 Rxa2 21. Qxh7 Ke7 22. h4 {and wins.}) 20. Kb1 Ra6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'or' Garry Kasparov: 'while if'} 20... Ra5 {Garry Kasparov: '– the immediate'} 21. Bh5 d5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 21... d6 22. Rxd6 $1) 22. Rxd5 $1 exd5 23. Re1+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-'' Garry Kasparov: '.'}) ({Or Andrew Soltis: 'Black had no defense to Bh5, e.g.' Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 20... Qa5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer gives'} 21. b3 $1 { h5 with the strong threat of 22 Bh5. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '!' Fischer,R: ' h5'' Garry Kasparov: 'and Bh5'}) 21. Bxb5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Another good option was Garry Kasparov: '"I was so intent on snatching material and not botching this one that I missed'} 21. Bh5 {Fischer,R: '!'} d6 ({or} 21... d5 22. Rxd5 $1 Rxg7 23. Rd3 Rg8 $2 24. Rd8+) 22. Rhe1 ({or} 22. f5 $1 {– G.K.}) 22... Qe7 23. Qh6 Kd7 24. Qxh7 $18 {with a quick win – Fischer. Garry Kasparov: 'with a quick win in view." (Fischer).'} Qe8 25. f5 e5 (25... Kc7 26. fxe6 Bxe6 27. Rxe6 $1) 26. Rxe5) 21... Rb6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Tal pointed out'} 21... Ra5 {Fischer,R} {Andrew Soltis: 'But White can sidestep the trap with'} 22. c4 $1 $18 {Tal Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-!' Fischer,R: 'Tal''} (22. Qh6 $2 Rxb5 23. Qxh7 {Andrew Soltis: 'allows'} Rxb2+ 24. Kxb2 Qb6+ 25. Kc1 Qe3+ 26. Rd2 Qa3+ 27. Kd1 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} Qf3+ $1 {Tal Andrew Soltis: 'with perpetual check Fischer,R: '!' Fischer,R: 'Tal''})) 22. Bd3 {Tal tries to reach an inferior endgame after} e5 {Fischer,R: '!?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Also lost is' Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 22... Qd8 23. Qh6 f5 {Andrew Soltis: 'because of'} 24. Qh5+ Ke7 {Garry Kasparov: ', both'} 25. g4 $40 {Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'and'} 25. Be2 {with the idea of} Rxg7 26. Qh4+ Ke8 27. Bh5+ {. The point of 22. ...e5, besides attacking the queen is that 23.Qxe5+ Qxe5 takes Black from a hopeless middlegame to a merely wretched endgame. Garry Kasparov: 'etc are possible.'})) 23. fxe5 $1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'This is stronger than'} 23. Qxe5+ Qxe5 24. fxe5 Rxg7 $16 {, but in vain... Garry Kasparov: ', although even here after'} 25. Rhe1 Kd8 26. Bf5 {it is doubtful whether Black can save the game. "In top-flight chess, you have to drive your advantage home unmercifully." (Fischer)}) 23... Rxf6 24. exf6 { xh7 Now the threat of 25 Bxh7 proves irresistible. Andrew Soltis: 'A rare case where it was Fischer who gave up his queen for two rooks. Fischer,R: ' xh7'' Garry Kasparov: 'Threatening Bxh7.'} Qc5 $8 {Fischer,R: '' Andrew Soltis: 'Black manages to trade off the pawns for a roo know Fischer,R: '''} (24... Qb6 {, then} 25. Rhf1 $18 {'!' – Fischer. '!' Johan Hellsten: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and 26. Bxh7 is kaput Fischer,R: '+-''}) 25. Bxh7 Qg5 26. Bxg8 Qxf6 27. Rhf1 Qxg7 28. Bxf7+ {With such a material advantage, the rest is easy.} Kd8 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-''} 29. Be6 ({White can choose between several winning plans. It may seem that} 29. Rd3 {followed by the advance of the h-pawn is fastest but Black get counterplay from} b6 30. h4 Ba6 31. c4 Qh7 32. Kc2 b5) ({Perhaps} 29. Bd5 {is a cleaner win but it's hard to resist doubling rooks on the seventh rank.}) 29... Qh6 (29... Kc7 30. Bf5 $36 {Garry Kasparov: 'was no better (Fischer).'}) 30. Bxd7 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} Bxd7 31. Rf7 Qxh2 32. Rdxd7+ {. White soon won.} Ke8 33. Rde7+ Kd8 34. Rd7+ ({Andrew Soltis: 'Tal gave an alternative win'} 34. Rxb7 $5 {Fischer,R Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '!?''} Qh1+ 35. Ka2 Qd5+ 36. b3 Qa5+ 37. Kb2 Qe5+ 38. c3 Qe2+ 39. Ka3 Qa6+ 40. Kb4 Qd6+ 41. Kb5 Qd3+ 42. Kb6 Qe3+ (42... Qd6+ 43. Ka7 {Andrew Soltis: 'and wins. That's fine for the armchair analyst - but the practical player would follow Fischer's example, which leaves White a much larger margin of error'} Qa3+ 44. Kb8 Qd6+ 45. Rfc7 Qxg3 46. b4 $18 {[%cal Yb4b6,Yc3c6] PS}) 43. Ka6 Qe6+ 44. Ka7 $18 {Tal}) 34... Kc8 35. Rc7+ Kd8 36. Rfd7+ Ke8 37. Rd1 b5 38. Rb7 Qh5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 38... Qxg3 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'White wins by both the crude'} 39. Rxb5 $18 (39. Rh1 Qe5 40. Rh8+ $1)) 39. g4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qh3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Again Black can't take the pawn because 40.Rh1 and the mate threat.'} (39... Qxg4 40. Rh1 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer'} Qd4 41. Rh8+ $1 Qxh8 42. Rb8+ $18 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 40. g5 Qf3 41. Re1+ Kf8 42. Rxb5 Kg7 43. Rb6 Qg3 44. Rd1 Qc7 45. Rdd6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qc8 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threat was Rg6+ Kh7 47.Rh6+ Kg7 48. Rbg6+ Kf8 49.Rh8+ and 50.Rh7+'} (45... Qe7 46. Rg6+ Kh7 47. Rh6+ Kg7 48. Rbg6+ Kf8 49. Rh8+ Kf7 50. Rh7+ $18) (45... Qc5 46. Rb7+ Kg8 47. Rd8+ $18) 46. b3 Kh7 47. Ra6 (47. Ra6 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threat of 48.Ra7 Kg8 49. Rdd7 has no answer'} Kg7 48. Ra7+ Kg8 49. Rdd7 $18) 1-0

