1. e4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) MIKHALCHISHIN DSZ} c5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = B36 - Sizilianisch-Maroczy Aufbau /Sicilian-Maroczy Bind'} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 ({Usually I play} 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Bc4 {, trying to reach a set-up similar to the Rauzer system. Since this 'visiting card' of mine was well known to the young Yugoslav master, who carefully prepares for every game, I decided to choose another continuation.}) 5... Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 {Mikhail Tal: 'I once analysed this variation with B. Gurgenidze, during preparations for a student event. The Georgian master used to adopt this continuation regularly, and not without success. The variation has also been thoroughly tested by Yugoslav players, and, in particular, B. Parma is one of the specialists on it.'} 8. Be2 (8. Bg5 $1 Bg7 9. Qd2) 8... Bg7 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 {Mikhail Tal: 'With his move'} Be6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black wants to emphasise that the position of the pawn on c4 also has its drawbacks.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'Gurgenidze prefers' Mikhail Tal: 'or' Mikhail Tal: 'It would be interesting to test the variation' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Gurgenidze prefers Mikhail Tal: 'or' Mikhail Tal: 'It would be interesting to test the variation''} 10... Ng4 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'so as to exchange off one of White's bishops. After 10...Ng4, play can go as follows: Bulletin: '!?''} 11. Bxg4 (11. Bd4 Bh6 12. Qd1 Ne5 {which occurred, for instance, in Cardoso-Tal (Portoroz 1958) and Tal-Gurgenidze (26th USSR Championship). In each case Black had no reason to complain about the results of the opening.}) 11... Bxg4 12. O-O (12. Bd4 {Mikhail Tal: 'which, as far as I know, has not yet occurred in serious tournament practice.'}) 12... Rc8 13. b3 b5 14. Nxb5 {Geller,E-Gurgenidze/URS-ch25/ Geller,E-Gurgenidze/URS-ch25/ Mikhail Tal: '(Geller-Gurgenidze, 25th USSR Championship) Bulletin: 'Geller,E-Gurgenidze/URS-ch25/'' MIKHALCHISHIN: '!?'}) 11. Rc1 Qa5 12. b3 {Mikhail Tal: 'White is in no hurry to castle, preferring for the moment to strengthen his c-pawn and to keep open the possibility of moving his knight, thus offering to transpose into an ending in which his king will still be in the centre.'} Rfc8 ({Mikhail Tal: 'More accurate was'} 12... a6 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'and if Bulletin: '!''} 13. O-O {Mikhail Tal: 'then'} (13. f3 {MIKHALCHISHIN} b5 14. Nd5 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2) 13... b5 {Mikhail Tal: '. After the text, however, the situation has changed: Black has used an extra move preparing the advance ...b5 and the absence of the rook has weakened his kingside. Now White's play is directed against the enemy king.'}) 13. O-O a6 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Diagram '} 14. f4 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'A natural move, and therefore I was astonished to discover that it was practically an innovation. Bulletin: '!'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'A natural move, and therefore I was astonished to discover that it was practically an innovation.''} ({In their games with Parma, Gligoric and Janoševic played} 14. Bf3 {which I consider to be inconsistent.}) 14... b5 15. f5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Bulletin: '!''} Bd7 16. fxg6 {Mikhail Tal: 'After'} hxg6 {Mikhail Tal: 'Following the opening of the f-file, it is clear that Black would be better off with his other rook on c8.' Mikhail Tal: 'the advance'} 17. c5 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'White is stealing up on the black knight. This move has one other significant virtue: Black's queen is not only unable to assist the development of his queenside attack, but is also a long way from his kingside. Bulletin: '!'' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'last book move Mikhail Tal: 'White is stealing up on the black knight. This move has one other significant virtue: Black's queen is not only unable to assist the development of his queenside attack, but is also a long way from his kingside.''} (17. e5 $5 {Mikhail Tal: 'was very tempting. After thinking for about half an hour, I rejected it because of Bulletin: '!?''} b4 18. exf6 (18. Na4 Ne4 19. Qd4 Bxa4 20. Qxe4 Bc6 21. Qf4 Qxe5 22. Qxf7+ Kh7) 18... bxc3 19. Rxc3 Bxf