1. e4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Marin,Mihail Marin,Mihail 1) Tal in Shakhmatyi (Riga) 1963. Reprinted and translated in 2)Kirillov: Michail Tal. Sein Lebenswerk 1962-1973.Chessgate. 2004. S. Band2,S.27-28} e5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = C93 - Spanisch-Smyslow Variante /Ruy Lopez-Smyslov Variation'} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 ({Marin,Mihail: 'Bronstein-Rojahn, Moscow Olympiad 1956, went'} 3. Bc4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} Nf6 4. Ng5 d5 5. exd5 Na5 6. d3 h6 7. Nf3 e4 8. dxe4 {Marin,Mihail: '!?'} Nxc4 9. Qd4 {Bronstein-Rojahn (11th Chess Olympiad, Moscow 1956), and subsequently the avalanche of white pawns swept away everything in its path. The idea of the sacrifice on move 19 in the game is roughly the same. White succeeds in setting up a solid pawn-roller, and at the first opportunity sets it in motion. Marin,Mihail: ', 'and subsequently the avalanche of white pawns swept away everything in its path. The idea of the sacrifice in the game is roughly the same.' (Tal)'}) 3... a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 Bf8 12. Nf1 Bd7 13. Ng3 Na5 14. Bc2 c5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'This position first occurred in the Tal-Smyslov game from the 29th USSR Championship. Since I had not the slightest desire to find out at what point Black's play could be improved (there could be no doubt that my opponent was acquainted with this game), I avoided 15 d5 here in favour of the rarely-played 15 b3.''} 15. b3 g6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'This reply is inaccurate because, firstly, it weakens the kingside, and secondly, the g6-square could have been used by a knight (after the manoeuvre ...Nc6-e7).''} ({Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Stronger was the immediate' Marin,Mihail: 'The move order relevant for our discussion is' Marin,Mihail: 'The move order relevant for our discussion is'} 15... Nc6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'as, incidentally, was played in the game Stein-Zilber from the 30th USSR Championship Semi-final.'} 16. d5 {Marin,Mihail} Ne7 17. Be3 {Marin,Mihail: 'and now 17...g6 would transpose to the game. Another neutral and not really accurate move is' and now 17...g6 would transpose to the game. Another neutral and not really accurate move is} Qc8 18. Qd2 Kh8 19. a4 Nfg8 20. axb5 axb5 21. Rxa8 Qxa8 {Marin,Mihail: 'The lack of coordination reigning in Black's camp allows the same combination as in the main game, but in an even more favourable form.' The lack of coordination reigning in Black's camp allows the same combination as in the main game, but in an even more favourable form.} 22. Bxc5 dxc5 23. Nxe5 Nf6 24. Nxf7+ Kg8 25. Nd6 Rb8 26. c4 $16 {Jansa,V-Geller,E/Budapest 1970/EXT 2009 (1-0, 83). Marin,Mihail: 'Jansa,V-Geller,E/Budapest 1970/EXT 2009 (1-0, 83). White has three pawns for a knight and a crushing space advantage.' White has three pawns for a knight and a crushing space advantage.}) 16. Be3 Nc6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'Black was worried about the positional threat of d5, cutting off his knight on a5.''} 17. d5 Ne7 18. Qd2 Kh7 {[#] Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'The somewhat disorganised state of the black pieces suggests to White the idea of a possible piece sacrifice.''} 19. Bxc5 $5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'last book move Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'In fact this sacrifice owes its existence to Bronstein, for it was on this very day that he told me about his game with Rojahn (see above).'' Marin,Mihail: 'This is thematic, very nice, but not necessary. Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: ''This sacrifice owes its appearance to Bronstein, since literally that same day he told me about his game with Rojahn (Moscow Olympiad 1956).' (Tal) But there is also a significant difference: with Bronstein it was the 'joke of a genius' (the sacrifice was incorrect), whereas with Tal it was completely justified play 'by position', which has become a typical procedure.'' Marin,Mihail: 'This is thematic, very nice, but not necessary.'} (19. a4) (19. c4) ({Marin,Mihail: 'Since Black has deprived his own knight of the g6-square, the plan based on' Since Black has deprived his own knight of the g6-square, the plan based on} 19. Nh2 {Marin,Mihail} Bg7 20. f4 {Marin,Mihail: 'is very dangerous. Black will have problems maintaining the control of the e5-square.' is very dangerous. Black will have problems maintaining the control of the e5-square.} exf4 21. Bxf4 c4 22. Nf3 Bc8 23. Kh1 Neg8 24. bxc4 bxc4 25. Rab1 $16 {Sigurjonsson,G-Smejkal,J/Raach 1969/EXT 2001 (1-0, 54)}) 19... dxc5 20. Nxe5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'White launches an attack'} Nc8 21. f4 (21. Nxf7 Qe7 22. Ng5+ hxg5 23. e5 Nxd5 24. Qxd5 Nb6 $11) 21... Qe7 22. c4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: '!'' Marin,Mihail: 'White has fantastic compensation for the sacrificed piece. All that Black can dream of is to return the piece for two pawns, but that is not easy to achieve. Garry Kasparov: '!'' Marin,Mihail: 'White has fantastic compensation for the sacrificed piece. All that Black can dream of is to return the piece for two pawns, but that is not easy to achieve.'} Bg7 (22... Nb6) 23. Nf3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'Hastily played.''} ({Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'After the preparatory' Marin,Mihail: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Here it was more accurate to play''} 23. Rad1 $1 {Tal Marin,Mihail: 'Garry Kasparov: '!''} Nd6 (23... Nb6 {Marin,Mihail} 24. Qa5 $44) 24. Bd3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Black would have been unable, as in the game, to give back the piece successfully.' Marin,Mihail: 'and Black cannot buy off his opponent by returning the piece.'}) 23... bxc4 ({Marin,Mihail: 'If'} 23... Nh5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ', and I was not against this since after' Marin,Mihail: '?!' Marin,Mihail: 'White intended'} 24. Nxh5 Bxa1 25. e5 $18 {Tal Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!' Marin,Mihail: '!' Marin,Mihail: 'with an irresistible attack;'} ({much stronger than Marin,Mihail: 'but not'} 25. Rxa1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Marin,Mihail: '?!'} gxh5 26. e5+ Kg8 27. Qd3 f5 {Marin,Mihail: '.'}) {White's attack is irresistible. The premature retreat of the knight allows Black to obtain counterplay.} 2