arin} Ne4 (14... Rc8 15. Rxc8 Qxc8 16. Qd3 Rd8 17. Qc4 Qxc4 18. Bxc4 Ne4 19. Bb5 h6 20. Nc6 Bxc6 21. Bxc6 Nc5 22. Bd4 f6 23. f4 Bd6 24. b4 Nd3 25. a3 e5 26. fxe5 Bxe5 27. Bb5 Rxd4 28. Rd1 Nb2 29. Rxd4 Bxd4 30. exd4 Kf7 31. Kf2 Ke6 32. Bc6 Kd6 33. Be4 Nc4 34. a4 a5 35. b5 Nb2 36. Bc2 Kd5 37. Ke3 Kc4 38. Ke4 Kc3 39. d5 Kxc2 40. d6 Nxa4 41. Kd5 Nc5 42. Kc6 a4 43. Kxb6 Ne6 44. Kc6 a3 45. Kd7 Nd4 46. Ke8 Nxb5 47. d7 Nd6+ 48. Ke7 Nb7 {0-1 Farstad,G-Mogstad,O/Norway 1999/}) 15. Bf3 Rc8 16. Qe2 Rxc1 17. Rxc1 Qd7 18. Qb5 Rd8 19. Qe2 e5 20. Nc2 f6 21. g3 Qd2 22. Rd1 Qxd1+ 23. Qxd1 Rxd1+ 24. Bxd1 Ng5 25. h4 Nf3+ 26. Kf1 Kf7 27. Be2 Ke6 28. Bc4+ Bd5 29. Bxd5+ Kxd5 30. Ke2 Ke4 31. Na3 g5 32. hxg5 fxg5 33. Nc4 h5 34. Nd2+ Nxd2 35. Kxd2 h4 36. gxh4 gxh4 37. Ke2 Bd6 38. f3+ Kf5 39. Kf2 e4 40. Bc3 h3 41. f4 Bc5 42. Bd2 Kg4 43. b4 Be7 44. Bc3 Bh4+ 45. Kg1 Kf3 46. Bd4 Bg3 47. a4 Ke2 48. f5 Bd6 49. a5 bxa5 50. bxa5 a6 51. f6 Kd3 52. f7 Kc4 53. Bg7 Kd3 54. f8=Q Bxf8 55. Bxf8 Kxe3 56. Kh2 Kd3 57. Kxh3 e3 58. Kg3 Kc4 59. Be7 Kd3 60. Kf3 e2 61. Kf2 Kd2 62. Bb4+ Kd1 63. Ke3 e1=Q+ 64. Bxe1 Kxe1 65. Kd4 Kd2 66. Kc5 Kc3 67. Kb6 Kc4 68. Kxa6 Kc5 69. Kb7 Kb5 70. a6 Ka5 71. a7 Kb5 72. a8=Q Kc5 73. Qa4 Kd5 74. Qf4 Kc5 75. Qe4 Kb5 76. Qd4 Ka5 77. Kc6 Ka6 78. Qb6# {1-0 Watai,M-Mohammed,R/Yerevan 1996/}) 14... Qd5 (14... Qxd4 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} 15. Bxd4 Rfc8 16. a4 Bc6 17. Be5 Ne8 18. Rfd1 Bd5 19. Rxc8 Rxc8 20. Nxa7 Ra8 21. Rxd5 exd5 22. Nc6 Bf6 23. Bg3 $11 {Myers}) 15. Qxd5 Nxd5 16. Rfd1 Bc5 {Wells: '!?' Myers Wells Marin: '!?' Wells: 'Clearly the point at which Black's idea takes shape since this move makes limited sense except in the context of the coming 'sacrifice'. It is an interesting question to muse on whether this was in Tal's view the best defence objectively to neutralise Whites' slight pull, or whether this is about what Rowson calls 'keeping the third result in play'. There is no doubt - White's probability of error in the resulting ending is greater than in this dull position. On the other hand, Petrosian is not prime candidate for such considerations!'} (16... a6 $2 {Myers Wells Marin} 17. Nc7 Rac8 18. Bxa6 Bxa6 19. Nxa6 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 $16 {Myers}) (16... Rac8 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} 17. Bf3 $1 (17. Nxa7 $6 Ra8 18. Nc6 Bc5 $11) 17... Rxc1 (17... a6 $6 18. Nc7 $1 Rfd8 19. a4 $16) 18. Rxc1 a6 19. Nc7 Rc8 20. Rc4 $14 {Myers}) 17. Bf3 {Mikhalchishin: 'Diagram '} (17. Bd4 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} Bxd4 18. Rxd4 Rfd8 19. Rcd1 a6 20. Nc7 Rac8 21. Nxa6 $16 {Myers}) 17... Nxe3 {Myers Wells Marin: '!'} (17... a6 {Myers Wells Marin} 18. Nc7 $1 Rad8 (18... Nxc7 19. Bxb7 Rab8 20. Bf3 $16 {Marin}) 19. Bd4 $1 Bxd4 20. Rxd4 Rc8 21. Rdc4 $16 {Myers}) 18. Bxb7 (18. fxe3 $2 {Myers Wells Marin} Bxf3 19. gxf3 Bxe3+ 20. Kf1 Bxc1 21. Rxc1 Rac8 $15 {Marin}) 18... Nxd1 19. Rxd1 (19. Bxa8 $2 {Myers Wells Marin} Bxf2+ 20. Kf1 Nxb2 21. Bb7 Bc5 $17 {Myers}) 19... Rad8 {Wells: '!' Myers Wells Marin: '!' Wells: 'As usual - the exchange of the other rooks is crucial.'} 20. Nc3 (20. Rxd8 $2 {Myers Wells Marin} Rxd8 21. Kf1 Rd2 $19 {Marin}) 20... Rxd1+ (20... Bd4 $5 {(Myers Wells Marin) Marin}) 21. Nxd1 Rd8 22. Bf3 $11 {[%cal Gf3d1,Gd1b2]} Rd2 {Myers Wells Marin: 'Marin: The series of forced moves has come to an end, and both players have obtained what they wanted. In conditions of relative material equality, all the white pieces defend themselves perfectly, preventing any further progress by Black. On the other hand, Tal lproves that it is not so easy to drive the rook away from the second rank. Although from here until the end of the game Petrosian is the only player seeking winning chances, the fight will be marked by the sign of equality.'} 23. Kf1 {Wells: 'I already discussed this in detail in the main text. The rook is active but its scope has been successfully limited - the coordination between White's minor pieces is not unimpressive.'} a5 (23... Bxf2 {Mikhalchishin Myers Wells Marin: '?!'} 24. Bc3 Rc2 25. Be4 Rc1 26. Ke2 (26. Kxf2 {Wells} Rxd1 {[%csl Ya1,Yb2,Yc2,Yd2][%cal Ge4c2,Gc3d2,Gc3b2,Gd1a1,Gf2e2]} 27. Ke2 Rh1 {Myers Wells Marin: 'Wells'} (27... Rd8 $5 {Myers Wells Marin}) 28. h3 {is in my view not an improvement. See the main text. The issue - how much is the bishop pair worth in these endings?} (28. Be5 {Myers Wells Marin} Rc1 29. Bb8 f5 30. Bd3 a5 31. Bf4 Rc8 32. Be3 $16 {Myers})) 26... Bh4 $17 {[%cal Ge2d2,Gh4g5,Gg5f6] Myers Wells Marin: '='}) 24. Be2 e5 {Wells: '!' Myers Wells Marin: '!' Wells: 'I like this. The plan of ...Bd4 seems logical. White will be weak on the dark squares and lose the potentially stabilising effect of the third minor piece.' Myers Wells Marin: 'Wells: The plan of ...Bd4 seems logical. White will be weak on the dark squares and lose the potentially stabilising effect of the third minor piece.'} (24... a4 $5) 25. a4 ({As we shall see later in the column, avoiding facing the queen-side pawns makes good sense in general. Hence I would not be tempted by} 25. Bxe5 {Wells} Rxa2 $132 26. Bb2 {Myers Wells Marin} g6 27. Bd3 Ba3 28. Be5 Rd2 29. Be2 Bc5 30. g4 $5 Bd4 31. Bxd4 Rxd4 $132 {Myers}) (25. Ke1 $2 {Myers Wells Marin} Bb4 26. Bc3 $2 (26. Kf1 $8) 26... Rxd1+ $1 27. Kxd1 Bxc3 28. Bc4 Kf8 $17 {Myers}) 25... Bd4 26. Bxd4 (26. Bc1 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} Ra2 27. Ke1 Ra1 28. Kd2 Ra2+ 29. Kd3 f5 30. f3 Kf7 31. h3 g6 32. g3 $14 {Myers}) 26... exd4 27. Ke1 Ra2 {Myers Wells Marin: 'Marin: Black has achieved a lot: the rook feels very comfortable on the second rank, and the d-pawn is a potential danger for White, especially after the centralization of the black king. As compensation, Petrosian as obtained the blockading square d3 for his knight.'} 28. g4 Kf8 (28... g5 $5 {Myers Wells Marin}) 29. f4 Ke7 30. Nf2 Ra1+ 31. Kd2 Ra2+ 32. Ke1 (32. Kd3 $2 {Myers Wells Marin} Rb2 {Marin} 33. Nd1 Rxb3+ 34. Kxd4 Rb4+ 35. Bc4 Rxa4 36. Nc3 Ra3 37. h3 $11 {Myers}) 32... Ra1+ 33. Bd1 Ra2 34. h4 f6 35. h5 Ke6 (35... f5 $5 {Myers Wells Marin}) 36. Nd3 {Myers Wells Marin: '?!' Wells: 'This is the good part from White's standpoint. The problem - how to make progress without allowing counterplay.'} (36. Ne4 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} Rh2 (36... g6 37. hxg6 hxg6 38. Nd2 Kd6 39. Ke2 $16) 37. Nf2 f5 38. Bf3 fxg4 39. Bxg4+ Kd5 40. Ke2 $16 {Myers}) (36. Be2 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} Ra1+ 37. Kd2 f5 38. Bc4+ Kf6 39. g5+ Ke7 40. Bd3 $5 $16 {Myers}) 36... g6 37. hxg6 hxg6 38. Be2 (38. b4 $5 {Myers Wells Marin}) 38... Rc2 {Wells: '?!' Myers Wells Marin: '?!'} (38... g5 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} 39. fxg5 fxg5 40. Kd1 Kd5 41. Bf3+ Kd6 42. Kc1 Rh2 $1 $14 {Marin}) 39. Kd1 Rc3 40. Kd2 {Myers Wells Marin: '!'} g5 (40... Rxb3 $2 {Mikhalchishin Wells: '??'} 41. Nc5+ $1 bxc5 42. Bc4+ $18 {Myers Wells Marin: 'Marin'}) 41. fxg5 fxg5 42. Bd1 Kd5 43. Nb2 (43. Bc2 {Myers Wells Marin} Ke6 44. b4 axb4 45. Nxb4 Rg3 46. Bf5+ Kd6 47. Nd3 Rg2+ $11 {Myers}) 43... Rg3 44. Nc4 Kc5 (44... d3 $1 {Myers Wells Marin} 45. Kc3 Ke4 46. Nd2+ Ke5 47. Bf3 Kf4 48. Bd5 Rxg4 49. Kxd3 Rg1 50. Bc6 Rg3+ $11 {Myers}) 45. Ne5 Kd5 (45... Rg2+ {Myers Wells Marin} 46. Kd3 Rg1 47. Bf3 (47. Be2 Kd5 48. Nf7 Rg3+ $11) 47... Rb1 48. Kc2 Rg1 49. Nc4 Re1 50. Kd2 Rg1 $11 {Myers}) 46. Nf7 Rg2+ (46... Kc5 $5 {Myers Wells Marin} 47. Nxg5 Kb4 48. Ne4 Re3 49. Nf2 Rg3 50. Nd3+ Ka3 51. Ne1 Kb2 52. Nd3+ Ka3 $11 {Myers}) 47. Kd3 (47. Kc1 $6 {Mikhalchishin} Ke4 {Myers Wells Marin: '!'} (47... Rf2 $1 {Wells} 48. Nxg5 d3 49. Nf3 Ke4 50. Nd2+ Kd4 {gives sufficient counterplay.}) 48. Nxg5+ Kd3 49. Nf3 (49. Nf7 {Myers Wells Marin} Ra2 50. Kb1 Rd2 51. Kc1 Ra2 $11 {Myers}) 49... Kc3) 47... Rg3+ 48. Kd2 Rg2+ 1/2-1/2

