1. e4 d6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = B07_23 Pirc Vert 4.g5 / Pirc Def 4.g5'} 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Bg5 c6 5. Qd2 b5 6. Bd3 Bg7 7. Nf3 {Tal's setup features a mixture of positional and aggressive elements. Gufeld's reaction strongly neglects development, allowing White to take over the initiative. Fritz 5.00 (5s): '='} (7. f4 O-O (7... b4 8. Nce2 Qb6 9. Nf3 Bg4 10. O-O Nbd7 11. Kh1 O-O 12. f5 c5 13. e5 Bxf3 14. exf6 exf6 15. Rxf3 fxg5 16. Qxg5 Qd8 17. Qxd8 Raxd8 {½-½ Horvath,T-Pfleger,H/BL9596 1996/BL 90}) 8. Nf3 Nbd7 (8... Bg4 9. O-O Qb6 10. Ne2 Nbd7 11. Kh1 d5 12. e5 Ne4 13. Qe3 f6 14. Bh4 fxe5 15. fxe5 Nxe5 16. Bxe7 Rfe8 17. Nxe5 Bxe5 18. Bxe4 Rxe7 19. Qg5 Rae8 20. Qxg4 Bxd4 21. Bd3 Bxb2 22. Rab1 Bg7 23. Nf4 {Hugentobler,P-Uhoda,P/Lenk op 1991/TD 91\08/0-1 (36)}) 9. O-O (9. e5 b4 10. Ne2 Nd5 11. exd6 f6 12. dxe7 Qxe7 13. Bh4 N7b6 14. O-O Bh6 15. Ne5 Qd6 16. Nc4 Nxc4 17. Bxc4 Bg4 18. Rae1 Rae8 19. Ng3 Rxe1 20. Qxe1 Kg7 21. Ne4 Qe7 22. Nc5 Qxe1 23. Rxe1 Bxf4 {Loeffler,M-Bernard,C/Candas op 1996/EXP 51/½-½ (72)}) 9... Nb6 10. Rae1 b4 11. Nd1 c5 12. c3 bxc3 13. bxc3 cxd4 14. cxd4 Bb7 15. f5 d5 16. Bxf6 exf6 17. e5 Nc4 18. Qf4 Rc8 19. e6 Qe7 20. Qg4 g5 21. h4 h6 {Byrne,R-Gipslis,A/Sousse izt 1967/IZT/1-0 (41)}) (7. Nge2 e5 8. dxe5 dxe5 9. a4 b4 10. Nd1 a5 11. f3 Be6 12. b3 Nbd7 13. Nb2 O-O 14. Be3 Re8 15. Ng3 Nb8 16. Nf1 Nbd7 17. Qe2 Bf8 18. Rd1 Bc5 19. Nc4 Bd4 20. Bxd4 exd4 21. Nfd2 Bxc4 {Minosian,A-Gipslis,A/URS-chT 1988/TD 88\11/0-1 (40)}) (7. Bh6 Bxh6 8. Qxh6 b4 $14) 7... h6 $6 (7... Bg4) (7... O-O) 8. Be3 Ng4 $6 9. Bf4 e5 {There is no way back already.} 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. Bg3 {With his central pawn hanging, Black has to opt for concrete play in a moment when his queenside is completely undeveloped.} h5 12. h3 (12. Bh4 Qc7 $16) 12... h4 13. hxg4 hxg3 14. Rxh8+ Bxh8 15. Qh6 gxf2+ {After this forced sequence, all Black's pieces are passive (and some of them exposed) on the back rank. His main pride is the far advanced pawn, facing White with a difficult choice.} 16. Ke2 $5 {The most natural move, leaving the first rank available for the rook. Fritz 5.00 (5s): '='} (16. Kxf2 $2 {should certainly be avoided, as in some lines this would offer Black a saving tempo after ...Qb6+. Let us compare this with similar lines examined below:} Bf6 (16... Qf6 $14) 17. g5 Be7 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxe5+ Be6 20. Nxb5 $2 ({The other combination does not work either:} 20. Nd5 $2 cxd5 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 22. exd5 Qb6+ 23. Nd4 O-O-O $1 $19) 20... cxb5 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 22. Rd1 Qb6+ 23. Kf1 O-O-O $19) ({But it appears that it would have been better to avoid placing the king on the e-file:} 16. Kf1 $1 Bf6 (16... Qf6 {allows} 17. Bxb5 {The bishop is taboo in view of Nd5, leaving Black hanging, while} Bg7 {is met with} 18. Nd5 $1 $16 {anyway.}) 17. g5 Be7 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxe5+ Qe7 20. Qf4 Bg7 21. e5 Nd7 22. e6 $1 {This is effective because the e-pawn is not pinned.} Qxe6 23. Kxf2 $18) 16... Bf6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (16... Qf6 {justifies the decision regarding the king's placement due to} 17. Rh1 $1 ({With the king on e2,} 17. Bxb5 Bg7 18. Nd5 {does not work anymore due to} Qxf3+ $1 {with check!}) (17. g5 Qg7 18. Qh4 $11) 17... Bg7 18. g5 Bxh6 19. gxf6 Bf8 20. Nxe5 $16 {, with a crushing domination and soon an extra pawn.}) 17. g5 Be7 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxe5+ Be6 $2 {Gufeld fails to use his chance.} (19... Qe7 $1 {works much better now than in the similar line above:} 20. Qf4 (20. Qxe7+ Kxe7 21. Kxf2 Bg7 {Planning ...Nd7-e5, with reasonable compensation for the pawn.} 22. e5 Bg4 {offers Black reasonable play.}) 20... Bg7 21. Rh1 (21. e5 Nd7 22. e6 {runs into} Nc5 $1) 21... Nd7 22. Rh7 Kf8 {, and Black is out of immediate danger.}) 20. Nxb5 $5 {Thematic, but not the most effective. Fritz 5.00 (5s): '='} (20. Nd5 $1 $16 {would have been an improved version of the game. The difference is that after} cxd5 $2 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 {White could insert his e-pawn into the attack with} 22. exd5 $18) 20... cxb5 21. Bxb5+ Nd7 22. Rd1 {Black is under a double pin and his position is critical. Some readers may remember the Morphy opera theatre combination with a similar pin system. But there still is hope for Black.} Qe7 $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '+-'} (22... Be7 $2 {allows White to use the first rank with Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!?'} 23. Bxd7+ (23. Qh8+ Bf8 $18) 23... Bxd7 24. Rh1 $18) ({But Black could improve over this line with} 22... f1=Q+ $1 {, forcing White to obstruct the first rank with his king.} 23. Kxf1 Be7 24. Rd5 $1 {The only way to keep the pressure. Since Black cannot display too much activity at this stage, White uses the available time to control the fifth rank.} (24. Qh8+ Bf8 25. Bxd7+ Bxd7 {does not offer much, as if} 26. Ne5 $2 Bb5+ $1 {wins. The point is that the rook is hanging, so that} 27. c4 {does not work.}) 24... Rb8 {There is little else Black could do.} 25. Bxd7+ Bxd7 26. Qh8+ Bf8 27. Ne5 Rb7 (27... Bb5+ $2 28. c4 $18) 28. Ng4 {White has excellent compensation for the piece  (three pawns and attacking chances), but things are not entirely clear yet.} Qc8 29. Nf6+ Ke7 30. Nh7 {Threatening 31.Qe5+ Be6 32.Qd6+.} Be6 31. Nxf8 Rb8 $3 {The only defence against the threat Qf6+, which can now be met with ...Kxf8..} (31... Qxf8 $2 32. Qf6+ Ke8 33. Rd8#) (31... Bxd5 $2 32. Nxg6+ $1 {wins the queen.}) 32. Nxg6+ fxg6 33. Qf6+ Ke8 34. Qxg6+ {Black's position remains dangerous but by no means lost.}) 23. Rxd7 $1 {This retrieves the sacrificed material with interest. Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Threatening mate... how?'} (23. Kxf2 $6 Rc8 $11) 23... Bxd7 24. Bxd7+ Kxd7 (24... Kd8 {just transposes after} 25. Qa5+ Kxd7 26. Qd5+) 25. Qd5+ Kc7 26. Qxa8 $18 {White has won several pawns and his king is relatively safe.} Qc5 27. c3 Qc4+ 28. Kxf2 Bc5+ 29. Kg3 Qxa2 30. Qd5 Qxd5 31. exd5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'The passed pawn on d5 will become decisive later'} Kd6 (31... Bd6+ 32. Kg4 $18) 32. c4 Be3 33. b4 a6 34. Kg4 Bc1 35. Ne1 (35. Nd4 Be3 36. Nc6 Bd2 $18) 35... Be3 36. Nd3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'White intends c5'} Bd2 37. g3 Kc7 (37... a5 38. bxa5 Bxa5 39. Kf3 $18) 38. c5 Kd7 39. Ne5+ (39. c6+ Kc7 $18) 39... Kc8 40. d6 Bxb4 41. d7+ Kc7 42. Nc6 Kxd7 43. Nxb4 a5 44. Nd3 Kc6 45. Kf4 $18 1-0

