{The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario. R.Scherbakov: 'A classic game, which was the origin of the strong attacking concept connected with sacrificing the central pawn. Lev Polugaevsky crushed Mikhail Tal in brilliant style and almost without a single inaccuracy - it's certainly one of the best games in the whole history of chess!''' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Myers: This game is given as #58 (in date order) by Burgess/Nunn/Emms in "The World's Greatest Chess Games". /// Knaak: Ein Läuferopfer auf h7 in einer Partie zweier Topgroßmeister sieht man nicht aller Tage. Doch hier ergibt es sich logisch aus einer Eröffnungsvariante--kein Wunder, dass es in späteren Partien zur Wiederholung des Opfers kam. Doch die Bilanz für die Nachziehenden ist schrecklich.' Knaak,Rainer: 'Ein Läuferopfer auf h7 in einer Partie zweier Topgroßmeister sieht man nicht aller Tage. Doch hier ergibt es sich logisch aus einer Eröffnungsvariante - kein Wunder, dass es in späteren Partien zur Wiederholung des Opfers kam. Doch die Bilanz für die Nachziehenden ist schrecklich.'} 1. c4 {Krasenkow,Michal Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak Belov Krasenkow,Michal Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov'' Krasenkow,Michal Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov'' Belov Krasenkow,Michal Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov'' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak'} Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. d4 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 (6. e3 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'}) 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ (9... Qa5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'} 10. Rb1 $1 {'' !} Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 $16) 10. Qxd2 O-O {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Burgess: Early in 1969 Polugaevsky analysed this position together with Spassky, who was then preparing for his second match against Petrosian. Their work proved fruitful, and they devised a plan that is to this day considered a critical test of Black's resources. Spassky indeed scored a win against Petrosian, but it was left to Polugaevsky to demonstrate the main line of their analysis.'} 11. Bc4 Nc6 (11... Nd7 {Belov} 12. O-O Nf6 13. Rfe1 b6 14. a4 Bb7 15. Bd3 Rc8 16. a5 $14 {Yusupov,A-Elson,J/San Pisafort/1981/1:0 (67)}) 12. O-O b6 13. Rad1 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!''} Bb7 14. Rfe1 {Diagram [#] Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Diagram [#]''} Na5 {This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.''} ({The aforementioned game went} 14... Rc8 15. d5 {!} exd5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'} ({I assume that both Petrosian during the game and Tal when commenting, thought that} 15... Na5 {was dangerous in view of} 16. dxe6 {but this sems to lead to a draw: Knaak,Rainer: '(Polugaevsky,Lev) but this sems to lead to a draw:'} Qxd2 17. exf7+ Kh8 18. Nxd2 Nxc4 19. Nxc4 Rxc4 20. e5 Bc8 21. e6 Bxe6 22. Rxe6 Rc7 $11 {Marin,Mihail. However, White can transpose to the main game with 16.Bd3}) 16. Bxd5 $16 {Spassky,Boris - Petrosian,Tigran V, Moscow (m/5) 1969 - 7/488} Qc7 {Belov} (16... Na5 17. Qf4 Qc7 18. Qf5 Bxd5 19. exd5 Qc2 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 20. Qf4 Qxa2 21. d6 Rcd8 22. d7 Qc4 23. Qf5 h6 24. Rc1 Qa6 25. Rc7 b5 26. Nd4 Qb6 27. Rc8 Nb7 28. Nc6 Nd6 29. Nxd8 Nxf5 30. Nc6 {1-0 Spassky,B-Petrosian,T/Wch Moscow 1969/}) 17. Qg5 h6 18. Qg4 $14) (14... Ne7 {Belov} 15. d5 exd5 16. exd5 Nf5 $14) 15. Bd3 Rc8 {Diagram [#] Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Diagram [#]'' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Keres: This position is crucial to the evaluation of the whole variation. White, having completed his development and obtained a fine position, must now choose a plan. Up to now the usual move has been 16 f4, which is best answered by 16...f6, having in view a favorable endgame. But Polugaevsky comes up with a bold new idea: a surprising and interesting pawn sacrifice that also makes the game quite important theoretically.'} 16. d5 $1 $16 {The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration. Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration. R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'White has been trying many options here but this direct pawn push is certainly one of the best, if not the best.''' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'N!' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Keres: Polugaevsky has been moving quickly up to this point, and since this move, too, was made without long deliberation, we may conclude that he had prepared the entire variation at home especially for this important tournament. [The 37th USSR Championship was also an FIDE Zonal Tournment.] Polugaevsky later told journalists that the idea of the sacrifice had occurred to him some time before, during his joint analysis with Spassky while the latter was preparing for his World Championship match with Petrosian. This shows once again how important detailed home analysis is in modern tournament play. It's hard to imagine anyone making this sacrifice over the board when facing this position for the first time--at least not unless he was prepared to spend a lot of valuable time considering it.' Knaak,Rainer: 'würde zur Partie führen.'} ({Of course, keeping good control over the center, White can make some preparations first and still get the result in case of Black's inaccurate play. For example,} 16. h3 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} Rc7 $6 (16... Qd6 $5) (16... Qe7 $5) (16... h6 $5) 17. Qf4 Rd7 $6 18. d5 exd5 19. e5 g6 20. Ng5 Re7 21. Qh4 $36 {with a strong initiative, Luther - Aring, Ditzingen 2009.}) 16... exd5 {Knaak,Rainer: '?!' '?!' '?!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Schwarz darf dieses Bauernopfer nicht annehmen.'} (16... Qe7 $5 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '(R.Scherbakov) is also possible:''} 17. Qf4 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} (17. dxe6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev}) (17. Bb1 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} exd5 18. exd5 Qf6 19. Ng5 Rfd8 20. Qd3 g6 21. Ne4 Qg7 22. Qf3 f5 23. Ng5 Qf6 24. h4 Rd6 25. a4 h6 26. Re6 Rxe6 27. Nxe6 Qxh4 28. Bxf5 Qf6 29. Bg4 Qxf3 30. gxf3 h5 31. Bh3 Rb8 32. Kg2 Nc4 33. d6 Ne5 34. Kg3 Bc6 35. f4 Nd7 36. Rd4 Nc5 37. f5 gxf5 38. Bxf5 Kf7 39. Nxc5 bxc5 40. Rf4 Rb3+ {½-½ Kievelitz,B-Demidenko,V/corr ICCF 1987/}) 17... Rfd8 18. h4 h6 19. h5 Qf6 20. Qg4 {with the better chances for White, Sasikiran - Golod, Ajaccio 2006.}) ({In case of} 16... Nc4 $6 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) White could have won a pawn by} 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. dxe6 Qxd2 19. exf7+ Rxf7 20. Rxd2 {with reasonable winning chances as} Rxe4 $2 {was impossible due to} 21. Rxe4 Bxe4 22. Ng5 {'+-'} Re7 23. Rd8+) (16... Qd6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) is worth considering, but it cannot completely solve the problems. In the game Dokhoian - Webb, EU Cup 1989 White opted for} 17. dxe6 $5 ({the tempting} 17. e5 $2 {failed to} Qxd5 18. Qf4 (18. Bxh7+ $2 Kxh7 19. Qg5 Qc5 $1 (19... Qc6 $2 20. Rd4 $1 $40) 20. Qh5+ (20. Rd4 Bxf3 $19) 20... Kg8 21. Ng5 Qc2 $19) 18... Qc5 ({even} 18... h6 $5 {!} 19. Bh7+ Kxh7 20. Rxd5 Bxd5 {was quite good for Black} 21. a3 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} Rc4 22. Qd2 Rfc8 23. Nd4 R4c5 24. f3 Nc4 25. Qd3+ Kg8 26. f4 Na5 27. f5 Rc3 28. Qb5 Nc6 29. fxe6 fxe6 30. Ne2 Rb3 31. Qa6 Rf8 32. Rf1 Rxf1+ 33. Kxf1 Nxe5 34. Qc8+ Kh7 35. Qc2+ Kg8 36. Qc8+ Kh7 37. Qc2+ Kg8 38. Qc8+ {½-½ Veen,H-Baumgartner,U/corr ICCF 1991/}) 19. Ng5 h6 20. Nh7 Rfd8 21. Nf6+ Kh8 {and White cannot create any real threats}) ({but other options, such as} 17. Qg5 $5 {were playable for White}) (17. Qe2 {Belov} e5 $14) 17... Qxe6 (17... fxe6 $5 {deserved attention} 18. Bb5 $1 $14 {Belov}) 18. Nd4 Qe5 19. Nf5 g6 20. Nh6+ Kh8 21. Ng4 Qe6 22. Qf4 f6 23. Bb5 $1 {and White has obtained a clear advantage.}) ({Black could also pay more attention to} 16... Rc5 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov}) ({but perhaps the most reliable way was} 16... h6 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 17. Qf4 Rc5 18. Nd4 exd5 19. e5 Bc8 $1 {- see the game Ponomariov - Vallejo Pons/Donostia 2009.}) 17. e5 $1 {Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. R.Scherbakov: 'This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.''' Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. R.Scherbakov: 'This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.''' Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern. R.Scherbakov: 'This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.''' Belov: '' '' '' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Keres: This is the point of the sacrifice. After giving up a pawn, White simply continues positionally, securing the strong central square d4 for his knight and concentrating his pieces against the enemy king. There are no direct threats: White threatens merely to strengthen his position gradually and eventually to launch a dangerous attack on the king. As experience shows, sacrifices like this are not easy for the defender to meet. And in fact Tal does lose this game--not, however, because his position is indefensible but because in the limited time available he cannot solve the complicated problems of the defense over the board.'} (17. exd5 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} Qxd5 18. Bxh7+ Kxh7 19. Qxd5 Bxd5 20. Rxd5 Rc2 21. a4 a6 22. Rd7 $11 {Myers}) 17... Nc4 {Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time. R.Scherbakov: 'A natural reply.''' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!?' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Keres: The most likely answer, which can hardly be criticized, since the badly placed knight has to be brought back into play. It isn't easy to recommend a suitable defense for Black; in all variations White gets some kind of compensation.' Knaak,Rainer: 'Mit 17...d4 wurde zweimal ein Remis geschafft, aber die Analyse ergibt forciert klaren Vorteil für Weiß:'} (17... g6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev'} 18. Qh6 $16 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Polugaevsky'} f5 19. exf6 Qxf6 (19... Rxf6 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 20. Ng5 Qd7 21. Re2 Qg7 22. Qxg7+ Kxg7 23. Re7+ Kg8 24. Rxh7 Rcf8 25. Re1 R6f7 26. Rxf7 Rxf7 27. Nxf7 Kxf7 28. Re5 Nc4 29. Rg5 b5 30. h4 a5 31. Rxg6 b4 32. Bc2 a4 33. h5 Bc8 34. h6 Ne5 35. Rg7+ {1-0 Muck,P-Wilk,A/corr POL 1993/}) 20. Ng5 $18) ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Black tried many possibilities. Let's briefly take a look at them:''} 17... d4 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} 18. Ng5 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?''} (18. Nxd4 $14 {Krasenkow,Michal Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!?'} Nc4 $5 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} (18... g6 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 19. Qh6 Qe7 20. Nf5 gxf5 21. Bxf5 f6 22. exf6 Rxf6 23. Rxe7 {1-0 Dourerassou,J-Perman,E/Chalkidiki 2003/}) (18... Kh8 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 19. Qf4 Rc3 20. Re3 Qd5 21. Rg3 Rfc8 22. Qg5 Rg8 23. Rh3 {1-0 Kanep,M-Soot,M/Tallinn EST 2003/}) 19. Qe2 $1 (19. Qc2 Ne3 $1 20. Bxh7+ Kh8 21. Rxe3 Rxc2 22. Bxc2 g6 23. Rh3+ Kg8 24. Rg3 Bd5 25. Nb5 $1 Qc8 $1 26. Rc3 $14 {Myers}) 19... Qd5 20. Nf3 Qc5 21. Ng5 h6 22. Ne4 Qc6 23. Rc1 b5 $8 24. Qg4 Nxe5 25. Qf5 Nxd3 26. Rxc6 Rxc6 27. Ng5 hxg5 28. Qxd3 $16 {Myers}) 18... h6 19. Nh7 Nc4 (19... Re8 $2 20. Nf6+ gxf6 21. Qxh6 $18) 20. Qf4 {Myers Bu