, Budapest 1969} Rf7 27. Qf5+ Kg8 28. Qe6 gxf6 29. Rg3+ Kh8 30. h6 Ne5 31. f4 Re7 32. Rg8+ Qxg8 33. Qxf6+ Kh7 34. Qxe7+ Nf7 35. Qxb7 Qf8 36. Re7 Kxh6 37. Rxf7 {1-0 Flesch, J-Kovacs, L/Budapest 1969 Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '1-0 Flesch,J-Kovacs,L/Budapest HUN 1969/'}) 22... Kh6 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. e6 Rcxf7 25. exf7 {with a decisive advantage.}) (18... g6 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!?'} 19. Qh6 Qd7 (19... f5 $8 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 20. Nd4 Qe7 21. e6 Nd6 22. Qf4 Ne4 23. f3 Nc3 24. Ra1 a6 $11 {Myers}) 20. Ng5 f6 21. exf6 Rxf6 22. Bxc4 Rxc4 23. Nxh7 {!}) (18... Qe7 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 19. Bxh7+ Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 21. h4 Rce8 22. h5+ Kh6 23. Ne4+ Kh7 24. Qf5+ Kg8 25. Nf6+ gxf6 26. exf6 Qxe1+ 27. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 28. Kh2 Rc8 29. Qg5+ Kf8 30. h6 Ke8 31. h7 Kd7 32. Qg4+ Re6 {0-1 Pietrzak,T-Jaskula,E/corr POL 1995/}) {Diagram [#] Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'Diagram [#]''} 19. Bxh7+ $1 {The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work. R.Scherbakov: 'Striking just in time!''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Das bekannte Läuferopfer auf h7 sieht man selten zwischen Weltklasseleuten, aber hier liegt der Erfolg keineswegs auf der Hand.' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Knaak: Das bekannte Läuferopfer auf h7 sieht man selten zwischen Weltklasseleuten, aber hier liegt der Erfolg keineswegs auf der Hand.'} ({A faint-hearted} 19. Rd2 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} Nxd3 20. Rxd3 Rc4 (20... Re8 $5 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak}) 21. Nd4 Qe7 {was okay for Black.} 22. h3 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} Bc8 23. Qe3 Re8 24. f4 f5 25. e6 g6 26. Qe5 Qc7 27. Qxc7 Rxc7 28. g4 {½-½ Heasman,M-Cooper,D/corr NZL 1997/}) 19... Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '' Knaak,Rainer: 'Natürlich.'} (20... Kg8 $2 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} 21. Qh4 Re8 (21... Qxg5 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} 22. Qxg5 Nxd1 23. Rxd1 {was clearly insufficient for Black.} Rc2 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 24. Qe7 Bc6 25. Qxa7 Ra8 26. Qxb6 Raxa2 27. h3 Rab2 28. Qd8+ Kh7 {1-0 Graeser,K-Eickhoff,H/corr GER 1986/}) 22. Qh7+ (22. e6 $1 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev !} fxe6 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. Qh8+ Ke7 25. Qxg7+ Kd6 26. Nf7+ $18) 22... Kf8 23. e6 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '+-' würde 24.e7+! drohen.}) 21. h4 $3 {According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#. Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#.'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#.' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#. R.Scherbakov: 'This cool pawn advance is the only way to maintain the initiative. Now Black has to parry a mating threat h4-h5.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '22.h5 h5 23.g4 g6 25.f5 h6 26.h7 g5 27.h5 f4 28.f5#/' ! 'with the idea' 22.h5! Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qh5 Kf4 27.Qf5# Polugaevsky Krasenkow,Michal: '22.h5 h5 23.g4 g6 25.f5 h6 26.h7 g5 27.h5 f4 28.f5#/' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Polugaevsky: This is the point of the combination! Of course to find the whole of the subsequent lengthy variation was possible only with prepared analysis. I think it was only here that Tal realized that he was battling under unequal conditions, but there was already no way out.' Knaak,Rainer: 'mit der Drohung 22.h5+ K:h5 23.g4+ Kg6 25.Df5+ Kh6 26.Dh7+ K:g5 27.Dh5+ Kf4 28.Df5#.  Der Textzug und sogar noch 25.e6! waren von Polugaevsky vor der Partie analysiert worden. Die Stellung kam später noch mehrfach aufs Brett - aus schwarzer Sicht wohl immer unwissend. Die Korrektheit des Opfers konnte dabei nie in Zweifel gezogen werden.' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Knaak: Der Textzug und sogar noch 25.e6! waren von Polugaevsky vor der Partie analysiert worden. Die Stellung kam später noch mehrfach aufs Brett--aus schwarzer Sicht wohl immer unwissend. Die Korrektheit des Opfers konnte dabei nie in Zweifel gezogen werden. '} Rc4 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'The best and probably the only defence!'''} ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'or'''} 21... f6 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '??'''} 22. h5+ {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!'''} Kxh5 23. g4+ Kh6 (23... Kg6 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 24. Qf5+ Kh6 25. Nf7+ $1 Rxf7 26. Qh5#) 24. Qh2+ $18 Kxg5 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 25. Qh5+ Kf4 26. Qf5# {Polugaevsky}) ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'other possible defences failed as well:''' Knaak,Rainer: 'Auf'} 21... f5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '?'''} 22. Rd4 $1 $18 {Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!' '+-!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: '(with the idea h4-h5!)''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!' '+-!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: '(with the idea h4-h5!)''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!' '+-!' Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: '(with the idea h4-h5!)''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' Belov: '!' '!' Belov: 'h5/' ! 'with the idea' 23.h5 'with the idea' 23.Qg3 Polugaevsky Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'droht 23.h5+'} (22. h5+ {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} Kxh5 23. Ne6 Qe7 24. Qh2+ Qh4 $132 {Keres}) 22... Qe7 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} (22... Rh8 $2 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 23. g4 $1 $18) 23. Re3 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-'' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!' verstärkt Weiß in aller Ruh