til move 25") he had stopped here. Of course, excellent intuition is needed to do that, as White still is a piece down, but Polugaevsky rightly evaluated that the e-pawn has a decisive strength. ***To the young modern player, Polugaevsky's analysis may not seem so impressive. But we have to take into account that nowadays one only has to push the engines a bit to work out such an analysis or longer. In 1969, a chess playing computer existed only in the dreams of such visionaries as Botvinnik.'' Belov: '+-!' '+-!' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Burgess: Polugaevsky had been analysing this position before the game, and had predicted to Grandmaster Efim Geller that it would occur in Polugaevsky's game that very day! Geller was understandably astonished when this prediction came true.'} (25. h6 $2 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) (R.Scherbakov) failed to} Rxf7 26. h7+ Kh8 27. Qxf7 Nxd1 (27... Rc7 $5 {was also good: !} 28. e6 $5 {!?} Rxf7 29. exf7 Kxh7 30. Re8 (30. Rc1 d4 $1 {!} 31. Re8 Qd5) 30... Qf6 31. f8=Q Qxf8 32. Rxf8 Nxd1 33. Rf7 Bc6 34. Rxa7 {with some drawish chances =}) 28. e6 Bc6 {and Black parries all the threats:} (28... Rc8 29. Qxb7 $18) 29. Rxd1 Rc3 $5 30. Rd4 Rc1+ 31. Kh2 Rc4 {and so on.}) 25... Qf6 {Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'The only move.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '' '' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'The only move.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'The only move.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '' Belov: '' '' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: ''} ({Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: 'In other cases the pawn push h5-h6 was decisive, for example:'''} 25... Qe7 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '?''' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '?'} 26. h6 $1 $18 {Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!' '+-!'' '+-' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!'} Rh4 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov''} 27. Rd4 $18 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '+-!'' mit durchschlagendem Angriff.} Rxf7 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 28. exf7+ Qxf7 29. Qxf7+ Kxf7 30. Rxh4 $18 {Polugaevsky}) (25... Nxd1 $2 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 26. e7 $1 Qc8 27. exf8=Q+ Qxf8 28. Nh6+ gxh6 29. Qg6+ Kh8 30. Re8 $18 {Myers}) (25... Rc7 $2 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 26. h6 $1 Rcxf7 27. exf7+ Rxf7 28. h7+ Kh8 29. Qxf7 Kxh7 30. Re5 $1 $18 {Morovic Fernandez}) 26. Qxf6 gxf6 27. Rd2 {Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'This move has been evaluated as "dubious" in various comments but in fact it's simply the best.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'This move has been evaluated as "dubious" in various comments but in fact it's simply the best.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov: '!' R.Scherbakov: 'This move has been evaluated as "dubious" in various comments but in fact it's simply the best.''' Krasenkow,Michal: '!' Belov: '?!' '?!' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '!' Knaak,Rainer: 'Nach der Partie wurde' Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: 'Keres: After all the preceding turbulence, White makes a quiet move intending to regain his piece and go into an endgame with an extra pawn. In view of White's many threats, such as 28 xb2, 28 d6, 28 e6-e7, etc, Black obviously can't keep his material advantage. The question is: What's the best way to return the extra material so as to end up with a more or less acceptable position? This is by no means an easy question, and Tal is hardly to be blamed for not finding the best answer during the game. There are many possible variations, and they are all complicated; even in later analysis it was no easy matter to determine what Black should have played.'} (27. Nd6 $5 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?' '!?'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?' '!?'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!?' '!?'' Belov: '+-' '+-' Knaak,Rainer: 'als noch besser gefunden, aber als diese Variante 24 (!) Jahre später in der Partie Naumkin-Nevanlinna tatsächlich aufs Brett kam - sicherlich unwissend - gelang es Weiß nicht zu gewinnen und auch die Analysen zeigten keinen Vorteil auf.'} Nxd1 ({surely not} 27... Rc6 $2 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) R.Scherbakov} 28. Rxd5 $18 Rc7 29. Nxb7 Rxb7 30. Rd7 $1 {!} Rbb8 31. Re3 $18) 28. e7 Rc1 $1 {Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!'' Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: '!' '!''} 29. h6 {Knaak,Rainer Knaak,Rainer: 'Polugaevsky,Lev: 'R.Scherbakov'' ! 'with the idea' 30.efQ Kf8 31.Re8# 'with the idea' 30.h7$18} Rb8 (29... Rfc8 $5 {(Knaak,Rainer) (Polugaevsky,Lev) deserves attention:} 30. Nxc8 Bc6 $1 {Krasenkow,Michal: '' ''} 31. Nd6 Kh7 32. e8=Q Bxe8 33. Nxe8 Kxh6 34. Nxf6 d4 $132 {with good counter chances}) 30. Nxb7 Rcc8 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak: '?'} (30... Re8 {(Knaak,Rainer) Polugaevsky,Lev} 31. Nd6 Rxe7 32. Rxe7 Ne3+ 33. Kh2 Ng4+ 34. Kg3 Nxh6 35. Rxa7 $14 {with only a slight edge for White in the game Naumkin - Nevanlinna, Jyvaskyla 1993. Krasenkow,Michal: '1/2 (58) Naumkin,I (2450)-Nevanlinna,R (2290) Jyvaskyla 1993'} Rc5 {Myers Burgess Keres Belov Polugaevsky Knaak} 36. Rd7 Rc6 37. Kf4 Rc2 38. f3 Rxg2 39. Ne8 Rxa2 40. Nxf6+ Kf8 41. Kg5 Nf7+ 42. Kf5 d4 43. f4 Kg7 44. Ne4 Kg8 45. Rxd4 Ra7 46. Kf6 b5 47. Nc5 Ra8 48. Ne6 Rb8 49. Rd7 Nh6 50. Kg6 Ng4 51. Kg5 Nf2 52. f5 b4 53. Kf4 Rb6 54. Re7 Nd3+ 55. Kg5 b3 56. f6 Ne5 57. Re8+ Kf7 58. Re7+ Kg8 {½-½ Naumkin,I-Nevanlinna,R/Jyvaskyla 1993/}) 31. Nd6 Re8 32. Nxe8 {Myers 