{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'What happens when a genius who attacks at any cost, meets a genius who is willing to suffer any indignity for material? Strangely enough, Tal was a regular customer of Korchnoi's, and accrued an abysmal lifetime score against him, mainly through games like this, where Tal's normally magical attacks, which worked against everyone else, just grew ill, coughed and died against his Laskerian opponent. Furthermore, Tal's tactics never seemed to work either on the computer-like number-crunching Korchnoi, who actually once had the colossal nerve to call Tal a weak calculator! '} 1. e4 {Myers Marin Kotov: 'Myers Marin Kotov'} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's favourite move in this position.'} ({His style isn't suited to the Karpovian} 5. exd5 exd5 {(Korchnoi's favourite response), when Black gets freedom, in exchange for a future isolated d-pawn.}) 5... cxd4 (5... dxe4 6. Nxe4 Bd7 7. Bg5 $1 Qa5+ 8. Nc3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bb4 10. O-O Bxc3 11. bxc3 Qxc3 $2 {(only Korchnoi could fall behind in development, and go pawn grabbing against Tal and get away with it). EXERCISE (combination alert): After Tal's next shot, his attack grew out of control. Can you find it? ANSWER: Line opening sac:} 12. Nf5 $3 exf5 13. Re1+ Be6 14. Qd6 $1 a6 15. Bd2 $1 {(the deep point behind the sac – Tal transfers his bishop to the a3-f8 diagonal with deadly effect)} Qxc2 16. Bb4 $1 axb5 17. Qf8+ Kd7 18. Red1+ Kc7 19. Qxa8 {1-0 M.Tal-W.Uhlmann, Moscow 1971.}) ({Tal had greater success against other moves:} 5... a6 6. exd5 axb5 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Qe2 Nf6 10. Nb3 Bb4+ 11. c3 Bd6 (11... Be7 {prevents future Nf5 tricks}) 12. Nbd4 Qc7 13. Nf5 Bf4 14. Bxf4 Qxf4 15. N5d4 Bd7 $6 ({Houdini suggests} 15... Ba6 $5 {and if} 16. Nxc6 {then} b4 17. Qe5 Qxe5+ 18. Nfxe5 bxc3 19. bxc3 Ne4 {, when Black may have enough compensation for the pawn}) 16. Ne5 {and Tal managed to impose a dark square bind, M.Tal-R.Debarnot, Las Palmas 1975.}) 6. Nxd4 Bd7 7. Nxc6 $6 (7. Bxc6 {!?} bxc6 8. c4 $14) 7... bxc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Shouldn't Black recapture with his bishop, following the principle: The cramped side should seek exchanges. ANSWER: Very true, but by recapturing with the pawn, Black gains something else: A tempo – which is also a valuable commodity when you lag behind in development. Actually both moves are playable.'} ({For example:} 7... Bxc6 {?} 8. Bxc6+ {!} bxc6 9. c4 Bd6 (9... Nf6 10. Qa4 Qd7 11. e5 $16) 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. exd5 exd5 12. O-O Ne7 13. Nf3 O-O 14. Qd3 Qd7 15. Rd1 Rfd8 16. Be3 {with just a tiny edge to White, who can play on the d4-square and Black's isolani, V.Topalov-G.Kamsky, Sofia 2009.}) 8. Bd3 Qc7 (8... e5 9. O-O Bd6 10. c4 d4 11. f4 Nf6 12. h3 Qc7 13. Nf3 $16) 9. Qe2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'intends to meet'} Ne7 ({In a later game Korchnoi played} 9... Bd6 10. Nf3 dxe4 11. Bxe4 (11. Qxe4 Nf6 12. Qh4 Qa5+ 13. Bd2 Qh5 14. Qxh5 Nxh5 15. O-O-O $16 {Adorjan - Webb, London, 1975}) 11... Nf6 12. O-O Rb8 13. Re1 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 f6 15. b3 O-O 16. Bd2 c5 17. Rad1 Rf7 18. h3 Bf8 19. Bf4 e5 20. Nxe5 fxe5 21. Bxe5 Qc8 22. Bxb8 Qxb8 {, when the bishop pair allowed Black to face the future with confidence, J.Timman-V.Korchnoi, London 2009.}) (9... Nf6 $6 {with} 10. e5 {, embarrassing the knight.}) 10. Nf3 Ng6 11. e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The e-pawn crosses into Black's territory. White seeds his ambitions upon a future kingside attack, while Black relies on his strong centre and open b-file to generate counter-chances.' ?!} (11. O-O {Myers Marin Kotov: '!?'} Be7 (11... Bd6 12. Re1 Nf4 (12... Rb8 13. c4 O-O 14. e5 Bb4 15. Rd1 Rfe8 16. h4 h6 17. h5 Nf8 18. Nh2 Bc8 19. Ng4 Be7 20. b3 Qd8 21. Nxh6+ gxh6 22. Qg4+ Bg5 23. f4 f5 24. Qg3 Nh7 25. fxg5 hxg5 26. Qf2 Qc7 27. Re1 Qf7 28. g4 Rb7 29. Ba3 Nf8 30. Rf1 f4 31. Bd6 Rd8 32. c5 Kg7 33. Qd2 Qe8 34. Rf3 Rf7 35. a4 Qd7 36. b4 a6 37. Qb2 Bb7 38. Qd4 Kh6 39. Bc2 Ra8 40. Kg2 Qd8 41. Rfa3 Rg7 42. Kf2 Qe8 43. Qd3 Nh7 44. R1a2 Nf8 45. b5 axb5 46. axb5 Rxa3 47. Rxa3 cxb5 48. Ra7 Nh7 49. Rxb7 {1-0 Tatai,S-Campos Lopez,M/Camaguey CUB 1974/}) 13. Bxf4 Bxf4 14. c4 O-O 15. g3 Bh6 16. e5 $14) (11... Rb8 12. c4 dxe4 13. Bxe4 Be7 14. Ng5 f5 15. Bc2 Bxg5 16. Bxg5 Rxb2 17. Rfe1 O-O 18. Qd3 h6 19. Be3 e5 20. Bb3 c5 21. Qc3 Rxb3 22. axb3 f4 23. Bd2 Bc6 24. f3 Qd7 25. Qc2 Nh4 26. Bc3 Bxf3 27. gxf3 Nxf3+ 28. Kf2 Nxe1 29. Rxe1 Qh3 30. Kg1 Rf5 31. Re2 Rg5+ 32. Rg2 Rxg2+ 33. Qxg2 Qxc3 34. Qd5+ Kh7 35. Qe4+ g6 36. Qb7+ Kg8 37. Qd5+ Kg7 38. Qe6 Qe1+ 39. Kg2 Qe2+ 40. Kg1 h5 41. Qe7+ Kh6 42. Qxc5 Kg5 43. Qxa7 f3 44. h4+ Kxh4 45. Qf2+ Kg4 46. Qxe2 fxe2 47. Kf2 h4 48. Kxe2 h3 49. Kf2 e4 {0-1 Tur Bonet,A-De Rivera,D/corr ESP 1985/}) 12. c4 (12. Re1 O-O 13. Bd2 Rab8 14. b3 Rfe8 15. e5 c5 16. c3 Bc6 17. h4 c4 18. Bc2 cxb3 19. axb3 Bb5 20. Qd1 Nf8 21. h5 Bc5 22. Nd4 Bxd4 23. cxd4 Rec8 24. Rc1 Qe7 25. Qg4 Nd7 26. Bg5 Qf8 27. Re3 Kh8 28. Rg3 h6 29. Be7 Be2 30. Qh4 Qe8 31. Bf6 gxf6 32. Qf4 Qf8 33. exf6 Bxh5 34. Rg7 Qxg7 35. fxg7+ Kxg7 36. g4 Bg6 37. Bxg6 Kxg6 38. Rc7 Nf6 39. Qc1 Rxc7 40. Qxc7 Rb4 41. Qc3 a5 42. f3 h5 43. Qd3+ Kh6 44. Qe3+ Kg6 45. g5 Ne8 46. f4 Nd6 47. Kh2 {½-½ Caprano-Petrillo,M/corr ASIGC 1991/}) 12... dxe4 (12... dxc4 13. Bxc4 O-O 14. Be3 c5 15. Ne1 Bc6 16. f3 Rab8 17. Nd3 Ne5 18. Bf4 Bd6 19. Nxe5 Bxe5 20. Bxe5 Qxe5 21. b3 Rfd8 22. Rad1 Rd4 23. Rd2 Rbd8 24. Rfd1 Kf8 25. Rxd4 cxd4 26. Bd3 Qc5 27. Rd2 e5 28. Rc2 Qb6 29. Kh1 g5 30. h3 h6 31. Rc1 Rd6 32. Qc2 Ke7 33. Qc5 f6 34. Qxb6 axb6 35. g4 Bd7 36. Kg2 b5 37. Rc7 Kd8 38. Rc2 b4 39. Kf2 Rb6 40. Ke2 Bc6 41. Bc4 Kd7 42. Kd3 Kd6 43. Rh2 Bb5 44. Bxb5 Rxb5 45. h4 Rc5 46. hxg5 Rc3+ 47. Kd2 hxg5 48. Rf2 Kc5 49. Rf1 d3 50. Ke3 d2+ 51. Kxd2 Kd4 52. Rf2 Rd3+ 53. Ke2 Kc3 54. Ke1 Rd8 55. Rh2 Ra8 56. Rd2 Rh8 57. Rd6 Rh1+ 58. Ke2 Rh2+ 59. Ke3 Rxa2 60. Rxf6 Kxb3 61. Rf5 Ra5 62. Kd2 Ka2 63. Rxg5 b3 64. Rg8 b2 65. Rb8 b1=Q 66. Rxb1 Kxb1 67. Kd3 Kb2 {0-1 Abu Rab,Z-Tobies,R/corr GER 1991/}) 13. Bxe4 f5 (13... Rd8 14. Re1 c5 15. Ng5 Kf8 16. Qf3 f6 17. Bc2 Nh4 18. Nxe6+ Bxe6 19. Qh5 Bxc4 20. Qxh4 Bf7 21. Be3 Bd6 22. b3 h5 23. f4 