Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Here he comes now. The players engage in radically divergent hopes and opposing primal energies soon collide. Tal begins the process of probing Black's defensive barrier.' Myers Marin Kotov: '!?'} (15. b3 { d2, ad1=  d2, ad1=  Bd2, Rad1= Myers Marin Kotov: '!?'} Rfd8 (15... dxc4 16. Bxc4 Bc6 17. Ng5 Bxg5 18. Bxg5 h6 19. Bd2 Nh4 20. f3 Rfd8 21. Rad1 g5 22. Qf2 Qb7 23. Ba5 Rxd1 24. Rxd1 Rc8 25. Rd6 Nf5 26. Rxe6 fxe6 27. Bxe6+ Kg7 28. Bxf5 Rf8 29. Bg4 c4 30. h4 cxb3 31. axb3 h5 32. Bxh5 Qd7 33. Qe3 Qd1+ 34. Kh2 Rf4 35. Bg4 Qd5 36. Qd2 Qxe5 37. Bc3 Rd4+ 38. Kh3 Rxd2 39. Bxe5+ Kg6 40. hxg5 Kxg5 41. Kg3 {½-½ Heisch-Wagner/corr DDR 1989/}) 16. Bg5 (16. Ng5 dxc4 17. Bxc4 Be8 18. f4 Bxg5 19. fxg5 Bc6 20. Bd3 Rb4 21. Bxg6 hxg6 22. Bb2 Re4 23. Qc2 Rg4 24. Re2 Qb7 25. Rae1 Bb5 26. Rd2 Rxd2 27. Qxd2 c4 28. h3 Re4 29. Rxe4 Qxe4 30. bxc4 Qxc4 31. Ba3 Qf1+ 32. Kh2 Bc4 33. Bd6 Bd5 {½-½ Goebel,W-Keller-Hermann,E/corr BRD 1976/}) 16... Bc8 17. cxd5 exd5 18. Rac1 Bg4 19. Bxe7 Qxe7 20. h3 Bxf3 21. Qxf3 Nf8 22. Qe3 Ne6 23. f4 Nd4 24. f5 Qf8 25. e6 fxe6 26. fxe6 Qe7 27. Qf4 g6 28. Qf7+ Qxf7 29. exf7+ Kxf7 30. Rxc5 Ne6 31. Rc6 Re8 32. Ra6 Rb7 33. Ra5 Rd8 34. Be2 Nd4 35. Ba6 Rbd7 36. Rf1+ Ke7 37. Rc1 Ne6 38. Be2 Nd4 39. Bg4 Nf5 40. Rc6 d4 41. Re5+ Kf8 42. Bxf5 gxf5 43. Rxf5+ Kg7 44. Rc2 d3 45. Rd2 Kg6 46. Rf3 h5 47. g3 Rd5 48. Kf2 R8d6 49. Ke1 Kg5 50. Kd1 a6 51. Rdf2 Rc5 52. Rb2 Re5 53. b4 Rf5 54. Rxf5+ Kxf5 55. Rf2+ Ke4 56. Kd2 Rg6 57. Rf4+ Kd5 58. g4 hxg4 59. hxg4 Ke5 60. Rc4 Rd6 61. a4 Ke6 62. b5 axb5 63. axb5 Rd5 64. Rb4 Kd7 65. b6 Kc8 66. b7+ Kb8 67. Rb1 Rg5 68. Rg1 Kxb7 69. Kxd3 Kc6 70. Ke4 Kd6 71. Kf4 Rg8 72. Re1 Rf8+ 73. Kg3 Kd7 74. Re5 Kd6 75. Re1 Kd7 76. g5 Re8 77. Rxe8 {½-½ Thorn Leeson,J-Van Daatselaar,J/corr NED 2002/}) ({QUESTION: Can White generate anything from} 15. Ng5 {- ? ANSWER: It looks like a waste of tempi. After} dxc4 $1 {(Principle: Meet a wing attack with a central counter)} (15... Bxg5 16. Bxg5 Qxe5 $1 17. Qxe5 Nxe5 18. Rxe5 f6 {Marin} 19. Re2 fxg5 20. b3 Rf4 21. cxd5 exd5 22. Re7 Rb7 23. Rc1 Rc7 $15 {Myers}) 16. Bxc4 ({or} 16. Bxg6 hxg6 17. Qxc4 Rb4 18. Qc3 Bc6 {and Black looks better with the bishop pair and light square power}) 16... h6 17. Nf3 Bc6 {, maybe it's stylistic preference but I prefer Black and have little fear of White's kingside attacking chances.}) 15... dxc4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The same principle applies, as in the previous note.'} ({Of course the h-pawn isn't really hanging:} 15... Nxh4 $4 16. Nxh4 Bxh4 17. Qh5 {and Black can resign.}) 16. Be4 {Myers Marin Kotov: '!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not'} 16. Bxc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} Bc6 (16... Bxh4 $1 {, since the h-pawn really does hang with White's bishop off the b1-h7 diagonal.} 17. Nxh4 Nxh4 18. Bg5 Nf5 $15) 17. h5 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 Nxe5 $17 {Myers Marin Kotov: ''}) (16. Bxg6 hxg6 (16... fxg6 17. Ng5 h6 18. Nh3 Rf5 $17 {Myers}) 17. Qxc4 Rb4 $15 (17... Bc6 18. Qc3 Qd7 $17)) 16... Kh8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now Black threatens to take on e5.'} 17. Bg5 Bb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Securing c4 for the moment, while also threatening ...c4-c3 tricks.' '' !} (17... Bxg5 $6 18. Nxg5 Bc6 19. Bxc6 Qxc6 20. Qc2 h6 21. h5 hxg5 22. hxg6 fxg6 23. Rad1 $14 {Myers}) 18. Qe3 Rfd8 (18... Bxg5 $5 19. Nxg5 Nxe5 20. Nxh7 Rfd8 21. Ng5 Ng4 22. Qh3 Nf6 23. Qc3 Nxe4 24. Rxe4 Rd3 $17 {Myers}) 19. h5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's attackers surge forward, all doing their own thing, but all with similar destinations – which merge into "That way!" as they point to Black's king in unison.'} Bxg5 20. Nxg5 ({After} 20. Qxg5 h6 $1 {(before White can play this)} 21. Qg3 Ne7 {, White's attack grows cold.}) 20... Nf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What was wrong with taking on e5?'} ({ANSWER: It would be inviting an early and sudden death to allow Tal a line like} 20... Nxe5 $6 21. Qf4 {Myers Marin Kotov: '!?'} (21. Bxh7 {Myers Marin Kotov: '?'} Ng4 {! Myers Marin Kotov: '!'} 22. Qh3 Nxf2 $19 (22... Qf4 $1 $19)) (21. Qg3 Rd7 22. h6 gxh6 23. Qh4 Kg7 24. Nxh7 f5 25. Nf6 Rd4 {Myers Marin Kotov: '='} 26. Nh5+ Kh7 27. Nf6+ Kg6 (27... Kg7 $11) 28. f4 fxe4 29. fxe5 Qxe5 30. Re3 $40 {Marin}) (21. Bg6 $6 hxg6 22. hxg6 Nxg6 23. Qh3+ Kg8 24. Qh7+ Kf8 25. Qxg6 fxg6 26. Nxe6+ Kf7 27. Nxc7 Rd2 $19) 21... Rd7 22. h6 $1 {with clear attacking chances for White.} Ng6 23. hxg7+ Kxg7 24. Qg4 Rh8 $11) 21. Rad1 ({Now if} 21. h6 {, then Myers Marin Kotov: '!?'} gxh6 {Myers Marin Kotov: ''} (21... g6 $2 22. Qf3 f5 23. exf6 Rd4 24. g3 (24. f7 $5) 24... Ba6 25. Qc3 Bb7 26. Nf3 $1 Bxe4 (26... e5 27. Bxb7 Rxb7 28. Nxe5 Qd6 29. f7 Re7 30. Nxc4 $18 {Myers}) 27. Nxd4 cxd4 28. Qxd4 {1-0 Lausch,S-Wolff,C/corr GER 1990/}) (21... f5 22. exf6 gxf6 23. Nf3 e5 24. Nh4 Ne6 25. Qf3 Qf7 26. Bd5 Rd7 27. Red1 Rbd8 28. Bxe6 Qxe6 29. Rxd7 Rxd7 30. Qa3 $5 $11 {Myers}) 22. Nf3 (22. Nxh7 Nxh7 23. Qxh6 f5 24. exf6 {Lausch} Rf8 $8 25. Qh4 Bc6 26. Bf5 Rf7 $1 27. Rxe6 Bd7 28. Rae1 Bxe6 29. Rxe6 Qd7 $17 {Marin}) 22... Kg7 $1 23. Qf4 (23. Kh1 Ng6 24. Rad1 Qa5 25. a3 c3 26. bxc3 Rxd1 27. Rxd1 Qxa3 28. Nh2 h5 $1 29. Nf3 Qa4 $15 {Myers}) 23... Ng6 24. Qf6+ Kg8 {and Black threatens to remove queens from the board with ...Qe7 next.}) 21... h6 22. Nf3 Nd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black already stands better. He threatens the e5-pawn once again and prepares a defensive cheapo, into which Tal falls.'} 23. Qf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's coming woes are squarely attributable to this move.' Myers Marin Kotov: '?'} (23. Qc3 {would keep his disadvantage to a minimum. Myers Marin Kotov: '!?'} Nb6 24. Rxd8+ Rxd8 25. Bb1 Ba4 $5 26. Qe3 $11 {Myers}) {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A dark malignance, seemingly relentless and indestructible, pursues Black's king with a vengeance. But Tal's last move was an error, adversely mutating the attacking matrix, after which his attack sags and sprawls in ungainly fashion. EXERCISE (planning): Korchnoi has a game-changing possibility which foils Tal's exertions by severing White's link to the attack? What is it? ANSWER: Pin – taking advantage of the fact that White can't capture en passant. '} 23... f5 $1 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi secures his perimeter, locking out unwanted intruders. For a sniper, patience is a crucial asset. Sooner or later, the target enters the crosshairs. The common notion that Tal out-tactic-ed his opposition 100% of the time is a fallacy.' Myers Marin Kotov: '!'} 24. Bc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop comes to the sad realization that he is empty of his previous blessings.'} Nf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal encounters unexpected truculence from the defenders, who no longer believe they are on the defence. I believe it was Shakespeare who warned of the ineffectuality of a twice-told tale, but in this case it just isn't so. Korchnoi works the same trick twice, exploiting the pin on the e5-pawn for a second time. Black's knight scythes through the illusionary barrier, attacking h5 and threatening to make itself comfortable on d5, while the f5-pawn secures his king, the way a car's bumper wards off accidental injury. '} (24... Bc6 $5 25. Qxc4 Bxf3 26. gxf3 Nxe5 27. Qf4 Rxd1 28. Bxd1 Rd8 29. Bb3 c4 30. Qxe5 Qxe5 31. Rxe5 cxb3 32. axb3 Re8 $17 {Marin}) 25. Rd6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal continues along, still under the sway of his now unprovable hypothesis.'} Nd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi knows when to grab and when to refrain.'} ({The hasty} 25... Nxh5 $2 26. Qh4 Be8 27. Rxe6 {allows the advantage to swing to White's side.}) 26. Qg3 Rb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hoping to eliminate White's only active piece.'} 27. Rxd8+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Just like that, White is busted. This stopgap measure fails to address the core issue at hand. Tal should have retained his rook on d6. Then if Black takes it, the-square e5 opens for White's knight. ' Myers Marin Kotov: '?'} (27. a4 $142 $1 Ba6 28. Nh4 Bb7 29. Ng6+ Kg8 30. Qh4 Rbxd6 31. exd6 Rxd6 32. Bxf5 $1 exf5 33. Re8+ Kf7 34. Rf8+ Ke6 35. f3 $1 Nf6 $1 36. Qe1+ Be4 37. Nf4+ Ke5 38. Ng6+ Ke6 $11 {Marin}) 27... Qxd8 28. Rd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): How did Korchnoi coax Tal into handing over another pawn? ANSWER: Double attack b2 and h5. '} Be8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The rearguard scrambles in a poisonous retreat.'} 29. b3 cxb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I don't see any reason to activate White's bishop again.'} (29... c3 {was better.}) 30. Bxb3 Bxh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi continues to slurp up pawns, which to him taste like wet noodles in a bowl of Udon soup.'} 31. Rc1 Rb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Another powerful shot. A second squadron enters the fight.'} 32. Bc4 ({After} 32. Rxc5 $2 Nf4 $1 {(threatening a monster fork on e2)} 33. Rc2 (33. Kh2 Ne2 34. Qh3 Bxf3 $19 {Myers Marin Kotov: 'Kotov'}) 33... Re4 $1 34. Kh2 Bxf3 35. gxf3 ({not} 35. Qxf3 $2 Qh4+ 36. Kg1 Re1#) 35... Rd4 {, Black's rook and knight dominate and White can comfortably resign.}) 32... Bxf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 33. Qxf3 Qg5 34. Qd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '"Be silent!" rages White's retreating queen to the taunting voices in her head, as the foraging expedition returns empty handed, tired and hungry. It's hard to shake off the feeling of inevitability in Black's play. Isn't it disorienting to watch Korchnoi strategically and tactically manhandle a legendary player such as Tal? '} Qf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The powerful rarely negotiate when they are in a position simply to reach out and take. The e5-pawn is destined to fall, and the queen emerges with the exultation of one who understands the degree of her true strength.'} 35. Bxd5 (35. Bd3 Rd4 (35... Rb2 36. Rc2 Nc3 37. g3 Qxf2+ 38. Rxf2 Nxd1 $19 {Myers})) 35... Rd4 (35... exd5 {?} 36. Rxc5) 36. Qc2 Rxd5 37. Re1 Rxe5 38. Rxe5 Qxe5 39. Qa4 Qe1+ 40. Kh2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Like this:'} Qxf2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: I realize that, at the top level, White's position is hopeless. But if I had Black, I wouldn't know exactly how to win the queen ending. How can Black push his c-pawn without walking into perpetual check?'} 41. Qe8+ Kh7 42. Qxe6 Qh4+ 43. Kg1 Qe4 $1 {(the perfect location for Black's queen, where it prevents all perpetual check attempts, while preparing to shepherd the c-pawn forward)} 44. Qa6 c4 $1 {(Principle: In queen endings, the number of pawns can be meaningless; all that matters is who promotes to a new queen first – here a7 isn't worth the bother to defend, since Black's c-pawn is way ahead in what is essentially a non-race)} 45. Qxa7 c3 46. Qc5 c2 47. Kh2 Qf4+ 48. g3 c1=Q {and wins. 0-1 (48) Tal,M-Kortschnoj,V Moscow 12/216 1971 CI1-130 [Kotov,Alexander]} 0-1

