1. e4 e5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = C95 - Spanisch-Breyer Variante /Ruy Lopez-Breyer Variation'} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 ({The leading Yugoslav Grandmaster is distinguished, I would say, by the 'seasonal constancy' of his play with Black. About ten years ago he only played the system with} 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Nd7 {.}) ({Then came the turn of the Smyslov system} 9... h6 {, to which Gligoric remained faithful for a long time, despite occasional setbacks. Since 1972, he, like many others, has been adopting the Breyer system. The Leningrad Tournament was, if I am not mistaken, the fourth event in a row in which Gligoric played this variation (true, in his own interpretation).}) 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 c5 ({The usual continuation is} 12... Re8 {as Kuzmin played against Rukavina in the same round. True, White has recently carried out with fair success a plan involving the advance of his queenside pawns; Black's last move is directed against this.}) 13. Nf1 Re8 14. Ng3 Bf8 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Gligoric closely studies his previous games, and constantly perfects the systems he adopts. This was especially brought home to me during our match in 1968, in which he succeeded in obtaining a comfortable game as Black. The present game is no exception.' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Gligoric closely studies his previous games, and constantly perfects the systems he adopts. This was especially brought home to me during our match in 1968, in which he succeeded in obtaining a comfortable game as Black. The present game is no exception.''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'The Yugoslav grandmaster improves on the game Keres-Gligoric (San Antonio, 1972), in which after'} 14... g6 15. Bh6 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!'} Bf8 16. Qd2 $16 {Mikhail Tal: 'White created strong pressure. For the moment White's queen's bishop has no convenient post (15 Bg5 is answered by the simple 15...h6), while in some cases Black is ready to play ...d5. Hence the decision to block the centre .'}) 15. d5 (15. dxe5 {!?} dxe5 16. Nf5) 15... g6 16. b3 {Mikhail Tal: 'In similar positions, White, after blocking the queenside, usually tries to shift the main battle over to the kingside. At the moment he intends to achieve his goal by 17 a4 b4 18 c4. Gligoric naturally does not want this. '} (16. a4) (16. Bg5 {!?}) 16... Nb6 17. Be3 {Mikhail Tal: 'An inaccurate move, after which there is no question of White gaining on opening advantage.'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'After the stronger'} 17. Qe2 {Mikhail Tal: ', I consider that White would have the better chances.' ''}) ({I rejected the immediate} 17. a4 {because of} bxa4 18. bxa4 Nc4 {. This was all the more reason for not playing the bishop to e3, where it makes the opponent a present of a tempo. In making this last move, White was planning to advance instead his b-pawn, but then thought better of it.}) 17... Bc8 (17... a5 $13) 18. a4 {Mikhail Tal: 'A confession that the previous move was a mistake.'} bxa4 19. bxa4 Nc4 20. Bg5 Bg7 ({Mikhail Tal: 'Avoiding the tactical possibility'} 20... h6 21. Bxf6 Qxf6 22. a5 $1 {when the knight is threatened.} Qd8 {!?} 23. Ba4 Nb2 {'with the idea' 24... Na4$13}) 21. Qe2 {Mikhail Tal: 'or else to retreat the queen to c1. I think that I would perhaps have settled for the latter. I consider that there was no need for Black to exchange off a pair of rooks, since it leaves his a-pawn less well defended.' Mikhail Tal: 'In making this move, White did not yet know whether he would be aggressively inclined (cf. the note to Black's 22nd move).'} Na5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'last book move'} 22. Qd2 $1 {Mikhail Tal: 'Now White likes the position of his bishop on g5.' ! Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (22. Rab1 $5 $16) 22... Rb8 {?!} ({Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Black could, of course, have played'} 22... Nc4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'after which White would have a choice: either to offer a draw by'} 23. Qc1 $14 (23. Qd3 Na5 24. c4 $14) 23... Rb8 $13) ({Stronger was the immediate} 22... Bd7 {'' !?}) ({or the manoeuvre} 22... Qe7 {followed by ...Qf8 and ...h6. 'with the idea' Qf8, h6$13 Tal}) 23. Reb1 Rxb1+ 24. Rxb1 (24. Bxb1 $143 Nb3 25. Qb2 Nxa1 26. Qxa1 Qc7 $17) 24... Bd7 $2 {Mikhail Tal: 'This is a tactical error, after which White gains the advantage. Black shouldn't have left his a-pawn completely undefended;' ? Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} ({Mikhail Tal: 'instead'} 24... Re7 { b7  Rb7 Mikhail Tal: 'would have left the game roughly equal.' ''}) (24... Nc4 25. Qc1 $14) {Mikhail Tal: 'Evidently Gligoric must have underestimated White's next move, otherwise he would have certainly continued'} 25. c4 $1 $16 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Black must capture this pawn, otherwise after 26 Qc3 and 27 Bd2 his position will be very difficult.' Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (25. Qe2 $5 $16) 25... Nxc4 $2 {?! Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (25... h6 {Mikhail Tal: ', although in this case, after' %03'better is'} {Mikhail Tal: 'stronger than'} 26. Bxh6 (26. Bxf6 {!} Bxf6 27. Qxh6 Nxc4 28. Bd3 {White also has the initiative. '' Tal Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'White also has the initiative.''}) 26... Nxc4) 26. Qd3 $1 {! Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: '!''} ({This is much stronger than} 26. Qc3 {?} Nb6 27. Qa5 Nc4 $1 {'' ! Tal} ({but not} 27... Nxa4 {?} 28. Qxa6 {when the knight is doomed. '+-'})) 26... Na5 (26... Nb6 27. Qxa6 Nc8 28. a5 {is also bad. '+-'}) 27. Qxa6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'The passed pawn on a4 quickly leads to threats'} Bc8 28. Qa7 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'The most convincing method. Supported from in front by the queen, who feels quite at home among the enemy pieces, and from the rear by two or three important enough pieces, the passed pawn will soon begin its inexorable advance. Black's badly placed pieces are unable to set up even the slightest bit of counterplay. '} Re7 29. Qa8 Qc7 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (29... Rb7 30. Rb5 Rxb5 31. axb5 $14) 30. Nd2 $1 {[ 31.b5 b7 32.c4+-] [ 31.Rb5 Nb7 32.Nc4+-] Mikhail Tal: 'Threatening 31 Rb5 Nb7 32 Nc4.' ! 'with the idea' 31.Rb5! Nb7 32.Nc4$18} c4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (30... Re8 31. Rb5 Nb7 32. Nc4 $16) 31. Be3 {!} ({The immediate} 31. Rb5 {is also strong. !}) 31... Nd7 32. Rb5 $18 ({When making my previous move I had intended to play} 32. Bb6 {but then decided that it was not worth parting with my bishop just to win a pawn after} Nxb6 33. Qxa5 c3 $1 {!} 34. Nf3 {''} Nc4 {.}) 32... Nb7 ({It is possible that} 32... c3 {would have enabled Black to put up a longer resistance, although White's extra pawn, together with all his positional advantages, should have ensured him an easy win. !?}) 33. a5 c3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '+-'} (33... Re8 $5 $16) 34. Nb3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (34. Nb1 $5 Nd8 $18) 34... Re8 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '+-'} (34... Nd8 $5 $16) 35. a6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (35. Qa7 $5 Qb8 36. Qxb8 Nxb8 $18) 35... Nbc5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '+-?'} (35... Nd8 $142 $16) 36. a7 {Mikhail Tal: '??' Mikhail Tal: 'Phenomenal!' ?! Fritz 5.00 (5s): '??'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'White wrote down on his scoresheet the (not exactly difficult) move'} 36. Nxc5 $1 $18 {Mikhail Tal: 'which finishes the game instantly, and after which my opponent was indeed going to resign, since the a-pawn reaches the eighth rank by force. Then White decided that the exchange on c5 was an unnecessary move, and completely forgot about his opponent's reply.' ! 'with the idea' 37.a7$18 Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} Nxc5 37. Bxc5 $18) 36... Nb7 37. Ne2 ({The funniest thing is that White's 36th move was objectively just as good as the one I had written down, since here also White could have won immediately, though this time with the help of a combination -} 37. Rb6 $1 {I saw this combination, but having overlooked my opponent's previous move, I completely lost confidence in myself, and stopped at the position after !} Nxb6 38. Qb8 ({but not} 38. Bxb6 Qxb6 39. Qb8 Bd7) 38... Na8 {. Yet after} 39. Qxa8 {it is time for Black to resign, since he comes out a piece down. However, I was not after a brilliancy, and in my confused state took what was perhaps the most practical decision - to make a few quiet moves, seeing that White's position is so strong, and adjourn the game. '+-' ! Tal}) 37... Rf8 38. Nec1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} (38. Nbc1 $142 $5 $16) 38... f5 39. f3 Bf6 $2 {Mikhail Tal: 'Black should definitely not have given up the pawn.' ? Fritz 5.00 (5s): '+-?'} ({After Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'After''} 39... fxe4 {the analysis of the adjourned position would have been by no means a formality. !? Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!?' Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Mikhail Tal: 'the analysis of the adjourned position would have been by no means a formality.''} 40. fxe4 Bh8 $14) 40. exf5 Kg7 41. fxg6 hxg6 42. Nd3 {[ b4-g4,  b4-c6, b8] [ Rb4-g4,  Nb4-c6, Qb8]} (42. Rb4 $5 Rg8 $18) 42... Bh4 {Mikhail Tal: '. Here the game was adjourned, but on the following day GJigoric resigned. In addition to all his other troubles, Black now has a weakness on g6.'} 43. Z0 (43. Nb4 {'with the idea' 44.Na6}) 1-0

