{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Keres, like Korchnoi, gave Tal a lot a trouble - but not in this game.'} 1. e4 {Chekhov Chekhov,Valery A Chekhov,Valery A Chekhov,Valery A Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 (4... Nf6) 5. O-O ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} 5. Bxc6+ {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'} bxc6 {Chekhov,Valery A} 6. d4 exd4 (6... f6 {.}) 7. Qxd4) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Also played here are'} 5. c3 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'}) (5. c4 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'}) 5... Bd7 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This line is more solid than the strategically suspect'} 5... Bg4 $6 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '?!'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} 6. h3 {Chekhov,Valery A} h5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. We all fall sway to extremes of stylistic bias, from time to time. When we play a line as sharp as this one, which has fallen into theoretical disrepute, our opening variation becomes inextricably entangled with our loyalty to logic. After'} (6... Bh5 7. c3 Nf6 8. d4 b5 9. Bc2 Be7 10. d5 Nb8 11. a4 {left White with a pleasant strategic advantage in G.Klompus-S.Arzumanyan, USSR 1967}) 7. d4 b5 8. Bb3 Nxd4 ({or} 8... Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nf6 10. c3 exd4 {, A.Gurevich-E.Rompteau, correspondence 1966, when I like White's chances after} 11. Qe2 dxc3 12. Rd1 cxb2 13. Bxb2 {with a kind of super-Danish Gambit for the two pawn investment; Black's king will never be safe and White leads big time in development}) 9. hxg4 hxg4 ({or} 9... Nxb3 10. axb3 hxg4 11. Ng5 Qd7 12. Qd3 c6 13. c4 b4 14. c5 f6 15. Nd2 fxg5 16. Nc4 Qe6 17. Rd1 Qh6 18. Kf1 Qh1+ 19. Ke2 Qxg2 20. cxd6 Qf3+ 21. Qxf3 gxf3+ 22. Kxf3 {when Black's ragged pawn structure and lag in development mean a busted ending, V.Krishnan-T.Taylor, South California State Championship 2012; this win allowed my student to go on to win the State Championship}) 10. Ng5 Nh6 11. g3 (11. Bd5 c6 12. c3 cxd5 13. cxd4 Be7 14. Qd2 dxe4 15. dxe5 d5 16. Nc3 e3 17. Qxd5 Bxg5 18. Qc6+ Kf8 19. Bxe3 Bxe3 20. fxe3 {and Black was fighting for survival, N.De Firmian-J.Timman, Reykjavik 2000.}) 11... Qf6 12. c3 Nxb3 13. axb3 Be7 14. Qd5 $1 O-O 15. Na3 Qg6 16. Nxb5 Rac8 {Ziatdinov,R-Katishonok,N/URS/1990/0,5 (31) Inf 51/315/} 17. Rxa6 Bxg5 18. Rc6 $16) 6. c3 (6. c4 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'}) (6. d4 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'} b5 7. Bb3 Nxd4 8. Nxd4 exd4 9. c3 $36) 6... Nge7 7. d4 Ng6 8. Re1 (8. d5 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo'} Nb8 9. c4) (8. Be3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is also played here.'} Be7 {Chekhov,Valery A} 9. Nbd2 h6 10. d5 Nb8 11. c4 O-O 12. b4 c5 $6 (12... a5 $5) 13. bxc5 $16 {Yudasin,L-Castro/JUG(ol)/1990/1:0 (36) Inf 50/357/}) 8... Be7 9. Nbd2 h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why ...h6? Black already covers g5. --- ANSWER: Black may be planning to 'goodify' his bad bishop with a future ...Bg5, in which case he has the ...hxg5 option.' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '!'} (9... O-O) 10. Nf1 Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'And here it is already. White isn't so tempted to chop it, since that would open the h-file for Black's rook.'} 11. Be3 (11. Ne3 {Chekhov,Valery A Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} Bxe3 12. Bxe3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black rids himself of his bad bishop and can be satisfied with the opening's outcome, M.Sagafos-T.Gareev, Cappelle la Grande 2007.'} O-O $13) (11. Bxg5) 11... Bxe3 (11... Qf6 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?'}) (11... exd4 12. cxd4 Bxe3 13. Nxe3 O-O 14. Rc1 b5 15. Bc2 Rc8 16. e5 Nh4 17. Nxh4 Qxh4 18. Bf5 Qd8 19. Qg4 Nb8 20. Bxd7 h5 21. Qxh5 Qxd7 22. Nd5 dxe5 23. Qxe5 Rfe8 24. Ne7+ Kf8 25. Qh5 f6 26. Nxc8 Rxc8 27. Rc3 Nc6 28. Rxc6 {1-0 Minasian,A-Lobjanidze,N/Erevan op 1996/EXP 52}) 12. Nxe3 (12. fxe3 O-O 13. Ng3 Nh4 (13... b5 14. Bc2 Nh4 15. Nf5 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 Kh7 17. a4 g6 18. Ng3 Qe7 19. b4 Kg7 20. axb5 axb5 21. d5 Rxa1 22. Rxa1 Nb8 23. Bd3 c6 24. Qe2 Rc8 25. Ra3 Qe8 26. Qf3 Qd8 27. Nf1 Qb6 28. Qf2 Na6 29. Qa2 Nc7 30. Ra7 cxd5 31. exd5 Be8 32. Be4 f5 33. Bd3 Bf7 34. c4 bxc4 35. Bxc4 Qxb4 36. Ba6 Nxa6 37. Rxa6 Rc1 38. e4 Qxe4 {0-1 Mateo,R-Abdelnabbi,I/Bled SLO 2002/}) 14. Nxh4 Qxh4 15. Rf1 Qg5 16. Qd2 Rad8 17. Bc2 Ne7 18. Rae1 c5 19. Rf2 cxd4 20. exd4 Qxd2 21. Rxd2 Be6 22. Bb3 Bxb3 23. axb3 g6 24. Red1 Nc6 25. dxe5 dxe5 26. Rd7 Rfe8 27. Nf1 Rxd7 28. Rxd7 Re7 29. Rd6 Kg7 30. Ne3 Nb8 31. Rd8 Nc6 32. Rd5 Nb8 33. Nc4 Nd7 34. Nd2 Nf6 35. Rd6 Rd7 36. Rxd7 Nxd7 37. b4 Kf6 38. Kf2 Ke6 39. Kf3 f5 40. Nb3 Kd6 41. Na5 b6 42. Nc4+ Ke6 43. g3 b5 44. Nd2 Nf6 45. h3 Nh7 46. h4 Nf6 47. b3 Kd6 48. c4 Kc6 49. Ke3 Kd6 50. Kf3 Ke6 {½-½ Sakharov,Y-Zurakhov,V/Kiev URS 1958/}) 12... O-O $11 13. Bc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop backs up e4, since it isn't of much use on a4.'} (13. dxe5 Ncxe5 14. Bc2 (14. Bxd7 Nxf3+ 15. Qxf3 Qxd7 16. Qg3 Rae8 17. Nf5 Qb5 18. f4 Qb6+ 19. Kh1 Qxb2 20. h4 Rxe4 21. h5 Rxe1+ 22. Rxe1 Qb5 23. Qg4 Qd3 24. hxg6 fxg6 25. Nh4 Kh7 26. f5 g5 27. Re7 Qxc3 28. Ng6 Rf6 29. Re8 Rxg6 30. fxg6+ Kxg6 31. Re6+ {1-0 Cmiko,J-Kopicka,K/Czechia CZE 2000/}) 14... Re8 15. g3 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 Ne5 17. Qd1 Bc6 18. f4 Nd7 19. Nf5 Nf6 20. Qd4 Bd7 21. Nh4 Qe7 22. e5 dxe5 23. fxe5 Nh7 24. Nf5 Bxf5 25. Bxf5 Ng5 26. Bg4 Rad8 27. Qe3 Ne6 28. Rad1 Qc5 29. Qxc5 Nxc5 30. Rd4 c6 31. Re3 Kf8 32. Kf2 Rxd4 33. cxd4 Ne6 34. Bxe6 fxe6 35. Rb3 Re7 36. Ke3 Ke8 37. Ke4 Rf7 38. h4 Kd8 39. Ke3 Kc7 40. Ke2 a5 41. Ra3 {½-½ Kholmov,R-Goldin,A/Klaipeda URS 1988/}) (13. a3 Nh4 14. Nxh4 Qxh4 15. d5 Nb8 16. Bc2 Bc8 17. b4 Nd7 18. g3 Qe7 19. c4 Nf6 20. Bd3 a5 21. Qd2 b6 22. f3 Bd7 23. Qc3 Rfb8 24. Bf1 axb4 25. axb4 Be8 26. Ra3 Qd8 27. Rea1 Rxa3 28. Rxa3 Ra8 29. Rxa8 Qxa8 30. Kf2 c5 31. dxc6 Bxc6 {½-½ Fedorowicz,J-Ivanov,I/Las Vegas USA 1995/}) (13. Qd2 Nxd4 14. Nxe5 dxe5 15. Bxd7 Qxd7 16. cxd4 exd4 17. Red1 Rad8 18. Qb4 Qe7 19. Nd5 Qxb4 20. Nxb4 Ne5 21. Rac1 Rd7 22. Rc5 f6 23. f4 Ng4 24. Rd5 Rfd8 25. R1xd4 Rxd5 26. Nxd5 Kf7 27. Rb4 b5 28. h3 c6 29. hxg4 cxd5 30. Rd4 Ke6 31. Kf2 Rd6 32. f5+ Ke5 33. Ke3 h5 34. gxh5 a5 35. Rxd5+ Rxd5 36. exd5 Kxd5 37. g4 Ke5 38. Kf3 Kd4 39. g5 fxg5 40. f6 gxf6 41. h6 Kd3 42. h7 Kc2 43. h8=Q {1-0 Stoma,P-Bajek,M/Krynica U16 POL 1998/}) (13. g3 Re8 14. Bc2 (14. d5 Nce7 15. h4 Nf8 16. Kg2 Nh7 17. Bc2 Rf8 18. Qd3 Nf6 19. Nd2 c6 20. dxc6 Bxc6 21. c4 Ne8 22. Nd5 Nc7 23. Rad1 f5 24. Kg1 Ncxd5 25. exd5 Be8 26. c5 b5 27. b4 Rc8 28. Nb3 Bf7 29. Na5 Qd7 30. c6 Qc7 31. Bb3 Bh5 32. Rd2 Kh8 33. Nb7 f4 34. a4 fxg3 35. fxg3 Rf3 36. Re3 Qb6 37. Nc5 Rxe3 38. Qxe3 bxa4 39. Bc2 Bg6 40. Bxg6 Nxg6 41. h5 Ne7 42. Kh2 Qxb4 43. Ne4 a3 44. Qa7 Nxc6 45. Qxa6 Ne7 46. Qxd6 Qxd6 47. Nxd6 Ra8 {0-1 Caspi,I-Zilberman,Y/Israel ISR 2008/}) 14... Bh3 15. a3 Qd7 16. Qd3 Nce7 17. Rad1 Rad8 18. b4 Qe6 19. Nd2 Qf6 20. Bb3 c6 21. Ndc4 exd4 22. Qxd4 Qxd4 23. Rxd4 Ne5 24. Nxe5 dxe5 25. Rdd1 Bc8 26. f3 Kf8 27. Kf2 Ng8 28. Nc4 Be6 29. Na5 Bxb3 30. Nxb3 Nf6 31. c4 Rd7 32. Rxd7 Nxd7 33. c5 Ke7 34. Na5 Rb8 35. Rd1 Kd8 36. Nc4 Kc7 37. Nd6 a5 38. Nxf7 axb4 39. axb4 b6 40. cxb6+ Rxb6 41. Nd6 Rxb4 42. Ne8+ Kd8 43. Nxg7 Rb2+ 44. Kg1 c5 45. Nf5 Rb6 46. Rd6 Rb1+ 47. Kg2 h5 48. Rd2 Kc7 49. h4 Rc1 50. Ne3 Kc6 51. Kf2 c4 52. Ke2 Nc5 53. Rc2 Rxc2+ 54. Nxc2 Ne6 55. g4 Nf4+ 56. Kd2 Kc5 57. gxh5 Nxh5 58. Ne3 Nf4 59. Nd5 Ne6 60. Ke3 Ng7 61. Nf6 Kd6 62. h5 Ke6 63. h6 Kxf6 64. hxg7 {1-0 Ivanchuk,V-Timman,J/Malmo SWE 2003/}) (13. Nf5 Qf6 14. dxe5 Ngxe5 15. Nxe5 Qxe5 16. Bxc6 bxc6 17. Nd4 Qf6 18. Qa4 Qd8 19. Qc4 c5 20. Nf5 Be6 21. Qd3 Qg5 22. Re3 Bxf5 23. exf5 Rfb8 24. Qd5 Qf6 25. Rae1 Kf8 26. Qc6 Rc8 27. Qd7 Qd8 28. Qc6 Qf6 29. Re4 a5 30. h3 Rab8 31. b3 Qxc3 32. Re7 a4 33. Qd7 axb3 34. axb3 Qxb3 35. R1e4 Qd1+ 36. Kh2 Qh5 37. f6 c4 38. fxg7+ {1-0 Wilfert,A-Vogt,T/Bayern GER 2005/}) 13... Re8 (13... Nh4 $5 {Chekhov,Valery A} 14. d5 Nxf3+ 15. Qxf3 Ne7 16. Nf5 Nxf5 17. exf5 Qf6 18. Re3 g6 19. fxg6 fxg6 20. Qg3 Kh7 21. Rf1 Bf5 22. Bb3 g5 23. f3 Bg6 24. Rc1 Qf4 25. Qxf4 gxf4 26. Ree1 Kg7 27. c4 b6 28. Ba4 a5 29. Bc6 Rad8 30. a3 Kf6 31. Kf2 h5 32. Ba4 h4 33. h3 Rg8 34. Bc2 Bxc2 35. Rxc2 a4 36. Re4 Ra8 37. Kf1 Rgb8 38. Ke2 Ra5 39. Kd3 Rc5 40. b4 axb3 41. Rb2 b5 42. cxb5 Rbxb5 43. Rb4 Rxd5+ 44. Kc4 Rxb4+ 45. Kxb4 Rd3 46. Kc4 Re3 47. a4 Ke6 48. a5 Kd7 49. Kb5 Kc8 50. a6 Kb8 51. Rb1 Ka7 52. Rb2 Rc3 53. Kb4 Rc6 {0-1 Kaveniemi,K-Kivimaki,J/Finland FIN 1998/}) 14. Qd2 Rc8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The idea is to keep c7 covered in case of a future Nd5.'} ({Also logical is} 14... Nh4 15. Nxh4 Qxh4 16. Nd5 Rac8 {. The swap on h4 helped Black, who was the more cramped side, I.Kopylov-B.Kovernikov, correspondence 2005.} 17. f4 exd4 18. cxd4 Ne7 19. Nc3 Rcd8 20. g3 Qh3 21. Qf2 c6 22. Re3 Bg4 23. f5 c5 24. d5 Qh5 25. Bd3 Nc8 26. Bf1 f6 27. Qf4 Qg5 28. Rae1 Qxf4 29. gxf4 b5 30. a4 b4 31. Nb1 a5 32. Nd2 Nb6 33. Bb5 Re7 34. Nb3 Ra8 35. e5 fxe5 36. fxe5 Bxf5 37. exd6 Rxe3 38. Rxe3 Rd8 39. Re5 Bd7 40. Bc6 c4 41. Nxa5 c3 42. bxc3 bxc3 43. Nb7 Rc8 44. a5 c2 45. Re1 Bxc6 46. dxc6 Rxc6 47. Rc1 Nd7 48. Kf2 Kf7 49. Ke3 Ke8 50. Kd2 Nb8 51. Rxc2 Rxc2+ 52. Kxc2 Kd7 53. Kd3 Kc6 54. Nd8+ Kd7 55. Nb7 {½-½ Kopylov,I-Kovernikov,B/corr RCCA 2005/}) 15. g3 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Keeping Black's knight out of f4.' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: ''} (15. Rad1 Nh4 16. Qe2 (16. Nxh4 Qxh4 17. d5 Ne7 18. Nf5 Nxf5 19. exf5 Qg5 20. Qxg5 hxg5 21. Re3 Kf8 22. g4 Ke7 23. Kg2 Rh8 24. Rh3 Kf6 25. Rxh8 Rxh8 26. Kg3 Rh4 27. h3 Rh8 {½-½ Heinrich,H-Hesselbarth,K/corr DDR 1981/}) 16... Qf6 17. Nxh4 Qxh4 18. Qf3 Nd8 19. Qg3 Qxg3 20. hxg3 Bc6 21. f3 Kf8 22. Bb3 f6 23. Kf2 Bd7 24. Rd2 Be6 25. Bc2 Nf7 26. Ba4 Red8 27. d5 Bd7 28. Bxd7 Rxd7 29. c4 a5 30. f4 Re7 31. Nf5 Ree8 32. Kf3 Ra8 33. Rc2 b6 34. Nh4 Kg8 35. Kg4 Nh8 36. b3 Kf7 37. a3 Ng6 38. Nf5 Nf8 39. b4 axb4 40. axb4 Nd7 41. Kf3 Ra4 42. Rb1 Ra3+ 43. Ne3 f5 44. fxe5 fxe4+ 45. Ke2 Nxe5 46. c5 bxc5 47. bxc5 g6 48. cxd6 cxd6 49. Rb6 Rd8 50. Rb4 Nd3 51. Rc7+ Kf6 52. Rxe4 Ne5 53. Rc2 Kg5 54. Rd2 Rc8 55. Rb2 {½-½ Ginzburg,M-Bielicki,C/Buenos Aires ARG 1992/}) (15. dxe5 Ncxe5 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 $11) 15... Qf6 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' Chekhov: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Attacking f3.'} (15... Bh3 $11 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!?' '!?' '!?' Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '='' Chekhov: '!?' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '=!?'}) 16. Nd5 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Chekhov: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hey, I said: "Attacking f3"! Tal's last move is the spark which is the precursor to a future fire which scorches the land. He intends to respond to ...Qxf3 with Bd1, trapping Black's queen.' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'On'} 16. Kg2 {Chekhov,Valery A Cyrus Lakdawala: ', Tal probably was worried about the line' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '!?'} Bh3+ $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which the comps like for White's side, after'} 17. Kxh3 Qxf3 18. Nd5 Qh5+ (18... exd4 $2 19. Bd1 $1) (18... Nh4 $6 19. Kxh4 Qg2 20. Ne3 Qxh2+ 21. Kg4) 19. Kg2 Qg5 $1 $10 {Keres/ Cyrus Lakdawala: '. This was given an exclam by Keres, who assessed this position as even, while Komodo gives White an edge after' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '=!'} 20. Qxg5 hxg5 21. Rad1 Nf8 (21... Na5 22. Ne3 b5 23. f3 $16 {Myers}) 22. Ne3 {.}) (16. Qd1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=!?'}) 16... Qxf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal's entire premise must now be rethought and re-evaluated. Keres cleverly self-traps his queen, having seen a resource.'} (16... Qd8) (16... Qe6) 17. Bd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): It's easy to pick off a stray soldier - but not so easy when she is part of a military formation. It looks like Keres is about to lose a miniature. His queen is trapped. Or is it? Black to play and not lose his queen. --- ANSWER: Knight fork/clearance. Black's queen is far from trapped.'} Nh4 {Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Chekhov: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's forces are drawn to f3, as if to a loadstone. Any retreat runs counter to our sense of honour, which in this case is even more apt, since Black has no viable retreat for his queen! Even near the end of his life, Keres remained tactically astute, having foreseen this trick which extricates his queen.'} 18. gxh4 ({Not} 18. Bxf3 $4 Nxf3+ 19. Kg2 Nxd2 {and White finds himself down two pieces for zero compensation.}) 18... Qh3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): For most of us, when we land in such weary looking positions, our imaginations go inoperative. Not so with Tal. Now what? It looks as if Tal's position is collapsing. After all, his kingside structure is decimated, seemingly without compensation. However Tal found a miraculous saving idea. What is it? --- ANSWER: Line opening/knight fork.'} 19. Nf6+ {Chekhov,Valery A: '!' '!' Chekhov: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The criminal knight melts discretely into the crowd. Amazingly. Tal is the only player I know who found hidden ways of seizing the initiative, while fettered and gagged. I don't know about you, but when I play over Tal's games, I get that disoriented, out-of-place feeling, as if I'm at a party where I don't know anyone. --- EXERCISE (critical decision): No time to rest. Should Black chop the knight, or should he give up the exchange with ...Kh8?' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '!' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: ''} Kh8 $2 {Chekhov,Valery A: '?!' '?!' Chekhov: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This attempt to quell the kingside commotion is an overreaction. Keres, undoubtedly startled by Tal's last move, unnecessarily hands over the exchange and now stands worse.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: He should accept the challenge with'} 19... gxf6 $1 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: '!'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(it's not so easy to find your plan when there is an absence of identifying characteristics within the position; here we only see a maze of seemingly random lines)' Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} 20. Qxh6 exd4 21. Kh1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(threatening a nasty rook check on g1)' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: ' '} (21. cxd4 Nxd4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} 22. Re3 Nf5 $1) 21... Ne5 $13 {Chekhov,Valery A: 'Nei,Iivo: ''' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(any defensive formation is only as strong as its weakest square, and somehow Black manages to hold the game by reinforcing g4)' Myers Nei Chekhov Keres: '!'} ({