{Chekhov,Valery A: 'Komodo 9 64-bit (20s): 'E30: Nimzo-Indian: Leningrad Variation '' Myers & Co: 'Myers: This game is given as #65 (in date order) by Burgess/Nunn/Emms in "The World's Greatest Chess Games", and it was selected as Best Game of Volume 15 by the jury of the Chess Informant.'} 1. d4 {Myers & Co Chekhov Myers & Co: 'Myers Timman Euwe Burgess Chekhov Nei Christiansen Keres'} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 {Myers & Co: 'Mikhail Tal: 'I took this move to be the ftrst signal of Spassky's aggressive intentions.''} ({When he is more peaceably inclined he plays} 3. Nf3 {(Myers & Co) , after which the comparatively quiet Queen's Indian Defence is normally reached.}) 3... Bb4 4. Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky's favourite Leningrad line of the Nimzo-Indian.' Myers & Co: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The gauntlet is thrown down. The Leningrad Variation, which Spassky frequently adopts, usually leads to a complicated and sharp struggle. ''} h6 5. Bh4 c5 6. d5 b5 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Is this some kind of a Blumenfeld Gambit of sorts? --- ANSWER: Tal's move is more goading than a direct slap across the cheek. I guess you can call it the Nimzo-Blumenfeld Indian. It seems to be a hybrid. Black is willing to offer a pawn to seize central control.' Myers & Co: 'Mikhail Tal: 'The challenge is accepted. This sharp continuation seems to me to be the most logical,'' Garry Kasparov: 'This is the most energetic rejoinder to the Leningrad Variation.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Normal in this position is' Myers & Co: 'although Black has other, quieter moves at his disposal, e.g.'} 6... d6 7. e3 {Chekhov} Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qe7 (8... e5 {.}) 9. Nf3 Nbd7 10. Nd2 O-O 11. Be2 Ne5 12. Ne4 $1 $36 {Bareev,E-Chandler,M/Hastings/1991/1:0 (37) Inf 53/528/}) ({or} 6... Bxc3+ {Myers & Co: '. The move 6...b5 is akin to the Volga Gambit, which is frequently adopted at present.'} 7. bxc3 d6) (6... e5 {Myers & Co}) 7. dxe6 {Garry Kasparov: 'A risky venture from the Zak-Tolush arsenal: White accepts the gift, but concedes the centre.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'There is also the tricky version' Garry Kasparov: 'White has no advantage after'} 7. e4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?'} g5 {Chekhov} 8. Bg3 Nxe4 9. Be5 $13 {Levenfish,G-Estrin,J/URS/1951/ Myers & Co: ', which occurred in the game Levenftsh-Estrin, USSR Championship Semifinal 1951, leads to very sharp play. Spassky prefers to accept the pawn sacrifice.'} O-O {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Levenfish-Estrin, Baku 1951).'} 10. Qh5 d6 11. Bd3 Nxc3 12. Qxh6 Ne4+ 13. Kf1 dxe5 14. Bxe4 f5 15. Qg6+ Kh8 16. Qh6+ {and the game ends in perpetual check, A.Yermolinsky-A.Shabalov, Parsippany 1996.}) ({But} 7. e3 $1 {as played by Timman against Ligterink (Wijk aan Zee 1977) and Yusupov (Hilversum 6th matchgame 1986) casts doubt on Black's sharp move.}) 7... fxe6 8. cxb5 ({White can also decline with} 8. e4 O-O 9. e5 Qa5 10. Nge2 ({but not} 10. exf6 $4 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qxc3+ 12. Ke2 Qxc4+ {and White's h4-bishop falls}) 10... Ne4 11. Qc2 Bb7 12. f3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Ba3 14. cxb5 a6 $1 {when White's underdeveloped position appears to be in some danger, E.Bareev-B.Gelfand, Moscow 1990.}) 8... d5 $1 {Myers & Co: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Now Black has a mobile pawn centre.''} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Not'} 8... Qa5 {Ü 12/575 Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'which is met by the reply'} 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Qc2 Kf7 11. e3 a6 12. Bd3 Bb7 13. Nge2 axb5 14. O-O Rg8 15. Be4 Bc6 16. Rad1 {with advantage to White  (Spassky-Posner, Vancouver 1971).}) 9. e3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' E30: Nimzo-Indian: Leningrad Variation'} O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Do you think Black has full compensation for the pawn? --- ANSWER: More than enough. For only one pawn, Black gets: 1. A development lead in an open position. --- 2. Central dominance. --- 3. An open f-file.'} ({QUESTION: Can Black try to disrupt with} 9... d4 {- ? --- ANSWER: This confrontation is premature. For example,} 10. a3 Bxc3+ ({or} 10... Ba5 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Qh5+ Qf7 13. Qxc5 {and White wins material}) 11. bxc3 Qa5 12. Rc1 dxe3 13. Qf3 $1 O-O 14. Qxa8 Qxa3 15. Ne2 Nbd7 16. Bxf6 exf2+ 17. Kxf2 Nxf6 18. Kg1 {. Black doesn't have enough of an attack to justify handing over a full rook.}) 10. Nf3 $2 {Chekhov: '?!' '?!' Chekhov,Valery A: '?!' '?!' Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Tal had prepared a dangerous novelty against this.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky also tried' Myers & Co: 'Mikhail Tal: 'Better perhaps was'' Garry Kasparov: 'A few months later the 10th world champion tried'} 10. Bd3 {Myers & Co: '' Myers & Co: 'Euwe'} d4 (10... a6 {Myers & Co Garry Kasparov: '!?'} 11. Nge2 (11. bxa6 $2 Nc6 $1 12. a3 Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 Ne5 14. Bc2 Qa5 $11 {Myers}) 11... c4 12. Bc2 axb5 13. O-O Nc6 14. Nxb5 e5 15. b3 Bg4 16. h3 Bh5 17. Bxf6 Rxf6 18. bxc4 dxc4 19. Qxd8+ Nxd8 20. Nec3 Rb6 21. a4 Bxc3 22. Nxc3 Rb2 23. Rfc1 Nc6 24. Be4 Ra6 25. Bxc6 Rxc6 26. Ra2 Rxa2 27. Nxa2 Bf7 28. Nc3 Rb6 29. Kf1 Kf8 30. Ke1 Rb2 31. Ra1 Rb3 32. Nd1 Ke7 33. a5 c3 34. Rc1 Ra3 35. Rxc3 Rxa5 36. Rc7+ Kf8 37. Nc3 Ra1+ 38. Ke2 Ra5 39. Kf3 Bh5+ 40. g4 Bg6 41. Ne4 Bf7 42. Nd6 Bd5+ 43. Kg3 Be6 44. Nb7 Rb5 45. Nc5 Bf7 46. h4 Ke8 47. Ne4 Rd5 48. g5 h5 49. Rc5 Rxc5 50. Nxc5 Ke7 51. f4 exf4+ 52. Kxf4 Kd6 53. Ne4+ Kd5 54. Ng3 g6 55. Ne4 Be6 56. Nd2 Bd7 57. e4+ Ke6 58. Ke3 Ba4 59. Kd4 Bd1 60. Nc4 Ba4 61. Ne3 Kd6 62. Nd5 Bc6 63. Nf4 Be8 64. e5+ Kc6 65. Nd5 Bf7 66. Nb4+ Kd7 67. Nd3 Ba2 68. Nf4 Bb1 69. Kd5 Ba2+ {0-1 Short,N-Aleksandrov,A/Pula CRO 1997/}) 11. exd4 cxd4 12. a3 Ba5 13. b4 dxc3 14. bxa5 Bb7 15. Nf3 ({Garry Kasparov: 'later'} 15. Ne2 {Myers & Co Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'was played'} Qxa5 16. O-O Nbd7 17. Bc4 Nd5 18. Be7 Rf7 19. Bb4 Qb6 20. Bxc3 Ne3 21. Bd4 Nxd1 22. Bxb6 Nxb6 23. Bxe6 Nb2 24. Rfc1 Bd5 25. Bxf7+ Bxf7 26. Nd4 N2a4 27. h3 a6 28. bxa6 Rxa6 29. Rab1 Ra7 30. Rb4 Kh7 31. Nb5 Ra5 32. Rc7 Be8 33. Nd6 Bg6 34. Rc6 Nd7 35. Rc7 Nab6 36. Nc4 Nxc4 37. Rbxc4 Nf6 38. Rc3 Be8 39. Rb3 h5 40. Rbb7 Bd7 41. Ra7 Rd5 42. a4 Rd1+ 43. Kh2 Rd2 44. f3 Ra2 45. h4 Kg8 46. a5 Kf8 47. Rc5 Kf7 48. Re5 Kf8 49. K