1. e4 c5 {Garry Kasparov: ''The first manifestation of activity – the Hungarian grandmaster rather often defends the Ruy Lopez.' (Tal) Incidentally, in that opening Portisch lost to Tal in Biel and gained a very important draw with him in the 4th cycle of the match-tournament.'} 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 ({In the game Smyslov-Portisch (2nd matchgame, Portoroz 1971) White seized the initiative after} 6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 $6 ({but then everyone reverted to} 7... Be7 $1) 8. f4 $1 {(a novelty!)} Qc7 9. f5 (9. g4 $1) 9... Bc4 10. Bf3 a5 $6 11. a4 Nc6 $6 12. Bg5 Nb4 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Be2 $1 Bxb3 15. Bb5+ {.}) 6... e6 7. f4 Qb6 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'A surprise. 'I gained the impression,' writes Tal, 'that in the entire period from Biel to Varese Portisch had been working on this sharp variation and that he had guessed very successfully – since 6 Bg5 might not have happened: in recent times I have preferred other moves. And one more thing. The tournament situation (Portisch was trailing by half a point) did not demand that his opponent should definitely play for a win, and did not force him to burn his boats. On the other hand, the score of my previous games against Portisch with White (+6=1) as though obliged me to push hard. By excellently exploiting both these factors, Portisch skilfully lured his opponent into a psychological trap – in the variation White has chosen he is obliged to act decisively, and there is no room for hesitation.''} ({I should add that previously Portisch had played only} 7... Be7 {– this was also how his later game with Tal (Niksic 1983) continued:} 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O Nbd7 10. Qg3 Nc5 11. Bd3 h6 $1 12. Bh4 O-O 13. Rhe1 Nh5 14. Qg4 Bxh4 15. Qxh4 Nxf4 16. Qxf4 e5 17. Qh4 $6 exd4 18. Nd5 Qd8 19. Ne7+ Kh8 20. Rf1 (20. e5 Bg4 $3) 20... Bg4 $3 21. Rf6 $1 Kh7 $1 22. Rdf1 $1 Nxd3+ 23. cxd3 {(threatening Rxh6+)} Rc8+ $3 24. Nxc8 Qc7+ $1 25. Kb1 Rxc8 {1/2-1/2 . A fighting draw!}) 8. Qd2 (8. Nb3 {is much safer and more pragmatic, as was later played against Portisch by many of his opponents, including Spassky (Tilburg 1979), and against me by Topalov  (Novgorod 1997) and Leko (Linares 2001). But could Tal really avoid the pawn sacrifice?! On the contrary, after 7...Qb6 it is probable that he mentally chalked up another point, since he had already gained a number of spectacular wins in this variation. However – and it was this that Portisch had reckoned on! – the value of each move is now very high, and if White's attack should peter out, things will be difficult for him without his 'base' b2-pawn.}) 8... Qxb2 9. Nb3 {Garry Kasparov: 'The fashion of those years, stemming from the Spassky-Fischer match.'} ({But the main line, known since the times of the games Fischer-Geller (Monaco 1967) and Kavalek-Fischer (Sousse Interzonal 1967), later became} 9. Rb1 Qa3 10. f5 $1 (10. e5 $6 {– Volume 2, Game No.121}) (10. Be2) ({or} 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Be2 {is more modest}) 10... Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e5 $5 {etc. In these variations Portisch achieved a score of +7=4 with Black, without any defeats!} (13. Be2 Be7 $1)) 9... Qa3 ({Also possible is} 9... Nc6 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Be2 (11. Na4 Qa3 12. Nb6 Rb8 13. Nc4 Qa4 {is stronger, as in Adams-Gelfand, 3rd matchgame, Wijk aan Zee 1994}) 11... d5 $1 12. Na4 $6 Qa3 13. Nb6 d4 $1 14. O-O Rb8 15. Nc4 $2 Qb4 16. Qd1 b5 $1 {... 0-1 (Nunn-Portisch, Toluca Interzonal 1982).}) 10. Bxf6 ({Not} 10. Bd3 Be7 11. O-O h6 $1 {, Spassky-Fischer, 7th matchgame, Reykjavik 1972.}) 10... gxf6 11. Be2 {Garry Kasparov: 'One of the critical positions. This move occurred in some training games of mine with Magerramov in the late 1970s to early 1980s, where I played White (later this unexpectedly came in useful when playing Black in my world championship match with Short).'} Nc6 ({The famous 11th game of the Spassky-Fischer match ( Reykjavik 1972) went} 11... h5 12. O-O Nc6 {and crushing Zilberstein  (Latvia-Russian Federation match 1973) with} (12... Nd7 $5 13. Kh1 h4 $1 14. h3 ({or} 14. Bg4 h3 $1 15. Bxh3 Rxh3 16. gxh3 b5 {with excellent compensation for the exchange}) 14... Be7 {with a double-edged game  (Short-Kasparov, Riga 1995).}) 13. Nb1 $1 (13. Kh1 Bd7 {Soon afterwards Tal twice improved on Spassky's idea, gaining the advantage against Robert Byrne (Leningrad Interzonal 1973) by} 14. Qe3 $5 (14. Nb1 $5 Qb4 (14... Qb2 $1 {is better – into the lion's mouth!}) 15. Qe3 d5 $6 16. exd5 Ne7 17. c4 $1 Nf5 18. Qd3 h4 $2 19. Bg4 Nd6 20. N1d2 f5 $2 21. a3 Qb6 22. c5 $1 Qb5 23. Qc3 $1 fxg4 24. a4 {... 1-0.}) (14. Nd1 Rc8 15. Ne3 Qb4 16. c3 Qxe4 17. Bd3 Qa4 18. Nc4 Rc7 19. Nb6 Qa3 {is also unclear, Short-Kasparov, 4th matchgame, London 1993.}) 14... Rc8 (14... Na5 15. Nd5 $1) 15. Nb1 $1 Qa4 (15... Qb2 $2 16. N1d2 b5 17. a3) 16. c4 Na5 17. Qc3 Nxc4 18. Qxf6) {Black also fails to equalise with} 13... Qb4 (13... Qa4 14. c4 Qb4 15. Qe3 Bg7 $2 16. a3 Qa4 17. Qg3 $1 Kf8 18. Nc3 $1 {.} (18. Z0)) (13... Qb2 $2 14. a3 $1) 14. Qe3 d5 ({or} 14... f5 15. exf5 d5 16. fxe6 fxe6 17. c3 Qe7 18. N1d2 {(Robatsch-Korchnoi, Palma de Mallorca 1972)}) 15. exd5 Ne7 16. Nc3 Nf5 17. Qd3 Qb6+ 18. Rf2 $1 Qe3 19. Ne4 $1 Qxd3 20. Bxd3 Be7 21. Re1 Kf8 22. dxe6 Bxe6 23. Nec5 {(Mecking-Tal, Las Palmas 1975).}) 12. O-O Bd7 {Garry Kasparov: 'A useful developing move: Black tries to manage without ...h7-h5, not fearing the standard attack with Bh5.'} 13. f5 {!? Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'One senses a certain underestimation of the opponent, as well as an overestimation of White's chances and the habit of attacking on general grounds.'} (13. Bh5 {Ü 20/526} Bg7 $1 14. Rf3 O-O {(the bishop at h5 merely hinders the attack)} 15. Raf1 $6 (15. Rd1) 15... Na5 16. Rh3 $6 Nxb3 (16... Rac8 $5) 17. axb3 Rac8 18. Kh1 f5 $1 {, Black achieved excellent prospects (although in the end he lost). On this occasion White first wants to break up the enemy king's defences and then play Bh5 with check. However, Black's knight acquires a convenient transit point at e5 and his king safely withdraws to the queenside.}) ({Therefore the waiting} 13. Kh1 {was better, when} h5 {(cf. the note to 11.. .Nc6)} ({or} 13... Rc8 {was possible.})) 13... Ne5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (13... h5 {!?}) ({More energetic than} 13... Be7 {.}) 14. fxe6 (14. Bh5 O-O-O 15. Nd4 {'with the idea' 16.Bf7! Nf7 17.fe} Qc5 16. Kh1 Bh6 {!}) ({Lepeshkin recommended} 14. Qd4 $6 Bg7 15. fxe6 fxe6 16. Bh5+ {, but after} Ke7 $1 17. Rad1 a5 {and ...Qb4 Black has an obvious advantage.}) 14... fxe6 15. Bh5+ {Garry Kasparov: 'White cheerfully follows his plan, driving the king precisely in the direction of where it is aiming – to b8 (after ... Ra8-c8).'} Kd8 {! Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'The triumph of Portisch's preparation; he spent just seven minutes on these 15 moves.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 15... Ke7 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: ', Tal very much liked the spectacular'} 16. Rad1 $1 { b1} (16. Rxf6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Objectively, the correct path is'} Kxf6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (16... Bg7 $1 17. Rf2 h6 18. Rd1 a5 $1 ({but not} 18... Rhf8 $2 19. Nb1 $1) 19. Qf4 Rhf8 20. Qg3 Bh8 21. Rdf1 Rf6 {with an unclear game:} 22. Nd4 $1 {(threatening Nd5+!)} Qc5 23. Nf5+ $1 exf5 24. Nd5+ Ke6 (24... Kf8 25. Qh4 $1 {is dangerous}) 25. Nf4+ Ke7 {with a draw. However, the move in the game is both more logical, and stronger.}) 17. Rf1+ Kg7 18. Qg5+ Ng6 19. Qf6+ Kh6 20. Bxg6 Bg7 21. Qh4+ Kxg6 22. Qg4+ Kh6 23. Rf3 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'and wins.'} (23. Z0))) 16. Rab1 {!} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Now'} 16. Rxf6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'is unfavourable on account of'} Be7 $13 (16... Rc8 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Lepeshkin), for example:'} {Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 17. Ne2 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (17. Qe3 Qb4 $1 18. Ne2 Be7 19. a3 Qa4 20. Qb6+ Rc7 21. Rf4 Qc6 {.}) ({or} 17. Rf2 Qb4 $1 18. Qg5+ Kc7 19. Qe3 Bg7) ({it is no better to play} 17. Nb1 Qb2 18. Rf2 Nc4 19. Qd3 Qe5 20. Be2 Bh6 $1) 17... Nc4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 18. Qf4 (18. Qg5 Be7) 18... Bg7) 17. Rf2 Kc7) 16... Rc8 {[ b4] 'with the idea' 17... Qb4 Garry Kasparov: 'With the same threat of ...Qb4.'} 17. Ne2 {! Garry Kasparov: ''The knight aims for an attack on the vulnerable e6-pawn.' (Tal)'} ({The recommendation of} 17. Qd4 $6 {, 'with good tactical chances' (Lepeshkin), is dubious in view of} b5 18. Rbd1 (18. Ra1 $2 Rc4 19. Qf2 Bg7) 18... Rc6 19. Nb1 Qa4 $1 {.}) 17... Be7 ({Another possibility is the immediate} 17... Kc7 $5 18. Rxf6 (18. Nbd4 Kb8) 18... Bg7 19. Rff1 Rhf8 20. Qg5 Rg8 $1 {with a comfortable game, but for the moment Portisch is aiming to retain his extra pawn.}) 18. Nf4 Kc7 19. Be2 {Garry Kasparov: 'The return of the 'prodigal son'.'} (19. Nd4 {!} Kb8 {!} (19... Qc5 {?} 20. Rf2 {!} Nc4 21. Ndxe6+ Bxe6 22. Nxe6+ Kb8 23. Nxc5 Nxd2 24. Rxb7+ Ka8 25. Rxe7 Rxc5 26. Bf3 {!}) (19... Qc5 20. Rf2 Kb8 21. Ndxe6 Bxe6 22. Nxe6 Qc3 23. Qd5 Qc6 24. Nf4) ({or} 19... Nc4 20. Ndxe6+ Kb8 21. Qd5 b5 22. Qd4 Qe3+) 20. Nfxe6 {or} Qc3 {(Lepeshkin)} (20... Bxe6 21. Nxe6 Qxa2 22. Nd4 (22. Qb4 b5) 22... d5) 21. Qf2 $5 Bxe6 22. Nxe6 Qxc2 23. Be2 $1 Ka8 24. Qb6 Qc6 25. Qb4 Rhe8 {.}) 19... Kb8 {Garry Kasparov: 'After spending about an hour on his last four moves, Portisch has achieved artificial queenside castling.'} ({If} 19... Ng6 {, in order, at the cost of the exchange of the powerful knight, to reduce the pressure on the e6-pawn, White would also have gained sufficient counterplay by} 20. Nd4 {with the sequel} Nxf4 ({or} 20... Qc5 21. Kh1 $1 Nxf4 22. Rxf4 Rhg8 23. Rh4 {etc}) 21. Rxf4 e5 $6 22. Rf3 Qxa2 23. Qb4 Kd8 24. Rb2 Qf7 25. Nf5 {.}) 20. Na5 $2 {?} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Of course, the correct move (suggested by all the commentators) was'} 20. Nd4 $142 {! Garry Kasparov: '!?' ', with a sufficient initiative to maintain the balance: or'} {Garry Kasparov: 'not immediately'} Qc3 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'on account of'} (20... Qxa2 {??} 21. Qb4 {'with the idea' 22.Ra1}) (20... Nc4 21. Bxc4 Rxc4 22. Nfxe6 $1 (22. Ndxe6 Rxe4 $1 23. Qd5 Qe3+ 24. Kh1 b5 25. Rf3 Rb4 $1) 22... Qc3 $1 ({now} 22... Qxa2 {is dangerous in view of} 23. Nf4 $1 Rhc8 24. Kh1) 23. Qxc3 ({it is no better to play} 23. Qf2 Bxe6 24. Nxe6 Rxe4) ({or} 23. Qd3 Bxe6 24. Nxe6 Rhc8) 23... Rxc3 24. Nf4 Rc4 25. Rbd1 Bc6 {and the position is a draw.}) (20... Ka8 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 21. Nfxe6 Qc3 $1) 21. Qxc3 Rxc3 22. Bxa6 Bc8 23. Nfxe6 Ra3 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} ({or} 23... Ka8 24. Bd3) 24. Be2 (24. Bc4 $1) 24... Rxa2 $14) {Garry Kasparov: 'by his own admission, after'} 20... b5 $1 {! Garry Kasparov: 'An indication of Tal's poor form;' Garry Kasparov: 'he was planning'} 21. Nb3 {Garry Kasparov: '(forced, alas)'} (21. c4 $2 {Garry Kasparov: ', but he overlooked the reply'} Bd8 $1 {! Garry Kasparov: '.'} 22. Nb3 Nxc4 $19 {!}) 21... Bd8 $1 22. Kh1 Bb6 $17 {! Garry Kasparov: 'Thanks to the gift of a couple of tempi, Black has activated his bishop and can look to the future with optimism. However, White's position is still far from hopeless: his knights are capable of displaying truly Chigorin-like agility.'} 23. Nh5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Rc7 {!? Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (23... f5 24. exf5 exf5 25. Nf6 (25. Bd3 Rhf8 26. Ng3 Nc4 $1) 25... Be6 {Garry Kasparov: 'the recommendation'} 26. Nd5 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is not good on account of'} (26. c3 Rhd8 $1 {and now:} ({not} 26... Bd8 27. Nd5 $1) ({or} 26... Rc7 27. Nd4 $1) 27. Nxh7 {(at least regaining material)} (27. Nd4 Bf7 28. Nxb5 $6 {(a desperate chance)} (28. Bxb5 $6 axb5 29. Rxb5 Ka8 $1) 28... axb5 29. Rxb5 Ka7 30. Rfb1 Rc6 $1 31. R5b4 Nc4 $1 32. Qe1 Na5 $1 33. Bf3 Qxc3 34. Bxc6 (34. Qe7+ $2 Rc7 35. Qxd8 Qc1+ 36. Bd1 Qd2 {and wins}) 34... Qxe1+ 35. Rxe1 Nxc6 36. Ra4+ Kb7 37. Nxh7 Ne5 {and the two bishops are superior to the rook and pawn.}) 27... Ng4 $5 28. Bxg4 fxg4 {and Black has an excellent attacking position, but the game is not yet over. The move in the game is more solid, but also slower. What can be done: dynamic play was never Portisch's forte.}) ({while if} 26. Bd3 {, then} Rcf8 $1 27. Nh5 Bc8 ({or} 27... Nc4)) 26... Bxd5 27. Qxd5 Rxc2 28. Bd1 Rc3 $1 {,}) 24. Nxf6 Bc8 {! Garry Kasparov: 'Another important moment.'} 25. Ng4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({In view of the immediate events, better chances of surviving were offered by the arrest of the black queen –} 25. c3 $1 Rd8 (25... Rf8 26. Rbd1) 26. Ng4 Nc4 ({if} 26... Nxg4 27. Bxg4 Rc4 {, then} 28. Be2 $1 Rxe4 29. Nd4 $1 {with the initiative for the pawn}) 27. Bxc4 Rxc4 28. Nf6 {with the ideas of Rf3 and Nd4.}) 25... Nc4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'An unpleasant reply.'} 26. Bxc4 (26. Qf4 $6 h5 27. Nf6 Ne3 28. Rf3 Rc3) 26... Rxc4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black's rook and two bishops are very strong, and it only remains for him to bring his queen back into play to achieve complete happiness.'} 27. Nf6 {? Garry Kasparov: 'Tal considered this move to be 'a mistake, perhaps comparable with 20 Na5?'.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Beforehand he had been intending to play'} 27. Rf7 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Qxa2 $1 28. Ra1 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (28. Rc1 Rd8 $1) 28... Qxc2 29. Qxd6+ Bc7 30. Rxc7 Rxc7 31. Rc1 {Garry Kasparov: ', but just in time he noticed'} Rd8 $1 $19 {'-+' ! Tal Garry Kasparov: '!!' Garry Kasparov: '– the weakness of the back rank!'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'Therefore he recommended 'the only correct continuation''} 27. Ne3 {! Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Rxe4 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 27... Rc7 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 28. c4 {! Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 28. Qc3 {'with the idea' 29.Qc6} Bd4 $1 {!} (28... Rg8 $2 29. Qc6 $1 Bxe3 30. Qxe4 Bb7 31. Qxe3 $18 {!} Bxg2+ 32. Kg1) 29. Qd3 {?!} (29. Qc6 {! Garry Kasparov: '?'} Bb7 30. Qd7 {'with the idea' 31.Rf7; |31.Nc4 Garry Kasparov: 'was also suggested, but this is bad in view of'} Bc8 (30... Bxe3 31. Rf7 Bd5 32. Qc7+ Ka8 33. Rbf1 Rb8 $1 34. Rf8 Ba7 $1 {, winning}) (30... Rc8 31. Nxd4 Qxe3 32. Nxb5 $40 {!}) 31. Qc6 Bb7 {=}) {Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 29... d5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (29... Bb7 30. Rf7 {Garry Kasparov: '– 'if I had then lost, it would not have been so distressing.' (Tal). In my view, after'} Qb4 {?} (30... d5 {!}) (30... Ba8 $1 31. Rbf1 Rc8 32. Rf8 d5 33. Rxc8+ Kxc8 34. Ng4 $1 Rxg4 35. Qd1 Re4 36. Nxd4 Bb7 $1 {, Black has every chance of converting his extra pawn. The variations given by Tal typify his optimistic nature : he was accustomed to playing instinctively, believing implicitly not so much in the objective evaluation of the position, as in his ability to confuse the opponent and get at his king. In fact the 'sluggish' move in the game, defending the e4-pawn, is still comparatively best, although after Portisch's reply White's game begins gradually to go downhill.}) 31. Rxb7+) 30. Nxd4 (30. Nc4 $18 {! Garry Kasparov: '!!' Garry Kasparov: 'and wins'}) 30... Qxd3 31. cxd3 Rxd4 $19) 27... Qb4 $1 {!} 28. Qd1 {Garry Kasparov: ''This makes things easier for Black.'} ({'Chances of resisting were offered by} 28. Qh6 {,' writes Lepeshkin, since 'bad is} Rxc2 ({he recommends} 28... Rc7) 29. Qg7 Rd8 30. Na1 {However, after} Rf2 $1 {(again the weakness of the back rank!)} (30... Rb2 $2 31. Rxb2 Qxb2 32. Nd7+ {and Qxb2.'} (32. Z0)) 31. Nb3 Rxa2 {White's position is unenviable.}) 28... Qc3 $1 {!} 29. Rc1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'It was not satisfactory to play'} 29. Qxd6+ Bc7 {!} 30. Qe7 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} ({rejecting the difficult defence after} 30. Qd3 Rd8 31. Qxc3 Rxc3) 30... Qe5 31. g3 ({or} 31. Ng4 Qxe4 {, winning}) {Garry Kasparov: 'but not Forintos's move'} 31... Rxc2 { h2 'with the idea' Rh2 Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'on account of'} (31... Bd8 $1 32. Qg7 Bxf6 33. Qxf6 Qxe4+ 34. Kg1 Bb7 {and wins.}) 32. Qg7 Rd8 33. Qxh7) ({A more lively alternative is the trappy} 29. a4 $5 {(very much in Tal's style)} Rxa4 (29... bxa4 $2 30. Qxd6+ {;}) (29... b4 $2 30. Qxd6+ Bc7 31. Qe7 $1 Qe5 32. Ng4 Qxe4 33. Nd2 {and wins}) 30. Qxd6+ Bc7 31. Qe7 $5 Qe5 32. Ng4 $1 {now this move is possible:} Qh5 $1 ({if} 32... Qxe4 $2 {White has} 33. Nc5) 33. Rf7 Rc4 34. h3 {, although here too Black has a great advantage:} Rd8 ({also not bad is} 34... h6) ({or even} 34... Rxc2 35. Nd4 Rc3 36. Nf3 Qc5 $1) 35. Rxh7 ({or} 35. Qf6 Rxc2 36. Nd4 Rc4 37. Nxe6 Rd6) 35... Qg6 36. Ne5 Qg3 {, and if} 37. Qxd8 Bxd8 38. Nxc4 {, then} Bc7 $1 {.}) 29... h5 $1 {!} 30. Rf3 (30. Qxd6+ {is again unfavourable, this time in view of} Bc7 31. Qd3 Qe5 32. Qh3 Bd8 33. Qh4 Ka8 {, for example:} 34. Rf3 ({or} 34. Nd2 Ra4 $1) 34... Bb7 35. Qf2 Bxf6 36. Rxf6 Rxe4 37. Rf8+ Rxf8 38. Qxf8+ Qb8 {etc.}) 30... Qe5 $19 {Garry Kasparov: 'After the centralisation of his queen Black dominates the entire board: White will always have a bad endgame, but... an even worse middlegame!'} 31. Qd2 Ka8 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Essential prophylaxis.'} ({It was premature to play} 31... Bc7 $6 32. Na5 d5 33. g3 Bxa5 34. Qxa5 dxe4 35. Qb6+ $1 Ka8 36. Ra3 {.}) ({Also not good was} 31... Bd8 32. Rcf1 Bxf6 $6 33. Rxf6 Rxe4 34. Qf2 $1 Bb7 35. Na5 Qc5 36. Qf3 Bd5 {on account of the unexpected} 37. c4 $3 {with a powerful attack.}) 32. Rd1 (32. Rcf1 Bc7 33. Na5 d5 $1 {would have come to roughly the same thing;}) ({while the amusing try} 32. c3 Bc7 (32... Bd8 $5 33. Qf2 Rf8 34. Nxh5 Rxf3 35. Qxf3 Bg5) 33. Qd4 $5 {otherwise 33...d5} Rxd4 34. cxd4 Qg5 35. Rxc7 {would have been parried by} Rf8 $1 36. Nd5 ({or} 36. Kg1 Kb8 37. Nh7 Qh4) 36... Qh4 {.}) 32... Bc7 {'-+' ! 'with the idea' 33... d5! Garry Kasparov: '(preparing the decisive ...d6-d5)'} 33. Na5 {Garry Kasparov: 'An ill-fated square for the knight!'} ({However, it was no longer any better to play} 33. Rdf1 d5 34. Rh3 Bd8 $1 35. Rhf3 dxe4 {.}) 33... d5 $1 {!} 34. g3 Bxa5 35. Qxa5 Rxc2 36. Qb6 {Garry Kasparov: '(in the time scramble Tal 'forgets' to resign)'} (36. exd5 $2 Qe2) 36... Qb2 (36... h4 $5) 37. Qg1 dxe4 38. Nxe4 Bb7 39. Re3 Rf8 40. a4 {?} Qe5 {!} 0-1

