{ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'The following game, between two former World Champions, is as much poker as purely chess psychology, and remarkably so. A simplistic view is that you have perfect knowledge in chess. One of the central points I am making in this book is that no player has perfect knowledge over the board, since no-one would be physically able to calculate enough moves to give a clear resolution of what is going on in a complicated position. A player will of course see where the pieces are, and will work out what he is aiming for, and what his opponent is trying to do. The uncertainty arises about what is going to happen several moves later. --- In the Spassky-Tal game, Tal sees a sacrifice, and offers the sacrifice. There are, continuing the poker analogy, a couple of cards on each side that have yet to be revealed, and neither player will know in advance what they are. --- What options does the defender have? He can go for broke, accepting the sacrifice, with chances perhaps of a win, but also chances of a quick loss. Or he can duck the critical decision, playing for a slight but manageable disadvantage. Spassky, it turns out, was wrong on both counts in this game. Tal's sacrifice is not winning, and with best play on both sides, it would seem to be losing. The only reason why Tal won was that Spassky's aiming for a quieter struggle left him in a bad position. --- What do you think about Tal's handling of this game? Personally, I would be more inclined than most to gambit and gamble, but I doubt whether I would have played Tal's sacrifice. --- There are two thoughts that need to be considered though. First, even a World Champion like Spassky can get it spectacularly wrong under the stress of attack, so much lesser players, like your opponent across the board, can easily get it wrong too. Second, if you can somehow develop a reputation as a strong and dangerous attacker, your opponent may well become frightened, and in poker terms, can sometimes bluff himself out of making the best moves. --- The game itself was brief and impressive, an aggressive piece of attacking play in which Spassky's position soon crumbled. The one segment which requires detailed analysis is Tal's attempted piece sacrifice, and Spassky's declining of it. To make it easier to read, we give the main part relatively briefly, to give the flavour of the game, and then, as a supplementary, we look in detail of White's 17th move options.''} {Tal,M: 'Chess Informant'} 1. d4 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase'' Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov'} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. e3 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'The Queen's Indian Defence is difficult to break down.''} ({The general reputation is that} 4. g3 {is equal and drawish, while 4 e3 is equal but occasionally, as we shall see, lively for both sides. --- Was Spassky playing for a steady game? Decidedly not. Tal notes that he sensed his opponent was in a "very aggressive" mood, presumably wanting a quick revenge for his first round loss. --- In such circumstances as White against an aggressive opponent, often the most appropriate strategy is to play quietly and solidly, waiting for him to overpress. As Black, it is rather more difficult. It is still possible to play quietly, but the upshot can be a long grind for equality, where the opponent applies long-term pressure without risk of losing. --- Tal takes a different approach. At first he just develops his pieces, but then, given the slightest opportunity, starts a wild sacrificial attack. This is unwelcome for Spassky, as he would prefer to sacrifice himself. He wants to attack rather than defend, and he is not given the chance.}) 4... Bb7 5. Bd3 d5 (5... Ne4 {Ue 27/(568)}) 6. b3 $5 {ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!?' '!?'' Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: ''That day Boris was very aggressively inclined, and the variation chosen by him emphasised unequivocally his desire for a full-scale battle.' (Tal)''} (6. O-O {!?}) 6... Bd6 {!} (6... Be7 7. Bb2 O-O 8. O-O Nbd7 9. Nc3 c5 10. Qe2 cxd4 11. exd4 Re8 12. Rad1 $14 {Spassky - Sigurjonsson, Munich, 1979}) 7. O-O O-O 8. Bb2 Nbd7 9. Nbd2 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'It would be possible to write a serious examination, if the knight belongs to d2 or c3. Even though the d2 square is more modest it helps the bishop to extend control towards e5 square. It is the choice of the most strongest players in this line.' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Es wäre möglich, eine ernsthafte Untersuchung zu schreiben, ob der Springer nach d2 oder c3 gehört. Obwohl das Feld d2 bescheidener ist, hilft es dem Läufer, die Kontrolle auf das Feld e5 auszudehnen. Es ist die Wahl der stärksten Spieler in dieser Variante.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'A more active alternative is'} 9. Nc3 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase'' Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov'} {Colin Crouch: 'or'} a6 (9... c5 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Qe7 12. Qe2 Rad8 13. Rfd1 Ne4 14. Ba6 Ba8 15. Bb5 Ndf6 $10 {Petrosian,T-Polugaevsky,L/URS-ch/1970/ Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Petrosian,T-Polugaevsky,L/URS-ch/1970/' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Petrosian,T-Polugaevsky,L/URS-ch/1970/'} 16. Ne5 Nxc3 17. Bxc3 Ne4 18. Bb2 f6 19. Nd3 Kh8 20. Nf4 {with a slight initiative (Petrosian-Polugayevsky, Moscow 4th matchgame 1970).}) (9... dxc4 10. bxc4 c5 11. d5 exd5 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. cxd5 Qe7 14. Qc2 g6 15. Rad1 Rad8 16. Qc3 f6 17. e4 Ne5 18. Nd2 Nxd3 19. Qxd3 Be5 20. Bxe5 Qxe5 {0-1 (53) Artemiev,V (2708)-Giri,A (2764) Chess.com INT 2020} 21. f3 $16) 10. Rc1 (10. Qe2 {...0-1, Spassky Boris V 2590 - Miles Anthony J 2588 , Bugojno 1984 It (cat.14)}) 10... Qe7 11. Na4 (11. cxd5 $1 exd5 12. Ne2) 11... dxc4 12. bxc4 Ne4 13. Qe2 f5 $10 {Portisch,L-Nikolic,Pr/Tunis izt/1985/ Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Portisch,L-Nikolic,Pr/Tunis izt/1985/' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Portisch,L-Nikolic,Pr/Tunis ET/1985/'}) (9. Ne5 {Garry Kasparov} c5 $1 {, Filip-Botvinnik, Varna Olympiad 1962.}) 9... Qe7 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'Many of the games from this type of opening have ended up with White playing Ne5 at some stage, and/or Black playing ...Ne4. As a refresher on opening tactics, we provide at the end the supplementary game, Dizdarevic-Miles, Biel 1985, a quick win for Tony Miles.''} (9... Ne4 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase''} 10. Qc2 f5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '='} 11. Ne5 $14) 10. Rc1 (10. Qe2 Ne4) (10. Qc2 c5 {'with the idea' 11... Rac8}) (10. Ne5) {Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: 'but now in the event of''} 10... Rad8 $5 {ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!?' '!?''} (10... c5) ({Colin Crouch: 'After'} 10... Ne4 {Colin Crouch: '(Garry Kasparov) Tal did not like'} 11. Qc2 (11. Qe2 a6 12. Rfd1 f5 13. Ne5 c5 14. Nxd7 Qxd7 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Nf1 Qe7 17. f3 Ng5 18. Re1 Ne6 {...1/2-1/2, Portisch Lajos 2583 - Cao Sang 2531 , Heves 2003 Ch Hungary}) 11... f5 12. Ne5 {Colin Crouch: ';'}) 11. Qc2 (11. Qe2 {Colin Crouch: '(Garry Kasparov) he would have replied'} Ne4 $13 {Colin Crouch: '.'}) (11. Ne5 c5 12. Qe2 Ne4 13. cxd5 exd5 14. f4 Nxd2 15. Qxd2 f6 16. Ng4 Rfe8 17. Rf3 Qe6 18. Rg3 Kh8 {...1-0, Colle Edgard 2490 - Yates Frederick, Carlsbad 1929 It}) 11... c5 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'It's nearly symmetrical, but don't be fooled, this is unlikely to be heading for quiet equality. White now has the option of c4xd5, and Black recapturing in one of three ways; or d4xc5, also with three possible recaptures; or quietly developing, in which case Black has the option of two captures, and White recapturing in three ways, and so on. In such a situation it's almost a relief to release the tension, as Spassky elects to do here.''} 12. cxd5 {?! Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: '.'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Spassky was also the world champion and he does not seem to fear his dangerous opponent. Creating hanging pawns for black raises the stake for the opponent, as he has to try using the dynamic potential of the exposed pawn duo c5,d5.' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Spassky war auch Weltmeister und er scheint seinen gefährlichen Gegner nicht zu fürchten. Die Schaffung von hängenden Bauern für Schwarz erhöht den Einsatz für den Gegner, da er versuchen muss, das dynamische Potenzial des exponierten Bauernduos c5,d5 zu nutzen.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'In reply to'} 12. Rfd1 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase'' !? Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov'} cxd4 13. exd4 (13. Nxd4 Ne5 14. Be2 $11) 13... Rc8 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: ''}) ({Colin Crouch: 'or'} 12. Ne5 $5 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase'' !? Colin Crouch: '(Garry Kasparov) , Black had prepared'} cxd4 13. exd4 Rc8 {: 'in such situations the loss of a tempo is not particularly significant.'}) 12... exd5 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'Although in fact there is only one good way of recapturing.''} 13. dxc5 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'Kasparov suggests that allowing the hanging pawns might have been a psychological mistake, giving Tal the chance of sharp dynamic play. Yes and no. A World Champion has fewer excuses than the rest of us in making psychological mistakes, as he is expected to find good moves whatever. If a few moves later, Tal had sacrificed a bishop without compensation, and Spassky had won, then Spassky would have been congratulated on his brilliant intuition, provoking the unsound sacrifice.'' ?! Colin Crouch: 'Kasparov suggests that allowing the hanging pawns might have been a psychological mistake, giving Tal the chance of sharp dynamic play. Yes and no. A World Champion has fewer excuses than the rest of us in making psychological mistakes, as he is expected to find good moves whatever. If a few moves later, Tal had sacrificed a bishop without compensation, and Spassky had won, then Spassky would have been congratulated on his brilliant intuition, provoking the unsound sacrifice. Garry Kasparov: 'Transposing into a position with hanging pawns was perhaps a psychological mistake: for Tal a dynamic situation was always more pleasant!''} (13. Rfd1) ({Having said that, provocation and counter-provocation is part of the chess balance.} 13. Nh4 $5 {looks promising} Z0 ({, and if Black defends with} 13... g6 {, White can quietly retreat the knight again, content with softening up the long diagonal.}) ({. However, players from Grandmaster - maybe even World Champion - to learner should consider what happens after the classic bishop sacrifice} 13... Bxh2+ 14. Kxh2 Ng4+ (14... Z0) 15. Kg3 {. Is this good or bad? I do not know, and it would take a lot of analysis to decide. My instinct is that the sacrifice does not work here, and therefore that 13 Nh4 is acceptable and good. But there is still some anxiety for White. Would you as a player happily allow this sacrifice over the board? Or would you avoid it on principle? --- We begin so see what problems Spassky is being faced with. But there is no need for us to sympathize with him. After all, he still has the chance of a win against Tal!})) ({Colin Crouch: 'or'} 13. Bf5 {!? Colin Crouch: '(Garry Kasparov) .'}) ({Colin Crouch: 'It is not surprising that he recommended'} 13. Bb5 {!? Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov'}) (13. Rfe1 Rc8 14. Qb1 Ne4) 13... bxc5 {ChessBase: 'd5-d4''d5-d4 d5-d4 Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase: 'd5-d4'' Colin Crouch: 'The position is equal. Neither player can force an edge, and if either player tries to force matters, it could collapse against him. It follows that potentially the most effective way of trying for a win can be by provoking the opponent.'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'd5-d4' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'd5-d4'} 14. Qc3 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: '!?' '!?' '!?' '!?' Colin Crouch: 'Spassky duly provokes him, which psychologically is not such a bad idea. Indeed, one could say it's an extremely good idea. After all, in about three moves time, Spassky's position is objectively better, in the sense that if both sides play the subsequent few moves completely accurately, Spassky would be clearly ahead. But sometimes a raging tiger in search of a good meal makes it difficult for the potential prey to be quietly objective.'' ?! Colin Crouch: 'Spassky duly provokes him, which psychologically is not such a bad idea. Indeed, one could say it's an extremely good idea. After all, in about three moves time, Spassky's position is objectively better, in the sense that if both sides play the subsequent few moves completely accurately, Spassky would be clearly ahead. But sometimes a raging tiger in search of a good meal makes it difficult for the potential prey to be quietly objective. Garry Kasparov: ''And this is simply a challenge... When after the game I asked Spassky what induced him to make this move, he spread his hands: "A beggar isn't afraid of being robbed." The idea of the ...d5-d4 breakthrough becomes very real.' (Tal)''} ({If White wants to avoid facing a Tal special sacrifice,} 14. Nh4 $5 {is still worth considering,} Z0 ({, again with the idea} 14... g6 15. Nhf3 {, having weakened the long diagonal.} a6 $11) ({. But here} 14... Be5 $5 15. Nf5 Qe6 16. Nf3 Bxb2 17. Qxb2 {is about equal. In this context, the pawn exchange on c5 was mistimed.} (17. Z0))) (14. Rfd1 {!? 'with the idea' 15.Nf1}) (14. Rfe1) 14... Rfe8 {ChessBase: '' '' '' '''Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase: ''' Colin Crouch: 'Tal now thought for 20 minutes, already, as he noted, considering his forthcoming pawn sacrifice. On his next move, he thought for another half-hour before taking the plunge. The tournament book, 'Le Tournoi International d'Échecs', Montreal 1979, gives useful information on the clock times.'' Colin Crouch: 'Tal now thought for 20 minutes, already, as he noted, considering his forthcoming pawn sacrifice. On his next move, he thought for another half-hour before taking the plunge. The tournament book, 'Le Tournoi International d'Échecs', Montreal 1979, gives useful information on the clock times. Garry Kasparov: 'Now the entire black army is ready for battle.''} (14... c4) (14... d4) 15. Rfd1 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: '"It would seem that Boris was betrayed by his sense of danger," wrote Tal. This is a psychological insight, not a purely analytical chess insight. What is significant here is that Spassky was about to be shocked. Obviously he would have known all about bishop sacrifices in front of the king from infant school age, but it seems he hadn't realized that one might arise in this position. If Spassky had anticipated the sacrifice well in advance, he would have had the chance of trying to refute it. Instead, in shock, he played weaker chess.'' Colin Crouch: '"It would seem that Boris was betrayed by his sense of danger," wrote Tal. This is a psychological insight, not a purely analytical chess insight. What is significant here is that Spassky was about to be shocked. Obviously he would have known all about bishop sacrifices in front of the king from infant school age, but it seems he hadn't realized that one might arise in this position. If Spassky had anticipated the sacrifice well in advance, he would have had the chance of trying to refute it. Instead, in shock, he played weaker chess. Garry Kasparov: ''It would seem that Boris was betrayed by his sense of danger.'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Brave decision, as the rook move to e1 would make advance of the d pawn rather unlikely. Fortunatelly for white, the c-pawn sacrifice would not have been that dangerous. After the mobilization of the last reserve both armies are ready to strike.' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Eine mutige Entscheidung, denn der Turmzug nach e1 würde das Vorrücken des d-Bauern eher unwahrscheinlich machen. Zum Glück für Weiß wäre das Opfer des c-Bauern nicht so gefährlich gewesen. Nach der Mobilisierung der letzten Reserve sind beide Armeen zum Angriff bereit.'} (15. Rcd1 c4 $5 16. bxc4 Bb4 $13) ({ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'Tal notes that he had expected' Colin Crouch: 'Kasparov suggests instead'' Colin Crouch: 'Kasparov suggests instead Garry Kasparov: ''I expected''} 15. Rfe1 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase' Colin Crouch: ', when'' Rcd1 Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: 'and I was intending to give serious consideration to''} c4 $5 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'was a possibility, since accepting the pawn sacrifice leaves White worse after'' ! Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: '– But it is better for him to play''} 16. bxc4 (16. Bf5 $1 {, with sharp play, but without an obvious advantage for either side.} Nc5 {Garry Kasparov} 17. Red1 {with a double-edged game.}) (16. Bf1 Bb4 17. Qd4 $11) 16... Bb4 17. Qc2 dxc4 {Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: '.' (Tal). And indeed, after''} 18. Bxc4 Bxf3 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: '!' '!' '!' '!''} {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'not''} 19. gxf3 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: '?' '?' '?' '?'' Colin Crouch: 'Garry Kasparov: '?!''} (19. Nxf3 Bxe1 20. Rxe1 Rc8 21. Qe2 {(Garry Kasparov) White has a somewhat inferior position.}) 19... Ne5 (19... Bxd2 20. Qxd2 Ne5 21. Qe2 Rd2 $1) 20. Bxe5 Bxd2 $17) (15. h3 {!?}) 15... d4 $1 {ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: '!' '!' Colin Crouch: '?!' '?!' '?!' '?!' Colin Crouch: 'A critical decision. Many would call this controversial, unsound, a purely speculative sacrifice, but the fact remains that Tal beat Spassky in 22 moves, and Spassky was World Champion for six years, and hugely experienced and competent at attack and defence. He was also an excellent chess psychologist, who could attack very hard himself, as well as defend resourcefully when under pressure. But somehow on that day he choked. --- For Tal to have won this game so comprehensively, he must have done something special. That he beat Spassky in the first round might also have created that little bit of doubt in Spassky's mind. If you are worried about losing quickly, in a sacrificial attack maybe, you can tend to duck the critical line, only, as here, to fall into the same disaster you were hoping to avoid. --- Tal's idea is to open up the diagonals, using the queen and bishops, assisted by the knights, for a direct attack on the white king. To do this, he jettisoned his central pawns, giving up two for one, with the intention that, if Spassky were to accept the pawn sacrifice, then Tal would immediately follow up with a bishop sacrifice to force the white king out into the open.'' Colin Crouch: 'A critical decision. Many would call this controversial, unsound, a purely speculative sacrifice, but the fact remains that Tal beat Spassky in 22 moves, and Spassky was World Champion for six years, and hugely experienced and competent at attack and defence. He was also an excellent chess psychologist, who could attack very hard himself, as well as defend resourcefully when under pressure. But somehow on that day he choked. --- For Tal to have won this game so comprehensively, he must have done something special. That he beat Spassky in the first round might also have created that little bit of doubt in Spassky's mind. If you are worried about losing quickly, in a sacrificial attack maybe, you can tend to duck the critical line, only, as here, to fall into the same disaster you were hoping to avoid. --- Tal's idea is to open up the diagonals, using the queen and bishops, assisted by the knights, for a direct attack on the white king. To do this, he jettisoned his central pawns, giving up two for one, with the intention that, if Spassky were to accept the pawn sacrifice, then Tal would immediately follow up with a bishop sacrifice to force the white king out into the open. Garry Kasparov: 'A committal decision! 'Black's pieces are so ideally placed, that there is no point in delaying this breakthrough.' (Tal). From the practical and psychological viewpoint this is certainly a successful move (the later Spassky was lazy about calculating complicated and dangerous variations – to be more precise, he basically didn't want to calculate them), but from the purely chess viewpoint, as we will see, it is dubious.'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Unless somebody noticed and written about this crucial decision we do not know how long it took Tal do decide to jump the gun. Strictly speaking move like 15..a6 was keeping the chances equal, but would rob black of his chances.' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Wenn nicht jemand diese wichtige Entscheidung bemerkt und aufgeschrieben hat, wissen wir nicht, wie lange es dauerte, bis Tal sich entschloss, die Sache zu überstürzen. Streng genommen hielt ein Zug wie 15..a6 die Chancen gleich, würde aber Schwarz seiner Chancen berauben.'} ({There were of course quieter ways to play, and Fritz gives a dozen or so anodyne moves, maintaining complete equality. Moves such as} 15... Ra8) (15... Nb8 {,}) (15... h6 {,}) (15... a6 {, Ftacnik,Lubomir: '='}) (15... Nf8 {,}) (15... Kh8 {, etc. It is hard to imagine Tal wanting to play any of these. He took the plunge, and went for the attack.}) 16. exd4 cxd4 ({Black might consider} 16... Nd5 17. Qc2 Nb4 {, but only briefly, as} 18. Bxh7+ Kh8 19. Qf5 {would see White's bishops powering up on the diagonals. The problem is not so much the loss of pawns, but the exposure of the king. Quite simply, Black cannot attack if he is forced to defend his own king all the time.}) 17. Qa5 $2 {ChessBase: '?!' '?!' '?!' '?!''Tal,Mihail: '?' '?' Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase: '?!'' Colin Crouch: '?' Colin Crouch: 'Dare one suggest that, after his best years of chess, Spassky was starting to get lazy in calculations, and tending to avoid complicated variations? In fact, this was Kasparov's opinion. He may very well think that; I couldn't possibly comment. --- All the same, this is an incredibly difficult position for White to handle. Snatching the pawn is dangerous, and it does not take long for the defender to appreciate that there is a way in which Black can sacrifice a piece to bring the king out far into the open. We are just about reaching the edge of what players are able to calculate confidently over the board, remembering that we are considering not just a one-track line reaching ten moves ahead, but rather a whole thicket of forest paths, some of which might be dangerous, while some turn out to be harmless. It all adds uncertainty to the game, and usually it is the defender who is under more pressure. The attacker has made his decision, good or bad, and all he has to do is find a few natural attacking moves. The defender is uncertain whether the attack is sound, and even if the attack is unsound, it remains extremely difficult to work out how best to defend against it. One small error, we all know, can mean a humiliating loss in a few moves time. --- Even an experienced World Champion sometimes makes serious mistakes under pressure. Spassky was able to beat Kasparov twice, in wild tactics, when Kasparov was a young and fast-improving grandmaster. Here Spassky went astray. White must take the pawn, right or wrong, "good or bad", as Tal put it.'' '' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Spassky was sowing the wind, but was too scared to try the extremely dangerous and only correct move 17.Nxd4! Tal would have a very difficult task to find the best peaceful move 17...Bf4.' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Spassky säte den Wind, war aber zu ängstlich, den extrem gefährlichen und einzig richtigen Zug 17.Sxd4! zu versuchen. Tal hätte würde eine sehr schwierige Aufgabe haben, den besten friedlichen Zug 17...Lf4 zu finden.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'Before plunging into the main complications of the knight capture, we should consider the alternative,' Colin Crouch: 'Before plunging into the main complications of the knight capture, we should consider the alternative, Garry Kasparov: 'But not''} 17. Qxd4 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '?' Colin Crouch: '.' Colin Crouch: '. Garry Kasparov: '?''} {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: '. Tal's notes in 'Informator 27' give simply''} Ne5 $17 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'with a clear advantage to Black, but this is presumably a misprint, since White could just take everything on e5, when he is a pawn up for nothing.''} ({Colin Crouch: '. Kasparov corrects it to (17 Qxd4?)'} 17... Nc5 $17 {ChessBase ! Colin Crouch: ', without further comment. It is worth going on a bit, as after'} 18. Kh1 $1 Nxd3 19. Qxd3 {, the obvious discovered attacks fail to achieve anything for Black.} Z0 (19... Bxh2 20. Qf5 Bb8 21. Bxf6 Qxf6 22. Qxf6 gxf6 23. Nc4 Bf4 24. Rxd8 Rxd8 25. Re1 {, and Black has no real advantage resulting from the bishop pair. White's knights are active, and Black has a slightly damaged pawn structure. Equal.}) (19... Be5 20. Qc2 Bxb2 21. Qxb2 Qe2 22. Re1 Qxf2 23. Rxe8+ Rxe8 24. Qd4 Qxd4 25. Nxd4 {might be very slightly better for Black, but one would expect this to end up as equal too. Black will have to be careful not to drop the a-pawn.}) (19... Bf4 $1 {, followed by 20...Bxf3, is the right way forward for Black, the two bishops putting pressure on the white knights.} 20. Z0 ({. For example,} 20. Qf1 Bxf3 21. Nxf3 Bxc1 {, winning the exchange.}) ({. If White tries} 20. Qc2 Bxf3 21. gxf3 $6 {then} Qe2 $1 {wins more material}) ({, or if} 20. Bd4 Bxf3 (20... Ne4 $1 {is also good}) 21. gxf3 $2 Qd7 $1 22. Rc4 Qh3 23. Nf1 Re5 {, and ...Rh5 is decisive.}))) (17... Z0)) ({ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: 'So that leaves''} 17. Nxd4 $1 {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: ', inviting Tal to sacrifice a piece with'' '' !?} Bxh2+ (17... Qe5 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase''} 18. N4f3 (18. N2f3 Qh5) 18... Qh5 $44) (17... Bf4 18. Nf5 (18. Bb5 Rc8 19. Qd3 Rxc1 20. Bxc1 a6 $11) 18... Qe6 19. Qb4 Ne5 20. Nxg7 Kxg7 21. Qxf4 Rxd3 22. Re1 Nfd7 23. Rc7 f6 24. Rxb7 Qd5 25. Bxe5 fxe5 26. Rxd7+ Qxd7 27. Qg5+ Kh8 28. Ne4 $11 {Even the world champions are only human so the likehood of them finding the best line was not extremely high. Auch die Weltmeister sind nur Menschen, so dass die Wahrscheinlichkeit, dass sie die beste Variante finden, nicht sehr hoch war.}) (17... Be5 18. Qc2 Nd5 19. Bf1 Nb4 20. Qb1 $14) 18. Kxh2 Ng4+ {ChessBase: 'Colin Crouch: '. We shall look at this more closely at the end of the game. Almost everyone who has written up this encounter has very much wanted to prove Tal's proposed sacrifice as winning, or at least equal, but no-one has convincingly found the way, and in the end the reluctant verdict must be that it does not quite work.''} 19. Kg3 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!'} (19. Kg1 {ChessBase ChessBase: 'Tal,Mihail: 'ChessBase''