1. e4 {Gligoric,Svetozar Gligoric,Svetozar Myers Gligoric,Svetozar} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. exd5 exd5 ({Today,} 4... Qxd5 {is a popular alternative to the isolani position.}) 5. Bb5+ Bd7 ({Karpov tested Korchnoi in their Candidates' final/World Championship match with} 5... Nc6 6. Ngf3 Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 {and Korchnoi held draws each time. I just wonder if chess history would have been different if Korchnoi had stuck with the French, rather than his disastrous outings with the Dragon and Petroff's Defence.}) 6. Qe2+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What is the purpose of this check? --- ANSWER: The idea is to induce ...Be7 and then play dxc5, followed by Nb3, stubbornly hanging on to the c5-pawn.'} Be7 (6... Qe7 {is also played, but doesn't make much sense to me, since the side with the isolani should generally avoid too many swaps.}) 7. dxc5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This pawn isn't as free as it looks since Black generates with a development lead, and White expends energy to win the pawn, and even more to try to hang on to it.'} Nf6 8. Nb3 O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Intending ...Bxc5.'} 10. Be3 a6 {Recommended after Baguio 1978.} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Portisch was probably influenced by Korchnoi's difficult ending against Karpov in their Baguio World Championship match of 1978, which went'} 10... Bxc5 11. Nxc5 Qa5+ 12. Qd2 Qxb5 13. O-O-O $16 {Karpov,An-Kortschnoj,V (22)/Wch Baguio/1-0/1978/} b6 $6 ({Black still looks okay to me after} 13... Bg4) 14. Nxd7 Nbxd7 15. Kb1 Ne4 16. Qd3 $1 ({Karpov isn't about to fall for} 16. Qxd5 $4 Nc3+ {, winning the queen}) 16... Qxd3 17. Rxd3 {White's position follows the Shaolin Kung Fu principle: the best victory is when you overcome an opponent without need of force, which should be your last resource. Karpov achieved an ideal Tarrasch isolani ending, with all the winning chances. However, Korchnoi, one of the all-time great defenders, held the draw here as well, A.Karpov-V.Korchnoi, 22nd matchgame, Baguio City 1978.}) 11. Bd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The most combative move, keeping pieces on the board.'} (11. Bxd7 {Tal,M-Karpov,An/Montreal (07)/1979/½-½/ Cyrus Lakdawala: 'helps Black's development and here the players agreed to a very likely prearranged draw, M.Tal-A.Karpov, Montreal 1979.' Tal,M-Karpov,An/Montreal (07)/1979/½-½/}) 11... Ba4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: This move looks very odd. What is its purpose? --- ANSWER: Portisch clears d7 for his b8-knight, followed by ...Rc8, after which he wins back his temporarily sacrificed pawn.'} 12. Nfd4 Nbd7 13. O-O-O $1 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Sharper than'} 13. O-O {Gligoric,Svetozar: 'Ü 26/234'} Bxb3 14. Nxb3 Nxc5 $10 {Larsen,B-Portisch,L/Tilburg/1978/ Cyrus Lakdawala: 'where Portisch achieved dynamic equality, B.Larsen-L.Portisch, Tilburg 1978.'}) 13... Nxc5 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I prefer'} 13... Bxb3 $1 14. Nxb3 a5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black begins to generate serious queenside play, Y.Yakovich-Y.Shereshevsky, USSR 1985.'} 15. Nd4 Bxc5 16. Qf3 a4 17. a3 Ne5 $15 {Yakovich,Y-Sherieshevsky,M/URS/1985/}) 14. Nf5 Bf8 15. Nxc5 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Following'} 15. Qf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} Nxd3+ 16. Rxd3 Bb5 17. Rdd1 Bc4 $132 {Portisch Portisch Portisch Myers: '(Portisch 79)'} (17... g6 18. Nfd4 Bd7 {(threat: ...Bg4)} 19. h3 h6 $1 {I like Black's attacking chances, since ...a5 and ...a4 are in the air.})) 15... Bxc5 16. Qf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal escapes the e-file pin and his queen creeps closer to Portisch's king.'} Bxe3+ 17. Nxe3 $14 {Myers: '(Keene/Taulbut 84)'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'For once, Tal behaves himself, avoiding the structurally risky'} 17. fxe3 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which opens the f-file, seizes control over d4 and keeps his knight on the advanced post, at the cost of weakening his structure.'} Bd7 (17... Qc7 {Myers}) 18. g4 Bxf5 (18... Re5) 19. Bxf5 Qe7) 17... Rc8 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '?!'} (17... Bc6 18. Nf5) ({Houdini suggests} 17... d4 $5 18. Ng4 Nxg4 19. Qxg4 Rc8 20. Kb1 Qf6 {and Black looks okay here as well.}) 18. Bf5 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' Gligoric,Svetozar: '[d5]'} Rc5 $5 {Gligoric,Svetozar: ''} (18... Qc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is playable, when White must avoid'} 19. Bxc8 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??'} (19. Kb1 $142 {Myers: '!'}) 19... Rxe3 $1 20. Qf5 g6 $19 {Myers: '(Keene/Taulbut 84)'} (20... Re2 21. Rd2 Rxd2 22. Kxd2 Ne4+ {and Black wins.}) 21. Qxf6 Qxc2#) 19. Rd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal plans to double rooks and add pressure to d5.'} (19. Qf4 $5 Bc6 20. b4) 19... Bc6 {Myers: '"... when one might expect 20 hd1, followed by a methodical seige of d5, Tal embarks on an amazing, imaginative and double-edged venture to persecute Black's somewhat exposed rook on c5." (Keene/Taulbut 84)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is a clear case of provocation. Portisch - clearly not a believer in the thought: the more grandiose your plan, the less likely it's going to work - dares Tal to play 20 b4, chasing the almost trapped c5-rook.'} (19... Bd7 $2 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!?'}) 20. b4 $5 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Each side wants war, but for different reasons. Portisch is willing to sacrifice material, while Tal is willing to do the same with structure. Okay, the pretence of positional play is over. Tal is certainly cavalier about the strategic obligation which this weakening thrust entails. The idea is to chase Black's rook to oblivion, at the cost of weakening the pawn front around his king.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'For those unwilling to risk,'} 20. Rhd1 $14 {×d5 Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is the move.' ×d5}) 20... Rb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The only move.'} (20... Rc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} 21. Kb2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'costs Black an exchange for insufficient compensation.'}) 21. a4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More accurate than'} 21. Bd3 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} Rxb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 22. Rxb4 d4 $44 {Myers: '(Portisch 79)'} 23. Qf4 dxe3 24. fxe3 Qa5 {. This time Black keeps queens on the board and gets promising play for the exchange, due to White's shaky king and even shakier pawns.}) 21... Rb6 {Myers: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One way to fool an opponent is to feign meek submission for the moment, while secretly preparing an insurgency. Portisch hedges his idea with a cautionary interpolation. The idea is to goad White's pawn to a5, after which Black generates play with ...b6!.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Can Portisch sacrifice an exchange for an attack with'} 21... Rxb4 $2 22. Rxb4 d4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '- ? --- ANSWER: This line would be promising for Black, if not for the dirty trick'} 23. Qd1 {Myers: '"... forcing the exchange of Queens." (Keene/Taulbut 84, Portisch 79)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which removes queens from the board. After'} dxe3 24. Qxd8 Rxd8 25. fxe3 Bxg2 26. Rd1 Re8 27. Bd7 $1 (27. e4 $1 g6 28. Rxb7 $1 {, Black lacks full compensation for the exchange.}) 27... Rxe3 28. Bc8 $18 {Tal}) 22. a5 Rb5 {Myers: '?!' Myers: '"It was difficult to realize, having rejected ...xb4 the previous move, that now was the time to sacrifice the rook." (Keene/Taulbut 84)'} (22... Rxb4 {Myers: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} 23. Rxb4 d4 24. Qd1 dxe3 25. Qxd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is the same old story. Black falls short of full compensation for the exchange when White forces queens off the board.'} Rxd8 26. fxe3 {Gligoric,Svetozar: ''} (26. f3 {Myers} Rd5 27. Bd3 Rxa5 $13 {"The difference is that the additional move 22 a5 has allowed White's a-pawn to fall prey to the Black rook. In the resultant ending, Black's chances, minus the exchange, would be, by no means, negligible." (Keene/Taulbut 84)}) 26... Bxg2 27. Rd1 Re8 28. Bd7 Rxe3 29. Bc8 $18) 23. Qf4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The position is a patchwork of conflicting ideas. Threat: Bd3. In this way Tal avoids ...Rxb4! and ...d4 counterplay.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'As we have seen in the notes earlier, White should avoid the greedy'} 23. Bd3 Rxb4 $1 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'} (23... Ne4 $2 24. Bxe4 Rxe4 25. Qxe4 $18) 24. Rxb4 d4 {Myers: '(Portisch 79)' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'when Black gets the promising queens-on-the-board version of the exchange sacrifice.'}) 23... b6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Portisch issues a little warning to Tal: when you face a multifaceted idea and enjoy a small victory (Tal will win the exchange), just remember: you merely destroyed a copy of a copy. The original idea still lives. In this instance Portisch inflicts as much damage as he can before he loses the exchange.'} 24. Bd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The b5 outlier is surrounded. Black should generate enough play for the exchange.'} bxa5 25. Bxb5 axb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} ({More accurate than} 25... Bxb5 26. Rhd1 axb4 27. Nxd5 Nxd5 28. Rxd5 Qa5 29. Qd4 {when Rd8 is coming and I don't believe in Black's full compensation.}) 26. Rhd1 $1 axb4 27. Kb2 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Avoiding'} 27. Rxb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} Qa5 28. Kb2 Ne4 $1 29. Rb3 Ra8 {, when Black's attack is serious.}) 27... Qc8 $1 (27... Qa5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met with'} 28. Ra1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) 28. Rxb4 Ne4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): How can Tal seize the initiative?'} 29. Rd3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Refugees crowd the streets of White's queenside. This is not the moment for the safe, slow path. After this unnecessary defensive move, Black gets decent compensation for the exchange, with one pawn and an active position.'} (29. Nf5 Bd7 30. Rxe4 Rxe4 31. Qxe4 Bxf5 32. Qe2 b4 $1 33. Qd2 d4 $1 $132 (33... h6 $132 {Myers: '=' Myers: '(Portisch 79)'})) ({ANSWER: Tal should return the exchange. Black struggles after} 29. Rxd5 $1 Bxd5 30. Nxd5 f5 ({or} 30... Nc5 31. Rxb5 Ne6 32. Qd6 Nd4 33. Qc7 $1 {; White is up a clean extra pawn, with the initiative}) 31. f3 Nf6 (31... Nc5 32. Rxb5 {leaves White up a pawn with all the play}) 32. Nxf6+ gxf6 33. Rxb5 Qa6 34. Qb4 Ra8 35. Rb8+ Rxb8 36. Qxb8+ Kg7 37. Qc7+ Kg6 38. c4 {and White has decent chances to win the pawn up queen and pawn ending.}) 29... Nc5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Slightly inaccurate.'} ({Black should play} 29... Qe6 30. Rbd4 Nc5 31. Ra3 Na4+ 32. Kc1 Qe7 33. Rb4 f6 {with dynamic equality.}) 30. Ra3 Na4+ {Gligoric,Svetozar: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move cuts off the a3-rook.'} (30... Ne6 {Gligoric,Svetozar: ' d4'} 31. Qd6 (31. Qh4 {Myers}) 31... Rd8 32. Qe5) 31. Kc1 Bd7 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '?' Gligoric,Svetozar: '?' Gligoric,Svetozar: '?' Myers: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Shifting into overdrive isn't of much use when you only have a few drops of gas in the tank.'} (31... h6 $1) ({Correct was} 31... Qe6 {.}) 32. Qd6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It is in the nature of a siege that day by day, the defender grows weaker and weaker through slow starvation. Except in this case, I'm not sure who is the attacker and who is defending. This is the second best continuation.'} ({Komodo suggests} 32. Nxd5 $1 {allowing} Re2 33. Qc7 $1 {and Black must trade queens, since} Qe8 $4 {is met with} 34. Raxa4 $1 {when the a4-rook can't be taken due to Rb8.}) 32... Bc6 (32... Bf5 33. Rxb5 $1 Rxe3 34. Rb8) ({After} 32... Re6 33. Qxd5 h6 34. Qd2 {White is on his way to consolidation.}) 33. Rd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal continues with uncharacteristic caution.'} ({Once again, correct was} 33. Nxd5 $1 Bxd5 34. Qxd5 Nc3 ({or} 34... Re2 35. Qd3 {and everything is covered}) 35. Qd2 Ne4 36. Qd4 {. White's powerfully centralized major pieces ensure a safe king, while pocketing an extra exchange.}) 33... h6 34. Rf4 $1 Qe6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The ending isn't holdable.'} ({Portisch still had practical chances with the line} 34... Qa8 35. Qa3 Qb8 36. Qb4 Nb6 37. Rg4 $1 Nc4 38. Nxc4 dxc4 39. Qc3 g6 40. Rh3 {and White is winning.}) 35. Qxe6 fxe6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why did you give Portisch's last move a dubious mark. It looks to me like Black has a pretty good ending. --- ANSWER: It appears as if Portisch generated good play for the exchange, since his centre rolls with ...e5 next. Tal's next move dispels this illusion.'} 36. Ng4 $1 {e5 Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One of the greatest psychological shocks in chess is when our opponent assaults us at a point we considered invulnerable. Tal intends to blockade with Ne5 next, which would be intolerable. Portisch's problem: Black is unable to support an ...e5 push without overextending.' e5} e5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is a case of the cure being worse than the original complaint.'} ({Portisch couldn't stomach} 36... Re7 37. Rg3 Kh7 38. Rf6 $1 {(threat: Rxh6+, followed by Nf6+ and Rg8 mate)} Kh8 39. Rf8+ Kh7 40. Rd8 $1 Be8 (40... e5 $2 {loses to} 41. Rd6 Be8 42. Rxh6+) 41. Ne5 {with a dominating position.}) 37. Rf5 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '+-' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The players fight for control of e5, like rival siblings squabbling for a toy.'} Nc5 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE  (combination alert): How gratifying when our once risky idea (Tal's 20 b4!?) is verified with a winning position later on. Find a few accurate moves and it becomes clear that Black's game is destitute of all forms of counterplay.'} (37... e4 {Gligoric,Svetozar} 38. Rg3 $1 Re6 39. Nf6+ Kh8 40. Nxd5 {wins.}) 38. Rc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Double attack/overloaded defender.'} Bd7 39. Rxe5 {Myers: '"I think that the whole opening line with 11 d3 followed by 0-0-0 is favorable for White, who also may have possible improvements in moves 18-20." (Portisch 79)' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. "Choose your words carefully. I have a literal mind and would not like to see you suffer, through an unfortunate 'misunderstanding'," threatens White's rook. Black pawns continue to vanish without a trace, as if behind a magician's veil.'} 1-0

