aetsky/Chetverik: '?' '?' Garry Kasparov: '?'} 19. Qxh6 $18 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Nh5 is coming.'}) 19. Nh5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is targeting g7 with all his ammunition.' GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Black is a whole rook up, but the knight on a1 does not count while the queenside is underdeveloped. In the meantime, White has dangerous kingside threats already.'} (19. Bxg7 {is premature:} Kxg7 20. Nh5+ Kg6) 19... e5 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Flesch is at the height of the moment. His best chance for survival is to speed up his development.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Not'} 19... Qb5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would not work because of'} 20. Nxg7 Kh7 21. Rxa1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'offers White too much compensation for the exchange:'} Kg6 22. Qf4 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'and wins.'} Qxb2 (22... Rg8 23. Be5 $18 {Tal}) 23. Qg3+ Kxf6 (23... Kh7 $2 24. Rf1 Qc2 25. Nxe6 Rg8 26. Nf8+ $1 {and Black is mated}) 24. Nh5+ Ke7 25. Qc7+ Bd7 26. Qc5+ Kd8 27. Qxf8+ Kc7 28. Qc5+ {and White's attack is very strong. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and White's attack is very strong.'}) (19... g5 $2 20. Bxg5 Qb5 21. d5 $1 $18 {[%cal Gb5d5,Gh5f6] cuts the queen off the kingside due to} Qxd5 $2 22. Nf6+) 20. dxe5 {GM Mihail Marin: '!' GM Mihail Marin: 'A very instructive phase of the game. Both sides are following their plans consistently without letting themselves distracted by mirages.'} (20. Bxg7 Qg6 21. Bxf8 Kxf8 22. Qe2 Nc2 23. Qxe5 Qg5 24. Qd6+ Kg8 25. Nf6+ $11 {only leads to a perpetual.}) 20... g5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'For the moment Flesch is equal to the occasion: Black has successfully repelled the first wave of the attack, and retains his extra material.'} (20... Nb3 {is not good, as Black might need the tempo White will have to use on Rxa1 later on: Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is not good, as Black might need the tempo White will have to use on Rxa1 later on:'} 21. axb3 Kh7 22. Nxg7 Qxb3 23. Rc1 {with an attack.}) 21. e6 $5 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Once again the sharpest choice – White wants to retain the bishop.' GM Mihail Marin: '!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'Another remarkable move while being a rook down. White prevents Black from developing naturally with ...Bg4 or ...Bf5. Other moves offer less.' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Once again the sharpest choice – White wants to retain the bishop.'} ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Also possible was' Garry Kasparov: 'There is no particular choice: unattractive are both'} 21. Rxa1 Bg4 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!' Garry Kasparov: '!'} 22. Ng3 Rfd8 $1 {GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal'} 23. Bxd8 Rxd8 24. Qc3 Rc8 {and Black is probably okay despite the pawn deficit. GM Mihail Marin: '=' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black is probably okay despite the pawn deficit.'}) ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'A further alternative is' Garry Kasparov: 'and'} 21. Be7 Bf5 $1 22. Nf6+ Kg7 23. Rxa1 (23. Bxf8+ Rxf8 24. Rxa1) 23... Rfd8 $1 24. Bxd8 Rxd8 25. Qe2 (25. Qc3 Rd3 $1 26. Qc2 Qa5 {and Black has reasonable counterplay. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black has reasonable counterplay.'}) 25... Bd3 $15 {GM Mihail Marin: '=' GM Mihail Marin: 'Tal' Garry Kasparov: 'with some advantage to Black.'}) (21. Ng7 Bg4 22. Rxa1 Rfd8 23. Qc2 Rd5 $11) 21... Qxe6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Alternatively:' GM Mihail Marin: 'Black has to spoil his coordination in order to keep the queen's communication with the kingside alive.'} (21... fxe6 $2 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would weaken the king fatally:' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'would weaken the king fatally:'} 22. Qd3 $18 (22. Rxa1 $2 e5 $1) 22... Rxf6 {Garry Kasparov: 'was dangerous for Black;'} 23. Nxf6+ Kf7 24. Nh5 Qxb2 25. Qh7+ Ke8 26. Rd1 {and Black will be mated.}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'but by'} 21... Bxe6 {Raetsky/Chetverik: 'is met by' GM Mihail Marin: '?'} 22. Bxg5 $1 $16 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!' '!' Garry Kasparov: '!'} (22. Bd4 $2 Rfd8 $1 23. Nf6+ Kf8 $11) 22... Qc5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Bxh6 Rfd8 25. Qf4 Qc4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ', he could have forced the exchange of queens:'} 26. Rxa1 ({not} 26. Qg5 $2 {because of} Qxf1+ 27. Kxf1 Bc4+) 26... Qxf4 27. Bxf4 Bxa2 28. Be5 {with an unclear game – Kasparov. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'with an unclear game – Kasparov.' Garry Kasparov: 'with a rather unclear ending.'}) 22. h3 $1 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '!?' '!?' GM Mihail Marin: '!!' GM Mihail Marin: 'The double exclamation mark does not refer to the objective merits of this move (after which Black could have forced a draw) but to the whole concept. Tal simply parries the threat ...Qg4 without rushing in with his attack, nor with winning back the knight.' Garry Kasparov: 'Typical Tal ingenuity! White, plays on a rook down, as though nothing has happened, although his attack is non-forcing and it can easily come to a standstill;'} ({Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is aiming to prevent ...Qg4 due to the following line:' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'White is aiming to prevent ...Qg4 due to the following line:' Garry Kasparov: 'for example, in the event of'} 22. Rxa1 {GM Mihail Marin: '?!' GM Mihail Marin: 'did does not offer much'} Qg4 $1 23. Ng3 Qf4 $1 24. Qc3 Be6 $13 (24... Bg4 {and Black is better. Raetsky/Chetverik: 'and Black is better.'})) ({However, an interesting alternative was Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 22. Bg7 $5 Qg6 23. Bxf8 Be6 (23... Qxh5 $6 24. Qd8 (24. Ba3 Be6 {.}) 24... b6 25. Bb4+ Kh7 26. Rxa1 {is dangerous for Black}) 24. Be7 Qxh5 25. Rxa1 {and Black's worse king gives White some pressure.}) ({But} 22. Bc3 $1 {was stronger:} f6 23. Rxa1 {Black's development problems and White's pressure on f6 offer better chances despite the missing exchange.} Bd7 (23... a5 24. Re1 Qf5 25. h3 Be6 26. g4 Qd5 27. Qe2 Rae8 28. Rd1 Qc4 29. Qe3 $16) 24. Nxf6+ Rxf6 25. Bxf6 Bc6 26. Bc3 Qd5 27. Qxd5+ Bxd5 28. f3 $16) 22... Qf5 $2 {Raetsky/Chetverik: '?!' '?!' Raetsky/Chetverik: 'Or:' GM Mihail Marin: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'The psychological effect of the extra material apparently has its effect: Black wants to punish his opponent without fail.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'and not'} 22... Bd7 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 23. R