{GM John Van der Wiel: 'Tactics can break steel, but not traditions by GM John van der Wiel If you are Dutch and it is January, you think about Wijk aan Zee. What else? The second oldest annual chess tournament in the world is our national pride. Having participated 28 times (including 1 Juniors and 2 IM groups) in Hoogovens/Corus/Tata, I know a little something about it. In this article I would like to tell you briefly about its history (which you can skip, if you only want to see chess positions) and then the real 'work' begins: tactical exercises this time. Most of them certainly doable and the final one really tough. A brief history In 1937 a few employees of Hoogoven Steel Company thought of organising a small chess tournament. It was to take place in the summertime, but due to some practical reasons it became winter, early 1938. Originally meant for other employees only, it was played in some groups of 4 participants, but very soon the tournament grew prosperously. Players of a national level appeared, doing battle in groups of 10. Even during wartime the tournament expanded, only once (I believe in 1942) it had to be cancelled. In 1945, during the so-called 'hunger winter', conditions were quite hard for many people. But at the closing ceremony the famous Dutch split-pea soup was served. It may not be to everyone's fine taste, but it is very nutritious and was widely appreciated then.     This is one of the traditions that have remained, just like the groups of 4 (first weekend + midweek) and 10 (later) for the amateurs. In theory it is still possible to start in the lowest group  (10) and promote all the way to GM-group A, now called the Masters. The tournament venue did change, from IJmuiden to Beverwijk to Wijk aan Zee, where it has been for many decades now. Part of the charm of the event is the social life. There are many participants in all those groups and those who live too far away need to sleep in or near Wijk aan Zee. In the evenings there are not that many places to go to in the seaside village, so by force chess players keep meeting each other and create their own entertainment. New friendships are forged and many players of all levels keep coming back year after year. 1982 In 1982 I got my second opportunity to play in the GM-group. Since 1979 I had been an IM and before the tournament some of my friends said:"John, it is time to make your first GM-norm, here in Wijk aan Zee." "Would be nice", I replied, "but have you seen the list of participants? 7,5 out of 13, not going to happen!" "Oh no? Wanna bet ?" So I bet, against myself. We had decided that the loser would have to swim in the North Sea, right at the end of the tournament. That is cold, but a surprisingly good cure against a common cold! And my friends, who had won the bet, joined me very sportingly. Only, the head of the tournament's press service had somehow found out about our private bet. He had made some phone calls and when we got to the beach, it was full of spectators, journalists and photographers!   In the first half of the tournament I had a great score (with two very lucky wins) and then I needed only 2,5 out of 6 for the title norm. Which I made very professionally with 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1/2. From that last week comes the following game:'} 1. c4 e6 2. Nc3 Bb4 3. e4 c5 4. Nb5 Nc6 (4... d6 {Ü 32/10 GM John Van der Wiel: 'looks a little safer. White can still opt for aggressive play with'} 5. a3 Ba5 6. b4 {though.}) 5. a3 Ba5 6. b4 cxb4 7. Bb2 Kf8 ({GM John Van der Wiel: 'not'} 7... Nf6 8. e5 Ne4 9. Qg4 $16 bxa3 10. Rxa3 Bxd2+ 11. Ke2) 8. e5 $16 Nge7 (8... f6 $5 9. Qf3 $5) 9. Nf3 a6 {GM John Van der Wiel: 'Looking for clarity when nothing else appealed'} (9... h6 10. Be2 (10. Bd3)) ({but maybe the odd-looking} 9... d5 10. exd6 Nf5 11. c5 b6 {could be tried.}) 10. Nd6 Nf5 11. Be2 $5 Nxd6 12. exd6 bxa3 13. Rxa3 (13. Bxa3 e5 $1) 13... Bb4 14. Rd3 Bc5 {GM John Van der Wiel: '?'} ({It was high time for some prophylactic stuff:} 14... f6 $1 15. Qa1 {and now probably} e5 16. Nxe5 $1 Qa5 17. Nxc6 Qxa1+ 18. Bxa1 bxc6 {and Black still lives}) (14... b5 15. Qa1 $1) 15. O-O $32 a5 ({GM John Van der Wiel: 'The intended'} 15... Nb4 {GM John Van der Wiel: 'would fail to'} 16. Rb3 Bxd6 ({or} 16... a5 17. Qa1 f6 18. d4) 17. Qa1 f6 18. Ba3 $18 {GM John Van der Wiel: '!'}) 16. Qa1 f6 {GM John Van der Wiel: '[#] * Exercise 1 Now it is time to step into the shoes of the great Mikhail Tal. How would you pummel the Dutch youngster? In this case, not only the first move is important, but after the normal reply certainly also White's second!'} 17. Ng5 $1 {GM John Van der Wiel: 'Threatening mainly 18. Ne4 and 18. Rf3'} e5 {GM John Van der Wiel: 'Black's only hope is to block the long diagonal (get square d4, sometimes Ra8-a6).'} 18. Kh1 $1 {GM John Van der Wiel: '[#] This sublime quiet move is the beginning of the end for Black. White's only wish is to play f2-f4, getting all his pieces involved in the attack. Well worth a tempo and there is not much Black can do about it.'} Bd4 ({GM John Van der Wiel: 'After'} 18... fxg5 19. Rf3+ { e5+-} (19. Bxe5 Rg8 20. Rf3+ Ke8 21. Bd3 {Bd3 there is no survival.})) ({Even after} 18... Nd4 {White could lauch} 19. f4) 19. f4 Qb6 {GM John Van der Wiel: '[#] Exercise 2 Victory must be near, but how do we continue the attack here?'} 20. Bc3 $1 {[ fe5] GM John Van der Wiel: 'All White needs to do, is to keep the threats (mainly f4xe5) intact and not allow Black to change the hopeless character of the position.'} (20. fxe5 Qxb2) (20. Rb1 Bxb2) 20... Bxc3 ({What else?} 20... Kg8 21. c5 $1) (20... Ke8 {when several things (such as 21. fe5) should do the job. A nice line is} 21. Bh5+ g6 22. Nxh7 gxh5 23. Nxf6+ Kd8 24. Nd5 $1 Qc5 25. fxe5 {and White wins}) (20... exf4 21. Rxd4 $1 {lose without a fight.}) 21. Qxc3 Kg8 (21... Qb4 22. Qa1) (21... Nd4 22. fxe5 Nxe2 23. Qa1) 22. fxe5 (22. c5 Qb4 23. Qa1 {should also work.}) 22... Nxe5 23. Ne4 h5 24. Nxf6+ (24. Nxf6+ {GM John Van der Wiel: 'and Black resigned. 1 - 0 A charming finish would have been'} gxf6 25. Qxe5 fxe5 26. Rg3+ Kh7 27. Bd3+ {and Black is mated As you saw, attacking skills are not only brute force, but include very efficient 'silent moves' as well. There is some of that in the next game, too. 1-0 (27) Tal,M (2605)-Van der Wiel,J (2470) Wijk aan Zee 1982 MCD [GM John Van der Wiel]}) 1-0

