{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Everyone has a boogie man out there. You know the one I mean: the guy is rated about the same as you, yet he beats you to a bloody pulp every time you play him! Korchnoi one of the greatest defensive players of all time, normally gave Tal fits. Here is a game where Tal overcame his great rival.'} 1. e4 {Wells Wells} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5 e6 7. Qd2 Be7 8. O-O-O O-O 9. Nb3 a5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Openings like the Richter-Rauzer Sicilian are so venomous, there is the danger that the snake will thrash about wildly and accidentally bite itself. If you recall, Koblencs played 9...Qb6 on Tal in the first chapter of the book. --- QUESTION: Isn't Black's last move strategically reckless? Korchnoi just gave Tal control over b5. --- ANSWER: It does appear on the surface that Black's ill-equipped army roars defiance by banging their weapons on their shields, the way Mel Gibson's army did, when facing Edward Longshanks' troops, in Braveheart. Black also gets something in return: the strategic threat ...a4 and ...a3, creating a puncture on the dark squares around White's king. --- QUESTION: But why can't White simply play a4 to prevent this problem? --- ANSWER: That is exactly what Tal did, and it doesn't come without cost. First, it allows Black a future ...Nb4, where it may be inconvenient for White to move his c3-knight and then play c3. Secondly, Korchnoi's ...a5 induced a weakening in the pawn front around Tal's king.'} 10. a4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Tal isn't about to allow the black a-pawn to run amok, and fixes it on its square.'} (10. Nd4 a4 11. a3 d5 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Bxe7 Ncxe7 14. Bc4 Qa5 15. Nxd5 Nxd5 16. Qxa5 Rxa5 17. Nf5 Nf6 18. Nd6 Bd7 19. Nxb7 Rg5 20. Rhg1 Bc6 21. Nd8 Bd7 {= Jansa - Pavlovic, Nis, 1985}) 10... d5 {Wells: 'No longer fully respectable. If Black could release the tension so early, 9 b3 would be out of commission.'} 11. Bb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I think this is White's only shot at an edge.'} (11. exd5 {got Karpov nothing after} Nxd5 12. Bxe7 ({not} 12. Nxd5 $6 Bxg5 {and Black stands slightly better}) 12... Ncxe7 13. Nb5 Bd7 14. Be2 Nf5 15. N3d4 Nxd4 {. The players agree to a draw in a position in which I already prefer Black, A.Karpov-G.Kasparov, World Championship (Game 37), Moscow 1985.}) 11... dxe4 $146 {Wells: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A mistake must be recognized, before it can be corrected. I don't like this move, which opens the game when lagging in development.'} (11... Nxe4 {Ü 39/235}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is better off with'} 11... Nb4 {Wells is rather better, although then too White has a decent choice between the 'French-style'} 12. e5 {or keeping the tension with} (12. Rhe1 $5) 12... Nd7 13. Bxe7 Qxe7 14. f4 b6 15. Rhe1 Nc5 16. Nd4 Bd7 {and maybe White holds a touch of an edge due to his grip on d4 and b5, Y.Balashov-A.Khalifman, Minsk 1986.}) 12. Qxd8 Bxd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Perhaps the idea is to transfer the bishop to either c7 or b6.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black also fails to equalize after the more natural'} 12... Rxd8 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', M.Steadman-T.Doyle, New Zealand 1997. Now'} 13. Nxe4 (13. Rxd8+ Bxd8 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Nxe4 {also looks like a tough ending for Black.}) 13... Nxe4 14. Bxe7 Rxd1+ 15. Rxd1 $16 Nxf2 16. Rd2 Ne4 $13) 13. Rhe1 $5 {!} (13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Nxe4 $13) (13. Kb1 e5 14. h3 Nb4 15. Nc5 Bf5 $15) 13... Na7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi fears further damage to his structure with Bxc6.'} (13... Bb6 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Nxe4 f5 16. Nf6+ Kg7 17. Nd7 $1 {!} Bxf2 18. Re2 Bxd7 19. Rxd7 $16) (13... h6 {!?} 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. Nxe4 $14 {Tal}) 14. Bc4 {!} (14. Bf1 {=}) ({Missing} 14. Nxe4 $1 {, and if} Nxb5 ({or} 14... Nxe4 15. Bxd8 Nxf2 16. Rd2 Nxb5 17. axb5 Ng4 18. Nc5 $1 {and White has pressure, since} Nxh2 $2 {can be met with} 19. Be7 Re8 20. Na4 $1 f6 21. Nb6 {and wins, as} Ra7 {is met with} 22. Bc5) 15. Nxf6+ gxf6 16. Bh6 Re8 17. Re3 $1 Kh8 18. axb5 Bb6 19. Rg3 Bxf2 20. Rf3 Bb6 21. Rxf6 {when Black is in deep trouble.}) 14... h6 {?!} (14... Nc6 {!?} 15. Nc5 $16 {!?} (15. h3 b6 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 17. Nxe4 Be5 18. g3 $13)) 15. Bxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} gxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'There is much hand-wringing and teeth gnashing in Black's kingdom, mainly due to his inability to develop his queenside pieces.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After'} 15... Bxf6 16. Nxe4 { d6} Be7 (16... b6 17. Nxf6+ gxf6 18. Rd6 Rb8 19. Red1 $16) 17. Nbc5 (17. f4 Nc6 (17... b6 {is met with} 18. c3 Bb7 19. Rd7 Bh4 20. g3 Bc6 21. Rxa7 Rxa7 22. gxh4 Bxa4 23. Nd4 {which also favours White}) 18. Bb5 {Black experiences serious problems completing his queenside development.}) 17... b6 18. Nd7 Bxd7 19. Rxd7 $14) 16. Nxe4 {Wells: 'Black has holes, dubious structure and developmental problems - an opening experiment not to repeat!'} f5 17. Nd6 Bc7 18. g3 $1 {! Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE: (combination alert): White's position looks promising, yet it seems like one where Tal can only build, rather than actually strike. His sensitive eye spotted a geometric anomaly, leading to a winning position. If you find his next move, profits await White. How would you continue here?'} (18. Re3 Rd8 19. Red3 Nc6 $11 {=}) (18. Nc5 $5 {!?} b6 19. Nxc8 Bf4+ $8 (19... Raxc8 20. Nxe6 $1 {'' !}) 20. Kb1 Raxc8 $1 {!} (20... Rfxc8 21. Nd7 Bc7 (21... Rc6 22. Bb5) (21... Rxc4 22. Nxb6 Rac8 23. Nxc8 Rxc8 24. Rd7 $14) (21... Rc7 22. Ba6) 22. Ba6) 21. Nxe6 Rxc4 22. Nxf8 Kxf8 23. Rd7 Nc6 $13 {Tal}) (18. Re2 Rd8 19. Red2 $14) 18... b6 $2 {? Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A small taste of freedom only whets your appetite for more. Black doesn't have the time to slowly unravel his queenside, and his last move is a bit like attempting to parry a belligerent bar drunk's intended blow with reasoned argument.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi should have tried'} 18... Rd8 {!? Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or'} 19. Nb5 (19. Nxf5 $6 {?!} Rxd1+ 20. Kxd1 exf5 21. Re7 Bd6 22. Rxf7 Kh8 $13) (19. Nxc8 Raxc8 (19... Rdxc8 20. Be2 Bb6 21. f4 Rc7 22. Rd6 Nc6 23. Red1 {. White retains pressure, but with no imminent breakthrough.}) 20. Rxd8+ Rxd8 21. Rd1 $14) 19... Nxb5 20. Bxb5 $14 {Tal} b6 21. Bc6 Rb8 22. Nd4 Bd6 23. Nb5 Be7 24. Rxd8+ Bxd8 25. Rd1 Be7 {and White stands better, yet has no easy inroad into Black's well-fortified camp}) 19. Nxf5 $1 $18 {Wells: '!' '!' !! Cyrus Lakdawala: '!!' '!!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Clearance. --- "Soon," murmurs the knight, as he plots petty revenges against his many enemies. Tal decides that his position, having reached its apex, isn't going to get any readier, so now is the time to strike.'} (19. f4 Bd7 20. Nxf5 Bxa4 21. Nxh6+ Kg7 22. Ng4 b5 23. Bd3 Bxb3 24. cxb3 $14 {/}) 19... exf5 20. Bd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Be6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop's lips move silently, reciting a prayer. This concession (it's psychologically difficult to return an insult with silence), can't be described as morale building for Black, yet it is necessary.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Korchnoi, a master of defence, makes a pragmatic lesser-evil choice, shorn of sentiment, correctly rejecting'} 20... Rb8 {? Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?'} 21. Re7 {'+-' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. The seventh rank isn't such an uncrossable barrier after all. Black must return the piece with a completely lost position.'}) 21. Bxa8 Rxa8 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After'} 21... Bxb3 $142 {%03'better is'} 22. Rd7 (22. cxb3 Rxa8 23. Rd7 Rc8 24. Kb1 Bb8 25. Rc1 Re8 26. Rb7 Kf8 27. f4 Kg7 28. Rxb6 {Black's pieces are completely tied down to the defence and he won't survive.}) 22... Bxa4 23. Rxc7 Rxa8 24. Ree7 {'' Tal}) 22. Nd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why is Tal's combination so great? He only got a rook and pawn for two bishops. --- ANSWER: I gave Tal the double exclam, not for the depth of his combination, but for his ability to assess the aftermath. An assessment is often a subjective mental construct, and it's possible for two players of even strength to come to opposite conclusions of a single position. But not in this one. Komodo assessment: '+1.63'. The problem is Black's light-squared bishop must move, after which White's rook infiltrates e7, and black pawns begin to fall at an alarming rate.'} Bd5 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Alternatively:'} 22... Re8 23. Nxf5 (23. Nxe6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} fxe6 24. Rd7 Bb8 25. Rb7 Nc6 26. Rxb6 Nd4 27. f4 $18 (27. c3 Nf3 28. Rexe6 Rxe6 29. Rxe6 {(threat: Re8+ and Rxb8)} Ba7 30. Kd1 $1 {is hopeless for Black.}))) (22... Bd7 23. Re7 $18) (22... Bc4 {is probably Black's best shot at resistance:} 23. b3 Bd5 24. Nb5 Nxb5 25. Rxd5 Nc3 26. Rd7 Rc8 27. Ree7 Bb8 28. Rxf7 Be5 29. Rxf5 Re8 30. Rff7 {and Black can play on, but is still losing in the long run.}) 23. Re7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Our opponent's progress makes our own failures all the more painfully apparent. Tal's threats spring up like unwanted mushrooms (the kind you can't put on a pizza!) in my backyard.'} (23. Nxf5 Be6) 23... Rc8 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Or'} 23... Bd8 24. Nxf5 {'+-'} Be6 25. Re8+ Kh7 26. Ne3 Nc6 27. Rd6 Be7 28. Rxe6 Rxe8 29. Rxc6 {with two extra pawns and an easy win.}) 24. Nb5 {Wells: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'What a blessed feeling when we get an accounting for past losses against us.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's tangled defenders act the way some of our not-so-wonderful friends do when they agree to help you, but actually have no intention of doing so:'} 24. Nb5 {! Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Nxb5 (24... Z0) 25. Rxd5 Nd6 ({after} 25... Na7 26. Rdd7 {White's rooks gaze at c7, a7 and f7 hungrily, like beggars outside a bakery window}) 26. Rxc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'} Rxc7 27. Rxd6 $18 Rb7 28. Rxh6 {once again leaves White up two pawns.}) 1-0

