1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {Timothy Taylor: 'I have played'} g6 {Timothy Taylor: 'myself to disconcert Exchange players, but the problem is that I don't see an equalizer after the theoretical and strong'} ({Once again, my preferred move order when Black wants a kingside fianchetto is} 3... a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. c3 Bd7 6. d4 Nf6 7. O-O g6 {which reaches Parma-Keres in Chapter Three.}) 4. O-O (4. c3 {The surprise works on Tal! Now Black gets back into the MS.} a6 5. Ba4 d6 6. d4 Bd7 7. O-O {½-½ (39) Tal,M-Mukhametov,E Moscow 1991 Slay the Spanish [Timothy Taylor]} ({Another reason I don't like Mukhametov's move order is the Capablanca finesse} 7. Bg5 $1 {– see the game Capablanca-Thomas for the conclusion.})) (4. d4 $1 {. Therefore I don't recommend this move order – except as a psychological surprise!}) 4... Bg7 5. c3 a6 6. Ba4 d6 7. d4 Bd7 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Nxe5 dxe5 10. f4 {Timothy Taylor: 'Tal tries to blow his much lower rated opponent off the board, but the MS is resilient!'} ({A more restrained approach was tried in the following encounter between two strong American GMs, though White also got nothing:} 10. Be3 Nf6 11. Nd2 Bxa4 12. Qxa4+ Qd7 13. Qb3 Qb5 14. f3 Qxb3 15. Nxb3 O-O-O {(once again we see long castling for Black!)} 16. Kf2 Ne8 17. c4 f5 $1 {and Black had good counterplay and made an eventual draw in N.De Firmian-R.Byrne, Reykjavik 1986.}) 10... Bxa4 11. Qxa4+ b5 12. Qb3 exf4 (12... Qe7 $5 13. fxe5 Bxe5 14. a4 Rb8 15. axb5 axb5 16. Nd2 { f3}) 13. Bxf4 {Timothy Taylor: 'At this point Tal probably didn't mind the isolated e-pawn, but it soon becomes a serious weakness.'} Nf6 14. Nd2 (14. Bg5 Qe7 $11) ({Tal has to stop to complete his development as} 14. e5 Nd5 15. Rd1 c6 {favours Black – White can't exploit the pin and the isolani is weaker than ever.}) 14... O-O 15. Rae1 Ng4 $1 (15... Nd7) 16. Nf3 Qe7 {[ e5]} 17. e5 $1 $146 ({If} 17. h3 $2 Qc5+ 18. Kh1 Nf2+ 19. Kh2 ({or} 19. Rxf2 Qxf2 20. Be3 Qg3 {and the queen escapes!}) 19... Nd3 {wins material.}) 17... Qc5+ 18. Kh1 Rae8 ({Timothy Taylor: 'But'} 18... Nf2+ $2 19. Rxf2 Qxf2 20. Be3 $18 {Timothy Taylor: 'now wins for White as there is no bolt hole on g3!'}) 19. e6 {Timothy Taylor: 'White must play for equality, as otherwise the e-pawn is a permanent weakness.'} fxe6 20. Rxe6 Kh8 21. Bg3 {[×f2]} Rxe6 {Timothy Taylor: '?' Timothy Taylor: 'This allows a fork;'} ({whereas after the obvious} 21... a5 {Black has good play; e.g.} 22. Rxe8 Rxe8 23. Nd4 b4 {softening up the long diagonal.}) 22. Qxe6 Ne5 $1 23. Qxa6 (23. Bxe5 Qxe5 24. Qxa6 Qe2 $11) 23... Nd3 (23... Qc4 $5 24. Kg1 Nd3 25. Qa3 $5 b4 26. Qb3 Qc5+ 27. Kh1 bxc3 $11) 24. a4 $1 $14 Nxb2 25. axb5 {Timothy Taylor: '?' Timothy Taylor: 'Back to back errors!'} ({Correct is} 25. Qxb5 Qxc3 26. Be5 Bxe5 27. Nxe5 {and White is just a good pawn up.}) 25... Qxc3 26. Qb7 (26. Qc6 Qxc6 27. bxc6 Nd3 $11) 26... Qc4 27. Ra1 $1 (27. Rg1 Nd3 28. h3 Nf4 $11) 27... Na4 $6 (27... Nd3 $1 28. Ra8 h5 $1 $11) 28. Re1 Nc3 29. b6 $1 $36 cxb6 30. Bd6 Qc8 $8 31. Qxb6 Re8 32. Ne5 Nd5 33. Qa7 Qf5 $1 (33... Nf6 $2 34. Qa2 $16) (33... Qe6 $2 34. Qf7 { g6}) 34. h3 $6 $138 (34. g4 $5 Qc8 35. Qf7 {( g6)} Nf6 36. g5 Qc3 37. Rf1 Rxe5 38. Bxe5 Qc6+ $1 (38... Qxe5 39. Rxf6 $1 $18) 39. Kg1 Qc5+ $1 40. Kg2 Qxe5 41. gxf6 Qe2+ $11) 34... Nf6 35. Qa2 h5 36. Qf7 Kh7 37. Kg1 Ne4 $1 {Timothy Taylor: 'Black finds a pretty drawing combination.'} 38. Rxe4 Rxe5 {Timothy Taylor: 'Since'} 39. Qxf5 Rxf5 {Timothy Taylor: 'is equal, Tal and Mukh leave this messy game behind them – one sees that both players need to keep their move order straight!'} 1/2-1/2

