1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. g3 b5 4. Bg2 Bb7 5. O-O c5 {Lautier chose a strategically ambitious system, aimed at gaining space but slightly delaying development. Instead of fighting for space, Tal counters it with a plan based on rapid piece development.} 6. Bg5 Qb6 7. a4 a6 8. Nc3 {Played in the spirit of the Sicilian Defence, with the c-pawn behind the knight, but with piece pressure in the centre.} Ne4 $6 {This oversteps the tolerable limit of risk. Black loses too much time already.} (8... b4 $2 {runs into} 9. a5 Qc7 10. Na4 $16) ({But the correct reaction was} 8... cxd4 9. Qxd4 Qxd4 (9... Bc5 $6 {is risky:} 10. Qh4 Be7 11. axb5 axb5 12. e4 d6 13. Be3 Qd8 {The only way to avoid losing the pawn on b5 at once.} 14. Qg5 $1 {, and White takes the pawn anyway, as if} b4 15. Qb5+ $1) 10. Nxd4 Bxg2 11. Kxg2 {After having reduced White's attacking potential, Black can release the tension with} b4 {, even though White maintains some chances on the light squares after} 12. Na2) 9. Nxe4 Bxe4 10. axb5 Qxb5 {An obvious positional concession.} ({But if} 10... axb5 11. Rxa8 Bxa8 {, the hanging bishop on a8 offers White tactical ideas:} 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. b4 $1 Bxb4 14. Qa1 f6 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. Qxa8 $16 {, with active play and the better structure and king's position.}) 11. Qd2 ({Missing the thematic} 11. c4 $1 Qb7 (11... Qxc4 12. Nd2 $18) 12. d5 $1 exd5 13. Ra3 $3 {, when Black is in trouble due to the threat Re3. If} d4 14. Rb3 Qa7 15. Nxd4 $1 Bxg2 $2 16. Re3+ {wins.}) 11... f6 {More provocation. Black weakens his light squares, probably planning ...e6-e5 already.} (11... h6 {was safer, but not entirely satisfactory:} 12. Bf4 Be7 13. c4 $1 Qxc4 (13... Qb7 14. Rfd1 {is simply better for White, who is better developed and has two half-open files for his rooks.}) 14. dxc5 {Threatening Ne5.} Nc6 15. Ne5 $1 {Nevertheless!} Nxe5 16. Rfc1 $1 Qd5 17. Qxd5 Bxd5 18. Bxd5 Nc6 19. Bxc6 dxc6 20. Ra4 $16) 12. Bf4 Qb7 13. c4 cxd4 14. Qxd4 e5 $6 {No matter how bad Tal looked in the last years of his life, one simply cannot underestimate him so badly. Could it be that Lautier did not know chess history? Did he not know that the wizard could envoke complications out of much lesser opportunities?} (14... Be7 15. Bd6 $14 {is no joy for Black, of course, but at least he could hang on.}) 15. Bxe5 $1 fxe5 16. Qxe5+ Be7 {A sad necessity.} (16... Kd8 $2 {runs into} 17. Ng5 $1 Bxg2 18. Nf7+ Kc8 19. Qe8+ Kc7 20. Qd8+ Kc6 21. Kxg2 Rg8 22. Rfd1 $5 $18 {Black is almost completely paralysed despite his extra piece. His king is the most advanced of his pieces...}) 17. Nd4 $5 {Typical for Tal, strong enough, but not strongest.} (17. Qxg7 {would allow Black to achieve some sort of coordination with} Rf8) ({But White could have thrown the last resources into play with} 17. Ra3 $3 {, threatening Re3.} Bxf3 {What else?} 18. Qxg7 Rf8 19. Re3 Nc6 20. exf3 $1 {Opening the e-file for the other rook.} Qb4 21. f4 $18 {followed by Rfe1.}) 17... Bxg2 18. Nf5 Qb4 19. Kxg2 {Tal plays in a principled way, avoiding any early commitments.} ({But} 19. Nxg7+ Kf7 20. Nh5 Rf8 21. Kxg2 $16 {was stronger. White has three pawns for the piece and a continuing attack.}) 19... Nc6 20. Qxg7 O-O-O 21. Rxa6 Qb7 22. Rfa1 {The black king has finally reached some relative safety. White has four pawns for the piece already, but his threats are not that strong anymore.} Nb4+ $2 {The second moment when the young player is chasing material, completely underestimating the danger.} (22... Bc5 $14 {would have maintained some stability. White could evacuate the long diagonal with} 23. Kh3 $5 {, with pleasant play.}) 23. Kg1 Nxa6 24. Qxe7 {Black is a rook up, but it will soon become clear that he cannot save the knight, leaving White with a whole lot of pawns for the exchange.} Qb6 (24... Kb8 25. Qd6+ Ka7 26. b4 Qb6 27. b5 $18) 25. Qa3 Rhf8 (25... Kb7 26. Nd6+ Ka7 27. c5 Qc6 28. b4 $18) 26. Nd6+ Kc7 27. Qxa6 Ra8 28. Qxb6+ Kxb6 29. Rd1 Ra2 30. Rd2 {The endgame is obviously winning for White, but Tal will soon pass through a less confident phase.} Kc6 31. f3 Rfa8 32. Nb5 R8a4 33. Rc2 Kc5 34. Nc3 Ra1+ 35. Kf2 Rxc4 36. Rd2 (36. b3 $1 {would have won at once, as the rook does not have any good squares.} Rd4 37. Ke3 Rb4 38. Na2+) 36... Ra7 {Even after retrieving a pawn, Black cannot be very happy with his position.} 37. e4 Kc6 38. Ke3 Rb7 39. Rc2 d6 40. Kd3 Rc5 41. f4 Rb4 42. g4 Kd7 43. g5 Ke6 44. h4 d5 45. Nxd5 Rxc2 46. Nxb4 Rxb2 47. Nc2 Rb3+ 48. Kc4 $6 (48. Ke2 $1 Rh3 (48... Rb2 49. Kd2) 49. Nd4+ Ke7 50. Nf3 $18 {White keeps three pawns for the exchange and good coordination of his pieces.}) 48... Rh3 49. Nd4+ Kf7 50. f5 $2 {Too optimistic.} (50. Nf5 $1 Rf3 51. Nh6+ Ke6 52. f5+ Ke5 53. f6 {Keeps White's winning chances intact, as if} Kxe4 $6 54. h5 Ke5 55. f7 Ke6 56. g6 $18) 50... Rxh4 51. Kd5 Rg4 52. Nf3 {Black has worked miracles, but now misses his only chance to make a draw.} Rg3 $2 (52... Kg8 {was a strong prophylactic move, moving the king away from the knight's range.} 53. Ke5 (53. f6 {is parried with} Rf4 54. Nd4 Rg4 55. Ne6 Kf7 $11) 53... h6 $1 54. g6 (54. gxh6 Rg3 55. Kf4 Rh3 {is drawn}) 54... h5 $11) ({The only saving retreat for the king is to g8.} 52... Ke8 $2 53. Ke5 $1 h6 54. gxh6 Rg3 55. h7 $18) (52... Kf8 $2 53. f6 Rg3 (53... Rf4 {runs into} 54. Nd4 Rg4 55. e5 {, as the pawn is taboo.}) 54. Ne5 Rxg5 55. Ke6 $18) 53. Ne5+ $1 Kg8 $6 {This allows the enemy king's advance along the e-file.} (53... Ke8 {was more stubborn, but would not have saved either.} 54. f6 Rb3 $5 {The relatively best square for the rook.} (54... Rxg5 $2 55. Ke6 $18) (54... Ra3 $6 55. Nc4 $1 {gains a tempo and prevents the check along the fifth rank.} Rb3 56. e5 Rb5+ 57. Kc6 $18) 55. Ke6 Rb6+ 56. Kf5 Ra6 57. Nd3 Ra3 58. Nf4 Ra5+ 59. e5 Kf8 60. Ne6+ Kf7 61. Nd4 Ke8 62. Ke4 Rc5 63. e6 Rxg5 64. Nf5 $18) 54. f6 Rxg5 (54... Rb3 {is ineffective now due to} 55. Ke6 Rb6+ 56. Ke7 Rb7+ 57. Nd7 $18) 55. Ke6 {Somewhere around this moment Black could have considered resigning.} Rg1 56. f7+ Kg7 57. Nd7 Rf1 58. f8=Q+ Rxf8 59. Nxf8 h6 60. Nd7 h5 61. Ne5 h4 62. Nf3 {The knight has returned to this square 60 moves after its first visit and Black finally resigned.} 1-0

