1. d4 {Nagesh,Havanur} Nf6 2. c4 e6 ({In his early years Tal played the King's Indian Defence with} 2... g6 {Nagesh,Havanur} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {and had great success. Decades later Kasparov followed his example.}) ({Similarly in those days Tal played the Moden Benoni with} 2... c5 {Nagesh,Havanur} 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 {followed by...g6, Bg7 and...0-0. Subsequently it became part of Garry's arsenal as well.}) 3. Nf3 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'A flexible move that allows the "softer" Queen's Indian Defence with 3...b6 and 4...Bb7.'} ({On the other hand he does not permit the strategically complex Nimzo-Indian,} 3. Nc3 {Nagesh,Havanur} Bb4 {offering greater possibilities for Black.}) 3... d5 ({After} 3... b6 {Nagesh,Havanur Garry had scored quite a few victories with the Petrosian Variation,} 4. a3 Bb7) 4. Nc3 c6 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Finally, the players have reached Semi-Slav Defence by transposition.'} 5. Bg5 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Here comes the Anti-Meran Variation.'} (5. e3 {Nagesh,Havanur} Nbd7 {leads to the tranquil waters of Meran Defence.}) 5... dxc4 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'The Botvinnik System with which the 6th World Champion scored a number of sterling victories. It's an exquisite irony that the foremost exponent for White against this system is the Patriarch's own disciple, Garry Kasparov.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'In their previous encounter at Moscow Interzonal 1982 Tal opted for'} 5... h6 {Nagesh,Havanur: '(Moscow Variation) and Garry responded with the Anti-Moscow Gambit, sacrificing the c-pawn.'} 6. Bh4 $5 (6. Bxf6 {Nagesh,Havanur} Qxf6 {is the main line.}) 6... dxc4 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'and the game was drawn in an unclear position after Garry had sacrificed a piece.'} 7. e4 g5 8. Bg3 b5 {Kasparov-Tal/Moskau izt/1982/½/24/}) 6. e4 b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Nxg5 (9. exf6 {Nagesh,Havanur} gxh4 10. Ne5 Qxf6 {does not promise much for White.}) (9. Bg3 {Nagesh,Havanur} Nd5 {is less played, although players like Nakamura and more recently Rogozenco have had some success with the line.}) 9... hxg5 10. Bxg5 Nbd7 11. exf6 Bb7 ({If} 11... Nxf6 $2 {Nagesh,Havanur} 12. Qf3 ({But not the other way round.} 12. Nxb5 $2 cxb5 13. Qf3 Bb4+ $19) 12... Be7 13. Nxb5 $16) 12. g3 c5 (12... Nxf6 {Nagesh,Havanur is seldom played and hardly mentioned in theory books. After} 13. Bg2 ({Not} 13. Qf3 $2 Be7 14. Bxf6 (14. Nxb5 $4 Qa5+ $1 $19) 14... Bxf6 15. Nxb5 Rb8 16. Nd6+ Qxd6 17. Qxf6 Qb4+ 18. Kd1 Rg8 $17) 13... Be7 14. O-O {White threatens 15. Bxb5. A possible line is} a6 15. a4 Qd7 16. Qf3 Nd5 17. Bxe7 Qxe7 18. Ne4 $16) 13. d5 Qb6 (13... Bh6) ({Now} 13... Nxf6 {Nagesh,Havanur can be played. After} 14. Bg2 Be7 15. O-O (15. Nxb5 Qa5+ 16. Nc3 Nxd5 $17) 15... Nxd5 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Nxb5 Qb6 18. Na3 $1 ({If} 18. Nc3 $2 Nxc3 19. bxc3 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 Qb7+ 21. Kg1 Rxh2 $1 $19) 18... c3 $13 {the position is unclear.} ({Not} 18... Qxb2 $2 19. Rb1 Qxa3 20. Rxb7+ Kf6 21. Bxd5 exd5 22. Qxd5 $18)) 14. Bg2 O-O-O 15. O-O b4 16. Na4 Qb5 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'This move has had its share of problems and is less seen in praxis.'} (16... Qa6 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur is the move in vogue. The queen targets the knight on a4, guards the vulnerable pawn on e6 and remains ready to occupy the diagonal b7-h1 in case the bishops are exchanged.} 17. a3 Bxd5 $1 18. Bxd5 Ne5 19. axb4 Rxd5 20. Qe2 cxb4 $13) 17. a3 Nb8 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'A paradoxical retreat. Black intensifies the pressure on d-file and hopes to return with the knight to c6. However, all this costs some precious tempi.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'After the standard'} 17... exd5 $16 18. axb4 {Nagesh,Havanur} cxb4 19. Bf4 {has come into vogue. If} Nxf6 $2 20. Qd4 Qa6 21. h4 {followed by 22.Bh3+ gives White the attack.}) (17... Bxd5 $2 {Nagesh,Havanur fails to} 18. Bxd5 Ne5 19. Bxe6+ {If the queen had retreated to a6, this idea would not have worked.}) 18. axb4 cxb4 19. Be3 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Garry is self-critical. Here he questions his own move and instead recommends 19. Qd4 or 19. Qg4.'} ({As tournament practice has shown,} 19. Qg4 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur is stronger. The queen moves away from the d-file and targets e6.} Bxd5 20. Rfc1 Nc6 21. Bxd5 Rxd5 22. Rxc4 Rxg5 23. Qe4) 19... Bxd5 20. Bxd5 Rxd5 21. Qe2 Nc6 22. Rfc1 Ne5 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'A daring move. He offers the a-pawn, clearing the path for the White rook when his own king remains exposed to attack. He is taking these appalling risks because he is preparing an attack of his own against the White king.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Two years before this encounter Kasparov had scored spectacular victories against'} 22... Na5 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?'  b3 Nagesh,Havanur: 'played by Timoschenko and Dorfman.'}) 23. b3 $5 (23. f4 Nd3 24. Rxc4+ Qxc4 25. Rc1 Qxc1+ 26. Bxc1 Nxc1 27. Qc4+ Kb7 28. Qxc1 Bd6 $132) ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Not'} 23. Bxa7 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?'} Kb7 $1 24. Be3 { b6} (24. Nb6 c3 $1) 24... Rd3 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'  25.... c6 Nagesh,Havanur: 'Threatening 25...Qc6 and ...Nf3+ sets problems for White. For example,'} (24... Bh6 25. f4 $1 Nd3 26. Rd1 Ra8 27. Nb6 Rxa1 28. Rxa1 Rd6 29. Qf3+ Qc6 30. Qh5 $16) (24... Nd3 25. Rxc4 $1 Qxc4 26. Nb6 Qe4 27. Nxd5 exd5 28. Rd1 d4 29. Rxd3 dxe3 30. Rxe3 {/+-}) (24... Qc6 { a5} 25. Nb6 $1 (25. f4 Rd2 26. Bxd2 Nf3+ 27. Kf1 Rxh2) 25... Rd3 (25... Rd2 26. Nxc4 $1 Nxc4 $1 (26... Nf3+ 27. Qxf3 {s.o.}) 27. Qxc4 Rd1+ 28. Rxd1 Qxc4 29. Ra7+ $18) (25... Rd4 26. Bxd4 Nf3+ 27. Qxf3 Qxf3 28. Nxc4 $40) (25... Rb5 26. Ra7+ $1 Kb8 (26... Kxa7 $2 27. Nxc4+ Kb7 28. Nxe5) 27. Ra8+ Kb7 28. Rca1 $1 Nf3+ 29. Qxf3 Qxf3 30. R1a7+ Kc6 31. Rc8+ Kd6 32. Nxc4+ Kd5 33. Rd7+ Ke4 34. Nd2+ $18) (25... Rd6 $1 26. Ra7+ $1 (26. Nxc4 Nf3+ 27. Qxf3 Qxf3 28. Na5+ Kb8) 26... Kb8 27. Ra8+ Kb7 28. Nxc4 $3 (28. Rca1 Nf3+ 29. Qxf3 Qxf3 30. R1a7+ Kc6 31. Rc8+ Kb5) 28... Nf3+ 29. Qxf3 Qxf3 30. Na5+ $1 Kxa8 31. Rc8#) 26. Nxc4 $1 Nf3+ 27. Qxf3 Qxf3 28. Na5+ Ka6 29. Nc6+ Kb7 (29... Kb5 30. Ra5#) 30. Ra7+ $18) 25. f4 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?'} (25. Nb6 Rxe3 $1 26. Qxe3 Bc5 $19) 25... Rxh2 $3 26. Kxh2 Rxe3 $1 27. Qd1 (27. Qf1 Ng4+ $19) 27... Rd3 28. fxe5 (28. Qe2 Nf3+ 29. Kg2 Qd5 $19) (28. Qh1+ {Nagesh,Havanur} Nf3+ 29. Kg2 Qc6 30. Rd1 Re3 $19 {If} 31. Kh3 e5 $1) 28... Rxd1 29. Rxd1 Qxe5 $19) 23... c3 24. Nxc3 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'The knight was doing nothing on a4. At least this sacrifice opens lines against Black king.'} bxc3 25. Rxc3+ Kb8 (25... Kb7 26. Rxa7+ Kb8 27. Qxb5+ Rxb5 28. Kg2 {/}) (25... Kd8 26. Qxb5 (26. Rxa7 $1) 26... Rxb5 27. Rxa7 $16) 26. Qc2 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Garry spent 42 minutes before settling for this move and much of it was to decide whether to take the a7 pawn or not.'} (26. Bxa7+ $6 Kb7 27. Qe4 (27. Qc2 {Nagesh,Havanur}) 27... Qb4 $1 28. Qc2 $8 (28. Rc4 Qxb3 29. Rb1 Nf3+ $1) 28... Bd6 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Not'} 29. Ra4 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?'} (29. h4 {Nagesh,Havanur} Ra5 30. Rxa5 Qxa5 {and Black should win as the knight outweighs the White pawns.}) 29... Nf3+ $3 30. Rxf3 (30. Kg2 {Nagesh,Havanur} Ne1+ 31. Kf1 Nxc2 32. Rxb4+ Nxb4 $19) 30... Qe1+ 31. Kg2 Rxh2+ 32. Kxh2 Rh5+ $19 33. Kg2 {Nagesh,Havanur} Qh1#) 26... Bd6 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Combining attack and defence.'} (26... Nf3+ {Nagesh,Havanur: '?!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'is weak.'} 27. Kg2 Rxh2+ {Nagesh,Havanur: '?'} (27... Ne1+ {Nagesh,Havanur} 28. Rxe1 Bb4 29. Rc1 Bxc3 30. Qxc3 $44) 28. Kxf3 Rf5+ 29. Kg4 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'} Rf4+ $4 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?!'  31.f4 h5#} 30. Bxf4+ $18 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'} (30. gxf4 $4 {Nagesh,Havanur} Qh5+ 31. Kg3 Qh3#) (30. Kxf4 $4 {Nagesh,Havanur} Bd6+ 31. Kf3 Qh5+ $19)) 27. Bxa7+ Kb7 28. b4 $1 { 29.a5 Nagesh,Havanur: 'Nagesh Havanur: 'transposes to the game.''} Nc6 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Threatening both 29...Nxa7 and 29...Be5 winning material. Yet it is not the strongest move according to Garry.'} (28... Ra8 29. Ra5 Qd7 30. Bb6 $1 Bb8 (30... Ra6 {Nagesh,Havanur Forcing exchange of rooks on his terms.} 31. Rxa6 Kxa6 32. Bc5 Kb7 {and Black should win with careful play.}) ({Not} 30... Raxa5 {Nagesh,Havanur} 31. bxa5 (31. Bxa5 Nc6 $17) 31... Ka6 $15) 31. Rxa8 Rd1+ (31... Kxa8 32. Qe4 $1 $146) 32. Kg2 Qd5+ 33. Kh3 Kxa8 34. Qa4+ Kb7 35. b5 $1 $40 {+-}) (28... Rd3 $1 { 30.... f3} 29. Rxd3 Qxd3 30. Qxd3 Nxd3 $1 31. Rd1 $1 Kxa7 32. Rxd3 Rd8 $1 33. h4 Bc7 $10) (28... Bxb4 $2 {Nagesh,Havanur} 29. Rc7+ Ka8 30. Bb6+ Kb8 31. Rb7+ $1 Kxb7 32. Qc7#) (28... Qxb4 $2 {Nagesh,Havanur} 29. Rb3 $18) 29. Be3 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Garry saves his bishop and gives up his rook.'} Be5 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Tal spent 32 minutes before deciding on this move.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'He was first inclined to play'} 29... Rc8 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!?' Nagesh,Havanur: 'and even wrote the move on his scoresheet. After the game in joint analysis both players concluded that'} 30. Rb1 $5 (30. Rc1 $2 Nxb4) (30. Rca3 $6 Be5 $1) (30. Qa2 $6 Bxb4 31. Rb1 Bxc3) (30. Qh7 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'is the best move here. After'} Be5 31. Qxf7+ ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Not'} 31. Rxc6 $2 Qxc6 $1 32. Qxf7+ Rc7 33. Ra7+ Kb8 $19 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'White cannot save himself against the threats of 34...Rxf7 and 34...Rd1+ at the same time.'}) 31... Rc7 32. Qe8 Rd8 33. f7 Bxc3 34. Qxd8 Nxd8 ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Or'} 34... Bxa1 35. Qxc7+ (35. Qg8 $5 Rd7 {Nagesh,Havanur} 36. f8=Q Rd1+ 37. Kg2 Qf1+ 38. Kf3 Ne5+ 39. Kf4 Qc4+ 40. Kg5 Qg4+ {Nagesh Havanur: '='} 41. Kh6 Qh3+ 42. Kg5 Qg4+ $11)) 35. Ra7+ {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Nagesh Havanur: '=''} Kc8 36. Rxc7+ (36. Ra8+ {Nagesh,Havanur} Kb7) 36... Kxc7 37. f8=Q $10) ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Tal did not like the reply'} 30. f4 $5 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'But this is easily met by'} e5 $5 ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'He calculated'} 30... Bxb4 31. Rb1 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'missing'} {Nagesh,Havanur: 'He only saw'} Bxc3 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?'} (31... Na7 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur} 32. Rxc8 Nxc8 33. Rc1 Qd7 34. Rb1 Qb5) 32. Rxb5+ Rxb5 33. Qxc3 {Nagesh,Havanur: '+-' / Tal})) 30... Rc7 (30... Nxb4 $2 31. Rxb4) 31. Rc5 $1 Qd3 32. b5 $1 Ne5 $5 (32... Bxc5 $2 33. bxc6+ Kxc6 (33... Kc8 34. Qxd3 Rxd3 35. Bxc5 $18) 34. Qa4+ Kd6 35. Bxc5+ Rdxc5 (35... Rcxc5 36. Rb6+) (35... Kxc5 36. Qb4+ Kc6 37. Rc1+ Kd7 38. Qe7+ $18) 36. Rd1 Qxd1+ (36... Rc1 37. Qa6+ $1) (36... Rc1 37. Qa6+) 37. Qxd1+ Ke5 38. Qf3 $16) (32... Nb8 33. Rxc7+ Bxc7 34. Qxd3 (34. Qa2 $6 Rxb5 35. Qa7+ Kc8 36. Rc1 Rb7) 34... Rxd3 35. g4 $1 Nd7 36. g5 Ne5 37. Rb4 $1 {/=}) (32... Nb4 $5 33. Qxd3 $1 Nxd3 $1 34. Rxd5 exd5 35. Rd1 Nb4 36. g4 $13) 33. Rxc7+ Bxc7 34. Qa2 $1 Kc8 $1 35. b6 $1 Nf3+ 36. Kg2 Qe4 $1 37. Rc1 Nh4+ (37... Nxh2+ 38. Kg1 Nf3+ 39. Kf1 $10) 38. Kh3 $1 (38. Kf1 $2 Qh1+ 39. Ke2 Qf3+ 40. Ke1 Ng2+) 38... Qg2+ (38... Qf5+ 39. g4 Qf3+ 40. Kxh4 Qxf6+ 41. g5 $1 Rd4+ $1 42. Bxd4 Qf4+ $10) 39. Kg4 (39. Kxh4 Qxh2+ 40. Kg4 Qh5#) 39... Qf3+ 40. Kh3 $10) 30. Rxc6 Bxa1 31. Rc7+ Kb8 $1 32. Ba7+ ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Not'} 32. Rxf7 $2 Rd7 $1 33. Re7 {Nagesh,Havanur} Bxf6 34. Rxe6 Bd4 $19) 32... Ka8 33. Be3 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Threatening mate with 34.Qa2+'} Kb8 34. Ba7+ Ka8 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'A useful repetition before time control. But now what?'} 35. Bc5 Kb8 36. Rxf7 $1 Be5 $1 (36... Qd3 $2 37. Ba7+ $18) (36... Rhd8 $2 37. Bd6+ $1 $18) (36... Rd7 37. Bd6+ $1 Kb7 38. Qe4+ Ka6 39. Qxe6 Rxf7 40. Qxf7 {/+-}) 37. Ba7+ ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Garry writes, at the board he was not able to assess the variation'} 37. Re7 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'} Rhd8 (37... Bxf6 38. Rxe6 {/+-} (38. Bd6+ $18 {Nagesh,Havanur})) 38. f7 Rd1+ 39. Kg2 Qc6+ $1 (39... Qf1+ $2 40. Kf3 Qh1+ 41. Kg4 R1d3 42. Qxd3 $18) 40. Kh3 Qf3 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'} 41. Qh7 $8 {Nagesh,Havanur: ''  42.e8 +- Nagesh,Havanur: 'Stopping 41... Rh8+ and also threatening 42.Re8 and f7-f8=Q.'} Bxg3 $1 42. fxg3 R1d5 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Forcing White to find a draw with "only" moves.'} ({Or Black can play the easier move} 42... Qf1+ $11 {Nagesh,Havanur and draw by perpetual check.}) 43. Rb7+ $1 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Nagesh Havanur: '=''} Kc8 (43... Kxb7 $4 {Nagesh,Havanur} 44. f8=Q+ $18) 44. Rc7+ $10 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'} Kb8 {Nagesh,Havanur}) (37. Kg2 $5 Rd7 $1 38. Rxd7 Qxd7 39. Qe4 Qd5 40. Qxd5 exd5 41. f7 Bg7 { 42.... f8} 42. g4 Kc7 (42... Bf8 $2 43. g5) 43. f4 d4 {=/}) 37... Ka8 38. Be3 Rd7 $1 (38... Kb8 39. Re7 Rhd8 40. f7 Rd1+ 41. Kg2 Qd5+ 42. f3 Rd3 (42... Qb5 $2 43. Qxd1) (42... Ra1 43. Re8 Bg7 44. b5 $1 $18) 43. Re8 Bg7 44. Rxd8+ $1 (44. Bf4+ $2 Kb7 45. Qc7+ Ka6) 44... Qxd8 45. Bf4+ Kb7 (45... e5 46. Bxe5+ $1 Bxe5 (46... Kb7 $1 47. Bxg7 $1 Rd2+ 48. Kh3 Rxc2 49. f8=Q Qd7+ 50. g4 Qc7 51. f4 $18) 47. Qxd3) 46. b5 $1 Qd7 47. Qa4 $18) 39. Qa2+ {Nagesh,Havanur: '?' Nagesh,Havanur: 'An error in time trouble.'} (39. Qe4+ $2 Qd5 40. Qxd5+ Rxd5 41. g4 Rxh2 42. Kf1 Bd4 $1) (39. Rxd7 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur} Qxd7 40. f7 $1 {would have maintained dynamic balance according to Garry. Note that} Qxf7 $4 {fails to} 41. Qe4+ Qb7 42. Qxe5 $18) 39... Kb8 40. Ba7+ {Nagesh,Havanur: '!'} (40. Qxe6 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?' Nagesh,Havanur: 'fails to'} Rd1+ 41. Kg2 Qf1+ ({Garry endorses Igor Stohl's recommendation} 41... Rd6 {Nagesh,Havanur} 42. Qb3 Rd3 43. Qe6 Rxe3 $1 {But after} 44. Rh7 (44. fxe3 $4 Qe2+ $19) 44... Re8 (44... Rxh7 45. Qg8+ Kb7 46. Qxh7+ Kb6 47. fxe3 Qe2+ 48. Kh3 Qf1+ 49. Kg4 Qxf6 $11 {and White cannot escape from checks.}) 45. Re7 Rf8 46. f7 Rd3 47. Re8+ Rd8 48. Qxe5+ Qxe5 49. Rxe5 Rxf7 {matters are not so clear. Black rooks have to cope with four White passed pawns!}) 42. Kf3 Qh1+ 43. Kg4 (43. Ke2 Re1+ 44. Kd2 Rd1+) 43... Qe4+ 44. Kg5 Rg8+ 45. Rg7 Bxf6+ $1 46. Kxf6 ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Or'} 46. Qxf6 Rd5+ 47. Kh6 Rh8+ {Nagesh,Havanur: '-+'}) 46... Qxe6+ 47. Kxe6 Rxg7 $19 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Analysis by ChessBase.'}) 40... Kc8 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'A resourceful monarch! All along he has saved himself with "only" moves.'} (40... Kb7 $2 41. Qxe6 Rxf7 42. Qxf7+ Bc7 43. Bc5 {/}) 41. Qxe6 Qd5 ({Not} 41... Kd8 $2 {Nagesh,Havanur} 42. Rxd7+ Qxd7 43. Qxe5 {Threatening both 44.Qb8+ and also 44.f7. Either way the rook is lost.} Qxa7 44. f7 Qxf7 ({Or} 44... Rf8 45. Qd6+ $18) 45. Qxh8+ Kd7 46. h4 $18) 42. Qa6+ Qb7 {Nagesh,Havanur: '?!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Fatigue!'} ({He could have played for a win with} 42... Kd8 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur But after} 43. Bb6+ Ke8 44. Rxd7 Kxd7 45. Qa7+ Ke6 46. Qe7+ Kf5 47. f7 Bg7 48. h4 Kg6 49. b5 Qxf7 {it is not easy for Black to escape from checks, if at all.}) 43. Qc4+ Qc7 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Threatening to exchange queens or rooks and so forcing back the White queen to a6. Draw on account of perpetual check.Times:2.35-1.30. ½-½ (43)'} (43... Kd8 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'also draws.But Black has to be careful.'} 44. Rxd7+ Qxd7 (44... Kxd7 $2 {Nagesh,Havanur: '??'} 45. Bc5 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur: '+-'}) 45. Bb6+ Bc7 $10 (45... Ke8 $2 46. Qe2 $1 {Nagesh,Havanur: '+-!'}) 46. Bc5 {Nagesh,Havanur} Bd6 $11) (43... Qc7 44. Qa6+ $10) 1/2-1/2

