{Quintiliano, Renato: 'Let's see the great Mikhail Tal giving the Traxler Variation a shot. This is a curious game as Tal is not facing another grandmaster, but the readers of Pravda, the main newspaper of the Soviet Union at that time. '} 1. e4 {Quintiliano, Renato} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. Ng5 Bc5 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?!'} 5. Nxf7 {Quintiliano, Renato: '!?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'This is the most ambitious and adventurous option for White.'} Bxf2+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '!' Quintiliano, Renato: 'This is the point behind Black's play, otherwise he would just be lost.'} 6. Kxf2 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?!' Quintiliano, Renato: 'Greedy, but likely wrong.'} (6. Kf1 $1 {Quintiliano, Renato is the correct answer.}) 6... Nxe4+ {Quintiliano, Renato: 'White now has the task of deciding where to put his king. '} 7. Kg1 {Quintiliano, Renato: 'The most played.'} ({The brave} 7. Ke3 $1 {Quintiliano, Renato should be the best try for White, see next game; the other options are just losing:}) (7. Ke2 $4 {Quintiliano, Renato} Nd4+ 8. Ke3 Qh4 $19) (7. Ke1 $2 {Quintiliano, Renato} Qh4+ 8. g3 Nxg3 $19) 7... Qh4 8. g3 (8. Qf1 $2 {Quintiliano, Renato was tried in a few games, but White ends in trouble after} Rf8 9. d3 (9. g3 Qe7 $1 {[%cal Ge7c5] White can't avoid fall into tactical ideas here, for example} 10. Kg2 d5 $1 11. Bxd5 Bh3+ $3 12. Kxh3 Qd7+ 13. Kg2 Qxd5 $40 {[%csl Rf7,Rg2]}) 9... Nd6 $1 10. Nxd6+ cxd6 {White is simply lost.} 11. Qe2 Nd4 12. Qd2 Qg4 $19 {[%cal Gd4f3,Gd4e2]}) 8... Nxg3 {Quintiliano, Renato: '!'} 9. hxg3 (9. Nxh8 {Quintiliano, Renato would end in a draw after} Nd4 $1 (9... Nxh1 10. d3 $1 d5 11. Bxd5 Qd4+ 12. Be3 $1 Qxe3+ 13. Kxh1 $18) 10. hxg3 (10. Nc3 $2 d5 $1 {[%cal Gc8g4] seems dangerous for White.}) 10... Qxg3+ 11. Kf1 Qf4+ $11) 9... Qxg3+ 10. Kf1 Rf8 11. Qh5 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'This is too optimistic.'} (11. Nc3 {Quintiliano, Renato was better, but the likely result should be a draw after} d5 $1 12. Bxd5 Bh3+ 13. Rxh3 Qxh3+ 14. Kg1 Qg3+ 15. Kh1 (15. Kf1 $2 Rxf7+ $1 16. Bxf7+ Kxf7 $19 {[%csl Rf1][%cal Ra8f8]}) 15... Qh3+ 16. Kg1 Qg3+ $11) 11... d5 {Quintiliano, Renato: '!' Quintiliano, Renato: 'Black is two pieces down, but keeps playing energetic chess. '} 12. Bxd5 Nd4 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'But here Tal went wrong.'} (12... Nb4 $1 {[%csl Yc2,Yd5] Quintiliano, Renato was the right continuation, although the knight seems misplaced at first sight.} 13. Qf3 $8 (13. Bb3 {doesn't avoid} Nxc2 $1) 13... Qxf3+ 14. Bxf3 Rxf7 {[%csl Rc2,Rf3] The only way to save White from big material losses is} 15. c3 Rxf3+ 16. Kg2 Rf6 17. cxb4 {Even here, Black is better after} b6 $1 18. Re1 Bb7+ 19. Kh2 O-O-O 20. Nc3 Rdf8 $40 {[%csl Gb7,Gf6,Gf8,Rh2] when White is still under unpleasant pressure due to Black's piece activity.}) 13. Qh2 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'White is the side which misses a big chance now.'} (13. Nc3 $1 {Quintiliano, Renato Quintiliano, Renato: 'was natural and good, trying to solve White's development issues:'} c6 {Quintiliano, Renato} (13... Bg4 {Quintiliano, Renato} 14. Qh2 $1 {forcing the queen's exchange is a great accomplishment here.} Qxh2 15. Rxh2 Nxc2 16. Rb1 Nb4 17. Bxb7 Rxf7+ 18. Kg1 $18) 14. Bc4 Bg4 (14... b5 15. Ne4 $1 Qf4+ 16. Kg1 bxc4 17. d3 $18) 15. Qh4 Qf3+ 16. Kg1 Rxf7 17. Bxf7+ Kxf7 18. d3 Rf8 19. Rh2 $1 $18 {[%cal Gh2f2]}) 13... Qg4 ({An objectively better try was} 13... Qxh2 {Quintiliano, Renato} 14. Rxh2 Nxc2 15. Rf2 Nxa1 $15) 14. Qxe5+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '?'} ({White could have transposed to the winning variation of the 13th move with} 14. Qh5 {Quintiliano, Renato}) 14... Be6 {Quintiliano, Renato: '!'} 15. Bxe6 Qf3+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'This seems clever, but it is only enough for a draw.'} ({The simple} 15... Nxe6 $1 {[%csl Rf1][%cal Yf8f7] Quintiliano, Renato was very good, as it is difficult now to imagine how White will save his king from a strong attack by Black's pieces, for example} 16. Qe2 Rxf7+ 17. Ke1 Rf3 $1 18. Rf1 (18. d3 O-O-O 19. Be3 Re8 20. Bf2 Qa4 $3 $19 {[%cal Ge6d4]}) 18... Rxf1+ 19. Kxf1 Qf5+ 20. Qf2 Qxc2 21. Nc3 O-O-O {[%cal Gd8f8] The white king is too much exposed, and the following line illustrates well how quickly White's position may collapse:} 22. Ke1 Rf8 23. Qe3 Nf4 24. Qe4 Nd3+ 25. Ke2 Nxc1+ 26. Ke3 Qxe4+ 27. Nxe4 Rf1 28. Nf2 Re1+ 29. Kf3 Nd3 $19) 16. Kg1 Ne2+ 17. Kh2 Qf2+ 18. Kh3 Qf3+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'This seems like a draw, but actually White can fight for more.'} (18... Rxf7 {Quintiliano, Renato was correct, when after} 19. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {although White wants more, he must be satisfied with a draw.} 20. Qe3 (20. d3 Qf3+ 21. Kh2 Qf2+ $11) (20. Rh2 $2 {is even losing after} Qf3+ 21. Kh4 Nf4 $1 {Black is a piece + rook down in this position, but the perfect coordination of queen + knight shows that White is the side who is lost!} 22. Qf5+ Kg8 23. Qg4 Ng6+ 24. Kh5 Qf7 $1 25. Qe4 Re8 $19 {and White faces big material losses.}) 20... Nf4+ 21. Kg4 Qg2+ $1 22. Qg3 (22. Kxf4 {leads to a draw after} g5+ $1 23. Kf5 Qd5+ 24. Kg4 Qg2+ 25. Kf5 Qd5+ {since} 26. Qe5 $2 {would be too much due to} Qf3+ 27. Kxg5 Rg8+ {with mate in a few moves.}) 22... Qxh1 23. Qxf4+ Kg8 {[%csl Yb1,Yc1,Rg4] Even being two pieces up, due to his very exposed king and poor development White is the side which needs to take care in this position; of course, these variations are by no means forced, but they provide a nice example of Black's possibilities in such positions in this line.} 24. Nc3 Rf8 25. Qe4 Qg1+ 26. Kh3 Qf1+ 27. Kg3 Qg1+ {the draw seems the wise choice now, as} 28. Qg2 $2 {seems already worse for White after} Qe1+ 29. Kh3 Qe6+ 30. Kh2 Qe5+ $1 31. Kh3 Qh5+ 32. Kg3 Qg5+ 33. Kh3 Qh6+ $1 34. Kg3 Qf4+ 35. Kh3 Rf5 $1 {[%cal Gf5h5] and Black should win the queen:} 36. Qe2 Qh6+ 37. Kg3 Rg5+ 38. Kf3 Qh5+ 39. Kf4 Qh4+ 40. Ke3 Rg3+ 41. Qf3 h5 $18) 19. Kh4 Qf2+ 20. Kh5 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?!' Quintiliano, Renato: 'Wrong square for the king.'} (20. Kg5 $1 {Quintiliano, Renato would probably solve White's problems, as} Rxf7 {is bad now:} 21. Bxf7+ Kxf7 22. Qf5+ $1 {White should win after this exchange.} Qxf5+ 23. Kxf5 Ng3+ 24. Kg4 Nxh1 25. Nc3 Re8 26. Kf3 $1 Re1 27. b3 $18 {[%csl Rh1][%cal Yc1b2]}) 20... Rxf7 {Quintiliano, Renato: '!'} 21. Bxf7+ Kxf7 22. Rh2 {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'This seems the decisive mistake.'} (22. Rh3 $1 {Quintiliano, Renato was the right move, preventing the check on f3; Black must be accurate now and play} Qg2 $1 (22... Nf4+ 23. Kg4 Nxh3 24. Qd5+ $1 Ke7 25. Qe4+ Kf7 26. Qf3+ $1 $18) 23. Rd3 $1 (23. Re3 $2 g6+ 24. Kh4 h5 $3 {[%cal Gg2g4] Once more, we have an almost artistic position: suddenly, Black has almost no pieces left, but yet he has a dangerous attack!} 25. Qxc7+ Kg8 26. Rg3 (26. Qc4+ Kg7 27. Rxe2 g5+ $1 28. Kxh5 Rh8#) 26... Qe4+ 27. Kg5 Nxg3 28. Qxg3 Qe7+ $1 29. Kh6 Qg7+ 30. Kg5 Rf8 31. Nc3 Kh7 $1 32. Nd5 Rf5+ 33. Kh4 Qd4+ 34. Kh3 Qxd5 $19) 23... Qh1+ 24. Kg4 Qg2+ 25. Kh4 Qf2+ 26. Rg3 Nxg3 27. Qxc7+ Kg8 28. Qxg3 Qf6+ 29. Kh3 Qf1+ 30. Qg2 Qxc1 $13) 22... Qf3+ 23. Kh4 g5+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '!'} 24. Qxg5 {Quintiliano, Renato: ''} (24. Kxg5 $2 {Quintiliano, Renato} Rg8+ 25. Kh6 Qc6+) 24... Rg8 25. Qh5+ (25. Qxg8+ {Quintiliano, Renato} Kxg8 26. Rxe2 Qxe2 27. Nc3 Qf3 $19 {[%cal Gh7h5]}) 25... Qxh5+ 26. Kxh5 Ng3+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'For some reason, Tal missed a simple win here.'} (26... Nf4+ $1 {Quintiliano, Renato: 'was the winning check:'} 27. Kh6 (27. Kh4 h5 $1) 27... Rg6+ 28. Kxh7 Rg7+ 29. Kh6 Kg8 $1 $19 {Quintiliano, Renato: 'with unavoidable mate.'}) 27. Kh6 $2 {Quintiliano, Renato: '??'} (27. Kh4 $11 {Quintiliano, Renato: 'would force the draw, since'} Nf5+ {Quintiliano, Renato} 28. Kh5 h6 $2 {[%cal Yg8g5] is bad due} 29. d3 $18) 27... Nf5+ {Quintiliano, Renato: '?' Quintiliano, Renato: 'And again!'} (27... Rg6+ $1 28. Kxh7 Ne4 $1 $19 29. Rf2+ {Quintiliano, Renato} Nxf2 30. Nc3 Ng4 31. d3 (31. Ne4 Rh6#) 31... Nf6+ 32. Kh8 Rg8#) 28. Kxh7 Rg7+ {Quintiliano, Renato: 'An interesting and strange game, which provided amusing analysis!'} 1/2-1/2

