1. d4 Nf6 (1... d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3) 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 dxc4 4. e3 c5 (4... e6 5. Bxc4) 5. Bxc4 e6 6. O-O a6 7. Bb3 b5 8. a4 bxa4 9. Bxa4+ Nbd7 (9... Bd7 $16 {(Gligoric, Euwe, Tal)} 10. Ne5 $5 Bxa4 11. Qxa4+ Nbd7 12. dxc5 $14 {(Flohr)} Bxc5 13. Rd1 Qc7 14. Nxd7 {(Moles 75)} Nxd7 15. b4 $1 Bd6 16. Ra2 O-O 17. Rc2 $15 {(Euwe)} Qb8 18. Qxd7 $16 {(Moles 75)} Rd8 $1 $10 {= (Pachman 68, eco 74)  (Botvinnik)} 19. Rxd6 $1 Qxd6 20. Qxd6 Rxd6 21. Kf1 Rd3 22. Nd2 $1) 10. e4 cxd4 (10... Nxe4 11. Bc6 $1 $18) (10... Bb7 11. d5 exd5 $5 12. exd5 Bxd5 13. Nc3 Bxf3 {(Thomas)} (13... Bb7 $5 {(Euwe)} 14. Ne5) (13... Be6 14. Ng5) 14. Qxf3 Be7 15. Bxd7+ Nxd7 16. Rd1 Ra7 {(Thomas)} 17. Nb5 $5) 11. e5 {This move was first played in Gligoric-Petrosian Belgrade 1959, but it had been suggested as early as 1948 by Euwe.} Nd5 $1 12. Bc6 Rb8 $1 13. Bxd5 exd5 $6 14. Nxd4 $1 {"This ugly move is practically forced." (Tal 75)} Rb6 15. Nc3 Nc5 16. Be3 $1 Be7 (16... Rxb2 17. Qf3 $18 {(Euwe)} Be6 18. Rfd1 $18) 17. Qc2 $6 O-O 18. b4 (18. Rfd1 Qd7 19. Nf5 Qxf5 20. Qxf5 Bxf5 21. Nxd5) 18... Ne6 $1 $8 19. Nf5 {White's e-pawn will be virtually impossible to defend, and after its elimination, the Black center pawn mass becomes menacing. However, White has the characteristically powerful passed h-pawn as compensation.} d4 $6 (19... Rb7 20. Rfd1) 20. Nxe7+ $1 $16 {(Schwarz 67)} Qxe7 $6 21. Nd5 Qb7 $6 22. Nxb6 $1 $16 {(Moles 75)} Qxb6 23. Bd2 Bb7 24. Qd3 Qc6 25. f3 Qd7 26. Rf2 $1 Re8 (26... Rc8 27. f4 Nc7 28. f5 Nb5 29. Re1 Bd5 30. Rf4 Bc4 31. Qg3 d3 32. f6 g6 33. Qh4) 27. f4 Nc7 28. f5 Nb5 $2 29. Re1 Bd5 30. Rf4 Bc6 31. Qg3 d3 32. f6 1-0

