1. d4 {1) Tal in Shakhmatyi (Riga) 1963. Reprinted in 2) Kirillov,V: Michael Tal. Sein Lebenswerk. 1962-1983. Chessgate. 2004. S.6-9.} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bd2 Nb6 7. Bf4 O-O (7... c5 8. dxc5 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qxd1+ 10. Rxd1 Na4 11. e3 Nxc5 12. Nd4 Bd7 13. Nb5 Bxb5 14. Bxb5+ Nbd7) 8. e3 (8. Rc1 c5 9. dxc5 Bxc3+ (9... N6d7 10. g3 Nc6 11. Bg2 Nxc5 12. O-O Bf5 13. Be3 Ne4 14. Nxe4 Qxd1 15. Rfxd1 Bxe4 16. Nd4 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 Nb4 18. Nf3 a5 19. Rc7 Bxb2 20. Rxb7 Bf6 21. a4 Na2) 10. Rxc3 (10. bxc3 Qxd1+ 11. Rxd1 Na4 12. Bh6 Re8 13. c4 Nc6 14. e3 Bg4 15. h3 Bxf3 16. gxf3 Red8 17. Bg2 Nc3 18. Rxd8+ Rxd8 19. O-O Nxa2) 10... Qxd1+ 11. Kxd1 Nd5 (11... Na4) 12. Rc4 Be6 13. Bxb8 Rfxb8 14. Rc1 Nf6) 8... c5 (8... Bg4 9. Be2 c5 10. dxc5 Bxc3+ (10... N6d7 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 Nxc5) 11. bxc3 Qxd1+ 12. Rxd1 (12. Bxd1 N6d7) 12... Na4 13. Bh6 (13. Rd4 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 Nxc5 15. Ke2 Rc8 16. Rhd1 Nc6 17. Rc4) 13... Rc8 14. Rd4 Bxf3 15. Bxf3 Nxc5 16. O-O Nc6 17. Rfd1 Ne5 18. Be2 f6 19. Bf4 Nf7 20. Bg3 e5) (8... Nc6 9. Be2 Nb4 10. O-O N4d5 11. Be5 (11. Bg3 c5 12. Rc1) 11... Bg4 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. Ne4 c6 14. Rc1 Nd7 15. a3 Qb6 16. Qd2 Bxf3 17. Bxf3 Rfd8 18. Rfd1 N5f6 19. Nc3 e5 20. Na4 Qb5 21. Qc2 exd4 22. Rxd4 Qa5) (8... Be6 9. Be2 (9. Ng5 Bd5) 9... c5 10. Rc1 (10. dxc5 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qxd1+ 12. Rxd1 Na4) 10... cxd4 11. Nxd4 Bxd4 12. exd4 Nc6 13. Bf3 Bd5 (13... Bc4) 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. Bh6 Qa5+ 16. Qd2 Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 Rfd8 18. Be3 e6) 9. dxc5 N6d7 (9... Bxc3+ $2 10. bxc3 Qxd1+ 11. Rxd1 N6d7 (11... Na4 $142 {Tal: Gegen diesen Zug konnte ich während der Vorbereitung keinen Vorteil für Weiß finden. So erging es wohl auch Fischer, denn im dritten Durchgang spielte er gegen Keres 12.d5, der sich als positionell gänzlich unbegründet erwies.} 12. Bh6) 12. Bg5 Re8 13. Bb5 {Fischer-Keres. Candidates Curacao.[7] 1962. Tal: Und Weiss steht wohl schon auf Gewinn.}) 10. Nd5 (10. Rc1 Nc6 11. Bb5 Nxc5 12. O-O $1 Bf5 13. Qe2 Bd3 14. Bxd3 Qxd3 15. Rfd1 $16 Qxe2 16. Nxe2 Rfd8) 10... e5 (10... Nxc5 $1 11. Nc7 (11. Bc7 Qd7 12. Rc1 Ne4 13. Bxb8 $11 (13. Ne5 {deserves attention.} Bxe5 14. Bxe5 Nc6) 13... Rxb8 14. Rc7 Qd8 15. Nxe7+ $11 Kh8 16. Qxd8 Rxd8) 11... Bxb2 $1 12. Rc1 ({Not} 12. Qxd8 $2 Rxd8 13. Rd1 Bc3+ 14. Ke2 Bd7 15. Ne5 $4 Bxe5 16. Bxe5 Nc6 $18 17. Nxa8 Nxe5 18. Nc7 e6) 12... Bxc1 13. Qxc1 Ne6 (13... Ne4 {and White is only slightly superior. A phenomenal might-have-been!}) 14. Nxa8 Nxf4 $1 15. exf4 $1 Nc6 $1 16. Qd2 Bd7) 11. Bg5 f6 {The Keres Variation. Black plays for the control of the point e5. The knight also vacates the f6 square for the occupation of the bishop. Keres had lost with this variation to Fischer in Round 7. The famous encounter is included in Fischer's book, "My 60 Memorable Games".} 12. Bh4 e4 (12... Nxc5 13. Bc4 Kh8 14. b4 {Here John Nunn found the stunning}) 13. Nd4 Nxc5 14. Rc1 {This move preventing ...Nb4 preserves the present position for the bishop on c2. But it would lose a tempo if White has to play a4 later. He has other alternatives, 14.Nf1, 14.d5 and 14.Nb3.} Nba6 15. b4 Nd3+ 16. Bxd3 exd3 17. Qb3 {'?!'} Kh8 18. b5 (18. O-O) 18... Qa5+ 19. Kd1 $6 {Keres wrote,"Tal tries for the greatest possible complications in every position....The combination initiated by the text move is indeed very complicated, but it eventually turns to Black's advantage."} Bd7 $1 20. Qxd3 $2 Rad8 {'!'} 21. Nc3 (21. Qd2) 21... Nc5 22. Qe2 Bf5 23. g4 Bd3 24. Qd2 g5 25. Bg3 f5 26. gxf5 Bxf5 27. h4 Nd3 28. Ke2 Nxc1+ 29. Rxc1 gxh4 30. Bxh4 Bg4+ 31. Kf1 Rd7 32. Qd3 Qa3 33. Qc2 Qc5 34. Qe4 Qh5 35. Bg3 Re7 36. Qd5 Qh3+ 37. Ke1 Rd7 38. Qe4 Bf3 39. Qh4 Qg2 40. Qh2 Qxh2 41. Bxh2 {and Tal resigned. A great battle in which so much action remained behind the scenes.} Bxd4 {also loses.} 42. exd4 Kg8 43. Be5 Rf5 44. Ne2 h5 45. Nf4 $1 Kf7 46. Kf1 $19 Rg5 $2 47. Re1 h4 48. Re3 $19 Bg4 $2 49. Rc3 $1 Rg8 50. a4 $19 Bf5 $2 51. a5 Re7 52. a6 $1 $40 bxa6 $2 53. Rc6 $1 Bd7 $2 54. Rf6+ $1 Ke8 $2 55. bxa6 $1 {juat as in the line after 20.Qh5} {Tal was a dangerous adversary even in bad positions.} Rh7 $2 56. Nd5 h3 57. Bh2 Rg2 58. Nc7+ $1 Kd8 59. Bd6 Rh8 60. d5 $40 {and White has an attack going for him.} h2 $5 61. Rf8+ $1 Rxf8 62. Kxg2 $1 Rxf2+ 63. Kh1 Bg4 64. Ne6+ $19 {If} Ke8 $2 65. Ng5 $1 {Tal: At this moment I thought up a very interesting combination and after making a preparatory move, I even went up to Petrosian and joked, "I am going for the brilliancy prize." The point was that I was planning to sacrifice my queen for only two minor pieces.} Rf5 66. Ne6 Rf1+ 67. Kxh2 Bxe6 68. dxe6 Ra1 69. Kg3 Rxa6 70. Bb8 $40 {Tal: ...but after writing down 20.Qh5 I saw} Ke7 71. Kf4 Kxe6 72. Ke4 Ra4+ 73. Kd3 Kd5 {and now on} 74. Kc3 {I conjured up 25... Rxd8!!? when Black would be a piece up. So I changed my mind.} a5 $2 75. Bc7 $1 Rc4+ $6 {During the game Keres had intended this move and calculated} 0-1

