1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 c6 $6 {This move order is consdered inaccurate according to present opening theory.} 6. Nc3 d5 7. Qb3 Qb6 $1 {At once stopping the e5 advance by White and freeing the diagonal for his light-squared bishop.} 8. O-O {The knight has no future on c3. But from d2 he may move to more useful posts like c4 and e3.} Bf5 (8... Qxb3 9. axb3 Bf5 10. Ne5 Rd8 11. f4 Na6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. Rxa6 dxc4 14. b4 Nd5 15. e4 Bc8 16. Rxc6 Bxd4+ 17. Kh1 Nxb4 18. Rc7 Rd7 19. Rxc8+ Rxc8 20. Bh3 e6 21. f5 Re8 22. Nb5 Be5 23. fxe6 fxe6 24. Na3) 9. Ne5 {This move prevents mobilisation of Black's queenside with...b5.} Be6 (9... Rd8 10. Bf4 (10. cxd5 Qxb3 11. axb3 cxd5 12. Bf4 h6 13. g4 Bxg4 14. Nxg4 Nxg4 15. Nxd5 Nc6 16. h3 Nf6 17. Nxf6+ Bxf6 18. e3 e5 19. dxe5 Bxe5 20. Bxh6 Rd7 21. Rfd1) 10... Na6 (10... Be6 11. Na4 Qxb3 12. axb3 Nbd7 13. Rfc1 Rac8 14. Nc3 a6 15. Nxd7 Nxd7 16. cxd5 cxd5 17. Nxd5 Rxc1+ 18. Rxc1 Bxd4 19. Nxe7+ Kg7 20. Bxb7 Bxb2 21. Rb1 Nc5 22. Bf3 Bf6 23. Nc6 Rc8 24. Na5) 11. c5 Qxb3 12. axb3 Rdc8 13. Ra5 Nc7 14. Rfa1 a6 15. b4 Nh5 16. Bd2 Rd8 17. h3 Be6 18. g4 Bxe5 19. dxe5 Ng7 20. Bg5 d4 21. Na4 Re8 22. Nb6 Rad8 23. Rd1 Bb3 24. Rd3) (9... Ng4 10. cxd5 (10. Nxg4 Bxg4 11. Be3 dxc4 12. Qxc4 Na6 13. Rfc1 Bf5 14. a3 Rac8 15. b4 Nc7 16. Bf3 Be6 17. Qd3 Nd5 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 19. Bxd5 cxd5 20. Rc5 Rxc5 21. bxc5 Qc6 22. Rb1 e5 23. dxe5 Bxe5 24. Qb5) 10... Qxb3 11. axb3 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Bxe5 13. Na4 Bc7 14. Nc5 cxd5 15. Bxd5 Nc6 16. Nxb7 Nd4 17. e3 Nc2 18. Ra4 Bd3 19. Be4 Bxe4 20. Rxe4 Rfb8 21. Nc5 Bd6 22. Rc4 Bxc5 23. Rxc5) 10. Rd1 {This move could have waited until he developed other minor pieces.} (10. Qd1 $18 {If} Na6 $1 11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. Na4 $19 Qc7 $2 13. e4 $1 Nb6 14. Bf4 g5 15. Nxb6 Qxb6 16. Be3 Qxb2 17. Rb1 Qc3 18. Rxb7 c5 19. Bd2 Qxd4 20. Nf3 Qd3 21. Nxg5 Bc4 22. Re1 Qd4 23. Qh5 h6 24. Nf3) 10... dxc4 11. Nxc4 Qa6 (11... Qxb3 12. axb3 Nbd7 13. Na5) 12. d5 {Keres is bent on spearheading a kingside attack with the knight.} cxd5 13. Nxd5 Nxd5 14. Bxd5 Bxd5 15. Rxd5 Qe6 (15... Nc6 $1 16. Rd7 (16. Be3 $1 Rfd8 17. Rad1 Rxd5 18. Rxd5 Rd8 19. Rxd8+ Nxd8 20. Qd3 $19 Ne6 $4)) 16. Rd3 Nc6 17. Be3 Rac8 ({Not} 17... Rfd8 $2 18. Rad1 {Instead he should have activated his minor pieces with} Rac8 $1 {and it would have demanded precise play by Black to overcome the resistance.}) 18. Rad1 {This move, surrendering the d-file to Black is risky.} b6 {[#]} 19. Qa4 $2 {The fatal error. White leaves the critical d4 square unattended.} h5 $1 {This move threatens .. .Qxe4, ...Qxb2 and...Qxf2+ picking up the loose bishop.} 20. b3 {Keres gives up the b2 pawn to deflect the Black queen and seize the d-file.} h4 {Tal takes up the gauntlet.} 21. Qb5 {Now White threatens both the direct Rd7 and the more insidious Rd8.} Qg4 $1 22. f3 {threatening both Rb3 and c3} (22. Kf1 $2 Qh3+ 23. Kg1 Qg4 $19) 22... Qh3 23. Bf2 {'!'} (23. g4 {Black prevents both Qc5 and Qb7. But White still has some counterplay left after} f5 24. Qd5+ Kh8 25. Qe6 $1 {would have ruled out any counter play for White.} fxg4 $2 26. Qxg4 $19 Qxg4+ $2 {would play into White's hands. After} 27. fxg4 {he would have the counterplay he wants.}) 23... hxg3 24. hxg3 {[#] "The point of White's play:the knight is lost, since neither can it be moved nor can the pawn be captured. Most of the onlookers, including a number of grandmasters, thought that Tal could not escape defeat, but they were wrong...." P.H.Clarke} e5 $1 {Brilliant play.Now White's attack fizzles out. For the rest of the game Keres tries to infiltrate Black's position. To no avail.} 25. Nd6 Rc7 26. Rc3 Nd4 (26... Qd7 $19 27. Rdd3 Nd4) 27. Bxd4 Qxg3+ 28. Kf1 {Keres tries to reach the back rank with the queen.} exd4 {'!' Centralisation at its best. From this point the queen controls both g3 and e5.} 29. Rxc7 Qxd6 30. Rc2 Bh6 (30... Qh2 $18 31. Qg5 $2) 31. Kg2 Kg7 32. Rc6 Qf4 33. Qd5 Re8 34. Rc2 (34. Qxd4+ Qxd4 35. Rxd4 Rxe2+ 36. Kg3 $1 $19 {Not} Rxa2 $4) 34... Re5 {"Playing for time, not a draw!"-P.H.Clarke} 35. Qxd4 Qf6 36. e4 {By now both players were in a fierce time scramble. So Tal rushes with the pawn.} Bf4 {[#] This does not spoil things, but it does give White an opportunity to "mix it up".} 37. Qd8 $2 Rg5+ 38. Kf2 Qe6 (38... Qe5 39. Rd5) (38... Qxd8 39. Rxd8) 39. Rh1 Rh5 40. Rxh5 gxh5 {and Keres resigned.(Some of the commentary is taken from the book," Mikhail Tal's Best Games of Chess" by P.H.Clarke.} 41. Rc8 $1 0-1

