1. c4 e6 2. g3 d5 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Bg2 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. d4 dxc4 7. Qc2 a6 8. Qxc4 b5 9. Qc2 Bb7 10. Bd2 Be4 11. Qc1 Bb7 12. Bf4 Nd5 13. Nc3 Nxf4 14. Qxf4 c5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 { d7, f8.} 16. Rfd1 Qb6 17. Ne5 Ra7 (17... Bxg2 $1 18. Kxg2 Ra7 $19 19. Ne4) 18. Bxb7 $5 {This generally desirable move, aiming to exchange the dark-squared bishops in order to block the hanging pawns, is connected with some tactical finesses, typical of Tal.} (18. Rac1 $2 Bxg2 $1 $19 {The transfer of the queen to the queenside, pursuing the same purpose,} 19. Kxg2 {allows Black obtain certain kingside counterplay:} Bd6 20. e3 (20. Ne4 $3 {[%cal Gg5c1]} Bc7) 20... h6 (20... f6 $11 {looks the simplest way to equality to me.} 21. Nc4 $6 {this is just careless. White should not leave the back rank undefended.} Bxf4 $1 22. Nxb6 Be5 23. f4 Bc7 (23... Bxc3 $15 {Haba,P (2435)-Milovanovic,R (2440)/Tuzla 1990/EXT 97 (0-1, 41)} 24. Rxc3 Rb7 25. Rd6) 24. Nd7 Nxd7 {[%csl Gg2][%cal Gd5d4]} 25. Rxd7 $6 Bb6 $1 26. Rxa7 Bxa7 27. Nd1 Rd8 $17 {[%csl Gg1]} 28. Kf3 $6) (20... Bc7 $15 21. Qe4 f5 {[%csl Rc2][%cal Gg6b1]} 22. Qd4 Qxd4 $15 23. exd4 $2 Rd8 $19 24. Ne2 Bxe5 25. dxe5 Rxd1 $15 26. Rxd1 $2) 21. Ne4 Bc7 (21... Be7 22. h4 $132) 22. Nc5 Bd6 (22... f6 23. Ned7 Nxd7 24. Nxd7 Qb7+ 25. Qf3 $13 {[%csl Ga2,Rg1] ½-½ Mokry,K (2505)-Milovanovic,R (2440)/Tuzla 1990/EXT 2000} Qxf3+ $5 26. Kxf3 Rd8 27. Rxc7 Rxc7 28. Nxf6+) 23. Qd4 Bxe5 (23... Rd8 24. Ne4) 24. Qxe5 Re8 (24... Rd8 25. Rxd8+ Qxd8 26. Nxe6 fxe6 27. Qxe6+ Kh7 $5 28. Rc8 $1) 25. Rd6) 18... Rxb7 19. Ne4 (19. Rac1 f6 (19... Bd6 20. Ne4 Bc7 21. Nc5) (19... Rc7 20. Ne4 Be7 21. Ng6 $11 {[%csl Gf1] Marin}) 20. Nd3 Bd4 21. e3 Bxc3 $132 {(=)} 22. Rxc3 Rd7 $11 23. Rdc1 Rfd8 $3 $11 {threatens with a draw by perpetual, winning time for defending the f7-pawn and to save the bishop.} 24. Nc5 Rd1+ 25. Rxd1 Rxd1+ 26. Kg2 Nd7 {(+- according to Tal. Maybe  is closer to the truth, but the line is clearly unappealing for Black.)} 27. Qg4 $2 Rd6 $1 $18 28. Ne4 Rd5) 19... Be7 20. Rac1 f6 21. Nd3 Nd7 (21... Rd7 {With the pawn on f7 still, this move is possible now, forcing the weakening of the enemy king's fortress.}) 22. Qf3 Kh8 (22... Rc7 $1 23. Rxc7 Qxc7 $1 {This move order was not mentioned by Tal in his comments.} 24. Rc1) 23. b4 { e8} Rc7 $8 24. Rxc7 { h5, e8, Tal,M} Qxc7 25. Rc1 Qb8 (25... Qb6 26. Nec5) 26. Nec5 (26. Rc6 $5 {Tal,M} {Without previous preparation, Portisch feared to embark on the dangerous line starting with} Ne5 {, but it appears that Black could more or less have forced a draw:} (26... f5 27. Nec5 Bxc5 28. bxc5 $1 e5 29. Qd5) 27. Nxe5 $1 Qxe5 28. a3) 26... Nxc5 $1 27. bxc5 (27. Nxc5 Bxc5 {Tal,M} 28. Rxc5 $6 (28. bxc5 {Tal} Qc7 $5 {Tal,M})) 27... Rc8 (27... Re8 $10 {Tal,M}) 28. Qe4 $2 e5 $18 {Tal,M} 29. f4 a5 $1 {Tal,M} 30. Kg2 Qc7 31. fxe5 fxe5 $10 {Tal,M} 32. Nxe5 Bf6 $1 $36 33. Nd3 b4 $8 34. Rc4 Kg8 35. Nf4 Qc6 36. Qxc6 Rxc6 37. Kf3 $1 Kf7 38. Ke4 Ke7 39. Kd5 Kd7 40. e4 Bc3 41. Ne2 (41. e5) 41... Bb2 42. e5 $16 {Tal,M. Well, maybe  would be closer to the truth, if Black knows this type of ending.} (42. Nf4 $8) 42... Bxe5 43. Nd4 $10 (43. Kxe5 $1 { e8 Tal,M} Re6+) 43... Bxd4 44. Rxd4 $1 Re6 45. Rd2 Re3 46. Kc4+ Kc7 47. Kb5 $16 b3 1/2-1/2

