1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. Bc4 (6. c3) 6... Bd6 7. Qh5 (7. Qe2+) 7... O-O 8. Ne2 Be6 9. Bd3 (9. Bxe6 fxe6 10. O-O Qc7) 9... g6 10. Qh4 Re8 11. O-O Nd7 12. Bf4 {To my taste, this is the most constructive move, developing the knight on an active square and leaving the c1-bishop's diagonal clear. But Portisch thinks this line is not dangerous as White has no pressure on the c-file. One way or another, Black has excellent equalizing chances in both cases, only that in this game the process is subtle and gradual while in the next one it has a forced character. This still speaks for the merits of 12.c3.} (12. Nf4 Nf8 13. Nxe6 Nxe6 $13 {It is not so easy to free the queen from the bishop's pressure:} 14. c3 h5) 12... Be7 13. Rfe1 Nb6 (13... Ne5) 14. Rad1 Bc4 (14... Nd5) 15. Bxc4 Nxc4 16. b3 Na3 (16... Nb6) 17. c4 (17. c3) 17... f5 (17... Bb4 $11 {Another possible regrouping is} 18. Bd2 Qa5 19. Bxb4 Qxb4 20. Qg4 b5 $5 {Taking advantage of the fact that the returning diagonal is obstructed, making a later h3-h4 inoffensive.}) 18. Qg3 Bb4 19. Bd2 Bxd2 (19... Qa5 20. Qd3) 20. Rxd2 Qa5 21. Red1 b5 22. d5 bxc4 23. dxc6 (23. d6 Nb1 24. Rc2 Na3 25. Rcd2 Nb1) 23... Rac8 24. Rd5 (24. h4 Rxc6 25. b4 Qxb4 26. Rd8 Rc8 27. Rxc8 Rxc8 28. Nc3) 24... Qc7 (24... Nb5 25. bxc4 Rxe2 26. Rf1 Qxa2 27. Rxb5 Qxc4 $11 28. Rxf5 $2 {[%csl Ge5]} Qxc6) 25. Nd4 Qe7 (25... Qxg3 26. hxg3 a6 27. Rc1 cxb3 28. axb3) 26. h4 Qb4 27. Qg5 c3 28. Rd7 (28. Rc1 c2 29. Nxf5 Re1+ $11 30. Rxe1 $132 Qxe1+ $132 31. Kh2 c1=Q 32. Rd8+ Qe8 33. Rxe8+ Rxe8 34. Qxc1) 28... c2 29. Nxc2 Nxc2 30. c7 Re1+ (30... Qb6 $2 31. R1d6 Re1+ 32. Kh2 Qxf2 33. Rd8+ Kg7 $17 34. Qf6+ Kh6 $17 35. Qg5+ Kg7) 31. Rxe1 Qxe1+ 32. Kh2 Nd4 33. Qf6 (33. Rd8+ Kg7 34. Rxc8 $2 Ne2 35. Rg8+ Kxg8 36. c8=Q+ Kg7 37. Qc7 Qg1+ 38. Kh3 Qh1+ 39. Qh2 Ng1+ 40. Kg3 Ne2+) 33... Nf3+ 34. Kg3 1-0

