1. d4 {Marin,Mihail 1) Tal in Shakhmatyi (Riga) 1963. Reprinted and translated in 2)Kirillov: Michail Tal. Sein Lebenswerk 1962-1973.Chessgate. 2004. S. Band2,S.27-28} d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nc3 e6 6. Nh4 Bg6 7. Be2 Nbd7 8. O-O Bd6 9. g3 O-O 10. Bd2 Qe7 (10... Re8 11. Rc1 a6 12. Nxg6 hxg6 13. f3 (13. c5 Bc7 14. f4) 13... e5 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Qb3 exd4 16. exd4) 11. Rc1 dxc4 12. Nxg6 hxg6 13. Bxc4 e5 14. Bb3 Nb6 15. Qc2 Kh8 (15... e4 16. f3 Rae8 (16... exf3 17. Qxg6) 17. Rce1) (15... Nbd5 16. Nxd5 cxd5 (16... Nxd5 17. e4) 17. dxe5 Bxe5 18. Rfd1 Rfd8 (18... Rac8 19. Qxc8 Rxc8 20. Rxc8+ Kh7 21. Rc2 g5 22. Bc3) 19. Bc3 Rac8 20. Qd3) (15... exd4 16. exd4 Nbd5 17. Bg5) 16. dxe5 Qxe5 (16... Bxe5 17. f4 (17. Rfd1) 17... Bxc3 18. Qxc3 Nbd5 19. Qd3 Rad8 20. Qe2 Rfe8) 17. f4 (17. e4) (17. Rce1) 17... Qf5 18. e4 (18. Kg2 Rae8 (18... Qxc2 19. Rxc2 Rae8 20. Kf3) 19. Rfe1 (19. Rce1 Ne4 (19... Qxc2 20. Bxc2 Nc4 21. Bc1) 20. Nxe4 Qxe4+ 21. Qxe4 Rxe4 22. Kf3 Nc4 23. Bc3 Rfe8 24. Bxc4 Rxc4) 19... Ne4 20. Rcd1 Nxd2 (20... Bc7 21. Nxe4 Qxe4+ (21... Rxe4 22. Qb1) 22. Qxe4 Rxe4 23. Kf3 f5 24. Bc3) (20... Nxc3 21. Bxc3) 21. Qxd2 Bc7 22. e4) 18... Qh3 {[#]} 19. Rce1 $5 {Marin,Mihail: 'This is thematic, very nice, but not necessary.'} Ng4 20. Bc1 Bc5+ 21. Kh1 Be3 (21... Rad8 22. Qg2 Qh5) 22. Qe2 {Marin,Mihail: 'White has fantastic compensation for the sacrificed piece. All that Black can dream of is to return the piece for two pawns, but that is not easy to achieve.'} (22. Bxe3 Nxe3) 22... Bxc1 23. Rxc1 Nd7 24. Rce1 {Marin,Mihail: '?!'} (24. Bc2 Nc5 25. Nd1 Rad8 26. Ne3 Nxe3 27. Qxe3) 24... Nc5 25. Bd1 f5 26. Qg2 (26. e5 Nd3) 26... Qxg2+ 27. Kxg2 {Marin,Mihail: ''} Nd3 (27... Rad8 28. Bxg4 fxg4 29. Re2) 28. Re2 Nc1 29. Ree1 (29. h3 $2 Nxe2 $18 (29... Nh6 30. Rc2 Nd3 31. e5) 30. hxg4 Nd4 (30... Nxc3 31. Bb3 {The lack of coordination reigning in Black's camp allows the same combination as in the main game, but in an even more favourable form.} (31. Rh1+ Kg8 32. Bb3+ Rf7) 31... Nd5 32. exd5 fxg4 33. Rh1+ (33. dxc6 $16 {Jansa,V-Geller,E/Budapest 1970/EXT 2009 (1-0, 83). White has three pawns for a knight and a crushing space advantage.} Rf5 34. c7 Rc8 35. Rc1) 33... Kg8 34. dxc6+ Rf7 35. Rd1) 31. Ne2 Rad8 (31... Nxe2 {Marin,Mihail} 32. Bb3) (31... Ne6 {and now 17...g6 would transpose to the game. Another neutral and not really accurate move is} 32. Bb3 Rfe8 33. Nd4 Nf8 34. Rh1+ Nh7 35. Nf3) 32. Nxd4 {[#]} Rxd4 $18 33. Rh1+ Kg8 34. Bb3+ Rf7 35. exf5 (35. gxf5 gxf5 36. e5 Rd2+ 37. Kf3 $16 {Sigurjonsson,G-Smejkal,J/Raach 1969/EXT 2001 (1-0, 54)} {Marin,Mihail: 'The move order relevant for our discussion is'} Rxb2) 35... gxf5 36. gxf5 Rd2+ (36... Rb4 $1 $15 {Marin,Mihail} 37. Be6 Kf8 {Marin,Mihail: 'He may have missed that'} 38. Rh8+ {Marin,Mihail: 'allows'} Ke7 {Tal Marin,Mihail: '!'} 39. b3 Rf6 ({Since Black has deprived his own knight of the g6-square, the plan based on} 39... Rf8 {Marin,Mihail} 40. Rh7 Rf7 {is very dangerous. Black will have problems maintaining the control of the e5-square.} (40... Kf6 41. Kf3 g6 42. Rh6) 41. Kf3) 40. Rg8 {Marin,Mihail} g6 41. Rg7+ Kf8 (41... Kd6 $44 42. Rd7+ Kc5 43. fxg6 Rxe6 44. f5 Rd6 45. g7 Rg4 46. b4+ $11 Kd5 47. Re7) 42. Rxg6 Rxg6 $15 43. fxg6 $1 {Marin,Mihail} Kg7 44. f5 Rd4 (44... c5 45. Kf3 b5 46. g4 $18 {Tal} c4 $1 {Tal} 47. Ke3 cxb3 48. axb3 {Marin,Mihail}) 45. Kf3 $40) 37. Kf3 Rxb2 38. Be6 Kf8 39. g4 Rb5 40. Rh8+ Ke7 41. a4 Ra5 $14 42. Rb8 Rf8 $142 43. Rxb7+ {Tal} Kd6 44. Rxg7 Rxa4 45. Rd7+ Kc5 $11 46. g5) 29... Nd3 30. Re2 {[#]} 1-0

