1. e4 {Dembo,Yelena} c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 6. Nf3 Bf5 (6... Qc7) (6... Bg4) 7. Bf4 (7. Bd3 Bxd3 (7... Bg6 $1 8. O-O Nd7 9. a4) (7... Rg8) (7... Na6 8. Bxf5 $14 {Sanguinetti-Szmetan, Argentina 1974. White is well ahead in his attack.} (8. O-O $1 $40 {begins to look like GM-Amateur; Black will soon be crushed.} Bxd3 {Dembo,Yelena does not work in this instance. The kingside is securely blocked for now, so White can focus his attention on the queenside.} 9. Qxd3 Qd5 (9... e6)) 8... Qa5+ $2 9. c3 Qxf5) (7... e6 8. c3 $16 (8. O-O) 8... Bg6 9. Bf4 $16 Nd7 10. Qd2 (10. O-O) 10... Be7 (10... Qb6 11. O-O-O O-O-O) 11. O-O Qa5 $13 12. Bg3 Rc8 13. Nh4 Bxd3 14. Qxd3 Qh5 15. Rae1 Kf8 16. f4 f5 17. Nf3 Rg8 18. a4 Rd8 19. Qc2 Nf6 20. Bh4 Ne4 21. Bxe7+ Kxe7 22. a5 f6 23. Nd2 Rxg2+ 24. Kxg2 Rg8+ 25. Kh1 Ng3+ 26. Kg2 Nxf1+ 27. Kxf1 Qxh2 28. Rxe6+ Kd7 29. Rd6+ Kc7 30. Rd7+ Kc8 31. Rg7 Rxg7 32. Qxf5+ Kc7 33. Ke1 Rg1+ 34. Nf1 Qg3+ 35. Ke2 Rg2+ 36. Kd1 Qf3+) 8. Qxd3 Na6 $36 {0-1 (56) Wade,R-Reshevsky,S Buenos Aires 1960} 9. Bd2 {[%cal Ye7g8] Dembo,Yelena}) 7... Nd7 (7... Qb6 $16 {[%cal Ye1f2,Ya2a3,Yb2b4,Yc4c5] is similar} 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Qxb2 10. O-O) (7... e6) 8. c3 (8. Bd3 $16 {[%cal Yb2b4] and White's play proceeds unhindered.} Bg6) 8... Qb6 9. b4 e5 10. Bg3 (10. dxe5 O-O-O (10... fxe5 11. Nxe5 Bg7)) 10... O-O-O 11. Be2 h5 {Dembo,Yelena: '?!'} 12. O-O Be4 {Dembo,Yelena: 'Against anything else White plays 13.h4 and Black's kingside play is over. His minor pieces have no squares and the only way to do anything is ...h8, ...g8, ...f7, ...f8-e7 and a sacrifice on g5; clearly impossible. Now Black wants to play ...f4 as well, and win the g5; but this is too slow. '} (12... Bg4 $44 {Markos; in both cases White's compensation is very good.} 13. Bh4 {Dembo,Yelena} Bxf3 14. Bxf3 exd4) 13. Nd2 (13. Bh4 Rg8) 13... Bd5 (13... Bg6 14. Nf3) (13... Bxg2 14. Kxg2 h4 15. Bf4 $14 {Markos} exf4 {Dembo,Yelena}) 14. Bxh5 $1 exd4 {Dembo,Yelena: '?!' Dembo,Yelena: 'Black must clear the g8-square for his e7 to move there, so that the  attacks the g5, but...'} 15. c4 Be6 {Dembo,Yelena: 'The captures on c5 both allow White too much play:'} 16. a3 $16 Ne5 17. Re1 d3 18. c5 Qb5 19. Rb1 (19. Bxe5 fxe5 20. Bg4 $18 (20. Rxe5 Bg7 21. Bxf7 Bxe5 22. Bxe6+ Kb8 $40 {with a powerful attack.} 23. Bc4 $6 {Dembo,Yelena} Bxh2+ 24. Kf1 Be5 $16 {Markos} 25. Kg1 {Dembo,Yelena} Bh2+) 20... Rh6 {Dembo,Yelena} {and now White has the powerful typical sac} 21. Rxe5 $1 Bg7 22. Bxe6+ $16 {[%csl Rd6] when Black's pieces are totally uncoordinated, for example} fxe6 23. Re4 Bxa1 24. a4 Qa6 25. Qxa1) 19... Bh6 20. a4 $16 {Dembo,Yelena: ''} Qa6 21. f4 {Dembo,Yelena: 'Black has been thrown on to the defensive.'} Nc4 22. b5 cxb5 23. Rxb5 ({Dembo,Yelena: 'The most forceful continuation here was'} 23. axb5 {Dembo,Yelena: '!'} Qa2) 23... Na3 (23... Nxd2 $11 24. Qxd2 Qxa4 25. Reb1 $1 $16 Bd5 (25... Qc2 $16 26. Qa5 d2 (26... Bd5 27. R5b2 $1 $16 {Tal}) 27. Bd1)) 24. Rb2 Nc2 {[#]} (24... Bf8) 25. Bf3 $2 $14 Bd5 26. Re7 Bf8 27. Bxd5 Rxd5 28. Rbxb7 (28. Rexb7 Bxc5+ 29. Bf2 $14 (29. Kh1 Bb6 $16 30. Rxf7 Ne3) 29... Bb6 (29... Bxf2+ $11 30. Kxf2 Qxb7 (30... Qxa4 31. Qg4+ f5 32. Qg5 Qd4+ 33. Kg3 $11 Qe3+ 34. Nf3))) 28... Bxe7 29. Rxe7 Qc6 30. Rxf7 Rxc5 (30... f5) 31. Qg4+ f5 (31... Kb8 32. f5+ $11 Ka8 33. Qg7) 32. Qg7 (32. Rxf5 Kb8) 32... Re8 33. h4 {Dembo,Yelena: 'The difference in coordination is evident.'} (33. h3 $18 {[#]} Ne3 {Sealed move} 34. Bf2 Rc1+ 35. Kh2 Qxg2+ 36. Qxg2 Nxg2 37. Kxg2 $16 Rc2 $11) 33... Ne3 $2 {[#]} 34. Bf2 $1 $16 Rc1+ 35. Kh2 Ng4+ 36. Kg3 Nxf2 37. Nf3 (37. Kxf2 Re2+ $2) 37... Ne4+ 38. Kh2 d2 39. Nxd2 Nxd2 1-0

