1. Nf3 d5 2. d4 e6 3. c4 dxc4 4. e3 Nf6 {The Noteboom Variation of the Slav Defence. But Little Misha was playing by instinct, not by book.} 5. Bxc4 c5 6. O-O {A deviation leading to a kind of Catalan position.} Nc6 $5 {A sharp move. Little Misha intends to undouble the pawns and aim for rapid development of queenside pieces. The flipside of the coin is that it opens up the diagonal for the White bishop on g2.} 7. Qe2 cxd4 8. Rd1 Be7 9. exd4 O-O 10. a3 Nd5 11. Nc3 Nxc3 (11... Re8 12. Ba2 Bxa3 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. Qb5 Bd6 15. Bxd5 h6 16. Ne5 Bxe5 17. dxe5 Qc7 18. Bf4 Be6 19. Bf3 a6 20. Qc5 Rac8 21. Rac1 Qe7 22. Be3 Bb3 23. Qxe7 Rxe7 24. Rd3 Ba4 25. Bg4 Rd8 26. Rd6 Ra8 27. f4 Nd8 28. Rc8 Rxc8 29. Bxc8 Ne6 30. f5 Nf8 31. Bd4 Rc7 32. Rd8 Bd7 33. Bxd7 Rxd7 34. Rxd7 Nxd7 35. Kf2 g6 36. g4 h5 37. e6 fxe6 38. fxe6 Nb8 39. Kf3 Nc6 40. Bf6 Kf8 41. gxh5) (11... a6) 12. bxc3 Bf6 $1 {This move leaves the e-pawn backward.But little Misha is least concerned about theoretical shibboleths.} 13. Bd3 {Fishing in troubled waters. But what was he to do?} Qa5 (13... Bd7 14. Qe4 g6 15. Bh6 Bg7 16. Qf4 Rc8 17. Be4 Bxh6 18. Qxh6 Qf6 19. h4 Rc7 20. h5 Rfc8 21. Rd3 Be8 22. Re1 Ne7 23. Ne5 Qg7 24. Qd2 f6 25. hxg6 hxg6 26. Ng4 f5 27. Nh6+ Kf8 28. Rf3 g5 29. Bxb7 Rxb7 30. Rxe6 Bf7 31. Nxf5 Nxf5 32. Rxf5 Rb1+ 33. Re1 Rcb8 34. Rfe5) 14. Rb1 a6 $1 (14... Qxc3 15. d5 exd5 16. Bb2 Qc5 17. Bxf6 gxf6) 15. c4 Rd8 $2 {Typically, little Misha avoids the exchange of queens.} 16. Be3 (16. d5 exd5 17. cxd5 Ne7 (17... Qxd5 18. Bxh7+) 18. d6 Nc6 (18... Nf5) 19. Qe4 g6 20. Qf4 Bg7 21. Ng5 Ne5 22. Bc4 Nxc4 23. Qxf7+ Kh8 24. Qxc4) 16... Qxa3 17. d5 exd5 18. Bb6 (18. cxd5 Nb4 (18... Rxd5 19. Bxh7+) (18... Ne7 19. d6 Rxd6 20. Qc2) 19. Bc5) 18... Be6 19. Bxd8 Rxd8 20. cxd5 Bxd5 $5 21. Bxh7+ $6 Kxh7 22. Rxd5 Rxd5 23. Qe4+ Kg8 24. Qxd5 Qe7 25. h3 (25. g3 Bc3 26. Kg2) 25... Bc3 26. Rb3 $2 {Anxious to support the bishop on d2 as the knight defending him is also under attack.But it should have proved inadequate in the end.} (26. g4 Qd8 (26... Bf6 27. Re1 (27. Kf1 Qd8 28. Qxd8+ Nxd8 29. Ke2 b5 30. Kd3 Be7 31. Nd4 b4 32. Kc4 a5 33. Kb5 Bd6 34. Kxa5) 27... Qd8 28. Qxd8+ Bxd8 (28... Nxd8 29. g5 Bc3 30. Re8+) 29. Re8+ Kh7 30. Ne5) 27. Qxd8+ Nxd8 28. Rc1 Bf6 29. g5 Be7 30. Rc7 Bd6 31. Rd7) 26... Bf6 27. g4 Qd8 $5 {An imaginative combination, if not winning outright.} 28. Qxd8+ $2 Nxd8 29. Kf1 {The experienced master keeps everything complicated with the hope that the kid could still make a mistake.} Be7 30. Ke2 b5 31. Nd4 b4 32. Kd3 a5 33. Kc4 a4 34. Re3 Kf8 35. Nf5 Bg5 36. f4 Bxf4 37. Re4 Ne6 38. Kxb4 g6 39. Nd4 Bd6+ $1 {Played with impish humour.} 40. Kc4 $4 (40. Kxa4 Nc5+) 40... Nxd4 $1 {And Black wins the rook. This is what the great master had overlooked.} {Saving the queen with} 41. Kxd4 {only eases Black's task.} a3 42. Kd5 Be7 43. Ra4 Kg7 $19 44. Kc6 {was the lesser evil, though after} (44. g5 Bxg5 (44... f5 45. gxf6+ Kxf6 46. Ke4 Kg5 47. Kf3) 45. Rxa3) 44... f5 $1 45. Ra7 Kf6 46. gxf5 (46. g5+ Ke6 47. h4 f4 48. Ra4 Bd6 49. Re4+ Be5 50. Ra4 Bd6 51. Re4+) 46... gxf5 47. Kd5 Bf8 $17 {Black should prevail.} {But not} 48. Ra8 $4 {and White turns the tables with} Bb4 49. Ra4 Bf8 50. Ra5 Kg5 51. Ra8 Bb4 {More precise was} 52. Ra4 $1 Bf8 53. h4+ Kh5 54. Ke6 a2 55. Kxf5 a1=Q 56. Rxa1 Kxh4 57. Ra8 Bd6 58. Rd8 $19 Bc7 59. Rd3 Bb8 {fails to} 60. Rd7 $1 $19 Kh3 $1 {was a better way of defending the bishop.} {He had to accept the Greek gift with} 61. Rg7 $1 Kh2 62. Ke4 Bd6 {and he would have rallied round with} 63. Kf3 Bc5 64. Rg2+ {(with the threat of Bf5+ cutting off the rook on f3)} Kh1 65. Kg3 Bd6+ 66. Kh3 Bc5 67. Rh2+ {Nevertheless,} Kg1 $1 {is stronger as Black's superior development tells in the end.} {If} 68. Rc2 Bd4 69. Kg3 Be5+ 70. Kf3 Bd6 71. Rc6 Be5 72. Rg6+ Kh2 73. Rg4 Bc7 74. Rh4+ Kg1 $19 {and the e3 pawn falls.} 75. Rg4+ Kh2 76. Rg2+ Kh1 $19 {is a variation on the same theme, the precarious position of Nd5 and e3 pawn.} 77. Rg7 Be5 78. Rg5 Bc7 79. Kf2 Bb6+ 80. Kg3 Bc7+ $13 81. Kh3 {would be more usual.} Bb6 82. Rb5 {is standard.} Bf2 83. Rb2 Bc5 84. Rh2+ Kg1 85. Rg2+ Kh1 86. Rg7 Bd4 87. Rg6 Bf2 88. Rh6 Be3 89. Rh7 Bc5 90. Rh5 Bb6 91. Kg3+ Kg1 92. Rh7 Bf2+ 93. Kf3 Bb6 94. Rg7+ Kh2 95. Rg2+ Kh1 96. Kg3 Bc7+ 97. Kh3 Bb6 98. Rg7 Bd4 99. Rh7 Bc5 100. Rc7 Bd4 101. Rc4 Bf2 102. Rc1+ Bg1 103. Rd1 0-1

