1. e4 {Stohl,Igor} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 8. Qd2 Qxb2 9. Rb1 Qa3 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Nfd7 12. Ne4 Qxa2 (12... h6 $142 $1 {Stohl,Igor is a far safer choice, but this was the first game, in which 12.e4 was played. Only later did Black begin to understand that slipping in this inconspicuous move improves his tactical chances.}) (12... Qa4 $2 {Stohl,Igor} 13. Bb5 $1 axb5 14. O-O Bc5 15. Nxc5 Nxc5 16. Nf5 $1 O-O 17. Ne7+ Kh8 18. Rxf7 Nbd7 19. Qf2 Rg8 20. Rf4 $1 $18 {[%cal Re7g6] g6,Alexander,C-Walther,E/Dublin/1957/}) (12... Nxe5 $2 {Stohl,Igor} 13. Nb5 Nd3+ $8 14. Bxd3 axb5 15. Bxb5+ Nd7 (15... Bd7 16. Bxd7+ Nxd7 17. Rxb7 $40) 16. O-O Bc5+ 17. Kh1 h6 18. Bh4 g5 19. Nf6+ Ke7 20. Nxd7 Qe3 21. Qxe3 Bxe3 22. Ne5 {1-0,White,K-Battaggia,P /Genova/2003/}) 13. Rb3 Qa1+ {Stohl,Igor: '?!' Stohl,Igor: 'And this in all probability loses almost by force.'} (13... Bc5 $2 {Stohl,Igor} 14. Qc3 $18 {c5,d6}) ({This was the last moment, when it's still possible to play} 13... h6 $142 $5 {Stohl,Igor Although now Black must already reckon with} 14. Qc3 Nc6 15. Nxc6 hxg5 16. Na7 {this probably isn't so dangerous due to} Qa4 17. Bd3 Rh4 18. Nd6+ Bxd6 19. exd6 Qf4 $1 (19... Re4+ $143 20. Kd2 Re5 21. Nxc8 Rc5 22. Qxg7 Raxc8 23. Qg8+ Nf8 24. Rf1 Qd7 25. Kc1 f5 26. Bxf5 Qxd6 27. Bg6+ Kd7 28. Qg7+ Qe7 29. Rd1+ {1-0,Jansen-Knuiman,J/Netherlands Corr/1960/}) 20. Kd1 Qxd6 21. Nxc8 Qc5 $13 {/}) (13... Nc6 {Stohl,Igor} 14. Nxc6 bxc6 15. Qc3 Qa4 16. Bc4 Nxe5 (16... Nc5 17. Qd2 f6 18. exf6 $1 Nxe4 19. f7+ Kxf7 20. Qf4+ Ke8 21. Qxe4 Bc5 22. Rd3 Bb6 23. Ra3 Qxa3 24. Qxc6+ {1-0,Ostrovjerhov-Koshelj/corr/1971/}) 17. O-O $1 $32 {} Nxc4 18. Nf6+ gxf6 19. Qxf6 $18) 14. Kf2 Qa4 $2 {[#]} (14... h6 $15) (14... Nc6 {Stohl,Igor} 15. Nxc6 bxc6 16. Nd6+ Bxd6 17. Qxd6 Nf6 18. exf6 gxf6 19. Rd3 Qa5 20. Bxf6 Qf5+ 21. Rf3 {1-0, Grabczewski,R-Matkowski,W/Warsaw/ 1957/}) 15. Bb5 $6 {Stohl,Igor: 'Tal rightly feels it's time to act and throws in a piece, but doesn't choose the right one. Correct was'} (15. Nxe6 $1 $18 fxe6 16. Nd6+ Bxd6 17. Qxd6 Rf8+ 18. Kg3 Nf6 (18... Rf7 {Stohl,Igor: '?'} 19. Qxe6+ Kf8 20. Bc4 $18) 19. exf6 $18 gxf6 {Stohl,Igor} 20. Be2 fxg5 (20... Nd7 21. Bh5+ Kd8 22. Be3 Rg8+ 23. Kh3 Rg6 24. Rc3 $1 Qe4 25. Rd1 $1 Qxg2+ 26. Kh4 Qe4+ 27. Bf4 $18) 21. Bh5+ Rf7 22. Bxf7+ Kxf7 23. Rf3+ Kg6 24. h4 $1 $18 {with a winning attack (Kasparov in My Great Predecessors 2 and 3).}) 15... axb5 16. Nxb5 f6 $17 17. exf6 gxf6 $2 (17... Qxe4 $15 {Stohl,Igor: ''} 18. fxg7 Bc5+ 19. Re3 (19. Kg3 {Stohl,Igor} Rg8 {} 20. Re1 Nf6 $1 21. Bxf6 Rxg7+ $1 22. Bxg7 Qg6+ 23. Kf3 Qxg7 24. Qf4 Na6 $17 {is an even more fantastic line shown by Kasparov.}) 19... Qf5+ 20. Ke2 Qg4+ 21. Ke1 Ra1+ 22. Kf2 Qf5+ 23. Ke2 Rg8 $17 24. Rxa1 Qg4+ 25. Ke1 Rxg7 $15) 18. Re1 $14 {Stohl,Igor: '!!' Stohl,Igor: 'A beautiful quiet move, now White again has a powerful attack'} Ra6 {[#]} (18... Nc6 19. Bxf6 Qxe4 20. Rxe4 Nxf6 21. Nc7+ $16) (18... fxg5 $2 {Stohl,Igor} 19. Nc7+ Kd8 20. Nxe6+ Ke8 21. Ng7+ $1 Kd8 22. Qxg5+ Kc7 23. Qf4+ $18) 19. Bxf6 $18 Nxf6 20. Nxf6+ Kf7 21. Rf3 {Stohl,Igor: '?' Stohl,Igor: 'Very natural, but the computer within seconds points out the far stronger'} (21. Ne4 $1 {[%cal Rd2g5,Rb3f3,Rd2f4] (Stohl,Igor) f3, f4, g5. Here Black's  can't join the defence and he is helpless against all the threats.}) 21... Qh4+ 22. Kf1 e5 {Stohl,Igor: '!'} 23. Qd5+ Be6 (23... Kg6 {Stohl,Igor} 24. Ne8 $1 $40) 24. Nd7+ Kg6 {Stohl,Igor: '?' Stohl,Igor: 'The final mistake, necessary was'} (24... Ke7 $1 {Stohl,Igor} 25. Qc5+ Ke8 26. Qc8+ (26. Rxf8+ Rxf8+ 27. Qxf8+ Kxd7 28. Rd1+ Kc6 29. Qxb8 $5 (29. Qd6+ Kxb5 30. Rb1+ Ka4 31. Ra1+ Kb5 32. Rb1+ $11) 29... Qc4+ 30. Kg1 Kxb5 31. Qxb7+ Kc5 32. Qc7+ Kb4 33. Qb8+ Kc5 34. Qxe5+ Bd5 {is interesting, but White has no immediate win in sight.}) 26... Ke7 27. Qxb8 Qc4+ 28. Kg1 Bg7 29. Qxb7 Qc8 $1 $14 (29... Bxd7 30. Qxa6 Qc5+ 31. Kh1 Qxb5 32. Qa3+ Ke8 33. Qd6 $18)) 25. Nxe5+ Kg7 (25... Kh5 {Stohl,Igor} 26. Qd1 $1 $18) 26. Rg3+ Qxg3 {Stohl,Igor: ''} (26... Kh6 {Stohl,Igor} 27. Nf7+ Bxf7 28. Qd2+ Kh5 29. Re5+ $18) 27. Qxb7+ {Stohl,Igor: '+-'} Nd7 28. hxg3 Rb6 29. Qc7 Bc5 30. Nxd7 Bc4+ 31. Re2 {Stohl,Igor: '' Stohl,Igor: 'A brilliant game, no wonder Kasparov chose it to open the chapter about Tal in his book.'} 1-0

