{Tigran Vartanovich Petrosian (also known as ''Iron Tigran'') was the brilliant champion of blocked pawn structures, and his ability to play them with both sides yielded him a great number of points. First of all we investigate the system where White tries to open up the queenside with b2-b3 and c3-c4.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 {White's main idea is to gain some space on the queenside, carrying out b2-b4.} c4 $5 {Black prefers to close the queenside. A logical idea against a2-a3 and a good choice against Mikhail Tal! White's initiative is temporarily frozen. This advance became a popular plan in the 1950s. The theory of this line was not very advanced, so players tended to make many mistakes here. Except for Petrosian!} 7. Be2 ({The more natural option is} 7. Nbd2 $5 {trying to achieve some activity with b2-b3 and c2-c4 – see the next games.}) ({A)} 7. Ng5 $2 {is premature:} f6 $1 8. exf6 Nxf6 9. f4 Bd6 10. g3 O-O 11. Bg2 Bd7 12. Qe2 Rae8 $17 {Acs-Kabatianski, Budapest 1994}) ({B) It is also too early for} 7. b4 $6 cxb3 8. Bb2 Na5 9. Nbd2 Bd7 10. a4 $2 (10. c4 $142 Ne7 $15) 10... Rc8 $17 {Littlewood-Chandler, Portsmouth 1976}) 7... Bd7 {Now Black can castle queenside. My advice, however, is to wait and first play some more useful moves.} ({Another idea is the classic French break} 7... f6 $5 {opening up the centre at once, although at this point it is still unclear which side will profit most from this manoeuvre. See Games 4, 11 and 12 for similar ideas.}) 8. Nbd2 Na5 $1 {Black's main trump is his continuous pressure on b3.} ({Remember!} 8... Nge7 $4 9. Bxc4 $1 {- 23 games in the MegaBase!}) 9. O-O {White has easily deployed his pieces, but there is no way that he can profit from his lead in development.} Ne7 {This is a very popular, flexible manoeuvre. The other knight retains a choice between four possible destinations (c6/c8 or g6/f5).} (9... f6 $5 {is still interesting, see game 4}) ({Or,} 9... Be7 $5 10. Rb1 Nh6 {looks also a nice setup}) ({Immediate castling is complicated:} 9... O-O-O 10. Rb1 $1 Nb3 $5 (10... Kb8 11. b3 cxb3 12. c4 $5 Ba4 13. Bb2 $13 {Delchev-Moreno Carnero,J Andorra 2002}) 11. Nxb3 Ba4 $1 12. Be3 Bxb3 13. Qc1 a5 14. Nd2 a4 15. f4 Ne7 16. g4 $6 h5 $132 {Mamedyarov-El Gindy, Dubai 2003}) 10. Rb1 $5 ({A) Now, White could continue} 10. Ng5 h6 11. Nh3 O-O-O 12. Nf4 g6 (12... f5 {or 12...f6!} 13. exf6 gxf6 14. Bf3 Qd6 15. Nh5 e5 $36 {Grosar-Bareev Bled 1991}) 13. Rb1 Kb8 $5 {The position is blocked and White has no successful plans at his disposal. But Black has!} 14. g4 $6 Bb5 (14... Nb3 $5 {} 15. Nxb3 Ba4 $132) 15. Re1 Nc8 16. Bf1 Be7 17. h3 Qc6 $5 {[%cal Gb5a4]} 18. Bg2 Ba4 19. Qe2 Bc2 20. Nxc4 Bxb1 21. Nxa5 Qc7 22. b4 g5 $1 $17 {Zaitsev-Petrosian,T Moscow URS-ch 1969; and Black won.}) ({B) Here is also my recent game:} 10. Ne1 O-O-O 11. g3 f6 12. exf6 gxf6 13. Bg4 e5 14. dxe5 fxe5 15. Rb1 h5 16. Bxd7+ (16. Bxh5 Kb8 $40) 16... Rxd7 17. b3 cxb3 18. Nxb3 Nxb3 (18... Nc4 $5) 19. Rxb3 Qe6 20. h4 Nf5 21. Nf3 Bc5 22. Kg2 e4 (22... Rf8 $17) 23. Nd4 Bxd4 24. cxd4 Nd6 25. Bf4 Nc4 26. Rb5 b6 27. a4 Kb7 28. Qb3 Rf8 29. Bg5 Rf3 30. Qa2 Rdf7 31. Rbb1 e3 32. fxe3 Rxf1 33. Rxf1 Qe4+ 34. Kg1 Rxf1+ {0-1 Montell Lorenzo,J (2270)-Moskalenko,V (2565)/Torredembarra 2010}) 10... h6 $5 {A useful move in this kind of position: it prevents the white pieces from occupying g5 and prepares an eventual ...g7-g5. Sometimes, the vacated h7-square can be occupied by the black queen.} ({Tactics:} 10... Nb3 $2 {is an awful mistake:} 11. Bxc4 $1 Nxc1 12. Qxc1 $16 {[%cal Gd2c4,Rc4b6,Yc4d6,Rd6f7,Rd6e8] Kerkhoff-Timman Rotterdam 1964}) ({A) Another interesting option is} 10... Ng6 $5 {intending ...f7-f6, although there are no high-level games with it}) ({B) The following line is unclear:} 10... Nec6 11. Qe1 $5 {[%cal Ge2d1]} (11. Re1 Be7 12. Qc2 O-O $11) 11... f5 12. exf6 gxf6 13. b4 $5 cxb3 14. c4 O-O-O $13 {Gdanski-Owczarzak, Warsaw 2002}) ({C) The immediate} 10... O-O-O $6 {is dangerous:} 11. b4 $36 {Lebedev-Petrosian,T Moscow 1958.}) 11. Qc2 ({For} 11. Re1 {see the next game}) (11. b4 $6 cxb3 12. c4 Qc7 $1 $146) (11. g3 Nec6 12. Nh4 Be7 13. Ng2 O-O-O $13 {Sveshnikov-Farago, Novi Sad 1979} (13... O-O $5 $11)) 11... Qc7 12. b3 {Mikhail Tal is obviously trying to open up the position in order to attack.} (12. Re1 $5 {is a good waiting alternative.}) 12... cxb3 13. Nxb3 Ba4 $1 {This pin by the bishop is an important tactical resource against b2-b3.} 14. Nfd2 {The position is balanced. White cannot exploit his lead in development.} Nec6 (14... Rc8 $5 {may be more precise:} 15. Qb2 Nxb3 16. Nxb3 Nf5 17. Bd2 Be7 {preparing to castle kingside}) 15. Qb2 O-O-O $2 {This must be a joke! Only an optimist can play this and keep his nerve.} (15... Be7 {was more than sufficient, when after 16...0-0 Black's position is very solid.}) 16. Nxa5 Nxa5 17. c4 $1 $40 {Now Petrosian is forced to display his best defensive skills.} Be7 18. cxd5 (18. c5 $5) 18... exd5 19. Bd1 Bd7 20. Bc2 Kb8 21. Bd3 Rc8 22. Nb3 Nc4 23. Qa2 Ba4 24. Bf5 Bd7 25. Bd3 Ba4 26. Bf5 Bd7 {Summary: with 12.b3 White starts action on the queenside, allowing Black the superior pawn structure. Logically, the correct counter-plan for Black was to prepare kingside castling, which would have given him good prospects. However, the game continuation also demonstrates the solidity of the setup with 6...c4. The analysis of other, secondary lines also shows that Black's fortress is made of hard rock!} 1/2-1/2

