1. Nf3 d6 2. d4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 g6 5. O-O Bg7 6. c4 O-O 7. Nc3 e6 8. Qc2 Nc6 9. Rd1 Qe7 10. e4 fxe4 (10... e5 11. dxe5 Nxe5 12. Nxe5 dxe5 {13...c6=} 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. cxd5 f4 {White's strategic superiority on the queenside is evident, but Black's attacking chances should not be neglected.} 15. b3 Rf7 16. a4 Qf6 17. Ba3 Bd7 18. d6 c6 $13 {Filippov,V (2605)-Jakubiec,A (2453)/Polanica Zdroj 1999/EXT 2000}) 11. Nxe4 e5 $1 12. dxe5 (12. Nxf6+ Qxf6 13. dxe5 dxe5 {f}) 12... Nxe4 13. Qxe4 dxe5 {[%cal Yc8f5] As compensation for the isolated e-pawn, Black has the nice d4-square at his disposal. Besides, White's blockade on e4 does not have a very stable character. Things would be different with a white knight on this square, but this would weaken d4 even more...} (13... Bf5 {Klein,J-Casas,F/Santa Fe 1960/MCL} 14. Qd5+ $1 Be6 15. exd6 $1 $16) 14. g4 $5 {[%csl Rf4,Gf5] The only way to maintain stability in the centre. On the other hand, the weakness of the f4-square could tell in the long run.} (14. Bg5 Qf7 15. Be3 (15. g4 Nd4 {×f2}) 15... h6 (15... Bf5 $5 16. Qh4 h6 17. Bxh6 Bxh6 18. Qxh6 Be4 19. Re1 Bxf3 20. Bxf3 Qxf3 21. Qxg6+ Kh8 22. Re4 Qxf2+ 23. Kh1 Qf3+ $11) 16. Nd2 Nd4 17. g4 c6 $15 {Srebrnic,A (2226)-Muzychuk,A (2387)/Nova Gorica 2005/CBM 104 ext}) 14... Kh8 (14... Be6 15. h3 Rae8 16. Be3 Nd4 $6 (16... Kh8 {transposes to the main game.}) 17. Nxd4 (17. Nxe5 Ne2+ 18. Kh1 Qf6 {×b2, c4}) 17... exd4 18. Bxd4 Qf7 19. Bc5 Bxc4 20. Qxb7 $16 {Jussupow,A (2645)-Zysk,R (2440)/Germany 1993/GER-chT}) (14... Qe6 15. h3 Nd4 16. Ng5 $2 (16. Be3 $1 Ne2+ 17. Kh2 Nf4 {or 16...c6}) (16. Nxd4 $1 exd4 17. Rxd4 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 $44) 16... Qf6 $15 {Bidkova,V (2023)-Muzychuk,M (2167)/Lviv 2006}) 15. h3 Be6 16. Be3 Rae8 ({Since Black cannot easily start active kingside play, he could turn his attention towards the other wing:} 16... a5 17. Rd2 Qb4 18. Rc1 Rad8 19. Rcc2 Rxd2 20. Bxd2 Qd6 21. b3 Bg8 $11 {Haba,P (2461)-Jakubiec,A (2501)/Czechia 2001/EXT 2002}) 17. Rd2 (17. Rac1 Bc8 18. Re1 Qb4 19. b3 Qa3 20. Rc2 Bd7 21. Bc1 Qb4 22. Bd2 Qd6 23. c5 Qf6 24. Bg5 {½-½ Bokuchava,M (1950)-Muzychuk,A (2378)/Urgup 2004/EXT 2005}) 17... Bc8 18. Rad1 Qf6 19. c5 (19. b4 Nxb4 20. Bc5 Nc6 21. Bxf8 Rxf8 $44 {×d4}) 19... a5 20. b3 (20. a3 Qf7 21. b4 Qb3 $1 $36) 20... Qf7 21. Ng5 Qf6 22. Nf3 Nd8 $6 (22... Qf7 $11) (22... Be6 $5) 23. Bg5 $1 Qc6 24. Qe3 $36 {Black has problems of coordination now.} Ne6 25. Nxe5 Qxc5 26. Rd5 Qb4 27. Nd3 Qa3 28. Bh6 c6 ({Such a master of attack as Tal could not consider isolating his own queen from the vulnerable kingside for the mere sake of pawn grabbing with} 28... Qxa2 29. Bxg7+ Nxg7 30. Ne5 $44) 29. Re5 Qd6 30. Bxg7+ Nxg7 31. Re1 $14 Rxe5 32. Nxe5 Qf6 33. Qf3 Qxf3 34. Nxf3 Ne6 35. Re5 b6 36. Nd2 Nf4 37. Nc4 Nd3 38. Re3 Nxf2 39. Nxb6 Ba6 40. Rc3 Nd1 41. Rxc6 Bb7 42. Rc1 Bxg2 43. Kxg2 Ne3+ 44. Kg1 h5 45. gxh5 gxh5 46. Rc8 Rxc8 47. Nxc8 Nd5 48. Nd6 Nc3 49. a3 Ne2+ 50. Kg2 Nd4 51. b4 axb4 52. axb4 Kg7 53. b5 Kf6 54. b6 Ke6 55. b7 Nc6 56. Nc4 Kd5 57. Na5 Nb8 58. Kg3 Kc5 59. Kh4 Kb6 60. Nb3 Kxb7 61. Kxh5 Nc6 62. Kg6 Ne5+ $2 {Black defines the placement of the knight too soon. He should have waited with thischeck until the g4-square becomes available.} ({For instance} 62... Kc7 {(not the only move)} 63. h4 Ne5+ 64. Kf6 Ng4+ 65. Kg5 Ne5 $11) 63. Kf6 $18 Nd3 64. h4 Nf4 65. Nc5+ $2 ({Several moves win here, for instance} 65. Kf5 $18 Nh5 66. Kg5 Ng3 67. Nd2 Kc7 68. Kg4 {trapping the knight}) 65... Kc6 $8 $11 66. Nd3 Nh5+ 67. Kg6 Ng3 68. Nf2 Kd6 69. Nh1 Ne2 70. Kf6 Nf4 71. Ng3 Kd7 72. Kf7 Kd6 73. Ne2 Nxe2 74. h5 Nf4 1/2-1/2

