1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 d6 4. O-O Bd7 5. Re1 Nf6 6. c3 a6 7. Ba4 ({The heavily analysed gambit} 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 8. d4 Bxe4 9. Bg5 {was not to Tal's taste against the well prepared World Junior Champion.}) 7... c4 $1 8. d4 cxd3 9. Bg5 e6 10. Qxd3 Be7 11. Bxf6 $5 gxf6 12. Bxc6 Bxc6 13. c4 O-O 14. Nc3 Kh8 $1 {Black's bishops are temporarily under control but, with Black preparing to attack along the g file, the game is likely to open up at any moment.} 15. Rad1 Rg8 16. Qe3 Qf8 17. Nd4 Rc8 18. f4 Bd7 19. b3 Bd8 20. Nf3 b5 $5 21. Qa7 $1 Bc7 $1 22. Qxa6 bxc4 23. b4 Qg7 24. g3 d5 25. exd5 $6 {A brave and perhaps foolhardy decision which tempts Black into a near decisive attack.} ({White's 23rd move, which tried to keep the position closed, clearly foreshadowed} 25. e5 {with an unclear position.}) 25... Bxf4 26. Kf2 $1 f5 $2 {Black is looking for the knock-out blow when any retreat of the bishop would leave White's king exposed.} 27. gxf4 Qxc3 28. Qd6 $1 Ba4 29. Rd4 Rg7 30. dxe6 Bc6 {Black still believes he is attacking but Tal has a typical surprise in store.} 31. Ng5 $1 Rxg5 ({Clearly} 31... fxe6 32. Qe5 {is hopeless for Black but now doesn't 32.fxg5 f3+ mate for Black?}) 32. Qe5+ $1 {Oops! Black now loses his queen whereupon the rest is easy.} Rg7 33. Rd8+ Rxd8 34. Qxc3 f6 35. e7 Ra8 36. Qxf6 Be4 37. Rg1 Rxa2+ 38. Ke1 1-0

