1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. c3 Be7 10. Be3 O-O 11. Nbd2 Qd7 12. Nd4 $2 {Another example of Mihail Tal's romantic style. However, this time it did not work out...} Nxd2 13. Qxd2 Nxe5 14. f4 Nc4 15. Bxc4 dxc4 16. f5 Bd5 17. f6 {White is being consequent. Otherwise he is just a pawn down, without any compensation.} Bxf6 18. Rxf6 gxf6 19. Bg5 $146 (19. Bh6 Rfe8 20. Rf1 Re4 21. h3 Qd6 22. Nf5 Qe5 23. Ne3 Be6 24. Rf3 Kh8 25. Kf2 Rg8 26. a3 Rh4 {0-1 Enevoldsen,J-Unzicker,W/Oldenburg 1949 (26)}) 19... Qg4 $1 (19... fxg5 $4 20. Qxg5+ Kh8 21. Qf6+ Kg8 22. Nf5 {and white is on top.}) 20. Bxf6 c5 {Driving away the knight from it's active position.} 21. Nc2 Qg6 ({Probably more accurate is} 21... Be4) 22. Rf1 ({Objectively better was} 22. Be7 {still retaining some chances. For instanse:} Rfe8 $6 23. Qxd5 Qxc2 24. Rf1 Qg6 25. Rf3) 22... Be4 23. Ne3 Rae8 $19 24. h3 Re6 25. Ng4 h5 26. Ne5 Qg3 27. Qf2 {White is forced to exchange queens and go for the hopeless endgame, otherwise heavy material losses are inevitable.} Qxf2+ 28. Rxf2 Rfe8 29. Nd7 Bc6 30. Nxc5 Re1+ 31. Rf1 R8e2 32. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 33. Kf2 Rb1 34. b4 cxb3 35. axb3 a5 0-1

