{A classic game, which was the origin of the strong attacking concept connected with sacrificing the central pawn. Lev Polugaevsky crushed Mikhail Tal in brilliant style and almost without a single inaccuracy - it's certainly one of the best games in the whole history of chess!} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. d4 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 Nxc3 7. bxc3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 Nc6 12. O-O b6 13. Rad1 Bb7 14. Rfe1 Na5 15. Bd3 Rc8 16. d5 $1 {White has been trying many options here but this direct pawn push is certainly one of the best, if not the best.} ({Of course, keeping good control over the center, White can make some preparations first and still get the result in case of Black's inaccurate play. For example,} 16. h3 Rc7 $6 (16... Qd6 $5) (16... Qe7 $5) (16... h6 $5) 17. Qf4 Rd7 $6 18. d5 exd5 19. e5 g6 20. Ng5 Re7 21. Qh4 $36 {with a strong initiative, Luther - Aring, Ditzingen 2009.}) 16... exd5 ({In case of} 16... Nc4 $6 {White could have won a pawn by} 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. dxe6 Qxd2 19. exf7+ Rxf7 20. Rxd2 {with reasonable winning chances as} Rxe4 $2 {was impossible due to} 21. Rxe4 Bxe4 22. Ng5 Re7 23. Rd8+) (16... Qd6 $5 {is worth considering, but it cannot completely solve the problems. In the game Dokhoian - Webb, EU Cup 1989 White opted for} 17. dxe6 $5 ({the tempting} 17. e5 $2 {failed to} Qxd5 18. Qf4 (18. Bxh7+ $2 Kxh7 19. Qg5 Qc5 $1 (19... Qc6 $2 20. Rd4 $1 $40) 20. Qh5+ (20. Rd4 Bxf3 $19) 20... Kg8 21. Ng5 Qc2 $19) 18... Qc5 ({even} 18... h6 $5 19. Bh7+ Kxh7 20. Rxd5 Bxd5 {was quite good for Black}) 19. Ng5 h6 20. Nh7 Rfd8 21. Nf6+ Kh8 {and White cannot create any real threats}) ({but other options, such as} 17. Qg5 $5 {were playable for White}) 17... Qxe6 (17... fxe6 $5 {deserved attention}) 18. Nd4 Qe5 19. Nf5 g6 20. Nh6+ Kh8 21. Ng4 Qe6 22. Qf4 f6 23. Bb5 $1 {and White has obtained a clear advantage.}) (16... Qe7 {is also possible:} 17. Qf4 (17. dxe6 $5) 17... Rfd8 18. h4 h6 19. h5 Qf6 20. Qg4 {with the better chances for White, Sasikiran - Golod, Ajaccio 2006.}) ({Black could also pay more attention to} 16... Rc5 $5) ({but perhaps the most reliable way was} 16... h6 $5 17. Qf4 Rc5 18. Nd4 exd5 19. e5 Bc8 $1 {- see the game Ponomariov - Vallejo Pons/Donostia 2009.}) 17. e5 $1 {This strong attacking plan was only introduced in this game. White's army looks really powerful and Black's life is really not so easy although he keeps defensive resources.} Nc4 {A natural reply.} ({Black tried many possibilities. Let's briefly take a look at them:} 17... d4 18. Ng5 $5 h6 19. Nh7 Nc4 20. Qf4 Nb2 21. Nxf8 Nxd1 22. e6 $1 Rc7 {and here in the game Gulkov - Tkebuchava, Moscow 1994 White should have played} 23. Nh7 $1 ({the complications after} 23. Qf5 Kxf8 24. Qh7 Ne3 $1 25. e7+ $1 Kxe7 26. Qxg7 Rc6 27. fxe3 dxe3 28. Rxe3+ Re6 29. Rxe6+ Kxe6 30. Bc4+ Kd6 31. Qxf7 Kc5 {ended with a drawish endgame}) 23... fxe6 (23... Nc3 $2 24. e7 Rxe7 25. Rxe7 Qxe7 26. Qb8+) 24. Rxd1 Qe7 25. Re1 {and White should be winning}) (17... Qe7 18. Qf4 f5 19. Nd4 g6 20. h4 Nc6 21. Nb5 {with advantage, V.Bagirov - Zhuravliov, USSR 1974}) (17... h6 18. Qf4 Nc6 19. Qf5 g6 20. Qg4 Kg7 21. h4 (21. e6 f5 $14 {Pogorelov - Planas Ferret, Andorra 2003}) 21... h5 22. Qg3 Qe7 23. Ng5 Nb4 24. Bb1 (24. Bf5 $1 Rc4 25. Ne6+ $1 {was even more energetic}) 24... Rc4 25. e6 f6 26. Nf7 Rg4 27. Qe3 Rxg2+ 28. Kf1 Rxf7 29. exf7 Qxf7 30. a3 {and White eventually converted his material advantage, N. Popov - Rumiantsev, USSR 1978.}) 18. Qf4 Nb2 {The principled way - Black is in a hurry to get rid of White's dangerous bishop. However, it does not bring the desired result...} ({Here Black also tried many possibilities:} 18... f6 19. e6 Qe7 20. Nd4 g6 21. Qg3 Kh8 22. h4 $36 {with the initiative, Toth - Gasthofer, Baden 2000}) ({a provocative} 18... Rc6 $5 {could nevertheless be met by} 19. Nd4 $1 (19. Ng5 $6 h6 20. Bh7+ Kh8 21. Nxf7+ Kxh7 (21... Rxf7 22. Qxf7 Kxh7 23. Qxb7 $18 (23. e6 Nd6 24. Qd7 $18)) 22. Nxd8 Rxf4 23. Nxb7 (23. e6 $2 Rf8 24. Nxb7 Rc7 25. e7 Re8 $17) 23... Re4 $13) 19... Rc7 {This position arose in the game Spasov - Kostakiev, corr. 1999. White could have secured the better chances by} 20. e6 $1 ({in the game White went for} 20. Bxh7+ $2 Kxh7 21. Rd3 Bc8 $19) 20... Nb2 (20... fxe6 21. Nxe6 $1 Rxf4 22. Nxd8 Nb2 23. Rb1 Rf8 24. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 25. Ne6) 21. Rc1 $1 Rxc1 (21... Nxd3 $2 22. Qxc7 Qxc7 23. Rxc7 Nxe1 24. e7 Re8 25. Rxb7 $18) 22. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 23. Qxc1 Qf6 (23... Nd3 24. Qb1) 24. Qxb2 Re8 25. Qb1+ Kg8 26. exf7+ Kxf7 27. Rxe8 Kxe8 28. Qe1+ Kf7 29. Qe3 {with a stable advantage for White.}) ({in case of an immediate} 18... Rc7 {White would come up with the thematic} 19. Bxh7+ $1 (19. Ng5 $5 h6 20. Nh7 Nb2 $1 (20... Re8 $2 21. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 22. exf6 Re6 23. Qg3+ Kf8 24. Qg7+ Ke8 25. Rxe6+ $18) 21. Nf6+ (21. Nxf8 Nxd1 22. Nh7 Nc3 $1 (22... Nb2 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Qf5 g6 25. Qf4 Kg7 26. Bb5 $36) 23. Nf6+ Kf8 $1 24. e6 gxf6 25. Qxh6+ Ke7 26. exf7+ Ne4 27. Qh7 Qf8 28. f3 Kd8 29. fxe4 Rxf7 $13) 21... Kh8 22. Qf5 g6 23. Qf4 Kg7 24. Rd2 Nxd3 25. Rxd3 Rc4 { Qd8-e7}) 19... Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 21. h4 $1 Qc8 (21... Rh8 22. Rd3 $18) 22. h5+ $1 (22. Rd3 f6 23. h5+ Kh6 24. Ne6+ Kh7 25. Nxf8+ Qxf8 26. exf6 {was also promising for White in the game Flesch - L. Kovacs, Budapest 1969} Rf7 27. Qf5+ Kg8 28. Qe6 gxf6 29. Rg3+ Kh8 30. h6 Ne5 31. f4 Re7 32. Rg8+ Qxg8 33. Qxf6+ Kh7 34. Qxe7+ Nf7 35. Qxb7 Qf8 36. Re7 Kxh6 37. Rxf7 {1-0 Flesch, J-Kovacs, L/Budapest 1969}) 22... Kh6 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. e6 Rcxf7 25. exf7 {with a decisive advantage.}) ({A cautious} 18... h6 {is maybe the best defence - it is considered in the game Huzman - Parligras/Turin (ol) 2006.}) 19. Bxh7+ $1 {Striking just in time!} ({A faint-hearted} 19. Rd2 Nxd3 20. Rxd3 Rc4 21. Nd4 Qe7 {was okay for Black.}) 19... Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 (20... Kg8 $2 21. Qh4 Qxg5 (21... Re8 22. e6 $1 fxe6 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. Qh8+ Ke7 25. Qxg7+ Kd6 26. Nf7+ $18) 22. Qxg5 Nxd1 23. Rxd1 {was clearly insufficient for Black.}) 21. h4 $3 {This cool pawn advance is the only way to maintain the initiative. Now Black has to parry a mating threat h4-h5.} Rc4 $1 {The best and probably the only defence!} (21... Nxd1 $2 {failed to} 22. h5+ $1 Kxh5 (22... Kh6 23. Ne6+ $18 {D.Gurevich - Massana, New York 1985}) 23. g4+ Kh6 24. Qh2+ $1 {Becker - Homs, Ruhrgebiet 2004}) (21... Qd7 $2 {was met by} 22. e6 $1 fxe6 23. Qg4 Rf6 24. Nxe6+ Kh6 25. Re5 Qf7 (25... g6 26. Qg5+ $18) 26. Rh5+ $1 Qxh5 27. Qxg7# {Dimov - Tsolov, BUL-ch sf 1990}) ({In case of} 21... Qe7 $6 {White would have continued his attack by} 22. Re3 $1 ({in the game Dreev - Jussupow, Mainz 2003 White was successful with} 22. Rd2 {but it could be rather unclear if Black found the strongest} f5 $1 ({after} 22... Rc4 $2 {White would have got a decisive advantage by} 23. h5+ $1 Kh6 (23... Kxh5 $2 24. g4+ Kg6 25. Qf5+ Kh6 26. Qh7+ Kxg5 27. Qh5+ Kf4 28. Qf5#) 24. Nxf7+ Kh7 25. Qf5+ Kg8 26. e6 $18) 23. Rxb2 Rc4 24. Qh2 Rfc8 {and Black is very much alive}) ({another promising idea was} 22. Rd4 $5 Rh8 (22... Nc4 $2 23. Rd3 $18) 23. g4 Kh6 24. Nxf7+ Kh7 25. e6 $36) 22... Rc4 23. h5+ Kh6 24. Ne4+ Kh7 25. Qf5+ Kh6 (25... Kh8 $2 26. Ng5) (25... Kg8 $2 26. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 27. exf6 Qxe3 28. fxe3 $18) 26. Rg3 $1 Rxe4 (26... Bc8 27. Rg6+ $1 Kh7 28. Ng5+ $1 Qxg5 29. Qxg5 fxg6 30. hxg6+ Kg8 31. Qh5 $18) 27. Rg6+ $1 Kh7 28. Re6+ Kg8 29. Rxe7 Bc8 $1 30. Qg5 Nxd1 31. h6 Rg4 32. Qd2 Nxf2 33. Kxf2 $16 {with good winning chances.}) ({other possible defences failed as well:} 21... f5 $2 22. Rd4 $1 {(with the idea h4-h5!)} Qe7 (22... Rh8 $2 23. g4 $1 $18) 23. Re3 $18) ({or} 21... f6 $4 22. h5+ $1 Kxh5 23. g4+ Kg6 24. Qf5+ Kh6 25. Nf7+ $1 Rxf7 26. Qh5#) 22. h5+ ({Perhaps} 22. Rd4 $5 {was a more reliable way to secure a clear advantage:} Rxd4 (22... Qe7 23. Re3 { Re3-g3}) 23. Qxd4 Bc8 (23... Qd7 24. e6 $1 fxe6 25. Nxe6 Rf5 26. Qg4+ Kf6 27. Nf8 $1 $18 {Kunath - Ruppe, corr. 1983}) 24. Qxb2 {and Black's life is difficult. However, the text move is also promising.}) 22... Kh6 ({In the game Avrukh - Donk, Antwerp 1998, Black got mated after} 22... Kxh5 $2 23. g4+ $1 Kh6 (23... Kg6 24. Qf5+ Kh6 25. Nxf7+ Rxf7 26. Qh5#) 24. Qh2+ {and he resigned, not waiting for} Kxg5 25. Qh5+ Kf4 26. Qf5#) 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. Qf5+ Kg8 25. e6 $1 (25. h6 $2 {failed to} Rxf7 26. h7+ Kh8 27. Qxf7 Nxd1 (27... Rc7 $5 {was also good:} 28. e6 $5 Rxf7 29. exf7 Kxh7 30. Re8 (30. Rc1 d4 $1 31. Re8 Qd5) 30... Qf6 31. f8=Q Qxf8 32. Rxf8 Nxd1 33. Rf7 Bc6 34. Rxa7 {with some drawish chances}) 28. e6 Bc6 {and Black parries all the threats:} 29. Rxd1 Rc3 $5 30. Rd4 Rc1+ 31. Kh2 Rc4 {and so on.}) 25... Qf6 $1 {The only move.} ({In other cases the pawn push h5-h6 was decisive, for example:} 25... Qe7 $2 26. h6 Rh4 27. Rd4 $1 $18) 26. Qxf6 gxf6 27. Rd2 $1 {This move has been evaluated as "dubious" in various comments but in fact it's simply the best.} ({On the other hand,} 27. Nd6 $6 {, which was supposed to be winning, can be easily met by} Nxd1 ({surely not} 27... Rc6 $2 28. Rxd5 $18 Rc7 29. Nxb7 Rxb7 30. Rd7 $1 Rbb8 31. Re3 $18) 28. e7 Rc1 $1 29. h6 Rb8 (29... Rfc8 $5 {deserves attention:} 30. Nxc8 Bc6 $1 31. Nd6 Kh7 32. e8=Q Bxe8 33. Nxe8 Kxh6 34. Nxf6 d4 $132 {with good counter chances}) 30. Nxb7 Re8 31. Nd6 Rxe7 32. Rxe7 Ne3+ 33. Kh2 Ng4+ 34. Kg3 Nxh6 35. Rxa7 $14 {with only a slight edge for White in the game Naumkin - Nevanlinna, Jyvaskyla 1993.}) 27... Rc6 ({Polugaevsky gave} 27... Na4 $5 {as the most stubborn continuation. White would have secured winning chances by} 28. Nd6 $1 (28. e7 $6 Kxf7 29. exf8=Q+ Kxf8 30. Rde2 Re4 $1) 28... Bc6 29. e7 Re8 30. Nxe8 (30. Re3 $5) (30. Nxc4 dxc4 31. Rd6 $16) 30... Bxe8 31. Rxd5 {but Black was still fighting after} Kf7) 28. Rxb2 Re8 ({In case of} 28... Bc8 {White had a pleasant choice:} 29. Nh6+ $5 (29. e7 Re8 30. Nd8 Rc7 31. Rd2 Rcxe7 32. Rxe7 Rxe7 33. Rxd5 $16) 29... Kh7 30. Nf5 Rxe6 31. Rc1 $16 {and so on.}) (28... Kg7 {was losing to} 29. e7 $18 {followed by Nf7-d8.}) 29. Nh6+ $1 {Shifting the knight to the dominating position on f5.} ({The pawn on e6 could not be saved anyway:} 29. Rbe2 $6 Bc8) 29... Kh7 30. Nf5 Rexe6 31. Rxe6 Rxe6 {Black has restored the material balance but it won't make his life easier.} 32. Rc2 $1 Rc6 ({After} 32... Bc6 {White would simply improve by} 33. g4 a5 34. f3 {etc.}) 33. Re2 {Securing a decisive invasion. White's powerful rook on the 7th rank, combined with no less a powerful knight on f6 and strong h5-passer give Black no chance to survive.} Bc8 ({Or} 33... Rc7 34. Re6 Bc8 35. Rxf6 Bxf5 36. Rxf5 $18) 34. Re7+ Kh8 35. Nh4 {With a simple idea to give checkmate by 36. Ng6 Kg8 37.h6 and 38.Rg7X} f5 36. Ng6+ Kg8 37. Rxa7 {Black resigned. Wonderful play by Lev Polugaevsky!} 1-0

