1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 d6 4. O-O Bd7 5. Re1 Nf6 6. c3 a6 7. Ba4 c4 {A straightforward way to prevent White building a full centre with d4.} 8. d4 {The most direct.} ({We've looked at} 8. Bc2 {several times in the Archives.}) 8... cxd3 9. Bg5 e6 10. Qxd3 Be7 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Bxc6 Bxc6 13. c4 {In his comments to this game on chess24, Peter Svidler identifies the most famous game in this structure as V.Ivanchuk - G.Kasparov, Linares 1991 (1-0 in 38). This also arose from an Anti-Sicilian: 1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 d6 3.Bb5+ Nd7 4.d4 Ngf6 5. 0-0 cxd4 6.Qxd4 a6 7.Bxd7+ Bxd7 8.Bg5 h6 9.Bxf6 gxf6 10.c4 e6. Interested readers should definitely check out this game and, in particular, the final position, which shows a lack of co-ordination amongst Kasparov's pieces which no other player ever achieved.} O-O (13... b5 14. Nc3 bxc4 15. Qxc4 Qb6 {L.Bronstein - L.Liascovich, Buenos Aires 2006 (1/2-1/2 in 18) allows the thematic} 16. Nd5 $1 exd5 17. exd5 Bd7 18. Qe4 $44 {with more than enough for the piece.}) 14. Nc3 Kh8 15. Rad1 (15. Nd4 Bd7 16. Rad1 Rg8 17. Kh1 Rc8 18. Re3 Qc7 19. b3 Qc5 20. a4 Rg6 21. Rg3 {was agreed drawn in D.Popovic - B.Vuckovic, Vrnjacka Banja 2006.}) 15... Rg8 16. Qe3 Qf8 (16... Bd7 17. a4 Rc8 18. b3 Qa5 {(S.Vratonjic - M.Perunovic, Kragujevac 2012 (1/2-1/2 in 36)) and now after something like} 19. Qd4 $14 {White has a slightly easier game.}) 17. Nd4 Rc8 18. f4 $6 ({Svidler gives the instructive suggestion} 18. Nxc6 Rxc6 19. b3 Bd8 20. Qf3 Qg7 21. g3 Bb6 22. Rd3 {followed by doubling on the d-file, bringing the king to g2 and aiming for h5 with the knight via e2 and f4.}) 18... Bd7 19. b3 Bd8 20. Nf3 b5 21. Qa7 Bc7 22. Qxa6 bxc4 23. b4 Qg7 24. g3 d5 $1 25. exd5 Bxf4 26. Kf2 $17 f5 $2 {As Ian Rogers says in his notes to this game, any retreat of the bishop would leave the white king exposed, which Svidler confirms in his analysis of 26...Be5.} 27. gxf4 Qxc3 28. Qd6 $1 Ba4 29. Rd4 $1 Rg7 $2 ({Kasparov gives} 29... Qb2+ 30. Rd2 Qf6) 30. dxe6 Bc6 31. Ng5 $1 (31. Re3 $2 Rg2+ $1 {(Kasparov)}) 31... Rxg5 ({Kasparov's analysis suggests that even after the better} 31... fxe6 {there is no defence after 32.Rxe6.}) 32. Qe5+ $1 {Retaining his tactical eye until the very end.} ({Akopian must have been counting on.} 32. fxg5 Qf3+ 33. Kg1 Qg2#) 32... Rg7 33. Rd8+ Rxd8 34. Qxc3 f6 35. e7 Ra8 36. Qxf6 Be4 37. Rg1 Rxa2+ 38. Ke1 1-0

