{Here only White has to solve problems in the opening with careful play. Luckily for him Black decided to sacrifice a piece for an attack which happened to be illusory.} 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 c6 4. f4 Qa5 5. Bd2 e5 $1 {The best reaction.} ({The alternative} 5... Qb6 {is weaker because of} 6. Nf3 Bg4 7. Na4 $1 ({in the case of} 7. e5 Nd5 8. exd6 Nxc3 9. Bxc3 e6 10. h3 Bf5 $5 ({not} 10... Bxf3 $6 11. Qxf3 Bxd6 12. f5 $1 O-O 13. O-O-O {with a clear advantage, Liberzon-Hodgson, Palma de Mallorca 1989}) 11. g4 Be4 12. Bd3 Bd5 {Black is more or less OK. The position is similar to some variations of the Centre Counter. The Bishop on d5 is in danger, but it's not easy to trap it! The game O'Connor - Hodgson, 1992 continued with} 13. Rf1 Bxd6 14. Ne5 Qc7 15. Qd2 b5 16. Ba5 Qe7 17. g5 f6 $5 {and after} 18. Bg6+ hxg6 19. Nxg6 Qf7 20. Nxh8 Qh5 {his chances already looked preferable}) 7... Bxf3 8. gxf3 $1 Qc7 ({Of course, the pawn is taboo:} 8... Qxd4 $4 9. c3 {and the Queen is trapped in the centre of the board!}) 9. f5 $1 g6 10. Bh3 Nbd7 11. Qe2 {Practice proves White's considerable central advantage secures him the better chances:} Bg7 (11... b5 12. Nc3 Bg7 13. f4 b4 14. Nd1 a5 15. e5 $36 {Rybak - J.Pribyl, Karvina 1989}) 12. c4 c5 13. d5 a6 14. Nc3 O-O 15. O-O {with good prospects for a K-side attack, Kudrin-Terrie, Los Angeles 1991.}) 6. Nf3 ({Here are some alternatives:} 6. dxe5 dxe5 7. fxe5 (7. f5 $2 {is weak in view of} Qb4 $1 8. a3 Qxb2 9. Nf3 Qb6 10. Nxe5 Nbd7 11. Nc4 Qd4 12. Bd3 Bc5 13. Qf3 Ne5 14. Nxe5 {Here in Bareev-Mokry ,Novi Sad 1990 draw was agreed although in my opinion after} Qxe5 {Black is clearly better.}) 7... Ng4 8. Be2 Nxe5 9. Nf3 Nxf3+ 10. gxf3 Qd8 11. Be3 Qxd1+ 12. Rxd1 Nd7 13. Rg1 g6 14. Kf2 Bc5 15. f4 Bxe3+ 16. Kxe3 Ke7 17. Rd2 Nb6 18. Rgd1 Be6 $10 {and Black has almost equalised, Dolezal - M.Pribyl, Praha CS-ch 1992}) (6. Nd5 {does not promise too much:} Qd8 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 8. dxe5 (8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Nf3 Bg4 $5 (9... exd4 $6 10. Bc4 $36) 10. dxe5 Qg6 11. Bd3 Nd7 $14 {-6.Nf3}) 8... dxe5 9. Nf3 exf4 10. e5 Qh6 (10... Qg6 {seems to be weaker due to} 11. Bd3 $1 Qxg2 12. Rg1 Qh3 13. Bxf4 Bg4 14. Rg3 Qh5 15. Be2 $36 {with the initiative, Burovic - Kramnik, Leningrad 1991}) 11. g3 Bg4 12. Bxf4 Qg6 13. Bg2 Qe4+ 14. Qe2 Qxe2+ 15. Kxe2 Na6 16. Rad1 Nc7 17. h3 Bxf3+ 18. Bxf3 Ne6 19. Bg4 Be7 20. Rd2 h5 21. Bxe6 fxe6 22. Rhd1 Rd8 {with equality, Brunner - Mokry, Graz 1991.}) 6... exd4 $5 ({Here} 6... Bg4 {is also unsuccessful:} 7. fxe5 dxe5 8. Nd5 Qd8 9. Nxf6+ Qxf6 10. dxe5 Qg6 11. Bd3 Nd7 12. Bc3 Bc5 13. Qd2 O-O 14. O-O-O Rae8 15. Kb1 $1 Bxf3 16. gxf3 Nxe5 17. f4 Nxd3 18. f5 $1 Qg4 19. cxd3 $16 {and White is clearly better, Ftacnik-Mokry, 1987.}) 7. Nxd4 Qb6 8. Nb3 a5 $1 {A good idea. Black tries either to push back the Knight or to provoke a weakening a2-a4.} 9. Rb1 (9. a4 $6 {is much weaker:} Na6 10. Be2 Nb4 11. Bf3 Be6 {(threatening 12...Nxc2)} 12. Nc1 d5 13. Nd3 (13. e5 Bf5 14. exf6 Nxc2+ 15. Kf1 Nxa1) 13... dxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxd3+ 15. cxd3 Nxe4 16. dxe4 O-O-O $1 $19 {and Black has an overwhelming position, Belaska - M.Pribyl, Morava 1991}) ({In the case of} 9. Qe2 a4 10. Be3 {Black has an interesting option:} Qb4 11. a3 axb3 $5 12. axb4 Rxa1+ 13. Kd2 Bg4 14. Qc4 bxc2 15. Kxc2 d5 $5 {and Black has taken the initiative:} 16. exd5 Na6 17. Bc5 Bxc5 18. bxc5 O-O 19. d6 Nd5 $13 {with sharp play, Alvarez Ibarra - Rivas Pastor, Albacete 1989.}) 9... a4 10. Nc1 g6 (10... Bg4 {does not solve the problems:} 11. Be2 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 Be7 13. Be3 Qa6 14. Nd3 $14 {with a definite advantage.}) 11. Qf3 Bg7 12. Bd3 O-O 13. N1e2 (13. Be3 Qa5 14. O-O Nbd7 $13 {was good for Black.}) 13... Bg4 14. Qg3 ({The endgame after} 14. Qf2 Qxf2+ 15. Kxf2 Nbd7 {was roughly equal.}) 14... Bxe2 15. Nxe2 Nbd7 {It is clear that Black's opening strategy has been successful - he has comfortably developed all his pieces while White still has some problems castling.} 16. Qf3 $8 ({The careless} 16. Be3 $2 {would have been immediately punished by} Nxe4 $1 17. Bxe4 (17. Bxb6 Nxg3 18. hxg3 Nxb6 $19) 17... Qb4+ $19 {followed by ...Qe4.}) 16... Nc5 17. Be3 Nfxe4 $2 {An interesting-looking sacrifice but as it turns out White soon has enough resources to extinguish Black's initiative.} ({By playing} 17... Qb4+ 18. Bd2 $8 (18. Nc3 $2 a3 $17) 18... Qb6 {Black could have forced a draw.}) 18. Qxe4 ({Of course not} 18. Bxe4 $2 Qb4+ $19) 18... Rfe8 19. Bxc5 dxc5 ({After} 19... Qxc5 20. Qc4 Qe3 21. Qb4 $1 $16 {White's chances were much better but it was much more stubborn.} (21. Rf1 $6 d5 22. Qb4 c5 23. Qb5 a3 $5 $36)) 20. Qc4 $8 (20. Qf3 $2 c4 $17) 20... Qa7 {Intending to play ...b7-b5 followed by ...c5-c4.} 21. b4 $6 {Not the best decision.} (21. f5 $1 {was the more precise way:} b5 22. Qf4 c4 23. Be4 Qe7 24. Bf3 {and so on.}) 21... axb3 22. Qxb3 b5 23. c4 Qa5+ 24. Kf1 $6 (24. Kf2 $1 $16 {should have been played.}) 24... Rad8 $2 {This centralising move loosens the initiative.} (24... Qxa2 $1 {was correct and it looks like it's already White who has to be careful:} 25. Qxa2 Rxa2 26. cxb5 Rd2 27. Bc4 (27. bxc6 Rxd3 28. c7 Rd2) 27... Rc2 {and here it looks reasonable to repeat the moves by} 28. Bd3 ({the attempt} 28. bxc6 $2 Rxc4 29. c7 {fails to} Rc2 $1 {and} 30. Rb8 $2 {is losing:} Rcxe2 31. c8=Q Re1+ 32. Kf2 Bd4+ 33. Kg3 R1e3+ 34. Kg4 (34. Kf2 Rd3+ 35. Kf1 Rd1#) 34... h5+ 35. Kg5 Kg7 $1 36. Qc6 Rxb8 $19 {and there is no defence against ...Re6 followed by ...Bf6 checkmate.}) 28... Rd2 29. Bc4) 25. Rd1 Rb8 (25... Re3 26. Kf2 $16) 26. Qc2 bxc4 27. Bxc4 Rb2 28. Qc1 {Everything is protected, now Black cannot prevent h4 and Rh3 bringing the last piece into play.} Qb4 29. h4 Re4 30. a3 $1 {An excellent defensive resource!} Qb8 (30... Qxc4 $2 {was just a blunder:} 31. Rd8+ Bf8 32. Qxb2 $18) ({while} 30... Qxa3 31. Rh3 Qb4 32. Bd3 Re8 33. h5 $18 {left Black with no hopes of surviving.}) 31. Rh3 Qe8 32. Nc3 $1 {Well-calculated!} Rxc4 (32... Bxc3 33. Qxc3 Ree2 {did not work due to} (33... Rxf4+ 34. Rf3 $18) 34. Rg3 Rec2 35. Qd3 $18) 33. Qxb2 Rxf4+ 34. Rf3 (34. Kg1 $5 {was also not bad:} Bd4+ (34... Rd4 35. Rb1 $5) 35. Kh1 Rf2 36. Qc1 Qe6 37. Rf1 $18 {keeping an extra rook.}) 34... Rxh4 35. Qe2 Rh1+ 36. Kf2 Bd4+ $8 37. Rxd4 Qxe2+ 38. Nxe2 cxd4 39. Nxd4 $18 {The position is winning for White though Black can organise some counterplay advancing his kingside pawns.} c5 40. Nc6 Ra1 41. Rc3 Ra2+ 42. Kf3 f5 43. Na7 Kg7 44. Nb5 h5 45. g3 g5 46. Rxc5 Kf6 47. Rc3 g4+ 48. Kf4 Rf2+ 49. Ke3 Rg2 (49... Rf3+ 50. Kd4 Rxc3 51. Nxc3 f4 52. gxf4 Kf5 53. Ke3 $18) 50. Kd4 f4 51. Rc6+ Kg5 52. Rc5+ Kh6 53. gxf4 h4 {The pawns lo ok dangerous but White finds a competent way of realising his extra piece using the awkward position of Black's King.} 54. Ke5 $1 h3 55. Nd6 h2 56. Nf5+ Kg6 57. Rc6+ Kf7 58. Rc7+ Kg8 (58... Kg6 59. Rg7+ Kh5 60. Rg5#) (58... Ke8 59. Rh7 $18) 59. Nh6+ $1 Kf8 $8 ({In the case of} 59... Kh8 {White could set a mating net by} 60. Kf6 $3 h1=Q 61. Nf7+ Kh7 (61... Kg8 62. Rc8+ Kh7 63. Rh8#) 62. Ng5+ $18 {with checkmate on the next move.}) 60. Kf6 Ke8 61. Nf5 $1 Kd8 {Once again Black has no time for the pawn's promotion! Here the time trouble was over and he resigned because White can simply stop the h-pawn by Rh7.} (61... h1=Q 62. Ng7+ Kd8 (62... Kf8 63. Rc8#) 63. Ne6+ Ke8 64. Re7#) (61... Kd8 62. Rh7 $18) 1-0

