1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Nbd7 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O b5 10. Bxb5 axb5 11. Ndxb5 Qb8 12. e5 Bb7 13. Qe2 dxe5 14. Qc4 Bc5 $6 {This looks attractive, but loses by force!} (14... Be7 $1 {is analysed in Naiditsch, A-Gelfand, B/Dortmund GER 2006.}) 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. Rxd7 $1 {Dashing chess!} (16. Kb1 {is a bit slow,} Qc8 17. f5 Nb6 18. Qh4 Ke7 {and Black has consolidated, Atakisi, U-Soylu, S/Istanbul 2001.}) 16... Be3+ 17. Kb1 (17. Rd2 $2 O-O $1 18. Qe2 exf4 19. Kb1 Bxd2 20. Qxd2 Rd8 {Black has a solid extra exchange, Clerides, P-Penttinen, J/Limassol 2000.}) 17... Kxd7 18. Rd1+ Bd4 $6 {A natural reaction, but it loses.} (18... Ke8 19. Nc7+ Kf8 20. fxe5 {also gives White a strong attack, Vitolins-Anikayev/Riga 1973}) (18... Ke7 $5 19. Qb4+ Ke8 20. Nd6+ Kf8 21. Nf5+ Kg8 22. Nxe3 exf4 23. Ng4 {looks dangerous for Black, to say the least.}) 19. fxe5 fxe5 (19... Qxe5 20. Nxd4 Ke8 21. Qb4 $1 Bd5 {might avoid what follows, but White has an attack plus a pawn for the exchange, and must be better.}) 20. Nxd4 exd4 21. Qxd4+ Ke7 (21... Bd5 {is no help:} 22. Nxd5 exd5 23. Qxd5+ Ke8 24. Re1+ Kf8 25. Qc5+ Kg8 26. Qg5+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Kg8 28. Re3 {and wins.}) 22. Qc5+ $1 (22. Qd7+ Kf8 23. Rf1 Qe8 24. Qxb7 {is also quite good, Krupenski, Y-Kanep, M/Tallinn 2000, but the text is much more incisive.}) (22. Qb4+ $1 {is the same:} Kf6 23. Qh4+ $6 (23. Rf1+ $1 Kg7 24. Qe7 {transposes to the main game}) 23... Kg7 $4 (23... Kg6 {might defend}) 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Rd7 Qe8 26. Qh6+ Kg8 27. Rd3 Qb8 28. Rd4 {Li Shilong-Becker, M/Amsterdam NED 2006.}) 22... Kf6 (22... Ke8 23. Nb5 f5 24. Nd6+ Ke7 25. Nc8+ $1 Kf7 26. Qe7+ {wins.}) 23. Rf1+ (23. Qd4+ $2 Kg6 24. Qg4+ Kf6 25. Rf1+ Ke7 {gives no more than a perpetual, Lacroix, S-Libersan, T/ Montreal 2005.}) 23... Kg6 24. Qe7 $1 f5 $2 (24... Qe5 {puts up more resistance:} 25. Qxf7+ Kg5 26. h4+ Kxh4 27. g3+ Kg5 28. g4 $1 h5 29. Qe7+ Kg6 30. gxh5+ Kh6 31. Qxb7 {but Black is hardly likely to survive long here.}) 25. Qxe6+ Kg7 26. Qe7+ Kg6 27. h4 $1 (27. g4 {also wins,} Rf8 $2 (27... f4 $1) 28. gxf5+ Kh5 {Dimitrov, V-Dichev, N/Plovdiv 1988, when} 29. Qxh7+ Kg4 30. Qg7+ Kh3 31. Nd1 {is the quickest.}) 27... Ra5 (27... Rf8 28. g4 $1 (28. Qg5+ Kf7 29. Rxf5+ Ke6) 28... Qg3 29. gxf5+ Kh6 30. Ne2 Qg4 31. Rd1 {and there is little to do against the threat of Rd6+.}) 28. h5+ {Forcing mate in 7.} Kxh5 29. Qf7+ Kh4 30. Qf6+ Kg3 31. Qg5+ Kh2 32. Qh4+ Kxg2 33. Rf2+ Kg1 34. Ne2# 1-0

