{Gormally,Daniel: '*Strokes gained putting/Tactical and combinational players* Drive for show, putt for dough, is an oft quoted maxim in golf. The player who can combine both attributes well is bound to have a great deal of success, but it tends to be the case that those who are fantastic tee to green, often shrivel up when faced with the complexity of the greens. Quite why that is, remains a mystery. It may well be that they put too much emphasis on ball-striking and fail to put sufficient work into their putting; or just that the part of their brain which makes them good drivers, doesn't work so well in that other part of the game. The same works in reverse of course and good putters are often lousy off the tee. For longevity purposes, it seems to be better to be a good ball striker, because an excellent run of putting can be difficult to maintain. All these truths can easily be applied to chess. Good strategic players often struggle with tactics. I recall once teaching a junior who was fantastic at positional chess and strategy but would make tactical oversights regularly. Eventually he drifted away from chess, whether this inability to overcome this weakness played a part, I'll never know. My top three tactics and combination players are as follows: 1. Garry Kasparov 2. Mihail Tal 3. Paul Morphy You could easily fill several volumes with examples of Kasparov's pyrotechnics. It would not be an exaggeration to say that he arguably has a larger lead in this category then any player has in any other, save for perhaps Magnus in endgames. Another player who was quite useful at tactics was Mihail Tal. In his prime he was a true chess "killer," so much so that his charisma and will to win provoked fear in the opposition. How else can you explain another player coming to the board in dark shades, to try and counteract the penetrating nature of Tal's stare? Reading through Douglas Griffin's Soviet chess blog gives an excellent overview of the great champion, particularly in his later years. What shines through is how much wisdom Tal displays in his annotations. Clearly he learned the lessons from early in his career, was aware that such a high-energy approach was difficult to sustain, and modified his approach. But despite all of this the hungry bear was still there, lurking beneath the surface.'} 1. e4 {Gormally,Daniel Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e6 7. O-O Be7 8. f4 O-O 9. Kh1 Qc7 10. a4 b6 $6 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Slightly uncommon. Ftacnik,Lubomir: '?!''} (10... Nc6 {(Gormally,Daniel) is of course more usual.}) 11. e5 $1 $146 {Gormally,Daniel: 'This was a novelty when played at the time, and now there are many games. The world champions show the way for the rest of us! Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'N!''} (11. Bf3 {Ü 38/(325) Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: '- 38/(325)'' Ü 38/(325)} Bb7 {Gormally,Daniel} 12. e5 {is still the mainline.}) 11... Ne8 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik doesn't respond to the novelty very well, because now this could be considered slightly inferior.'} (11... dxe5 $142 {Gormally,Daniel: '!' Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: '''} 12. fxe5 Nfd7 13. Bf4 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} (13. Bf3 {Gormally,Daniel} Bb7 {seems to be fine for Black.}) 13... Bb7 14. Bf3 Nc6 $14) 12. exd6 Bxd6 {Gormally,Daniel: '?' Gormally,Daniel: 'Diagram [#]'} (12... Nxd6 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 13. f5 $1 exf5 (13... Nxf5 14. Nxf5 exf5 15. Nd5 Qd8 16. Bf4 Bd6 17. Nxb6 $1 $16) (13... e5 14. Nd5 Qd8 15. Bf3 Bb7 16. f6 $1 $40) 14. Nd5 Qd8 15. Bf4 Bb7 16. Bf3 $1) (12... Qxd6 {(Gormally,Daniel) was the only way to keep the game within manageable limits.} 13. Be3 Bb7 14. Bf3 Qc7 $16) 13. f5 $1 {Gormally,Daniel: '+-!' Gormally,Daniel: 'Tal gave this !? but perhaps this is his typical modesty, because we can now see with engine analysis that this is just winning for White. Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!''} e5 (13... exf5 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 14. Nd5 Qd8 15. Nxf5 Bxf5 16. Rxf5 Qh4 17. Bf4 $16 { }) (13... Qe7 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. Rxf8+ Qxf8 16. Bc4 $16) (13... Bxh2 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 14. Bf3 Ra7 15. Ndb5 axb5 16. Nxb5 Qc4 $8 {(Tal,M) Gormally,Daniel: '(Tal,Mihail)'} (16... Qg3 17. Nxa7 Qh4 18. Bg5 $1 $18) 17. Nxa7 Bg3 18. Bg5 $1 (18. Bg4 exf5 19. Bh3 Be6 $44) 18... Ba6 19. Nb5 Bxb5 20. axb5 f6 21. b3 Qxb5 22. Be3 $16) 14. Nd5 Qd8 (14... Qb7 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 15. Bf3 exd4 16. f6 $1) 15. f6 $1 {Gormally,Daniel: 'The old instincts do not desert him. This feels very natural, doesn't it? And yet many players would find it hard to commit to this, because a piece is a piece after all. If we lack the ability to sacrifice or speculate then that will always hold us back, at least to a degree.'} (15. Nb3 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} Bb7 (15... Nd7 16. Bf3 Rb8) 16. Be3 Nd7 $13) 15... exd4 (15... Nxf6 16. Nxf6+ gxf6 17. Nf5 {Gormally,Daniel: 'promised White "a brilliant attack" according to Tal.'} Bxf5 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 18. Rxf5 Nc6 19. Bd3 Ne7 20. Qg4+ Ng6 21. h4 $18 { }) 16. Qxd4 {Gormally,Daniel: '?'} (16. fxg7 Kxg7 $1 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!''} (16... Nxg7 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 17. Nf6+ Kh8 18. Qd3 Bf5 19. Rxf5 $18) 17. Qxd4+ f6 18. Qh4 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} (18. Bd3 {Gormally,Daniel} Kh8 {and Tal in his calculations felt that Black could then play ...Ra7 and repulse the attack, but the computer points out the very powerful} 19. Bg5 $1 {when the attack remains overwhelming.} Nd7 (19... Ra7 20. Nxf6 Nxf6 21. Bxf6+ $18 {is also decisive.}) (19... Nc6 20. Qh4 Ra7 21. Nxf6 Nxf6 22. Rxf6 $18) 20. Rae1 Bb7 21. Nxf6 Nexf6 22. Qxd6 $18) 18... Kh8 19. Bd3 Ra7 $13) 16... Nc6 (16... Bc5 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 17. Qe4 Nc6 $8 (17... Bb7 18. Ne7+ Qxe7 19. Qxe7 $18) (17... Ra7 18. fxg7 Kxg7 19. Qe5+ f6 20. Qxb8 $18) 18. Bg5 (18. fxg7 Kxg7 19. b4 Bd4 20. Ra3 Kh8 21. Rg3 Rg8 $13) (18. Bh6 $5 Be6 $1) 18... Ra7 $8 (18... Be6 19. Ne7+ Nxe7 20. fxe7 $18) 19. Rad1 Bd6 (19... Rd7 20. b4 $1 Bd6 21. Bg4 $18) 20. Qh4 g6 21. Ne7+ Nxe7 22. fxe7 Rxe7 23. Rxd6 Qxd6 24. Bxe7 $18) ({In a position that was tough to defend,} 16... Nd7 $1 {(Gormally,Daniel) was Black's best shot.} 17. Qh4 Ndxf6 18. Bd3 g6 19. Rxf6 Be6 20. Bg5 Qb8 21. Nc3 Nxf6 22. Bxf6 Be5 23. Ne4 Bxf6 24. Nxf6+ Kg7 25. Rf1 Rh8 26. Qd4 {when the White initiative on the dark squares will be hard to dispell.}) 17. Qh4 $1 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Diagram [#] Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!''} Bg3 $5 {Gormally,Daniel: '?!' Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!?''} (17... Ra7 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 18. Bg5 g6 19. Rad1 Ne5 20. Ne7+ Kh8 21. Rf4 { Qh7!+-  h7!+-}) (17... gxf6 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 18. Rxf6 $1 Bf5 19. Bg5 Bxc2 (19... Qd7 20. Rxf5 $18) 20. Rc1 $1 Be5 (20... Be7 21. Nxe7+ Nxe7 22. Rxc2 Nxf6 23. Bxf6 $18) 21. Rxc6 Qxd5 22. R6xc2 $16) (17... Be6 18. Bg5 $1 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!''} Bxd5 19. fxg7 Nxg7 $5 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!?''} (19... f6 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 20. Bd3 $1 $18) (19... Qc7 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} 20. Bd3 f5 21. gxf8=Q+ Bxf8 22. Bxf5 { Bh7+-  h7+-}) 20. Bd3 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} (20. Bxd8 {Gormally,Daniel} Raxd8 {lead to a position that Tal could not easily assess, but the engine thinks that White is completely winning.}) 20... f5 21. Bxd8 Raxd8 22. Bc4 $16) (17... Nxf6 {Gormally,Daniel} 18. Nxf6+ ({Tal felt that} 18. Rxf6 {was very strong, but Stockfish comes up with a remarkable resource:} Be6 $1 19. Bd3 g6 20. Nc3 $14) (18. Bd3 Ne4 19. Qxe4 f5 20. Qf3 {is better for White, but not as convincing.}) 18... gxf6 19. Rxf6 Ne5 20. Bf4 {White has too many threats, it would seem.}) 18. Qxg3 (18. Ne7+ $2 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} Nxe7 19. fxe7 Bxh4 $19) (18. Qg5 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} Bh4 19. Qxh4 Qxd5) (18. hxg3 {Gormally,Daniel: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir'} Qxd5 19. Bf3 Qc5 20. Be3 $5) 18... Qxd5 