{The following game, doubtlessly known by Carlsen, is absolute classic related to our theme. It may actually be the first one featuring this type of scenario.} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. d4 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 (6. e3) 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ (9... Qa5 10. Rb1 $1 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 $16) 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 Nc6 12. O-O b6 13. Rad1 $1 Bb7 14. Rfe1 {Diagram [#]} Na5 {This was recommended by Tal in his comments to a game from the Petrosian - Spassky match, earlier that year. ***Polugaevsky was aware of Tal's suggestion and spent several hours analysing the position before the game. His results were stunning.} ({The aforementioned game went} 14... Rc8 15. d5 exd5 ({I assume that both Petrosian during the game and Tal when commenting, thought that} 15... Na5 {was dangerous in view of} 16. dxe6 {but this sems to lead to a draw:} Qxd2 17. exf7+ Kh8 18. Nxd2 Nxc4 19. Nxc4 Rxc4 20. e5 Bc8 21. e6 Bxe6 22. Rxe6 Rc7 $11 {Marin,Mihail. However, White can transpose to the main game with 16.Bd3}) 16. Bxd5 $16 {Spassky,Boris - Petrosian,Tigran V, Moscow (m/5) 1969 - 7/488}) 15. Bd3 Rc8 {Diagram [#]} 16. d5 $1 $16 {The same pawn break as Carlsen's, but with a different central configuration.} exd5 17. e5 $1 {Ever since this game, this way of reaching a threatening kingside majority has become a well-known pattern.} Nc4 {Unlike Nakamura, Tal seems to be in time to bring his knight into play in time.} (17... g6 18. Qh6 $16) 18. Qf4 Nb2 (18... h6 19. Qf5 g6 20. Qh3 Kg7 21. e6 $16) {Diagram [#]} 19. Bxh7+ $1 {The same as in Carlsen's game, after the initial operation in the centre White sacrifices on the kingside. Tal had doubtlessly seen this sacrifice, but with the rook on d1 hanging and with the possibility of ...Rc4 he might have thought it would not work.} Kxh7 20. Ng5+ Kg6 21. h4 $3 {According to Polugaevsky, this must have been the moment when Tal understood that he was fighting under "unfair" circumstances. Indeed, Polugaevsky's analysis was far from its end yet. ***Polugaevsky mentiones that the last move threatens 22.h5 Kh5 23.g4 Kg6 24.Qf5 Kh6 25.Qh7 Kg5 26.Qf5 Kh4 27.Qh5#.} Rc4 $1 (21... f6 22. h5+ Kxh5 23. g4+ Kh6 24. Qh2+ $18) (21... f5 22. Rd4 $1 $18) 22. h5+ Kh6 23. Nxf7+ Kh7 24. Qf5+ Kg8 25. e6 $1 {Diagram [#] Polugaevsky had reached this position during his analysis when Geller entered his room. Judging from a later comment by Polugaevsky, ("an analysis until move 25") he had stopped here. Of course, excellent intuition is needed to do that, as White still is a piece down, but Polugaevsky rightly evaluated that the e-pawn has a decisive strength. ***To the young modern player, Polugaevsky's analysis may not seem so impressive. But we have to take into account that nowadays one only has to push the engines a bit to work out such an analysis or longer. In 1969, a chess playing computer existed only in the dreams of such visionaries as Botvinnik.} Qf6 (25... Qe7 26. h6 $1 $18) 26. Qxf6 gxf6 27. Rd2 (27. Nd6 $5 Nxd1 28. e7 Rc1 $1) 27... Rc6 (27... Rb4 28. a3 $18) (27... Na4 $5 28. e7 Re8 29. Nd6 Bc6 30. Nxc4 dxc4 31. Rd6 $16) 28. Rxb2 Re8 $2 (28... Bc8 $1 29. Nh6+ $1 (29. e7 Re8 30. Nd8 $16) 29... Kh7 30. Nf5 Rxe6 31. Rc1 $16) 29. Nh6+ $1 Kh7 30. Nf5 Rexe6 31. Rxe6 Rxe6 32. Rc2 Rc6 33. Re2 $18 Bc8 (33... Rc7 34. Re6 $18) 34. Re7+ Kh8 35. Nh4 f5 36. Ng6+ Kg8 37. Rxa7 1-0

