{Grandmaster Aleksandar Matanovic, Chess Informant co-founder turns 90!  Matanovic (Belgrade, 23.V 1930.) won the master title in 1949. after a great success at the Yugoslav national Championship. In 1955 he was awarded the Grandmaster title by FIDE. During his illustrious career he won the Yugoslav Championship on three occasions (1962, 1969 and 1978), and achieved a great number of respectable tournament results (Vienna 1952, Hamburg 1955, Beverwijk 1957, Mar del Plata 1961, Buenos Aires 1961, Bordeaux (1964), Tel Aviv 1966, Luxembourg 1974, Houston 1974 etc.). Matanovic was always highly motivated representing Yugoslavia at the Chess Olympiads and other team competitions, winning a great number of medals with the mighty Yugoslav squad.  Being a chess journalist and publicist, he devoted later years of his career to Chess Informant publications. Since the foundation of our publishing house in 1966, Matanovic engaged himself in creating the System of Signs which became universally accepted, as well as the Encyclopedias of Chess Openings (ECO). The "Informant" periodical, containing all the best games played worldwide, gained respect very quickly, becoming invaluable source for preparation and training of both the chess elite, and chess enthusiasts all over the world. Also, Matanovic published several game collections and instructive chess manuals, for both the chess novice and experienced players. On this occasion, we wish Mr.Matanovic happy Birthday and good health...  *** Aleksandar Matanovic  A Tribute to Chess Legend  Ever since the end of the 5th century AD, when a chess new-born cried for the very first time in the South-eastern India, until today, after so many centuries that embellished its 64 magical squares, many famous people left their traces on those squares and polished that child of civilization to its modern-day perfection. The chess journey from the Far and Middle East to Europe and America ended with a great epilogue in the form of increased public interest for the struggles of elite players.  The evolution led to the organization of a match for the title of World Champion in 1886 and, then, subsequently to the foundation of the World Chess Organization - FIDE in 1924 in Paris. The first half of the twentieth century thundered dramatically with its real cannons and armies several times, forcing the chess muse to be silent. But, after the World War II a new life began and raised chess to the very peaks of popularity. The first country to establish its chess supremacy and global domination was the USSR. On the other hand, Yugoslavia became its closest rival on the planetary scene; a role which Yugoslavs played confidently for 25 years. After the great successes achieved by Pirc, Trifunovic and Gligoric, the talented trio - Matanovic, Fuderer and Ivkov entered the scene, announcing new astounding achievements of chess in the post-war Jugoslavija.  While Fuderer abandoned chess and devoted himself to another profession, Matanovic and Ivkov continued on chess paths and achieved results of great respect. Their success is all the more valuable because at that time it was difficult to gain theoretical knowledge in Jugoslavija (or anywhere outside the USSR), as it was available to Soviet chess players. So, our players discovered the truth and gained knowledge thanks to their enormous talent. We should ask ourselves how much they would have accomplished if they had had the conditions like their colleagues from the USSR.  Matanovic was one of only forty grandmasters in the world when he won the title in 1955. Quite a lot has been written about him, along with biographical data and games, so I will try to depict his chess biography and portrait with a few spicy details from my memory. Aleksandar Matanovic (May 23, 1930), called Beka by his close friends, was a chess-player of a classical positional style, very strong in manoeuvring play. He was able to outplay weaker opponents routinely as an experienced strategist, while keeping the main weapons for the chess elite. He seems to have been strongly influenced by Mikhail Botvinnik, whom Matanovic greatly appreciated. When I got to know him better, I wondered why a player of his class did not have greater ambition, because in my opinion, that lack of ambition denied him a breakthrough into the very elite of chess.  Similar to Matanovic was Carl Schlechter, a great intellectual, editor of the most respected chess magazine "Deutsche Schachzeitung", which means that he was well versed in theoretical achievements of his era. Emanuel Lasker praised him greatly, noting that "because of the peaceful life he leads, Schlechter is not ready to make maximum efforts to take the title of the World Champion".  I was a junior chess-player when I first met Aleksandar Matanovic. It was back in 1963 when he was a guest in Montenegro and played a simultaneous session in Cetinje. Chess was adored in the town under the mountain Lovcen, so the participants of the simultaneous display, as well as the observers, stirred the occasion on the actual games and interesting conversation with the grandmaster. I was among the players as the best junior in Montenegro. Matanovic's next performance in Montenegro occurred two years later, at the Yugoslav Championship in Podgorica (Titograd). The participation of three favourites caused great interest of a large audience. Gligoric, Ivkov and Matanovic were all playing (Podgorica 24.11-19.12.1966.).  Interesting battles for the crown run thorough the entire tournament. Eventually Gligoric managed to win the Championship in the finish. That event gave a huge boost to the development and popularization of chess in Montenegro. I had the opportunity to meet Gligoric, Ivkov, Parma and other players, but Matanovic remained a mystery to me for a long time because of the serious and icy expression on his face, never showing any emotions. Later I realized that his expression hid a modest and, above all, wise man with a lot of revolutionary ideas, a man who knew how to analyze all the circumstances in life as well as any given position on a chessboard. This is best confirmed by his dedication to the edition of "Informator" (Chess Informant) and "Encyclopedia of Chess Openings", which were created as a result of practical considerations in the process of improving the training of chess players outside the USSR, who were handicapped by the lack of theoretical knowledge and information. Together with his associates, Matanovic proved to be a forerunner of the computer era, since the Chess Informant publications became important sources for theoretical preparation of professional players, as well as universal chess manuals that conquered the world and found place in the cultural institutions of many countries. Chess players from Cetinje were frequent visitors to the local library and learned chess by following the Chess Informant editions. In the mid-1990s, Matanovic provided several volumes of the Informant periodical and two volumes of the "Encyclopedia" that were missing from the library. That wonderful gesture of his remained in the memory of the chess-players from Cetinje.  My personal encounters with Matanovic over the chess board resulted in fighting and beautiful games. Also, I remember two interesting blitz games in which I discovered the cheerful and witty side of his character, as well as need to warn a friend of possible troubles and suspicious circumstances he may face during the chess battle. The first meeting took place in Pula, when I passed by a tennis court in the company of my team mates. Some people with children came across, and I noticed a man who, due to being terribly overweight, was moving slowly. - Hi Beka, how are you feeling here? - the man said, smiling. - Thank you, it's really nice, Matanovic said briefly. - And I got too much weight, the stranger continued, so I go down the rocks every day to jump into the sea and swim. - I see. I was really surprised, said Matanovic, and almost asked you if you hid the ball from the children?! I was really glad to witness this episode, because I met Matanovic who, for a moment, replaced his icy facade with a warm smile, showing that it protects him from the interrogative glances of his curious rivals. Next time we meet in 2001 in Backa Palanka, where the Assembly of the Chess Federation of Serbia and Montenegro was held. After I was elected President of the Federation, numerous delegates, chess players and guests approached to congratulate me and wish me successful work. Finally, Matanovic approached, shook my hand and said quietly: - Boza, accept my condolences! This was a perfectly clear and short message in the moments when there was no much time to talk, and it was said by an excellent connoisseur of the situation in our chess organization. I took it seriously given that events in the country had taken a sad turn a while back.  Couple of months later, I felt all the intensity of Matanovic's "congratulations" when a group of chess conspirators tried to blame me for their own misconducts. Unfortunately, it was the end of once highly respectable Chess Federation of Jugoslavija, and an extremely sad moment for chess in Belgrade, a city which along with Moscow and Saint Petersburg, was considered one of the chess centres in the world.  Nevertheless, Aleksandar Matanovic, who spoke about his friend Svetozar Gligoric at the commemoration in 2012, took on the role of the oldest legend and, along with Bora Ivkov and Ljubomir Ljubojevic, remained the greatest role model of young generations as a man whose life achievements inspire hope in the renaissance of chess in Beograd.  Grandmaster Bozidar Ivanovic   Cetinje, May 2020.  ***  We will begin the short selection of games with a thrilling encounter against the strongest player of that era!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 Nbd7 7. Bc4 Qa5 {This move was one of the products of the Soviet chess cuisine. Black makes a queen sortie, develping his queenside and postponing castling, and trying to prove advantage on that side of the board. On his part, White must bring his king to the safety first.} 8. Qd2 e6 9. O-O-O b5 10. Bb3 Bb7 11. f3 {This is quite an important concession for Black in this line.} ({In case of} 11. Rhe1 Be7 (11... Rc8 12. f4 b4 13. Nd5 exd5 14. exd5+ Kd8 {is not satisfactory since White covers the e4 square and is prepared for the advance of his kingside pawns, h4, g4 and so on.}) 12. f4 Nc5 13. e5 dxe5 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. fxe5 Bh4 16. g3 Be7 17. Bxe6 O-O 18. Bb3 Rad8 19. Qf4 b4 20. Na4 Nxb3+ $1 (20... h6 $2 21. Nxc5 $16 Qxc5 22. h4 Bd5 23. Nf5 Bxb3 24. axb3 Rxd1+ 25. Rxd1 Rc8 26. Qe4 Bf8 27. e6 fxe6 28. Qxe6+ Kh8 29. Qe4 Qc6 30. Qd3 Re8 31. h5 Be7 32. Nxe7 Rxe7 33. Qg6 {Diagram [#]} Qxg6 (33... Qe8 $2 34. g4 $1 $18 {Spassky,Boris - Polugaevsky,Lev, USSR (ch) 1958}) 34. hxg6 Re8 35. Rd4 a5 36. c4 $16) (20... Nxa4 21. Nf5 Rxd1+ 22. Rxd1 Bd8 23. e6 Nc5 (23... fxe6 24. Nh6+ $18) 24. e7 $16) 21. axb3 g6 $11) 11... Be7 12. Kb1 $5 {With the black bishop on e7, the idea of Nd5 is more promising. It is questionable if Black can secure enough defensive resources.} b4 13. Nd5 {Diagram [#]} exd5 14. Nf5 Bf8 15. exd5 O-O-O ({A month later, at the Muenchen Chess Olympiad, in the game against Lokvenc, Tal played the stronger} 15... Qb6 {(Lokvenc,Josef - Tal, Mihail, Muenchen (ol) 1958)} 16. Ba4 O-O-O 17. Bxd7+ Rxd7 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Rhe1 { Qf4-h4 with compensation. The Austrian player did not manage to find the best formation for his forces and quickly lost the game.}) 16. a3 h6 17. axb4 Qc7 18. Bf4 g6 {White intends c4 and Nd4 with strong pressure against the black king. Black correctly options for a pawn sacrifice, displacing the white pieces in the process.} 19. Nxh6 Ne5 20. Bg5 {White intends Rhe1 and f4. With the material equality on the board, White should have the upper hand in the endgame, while Black hopes to clinch a draw.} Bxh6 21. Bxh6 Nc4 22. Bxc4 Qxc4 23. Bg7 Nxd5 24. b3 Qxb4 ({Also possible is} 24... Qf4 25. Qxf4 Nxf4 26. Bxh8 Rxh8 27. Rxd6 Nxg2 28. Rf6) 25. Qxb4 Nxb4 26. Bxh8 Rxh8 27. Rxd6 Nd5 28. c4 Ne3 ({In case of} 28... Kc7 {then} 29. c5) 29. Rf6 Nxg2 30. Rxf7 Rd8 31. Kc2 Nh4 32. Re1 {White is ready to position the other rook on the 7th rank. Here, Black's chances for survival are minuscule.} Rd7 33. Re8+ Kc7 34. Ree7 Rxe7 35. Rxe7+ Kb6 36. f4 {White is able to protect all his pawns and bring the king to the centre, or to the queenside. On his part, Black is trying to reach safe waters.} Ng2 37. Re6+ Ka7 38. Rf6 Be4+ 39. Kc3 a5 ({After} 39... Bf5 40. Kb4 Nxf4 41. Ka5 {the two protected passers should decide the game.}) 40. Kb2 a4 {Diagram [#]} 41. b4 {Threatening Ka3-a4...} ({White wins ever quicker after} 41. bxa4 Bf5 42. Kb3 Nxf4 43. h4 {and Black is hopeless.}) 41... Ne3 42. Re6 ({A bit weaker is} 42. Kc3 a3 43. b5 Nd1+ 44. Kd4 (44. Kd2) 44... Bb7) 42... Nxc4+ 43. Kc3 Bd5 44. Rxg6 {The ending is clearly better for White, since he has two strong passed pawns on the kingside and a well positioned rook. The only trump in Black's hands is his a-passer.} Nb6 45. h4 a3 46. b5 {Making room for the king, and preparing Ra6 check when possible and necessary.} Bf7 47. Rg1 a2 {Immediately leads to defeat.} ({In case of} 47... Nd5+ 48. Kb3 Kb6 {White is winning after Rg5}) 48. Kb2 Kb8 49. Rg7 Bb3 50. Rg1 Bf7 51. Ka1 Kc8 52. Rg7 Be8 53. Rg5 Nd7 54. h5 Nf6 55. h6 Kc7 56. Rg7+ Kb6 57. Re7 {Black resigns.} 1-0

