{Shirov made his first top class appearence at the Manila 1990 Interzonal Tournament. Coincidence made that this was the last Interzonal of another brilliant tactician from Riga, Mihail Tal.  In the first Informant, there are several games by Tal, but his overall score is not as impressive as one might expect: five wins, three losses and one draw. Moreover, most of his opponents were supposedly less strong than him.  There is one interesting game, though, which bears some mechanical similarity with Shirov's. Shirov made his first top class appearence at the Manila 1990 Interzonal Tournament. Coincidence made that this was the last Interzonal of another brilliant tactician from Riga, Mihail Tal.  In the first Informant, there are several games by Tal, but his overall score is not as impressive as one might expect: five wins, three losses and one draw. Moreover, most of his opponents were supposedly less strong than him.  There is one interesting game, though, which bears some mechanical similarity with Shirov's. Shirov made his first top class appearence at the Manila 1990 Interzonal Tournament. Coincidence made that this was the last Interzonal of another brilliant tactician from Riga, Mihail Tal. In the first Informant, there are several games by Tal, but his overall score is not as impressive as one might expect: five wins, three losses and one draw. Moreover, most of his opponents were supposedly less strong than him. There is one interesting game, though, which bears some mechanical similarity with Shirov's.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Qb6 4. Nc3 e6 5. O-O Nd4 6. Ba4 $1 Qa5 $2 {It is obvious that Black is messing too much around with the time. It is obvious that Black is messing too much around with the time. It is obvious that Black is messing too much around with the time.} 7. a3 $1 b5 8. b4 $1 Qb6 (8... cxb4 9. axb4 Qb6 10. Nxd4 bxa4 11. Nf3 $16) (8... Nxf3+ 9. Qxf3 cxb4 10. axb4 Bxb4 11. Nxb5 $16 {Marin,Mihail}) 9. bxc5 Bxc5 10. Rb1 (10. Nxd4 $1 Bxd4 11. Rb1 {Marin,Mihail}) 10... a6 (10... Nxf3+ $1 11. Qxf3 a6 $13 {Marin,Mihail}) 11. Nxd4 Bxd4 12. Ne2 Nf6 13. d3 Be5 {Diagram [#]} 14. c4 {It may seem that Tal intended to tempt his opponent to play his next move. It may seem that Tal intended to tempt his opponent to play his next move. It may seem that Tal intended to tempt his opponent to play his next move.} (14. d4 Bb8 15. e5 $16 {Marin,Mihail}) 14... Bxh2+ $2 {This allows White to reach a winning setup.} (14... Qc7 {was a better way to unpin the b-pawn.} 15. f4 (15. Bc2 Bxh2+ 16. Kh1 Bd6 17. d4 Be7 18. e5 Ng4 19. f3 Nh6 $14) 15... Bd6 16. Bc2 Be7 17. e5 Ng4 18. d4 Bb7 $14 {In both cases, Black is not without counterplay.}) 15. Kxh2 Qc7+ 16. Bf4 e5 17. Bg5 bxa4 18. Bxf6 $16 gxf6 {Now is the moment when we can see some vague similarity with the previous game. Black's structure is almost identical to that of Erdos, but this time Tal did not have to sacrifice anything (yet). Diagram [#] Now is the moment when we can see some vague similarity with the previous game. Black's structure is almost identical to that of Erdos, but this time Tal did not have to sacrifice anything (yet). Diagram [#] Now is the moment when we can see some vague similarity with the previous game. Black's structure is almost identical to that of Erdos, but this time Tal did not have to sacrifice anything (yet). Diagram [#]} 19. Nc3 Bb7 20. Qf3 Rg8 (20... Ke7 21. Rxb7 Qxb7 22. Nd5+ {leads to similar play as in the game.}) 21. Qxf6 Rg6 22. Qf5 Ke7 23. g3 Rag8 (23... Bc6 24. Nd5+ Bxd5 25. exd5 $18 {Marin,Mihail}) {Diagram [#]} 24. Rxb7 $5 Qxb7 25. Rb1 Qc8 26. Qxe5+ Re6 27. Nd5+ Kf8 28. Qf4 Kg7 29. Kg2 Qc5 30. Rh1 $1 (30. Rb7 Qd6 31. Qf3 Rc8 32. d4 Rxc4 33. Ne3 $18 {Marin,Mihail}) 30... Qxa3 31. Qg5+ Kf8 32. Qe3 Qd6 {White is obviously winning, but Black still has some feeble hopes connected with his passed a-pawn. White is obviously winning, but Black still has some feeble hopes connected with his passed a-pawn. White is obviously winning, but Black still has some feeble hopes connected with his passed a-pawn.} 33. Rxh7 a3 34. Rh1 Re5 35. d4 Rxd5 {Black could not tolerate this knight any longer.} 36. exd5 Qb4 37. c5 a2 38. Qe5 Qb1 {It may seem that Black has got some chances, as his pawn will inevitably promote. While Tal played well in the previous phase, the final attack (probably planned in advance) is typical for his style. Diagram  [#] It may seem that Black has got some chances, as his pawn will inevitably promote. While Tal played well in the previous phase, the final attack (probably planned in advance) is typical for his style. Diagram  [#] It may seem that Black has got some chances, as his pawn will inevitably promote. While Tal played well in the previous phase, the final attack (probably planned in advance) is typical for his style. Diagram [#]} 39. Rh8 $3 a1=Q (39... Rxh8 40. Qxh8+ Ke7 41. d6+ Ke6 42. Qe5# {#}) 40. Qd6+ Kg7 41. Qh6# {#} 1-0

[Event "(m/8)"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
