1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Bg5 O-O 8. e3 Re8 9. Nd2 d6 10. Be2 a6 11. a4 Nbd7 12. O-O Qc7 13. Qc2 Nb6 14. Bf3 {Diagram  [#]} c4 $2 {Tal had an excellent start in the match and he might have felt that he could allow himself all kinds of tactical experiments. However, the text move gives up a pawn for nothing.} (14... Bf5 15. e4 Bd7 16. a5 Nc8 $132 {Tal,Mihail}) 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. a5 Nd7 17. Nce4 Be5 18. Qxc4 Qd8 19. Qa2 f5 20. Nc3 g5 {Nevertheless we cannot but admire him for the serenity with which he launches a not very promising attack a pawn down.} 21. Nc4 g4 22. Be2 Qf6 23. Na4 Kh8 24. g3 h5 25. f4 Bd4 26. Qa3 (26. exd4 Qxd4+ 27. Rf2 Rxe2) 26... Rb8 27. Nab6 h4 28. Rad1 Bxb6 29. axb6 Nc5 {Diagram [#] In his comments Tal displays obvious optimism, considering that White does not have a clear plan, whereas Black’s initiative develops naturally. However, things are rather different from that.} 30. gxh4 $6 {This and the next move, two important strategic concessions, suddenly turn the tables.} (30. Rc1 $1 {( Nxd6)} hxg3 31. hxg3 Ne4 32. Bd3 $5 (32. Kg2 {is also good.}) 32... Nxg3 33. Rf2 {The d6-pawn is doomed and it will soon be White who will attack along the h-file.}) 30... Bd7 31. Qc3 $6 Qxc3 32. bxc3 Bb5 $44 33. Rfe1 {(Pe3)} Ne4 34. Rc1 $2 {Diagram [#]} (34. Rd4 {Tal,Mihail}) 34... Rbc8 $2 {Feeling the course of events to be turning in his favour, Tal had already started to play quickly some moves earlier, even though it was Botvinnik who was in time trouble. He saw that by attacking the c4-knight he would win the exchange and it was only natural to keep the other rook on the e-file.} ({However, the winning move was} 34... Rec8 $1 {as it is essential to keep the b7-pawn defended:} 35. Na5 Bxe2 36. Rxe2 Nxc3 37. Rxc3 Rxc3 38. e4 Kg7 $5 $19 {This is one of the most dramatic cases in which one of the players gave the wrong answer to the question “which rook?”. And it once again highlights the importance of every single move in the Benoni.}) 35. Na5 Bxe2 36. Rxe2 Nxc3 $2 {Going all the way.} (36... Re7 $1 37. c4 $13 {remains unclear.}) 37. Rxc3 $1 Rxc3 38. Nxb7 Rexe3 39. Rxe3 Rxe3 40. Nxd6 Rd3 {Diagram [#] At this point the game was adjourned and Botvinnik sealed his move. Tal started his home analysis in a good mood, thinking he was winning, but it was not long before he discovered the opposite was true.} 41. Nf7+ $1 {And on the day of resumption of play, after checking that Botvinnik had sealed the winning move, Tal resigned at once.} ({Initially he had calculated} 41. b7 Rb3 42. Nf7+ Kh7 $1 43. Nd8 a5 44. d6 a4 45. d7 a3 46. Ne6 a2 $19) (41. Nf7+ $1 {forces the king to step on to an exposed square:} Kg7 (41... Kh7 {fails to attack the knight and loses to} 42. d6) 42. b7 Rb3 43. Nd8 a5 (43... Kf6 44. d6 a5 45. d7 Ke7 46. Nc6+ Kxd7 47. b8=Q Rxb8 48. Nxb8+ Kd6 49. h5 {Marin,Mihail}) 44. d6 a4 45. d7 a3 46. Ne6+ {gaining a tempo on the line above. Tal,Mihail}) 1-0

