1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Nge2 c5 7. Be3 Nbd7 8. Qd2 a6 9. O-O-O Qa5 10. Kb1 b5 11. Nd5 {Diagram [#]} Nxd5 $3 {At the time a really brilliant queen sacrifice. Nowadays it is thoroughly studied and considered to be completely correct. Therefore, White players tend to avoid this variation.} 12. Qxa5 ({White may choose to ignore the offered queen, but in that scenario Black surely gets the edge. After a possible queen swap, Black should be capable of exploiting a really poor development of White`s pieces and execute a favourable break in the centre} 12. cxd5 Qxd2 13. Rxd2 f5 $1 $36) 12... Nxe3 13. Rc1 ({Ten years later, an interesting correspondence game was published by Chess Informant} 13. Rd3 Nxc4 14. Qe1 Rb8 15. h4 cxd4 $1 16. Nxd4 Nc5 17. Rd1 Na4 $17 {Beyen,Roland - Klompus,Grigory Yakovlevich, corr. 1968 - 5/650}) 13... Nxc4 14. Rxc4 bxc4 $15 15. Nc1 Rb8 16. Bxc4 Nb6 17. Bb3 Bxd4 $17 18. Qd2 Bg7 19. Ne2 c4 20. Bc2 c3 21. Qd3 cxb2 22. Nd4 Bd7 23. Rd1 Rfc8 24. Bb3 Na4 25. Bxa4 Bxa4 26. Nb3 Rc3 27. Qxa6 Bxb3 28. axb3 Rbc8 29. Qa3 Rc1+ 30. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 0-1

