1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. Bd3 c5 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Qe2 O-O $6 {Already a significant inaccuracy, allowing White an optimum attacking setup. Black should play 9...Qc7.} 10. Bg5 Qa5+ 11. c3 Be7 12. Ne5 b6 $6 {An incredibly risky attempt to solve the problem of developing Black's Q bishop.} 13. Bb5 $1 ({Both} 13. Nc6 Qxg5 {and}) (13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Nc4 Qg5 15. Qe4 Qf5 $1 16. Qxa8 $2 Qxd3 {are fine for Black, so Tal sets his sights on trapping the Black queen.}) 13... a6 14. Bc6 Ra7 15. b4 Qa3 16. Qc2 {"And wins," continued Tal's analysis (when playing 13.Bb5), e.g. 16...b5 17.Nc4! bxc4 18.Bc1 etc. However Black has another way to save his queen.} Nd5 $1 17. Bxd5 exd5 18. Bc1 Bf5 $1 {The point behind Black's play. I was beginning to feel confident for the first time in the game but Tal hadn't finished with the tactical tricks.} 19. Bxa3 $1 Bxc2 20. Nc6 {White was now anticipating 20...Rc7 21.Nxe7+ Rxe7+ 22.Kd2 Be4 23.b5 1-0. However White's underdeveloped army and king in the centre gives Black a chance to confuse matters with a rook sacrifice.} Bf6 $1 21. Nxa7 ({Played reluctantly after great thought. It is too late to bail out with} 21. Kd2 {since} Rc7 {is good for Black, e.g.} 22. b5 Ra8 $3 23. Kxc2 {axb5.}) 21... Bxc3+ 22. Ke2 Re8+ 23. Kf3 Re6 $2 ({When considering 21.Nxa7 Tal was very worried by} 23... d4 $1 {keeping alive options such as ...Be4+ and ...Ra8. Post-game analysis tended to give White an edge after} 24. Rac1 Be4+ 25. Kg3 Ra8 26. Rhe1 $1 {but in any case,}) (23... d4 {was the only consistent way to play since after Tal's reply to}) (23... Re6 $2 {the game is essentially over.}) 24. Rhc1 $1 Be4+ 25. Ke2 $1 d4 26. f3 {1-0 After} Bf5+ 27. Kf2 Re3 $5 28. Rab1 $1 {wins comfortably enough.} 1-0

